window.EXAM_QUESTIONS = [{"id": 1001, "domain": "communication_and_collaboration", "chapter": 11, "question": "KP, a 63 year old man who has used inhalers for 20 years, is prescribed a new fluticasone and vilanterol inhaler. He waves away counselling, saying he knows how to use it. The pharmacist has noticed him exhaling into the device in the past. How should the pharmacist respond?", "options": {"A": "Insist on full counselling and withhold the inhaler until he sits through the whole explanation", "B": "Hand him the leaflet and mention that incorrect technique can reduce how well the inhaler works", "C": "Respect his experience, then ask permission to watch his technique with this device", "D": "Accept his refusal and move on, since the pharmacist cannot require counselling from an experienced user"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Respect his experience, then ask permission to watch his technique with this device): Respecting the patient's expertise while asking permission to observe invites cooperation and lets the pharmacist check technique, which is commonly faulty even in long term users. Refusing to engage misses a real problem, forcing counselling creates a power struggle, and a leaflet alone does not confirm technique."}, {"id": 1002, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 4, "question": "JT is a 9 month old girl with diaper area dermatitis that is bright red and shiny with satellite pustules. Her father asks about using the hydrocortisone cream that was left over from his own prescription. Which statement about this situation is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "A zinc oxide barrier alone will clear pustules caused by yeast within two days of use", "B": "Satellite pustules suggest candidiasis, which needs an antifungal and may worsen with a topical steroid", "C": "Hydrocortisone use on this infant is acceptable if combined with talc powder to keep skin dry", "D": "Hydrocortisone is first line for any diaper rash and should be applied for two weeks"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Satellite pustules suggest candidiasis, which needs an antifungal and may worsen with a topical steroid): Bright red, shiny skin with satellite pustules is the typical picture of Candida infection. An antifungal such as clotrimazole or nystatin is needed, and a steroid can worsen the yeast. Barrier cream helps prevention but does not clear yeast, and talc is discouraged in infants because of inhalation risk."}, {"id": 1003, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "A girl of about twelve arrives to collect her mother's tramadol prescription, saying her mother is at work. She has the prescription receipt but no note, and the pharmacy has the mother's phone number on file. What is the best action for the pharmacist?", "options": {"A": "Tell her to come back with an adult, without trying to contact her mother", "B": "Phone the mother to confirm who may collect it, and hold the opioid until confirmed", "C": "Release it because she has the receipt and the pharmacy knows the family well", "D": "Release it after the girl signs the book, since opioids need a signature at release"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Phone the mother to confirm who may collect it, and hold the opioid until confirmed): Opioids are higher risk and a child cannot be assumed to be an authorized agent. Because the mother can be reached, a call can resolve it safely. Releasing on a receipt, or sending the girl away without trying to reach the patient, either risks harm or creates an unnecessary delay."}, {"id": 1004, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "A pharmacist designs a compounded 10 mg/mL oral solution and adds a preservative that is effective against bacteria and fungi in the product. A colleague suggests this allows the pharmacy to assign a beyond-use date of one year. What is the pharmacist's best response?", "options": {"A": "Preservatives matter only at room temperature, so a refrigerated solution may keep a one year date", "B": "A preservative removes the need for stability data, so the date may follow supplier expiry dates", "C": "A preservative controls microbial growth but does not extend the date beyond what stability data support", "D": "A preservative protects drug from chemical breakdown as well, so a one year date is supportable"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A preservative controls microbial growth but does not extend the date beyond what stability data support): A preservative reduces the risk of microbial contamination, but the beyond-use date also depends on chemical stability, the container, and storage. Without stability evidence for the full period, the date cannot be extended, and it cannot exceed the earliest expiry of an ingredient. Refrigeration and preservatives address different risks."}, {"id": 1005, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 6, "question": "TS is a 33 year old starting leuprolide depot 3.75 mg monthly for endometriosis pain. Her gynecologist also prescribed norethindrone acetate 5 mg daily and asks the pharmacist to counsel her. She asks why a second hormone is needed when the first already controls her pain. What should the pharmacist explain?", "options": {"A": "It prevents the initial flare of pain that occurs in the second month of leuprolide", "B": "It is needed to prevent pregnancy, because leuprolide alone does not suppress ovulation reliably", "C": "It increases the leuprolide effect on the lesions so a lower dose can be used", "D": "Add-back therapy limits bone loss and hot flashes while preserving pain relief"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Add-back therapy limits bone loss and hot flashes while preserving pain relief): Leuprolide lowers estrogen to menopausal levels, which causes bone loss and vasomotor symptoms. Low dose add-back therapy with a progestin restores enough hormone to protect bone and ease symptoms without losing most of the pain benefit. The add-back drug does not enhance leuprolide's effect, and the flare occurs early in treatment."}, {"id": 1006, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "PW, a registered pharmacist, is charged with impaired driving after an evening off. No pharmacy duties are involved and no patients are affected. PW asks a colleague whether the college needs to be told. The colleague, who knows registrant obligations, should say that PW:", "options": {"A": "Must report the charge within the by-law deadline, because registrants disclose charges in any setting", "B": "Should wait for a finding of guilt, since only convictions must be reported to the college", "C": "Should tell the employer only, because off-duty offences fall under employment law rather than regulatory authority", "D": "Should report only if the charge involves pharmacy practice, since the college regulates only practice"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Must report the charge within the by-law deadline, because registrants disclose charges in any setting): Most pharmacy regulators require registrants to report criminal charges and findings promptly, because conduct outside work can bear on trust and fitness to practise. The duty is triggered by the charge itself, not by a conviction or a link to pharmacy work."}, {"id": 1007, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "NA is a 58 year old man who telephones the pharmacy with chest pain and asks whether to take his enteric-coated aspirin 81 mg for a suspected heart attack while waiting for the ambulance. What should the pharmacist advise regarding the dosage form?", "options": {"A": "Chew the tablets rather than swallowing them whole, since the coating delays absorption", "B": "Take the tablet only after the ambulance arrives, since the coating cannot be altered earlier", "C": "Dissolve the tablet under the tongue because the coating allows direct absorption there", "D": "Swallow the tablet whole with water, since chewing makes the drug irritate the stomach lining"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Chew the tablets rather than swallowing them whole, since the coating delays absorption): In suspected acute coronary syndrome, rapid antiplatelet effect is needed, so aspirin should be chewed to bypass the delay caused by an enteric coating. The patient should also call emergency services. Swallowing whole delays the benefit."}, {"id": 1008, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "A pharmacy prepares a weekly blister pack for a patient who takes an orally disintegrating olanzapine tablet in the evening, along with three other tablets. The technician asks if all four drugs can go in the same cell. Which statement about the olanzapine product is correct?", "options": {"A": "Include it, since all tablets are equally stable once they are placed in blister packaging", "B": "Include it, but place the sealed card in the refrigerator to protect the tablet from humidity", "C": "Exclude it, since orally disintegrating tablets break down when exposed to moisture in the card", "D": "Exclude it only if the patient takes more than two other tablets in that evening cell"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Exclude it, since orally disintegrating tablets break down when exposed to moisture in the card): Orally disintegrating tablets dissolve with a small amount of moisture and are fragile, so they do not tolerate repackaging into cards that are exposed to air and handling. They are best left in their original blister and dispensed separately. Chilling the card adds condensation rather than protection."}, {"id": 1009, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "A 17 year old patient who uses they/them pronouns presents a prescription for estradiol from a gender clinic. The parent who accompanies them asks the pharmacist to explain why the patient takes the drug. The patient, who is capable of making their own health decisions, has not agreed to any sharing. What should the pharmacist do?", "options": {"A": "Protect confidentiality, politely decline to explain, and invite the parent to talk with the patient", "B": "Tell the parent only that the drug is a hormone, which reveals no specific diagnosis", "C": "Refuse to dispense until the clinic confirms that the parent has been informed of the treatment", "D": "Explain the purpose to the parent, since parents are entitled to information about their minor children"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Protect confidentiality, politely decline to explain, and invite the parent to talk with the patient): A capable young person has the right to confidentiality about their own health information, including gender-affirming treatment, and the pharmacist should not disclose the drug's purpose or class without consent. Inviting the parent to speak with the patient respects both parties. Withholding the drug, or partially disclosing the drug class, still breaches privacy or harms the patient."}, {"id": 1010, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "LN is a 69 year old woman with cancer pain who uses a fentanyl 25 mcg/h patch changed every 72 hours and nothing else. For a refill review, the pharmacist converts the patch into morphine milligram equivalents using the Canadian guideline factor of 2.4 per mcg/h. What daily MME applies?", "options": {"A": "About 25 MME per day, found by using the mcg/h figure as written", "B": "About 60 MME per day, found by multiplying 25 by 2.4", "C": "About 150 MME per day, found by multiplying 25 by a factor of 6", "D": "About 600 MME per day, found by multiplying 25 by a factor of 24"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (About 60 MME per day, found by multiplying 25 by 2.4): The Canadian guideline converts transdermal fentanyl at 2.4 MME per mcg/h, so 25 x 2.4 = 60 MME per day. The patch strength is not a morphine figure, and using it directly would understate the opioid load."}, {"id": 1011, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "A 2 year old girl weighing 12 kg is prescribed acetaminophen 160 mg orally every 4 hours as needed for fever, up to six doses daily. The pharmacist applies the maximum of 75 mg/kg/day. What is the best course of action?", "options": {"A": "Change the order to 120 mg every four hours, which keeps the total under 750 mg", "B": "Dispense as written, since the 75 mg/kg/day maximum applies only to scheduled doses and not prn", "C": "Contact the prescriber, since six doses total 960 mg, above her 900 mg daily limit", "D": "Dispense as written, since 160 mg per dose is below 15 mg/kg and is therefore safe"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Contact the prescriber, since six doses total 960 mg, above her 900 mg daily limit): At 75 mg/kg/day a 12 kg child can receive 900 mg in 24 hours. Six doses of 160 mg total 960 mg, so the order allows more than the limit even though each single dose is reasonable. The prescriber should correct the order, for example to five doses, rather than the pharmacist altering it alone, and the maximum applies to prn orders as well."}, {"id": 1012, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "A pharmacy bulletin summarizes a bioequivalence study that compares a new generic tablet with the reference product in healthy volunteers. A pharmacy student asks which pharmacokinetic parameters describe the rate and the extent of drug absorption. Which pair is correct?", "options": {"A": "Cmax reflects the extent of absorption and clearance reflects the rate of absorption", "B": "Cmax reflects the rate of absorption and AUC reflects the extent of absorption", "C": "Tmax reflects the extent of absorption and half-life reflects the rate of absorption", "D": "AUC reflects the rate of absorption and Cmax reflects the total amount eliminated"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Cmax reflects the rate of absorption and AUC reflects the extent of absorption): Cmax is the peak concentration and is used as a measure of absorption rate, while AUC represents total exposure and therefore extent of absorption. Half-life and clearance describe elimination, not absorption."}, {"id": 1013, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "FB is a 60 year old man on testosterone cypionate 200 mg intramuscularly every two weeks. His hematocrit today is 0.55 and his PSA is stable at 1.5 mcg/L. He feels well and reports no leg swelling. What should the pharmacist recommend to the physician?", "options": {"A": "Switch to oral testosterone undecanoate, because oral products do not affect red cell production in men", "B": "Continue the same dose since a hematocrit up to 0.60 is acceptable in treated men", "C": "Add aspirin 81 mg daily and continue testosterone, because antiplatelet therapy removes the clot risk", "D": "Hold or reduce the testosterone dose and recheck hematocrit, since erythrocytosis raises thrombosis risk"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Hold or reduce the testosterone dose and recheck hematocrit, since erythrocytosis raises thrombosis risk): Testosterone stimulates erythropoiesis, and a hematocrit above about 0.54 calls for dose reduction or a temporary hold and reassessment. Aspirin does not correct the underlying viscosity problem, and other formulations also raise hematocrit."}, {"id": 1014, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A pharmacist designs a case-control review that selects 150 hospitalized patients and 150 non-hospitalized patients, then records prior use of either of two diabetes drugs. The team wants a ratio summarizing the association with drug exposure. Which measure can be validly calculated?", "options": {"A": "A hazard ratio, because hospitalization is a time to event outcome in people with diabetes", "B": "A relative risk, because the investigators fixed the numbers of hospitalized and non-hospitalized patients in advance", "C": "An odds ratio, comparing the odds of prior drug use in cases and controls", "D": "A number needed to treat, because it converts the exposure ratio into a count of patients"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (An odds ratio, comparing the odds of prior drug use in cases and controls): A case-control design selects participants by outcome, so the proportions of cases and controls are set by the investigators and risks cannot be calculated. The odds ratio of exposure is valid in this design. A hazard ratio needs follow-up time data, and a number needed to treat needs actual risks."}, {"id": 1015, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "Using the oxycodone conversion factor of 1.5 MME per mg, what is OD's total daily opioid dose in morphine milligram equivalents from her controlled release and immediate release oxycodone, and how does it compare with Canadian guideline thresholds for chronic pain?", "options": {"A": "60 MME per day, which exceeds the 50 MME watchful dose but stays under 90 MME", "B": "130 MME per day, which exceeds the 90 MME threshold by a wide margin", "C": "97.5 MME per day, which exceeds the 90 MME threshold in the Canadian guideline", "D": "65 MME per day, which exceeds the 50 MME watchful dose but stays under 90 MME"}, "cluster_id": "CHS1", "cluster_text": "OD is a 64 year old woman with chronic low back pain and osteoarthritis. Her medications are oxycodone CR 30 mg twice daily, oxycodone IR 5 mg once daily, lorazepam 1 mg at bedtime, and acetaminophen 500 mg as needed. She drinks a glass of wine most evenings and has untreated mild sleep apnea. Use 1.5 MME per mg of oxycodone. Her physician plans a medication review.", "cluster_size": 3, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (97.5 MME per day, which exceeds the 90 MME threshold in the Canadian guideline): Oxycodone totals 30 mg x 2 + 5 mg = 65 mg per day, and 65 x 1.5 = 97.5 MME. The factor for oxycodone must be applied, so the raw milligram total of 65 is not the answer. The result lies above the 90 MME level at which guidelines advise strong caution."}, {"id": 1016, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "OD's physician asks the pharmacist to identify the greatest safety concern in her current regimen and health history, which includes oxycodone, lorazepam, nightly wine and untreated sleep apnea. Considering all of her medications, alcohol use and conditions, which combination poses the highest risk?", "options": {"A": "Lorazepam with wine alone is the main concern, and the opioid contributes little to the risk", "B": "Acetaminophen and oxycodone together raise hepatotoxic risk to a level well above any other concern", "C": "Oxycodone CR and oxycodone IR together raise the risk of serotonin syndrome in older women", "D": "Opioid, benzodiazepine, alcohol and untreated sleep apnea together raise the risk of fatal respiratory depression"}, "cluster_id": "CHS1", "cluster_text": "OD is a 64 year old woman with chronic low back pain and osteoarthritis. Her medications are oxycodone CR 30 mg twice daily, oxycodone IR 5 mg once daily, lorazepam 1 mg at bedtime, and acetaminophen 500 mg as needed. She drinks a glass of wine most evenings and has untreated mild sleep apnea. Use 1.5 MME per mg of oxycodone. Her physician plans a medication review.", "cluster_size": 3, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Opioid, benzodiazepine, alcohol and untreated sleep apnea together raise the risk of fatal respiratory depression): Opioids, benzodiazepines, alcohol and sleep apnea each depress respiration or arousal, and together their effects are additive or synergistic, creating a high risk of overdose and death. Acetaminophen at 500 mg as needed is not near toxic levels. Oxycodone is not a serotonergic drug, and the opioid is a major contributor to the risk."}, {"id": 1017, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "OD takes oxycodone CR 30 mg twice daily plus oxycodone IR and lorazepam, drinks wine most evenings and has untreated sleep apnea. The physician wants to reduce her overall risk of overdose and harm at this visit and asks the pharmacist for a practical, stepwise plan. Which plan should the pharmacist suggest?", "options": {"A": "Offer naloxone, and raise oxycodone IR to three times daily so CR dose can be lowered", "B": "Stop oxycodone abruptly tomorrow, and use acetaminophen alone because it carries no overdose risk", "C": "Offer naloxone, begin a gradual opioid taper, and discuss non-opioid options and tapering the lorazepam later", "D": "Stop lorazepam and wine tonight, and continue oxycodone unchanged since opioid dose is not the problem"}, "cluster_id": "CHS1", "cluster_text": "OD is a 64 year old woman with chronic low back pain and osteoarthritis. Her medications are oxycodone CR 30 mg twice daily, oxycodone IR 5 mg once daily, lorazepam 1 mg at bedtime, and acetaminophen 500 mg as needed. She drinks a glass of wine most evenings and has untreated mild sleep apnea. Use 1.5 MME per mg of oxycodone. Her physician plans a medication review.", "cluster_size": 3, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Offer naloxone, begin a gradual opioid taper, and discuss non-opioid options and tapering the lorazepam later): Naloxone reduces harm from overdose, and a slow taper in steps lowers the opioid burden while limiting withdrawal and pain flares. Non-opioid and non-drug options help sustain function, and the benzodiazepine can be addressed gradually afterward. Abrupt cessation of any of these drugs is hazardous, and increasing the short-acting dose adds risk."}, {"id": 1018, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "A 58 year old man in the coronary care unit has completed his amiodarone loading bolus and now needs the maintenance infusion of 1 mg per minute for 6 hours. The premixed bag contains 450 mg in 250 mL. The pharmacist is asked to verify the pump rate. Which rate is correct?", "options": {"A": "About 17 mL per hour, since the bag holds 3.6 mg per mL as labelled", "B": "About 108 mL per hour, since the bag holds 0.56 mg per mL as labelled", "C": "About 60 mL per hour, since the bag holds 1 mg per mL as labelled", "D": "About 33 mL per hour, since the bag holds 1.8 mg per mL"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (About 33 mL per hour, since the bag holds 1.8 mg per mL): The bag concentration is 450 / 250 = 1.8 mg per mL. One milligram per minute is 60 mg per hour, so 60 / 1.8 = 33.3 mL per hour. Assuming 1 mg per mL would give 60 mL per hour and nearly double the intended dose."}, {"id": 1019, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "A pharmacy's social media coordinator wants to share a patient's handwritten thank-you card, which names the pharmacist who counselled her on a new antidepressant. The card includes the patient's full name and the drug. What is the most appropriate step before posting it?", "options": {"A": "Post it unchanged, because patients who send unsolicited cards are presumed to accept public sharing", "B": "Obtain the patient's written consent and remove the drug name before posting the card", "C": "Post it once the named pharmacist agrees, since the card describes that pharmacist's own work", "D": "Post it with the name blurred, since a thank-you card is a gift to the pharmacy"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Obtain the patient's written consent and remove the drug name before posting the card): A card naming a patient and her medication contains personal health information, so sharing it needs the patient's consent. Removing the drug name also avoids promoting a prescription drug to the public. Consent from the pharmacist or an assumption that a sent card is public does not substitute for the patient's own agreement."}, {"id": 1020, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "A prescription for prednisone 10 mg tablets reads: 40 mg daily for 3 days, then 30 mg daily for 3 days, then 20 mg daily for 3 days, then 10 mg daily for 3 days, then stop. The pharmacist enters the quantity and days supply. Which entry is correct?", "options": {"A": "Quantity 30 tablets with a days supply of 12 days, covering the full taper", "B": "Quantity 40 tablets with a days supply of 12 days, rounded up to a full vial", "C": "Quantity 36 tablets with a days supply of 9 days, based on the highest dose steps", "D": "Quantity 20 tablets with a days supply of 12 days, based on the lowest dose steps"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Quantity 30 tablets with a days supply of 12 days, covering the full taper): The tablets needed are 4 x 3 = 12, then 3 x 3 = 9, then 2 x 3 = 6, then 1 x 3 = 3, for a total of 30 tablets. The taper lasts 12 days, so the days supply is 12. The other entries miscount the dose steps."}, {"id": 1021, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 6, "question": "ZR is a 27 year old with polycystic ovary syndrome, a BMI of 33, and fasting insulin resistance. Her prescriber starts metformin immediate release for metabolic benefit. She has had stomach upset with other medications and asks how to start it. Which titration should the pharmacist describe?", "options": {"A": "Start 850 mg three times daily with meals for the first month, then reduce", "B": "Start 1000 mg twice daily at once, to reach the target dose before her next period", "C": "Start 500 mg at bedtime on an empty stomach, then double the dose every day", "D": "Start 500 mg with supper, then add 500 mg each week as tolerated"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Start 500 mg with supper, then add 500 mg each week as tolerated): Metformin is started low with a meal and increased slowly, commonly by 500 mg each week, to limit nausea and diarrhea. Beginning at full dose or doubling daily increases gastrointestinal effects, and taking it on an empty stomach worsens them. Three daily doses of 850 mg is a full dose, not a starting dose."}, {"id": 1022, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "A pharmacist compounds a 30 g jar of urea 20% in a hydrophilic ointment base on 20 January for a patient (BP) with hyperkeratosis. A published study supports 90 days of room temperature storage in this base. The earliest ingredient expiry is 30 June of the same year, and the year is not a leap year. Which date applies?", "options": {"A": "20 April, because the 90 day study period ends before the earliest ingredient expiry date", "B": "19 February, because preparations that contain urea are limited to 30 days from the preparation date", "C": "20 July, about six months after preparation, a common default for ointments over the study period", "D": "30 June, because the beyond-use date is the earliest ingredient expiry and the study is secondary"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (20 April, because the 90 day study period ends before the earliest ingredient expiry date): Counting 90 days from 20 January in a year with 28 days in February gives 20 April: 11 days left in January, 28 in February, 31 in March and 20 in April. That is earlier than the 30 June ingredient expiry, so the study period is the limit. The June date would only apply if the study supported a longer period."}, {"id": 1023, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A trial reports that a new antidepressant works better in women than in men, based on one of six prespecified subgroup comparisons. A pharmacist wants to judge how credible this subgroup finding is. Which feature would most strengthen its credibility?", "options": {"A": "A statistically significant test of interaction that was specified in advance and has biological plausibility", "B": "Presentation of the subgroup results in the abstract, which signals that the authors considered them important", "C": "A larger number of women than men in the trial, giving more power to detect differences", "D": "A p-value below 0.05 within the female subgroup alone, showing that women respond better than men"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A statistically significant test of interaction that was specified in advance and has biological plausibility): A true subgroup effect is shown by a significant interaction test, which compares the effect between subgroups directly. Prespecification and plausibility reduce the chance of a spurious finding. A p-value within one subgroup says nothing about whether the effect differs from the other group."}, {"id": 1024, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "The order 'Metoprolol 50 mg, 1/2 tab HS' is presented for a 74 year old woman. The pharmacist knows HS can mean at bedtime or half-strength, and that the abbreviation appears on a list of error-prone abbreviations. What should the pharmacist do before labelling?", "options": {"A": "Replace HS with qam on the label so that the half tablet is taken each morning", "B": "Label it as taken at bedtime, since most patients take beta-blockers in the evening", "C": "Confirm the intended timing with the prescriber, then write the directions in full words", "D": "Label it using the abbreviation as written, since the prescriber's own order should not be altered"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Confirm the intended timing with the prescriber, then write the directions in full words): HS is ambiguous because it may mean at bedtime (hora somni) or half-strength, so the pharmacist should confirm the intent and then write the directions out in words. Guessing the meaning, changing the time of day or copying the abbreviation onto the label all leave a risk that the patient takes the dose incorrectly."}, {"id": 1025, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "A pharmacy cannot obtain the 10 mg tablets of a drug prescribed for GM. The wholesaler offers a different manufacturer's product with the same medicinal ingredient, strength and dosage form that is listed as interchangeable. Which action can the pharmacist take without contacting the prescriber?", "options": {"A": "Dispense the other manufacturer's interchangeable product and update the label and profile", "B": "Dispense a smaller quantity of a product with another active ingredient and notify the patient", "C": "Dispense a different drug in the same class at an equal dose and notify the patient", "D": "Dispense a different drug from the same class at half the usual dose and monitor"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Dispense the other manufacturer's interchangeable product and update the label and profile): Interchangeable products with the same ingredient, strength and form can generally be substituted by the pharmacist under provincial interchangeability rules, with the label and profile updated. Switching to a different drug is a therapeutic change that needs prescriber authorization or specific prescribing authority, and altering the dose or quantity of another active ingredient is a clinical decision."}, {"id": 1026, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "JA, a 71 year old man, has been taking his own medications from vials for years, and his daughter says he occasionally gets confused about timing. His pharmacist is considering whether blister packaging suits him. Which step should the pharmacist take before starting blister packaging?", "options": {"A": "Confirm only that the patient has no drugs on a short list of excluded products", "B": "Assess the patient's need and ability to use the pack, and record the assessment", "C": "Start the pack as soon as the patient asks, since packs improve adherence for everyone", "D": "Ask the prescriber to write a separate prescription authorizing the blister packaging service"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Assess the patient's need and ability to use the pack, and record the assessment): Compliance packaging helps some patients but can hide problems or cause errors in others, so the pharmacist assesses need, dexterity, understanding and whether other supports would work better. Recording the assessment shows the decision was reasoned. Prescriber authorization is not required for the packaging itself."}, {"id": 1027, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 1, "question": "DP is a 50 year old man with a pressure of 144/90 mmHg who is otherwise healthy and mostly sedentary. He asks how much exercise would lower his blood pressure. He has no cardiac symptoms and wants a plan he can start safely this month. What should the pharmacist suggest?", "options": {"A": "Rest until his pressure is fully controlled by medication, then begin light stretching three times weekly", "B": "Do 10 minutes of heavy weightlifting on two days each week, since lifting lowers pressure most", "C": "Do 30 to 60 minutes of brisk walking on four to seven days weekly", "D": "Do 15 minutes of vigorous running once weekly, as intense exercise lowers pressure fastest"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Do 30 to 60 minutes of brisk walking on four to seven days weekly): Hypertension Canada advises 30 to 60 minutes of moderate intensity dynamic exercise on four to seven days a week, such as walking, jogging, cycling or swimming. Resistance training can supplement but not replace this. Exercise is encouraged before pressure is fully controlled when there are no cardiac symptoms or severe elevation."}, {"id": 1028, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "MP is a woman at 32 weeks of pregnancy with a tension headache and no other medical problems. She finds ibuprofen 400 mg in her medicine cabinet and asks the pharmacist whether it is acceptable to take it today. What is the best recommendation?", "options": {"A": "Use ibuprofen with food, since the fetal risk comes from stomach irritation in the mother", "B": "Use aspirin 650 mg instead, since it is the safest analgesic in the third trimester", "C": "Use ibuprofen, since NSAIDs are safe throughout pregnancy when taken in doses below 400 mg", "D": "Use acetaminophen, because NSAIDs can harm the fetal ductus arteriosus and kidneys late in pregnancy"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Use acetaminophen, because NSAIDs can harm the fetal ductus arteriosus and kidneys late in pregnancy): NSAIDs given in later pregnancy can cause premature narrowing of the ductus arteriosus and reduced fetal kidney function with low amniotic fluid, so they are avoided from about 20 weeks. Taking them with food does not lessen fetal effects, and aspirin at analgesic doses carries the same concerns. Acetaminophen is the preferred analgesic."}, {"id": 1029, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 4, "question": "A mother reports that permethrin 1% rinse has failed twice in her 10 year old daughter, who still has live lice that move quickly. She followed directions carefully, and no one else in the home has lice. What is the best next recommendation?", "options": {"A": "Use dimeticone 4% lotion on dry hair, a physical-acting product, repeated after one week", "B": "Use hydrocortisone cream on the scalp for a week, since itching means the lice have died", "C": "Repeat permethrin 1% rinse each day for ten days, since more frequent use overcomes lice resistance", "D": "Apply the stronger permethrin 5% scabies cream to the scalp overnight and rinse it off"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Use dimeticone 4% lotion on dry hair, a physical-acting product, repeated after one week): Treatment failure despite correct use suggests permethrin-resistant lice, so a different mechanism is needed. Dimeticone coats and suffocates lice and is not affected by neurotoxin resistance. The 5% scabies cream is not approved for scalp lice, and more frequent permethrin adds irritation only."}, {"id": 1030, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A trial of a new antibiotic for urinary infection reports both intention to treat and per protocol results, and the benefit appears larger in the per protocol analysis. For a superiority claim, why is the intention to treat analysis generally preferred as primary?", "options": {"A": "It increases the sample size by adding patients who were screened but not randomized", "B": "It eliminates missing data, because every participant is assigned an outcome by the study statistician", "C": "It preserves randomization and reflects the effect of the treatment as patients actually use it", "D": "It removes patients who did not take the drug, so the estimate reflects true treatment response"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (It preserves randomization and reflects the effect of the treatment as patients actually use it): Excluding patients who stopped or switched treatment breaks the comparability produced by randomization and often exaggerates benefit. Intention to treat keeps the groups comparable and describes the real world effect of offering a treatment, including imperfect adherence."}, {"id": 1031, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "IV is a 7 year old girl who cannot swallow capsules whole. Her physician prescribed methylphenidate extended-release capsules approved for opening, and her father asks how to give them each morning. She likes applesauce. What counselling should the pharmacist provide about administration?", "options": {"A": "Have her chew the capsule thoroughly with applesauce so the medication absorbs fully before school", "B": "Open the capsule and store the beads in applesauce overnight so the morning dose is ready", "C": "Sprinkle the opened capsule's beads on applesauce and have her swallow them without chewing", "D": "Crush the beads into a fine powder and mix in juice that she can sip slowly"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Sprinkle the opened capsule's beads on applesauce and have her swallow them without chewing): Some extended-release methylphenidate capsules may be opened and the beads sprinkled on a small amount of soft food and swallowed right away without chewing, which keeps the extended-release mechanism intact. Crushing or chewing the beads releases the dose at once and risks toxicity. Stored mixtures should not be kept because the dose must be taken immediately."}, {"id": 1032, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 1, "question": "AG is a 61 year old man arriving at hospital with an ST elevation myocardial infarction and no bleeding risk factors. The cardiologist plans primary PCI and orders ticagrelor as the P2Y12 inhibitor. The pharmacist is asked to confirm the loading dose to give with chewed acetylsalicylic acid. What loading dose of ticagrelor is correct?", "options": {"A": "60 mg once, followed by 10 mg once daily starting the following morning", "B": "300 mg once, followed by 75 mg once daily starting the next day afterward", "C": "90 mg once, followed by 90 mg twice daily with the first maintenance dose that evening", "D": "180 mg once, followed by 90 mg twice daily starting twelve hours later"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (180 mg once, followed by 90 mg twice daily starting twelve hours later): Ticagrelor is loaded at 180 mg and continued at 90 mg twice daily, with acetylsalicylic acid maintenance at 81 mg. The 300 mg and 75 mg doses belong to clopidogrel and the 60 mg and 10 mg doses to prasugrel. A 90 mg load would be an underdose and delays full platelet inhibition during the procedure."}, {"id": 1033, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 4, "question": "NK is a 36 year old woman with Crohn disease taking adalimumab 40 mg every two weeks. Today she has a hot red swollen lower leg, shivering and a temperature of 38.9 C. Her injection is due tonight, and she asks whether to proceed. What should the pharmacist recommend?", "options": {"A": "Hold tonight's injection, seek urgent medical assessment for possible serious infection, and inform the gastroenterologist", "B": "Give the injection as planned, then watch the leg and see her doctor in the morning", "C": "Apply a cold compress, take acetaminophen, and postpone the injection by one week for review", "D": "Give the injection tonight and start leftover antibiotics at home, since she has taken them before"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Hold tonight's injection, seek urgent medical assessment for possible serious infection, and inform the gastroenterologist): Fever with a hot, swollen limb suggests cellulitis, a serious infection that can progress rapidly in someone on a tumour necrosis factor inhibitor. The biologic should not be given tonight, and she needs urgent assessment and appropriate antibiotics prescribed by a clinician. Home measures or leftover antibiotics delay proper care."}, {"id": 1034, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "JW, 24, requests her third salbutamol inhaler in five weeks. She says she uses it 'a few times most days' and wakes some nights coughing. She has not filled her fluticasone inhaler in four months. What is the best approach?", "options": {"A": "Assess control, discuss the missed controller, and arrange a review of her asthma therapy", "B": "Dispense two inhalers so that she has a spare and does not need frequent refills", "C": "Dispense the salbutamol and tell her to call back if she needs another one soon", "D": "Decline the refill until she has filled the controller inhaler at another pharmacy"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Assess control, discuss the missed controller, and arrange a review of her asthma therapy): Frequent reliever use and night symptoms indicate poorly controlled asthma, and the missed inhaled corticosteroid is the likely cause. The refill is needed for safety, but the pharmacist should address adherence and arrange a review rather than simply supplying more reliever."}, {"id": 1035, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 3, "question": "JZ is a 30 year old woman at 26 weeks gestation who is HBsAg positive with HBV DNA of 9,000,000 IU/mL and a normal ALT. Her obstetrician asks the pharmacist how to lower the risk of transmission to the baby beyond infant vaccine and immune globulin. What should the pharmacist recommend?", "options": {"A": "Give peginterferon during the second trimester, which lowers viral load without risk to the fetus", "B": "Start entecavir at 28 weeks, which has the most pregnancy safety data of the antivirals", "C": "Start tenofovir at 28 to 32 weeks and continue until four weeks after delivery", "D": "No maternal antiviral is warranted, since infant immunoprophylaxis alone is considered sufficient for this viral load"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Start tenofovir at 28 to 32 weeks and continue until four weeks after delivery): A maternal HBV DNA above about 200,000 IU/mL is the main risk factor for immunoprophylaxis failure, so tenofovir is started in late pregnancy and continued to about four weeks postpartum. Tenofovir is preferred for its pregnancy safety data and potency, while entecavir and interferon are not recommended in pregnancy. Infant vaccine and immune globulin are still given."}, {"id": 1036, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "BO is a 29 year old woman who wants her oral contraceptive prescription transferred from a pharmacy in another city. The receiving pharmacist is speaking with the other pharmacist by telephone. Which information must the receiving pharmacist record from the first pharmacy?", "options": {"A": "Only the date of the last fill, since the prescription is then rewritten as new", "B": "Original prescriber, date written, quantity, refills remaining, and date of the last fill", "C": "Only the quantity and refill details, since prescriber information is not needed for a transfer", "D": "Only the patient's name and drug, since the rest is found in the provincial database"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Original prescriber, date written, quantity, refills remaining, and date of the last fill): A transferred prescription keeps its original authorization, so the receiving pharmacist records the prescriber, original date, quantity authorized, refills left and the last fill. Without these the new record cannot show how much of the original authorization remains."}, {"id": 1037, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "While preparing a drug information response, a pharmacist finds a favourite textbook that recommends a treatment for a skin infection, but the edition is nine years old. The patient, GH, is a 31 year old woman. Which step best ensures the advice is current before it is given?", "options": {"A": "Check the revision date and confirm with a current guideline or product monograph before advising", "B": "Ask the patient which treatment she prefers and use that even if the sources omit it", "C": "Search only the journals that the textbook cited, since these will contain the most relevant data", "D": "Use the textbook recommendation, since long-standing treatments rarely change enough to alter practice in this area"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Check the revision date and confirm with a current guideline or product monograph before advising): Tertiary sources age quickly, especially for infections where resistance patterns and guidelines change. Checking the edition date and confirming against a current guideline or monograph protects the patient. Patient preference matters but cannot replace verification."}, {"id": 1038, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 6, "question": "MN has used a combined contraceptive patch for two years and is a nonsmoker with a BMI of 27 kg/m2. Her blood pressure today is 168/104 mmHg on repeat measurement. Considering her history and today's readings, what do her findings mean for her continued use of the patch?", "options": {"A": "This level of hypertension makes an estrogen patch inappropriate, so it should be replaced", "B": "The patch is safer than the pill here because it bypasses first-pass liver metabolism of estrogen", "C": "The patch can continue safely if she lowers the dose by cutting each patch in half", "D": "Her blood pressure is acceptable because she is a nonsmoker without diabetes or migraine"}, "cluster_id": "CHS2", "cluster_text": "MN is a 34 year old woman who has used a combined contraceptive patch (norelgestromin and ethinyl estradiol) for two years. She is a nonsmoker with a BMI of 27 kg/m2, no diabetes and no migraine. Today her blood pressure is 168/104 mmHg on repeat measurement after five minutes of rest, and her home readings average 162/100 mmHg. Creatinine is 70 umol/L. She takes no other medications and does not want to become pregnant this year.", "cluster_size": 3, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (This level of hypertension makes an estrogen patch inappropriate, so it should be replaced): A blood pressure of 160/100 mmHg or higher is a contraindication to estrogen-containing contraception because of the risk of stroke and myocardial infarction. A transdermal route does not remove estrogen exposure, and cutting a patch gives unpredictable dosing. She needs an alternative method."}, {"id": 1039, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 6, "question": "Considering her history of a combined contraceptive patch, her blood pressure readings of 168/104 mmHg in the office and 162/100 mmHg at home, and her wish to avoid pregnancy this year, which contraceptive method is the most appropriate alternative to start after the patch is stopped today?", "options": {"A": "A combined pill with a lower ethinyl estradiol dose and a different progestin", "B": "A combined vaginal ring, which delivers a lower daily estrogen dose than the patch", "C": "A weekly estrogen-containing patch worn on the buttock in place of her current site", "D": "A levonorgestrel intrauterine system, which is highly effective and has little effect on blood pressure"}, "cluster_id": "CHS2", "cluster_text": "MN is a 34 year old woman who has used a combined contraceptive patch (norelgestromin and ethinyl estradiol) for two years. She is a nonsmoker with a BMI of 27 kg/m2, no diabetes and no migraine. Today her blood pressure is 168/104 mmHg on repeat measurement after five minutes of rest, and her home readings average 162/100 mmHg. Creatinine is 70 umol/L. She takes no other medications and does not want to become pregnant this year.", "cluster_size": 3, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A levonorgestrel intrauterine system, which is highly effective and has little effect on blood pressure): The levonorgestrel intrauterine system acts locally and has minimal systemic hormone levels, making it suitable in hypertension. The ring, the lower-dose pill and a patch at a new site all still expose her to estrogen and carry the same contraindication. Her goal of avoiding pregnancy for a year is met by a long-acting method."}, {"id": 1040, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 6, "question": "After the contraceptive patch is stopped and a new method is chosen, which blood pressure follow up should the pharmacist arrange for her sustained elevated office readings of 168/104 mmHg and home readings averaging 162/100 mmHg over the next few days?", "options": {"A": "Recheck her blood pressure at the pharmacy in six months after stopping the patch", "B": "Prompt physician assessment to confirm the diagnosis and begin management of hypertension", "C": "Recommend an over-the-counter diuretic until her blood pressure falls below 140/90 mmHg", "D": "Advise a low-sodium diet only and a physician visit if she develops symptoms"}, "cluster_id": "CHS2", "cluster_text": "MN is a 34 year old woman who has used a combined contraceptive patch (norelgestromin and ethinyl estradiol) for two years. She is a nonsmoker with a BMI of 27 kg/m2, no diabetes and no migraine. Today her blood pressure is 168/104 mmHg on repeat measurement after five minutes of rest, and her home readings average 162/100 mmHg. Creatinine is 70 umol/L. She takes no other medications and does not want to become pregnant this year.", "cluster_size": 3, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Prompt physician assessment to confirm the diagnosis and begin management of hypertension): Readings of 168/104 mmHg in the office and 162/100 mmHg at home confirm sustained severe hypertension that needs medical assessment and likely treatment. Waiting months or until symptoms appear risks organ damage. Over-the-counter diuretics are not an appropriate treatment for hypertension."}, {"id": 1041, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "KS, 28, has been seizure-free for three years on one manufacturer's phenytoin capsules 300 mg daily. The supplier now sends another interchangeable product. KS has no other medical problems and asks if he should be concerned about the change. What should the pharmacist recommend?", "options": {"A": "Dispense it, advise watching for unsteadiness or drowsiness, and inform the prescriber about the switch", "B": "Dispense it and tell him that no monitoring is needed after any interchangeable product switch", "C": "Dispense it and lower the dose by one capsule weekly during the first month", "D": "Refuse the switch and order the original manufacturer only, because phenytoin generics are not approved"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Dispense it, advise watching for unsteadiness or drowsiness, and inform the prescriber about the switch): Phenytoin is a narrow therapeutic index drug with nonlinear kinetics, so small exposure changes can cause toxicity or breakthrough seizures. Interchangeable products are acceptable, but the patient should know the signs of toxicity and the prescriber can decide whether a level is warranted."}, {"id": 1042, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A 58 year old postmenopausal woman with estrogen receptor positive breast cancer takes tamoxifen 20 mg daily and paroxetine 20 mg daily for hot flashes and mood. The pharmacist knows paroxetine strongly inhibits CYP2D6 and that endoxifen is the key active metabolite. The best recommendation is to:", "options": {"A": "Replace paroxetine with an antidepressant that does not strongly inhibit CYP2D6, such as venlafaxine", "B": "Continue both drugs and double the tamoxifen dose to compensate for reduced formation of endoxifen", "C": "Stop tamoxifen and rely on paroxetine, because the combination causes dangerous serotonin toxicity", "D": "Continue both drugs and separate their administration times by twelve hours to avoid enzyme inhibition"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Replace paroxetine with an antidepressant that does not strongly inhibit CYP2D6, such as venlafaxine): Strong CYP2D6 inhibitors act like phenotypic poor metabolizers and cut endoxifen formation, which may reduce tamoxifen benefit. Switching to an antidepressant with little CYP2D6 inhibition keeps tamoxifen effective while treating hot flashes. Spacing doses does not help because the inhibition persists, and stopping cancer therapy is not warranted."}, {"id": 1043, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "A pharmacy has 150 tablets of expired hydromorphone 2 mg in inventory that were never dispensed. The owner asks a pharmacist whether the tablets can simply be denatured at the pharmacy with a witness, as is done with patient returns. What is the best answer?", "options": {"A": "Place them in the regular returns bin, because narcotics are treated like any other expired drug", "B": "Destroy them in the store with a witness, because the witness signature makes destruction lawful", "C": "Keep them in the cabinet indefinitely, because destruction of inventory is not permitted by the regulations", "D": "Return them to a licensed dealer with records, or obtain Health Canada authorization before destroying"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Return them to a licensed dealer with records, or obtain Health Canada authorization before destroying): Expired narcotic stock belonging to the pharmacy is handled through a licensed dealer return or after written authorization for destruction from Health Canada. A witness alone does not authorize destruction of inventory, regular returns channels are not set up for narcotics, and keeping them forever is not required."}, {"id": 1044, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 1, "question": "HS is a 78 year old woman with isolated systolic hypertension, averaging 168/72 mmHg in the office and at home. She has no diabetes, kidney disease, heart failure or coronary disease. She walks independently and her sodium and potassium are normal. The prescriber asks for a first-line drug suggestion. Which is best?", "options": {"A": "Doxazosin, an alpha-blocker, which is recommended first-line because it lowers systolic pressure alone", "B": "Spironolactone, a potassium-sparing diuretic, which is recommended as initial therapy for her condition", "C": "Amlodipine, a long-acting dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker, is appropriate first-line; watch for ankle edema", "D": "Atenolol, a beta-blocker, which is recommended first-line for isolated systolic hypertension to slow the heart rate"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Amlodipine, a long-acting dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker, is appropriate first-line; watch for ankle edema): For isolated systolic hypertension, Hypertension Canada lists a thiazide or thiazide-like diuretic, an ARB or a long-acting dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker as first-line options, and amlodipine is the only one of these offered. Beta-blockers are not first-line in adults older than 60 without a compelling indication, and alpha-blockers and spironolactone are later choices."}, {"id": 1045, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "GK, a 47 year old patient, has legally purchased cannabis chocolates at home and asks how to store them because her two grandchildren under five visit on weekends. The chocolates look like ordinary candy bars. Which advice best reflects Health Canada safety guidance?", "options": {"A": "Keep them in the original child-resistant package, locked away and out of children's reach", "B": "Transfer them to an unlabelled container so children are not attracted by the colourful original packaging", "C": "Store them in the refrigerator beside regular chocolate, since cold temperatures deter children from eating them", "D": "Keep them in a high kitchen cupboard, since children under five cannot reach above counter height"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Keep them in the original child-resistant package, locked away and out of children's reach): Cannabis edibles should remain in the original child-resistant packaging, locked away and out of children's sight and reach. Unlabelled containers, refrigerators beside regular food, or a high cupboard do not prevent accidental ingestion and can encourage it. Children under five can climb and open ordinary containers."}, {"id": 1046, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 6, "question": "IR is a 25 year old woman with ulcerative colitis who calls the pharmacy reporting ten bloody stools daily for two days, a temperature of 38.8 C, a heart rate of 112 beats per minute and severe abdominal pain. She asks to double her mesalamine dose. The pharmacist should:", "options": {"A": "Recommend a clear fluid diet and rest, with a call to her gastroenterologist next week", "B": "Suggest oral prednisone 40 mg from her old supply and book a routine clinic visit", "C": "Advise doubling mesalamine and adding loperamide 4 mg after each stool to control the diarrhea", "D": "Direct her to emergency care for assessment of severe colitis rather than adjusting oral therapy"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Direct her to emergency care for assessment of severe colitis rather than adjusting oral therapy): Frequent bloody stools with fever and tachycardia meet criteria for acute severe ulcerative colitis, which needs hospital assessment and intravenous therapy. Loperamide can precipitate toxic megacolon in severe colitis. Delaying care or treating at home risks perforation and sepsis."}, {"id": 1047, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "A pharmacy restructures its workflow so that regulated technicians complete the technical check and pharmacists complete the clinical check. A new employee asks which activity is included in the technical check. The pharmacist is explaining the difference in a staff training session. Which activity belongs to the technical check?", "options": {"A": "Confirming the drug, strength, quantity and label match the prescription as entered", "B": "Reviewing the indication and monitoring parameters for a newly started medication", "C": "Assessing whether the dose is appropriate for the patient's renal function and age", "D": "Deciding whether a drug interaction requires contacting the prescriber about changing therapy"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Confirming the drug, strength, quantity and label match the prescription as entered): The technical check confirms that the prepared product matches the prescription: right drug, strength, quantity and label. Judging dose appropriateness, interactions, indication and monitoring are clinical decisions that belong to the pharmacist."}, {"id": 1048, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 1, "question": "JH is a 72 year old man with atrial fibrillation who takes apixaban 5 mg twice daily. He receives a drug-eluting stent for stable angina. His CHADS-65 status requires continued anticoagulation, and his bleeding risk is moderate. The cardiologist asks the pharmacist to suggest an antithrombotic regimen after PCI. What should be recommended?", "options": {"A": "Continue apixaban with clopidogrel, and stop acetylsalicylic acid after the peri-procedural period", "B": "Stop apixaban and use acetylsalicylic acid with clopidogrel alone, since stents need dual antiplatelets", "C": "Add acetylsalicylic acid and clopidogrel to apixaban for twelve months of full triple therapy", "D": "Continue apixaban with ticagrelor and acetylsalicylic acid for one month before reducing to apixaban alone"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Continue apixaban with clopidogrel, and stop acetylsalicylic acid after the peri-procedural period): In patients with atrial fibrillation who need PCI, current practice favours a direct oral anticoagulant plus clopidogrel, with acetylsalicylic acid limited to the periprocedural period, because triple therapy raises bleeding substantially. Clopidogrel is preferred over ticagrelor in combination with an anticoagulant. Dropping the anticoagulant leaves stroke risk unprotected."}, {"id": 1049, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "OM is a 72 year old woman who says she reads poorly and relies on her daughter for forms. You start counselling on her new levothyroxine and she nods at every point but then repeats the same question. Which technique best confirms her understanding?", "options": {"A": "Give her a detailed written leaflet with all the possible side effects and counselling points listed", "B": "Ask her to describe in her own words how she will take the tablet", "C": "Repeat the full counselling more loudly and more slowly until she stops asking the question", "D": "Ask whether she has understood everything and proceed once she answers yes or nods in agreement"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Ask her to describe in her own words how she will take the tablet): Asking a patient to explain in her own words is the teach-back method, which reveals gaps that nodding hides. A yes or nod does not prove understanding, and a long leaflet is hard to use for someone who reads poorly. Repeating the same explanation louder does not address a comprehension or literacy barrier."}, {"id": 1050, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 7, "question": "LD is 12 weeks pregnant and asks for something for a tension headache and sore back after work. She takes a prenatal vitamin only and has no medical conditions. She is considering ibuprofen because it worked well before. What is the best recommendation?", "options": {"A": "Recommend aspirin 650 mg every four hours, since aspirin is preferred over acetaminophen in pregnancy", "B": "Recommend ibuprofen 200 mg with food, because NSAIDs are safe throughout the first two trimesters", "C": "Recommend acetaminophen at the lowest effective dose and advise against ibuprofen during pregnancy", "D": "Recommend a codeine combination product, since opioid containing analgesics are the safest choice in pregnancy"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Recommend acetaminophen at the lowest effective dose and advise against ibuprofen during pregnancy): Acetaminophen at the lowest effective dose is the usual preferred drug for pain in pregnancy. NSAIDs are generally avoided because of risks to the fetus, and analgesic dose aspirin and regular opioids are not preferred. The pharmacist should also suggest non-drug measures such as heat and rest."}, {"id": 1051, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "Realizing she dispensed 10 mg warfarin tablets instead of 1 mg tablets to PL, a 58 year old patient who has not yet started them, pharmacist CN considers quietly swapping the supply at PL's next visit. Which action reflects the ethical duty of veracity?", "options": {"A": "Wait for PL to ask questions, since no harm occurred and disclosure would cause worry", "B": "Swap the tablets quietly when PL returns and record it only if PL notices a difference", "C": "Tell PL about the error promptly, correct the supply, and complete an incident report", "D": "Call PL's prescriber first and let the prescriber decide whether PL should be told about it"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Tell PL about the error promptly, correct the supply, and complete an incident report): Veracity requires honest disclosure, and a dispensing error that reached the patient must be disclosed to the patient even when no harm has occurred yet. The pharmacist should correct the supply and report the incident so the system can learn from it. Quiet swaps, waiting for questions, or leaving disclosure to the prescriber conceal the error."}, {"id": 1052, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 6, "question": "BQ is a 64 year old man started on clopidogrel 75 mg and aspirin 81 mg after coronary stenting. He has dyspepsia and a past bleeding ulcer. His physician plans to add omeprazole 40 mg daily. Which recommendation should the pharmacist make about the PPI choice?", "options": {"A": "Continue omeprazole 40 mg, since all PPIs inhibit CYP2C19 to the same extent", "B": "Use pantoprazole instead, since it has less effect on clopidogrel activation through CYP2C19", "C": "Replace it with an antacid taken with clopidogrel to protect the stomach lining adequately", "D": "Omit the PPI altogether, since gastroprotection is unnecessary when dual antiplatelet therapy is in place"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Use pantoprazole instead, since it has less effect on clopidogrel activation through CYP2C19): Omeprazole and esomeprazole inhibit CYP2C19, which activates clopidogrel, so they can reduce antiplatelet effect, whereas pantoprazole has minimal effect. Given his prior bleeding ulcer and dual antiplatelet therapy, gastroprotection is clearly indicated. An antacid does not provide adequate protection."}, {"id": 1053, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "A 33 year old woman has just been diagnosed with generalized anxiety disorder. She has no other medical conditions and takes no medications. She asks the pharmacist which type of drug is usually tried first. What is the most appropriate answer?", "options": {"A": "An SSRI such as escitalopram or sertraline, ideally combined with cognitive behavioural therapy", "B": "Zopiclone nightly for an indefinite period, since improved sleep is the main goal of treating anxiety", "C": "Quetiapine at bedtime as the first agent, keeping an SSRI for those who cannot tolerate it", "D": "Alprazolam three times daily as the main long-term treatment, adding an SSRI only if it fails"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (An SSRI such as escitalopram or sertraline, ideally combined with cognitive behavioural therapy): Canadian guidance supports SSRIs or SNRIs as first-line drug therapy for generalized anxiety disorder, with psychotherapy such as CBT. Benzodiazepines are reserved for short-term use because of dependence risk. Antipsychotics and hypnotics are not first-line choices."}, {"id": 1054, "domain": "leadership_and_stewardship", "chapter": 11, "question": "NC is a 45 year old woman who books a COVID-19 vaccine at the pharmacy before travel tomorrow. At the visit she has a temperature of 38.6 C with a new cough and body aches that began this morning. She is eager to be vaccinated today. What should the pharmacist do?", "options": {"A": "Vaccinate her today because a mild fever does not alter immune response and time is short", "B": "Vaccinate her after giving acetaminophen 1000 mg to bring her temperature below 38 C", "C": "Vaccinate her with half the usual dose to reduce the risk of worsening her symptoms", "D": "Postpone vaccination until she recovers, and advise her on testing and staying home"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Postpone vaccination until she recovers, and advise her on testing and staying home): Vaccination is deferred for people with acute moderate to severe illness, and a febrile respiratory illness also means she may be infectious and should avoid public places. Symptoms can be mistaken for vaccine reactions, which confuses safety monitoring. Antipyretics do not make a feverish patient suitable, and reduced doses have no evidence."}, {"id": 1055, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 3, "question": "OB is a 69 year old man on day 5 of a 14 day course of cephalexin for a diabetic foot infection that is clearly improving. He develops mild non-severe C. difficile diarrhea with a positive toxin test. What is the best plan?", "options": {"A": "Continue cephalexin to the full course and add oral metronidazole as the only C. difficile therapy", "B": "Treat the infection with oral vancomycin, and ask the prescriber whether cephalexin can stop early", "C": "Stop cephalexin and treat only with loperamide and fluids until the diarrhea has resolved", "D": "Switch cephalexin to clindamycin and give the same dose for the rest of therapy"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Treat the infection with oral vancomycin, and ask the prescriber whether cephalexin can stop early): The inciting antibiotic should be stopped or shortened when possible, and the infection treated with oral vancomycin or fidaxomicin. Metronidazole is no longer preferred and loperamide does not treat the infection. Clindamycin carries an even higher C. difficile risk."}, {"id": 1056, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "KM is an 88 year old long-term care resident with moderate dementia who has become newly agitated and aggressive over two days, shouting and refusing care. The nurse asks the pharmacist whether to request an antipsychotic. What should the pharmacist suggest as the first step?", "options": {"A": "Check for pain, infection, constipation, and urinary retention before considering a psychotropic", "B": "Increase donepezil to the maximum dose, because agitation is a sign the cognitive treatment is inadequate", "C": "Start low-dose risperidone now, since a sudden behaviour change in dementia is an indication for treatment", "D": "Give lorazepam 1 mg each evening, since benzodiazepines are the preferred first treatment for dementia agitation"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Check for pain, infection, constipation, and urinary retention before considering a psychotropic): Sudden agitation in a person with dementia is often caused by delirium, pain, infection, constipation, or urinary retention, so these should be sought and treated first, alongside non-drug approaches. Antipsychotics and benzodiazepines carry risks of falls, stroke, and death and are not first-line. Escalating donepezil does not address an acute cause."}, {"id": 1057, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 1, "question": "MY is a 67 year old man who had an ischemic stroke 6 months ago. He takes enteric-coated aspirin 325 mg daily and now has frequent dyspepsia. His hemoglobin is normal. What is the most appropriate recommendation for his antiplatelet therapy?", "options": {"A": "increase aspirin to 650 mg daily, since higher doses give more protection against another stroke", "B": "stop all antiplatelet therapy, since dyspepsia on aspirin means long-term use is no longer safe", "C": "change to a twice daily aspirin schedule, since divided dosing prevents irritation of the stomach", "D": "reduce aspirin to 81 mg daily, since higher doses add gastrointestinal bleeding without more protection"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (reduce aspirin to 81 mg daily, since higher doses add gastrointestinal bleeding without more protection): Low-dose aspirin of 81 mg is as effective as higher doses for secondary stroke prevention, while gastrointestinal bleeding and dyspepsia rise with dose. Stopping antiplatelets removes needed protection, and enteric coating does not remove the systemic effect."}, {"id": 1058, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 6, "question": "RB is a 26 year old exclusively breastfeeding her four week old infant and wants contraception that will not harm her milk supply. She smokes and has no history of clots or migraine. Her prescriber suggests norethindrone 0.35 mg daily. What should the pharmacist tell her about this method?", "options": {"A": "It is unsuitable because smokers cannot use any hormonal contraceptive method safely while breastfeeding", "B": "It keeps full protection even when a dose is taken up to twelve hours late", "C": "It lowers milk volume like estrogen products, so it should be delayed until six months", "D": "It does not reduce milk volume, but it needs strict timing each day"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (It does not reduce milk volume, but it needs strict timing each day): Progestin-only pills do not reduce milk production and can be started during breastfeeding. Norethindrone has a narrow dosing window of about three hours, so a dose taken much later needs backup protection. Smoking is a concern for estrogen containing products, not for a progestin-only pill."}, {"id": 1059, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A study reports the mean age of participants as 62 years with a standard deviation of 8 years, and compares categorical smoking status between groups. A pharmacist is asked to match each variable type with the correct descriptive summary and test. Which pairing is correct?", "options": {"A": "Both variables use proportions with chi-square tests because both describe groups of enrolled study participants", "B": "Age uses a mean and t-test, while smoking status uses proportions and chi-square", "C": "Both variables use means with t-tests because both can be summarized by a single number", "D": "Age uses proportions with a chi-square test, while smoking status uses a mean with a t-test"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Age uses a mean and t-test, while smoking status uses proportions and chi-square): Age is continuous, so it is summarized with a mean and standard deviation and compared with a t-test. Smoking status is categorical, so it is summarized with proportions and compared with a chi-square test. Swapping the pairings or using one method for both ignores the variable type."}, {"id": 1060, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "A hospital pharmacist reviews two plasma concentrations from QS, a 44 year old man, after an intravenous bolus of a drug that follows first-order elimination. The concentration was 40 mg/L at 2 hours and 10 mg/L at 6 hours. Assuming no further distribution, what is the elimination half-life?", "options": {"A": "2 hours, because the concentration fell by four times over two half-life periods", "B": "3 hours, because the concentration fell by three quarters in six hours of elimination", "C": "4 hours, because the concentration dropped between the two sampling times that are four hours apart", "D": "1 hour, because the concentration fell by four times in the first two hours after dosing"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (2 hours, because the concentration fell by four times over two half-life periods): A fall from 40 to 10 mg/L is a fourfold drop, which is two half-lives. The four hour sampling gap therefore contains two half-lives, giving 2 hours each. Using the interval itself as the half-life ignores the fold change."}, {"id": 1061, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 6, "question": "HD is a 48 year old woman with an H. pylori positive duodenal ulcer and a documented anaphylactic reaction to penicillin. She has previously received clarithromycin for a respiratory infection. She is not pregnant. Which first line eradication regimen should the pharmacist recommend?", "options": {"A": "A PPI with bismuth, metronidazole and tetracycline given together for 14 days", "B": "A PPI with clarithromycin and amoxicillin for 14 days, since amoxicillin cross reactivity is rare", "C": "A PPI with amoxicillin and levofloxacin for 14 days, which avoids both macrolide and bismuth exposure", "D": "A PPI with clarithromycin and metronidazole for 14 days as penicillin free triple therapy"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A PPI with bismuth, metronidazole and tetracycline given together for 14 days): Penicillin anaphylaxis rules out amoxicillin, and prior clarithromycin exposure raises the chance of macrolide resistance, so clarithromycin triple therapy is a poor fit. Bismuth quadruple therapy with a PPI, bismuth, metronidazole and tetracycline for 14 days is the recommended alternative. Regimens containing amoxicillin are unsuitable for this patient."}, {"id": 1062, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "IH is a 47 year old man who has taken zopiclone 7.5 mg nightly for seven months after a divorce, and now wants a refill. He reports that it no longer helps much, and he feels foggy in the morning. What should the pharmacist explain about long-term use?", "options": {"A": "Switching to a different sleeping pill each month prevents tolerance and keeps long-term use safe", "B": "Tolerance does not develop with zopiclone, so lack of benefit means the dose should be raised", "C": "Stopping zopiclone abruptly is safe after seven months, so he can quit tonight and sleep normally", "D": "Tolerance reduces benefit, so long courses are discouraged and a gradual taper is advised"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Tolerance reduces benefit, so long courses are discouraged and a gradual taper is advised): Tolerance and rebound insomnia develop with continued use of benzodiazepine receptor agonists, which is why guidelines limit them to short courses and advise CBT-I with a gradual taper after longer use. Abrupt stopping can cause rebound insomnia and withdrawal. Rotating hypnotics does not avoid dependence."}, {"id": 1063, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "An 80 year old patient, IV, in hospital has advanced dementia and has been prescribed a new antipsychotic by the team for agitation. The pharmacist notes there is little evidence of benefit and a stroke risk in dementia. What ethical principle most directly guides the pharmacist to question the order?", "options": {"A": "Justice, since antipsychotic supplies on the ward should be shared among all patients with dementia", "B": "Fidelity, since the pharmacist promised the family that no new drugs would be started", "C": "Non-maleficence, since the expected harm may exceed the likely benefit for this patient", "D": "Autonomy, since IV has clearly expressed a wish to refuse every sedating medication in advance"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Non-maleficence, since the expected harm may exceed the likely benefit for this patient): Non-maleficence is the duty to avoid harm, and an antipsychotic with weak evidence of benefit and a stroke risk in dementia may do more harm than good. Questioning the order is a way of acting on that duty. Nothing in the case suggests a prior refusal, a promise to the family, or a supply problem."}, {"id": 1064, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 1, "question": "DB is a 62 year old woman started on atorvastatin 80 mg daily after an ischemic stroke. At her six-week check her ALT is 1.8 times the upper limit of normal, and she has no symptoms. What is the most appropriate action?", "options": {"A": "continue atorvastatin and recheck enzymes in weeks, since values under three times normal are acceptable", "B": "switch to a fibrate, since fibrates are processed without any effect on the liver", "C": "stop atorvastatin now and avoid statins in the future, since any enzyme rise indicates liver injury", "D": "halve the dose immediately and recheck in a year, since enzyme rises resolve without follow-up"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (continue atorvastatin and recheck enzymes in weeks, since values under three times normal are acceptable): Small transaminase rises under three times the upper limit of normal are common with statins and usually do not require stopping. The enzymes are rechecked to confirm they are stable or improving, and the stroke prevention benefit is large."}, {"id": 1065, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A 55 year old woman takes levothyroxine 100 mcg each morning with breakfast. Her family physician adds ferrous fumarate 300 mg once daily for iron deficiency anemia. Her TSH rises to 9.2 mIU/L two months later. The best explanation and management is:", "options": {"A": "Iron raises thyroid binding globulin and lowers free hormone; stop iron until the TSH normalizes", "B": "Iron binds levothyroxine in the gut; separate the doses by at least four hours", "C": "Iron competes for renal tubular transport, so the levothyroxine should be taken with more water", "D": "Iron induces hepatic levothyroxine metabolism; double the levothyroxine dose and recheck the TSH in six weeks"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Iron binds levothyroxine in the gut; separate the doses by at least four hours): Iron, calcium and similar cations form insoluble complexes with levothyroxine, reducing absorption and raising TSH. The usual fix is to separate the doses by about four hours, with levothyroxine taken on an empty stomach, rather than changing the dose. Iron has no effect on hepatic metabolism, thyroid binding globulin or renal transport of thyroid hormone."}, {"id": 1066, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "JC is a 49 year old woman starting erenumab 70 mg subcutaneously once monthly for migraine prevention. She has mild hypertension controlled with amlodipine. At the first injection teaching visit she asks what to watch for. Which counselling point is most important?", "options": {"A": "Take the injection with a meal, and avoid sunlight exposure for seven days afterward", "B": "Report new or worsening constipation, and have her blood pressure checked after starting treatment", "C": "Expect daily injection site rashes, and stop the drug if mild redness appears at the site", "D": "Stop amlodipine on the day of each injection, since it neutralizes the antibody activity"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Report new or worsening constipation, and have her blood pressure checked after starting treatment): CGRP receptor antagonist antibodies such as erenumab are associated with constipation, sometimes severe, and with new or worsened hypertension, so both need monitoring. Mild injection site redness is common and not a reason to stop. There is no interaction requiring amlodipine to be held, and food or sunlight does not affect the injection."}, {"id": 1067, "domain": "communication_and_collaboration", "chapter": 11, "question": "A mother brings her 10 year old son, who has asthma, to collect his controller inhaler, which was newly prescribed after a recent emergency visit. The boy sits quietly while his mother talks. Which approach best supports effective counselling for this child at the pharmacy counter?", "options": {"A": "Give the boy a coloured leaflet and speak with his mother only about adherence", "B": "Direct all counselling to the mother since she is legally responsible for his treatment decisions", "C": "Include the boy directly, using simple words and having him demonstrate the technique", "D": "Ask the boy to wait elsewhere while the mother receives the full counselling"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Include the boy directly, using simple words and having him demonstrate the technique): School-age children can take part in their own care, and engaging them with simple language and demonstration improves technique and ownership. The caregiver is still involved, but excluding the child loses the person who actually uses the inhaler."}, {"id": 1068, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 1, "question": "LV is a 67 year old man with heart failure who started spironolactone 25 mg daily four months ago. His potassium is 4.6 mmol/L and creatinine is stable. He now reports breast tenderness and enlargement and wants to stop all his heart failure medicines because of it. What should the pharmacist recommend?", "options": {"A": "Suggest switching to eplerenone, which blocks the same receptor with fewer antiandrogen effects", "B": "Stop all four heart failure drugs for a month to find which one is the cause", "C": "Stop spironolactone for good and replace it with digoxin, which provides the same mortality benefit", "D": "Add tamoxifen to treat the breast tissue growth and continue spironolactone at the same dose"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Suggest switching to eplerenone, which blocks the same receptor with fewer antiandrogen effects): Spironolactone also blocks androgen and progesterone receptors, which causes gynecomastia in a notable minority of men. Eplerenone is a more selective mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist and keeps the mortality benefit without that effect. Digoxin has no mortality benefit, and stopping all therapy removes proven protection."}, {"id": 1069, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "A resident on a 400 bed hospital ward develops serious hepatotoxicity attributed to a recently marketed anticoagulant. A new hospital pharmacist asks the pharmacy director what reporting duty applies to the hospital under the Vanessa's Law amendments to the Food and Drugs Act. What does the hospital have to do?", "options": {"A": "Report the reaction to Health Canada only after a hospital committee confirms the drug caused it", "B": "Report the reaction to the provincial college within seven days, which forwards it to Health Canada", "C": "Report the serious reaction to Health Canada within thirty days of first documenting it", "D": "Report only if the manufacturer requests the information in writing during a post-market safety review"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Report the serious reaction to Health Canada within thirty days of first documenting it): Vanessa's Law requires hospitals to report serious adverse drug reactions and medical device incidents to Health Canada within 30 days of first documenting them. The duty does not depend on a manufacturer request or on a committee finding causation, and the report goes to Health Canada, not the college."}, {"id": 1070, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A 55 year old man with chest pain has a pretest probability of coronary disease of about 20%. A stress test is positive with a positive likelihood ratio of 6. A pharmacist assisting a cardiology clinic is asked to estimate the post-test probability using the odds method.", "options": {"A": "26%, because the likelihood ratio of 6 is added to the pretest probability of 20%", "B": "86%, because the likelihood ratio of 6 is used directly as the post-test odds", "C": "120%, because the pretest probability of 20% is multiplied directly by the likelihood ratio of 6", "D": "60%, because pretest odds of 0.25 multiplied by 6 give post-test odds of 1.5"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (60%, because pretest odds of 0.25 multiplied by 6 give post-test odds of 1.5): Pretest odds are 0.20 divided by 0.80, or 0.25. Multiplying by the likelihood ratio of 6 gives post-test odds of 1.5, and converting back gives 1.5 divided by 2.5, or 60%. Multiplying probability by the ratio, adding it, or using it as the odds directly are common errors."}, {"id": 1071, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "On arriving at the pharmacy, the pharmacist finds the pharmacy refrigerator at 11 C, and the data logger shows temperatures above 8 C for roughly six hours overnight. It holds insulin pens, a vaccine supply and several eye drops. What should the pharmacist do first?", "options": {"A": "Quarantine the products, label them do not use, and contact the manufacturers about stability data", "B": "Dispense only the insulin pens since insulin remains stable after any temperature excursion", "C": "Return all products to the fridge once it reaches 5 C and continue to dispense them", "D": "Discard everything immediately and record the loss without contacting manufacturers or the public health unit"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Quarantine the products, label them do not use, and contact the manufacturers about stability data): After a cold chain failure the products are separated and marked so they cannot be used while the pharmacy gets stability information from manufacturers and, for vaccines, the public health unit. Resuming use after cooling, or assuming one product type is safe, can expose patients to reduced potency. Discarding without asking may waste products that are still usable."}, {"id": 1072, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "A new pharmacist is asked which products in the community pharmacy belong on the pharmacy's high-alert medication list, which triggers an extra verification step. The pharmacy stocks several common drugs. Which of the following is best classified as high-alert based on ISMP Canada guidance?", "options": {"A": "Atorvastatin tablets, because they are taken for many years by most patients who receive them", "B": "Insulin products, because errors with them are likely to cause serious patient harm", "C": "Ibuprofen tablets, because they are among the most frequently dispensed products in community practice", "D": "Amoxicillin capsules, because they are the antibiotic most often dispensed to young children"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Insulin products, because errors with them are likely to cause serious patient harm): High-alert medications are those that carry a heightened risk of significant patient harm when used in error, and insulin is a standard example. Frequency of dispensing, duration of therapy or use in children does not make a drug high-alert on its own."}, {"id": 1073, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "New patients from a nearby homeless shelter are asked for photo ID and a permanent address before counselling is offered at the community pharmacy where MP works, while other patients are not. MP notices the pattern. Which ethical principle is most clearly compromised?", "options": {"A": "Autonomy, because the patients chose to enter the pharmacy and therefore accepted every pharmacy rule", "B": "Fidelity, because the pharmacy promised in its policy to counsel shelter residents first", "C": "Beneficence, because verifying identification is the pharmacist's main way of doing good for the patient", "D": "Justice, because patients with similar needs should receive the same counselling without unfair barriers"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Justice, because patients with similar needs should receive the same counselling without unfair barriers): Justice calls for fair treatment of patients with similar needs. Applying identification and address conditions only to shelter residents creates an unfair barrier to counselling. The case does not describe a broken promise or a physical harm, and entering a pharmacy does not mean agreeing to any rule."}, {"id": 1074, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "QS is a 35 year old woman with asthma and nasal polyps who had wheezing after taking ibuprofen last year. She now has menstrual cramps and asks the pharmacist whether naproxen would be better tolerated. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Naproxen is fine, since only ibuprofen causes respiratory reactions in people with asthma", "B": "Naproxen is fine if taken with an inhaled corticosteroid before each dose to prevent wheeze", "C": "Use aspirin 325 mg instead, since aspirin is less likely than naproxen to cause the reaction", "D": "Avoid naproxen and other COX-1 inhibiting NSAIDs, because of cross reactivity, and suggest acetaminophen"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Avoid naproxen and other COX-1 inhibiting NSAIDs, because of cross reactivity, and suggest acetaminophen): Her history suggests NSAID-exacerbated respiratory disease, in which inhibition of COX-1 by any traditional NSAID, including naproxen and aspirin, can provoke bronchospasm. Pre-treatment with an inhaler does not reliably prevent the reaction. Acetaminophen at usual doses is generally well tolerated and is the appropriate agent."}, {"id": 1075, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "A pharmacist has been informed that a prescription is for medical assistance in dying. When the patient's son collects related supplies, he mentions that his mother seemed unsure last week and that a relative pushed her to proceed. The prescriber is available by phone. What should the pharmacist do?", "options": {"A": "Pause dispensing, share the concern with the prescriber directly, and document the conversation", "B": "Contact the patient privately by phone, reassess her capacity, and decide whether to dispense yourself", "C": "Report the family to the police now since pressure on the patient may be an offence", "D": "Complete dispensing because eligibility was already established and the pharmacist cannot question the assessment"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Pause dispensing, share the concern with the prescriber directly, and document the conversation): A report of hesitation or outside pressure relates to voluntariness, which is a core safeguard, and the person responsible for assessing it is the prescriber. The pharmacist should pause and pass on the concern directly instead of acting on secondhand information alone. Assessing the patient personally or going to the police exceeds the pharmacist's role and may breach confidentiality, while completing the dispensing ignores the concern."}, {"id": 1076, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "EJ is a 78 year old man with knee osteoarthritis and a gastrointestinal bleed two years ago. He asks the pharmacist for oral diclofenac, which his neighbour uses. He takes warfarin for atrial fibrillation. What is the best recommendation? He has no other kidney or liver problems.", "options": {"A": "Suggest oral diclofenac with a meal, since it is the safest NSAID for people on warfarin", "B": "Suggest topical diclofenac gel to the knee, which has low systemic exposure, along with acetaminophen", "C": "Suggest oral diclofenac at half dose, since reduced dosing removes the bleeding risk with warfarin", "D": "Suggest oral naproxen instead, since it has the lowest gastrointestinal risk of the available NSAIDs"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Suggest topical diclofenac gel to the knee, which has low systemic exposure, along with acetaminophen): Oral NSAIDs raise bleeding risk, especially with prior ulcer bleeding and warfarin, and no oral NSAID is safe for him. Topical diclofenac delivers drug locally with far lower systemic levels, so it is preferred for knee osteoarthritis, with acetaminophen added as needed. Halving a dose or taking it with food does not remove the risk."}, {"id": 1077, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "A man who identifies himself as a journalist phones a community pharmacy and asks whether a named local woman recently received drugs for medical assistance in dying. The pharmacist knows the woman's family. The caller says the family agreed. What should the pharmacist do?", "options": {"A": "Tell the caller to get written consent from the family and submit it by email", "B": "Confirm only that she was a customer, without naming any drug or reason", "C": "Decline to confirm or discuss any patient information and refer the caller to the family", "D": "Confirm the information because medical assistance in dying is a legal service and is not confidential"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Decline to confirm or discuss any patient information and refer the caller to the family): Health information, including the fact that a patient received any drug, is confidential under provincial health privacy law and professional standards, and it cannot be shared with a caller whose identity and authority cannot be established. A statement that the family agreed does not replace verified consent from the patient or an authorized representative. Referring the caller to the family leaves it to them to decide what to share."}, {"id": 1078, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 4, "question": "XT is a 17 year old girl in her eighth week of isotretinoin 30 mg daily. Her mother tells the pharmacist that XT has become withdrawn, sleeps poorly and recently said she feels hopeless. XT says her skin is clearing and wants to keep going. What is the most appropriate action?", "options": {"A": "Advise her to continue treatment and see her physician at the routine visit after the course", "B": "Suggest an over the counter sleep aid and a lower isotretinoin dose until her mood settles", "C": "Ask directly about self harm, contact the prescriber urgently, and share crisis resources if needed", "D": "Reassure the family that mood changes are expected and resolve once the acne improves further"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Ask directly about self harm, contact the prescriber urgently, and share crisis resources if needed): Isotretinoin has been associated with depression and suicidal thoughts, and hopelessness with withdrawal needs prompt action. Asking directly about self harm, involving the prescriber the same day and providing crisis supports protects her. Reassurance, delay or a sleep aid does not address the risk."}, {"id": 1079, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "A locum pharmacist, SQ, accepts a one-day shift in a small pharmacy without telling the agency or owner that SQ objects to dispensing one lawful drug. At noon a patient requests it, and SQ is alone. What is the key failing?", "options": {"A": "SQ made no error, since personal objections need not be shared with an employer or agency", "B": "SQ failed because objecting pharmacists must leave the pharmacy and close it until another pharmacist arrives", "C": "SQ failed only by accepting the shift, since pharmacists with objections cannot work as locums", "D": "SQ should have disclosed the objection beforehand and must now ensure timely patient access"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (SQ should have disclosed the objection beforehand and must now ensure timely patient access): Objecting pharmacists are expected to disclose their objection in advance, such as to a locum agency or owner, so that access can be arranged. SQ did not, and now, alone, must still ensure the patient gets timely access, for example through referral. An objection is not a bar to locum work, and closing the pharmacy is not required."}, {"id": 1080, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "VR is a 47 year old woman who stopped duloxetine 60 mg daily three days ago after running out of tablets. She now has dizziness, nausea, irritability and brief electric shock sensations in her head. Her fibromyalgia pain has not changed. What should the pharmacist advise?", "options": {"A": "These symptoms will pass without treatment, so she should stay off duloxetine and wait it out", "B": "These are discontinuation symptoms, so she should resume duloxetine and taper gradually with her prescriber", "C": "These are signs of a new allergy, so she should avoid duloxetine and all related drugs", "D": "These are early signs of fibromyalgia worsening, so she should double the dose when restarting"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (These are discontinuation symptoms, so she should resume duloxetine and taper gradually with her prescriber): Dizziness, nausea, irritability and brief electric shock sensations soon after abruptly stopping an SNRI are the classic discontinuation syndrome. Restarting the drug usually relieves them within a day or so, and any later discontinuation should be a slow taper over weeks. They are not an allergy, and doubling the dose is not warranted."}, {"id": 1081, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 1, "question": "MR is a 74 year old man who bought nitroglycerin sublingual tablets two years ago and now keeps them in a weekly pill organizer with his other medications to carry easily. He has used the tablets only twice. He asks the pharmacist whether this storage is acceptable. What is the best advice?", "options": {"A": "Keep one tablet in the organizer for convenience and the rest in a plastic bag", "B": "Keep them in the original glass vial, tightly closed, and replace them when expired", "C": "The organizer is acceptable, because the tablets stay potent for years if they are kept dry", "D": "Store them in the refrigerator door, because cold storage preserves potency for a full two years"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Keep them in the original glass vial, tightly closed, and replace them when expired): Nitroglycerin tablets lose potency when exposed to heat, light, moisture and air, and pill organizer or plastic storage speeds that loss. The original tightly closed glass vial protects them, and the tablets should be replaced once the expiry date passes. Refrigeration is not needed and does not extend their shelf life."}, {"id": 1082, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A study reports a pooled adjusted relative risk of 1.9 for an exposure. Stratified analysis shows a relative risk of 3.0 in patients younger than 50 and 1.1 in patients aged 50 or older. How should the findings be reported?", "options": {"A": "Discard the older stratum, because its ratio is close to one and therefore must reflect confounding", "B": "Average the two stratum estimates and adjust for age, giving one summary figure for every patient", "C": "Report only the pooled estimate of 1.9, because it summarizes the effect across all participants", "D": "Report the age-specific estimates, because age modifies the effect and one pooled value would mislead"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Report the age-specific estimates, because age modifies the effect and one pooled value would mislead): A large difference in effect between strata signals effect modification, which is information to be reported, not a nuisance to be adjusted away. A single pooled estimate overstates risk in older patients and understates it in younger ones."}, {"id": 1083, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "TL is a 40 year old man who weighs 70 kg and has newly diagnosed primary hypothyroidism with a TSH of 22 mIU/L. He has no heart disease. Using full replacement of 1.6 mcg/kg daily, which levothyroxine daily dose is closest to the calculated dose?", "options": {"A": "88 mcg once daily, calculated from 1.25 mcg/kg so that titration is gradual in him", "B": "137 mcg once daily, calculated from 2.0 mcg/kg to speed correction of the raised TSH", "C": "112 mcg once daily, the strength that matches 1.6 mcg/kg for his weight", "D": "50 mcg once daily, calculated from 0.7 mcg/kg because men need less hormone than women"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (112 mcg once daily, the strength that matches 1.6 mcg/kg for his weight): Seventy kilograms multiplied by 1.6 mcg/kg gives 112 mcg, which is a commercially available strength. A young adult with no cardiac disease can start near full replacement. Higher or lower figures come from using the wrong per-kilogram factor, and sex does not change the weight-based calculation."}, {"id": 1084, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "The owner of a community pharmacy asks a pharmacist to be named as the pharmacy manager with the college even though she works at another location most days and visits only monthly. The owner says paperwork is the only requirement. What should the pharmacist do?", "options": {"A": "Decline, since standards require the manager to be directly involved in daily operations", "B": "Accept if the owner agrees to indemnify her for any complaint arising from the pharmacy's operations", "C": "Accept, since the manager's role is an administrative title and does not carry personal professional accountability", "D": "Accept if a technician is named as a delegate who handles the pharmacy's records and inspections"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Decline, since standards require the manager to be directly involved in daily operations): In most provinces a pharmacy manager is accountable to the college for the pharmacy's operation and must be actively and directly engaged in it. A title held on paper does not satisfy the standard, and owner indemnity or delegation to a technician does not remove the pharmacist's own accountability."}, {"id": 1085, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "HJ is a 58 year old woman taking moclobemide 300 mg twice daily for depression. She asks for a cough syrup containing dextromethorphan and guaifenesin for a dry cough that has lasted three days. She is otherwise well. Which recommendation is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Dispense the syrup, since moclobemide is a reversible inhibitor and interacts only with tyramine rich foods", "B": "Dispense the syrup and advise stopping moclobemide for the duration of the cough illness", "C": "Avoid dextromethorphan because of serotonin syndrome risk, and suggest honey or a plain lozenge", "D": "Dispense the syrup but halve the recommended dose, since lower doses avoid serotonergic effects"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Avoid dextromethorphan because of serotonin syndrome risk, and suggest honey or a plain lozenge): Dextromethorphan has serotonergic activity and combining it with an MAO inhibitor, even a reversible one such as moclobemide, carries a risk of serotonin syndrome. Dose halving does not remove the interaction, and stopping an antidepressant for a minor cough is inappropriate. Honey or a non serotonergic approach manages a dry cough safely."}, {"id": 1086, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "In a trial of a new antihypertensive, the primary outcome occurred in 4% of the control group and 3% of the treatment group over three years. The sponsor advertises a 25% reduction in events. A pharmacist reviewing the claim is asked which statement about the result is correct. What is the best answer?", "options": {"A": "Relative risk reduction 75%, absolute risk reduction 1%, and number needed to treat 100 patients", "B": "Relative risk reduction 25%, absolute risk reduction 25%, and number needed to treat 40 patients", "C": "Relative risk reduction 25%, absolute risk reduction 1%, and number needed to treat 100 patients", "D": "Relative risk reduction 1%, absolute risk reduction 25%, and number needed to treat 4 patients"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Relative risk reduction 25%, absolute risk reduction 1%, and number needed to treat 100 patients): One percentage point fewer events from a control rate of 4% is a relative reduction of 25%, yet an absolute reduction of only 1%. The number needed to treat is 1 divided by 0.01, or 100. The sponsor's 25% figure is accurate but gives a more impressive picture than the absolute benefit."}, {"id": 1087, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "FZ is a 72 year old woman weighing 68 kg, recovering from knee replacement, with a creatinine clearance of 25 mL/min. She is ordered enoxaparin 40 mg subcutaneously once daily to prevent clots. The pharmacist reviewing the order should recommend which change?", "options": {"A": "Continue 40 mg once daily, because prophylactic doses do not depend on kidney function in practice", "B": "Change to 30 mg every 12 hours, because low clearance requires more frequent smaller doses", "C": "Increase to 40 mg twice daily, because low clearance reduces enoxaparin effect per dose", "D": "Reduce to 30 mg once daily, because enoxaparin accumulates when clearance falls below 30 mL/min"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Reduce to 30 mg once daily, because enoxaparin accumulates when clearance falls below 30 mL/min): Enoxaparin is cleared by the kidneys, and below a creatinine clearance of 30 mL/min the prophylactic dose is reduced to 30 mg once daily to limit accumulation and bleeding. Twice daily dosing would double the exposure instead of reducing it."}, {"id": 1088, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 3, "question": "ST is a 33 year old man with newly diagnosed HIV who has no known drug allergies. His physician has written a single tablet of abacavir, dolutegravir and lamivudine to start today, but the HLA-B*5701 result has not returned. Which action should the pharmacist take?", "options": {"A": "Dispense it with counselling to take the tablet with food, which lowers the hypersensitivity risk", "B": "Dispense it, since HLA-B*5701 testing is needed only for patients with a prior sulfa allergy", "C": "Dispense it, since hypersensitivity can be managed by stopping abacavir if a rash appears later on", "D": "Withhold abacavir until the HLA-B*5701 result is negative, and inform the prescriber of the delay"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Withhold abacavir until the HLA-B*5701 result is negative, and inform the prescriber of the delay): Abacavir causes a potentially fatal hypersensitivity reaction in people carrying HLA-B*5701, so screening must be negative before the first dose. Waiting for the result is safe, as another regimen can be used meanwhile if needed. Food does not alter the genetic risk, and rechallenge after a reaction can be fatal, so watching for a rash is not an adequate plan."}, {"id": 1089, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 1, "question": "RS is a 64 year old man taking warfarin for a pulmonary embolism, with a target INR of 2.0 to 3.0. He phones the pharmacy because he had a nosebleed that stopped after ten minutes of firm nasal pressure. Today's INR is 2.6, and he has no other bleeding. What should the pharmacist recommend?", "options": {"A": "Send him to the emergency department for reversal, since nosebleeds on warfarin need immediate treatment", "B": "Hold warfarin for three days and give vitamin K 5 mg orally to prevent another nosebleed", "C": "Continue the usual warfarin dose, use local pressure if it recurs, and report prolonged bleeding", "D": "Reduce the weekly warfarin dose by 20% because bleeding means the target INR is too high"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Continue the usual warfarin dose, use local pressure if it recurs, and report prolonged bleeding): A minor nosebleed controlled with pressure in a patient with an in-range INR does not call for a change in therapy. Reversal or dose reduction would increase the risk of clot recurrence without benefit. He should seek care if bleeding lasts beyond 20 minutes or recurs heavily."}, {"id": 1090, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "A prescription for a 50 year old man with hypothyroidism reads: levothyroxine 50 mcg, 1 tab po qam ac. He eats breakfast at 7 am and asks whether to take the tablet with it or before. How should the pharmacist interpret and label the instruction?", "options": {"A": "Take one tablet by mouth every morning before eating breakfast", "B": "Take one tablet by mouth every evening before the main meal of the day", "C": "Take one tablet by mouth every morning after breakfast, once the meal has been finished", "D": "Take one tablet by mouth every morning with the first bite of breakfast"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Take one tablet by mouth every morning before eating breakfast): Qam means every morning and ac means before meals, so the label should read one tablet every morning before breakfast. After breakfast would be pc, which reverses the instruction. An evening schedule contradicts qam, and taking the tablet with food does not meet the before meals direction, which also supports consistent levothyroxine absorption."}, {"id": 1091, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "A 43 year old man with a BMI of 36 kg/m2 asks about starting weekly semaglutide. During the interview he tells the pharmacist that his mother had medullary thyroid carcinoma. He has no personal thyroid history. What is the most appropriate response?", "options": {"A": "Proceed at a lower starting dose, since a family history only calls for slower titration", "B": "Proceed normally, because only a personal history of thyroid cancer contraindicates this drug class", "C": "Advise against it and notify the prescriber, as this family history is a contraindication", "D": "Proceed after a normal thyroid ultrasound, since a normal scan removes any inherited risk"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Advise against it and notify the prescriber, as this family history is a contraindication): GLP-1 receptor agonists caused thyroid C-cell tumours in rodents, so the product monographs list personal or family history of medullary thyroid carcinoma and MEN 2 as contraindications. A normal ultrasound does not remove a heritable risk. Dose reduction does not make the drug acceptable."}, {"id": 1092, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "On a surgical ward, a pharmacist reviews a trial in which 12% of patients given a prophylactic regimen developed postoperative delirium, compared with 20% of patients given usual care. The pharmacist wants to report the relative risk reduction to the ward team during rounds this afternoon.", "options": {"A": "67%, the difference of 8 percentage points divided by the prophylaxis group risk of 12%", "B": "60%, the prophylaxis group risk of 12% divided by the usual care risk of 20%", "C": "40%, the 8 percentage point difference divided by the usual care risk of 20%", "D": "8%, the simple subtraction of the prophylaxis group risk from the usual care group risk"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (40%, the 8 percentage point difference divided by the usual care risk of 20%): Relative risk reduction is the difference in risk divided by the control risk: 20 minus 12 is 8 percentage points, and 8 divided by 20 is 40%. Subtracting alone gives the absolute reduction. Dividing 12 by 20 gives the relative risk of 0.6, and dividing by the treated risk overstates the effect."}, {"id": 1093, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 4, "question": "MO is a 40 year old man prescribed mometasone nasal spray 50 mcg per spray, two sprays in each nostril once daily. The new bottle contains 140 sprays, and priming sprays are ignored in this calculation. For how long will one bottle last, and what is his daily dose?", "options": {"A": "70 days, using only two sprays and a total of 100 mcg each day", "B": "17 days, using eight sprays daily and a total of 400 mcg each day", "C": "28 days, using five sprays daily and a total of 250 mcg each day", "D": "35 days, using four sprays and a total of 200 mcg each day"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (35 days, using four sprays and a total of 200 mcg each day): Two sprays in each of two nostrils is four sprays daily, which is 4 x 50 = 200 mcg. A bottle of 140 sprays divided by 4 sprays per day lasts 35 days. The other values come from miscounting the sprays per day."}, {"id": 1094, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 7, "question": "HR is a 70 year old man taking warfarin 5 mg daily for atrial fibrillation, with a stable INR of 2.4. He asks for an over-the-counter product for a tension headache and picks up an aspirin and caffeine tablet. What is the best advice?", "options": {"A": "Most analgesics are acceptable if he takes warfarin at the same time each evening, with food", "B": "Ibuprofen 400 mg is preferred over aspirin because it has a smaller effect on the INR", "C": "Avoid aspirin, use low dose acetaminophen, and tell the prescriber if regular use is needed", "D": "Aspirin is acceptable since his INR is stable, if he limits use to one tablet daily"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Avoid aspirin, use low dose acetaminophen, and tell the prescriber if regular use is needed): Aspirin and other NSAIDs add to the bleeding risk with warfarin through platelet inhibition and gastric irritation, even when the INR is stable. Acetaminophen is the preferred analgesic, but regular use can raise the INR, so the prescriber should know if he needs it often. Taking warfarin at a regular time does not remove the interaction."}, {"id": 1095, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "An independent pharmacy is closing permanently next month with no buyer. The records cover about 6,000 patients in paper and electronic form, and some date back eight years. Patients will still need access to them. The owner asks which plan is appropriate in most provinces.", "options": {"A": "Keep the records in the locked store until the lease ends, then discard them with fixtures", "B": "Return the paper prescriptions to patients and keep only electronic files for the last two years", "C": "Notify the college and securely transfer records to a custodian for the retention period", "D": "Shred paper records and delete electronic files at closing, since the pharmacy will stop operating"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Notify the college and securely transfer records to a custodian for the retention period): A closing pharmacy remains responsible for the retention and confidentiality of its records, so the college is notified and records are transferred securely to a custodian who can provide patient access. Destroying them early breaches retention rules. Leaving them in vacated premises or discarding them with fixtures exposes patient information."}, {"id": 1096, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "WE is a 77 year old man with stage 3b CKD, eGFR 36 mL/min/1.73 m2, and potassium 5.1 mmol/L. The prescriber proposes starting one of four drugs for his new heart failure. Which proposed drug should the pharmacist question first because of his laboratory results?", "options": {"A": "Spironolactone 25 mg daily, since it can raise potassium further when already above 5.0 mmol/L", "B": "Ivabradine 5 mg twice daily, since it blocks sodium potassium exchange in the collecting duct", "C": "Atorvastatin 20 mg daily, since statins release intracellular potassium from muscle when kidney function is impaired", "D": "Furosemide 40 mg daily, since loop diuretics retain potassium by reducing distal sodium delivery in CKD"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Spironolactone 25 mg daily, since it can raise potassium further when already above 5.0 mmol/L): Aldosterone antagonists are generally not started when potassium exceeds 5.0 mmol/L or eGFR is below 30 to 45, because hyperkalemia is a major risk. Loop diuretics increase potassium excretion. Statins and ivabradine do not meaningfully raise potassium."}, {"id": 1097, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "GH is a 70 year old man taking pioglitazone 30 mg and metformin for type 2 diabetes. He mentions that for the past week he has noticed pink urine without pain or burning. He asks whether it is a harmless effect of his diabetes tablets. What should the pharmacist advise?", "options": {"A": "See his physician promptly, since visible blood in the urine needs investigation for bladder disease", "B": "Increase his water intake and recheck the urine colour in a week before seeing anyone", "C": "Pink urine is a known harmless effect of metformin, so he can continue both drugs", "D": "Switch from pioglitazone to a sulfonylurea at home, since pink urine signals a glucose problem"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (See his physician promptly, since visible blood in the urine needs investigation for bladder disease): Painless visible hematuria is a red flag for urinary tract malignancy, and pioglitazone carries a bladder cancer caution, so he needs prompt medical evaluation. Metformin does not cause pink urine. Waiting, or changing diabetes therapy without assessment, delays diagnosis."}, {"id": 1098, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A nurse in a long-term care home asks whether a resident's metoprolol succinate extended-release tablet can be crushed because the resident is struggling to swallow. The pharmacist confirms the product and the resident's difficulty. The most appropriate response, after checking references, is to explain that the tablet should:", "options": {"A": "Be crushed and the dose reduced by one half to offset faster absorption", "B": "Not be crushed, and suggest an alternative such as divided doses of metoprolol tartrate", "C": "Be dissolved in warm water for ten minutes and then given with the next meal", "D": "Be crushed and mixed in applesauce because the coating only affects taste and not drug release"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Not be crushed, and suggest an alternative such as divided doses of metoprolol tartrate): Crushing an extended-release product destroys the release mechanism and delivers the full daily dose at once, risking bradycardia and hypotension. Metoprolol succinate tablets may be halved along the score but not crushed or chewed. An immediate-release salt such as tartrate, in divided doses, can be prescribed instead."}, {"id": 1099, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 6, "question": "ER is a 59 year old man living at home with hepatic encephalopathy taking lactulose 30 mL twice daily. He reports one firm stool daily and his family says he is still confused and sleepy. What adjustment should the pharmacist suggest to the prescriber?", "options": {"A": "Replace lactulose with a high protein diet, since protein restriction has no benefit", "B": "Hold lactulose and start a stimulant laxative such as bisacodyl as the sole therapy", "C": "Increase lactulose until he has two to three soft stools each day", "D": "Reduce lactulose to once daily, since one stool daily shows the dose is already effective"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Increase lactulose until he has two to three soft stools each day): Lactulose is titrated to produce two to three soft stools daily, which traps ammonia in the colon and lowers its absorption. One firm stool means the dose is inadequate, and his persisting confusion supports this. Stimulant laxatives do not lower ammonia, and protein restriction is no longer advised in encephalopathy."}, {"id": 1100, "domain": "communication_and_collaboration", "chapter": 11, "question": "A child weighs 18 kg and has a prescription for acetaminophen 15 mg per kg per dose. The liquid is 160 mg per 5 mL. How many mL should the pharmacist tell the caregiver to give for one dose using an oral syringe?", "options": {"A": "13.5 mL, based on 15 mg per kg and 20 mg per mL", "B": "8.4 mL, based on 15 mg per kg and 32 mg per mL", "C": "1.7 mL, based on 15 mg per kg and 160 mg per mL", "D": "5.6 mL, based on 10 mg per kg and 32 mg per mL"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (8.4 mL, based on 15 mg per kg and 32 mg per mL): The dose is 15 x 18 = 270 mg. The liquid contains 160 mg per 5 mL, or 32 mg per mL, so 270 divided by 32 gives 8.4 mL. Using 160 mg per mL or 10 mg per kg gives wrong volumes."}, {"id": 1101, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 6, "question": "YC is 10 weeks pregnant and has vomited eight times daily for four days. She cannot keep fluids down, has lost 4 kg since her last visit, and feels dizzy on standing. She asks whether she should double her doxylamine-pyridoxine tonight. The pharmacist should:", "options": {"A": "Suggest dimenhydrinate 50 mg every four hours and an oral rehydration solution at home", "B": "Advise doubling doxylamine-pyridoxine tonight and reassess at her next prenatal visit", "C": "Recommend ginger capsules and small frequent meals, with follow-up in a week", "D": "Refer her urgently for assessment of hyperemesis gravidarum and possible intravenous rehydration"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Refer her urgently for assessment of hyperemesis gravidarum and possible intravenous rehydration): Persistent vomiting with dehydration, orthostatic symptoms and weight loss over 5 percent suggests hyperemesis gravidarum, which can need intravenous fluids and thiamine. Doubling the dose exceeds the labelled maximum and does not address dehydration. Home measures delay needed care."}, {"id": 1102, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "A prescription calls for a powder containing camphor, menthol and zinc oxide for a topical preparation. The technician triturates the camphor and menthol together in a mortar, and the crystals turn into a sticky liquid. The technician thinks the ingredients are spoiled and asks to start over. What should the pharmacist advise?", "options": {"A": "Discard the mixture because liquefaction shows the drugs reacted with each other and then degraded", "B": "It is a eutectic mixture, so blend in the zinc oxide to absorb it", "C": "Add water to the liquid until the camphor and menthol dissolve fully, then continue the preparation", "D": "Heat the mortar gently to drive off the liquid, then triturate the dry residue again"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (It is a eutectic mixture, so blend in the zinc oxide to absorb it): Camphor and menthol form a eutectic mixture that has a lower melting point than either ingredient, so they liquefy on trituration without any chemical change. The liquid can be absorbed onto an inert powder such as zinc oxide or incorporated into the base. Heating would risk losing volatile ingredients, and water does not dissolve either one."}, {"id": 1103, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "A pharmacy resident is asked to explain pharmaceutical equivalence to a patient who insists that all tablets with the same ingredient and strength must work identically in the body. The patient asks what the term actually means. Which statement best describes pharmaceutical equivalents?", "options": {"A": "They have the same active drug, strength and dosage form but may differ in absorption", "B": "They are products shown to be equal in clinical effect in two large comparative patient trials", "C": "They share the same manufacturer and release system but may be sold under different strengths", "D": "They have the same absorption profile and the same fillers as long as the strength matches"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (They have the same active drug, strength and dosage form but may differ in absorption): Pharmaceutical equivalence concerns what is in the product, not how the body handles it. Products can be pharmaceutical equivalents yet differ in bioavailability, which is why bioequivalence testing is still required before products are called interchangeable."}, {"id": 1104, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "A pharmacist in a rural pharmacy meets the parent of a First Nations child who needs a prescribed nutritional formula that the provincial plan does not cover. The parent says the family cannot afford it. Which federal initiative may help the child obtain needed products without delay?", "options": {"A": "The Interim Federal Health Program, which covers products for children whose families recently arrived in Canada", "B": "Jordan's Principle, which ensures First Nations children get needed products and services without jurisdictional delay", "C": "The Canada Child Benefit, which pays families a monthly amount for each child under eighteen", "D": "Joyce's Principle, which sets out the right to equitable access to health care free of racism"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Jordan's Principle, which ensures First Nations children get needed products and services without jurisdictional delay): Jordan's Principle is a child-first principle under which First Nations children can receive needed health, social and educational products and services, with the first government contacted paying so that jurisdictional disputes do not delay care. The Canada Child Benefit is an income benefit, the Interim Federal Health Program serves refugee claimants, and Joyce's Principle is a declaration about equitable access rather than a funding route."}, {"id": 1105, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 3, "question": "MC is a 5 year old girl who weighs 20 kg and has a positive influenza test after one day of fever and cough. Her pediatrician prescribes oseltamivir suspension 12 mg/mL for five days. Her renal function is normal. What dose should the pharmacist verify for her weight?", "options": {"A": "60 mg twice daily, which equals 5 mL of the suspension for each dose", "B": "75 mg twice daily, which equals 6.25 mL of the suspension for each dose", "C": "30 mg twice daily, which equals 2.5 mL of the suspension for each dose", "D": "45 mg twice daily, which is 3.75 mL of the suspension each dose"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (45 mg twice daily, which is 3.75 mL of the suspension each dose): Weight based oseltamivir dosing for children above 15 kg up to 23 kg is 45 mg twice daily. At 12 mg/mL, 45 mg is 3.75 mL. The 30 mg dose fits 15 kg or less, 60 mg fits over 23 kg to 40 kg, and 75 mg is the adult dose."}, {"id": 1106, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "A pharmacist reviews a moderate interaction alert between sertraline and tramadol for DC, 47, and decides to proceed after speaking with the prescriber about monitoring for serotonin toxicity. The pharmacy policy requires documentation of every alert override. Which entry is most complete?", "options": {"A": "Override selected by the pharmacist on duty with a short system code and no further comment", "B": "Override recorded by the technician after the pharmacist verbally approved the prescription at the counter", "C": "Alert overridden because the prescriber was aware of the medication list and approved the fill", "D": "Alert reviewed, prescriber contacted, rationale and monitoring plan recorded with date, time and pharmacist identity"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Alert reviewed, prescriber contacted, rationale and monitoring plan recorded with date, time and pharmacist identity): A useful override record shows what was identified, who was consulted, why therapy was continued and what monitoring was agreed, with a time and the responsible pharmacist. A code without reasoning or an entry by someone else does not show clinical judgement."}, {"id": 1107, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "DV is a 44 year old man who presents a lorazepam 1 mg prescription from Dr. A. The provincial profile shows a lorazepam prescription from Dr. B filled six days ago, and neither prescriber appears aware of the other. What should the pharmacist do?", "options": {"A": "Fill the new prescription and warn the patient to keep both bottles locked up at home", "B": "Contact both prescribers, share the information as permitted, and hold the fill until concerns are resolved", "C": "Fill the prescription since each prescriber is entitled to treat the patient independently", "D": "Phone the police immediately because duplicate prescribing is a criminal offence by patients"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Contact both prescribers, share the information as permitted, and hold the fill until concerns are resolved): Overlapping benzodiazepine prescriptions from two prescribers raise safety and diversion concerns. The pharmacist contacts both to coordinate care, using disclosure allowed under privacy law. Filling without discussion ignores the duplicate, and a police call is not the first step."}, {"id": 1108, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "A 33 year old woman has just been diagnosed with Graves disease, and her physician plans to begin methimazole. A clinic pharmacist is asked which baseline tests should be on file before treatment starts. Which set of tests is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Serum creatinine, serum potassium, and fasting glucose, since these identify the most common serious reactions", "B": "Chest radiograph, electrocardiogram, and pulmonary function testing, because the main drug toxicity is cardiopulmonary", "C": "White blood cell count with differential, liver enzymes, and baseline thyroid hormone levels", "D": "Fasting lipid profile, urinalysis, and a baseline bone density scan, because those predict drug safety"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (White blood cell count with differential, liver enzymes, and baseline thyroid hormone levels): Antithyroid drugs can cause agranulocytosis and liver injury, so a baseline white cell count with differential and liver enzymes lets later changes be recognized. Thyroid hormone levels establish the starting point for dose titration. The other tests do not relate to the characteristic toxicities of these drugs."}, {"id": 1109, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "Bisoprolol 10 mg tablets, 1/2 tab po bid, are prescribed for a 72 year old man with heart failure. His drug plan covers a 90 day supply, and the tablets can be split. How many tablets should the pharmacist dispense for the 90 days?", "options": {"A": "180 tablets, since each dose is one tablet and the drug is taken twice daily", "B": "45 tablets, since only one half tablet is taken each day over ninety days", "C": "135 tablets, since an extra half tablet is added daily to allow for splitting waste", "D": "90 tablets, since one whole tablet daily for ninety days uses that quantity"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (90 tablets, since one whole tablet daily for ninety days uses that quantity): Half a tablet twice daily is one whole tablet per day, so 90 days needs 90 tablets. Counting a whole tablet at each dose gives 180, counting one half tablet daily gives 45, and adding extra for splitting waste is not part of a prescribed quantity."}, {"id": 1110, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "A 31 year old man on sertraline 100 mg daily has had full remission of depression but reports delayed ejaculation that bothers him a great deal. He wants to keep his mood stable. What strategy could the pharmacist suggest to his prescriber?", "options": {"A": "Double the sertraline dose, since higher doses usually reduce sexual side effects within a few weeks", "B": "Switch to venlafaxine, since the SNRIs are free of sexual side effects and are better tolerated", "C": "Consider switching to or adding bupropion or mirtazapine, which cause fewer sexual side effects", "D": "Switch to paroxetine, since it has the lowest rate of sexual dysfunction among the SSRIs"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Consider switching to or adding bupropion or mirtazapine, which cause fewer sexual side effects): Sexual dysfunction is common with SSRIs and SNRIs and is dose related. Bupropion and mirtazapine are options with fewer sexual effects. Paroxetine tends to be among the worst offenders, venlafaxine causes similar problems, and raising the dose usually worsens the effect."}, {"id": 1111, "domain": "communication_and_collaboration", "chapter": 11, "question": "A pharmacist phones a physician about a patient on apixaban who has been prescribed naproxen 500 mg twice daily. She says, 'I think this combination puts her at high risk of gastrointestinal bleeding.' Which SBAR element does this statement represent?", "options": {"A": "Assessment, because it gives the pharmacist's judgement about the problem", "B": "Recommendation, because it proposes a specific action for the prescriber", "C": "Situation, because it states who is calling and the immediate concern", "D": "Background, because it summarizes the patient's relevant medical and medication history"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Assessment, because it gives the pharmacist's judgement about the problem): The Assessment is the clinician's interpretation of the problem, here the bleeding risk from combining an anticoagulant with an NSAID. Situation identifies the call, Background gives history, and Recommendation states what the pharmacist wants done."}, {"id": 1112, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 1, "question": "RE is an 84 year old woman, weight 55 kg, with serum creatinine 175 umol/L, taking dabigatran 110 mg twice daily for atrial fibrillation started two years ago when her kidney function was normal. At a medication review today, the pharmacist estimates her renal function. What is the most appropriate action?", "options": {"A": "Reduce to 75 mg twice daily, the dose used for creatinine clearance below 30 mL/min", "B": "Continue the current dose but check the INR weekly to confirm that dabigatran is not accumulating", "C": "Continue 110 mg twice daily, because this is already the reduced dose designed for older patients", "D": "Suggest another anticoagulant, since dabigatran is contraindicated when creatinine clearance is below 30 mL/min"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Suggest another anticoagulant, since dabigatran is contraindicated when creatinine clearance is below 30 mL/min): Cockcroft-Gault gives (140 minus 84) x 55 x 1.04 / 175, about 18 mL/min. Dabigatran is mostly cleared by the kidneys and is contraindicated in Canada below 30 mL/min, so another agent is needed. A 75 mg dose exists only in the United States, and the INR does not measure dabigatran accumulation."}, {"id": 1113, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 4, "question": "BA is a 63 year old man with rheumatoid arthritis, a coronary stent history and a current smoking habit. His rheumatologist proposes tofacitinib 5 mg twice daily after methotrexate failure. The pharmacist reviews the safety warning for Janus kinase inhibitors. Which concern is most important?", "options": {"A": "Increased risk of serotonin syndrome when taken with nicotine replacement products used for smoking cessation", "B": "Increased risk of hypoglycemia and weight gain in patients who also smoke tobacco daily", "C": "Increased risk of major cardiovascular events, clots and malignancy in patients with cardiovascular risk factors", "D": "Increased risk of optic neuritis and retinal toxicity requiring a baseline eye examination and yearly review"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Increased risk of major cardiovascular events, clots and malignancy in patients with cardiovascular risk factors): Safety reviews of Janus kinase inhibitors found higher rates of major cardiovascular events, venous thromboembolism and malignancy in patients with cardiovascular risk factors or who smoke, so his history makes this the key concern. The other adverse effects listed are not characteristic of this drug class."}, {"id": 1114, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 6, "question": "KL is a 38 year old woman with recurrent bacterial vaginosis who asks whether her regular male partner should take metronidazole as well, since a friend said both partners need treatment. She has been symptom free for two months. What should the pharmacist tell her?", "options": {"A": "Routine treatment of a male partner has not been shown to reduce recurrence", "B": "Partner treatment is needed only after she has had three episodes within twelve months", "C": "Her partner should take the same course, as this condition is a classic sexually transmitted infection", "D": "Her partner should use a topical antifungal for one week because he carries the organism"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Routine treatment of a male partner has not been shown to reduce recurrence): Bacterial vaginosis is an imbalance of vaginal flora rather than a classic sexually transmitted infection, and trials have not shown that treating a male partner lowers recurrence. This differs from trichomoniasis, where partner treatment is required. Antifungals have no role in this condition."}, {"id": 1115, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 6, "question": "GM is a 66 year old woman with a known long QT interval (QTc 510 ms) who is prescribed ondansetron 8 mg every eight hours for gastroenteritis. The pharmacist notices the QTc on her chart from last month. The pharmacist should:", "options": {"A": "Dispense it, because oral ondansetron does not affect the QT interval at 8 mg doses", "B": "Dispense it with advice to check her pulse daily and stop if it is slow", "C": "Dispense it with advice to take it only with food to lower the cardiac risk", "D": "Contact the prescriber about QT prolongation and propose an alternative antiemetic with less cardiac effect"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Contact the prescriber about QT prolongation and propose an alternative antiemetic with less cardiac effect): Ondansetron prolongs the QT interval in a dose-dependent way, and a baseline QTc of 510 ms already carries a high risk of torsades de pointes. Food does not change the cardiac effect, and a pulse check cannot detect QT prolongation. The prescriber should choose a safer drug."}, {"id": 1116, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "On January 3, a pharmacist realizes her annual registration renewal deadline of December 31 passed and she has not completed the renewal or declared her continuing education. She has been dispensing at the pharmacy since January 1 and has a full schedule today. What should she do?", "options": {"A": "Practise under a colleague's supervision until the college acknowledges receipt of her late renewal application", "B": "Stop practising until renewal is completed, since practising without current registration can be professional misconduct", "C": "Continue practising and complete renewal by the end of the week, since short delays are routine", "D": "Continue practising and notify the college only if a patient or inspector raises the registration question"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Stop practising until renewal is completed, since practising without current registration can be professional misconduct): A pharmacist must hold current registration to practise, and renewal lapses at the deadline, so dispensing afterwards can be unauthorized practice. She should stop, contact the college and complete renewal promptly. Supervision by a colleague does not replace registration."}, {"id": 1117, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "For a patient with a 5% annual risk of stroke, a drug reduces stroke risk by 40% relative but causes major bleeding in 1.5% more patients per year. A pharmacist calculates the number needed to treat for stroke prevention and compares it with the harm rate for a clinic decision.", "options": {"A": "NNT is 50 and NNH is about 1.5, so harm exceeds the benefit for each patient", "B": "NNT is 20 and NNH is about 67, so benefit greatly exceeds the harm seen", "C": "NNT is 50 and NNH is about 67, so benefit slightly exceeds harm", "D": "NNT is 2.5 and NNH is about 67, so the benefit is very large for patients"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (NNT is 50 and NNH is about 67, so benefit slightly exceeds harm): The absolute risk reduction is 40% of 5%, or 2%, giving an NNT of 50. The NNH is 1 divided by 0.015, about 67. Per 1,000 patients, about 20 strokes are prevented and 15 major bleeds caused, so benefit is modestly larger than harm. Using the baseline risk alone as the denominator ignores the relative reduction."}, {"id": 1118, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "A 34 year old patient with schizophrenia, stable on a long-acting injection, declines a switch to a new antipsychotic proposed by the clinic. A colleague says that the patient should be presumed unable to consent. Which principle is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "Adults are presumed capable unless assessment shows they cannot understand or appreciate the decision", "B": "Capacity is a single status that applies to all of a person's decisions once questioned", "C": "Capacity is determined by whether the patient agrees with the clinician's recommendation after a thorough explanation", "D": "Patients with a psychiatric diagnosis lack capacity until a court or review board confirms otherwise"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Adults are presumed capable unless assessment shows they cannot understand or appreciate the decision): The law presumes that adults have capacity, and capacity is decision specific. A mental illness does not remove it, and disagreement with a recommendation is not evidence of incapacity."}, {"id": 1119, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "A community pharmacist receives a prescription for preservative-free tobramycin 0.3% eye drops, 15 mL, to be compounded for a patient (YC) from the powder with sterilization by a 0.22 micron filter. The pharmacy has only a segregated compounding area with a laminar flow hood and no cleanroom. What is the most appropriate action?", "options": {"A": "Refer the order to a pharmacy that meets NAPRA requirements for compounding from non-sterile ingredients", "B": "Prepare the drops at a clean bench, then pass them through the 0.22 micron filter", "C": "Prepare the drops in the hood, filter into sterile vials, and assign a 24 hour date", "D": "Prepare only 5 mL in the hood, since a smaller batch needs a lower cleanroom standard"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Refer the order to a pharmacy that meets NAPRA requirements for compounding from non-sterile ingredients): Sterilizing a preparation made from non-sterile ingredients is a higher-risk sterile compounding activity that NAPRA standards restrict to facilities with the required cleanroom design, personnel qualifications and testing. A segregated area with a hood does not meet that standard, and the filter does not make up for the environment. Batch size and a short date do not change the requirement."}, {"id": 1120, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "TQ is a 36 year old woman with type 1 diabetes who treated a reading of 3.1 mmol/L with 15 g of glucose tablets. Fifteen minutes later at the counter her meter shows 3.6 mmol/L and she still feels weak. Her next meal is in 40 minutes. What should the pharmacist advise?", "options": {"A": "Eat a large meal immediately instead, since the next meal is already close at hand", "B": "Eat a 30 g slow carbohydrate sandwich, then recheck her glucose in an hour", "C": "Repeat 15 g of fast carbohydrate and recheck in 15 minutes until glucose exceeds 4.0 mmol/L", "D": "Wait another 30 minutes to let the first dose work fully, then recheck her glucose again"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Repeat 15 g of fast carbohydrate and recheck in 15 minutes until glucose exceeds 4.0 mmol/L): If glucose remains below 4.0 mmol/L after 15 minutes, the same 15 g treatment is repeated and glucose rechecked until it recovers. Waiting leaves her symptomatic, and slow carbohydrate foods do not raise glucose quickly enough. Since the next meal is within an hour, a snack is not needed once she recovers."}, {"id": 1121, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "A 69 year old man with osteoporosis takes a multivitamin containing vitamin D3 400 IU. His 25-hydroxyvitamin D result is 38 nmol/L. He has normal kidney function. His physician asks the pharmacist to recommend an appropriate vitamin D dose. Which recommendation is best?", "options": {"A": "Increase to D3 1000 to 2000 IU daily, rechecking the level in three months", "B": "Keep 400 IU daily because it is adequate, and recheck the level in five years", "C": "Switch to D2 400 IU monthly, since monthly dosing corrects deficiency better than daily dosing", "D": "Increase to D3 50 000 IU daily for three months, as the usual osteoporosis dose"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Increase to D3 1000 to 2000 IU daily, rechecking the level in three months): A level below 50 nmol/L is insufficient, and Osteoporosis Canada suggests 800 to 2000 IU daily for people at risk, with the higher end used to correct low levels. A recheck after about three months confirms the response. A daily 50 000 IU dose is a toxic overshoot, and 400 IU will not correct a level this low."}, {"id": 1122, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 3, "question": "A nurse draws gentamicin levels for ST, a 55 year old patient on traditional dosing of 1 mg/kg every 8 hours for endocarditis. The pharmacist is asked when the trough sample should be collected in relation to the next scheduled dose. When is the correct time?", "options": {"A": "Within 30 minutes before the next scheduled dose, after steady state has been reached", "B": "Exactly midway between doses, which estimates the average drug level over the interval", "C": "One hour after the end of the infusion, once distribution is complete", "D": "Two hours after the dose has been started, to capture the highest level reached"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Within 30 minutes before the next scheduled dose, after steady state has been reached): A trough reflects the lowest concentration and is drawn just before the next dose, usually within 30 minutes, once steady state is reached. It is used to judge accumulation and toxicity risk. Samples drawn one to two hours after infusion describe the peak, and a mid-interval sample gives neither value."}, {"id": 1123, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "A patient starting buprenorphine and naloxone sublingual tablets takes the first witnessed dose at the pharmacy counter. The pharmacist wants to make sure the dose is absorbed properly and is not diverted, and the patient asks what to do. Which instruction best supports both goals?", "options": {"A": "Chew the tablets and drink water so that the dose acts more quickly", "B": "Swallow the tablets with water to speed up absorption before leaving the counter", "C": "Place the tablets under the tongue and stay until they have fully dissolved", "D": "Place the tablets under the tongue and leave the pharmacy to finish dissolving at home"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Place the tablets under the tongue and stay until they have fully dissolved): Sublingual buprenorphine and naloxone is absorbed through the mucosa under the tongue, so swallowing greatly reduces effect. Staying until the tablets dissolve lets the pharmacist confirm the dose was taken. Leaving early defeats the witnessing, and chewing or swallowing changes absorption."}, {"id": 1124, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A trial in 600 women with osteoporosis found vertebral fractures in 18 of 300 women taking a bisphosphonate and 36 of 300 women taking placebo over three years. A pharmacist presents the findings at an inservice and calculates the number needed to treat.", "options": {"A": "17 women need treatment for three years to prevent one additional vertebral fracture", "B": "50 women need treatment for three years, found by dividing 1 by the 2% annual difference", "C": "6 women need treatment for three years, the absolute risk reduction as a whole number", "D": "8 women need treatment for three years, found by dividing 1 by the 12% placebo rate"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (17 women need treatment for three years to prevent one additional vertebral fracture): Event rates are 18 of 300, or 6%, and 36 of 300, or 12%, so the absolute risk reduction is 6 percentage points over three years. The NNT is 1 divided by 0.06, or 16.7, rounded up to 17. Dividing by the placebo rate or annualizing the difference gives values that do not match the stated time frame."}, {"id": 1125, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "After quarantining recalled bottles, the pharmacy has still received no return instructions from the manufacturer. A technician proposes putting the quarantined bottles back on a distant shelf with a note until the instructions arrive. How should the pharmacist respond to this proposal?", "options": {"A": "Destroy it on site and discard the packaging, since waiting for instructions adds no benefit", "B": "Shelve it in a distant aisle with a note, since only dispensed units carry risk", "C": "Keep it segregated and labelled recalled until the manufacturer gives return or destruction instructions", "D": "Sell it to staff at a discount, since the recall covers only patient dispensing"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Keep it segregated and labelled recalled until the manufacturer gives return or destruction instructions): Recalled stock must stay physically separate and clearly labelled so it can never be dispensed by mistake, and it is kept until the manufacturer directs return or destruction. A note on a regular shelf invites error, and destroying it early may violate the recall instructions."}, {"id": 1126, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A guideline gives a conditional (weak) recommendation for a bone-protective drug in women over 75 with moderate fracture risk. A pharmacist in a community clinic is asked how to apply this recommendation when speaking with a patient. Which approach is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Recommend the drug to all patients, because a weak recommendation still favours treatment over none", "B": "Advise against the drug, since a weak recommendation means the panel found the evidence unreliable", "C": "Defer the decision to the guideline panel, because patients cannot weigh benefits and harms themselves", "D": "Discuss the alternatives with the patient, since the best fit depends on personal values"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Discuss the alternatives with the patient, since the best fit depends on personal values): A conditional recommendation means that reasonable patients could choose differently because the balance of benefit, harm and burden is close or uncertain. Shared decision making is therefore central. It does not mean the evidence is unreliable, and it does not mandate treatment for everyone."}, {"id": 1127, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "A 55 year old man with a BMI of 38 kg/m2 and a clinic blood pressure of 168/102 mmHg on two visits, not yet treated, asks his prescriber about naltrexone-bupropion. The pharmacist is asked to comment. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Appropriate at half the usual starting dose, since lower doses do not affect blood pressure", "B": "Appropriate, because naltrexone lowers blood pressure and balances the bupropion effect on the heart", "C": "Not until hypertension is controlled, as bupropion can raise blood pressure and heart rate", "D": "Appropriate if started in the evening only, because bupropion affects blood pressure mainly during daytime"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Not until hypertension is controlled, as bupropion can raise blood pressure and heart rate): Bupropion can increase blood pressure and heart rate, so naltrexone-bupropion is not used in uncontrolled hypertension. Blood pressure should be treated first and monitored if therapy is started later. Dose timing and dose reduction do not remove the pressor effect."}, {"id": 1128, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 6, "question": "PL is a 26 year old requesting emergency contraception 80 hours after unprotected intercourse. She also reports yellow vaginal discharge, pelvic tenderness, and new partner exposure. She prefers a copper intrauterine device, which her clinic can insert this week. What should the pharmacist recommend first?", "options": {"A": "Give ulipristal now and send her for infection testing before any intrauterine device insertion", "B": "Advise that no emergency method is suitable until her symptoms have fully resolved with treatment", "C": "Give levonorgestrel 1.5 mg now, since it is as effective as ulipristal at 80 hours", "D": "Arrange same-day copper device insertion, since it is most effective and infection can be treated afterward"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Give ulipristal now and send her for infection testing before any intrauterine device insertion): Her symptoms suggest cervicitis or pelvic infection, and inserting an intrauterine device through an infected cervix risks spreading the infection upward. Ulipristal remains effective at 80 hours and covers the pregnancy risk while she is tested and treated. Levonorgestrel loses effectiveness beyond 72 hours, and delaying all emergency contraception leaves her unprotected."}, {"id": 1129, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 7, "question": "PT is a 33 year old contact lens wearer using preserved artificial tears about eight times daily for dry, gritty eyes. Her eyes have no redness or pain, and her vision is normal. She asks whether another product would suit her better. What should the pharmacist suggest?", "options": {"A": "Decongestant drops used each morning, because redness relief also restores the tear film", "B": "A topical antibiotic ointment at bedtime, since gritty eyes indicate an early bacterial infection", "C": "Preserved drops used more often, since greater frequency improves comfort on the eye surface", "D": "Preservative free single use artificial tears, since frequent preserved drops can irritate the ocular surface"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Preservative free single use artificial tears, since frequent preserved drops can irritate the ocular surface): Preservatives such as benzalkonium chloride can damage the corneal surface when drops are used more than four times daily, so preservative free units are preferred for frequent use. Decongestants do not treat dryness and antibiotics are not indicated without signs of infection."}, {"id": 1130, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "RC is a 52 year old man with diabetes who started sildenafil 50 mg for erectile dysfunction and reports no benefit after two attempts. He took it right after a large fatty dinner each time. How should the pharmacist counsel him?", "options": {"A": "Take it on an emptier stomach one hour before activity, since fatty meals delay absorption", "B": "Switch to taking it every morning, since daily dosing is required for sildenafil to work", "C": "Take it after the meal and double the dose to 100 mg, since food increases absorption", "D": "Stop sildenafil, since two failed attempts show that PDE5 inhibitors are ineffective in men with diabetes"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Take it on an emptier stomach one hour before activity, since fatty meals delay absorption): A heavy fatty meal delays absorption and lowers peak levels of sildenafil, so response can be missed. It should be taken about an hour before activity on a lighter stomach, with sexual stimulation required. At least several attempts are advised before judging failure."}, {"id": 1131, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "MN, a 66 year old woman, starts simvastatin 40 mg nightly and also takes amlodipine 5 mg daily. She drinks a large glass of grapefruit juice every morning. Which auxiliary label and counselling point should the pharmacist prioritize for the simvastatin?", "options": {"A": "Avoid dairy products for two hours because they reduce simvastatin absorption by the gut", "B": "Avoid large amounts of grapefruit juice because it increases simvastatin blood levels", "C": "Take it with a high fat meal because food prevents muscle pain", "D": "Take it with grapefruit juice in the morning to improve absorption of the tablet"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Avoid large amounts of grapefruit juice because it increases simvastatin blood levels): Grapefruit juice inhibits intestinal CYP3A4, which raises simvastatin levels and the risk of myopathy, so large amounts should be avoided. Grapefruit does not improve absorption safely, dairy has no such effect on simvastatin, and food does not prevent statin muscle symptoms."}, {"id": 1132, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 4, "question": "A father asks for a cough and cold syrup for his 4 year old daughter, who has had a runny nose and cough for two days with no fever. He plans to use a multi-symptom syrup labelled for adults at a smaller measured dose. The pharmacist should advise:", "options": {"A": "avoiding over-the-counter cough and cold products under age six, and using saline drops and fluids", "B": "using the adult syrup at one quarter of the label dose, measured with an oral syringe", "C": "using the adult syrup only at bedtime, because a single nightly dose limits side effects", "D": "using a children's decongestant syrup only, since cough suppressants are the ones considered unsafe"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (avoiding over-the-counter cough and cold products under age six, and using saline drops and fluids): Health Canada advises against over-the-counter cough and cold products in children under six because they offer little benefit and carry a risk of side effects and overdose. Cutting an adult dose is not a safe workaround, and decongestants are not safer than suppressants. Saline, fluids and rest are appropriate."}, {"id": 1133, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A resident asks why a randomized trial can control confounding that no regression analysis of observational data can fully control. The pharmacist points to variables that nobody measured or even knew about. What is the key advantage of randomization for this purpose?", "options": {"A": "It removes the need for large samples, because chance imbalance is impossible once allocation is random", "B": "It makes groups identical on all measured variables, so no confounder remains in any trial", "C": "It distributes unmeasured as well as measured confounders evenly between the groups, on average", "D": "It allows adjustment for unmeasured variables by adding more covariates to the final statistical model"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (It distributes unmeasured as well as measured confounders evenly between the groups, on average): Regression can only adjust for variables that were recorded. Random allocation balances unknown factors in expectation, which is why it supports causal conclusions. Chance imbalance can still occur in small trials, so balance is not guaranteed in any single study."}, {"id": 1134, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "KC is a 70 year old woman with normal renal function who has received levofloxacin 750 mg intravenously once daily for pneumonia and is now eating and improving. The pharmacist considers switching to oral therapy. Given the drug's high oral bioavailability, which dose is appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Levofloxacin 1500 mg orally once daily, since oral doses must be doubled to match injection", "B": "Levofloxacin 375 mg orally once daily, since the oral route delivers more of the dose", "C": "Levofloxacin 750 mg orally once daily, since intravenous and oral doses are equivalent", "D": "Levofloxacin 500 mg orally twice daily, since divided doses are needed for oral absorption"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Levofloxacin 750 mg orally once daily, since intravenous and oral doses are equivalent): Levofloxacin is almost nearly fully absorbed, so intravenous and oral doses are interchangeable at the same milligram amount. A switch to oral therapy at the same dose is a common step-down. Doubling or halving the dose has no pharmacokinetic basis."}, {"id": 1135, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "JK, a 52 year old man, has a prescription for tobramycin eye drops written 'ii gtt OU qid x 1/52'. The pharmacist is writing the label in plain language for a patient who reads at a grade 6 level. Which label wording is most appropriate for the directions?", "options": {"A": "Put 2 drops in each eye 4 times a day for 1 week", "B": "Put 2 drops in the right eye only, 4 times a day for 1 week", "C": "Put 2 drops in each eye 4 times a day for 1 month, then stop", "D": "Put 2 drops in each eye every 4 hours while awake for 1 week"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Put 2 drops in each eye 4 times a day for 1 week): OU means both eyes, qid means four times a day, and 1/52 means one week. The wording must convert all three correctly. OD would be the right eye alone, every 4 hours is a different schedule from four times daily, and 1/52 is a week rather than a month."}, {"id": 1136, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "At month-end a pharmacist reconciles the narcotic count for oxycodone 5 mg tablets. The perpetual inventory shows 482 tablets, while the physical count is 478. The pharmacist has recounted twice. Which action meets the federal requirements for a loss or discrepancy of a narcotic?", "options": {"A": "Investigate the cause, and report an unexplained loss to Health Canada within 10 days", "B": "Report the loss to Health Canada within 30 days after the next quarterly narcotic audit", "C": "Adjust the perpetual inventory to 478 and keep a note in the narcotic register file", "D": "Order four tablets from the wholesaler and record them as returned stock"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Investigate the cause, and report an unexplained loss to Health Canada within 10 days): The narcotic discrepancy is first investigated for recording errors, and a loss that cannot be explained must be reported to Health Canada within 10 days under the Narcotic Control Regulations. Adjusting the count silently, waiting for an audit, or reordering to hide the gap does not meet the reporting duty."}, {"id": 1137, "domain": "communication_and_collaboration", "chapter": 11, "question": "ER, a 74 year old woman, is being transferred from hospital to long-term care. Her pre-admission list shows ramipril 5 mg daily, but the discharge summary lists lisinopril 10 mg daily with no comment. Both are ACE inhibitors. What should the pharmacist do?", "options": {"A": "Continue both ACE inhibitors until her potassium and creatinine are rechecked at the long-term care home", "B": "Transcribe the discharge summary as written, because it is the most recent document from the hospital", "C": "Confirm with the prescriber that lisinopril replaces ramipril and ensure only one is continued", "D": "Send both to the home and let nurses choose between them at each morning medication pass"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Confirm with the prescriber that lisinopril replaces ramipril and ensure only one is continued): An unexplained change from one ACE inhibitor to another, with the original absent, is a discrepancy that could produce therapeutic duplication or an unplanned change. The prescriber must confirm intent, and only one ACE inhibitor should remain on the list. Copying the summary or leaving the decision to nurses transfers an unresolved risk."}, {"id": 1138, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "RB is a 66 year old woman who takes insulin glargine U100 from a pen. She keeps the opened pen in her purse and stores the spare pens in the kitchen cupboard beside the stove. She asks how long an opened pen lasts. What should the pharmacist tell her?", "options": {"A": "An opened pen lasts 56 days if kept cool, and spares can stay in the cupboard", "B": "An opened pen lasts until the printed expiry date, whatever the storage conditions it has seen", "C": "An opened pen lasts 7 days at room temperature, so she should discard it weekly", "D": "An opened pen lasts 28 days at room temperature, with spares kept in the refrigerator"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (An opened pen lasts 28 days at room temperature, with spares kept in the refrigerator): Glargine in use is stable at room temperature for 28 days, after which it should be discarded. Unopened spare pens should be refrigerated, and a cupboard beside a stove is too warm. Insulin exposed to heat can lose potency before the printed expiry date."}, {"id": 1139, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "A technician finishes a blister card for a long term care resident and hands it to the pharmacist. The resident takes twelve medications at four dose times each day. The pharmacy policy requires a final check before the card goes to the facility. Which check does the pharmacist perform?", "options": {"A": "Confirm that the technician has initialled the packaging record for the card", "B": "Count the cells on the card and confirm the dates printed on the front", "C": "Verify the contents of each cell against the profile and the source product identification", "D": "Compare the card only to the card prepared the previous week for the same resident"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Verify the contents of each cell against the profile and the source product identification): The final pharmacist check confirms that the right drugs, strengths and numbers are in the correct cells, using the profile and source product identification. Counting cells or looking at initials does not prove the contents are correct. Comparing to last week's card would repeat any earlier error."}, {"id": 1140, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "A technician uses a balance to check an automated fill. A sample of 50 tablets of drug X weighs 30.5 g. The filled vial for a prescription of 30 tablets weighs 19.5 g of tablets, excluding the vial. How many tablets are in the vial, and is it acceptable?", "options": {"A": "About 30 tablets, so the vial is acceptable and can be released", "B": "About 40 tablets, so the vial holds 10 too many and must be corrected", "C": "About 28 tablets, so the vial is short by 2 and must be topped up", "D": "About 32 tablets, so the vial holds 2 too many and must be corrected"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (About 32 tablets, so the vial holds 2 too many and must be corrected): Each tablet weighs 30.5 / 50 = 0.61 g, so 19.5 g holds 19.5 / 0.61 = about 32 tablets. That is 2 more than prescribed, and the extra tablets must be removed and the count verified."}, {"id": 1141, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "PJ is a 52 year old man who started allopurinol 100 mg daily three weeks ago. He now has a moderate flare in his left ankle, with serum urate 410 umol/L. His eGFR is 75 mL/min/1.73 m2 and he has no rash. What should the pharmacist advise?", "options": {"A": "Double the allopurinol dose at once to suppress the flare through faster urate reduction", "B": "Continue allopurinol and treat the flare, since stopping urate lowering therapy mid flare is unnecessary", "C": "Switch to a uricosuric drug, since flares during allopurinol therapy mean xanthine oxidase inhibition is ineffective", "D": "Stop allopurinol because the flare shows failure and restart only after a full year without attacks"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Continue allopurinol and treat the flare, since stopping urate lowering therapy mid flare is unnecessary): Flares are common in the first months of urate lowering because crystals mobilize as urate falls, and they do not signal failure. The drug is continued while the flare is treated, and prophylaxis is considered. Rapid dose escalation can provoke more flares."}, {"id": 1142, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "A 45 year old woman has a TSH of 6.5 mIU/L with a normal free T4, no symptoms, and negative thyroid peroxidase antibodies. Her physician is deciding whether to treat. The pharmacist is asked about the recommended approach. What is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Start liothyronine 25 mcg daily, because mild elevation reflects a conversion problem, not gland failure", "B": "Start a thyroid extract supplement, because one mildly raised TSH confirms lasting thyroid failure", "C": "Start levothyroxine 100 mcg daily today, since any TSH above the reference range needs treatment", "D": "Repeat TSH and free T4 in two to three months before deciding about treatment"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Repeat TSH and free T4 in two to three months before deciding about treatment): A mildly raised TSH with normal free T4 is often transient, so guidelines advise repeating the tests before committing to lifelong therapy. Treatment is considered when TSH is persistently high, especially above 10 mIU/L, or in pregnancy or with symptoms and positive antibodies. This patient meets none of those criteria yet."}, {"id": 1143, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 4, "question": "A mother brings her 7 year old son, who has round scaly patches of hair loss on his scalp with broken hairs. She asks for clotrimazole cream from the shelf. The boy is otherwise healthy. What should the pharmacist recommend?", "options": {"A": "Permethrin 1% rinse repeated in one week, since scaling with broken hairs points to head lice", "B": "Hydrocortisone cream twice daily for one week to calm the scaling, then reassess the hair loss", "C": "Refer for oral antifungal therapy, since topical products alone do not penetrate the hair follicles", "D": "Clotrimazole cream twice daily for four weeks with an antifungal shampoo that reaches the hair shaft"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Refer for oral antifungal therapy, since topical products alone do not penetrate the hair follicles): Tinea capitis involves the hair shaft and follicle, so oral griseofulvin or terbinafine is needed. Topical antifungals and shampoos can reduce spread but do not cure it. Lice do not cause patchy hair loss with broken stubs, and steroids would not help."}, {"id": 1144, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "A 45 year old woman has taken prednisone 30 mg daily for five weeks for nephrotic syndrome. She plans a trip abroad and asks about receiving a live attenuated vaccine at the pharmacy this week. What should the pharmacist advise?", "options": {"A": "Give the vaccine today, because corticosteroid dose has no effect on live vaccine safety", "B": "Give a double dose today, because immune suppression reduces the response to a single dose", "C": "Give the vaccine only after her daily prednisone dose, because timing removes the infection risk", "D": "Defer live vaccines, as prednisone at 20 mg daily beyond 14 days suppresses immunity"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Defer live vaccines, as prednisone at 20 mg daily beyond 14 days suppresses immunity): High-dose corticosteroid therapy, about 20 mg of prednisone daily or more for 14 days or longer, impairs immunity enough that a live attenuated vaccine can cause disease. Live vaccines are deferred until the dose is lowered and a period has passed after stopping. Inactivated vaccines are generally safe but may produce a weaker response."}, {"id": 1145, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "A pharmacist screens sildenafil 50 mg for BG, 60, who uses nitroglycerin spray as needed for angina. The system alert says the combination is contraindicated and the technician asks why a one-time use matters. What is the main concern behind this alert?", "options": {"A": "profound hypotension from additive vasodilation, which may cause syncope or myocardial ischemia", "B": "serotonin syndrome from combined effects on serotonin reuptake in the central nervous system", "C": "severe bradycardia from additive suppression of sinoatrial node conduction in patients with angina", "D": "prolonged QT interval with torsades from additive effects on cardiac repolarization in older patients"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (profound hypotension from additive vasodilation, which may cause syncope or myocardial ischemia): Nitrates and PDE-5 inhibitors both increase nitric oxide signalling, so together they can cause dangerous drops in blood pressure that may reduce coronary perfusion. For this reason the combination is contraindicated, including with as-needed nitroglycerin."}, {"id": 1146, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "A patient uses lancets four times a day and pen needles four times a day for 30 days. A sharps container holds 150 items to its fill line, and the pharmacist is dispensing for 30 days. How many sharps containers does the patient need for the month, rounded up?", "options": {"A": "8 containers, because one container is needed for each daily use of lancets and needles", "B": "4 containers, because each device type needs its own two containers each month", "C": "1 container, because 240 items divided by 150 is simply rounded down to 1 container", "D": "2 containers, because 240 items divided by 150 is 1.6 and must be rounded up"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (2 containers, because 240 items divided by 150 is 1.6 and must be rounded up): Eight sharps per day for 30 days is 240 items. Divided by 150 per container this is 1.6, so the patient needs 2 containers to avoid overfilling. Rounding down would force an overfilled container, which raises needle-stick risk for anyone handling it."}, {"id": 1147, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "An older adult asks a pharmacist how quickly medical assistance in dying can proceed. She has terminal cancer, her death is reasonably foreseeable, and two independent assessments have approved her signed written request. Under current federal law, which statement about minimum timing is accurate for her situation?", "options": {"A": "A thirty day waiting period applies once the independent witness has signed the written request", "B": "A period of 90 clear days must pass after the first assessment begins for all patients", "C": "A mandatory reflection period of ten clear days must pass between the request and provision", "D": "No minimum waiting period is required, though the provider must be satisfied she still consents"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (No minimum waiting period is required, though the provider must be satisfied she still consents): Since the 2021 amendments, people whose natural death is reasonably foreseeable are not subject to the former ten day reflection period, but the provider must still confirm the person consents. The 90 day assessment period applies only when natural death is not reasonably foreseeable. A thirty day period is not part of the federal law."}, {"id": 1148, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "UB is a 58 year old man who injects basal insulin glargine into the same area of his abdomen every day. His pharmacist notices a firm lump at the site, and his morning glucose readings vary widely. What counselling is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "rotate sites within a region, since repeated injection in one spot causes lipohypertrophy", "B": "switch to intramuscular injection into the thigh, since deeper delivery avoids tissue changes", "C": "inject at a shallower angle with a longer needle, since this prevents the lumps from forming", "D": "inject directly into the lump, since the thickened tissue holds the insulin and slows absorption evenly"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (rotate sites within a region, since repeated injection in one spot causes lipohypertrophy): Repeated injections in one spot cause lipohypertrophy, and insulin absorbed from these areas is erratic, which explains the variable readings. Rotating sites and avoiding the lump restores more reliable absorption. Intramuscular injection of basal insulin is not recommended."}, {"id": 1149, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 7, "question": "OJ is an 8 year old boy who has difficulty swallowing tablets and was prescribed a sustained release methylphenidate tablet 18 mg for ADHD. His mother asks whether she may crush the tablet into applesauce so he can take it. What should the pharmacist advise?", "options": {"A": "Crush the tablet finely and mix it into applesauce, since the smaller particles still release slowly", "B": "Crush the tablet and give half the dose, since crushing speeds up the release of drug", "C": "Do not crush it, and ask the prescriber about a suitable alternative formulation", "D": "Halve the tablet with a pill cutter and give each half separately to make swallowing easier"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Do not crush it, and ask the prescriber about a suitable alternative formulation): Sustained release tablets rely on an intact release mechanism, and crushing or splitting can deliver a large dose at once. The pharmacist should ask the prescriber about a liquid, chewable or sprinkle capsule product. Giving half the crushed dose would still be unpredictable."}, {"id": 1150, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 4, "question": "QH is a 20 year old student with moderate inflammatory acne who takes doxycycline 100 mg twice daily, once with breakfast and once with a calcium supplement at bedtime. His physician also added benzoyl peroxide gel. He asks how to improve his routine. Which advice is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Take both together at bedtime, since calcium protects the stomach from doxycycline irritation and burning", "B": "Stop the gel while on the antibiotic, since the two ingredients reduce one another's activity", "C": "Separate doxycycline and calcium by two hours, and use the gel to limit resistance", "D": "Continue the antibiotic alone for six to twelve months, since combining agents increases photosensitivity risk"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Separate doxycycline and calcium by two hours, and use the gel to limit resistance): Calcium binds doxycycline in the gut and reduces absorption, so the two should be separated by about two hours. Benzoyl peroxide should be combined with oral antibiotics because it lowers the development of resistant bacteria. Antibiotic monotherapy for a long period is discouraged."}, {"id": 1151, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "A telephone message from a clinic for TR, 66, reads 'insulin glargine 30 at bedtime'. The profile shows insulin glargine 20 units at bedtime for the past year, and the message gives no units and no mention of a dose change. What should the pharmacist do?", "options": {"A": "Enter 20 units, the established dose, since the message does not state a deliberate change", "B": "Call the prescriber to confirm units and whether the dose was meant to rise above 20", "C": "Enter 30 units and add a note for the final check to compare with the profile", "D": "Enter 30 units and tell TR to increase slowly if his morning glucose readings are low"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Call the prescriber to confirm units and whether the dose was meant to rise above 20): The order lacks units and conflicts with the profile, so it is ambiguous and a ten unit change in a high-alert drug could cause serious hypoglycemia. The prescriber must clarify before dispensing. Assuming either dose is a guess."}, {"id": 1152, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 1, "question": "HL is a 64 year old woman after an ischemic stroke who stopped atorvastatin 40 mg after three weeks because of muscle aches. Her creatine kinase was normal. She is worried about another stroke. Which approach is most appropriate? She takes no interacting drugs.", "options": {"A": "accept that she cannot take any lipid therapy and rely on a low-fat diet alone", "B": "restart atorvastatin 80 mg to overcome the muscle symptoms through stronger enzyme blockade", "C": "switch to a fibrate, because fibrates reduce stroke recurrence more than any statin", "D": "try a different statin such as rosuvastatin 5 mg, adding ezetimibe if needed"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (try a different statin such as rosuvastatin 5 mg, adding ezetimibe if needed): Most statin muscle complaints with a normal creatine kinase can be managed by switching statins, lowering the dose or alternate-day dosing, with ezetimibe added if the LDL target is not reached. Stopping lipid therapy entirely leaves a high-risk patient unprotected."}, {"id": 1153, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 4, "question": "A father brings in his 5 year old daughter, who has impetigo near her chin. The pharmacist has just dispensed topical fusidic acid. He asks when she can return to daycare, where several other children have had skin infections this month.", "options": {"A": "She can return immediately, since impetigo stops being contagious once the first topical dose is applied", "B": "She can return 24 hours after starting treatment, once lesions are covered and no longer weeping", "C": "She can return after a full ten day course ends and a negative swab is confirmed", "D": "She can return once all crusts have fallen off, which usually takes two to three weeks"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (She can return 24 hours after starting treatment, once lesions are covered and no longer weeping): Children with impetigo are generally excluded until 24 hours after antibiotic treatment starts, with lesions covered. Waiting for all crusts to fall off is unnecessarily long, and swabs are not required. Contagiousness falls over a day of treatment, not after a single dose."}, {"id": 1154, "domain": "communication_and_collaboration", "chapter": 11, "question": "DV, a 38 year old woman with post-traumatic stress disorder, becomes visibly distressed when the pharmacist begins counselling about her new sertraline at the busy dispensary counter. She is breathing quickly, her voice is shaking and she glances at the door. Which action best reflects trauma-informed practice?", "options": {"A": "End the visit and ask her to return with a family member who hears the instructions", "B": "Pause, offer to move to a quieter private area, and let her choose how to continue", "C": "Continue counselling at a faster pace so that the experience is over as soon as possible", "D": "Ask her to describe the event that triggered the distress so staff can avoid it"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Pause, offer to move to a quieter private area, and let her choose how to continue): Trauma-informed care recognizes triggers, restores a sense of safety and gives choices. Pausing and offering privacy lets her regain control. Rushing, probing into the trauma and sending her away to return with someone else each ignore her immediate need for safety and agency."}, {"id": 1155, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 4, "question": "PG is a 29 year old kidney transplant recipient who was switched from cyclosporine to tacrolimus six weeks ago. She asks why her gum swelling and excess facial hair have improved. Her kidney function is stable. Which statement best explains the change?", "options": {"A": "Tacrolimus is less toxic to the kidney, and the improved renal function reduces hair growth", "B": "Both drugs cause these effects equally, so the change reflects her recent reduction in prednisone dose", "C": "Tacrolimus acts at a different receptor than cyclosporine, which removes all effects on connective tissue", "D": "Gingival hyperplasia and hirsutism are typical cyclosporine effects and occur less often with tacrolimus"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Gingival hyperplasia and hirsutism are typical cyclosporine effects and occur less often with tacrolimus): Gingival hyperplasia and hirsutism are well known cosmetic adverse effects of cyclosporine and are much less frequent with tacrolimus, although tacrolimus carries more tremor, hyperglycemia and alopecia. Kidney function and steroid dose do not account for these changes."}, {"id": 1156, "domain": "communication_and_collaboration", "chapter": 11, "question": "FJ is a 63 year old man collecting a new insulin glargine prescription who says he will read the label later and asks that forms be given to his wife to complete. The pharmacist suspects limited health literacy. Which approach to assessing this is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Assume he has limited literacy and use only pictures with no written information", "B": "Judge his literacy from the years of education and occupation recorded in his pharmacy file", "C": "Give a standard reading test in front of other patients so results are objective", "D": "Ask a non-judgmental question about how comfortable he is reading medication information"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Ask a non-judgmental question about how comfortable he is reading medication information): Brief, respectful questions about confidence with reading health information screen for literacy without embarrassment. Public testing humiliates patients, and education or job history are poor predictors of health literacy."}, {"id": 1157, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 7, "question": "The pharmacist is teaching the father of a 2 year old girl with swimmer's ear how to instill prescribed ear drops. She has had pain for two days and the father is anxious about getting the technique right. Which instruction describes correct technique for a child this age?", "options": {"A": "Press the drops straight onto the eardrum with a cotton swab, then she may sit up", "B": "Pull the top of the ear up and back, then she stands upright for one minute", "C": "Pull the earlobe down and back, then she lies on her side for five minutes", "D": "Heat the bottle in hot water for ten minutes, then instill with the head upright"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Pull the earlobe down and back, then she lies on her side for five minutes): In children under about three years, the ear canal is straightened by pulling the earlobe down and back, and the child then needs several minutes lying with the treated ear upward so the drops reach the canal. Pulling up and back is the technique for older children and adults. Cotton swabs placed against the eardrum and very hot bottles are unsafe."}, {"id": 1158, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "GO is a 35 year old man who started mixed amphetamine salts extended-release 20 mg three weeks ago. At a pharmacy clinic his blood pressure is 152/96 mm Hg and heart rate 104 beats per minute, up from 124/78 and 72 before treatment. He feels jittery. What is the best recommendation?", "options": {"A": "Advise taking the dose every second day to remove the blood pressure effect and keep benefit", "B": "Reassure him that increases of this size are expected with stimulants and settle on their own", "C": "Suggest an immediate-release amphetamine instead, since it has a smaller effect on blood pressure", "D": "Contact the prescriber promptly, since the stimulant has raised his pressure and heart rate"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Contact the prescriber promptly, since the stimulant has raised his pressure and heart rate): A rise of about 28 mm Hg systolic and 18 mm Hg diastolic with a heart rate over 100 is more than the small average increase expected with stimulants and needs prescriber reassessment of dose, drug selection, and cardiovascular risk. Reassurance delays care. Skipping days or switching to an immediate-release product on the pharmacist's own advice is not supported."}, {"id": 1159, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 6, "question": "OT is a 29 year old woman with ulcerative colitis, stable on sulfasalazine 1 g twice daily, who plans to conceive within the next few months. She asks whether she needs to change her therapy before becoming pregnant. Which advice is most appropriate for the pharmacist to give?", "options": {"A": "Stop sulfasalazine before conception because it harms the fetus in the first trimester", "B": "Switch to methotrexate since it carries the safest pregnancy profile among IBD agents", "C": "Continue sulfasalazine and add higher-dose folic acid, since it impairs folate absorption", "D": "Continue sulfasalazine with no supplement, because folate status is unaffected by the sulfa component"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Continue sulfasalazine and add higher-dose folic acid, since it impairs folate absorption): Sulfasalazine is considered compatible with pregnancy, and stopping it risks a flare, which is more harmful to the fetus. The sulfapyridine component interferes with folate absorption, so a higher folic acid dose is recommended. Methotrexate is teratogenic and must be avoided when planning pregnancy."}, {"id": 1160, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "After a snowstorm, a pharmacy delivers insulin pens and a prefilled syringe kit to a patient's home. The driver reports the insulated bag sat in a cold car for several hours. The pharmacist wants to choose the right next step about these refrigerated products. What is best?", "options": {"A": "Ask the patient to warm the pens in hot water before use to restore potency", "B": "Assess the temperature record and product stability data before deciding whether to replace the insulin", "C": "Tell the patient to use the pens as usual because cold exposure cannot damage insulin", "D": "Replace the insulin only if the patient reports it looks cloudy or has crystals"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Assess the temperature record and product stability data before deciding whether to replace the insulin): Insulin must not freeze, and a cold car could have caused freezing. The pharmacist should review exposure and manufacturer stability information and replace product if doubt exists. Appearance is not a reliable test for freezing damage, and heating a pen in hot water can itself destroy the insulin."}, {"id": 1161, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "The technician plans to use the reconstituted vials for LP's dose. What total volume of reconstituted 50 mg/mL vancomycin solution must be withdrawn from the vials to prepare the full ordered dose, and from how many vials will it come?", "options": {"A": "62.5 mL from four vials, because the 1,250 mg dose is divided by 20 mg/mL", "B": "25 mL, using the whole first vial and 5 mL from a second vial", "C": "12.5 mL from one vial, because 1,250 mg is divided by a strength of 100 mg/mL", "D": "20 mL, using one whole vial, since the nearest standard 1 g dose is acceptable here"}, "cluster_id": "CHS3", "cluster_text": "LP is a 72 year old woman, 58 kg, admitted with a bone infection. The physician orders vancomycin 1,250 mg intravenously every 12 hours, to be diluted in 250 mL of 0.9% sodium chloride and infused over 2 hours. The pharmacy stocks vancomycin 1 g vials of powder. Each vial is reconstituted with 20 mL of sterile water for injection to give 50 mg/mL. Preparation takes place in a biological safety cabinet by a trained technician. Her creatinine clearance is 55 mL/min.", "cluster_size": 3, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (25 mL, using the whole first vial and 5 mL from a second vial): At 50 mg/mL, the 1,250 mg dose requires 1,250 divided by 50, which is 25 mL. One reconstituted 1 g vial contains 20 mL, so the technician uses one whole vial plus 5 mL from another, which supplies the remaining 250 mg. Rounding down to one vial gives a 20% underdose, and dividing by 20 mg/mL or 100 mg/mL misreads the concentration."}, {"id": 1162, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "After adding the vancomycin to the 250 mL bag of sodium chloride, the nurse asks the pharmacist for the infusion rate to deliver the whole bag over the ordered 2 hours using an infusion pump. Ignoring the small volume of added drug, which rate should be programmed on the pump?", "options": {"A": "125 mL per hour, because 250 mL divided by 2 hours gives that rate", "B": "62.5 mL per hour, because the bag is spread over four hours to reduce infusion reactions", "C": "250 mL per hour, because vancomycin doses in this volume are infused over a single hour", "D": "500 mL per hour, because 250 mL multiplied by the 2 hours gives the rate"}, "cluster_id": "CHS3", "cluster_text": "LP is a 72 year old woman, 58 kg, admitted with a bone infection. The physician orders vancomycin 1,250 mg intravenously every 12 hours, to be diluted in 250 mL of 0.9% sodium chloride and infused over 2 hours. The pharmacy stocks vancomycin 1 g vials of powder. Each vial is reconstituted with 20 mL of sterile water for injection to give 50 mg/mL. Preparation takes place in a biological safety cabinet by a trained technician. Her creatinine clearance is 55 mL/min.", "cluster_size": 3, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (125 mL per hour, because 250 mL divided by 2 hours gives that rate): The rate equals volume divided by time, so 250 mL over 2 hours is 125 mL per hour. Multiplying instead of dividing, or using a different time, produces a rate that is far too fast or too slow. The added vancomycin volume is small and the order specifies infusing the full bag."}, {"id": 1163, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "Before releasing LP's completed vancomycin bag, the pharmacist performs the final check in the dispensing area. The bag is labelled, sealed and free of obvious leaks. Which additional inspection step is required for this intravenous admixture before it is released from the pharmacy?", "options": {"A": "Culture a 5 mL sample from the bag to confirm sterility before it leaves the pharmacy", "B": "Check against light and dark backgrounds for particles, cloudiness or colour change before release", "C": "Shake the bag vigorously for one minute so that foaming confirms the drug is fully mixed", "D": "Confirm the bag feels cold to the touch, which indicates that the drug has stayed stable"}, "cluster_id": "CHS3", "cluster_text": "LP is a 72 year old woman, 58 kg, admitted with a bone infection. The physician orders vancomycin 1,250 mg intravenously every 12 hours, to be diluted in 250 mL of 0.9% sodium chloride and infused over 2 hours. The pharmacy stocks vancomycin 1 g vials of powder. Each vial is reconstituted with 20 mL of sterile water for injection to give 50 mg/mL. Preparation takes place in a biological safety cabinet by a trained technician. Her creatinine clearance is 55 mL/min.", "cluster_size": 3, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Check against light and dark backgrounds for particles, cloudiness or colour change before release): A final visual inspection against a light and a dark background detects visible particles, haze and discolouration that indicate a problem with the preparation, and any defect means the bag is not released. Temperature, foaming and routine culture of a single dose do not demonstrate sterility or correct mixing, and sterility culture of a dose that must be given promptly is impractical."}, {"id": 1164, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "A 78 year old man, 70 kg, with atrial fibrillation and no active bleeding has a serum creatinine of 130 umol/L. A prescription for rivaroxaban 20 mg daily with the evening meal is received. Using Cockcroft-Gault, which assessment is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "CrCl is about 41 mL/min, so 15 mg once daily with food is the usual dose", "B": "CrCl is about 52 mL/min, so the ordered 20 mg daily dose is appropriate as written", "C": "CrCl is about 41 mL/min, so the ordered 20 mg dose is appropriate given his age", "D": "CrCl is about 41 mL/min, so rivaroxaban cannot be used and apixaban must be started"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (CrCl is about 41 mL/min, so 15 mg once daily with food is the usual dose): CrCl = (140 - 78) x 70 x 1.23 / 130 = about 41 mL/min. For atrial fibrillation, a CrCl of 15 to 49 mL/min calls for 15 mg once daily with food, so the order should be clarified. Age alone does not justify the higher dose."}, {"id": 1165, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A 9 year old boy returns home after tonsillectomy. The hospital prescribed an acetaminophen with codeine liquid before CYP2D6 results were known. The mother asks whether it is safe to give. Considering Health Canada restrictions, the pharmacist should advise that:", "options": {"A": "Codeine is acceptable after age six if given with food, which slows conversion to morphine", "B": "Codeine is acceptable once CYP2D6 testing has been completed and shows a normal metabolizer result", "C": "Codeine is acceptable at a reduced dose of 0.5 mg/kg every six hours for two days", "D": "Codeine is contraindicated in children under 12 years, so the prescriber should choose another analgesic"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Codeine is contraindicated in children under 12 years, so the prescriber should choose another analgesic): Health Canada contraindicates codeine in children under 12 and for pain after tonsillectomy or adenoidectomy in those under 18, because unpredictable conversion to morphine has caused deaths. Genotype testing or dose reduction does not remove this contraindication. Food does not alter CYP2D6 conversion."}, {"id": 1166, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 6, "question": "VJ is a 32 year old woman, four weeks postpartum and exclusively breastfeeding, who asks to start a combined contraceptive pill she used before pregnancy. She has no other medical conditions. Which advice should the pharmacist give her about starting it now?", "options": {"A": "Estrogen can lower milk supply and clot risk is raised, so progestin-only methods are preferred", "B": "Any hormonal method harms the baby through breast milk, so only condoms can be used safely", "C": "Combined pills are safe at four weeks, but she needs a higher dose during lactation", "D": "The combined pill is the preferred method while breastfeeding because it also improves milk supply"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Estrogen can lower milk supply and clot risk is raised, so progestin-only methods are preferred): Estrogen-containing methods can decrease milk production, and the risk of venous thromboembolism is elevated in the first weeks after delivery. Progestin-only methods such as the progestin pill, implant, injection or IUS are preferred during lactation. Hormones in these methods pass into milk in very small amounts and are not considered harmful."}, {"id": 1167, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 3, "question": "A hospital stewardship pharmacist reviews the medication lists of four patients on a medical ward to identify who has the greatest risk of developing Clostridioides difficile infection this week, based on age, setting and antibiotic exposure. Which patient and drug exposure carries the highest risk?", "options": {"A": "Fosfomycin taken as a single dose for cystitis by a healthy 35 year old woman", "B": "Doxycycline taken for acne by a healthy 25 year old community outpatient", "C": "Clindamycin taken for a skin infection by a hospitalized 80 year old patient", "D": "Nitrofurantoin taken for cystitis by a healthy 40 year old community outpatient"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Clindamycin taken for a skin infection by a hospitalized 80 year old patient): Clindamycin, cephalosporins and fluoroquinolones carry the highest risk of C. difficile, and advanced age and hospitalization add to it. Doxycycline, nitrofurantoin and fosfomycin disturb the gut flora much less."}, {"id": 1168, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 3, "question": "CY is a 49 year old man whose H. pylori infection persisted after 14 days of bismuth quadruple therapy. He has no allergies and has never taken a fluoroquinolone. His physician requests a rescue regimen. Which regimen should the pharmacist suggest?", "options": {"A": "A PPI with levofloxacin and amoxicillin for 14 days, ideally guided by susceptibility testing", "B": "A PPI with clarithromycin and metronidazole for seven days, using lower doses to limit side effects", "C": "A PPI alone for eight weeks, since a persistent infection is treated by acid suppression alone", "D": "Repeat the identical bismuth quadruple regimen for 14 days adding a second daily PPI dose"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A PPI with levofloxacin and amoxicillin for 14 days, ideally guided by susceptibility testing): After bismuth quadruple failure, a fluoroquinolone based regimen such as a PPI with levofloxacin and amoxicillin for 14 days is a standard rescue regimen, and susceptibility testing improves the odds. Repeating the same regimen or using a short clarithromycin course risks the same resistance, and acid suppression alone does not clear the bacteria."}, {"id": 1169, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "A statin shortage affects the 20 mg tablets in all local pharmacies. YN is a 59 year old woman who presents a prescription for the 20 mg tablet once daily, and your pharmacy has the 10 mg tablets in stock. In most provinces, which action is within the pharmacist's adaptation authority?", "options": {"A": "Tell her the prescription cannot be filled until the 20 mg tablets are restocked soon", "B": "Substitute a different statin at a dose the pharmacist chooses, leaving the record unchanged", "C": "Dispense one 10 mg tablet daily and tell the patient it equals the 20 mg dose", "D": "Dispense two 10 mg tablets once daily, record the change, and notify the prescriber"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Dispense two 10 mg tablets once daily, record the change, and notify the prescriber): Two 10 mg tablets provide the prescribed 20 mg daily dose, so the pharmacist can adapt the number of tablets and document the change. One 10 mg tablet delivers half the dose. Changing the statin is a larger decision and records must be kept up to date."}, {"id": 1170, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "An older adult picks up a 30 day supply of ramipril 10 mg once daily on March 4. The drug plan allows a refill once 80% of the days supply has passed. On which date is the earliest refill permitted?", "options": {"A": "March 24, because 20 days of the 30 day supply have elapsed by that date", "B": "March 31, because 27 days of the 30 day supply must have elapsed first", "C": "March 28, because 24 days of a 30 day supply have elapsed", "D": "April 3, because the full 30 days of the supply must have passed in advance"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (March 28, because 24 days of a 30 day supply have elapsed): 80% of 30 days is 24 days. Counting 24 days from March 4 gives March 28. The other dates correspond to higher or lower percentages of the supply."}, {"id": 1171, "domain": "leadership_and_stewardship", "chapter": 11, "question": "A patient enrolled in a prescribed safer supply program receives hydromorphone 8 mg tablets from a prescriber to reduce her use of unregulated fentanyl. The pharmacist notices she sells a few tablets outside the store. Which response best reflects program principles?", "options": {"A": "Stop dispensing immediately and report her to police because diversion ends the pharmacist's duty of care", "B": "Switch her to weekly witnessed ingestion on the pharmacist's own authority without involving the prescriber", "C": "Continue dispensing without comment, since program participants are exempt from medication safety monitoring", "D": "Discuss the concern without judgement and inform the prescriber so dispensing arrangements can be adjusted"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Discuss the concern without judgement and inform the prescriber so dispensing arrangements can be adjusted): Safer supply is a prescriber-led program meant to reduce overdose from the toxic drug supply, so concerns such as diversion are handled collaboratively with the prescriber, who can adjust dispensing frequency or witnessing. Abrupt discontinuation and police involvement may push her back to unregulated drugs. Ignoring the problem fails the pharmacist's monitoring duty."}, {"id": 1172, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 1, "question": "WS is a 57 year old woman who will start home blood pressure monitoring to confirm a diagnosis of hypertension. She plans to measure once each morning for three days and bring her single best reading to her appointment. The pharmacist reviews the protocol with her. Which instruction is consistent with Hypertension Canada recommendations?", "options": {"A": "Take two readings each morning and evening for seven days, then discard day one", "B": "Take a single reading whenever she feels unwell, as symptoms identify the most accurate readings", "C": "Take three readings on one morning each week for a month and average the results", "D": "Take one reading each evening for ten days, then report only the highest value"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Take two readings each morning and evening for seven days, then discard day one): Home monitoring for diagnosis uses duplicate morning and evening readings over seven days, with the first day discarded and the rest averaged. A few isolated readings, or only the highest value, do not represent her usual pressure. Readings prompted by symptoms are also biased."}, {"id": 1173, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 6, "question": "CT is a 20 year old who had unprotected intercourse on Monday at 9 p.m. It is now Thursday at 6 p.m., and she requests emergency contraception. The pharmacist considers ulipristal 30 mg and notes its effective window. Until what time can ulipristal still be used for this exposure?", "options": {"A": "Sunday at 9 p.m., which is 144 hours after the unprotected intercourse occurred", "B": "Friday at 9 p.m., which is 96 hours after the unprotected intercourse occurred", "C": "Thursday at 9 p.m., which is 72 hours after the unprotected intercourse occurred", "D": "Saturday at 9 p.m., which is 120 hours after the unprotected intercourse"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Saturday at 9 p.m., which is 120 hours after the unprotected intercourse): Ulipristal is effective up to 120 hours, which is five days, after intercourse. Five days after Monday at 9 p.m. is Saturday at 9 p.m., so CT is well within the window now. The 72 hour limit belongs to levonorgestrel, and 96 or 144 hours are not the limit."}, {"id": 1174, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "WA, a 66 year old retired man seen monthly for diabetes management, offers to lend his pharmacist, FI, two thousand dollars interest-free to cover a family emergency, saying he trusts FI completely. The loan would be repaid within a year. How should FI respond?", "options": {"A": "Ask WA to give the money as a gift, since gifts are exempt from conflict rules", "B": "Decline politely, because lending arrangements with a patient create conflict and blur professional roles", "C": "Accept a smaller loan of $500 since the lower amount is unlikely to influence professional judgment", "D": "Accept the loan and sign a written repayment agreement so the arrangement is transparent and documented"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Decline politely, because lending arrangements with a patient create conflict and blur professional roles): A financial arrangement with a patient creates a conflict and blurs the professional relationship, even when the loan is small or well documented. A written agreement or a smaller sum does not remove the influence the loan could have on care. Politely declining protects both parties."}, {"id": 1175, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 1, "question": "AK is a 72 year old man taking rivaroxaban 20 mg once daily for atrial fibrillation. He usually takes it on waking before a coffee and often skips breakfast. He rarely feels any difference, and asks whether timing matters. What should the pharmacist advise?", "options": {"A": "Take it at the same time daily and avoid dairy products, which can block its absorption", "B": "Take it on an empty stomach for the best effect, and wait before eating breakfast", "C": "Take it with a meal, since fasting reduces absorption of the 20 mg dose", "D": "Take it at bedtime with a glass of grapefruit juice to boost absorption of the dose"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Take it with a meal, since fasting reduces absorption of the 20 mg dose): Absorption of rivaroxaban 15 mg and 20 mg falls markedly when taken fasting, so these doses are taken with food, usually the evening meal. Dairy does not block it, and grapefruit juice inhibits CYP3A4 and can raise levels unpredictably."}, {"id": 1176, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 6, "question": "LF is a 55 year old woman receiving moderately emetogenic chemotherapy. On day three she has breakthrough nausea despite scheduled ondansetron 8 mg twice daily and dexamethasone. She asks what can be added to help. The pharmacist should suggest: Her last blood work showed normal kidney and liver function.", "options": {"A": "Lorazepam 2 mg at bedtime as the sole added agent, since anxiety drives breakthrough symptoms", "B": "A dopamine antagonist of another class, such as prochlorperazine 10 mg as needed", "C": "Increasing ondansetron to 24 mg three times daily, since higher doses add further benefit", "D": "Replacing ondansetron with granisetron, another serotonin antagonist, for the remaining days of chemotherapy"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A dopamine antagonist of another class, such as prochlorperazine 10 mg as needed): Breakthrough nausea is best treated with an agent from a different class than the scheduled drug. Prochlorperazine blocks dopamine receptors and is a standard rescue agent. Higher ondansetron doses add QT risk without more benefit, and switching to another 5-HT3 drug adds nothing."}, {"id": 1177, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A hospital drug committee asks a pharmacist to confirm the Health Canada approved indications, contraindications and recommended dosing for a drug that was marketed in Canada only last month. No textbook chapter yet covers it. The most authoritative source for this information is the:", "options": {"A": "Canadian product monograph, accessible through the Health Canada Drug Product Database", "B": "Most recent review article on the drug class published in a general medicine journal", "C": "American prescribing information leaflet, since the same manufacturer markets the product in both countries", "D": "Provincial drug formulary listing, which summarizes all approved uses and doses for each product"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Canadian product monograph, accessible through the Health Canada Drug Product Database): The Canadian product monograph is the regulator approved document describing authorized uses, warnings and dosing, and it is available through Health Canada's database soon after authorization. American labelling can differ from Canadian approval. Reviews and formularies are secondary summaries that may lag or be incomplete."}, {"id": 1178, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 4, "question": "EV is a 76 year old man with benign prostatic hyperplasia and mild memory problems who has itchy eyes and sneezing from ragweed. He asks to buy diphenhydramine 25 mg tablets because they worked for him years ago. The pharmacist should recommend instead:", "options": {"A": "a pseudoephedrine product combined with diphenhydramine, to offset the sedating effect of the antihistamine", "B": "chlorpheniramine 4 mg tablets, since it is less likely than diphenhydramine to cause confusion", "C": "a second generation antihistamine such as loratadine, which is far less sedating and anticholinergic", "D": "diphenhydramine 12.5 mg at bedtime, since the lower dose avoids urinary retention and confusion"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (a second generation antihistamine such as loratadine, which is far less sedating and anticholinergic): First generation antihistamines have strong anticholinergic effects that worsen urinary retention and cognition in older men, so they are avoided. Loratadine and similar agents cross the blood-brain barrier far less. Chlorpheniramine is also a first generation drug, and pseudoephedrine can worsen urinary retention."}, {"id": 1179, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "HG is an 81 year old man with severe hand osteoarthritis who has had trouble opening his child-resistant vials of furosemide and diltiazem; last week he left them open on the table. He lives alone and has no young children visiting. What is the most appropriate dispensing change?", "options": {"A": "Keep child-resistant vials for safety and ask a staff member to open each vial at pickup", "B": "Dispense with easy-open caps or blister packaging at his request, and record the change", "C": "Advise him to combine both tablets in a single unlabeled jar so only one container opens", "D": "Dispense larger supplies in one vial each so that fewer containers are opened each month"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Dispense with easy-open caps or blister packaging at his request, and record the change): Easy-open closures or blister packaging can be provided at a patient's request and let him use his medicines independently and safely. Opening vials at pickup does not help him at home, and combining different tablets in an unlabeled jar creates a serious identification risk. Larger supplies do not remove the difficulty of opening each container."}, {"id": 1180, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "A 47 year old patient asks to switch from levothyroxine to desiccated thyroid extract that she read about online, calling it more natural. Her TSH is currently in the target range on levothyroxine. What is the best response from the pharmacist?", "options": {"A": "Extract is safe for her only if taken with meals, since food prevents T3 spikes", "B": "Extract can be swapped milligram for milligram, so her TSH will remain unchanged after switch", "C": "Extract is guideline-preferred because it supplies both hormones in the proportions the gland releases", "D": "Levothyroxine remains first-line because extract has variable T3 content and lacks guideline support"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Levothyroxine remains first-line because extract has variable T3 content and lacks guideline support): Desiccated extract contains animal-derived T4 and T3 in fixed ratios that differ from human physiology, and its hormone content can vary, causing swings in T3 levels. Guidelines recommend levothyroxine monotherapy as standard because it is consistent and well studied. Switching a stable patient without a clear reason risks destabilizing her thyroid control."}, {"id": 1181, "domain": "communication_and_collaboration", "chapter": 11, "question": "The pharmacist has gathered MD's own list, the community dispensing record and his daughter's report, and found that pantoprazole, zopiclone and ibuprofen each appear in only one source. Which action should the pharmacist take next to complete the best possible medication history?", "options": {"A": "Remove pantoprazole, zopiclone and ibuprofen from the history because MD did not list them himself", "B": "Add all three products to the admission orders to match the pharmacy and the daughter's report", "C": "Ask MD and his daughter about the pantoprazole, zopiclone and ibuprofen, then record the confirmed use", "D": "Accept the dispensing record as the final source and disregard the daughter's report on ibuprofen use"}, "cluster_id": "CHS4", "cluster_text": "MD, an 81 year old man, is admitted with a heart failure exacerbation. His own list includes furosemide 40 mg daily, ramipril 5 mg daily, metoprolol 50 mg twice daily and apixaban 5 mg twice daily. The community pharmacy record also shows pantoprazole 40 mg daily and zopiclone 3.75 mg at bedtime. His daughter says he takes ibuprofen 400 mg twice daily for knee pain. Admission labs: creatinine 142 umol/L, potassium 5.3 mmol/L, weight 74 kg.", "cluster_size": 3, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Ask MD and his daughter about the pantoprazole, zopiclone and ibuprofen, then record the confirmed use): A discrepancy between sources has to be resolved by asking the patient and caregiver, not by deleting or adding drugs on the strength of a single source. The over the counter ibuprofen is especially likely to be missed in a patient list. Once use is confirmed, the findings are recorded and any decisions about continuing a drug are left to the prescriber."}, {"id": 1182, "domain": "communication_and_collaboration", "chapter": 11, "question": "Considering MD's heart failure exacerbation, his creatinine of 142 umol/L, his potassium of 5.3 mmol/L and his ongoing apixaban therapy, what is the most appropriate pharmacist recommendation regarding the daughter's report that he takes ibuprofen 400 mg twice daily for knee pain?", "options": {"A": "Replace ibuprofen with naproxen, since it has a longer half life and needs fewer daily doses", "B": "Continue ibuprofen but hold furosemide while potassium and creatinine are rechecked within the next day", "C": "Recommend stopping ibuprofen and using acetaminophen, and explain the kidney, heart and bleeding risks", "D": "Continue ibuprofen at the lowest dose with a proton pump inhibitor to protect his stomach lining"}, "cluster_id": "CHS4", "cluster_text": "MD, an 81 year old man, is admitted with a heart failure exacerbation. His own list includes furosemide 40 mg daily, ramipril 5 mg daily, metoprolol 50 mg twice daily and apixaban 5 mg twice daily. The community pharmacy record also shows pantoprazole 40 mg daily and zopiclone 3.75 mg at bedtime. His daughter says he takes ibuprofen 400 mg twice daily for knee pain. Admission labs: creatinine 142 umol/L, potassium 5.3 mmol/L, weight 74 kg.", "cluster_size": 3, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Recommend stopping ibuprofen and using acetaminophen, and explain the kidney, heart and bleeding risks): NSAIDs promote sodium and fluid retention and can worsen heart failure, they raise potassium and reduce kidney function when combined with an ACE inhibitor and a diuretic, and they increase bleeding risk with apixaban. Acetaminophen is the safer analgesic for knee pain. Switching to another NSAID or adding gastroprotection leaves the kidney and heart risks in place."}, {"id": 1183, "domain": "communication_and_collaboration", "chapter": 11, "question": "MD's team plans discharge in five days after his ibuprofen is stopped and his diuretic dose is adjusted. Which follow up should the pharmacist suggest to the community care team, given his admission laboratory results and the medications he will continue taking at home?", "options": {"A": "Repeat creatinine and potassium within a week of discharge and review his daily weights", "B": "Check a repeat INR within two days, since apixaban levels are measured by that test", "C": "Recheck liver enzymes at four weeks, since furosemide is a frequent cause of liver injury", "D": "Arrange an electrocardiogram every month for a year to monitor the effect of metoprolol"}, "cluster_id": "CHS4", "cluster_text": "MD, an 81 year old man, is admitted with a heart failure exacerbation. His own list includes furosemide 40 mg daily, ramipril 5 mg daily, metoprolol 50 mg twice daily and apixaban 5 mg twice daily. The community pharmacy record also shows pantoprazole 40 mg daily and zopiclone 3.75 mg at bedtime. His daughter says he takes ibuprofen 400 mg twice daily for knee pain. Admission labs: creatinine 142 umol/L, potassium 5.3 mmol/L, weight 74 kg.", "cluster_size": 3, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Repeat creatinine and potassium within a week of discharge and review his daily weights): After a nephrotoxic drug is stopped and diuretic therapy changed in a patient on an ACE inhibitor, kidney function and potassium should be rechecked soon after discharge, and daily weights track fluid status in heart failure. Apixaban is not monitored with an INR, and furosemide rarely causes liver injury. Monthly ECGs are not indicated for metoprolol."}, {"id": 1184, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "A pharmacist arrives on a Saturday morning to find the back door forced and the narcotic safe pried open. Several bottles of oxycodone and hydromorphone are gone, and the store is otherwise quiet. Which action is most appropriate right now?", "options": {"A": "Begin a complete recount of the safe before notifying anyone, so the report is accurate", "B": "Write an internal incident report and send it to the provincial college on Monday", "C": "Contact police, secure the premises, and notify Health Canada's Office of Controlled Substances", "D": "Wait for the owner to arrive and decide whether the loss is large enough to report"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Contact police, secure the premises, and notify Health Canada's Office of Controlled Substances): A break-in with stolen narcotics is a theft that calls for a police report, securing the scene, and notification of Health Canada's Office of Controlled Substances. Counting can follow once authorities are involved. Waiting for the owner, or deferring a report to Monday, delays duties that apply on discovery."}, {"id": 1185, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 7, "question": "A mother of a 5 month old girl says her baby spits out the antibiotic suspension whenever she squirts it from an oral syringe. She asks for advice before the next dose is due this evening. Which technique should the pharmacist recommend?", "options": {"A": "Squirt the full dose quickly toward the back of the throat so that she must swallow", "B": "Pinch her nose gently while giving the full dose so that she swallows it", "C": "Place the tip inside her cheek and give the dose slowly in small amounts", "D": "Mix the full dose into a full bottle of formula to disguise the taste for her"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Place the tip inside her cheek and give the dose slowly in small amounts): Placing the syringe tip along the inner cheek and giving small amounts lets the infant swallow safely and reduces spitting. Squirting toward the back of the throat risks choking, and pinching the nose is unsafe. Mixing with a full bottle risks an incomplete dose if she does not finish it."}, {"id": 1186, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "At a pharmacy screening clinic, a pharmacist reads that a screening test has high sensitivity. A colleague says a negative result from such a test is therefore useful for ruling out the disease. Is the colleague correct, and what is the reason?", "options": {"A": "No, because a negative result is only informative when the test has a high specificity", "B": "Yes, because high sensitivity means few healthy patients are called positive, so negatives are definitive", "C": "No, because high sensitivity makes the positive predictive value fall in screening programs", "D": "Yes, because few diseased patients are missed, so a negative result makes disease unlikely"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Yes, because few diseased patients are missed, so a negative result makes disease unlikely): High sensitivity means few diseased patients are missed, so a negative result makes the disease unlikely, a use remembered as SnNout. Specificity governs how reliable a positive result is. Sensitivity says nothing about healthy patients called positive, and a high sensitivity does not by itself lower the positive predictive value."}, {"id": 1187, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A 66 year old man with severe COPD has a steady state theophylline trough of 8 mg/L on 300 mg every 12 hours. His prescriber wants a level near 12 mg/L, within the 10 to 20 mg/L range. Assuming linear kinetics and adherence, the new dose should be:", "options": {"A": "600 mg every 12 hours, because a 50% increase in level requires doubling of the dose", "B": "400 mg every 12 hours, because theophylline doses are raised in fixed 100 mg steps", "C": "375 mg every 12 hours, because theophylline clearance is saturable and only small increases are acceptable", "D": "450 mg every 12 hours, increasing the daily dose in proportion to the desired level"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (450 mg every 12 hours, increasing the daily dose in proportion to the desired level): With linear kinetics, steady state concentration is proportional to dose, so the dose rises by 12 / 8 = 1.5 times, from 300 mg to 450 mg per dose. Doubling would overshoot to about 16 mg/L, and fixed step increases ignore the calculation requested. The question specifies linear kinetics, so saturable clearance does not apply."}, {"id": 1188, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "AR is a 69 year old woman started on morphine SR 30 mg twice daily for chronic pain five days ago. She is now straining with hard stools and bloating and asks the pharmacist what to take. What is the best recommendation?", "options": {"A": "Use a daily dose of mineral oil at bedtime, since lubricants are preferred for chronic use", "B": "Take psyllium fiber with minimal fluid daily, since bulk forming agents work best for opioid constipation", "C": "Start a stimulant laxative such as senna daily, and add polyethylene glycol if stools stay hard", "D": "Stop the morphine and wait seven days, since constipation from opioids resolves by itself"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Start a stimulant laxative such as senna daily, and add polyethylene glycol if stools stay hard): Opioids slow gut motility and tolerance to constipation does not develop, so a stimulant laxative is started with the opioid and an osmotic agent such as polyethylene glycol is added if needed. Bulk formers can worsen constipation without large fluid intake, and mineral oil poses aspiration risk. Stopping the opioid is not an appropriate step."}, {"id": 1189, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "The pharmacist reviews EQ's chart and wants to identify the drug-related cause of her slow pulse of 46 beats per minute, near-fainting, and nausea reported by nursing staff over the past week. Which explanation is best supported by her current medications?", "options": {"A": "Alzheimer disease progression alone slows the sinus node, so no medication is responsible for these findings", "B": "Donepezil's vagal effect adds to metoprolol, further slowing the heart and causing nausea", "C": "Apixaban is slowing her pulse and causing nausea through a direct effect on cardiac conduction", "D": "Acetaminophen 500 mg as needed accumulates with her low weight, slowing the heart and causing nausea"}, "cluster_id": "CHS5", "cluster_text": "EQ is an 82 year old woman living in long-term care with Alzheimer disease. Medications: donepezil 10 mg every morning, metoprolol tartrate 25 mg twice daily for atrial fibrillation rate control, apixaban 2.5 mg twice daily, and acetaminophen 500 mg as needed. Weight 48 kg, serum creatinine 110 umol/L. Over the past week staff report poor appetite, nausea, one near-fainting episode, and a pulse of 46 beats per minute. She had one fall last month. No known allergies.", "cluster_size": 3, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Donepezil's vagal effect adds to metoprolol, further slowing the heart and causing nausea): Donepezil increases vagal tone and can cause bradycardia, syncope, and nausea, and combined with a beta-blocker such as metoprolol the slowing of the sinus and AV nodes is additive. Apixaban and acetaminophen do not slow the heart. Alzheimer disease does not itself cause bradycardia."}, {"id": 1190, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "EQ's pulse remains 46 beats per minute this morning after a week of nausea and poor appetite, and she nearly fainted again while standing for breakfast. The nurse calls the pharmacist for advice. What should the pharmacist recommend to the nurse now?", "options": {"A": "Give the morning metoprolol as scheduled and recheck her pulse at the next routine nursing round", "B": "Add digoxin to stabilize her rhythm because atrial fibrillation rather than medication is causing bradycardia", "C": "Urgent physician review with an ECG, and reassessment of both donepezil and metoprolol", "D": "Hold apixaban now, because bleeding around the heart is the likely cause of slowing"}, "cluster_id": "CHS5", "cluster_text": "EQ is an 82 year old woman living in long-term care with Alzheimer disease. Medications: donepezil 10 mg every morning, metoprolol tartrate 25 mg twice daily for atrial fibrillation rate control, apixaban 2.5 mg twice daily, and acetaminophen 500 mg as needed. Weight 48 kg, serum creatinine 110 umol/L. Over the past week staff report poor appetite, nausea, one near-fainting episode, and a pulse of 46 beats per minute. She had one fall last month. No known allergies.", "cluster_size": 3, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Urgent physician review with an ECG, and reassessment of both donepezil and metoprolol): A heart rate in the 40s with near-fainting in a frail older adult is a safety concern that needs same-day physician assessment and an ECG, plus a review of the additive effects of donepezil and metoprolol. Giving the next beta-blocker dose without review risks syncope and falls. Digoxin would further slow conduction, and apixaban bleeding is not suggested by the findings."}, {"id": 1191, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "The physician continues apixaban 2.5 mg twice daily for atrial fibrillation. The nurse asks the pharmacist to confirm this reduced dose is appropriate for EQ in long-term care given her age, body weight and kidney function. Which statement correctly justifies the dose?", "options": {"A": "The dose is reduced because donepezil inhibits apixaban metabolism, which doubles apixaban exposure in this patient", "B": "Two dose-reduction criteria apply: age at least 80 years and weight 60 kg or less", "C": "Her creatinine of 110 umol/L exceeds the cutoff and her age meets the second criterion", "D": "Only age is met, but the reduced dose is standard for all residents of long-term care"}, "cluster_id": "CHS5", "cluster_text": "EQ is an 82 year old woman living in long-term care with Alzheimer disease. Medications: donepezil 10 mg every morning, metoprolol tartrate 25 mg twice daily for atrial fibrillation rate control, apixaban 2.5 mg twice daily, and acetaminophen 500 mg as needed. Weight 48 kg, serum creatinine 110 umol/L. Over the past week staff report poor appetite, nausea, one near-fainting episode, and a pulse of 46 beats per minute. She had one fall last month. No known allergies.", "cluster_size": 3, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Two dose-reduction criteria apply: age at least 80 years and weight 60 kg or less): For atrial fibrillation, apixaban is reduced to 2.5 mg twice daily when at least two of three criteria are present: age 80 or older, weight 60 kg or less, and serum creatinine 133 umol/L or higher. EQ is 82 years old and weighs 48 kg, so she qualifies, even though her creatinine of 110 umol/L is below the threshold. Donepezil does not affect apixaban."}, {"id": 1192, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 4, "question": "MD is a 29 year old woman with asthma controlled for four months on budesonide 400 mcg twice daily, with no night symptoms and rare reliever use. She asks the pharmacist whether she can reduce her inhaler. What is an appropriate suggestion for the prescriber?", "options": {"A": "Consider reducing the dose by 25 to 50%, to 200 mcg twice daily", "B": "Stop the budesonide this month and keep salbutamol available for any symptoms", "C": "Reduce the dose by 90% to 40 mcg twice daily, then review in one year", "D": "Keep the dose unchanged for at least two years before any step down is considered"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Consider reducing the dose by 25 to 50%, to 200 mcg twice daily): After about three months of sustained control, the inhaled steroid can be reduced by 25 to 50% with close follow up. Going from 400 to 200 mcg twice daily is a 50% reduction. Abrupt cessation or a 90% cut risks loss of control, and delaying any step down for years is not required."}, {"id": 1193, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 4, "question": "A pharmacist in a community pharmacy sees a 26 year old woman who was stung by a wasp in the parking lot. She now has widespread hives, a tight throat, a hoarse voice and lightheadedness. She carries an epinephrine auto-injector in her purse. The first action should be to:", "options": {"A": "give an oral antihistamine and observe her for thirty minutes before deciding on epinephrine", "B": "apply a cold compress to the sting site and have her sit upright to breathe", "C": "help her inject the epinephrine auto-injector into the outer thigh and then call 911", "D": "call 911 and wait for paramedics before using the epinephrine, so they can assess her"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (help her inject the epinephrine auto-injector into the outer thigh and then call 911): Skin signs with throat tightness and lightheadedness meet the definition of anaphylaxis, and epinephrine given early is the treatment that prevents deterioration. Emergency services are called at the same time. Antihistamines and compresses do not reverse airway involvement, and delaying epinephrine until paramedics arrive risks collapse."}, {"id": 1194, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A pharmacist ranks four sources supporting a drug's use in refractory migraine: a systematic review of randomized trials, a prospective cohort study, a case series, and a statement from an expert panel. Which source sits lowest in a conventional hierarchy of evidence for treatment effect, and why?", "options": {"A": "The expert panel statement, because it is not derived from systematic observation of patient outcomes", "B": "The case series, because it enrolls more patients than the cohort and gives precise estimates", "C": "The prospective cohort study, because it follows patients forward in time and suffers from recall bias", "D": "The systematic review, because it combines trials with different designs and populations into a single estimate"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The expert panel statement, because it is not derived from systematic observation of patient outcomes): Conventional hierarchies place systematic reviews of randomized trials at the top and expert opinion at the bottom. Expert statements may be informed by evidence but do not themselves systematically collect outcome data. Prospective cohorts avoid recall bias, and case series, though weak, still report observed patient outcomes."}, {"id": 1195, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "Mid-afternoon on a busy day, the pharmacist sees a pharmacy assistant place several alprazolam tablets from a vial held for destruction into her pocket. She admits she has been struggling with anxiety and offers to pay them back. What obligations apply to the pharmacist?", "options": {"A": "Secure the stock, document it, and report the theft as required rather than accept repayment", "B": "Accept repayment, give a verbal warning, and treat the matter as an internal human resources issue", "C": "Send her home on sick leave and urge her to see her physician about the anxiety", "D": "Take no action, since the tablets were destined for destruction and no patient was harmed"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Secure the stock, document it, and report the theft as required rather than accept repayment): Removing a controlled substance for personal use is theft and diversion even when the drug was due for destruction. The pharmacist must secure the stock, document the incident and report it as required by law and the regulator. Accepting repayment or handling it as an internal matter does not discharge those duties, although a supportive referral can follow."}, {"id": 1196, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 4, "question": "IZ is a 33 year old woman with rheumatoid arthritis taking leflunomide 20 mg daily. She tells the pharmacist she hopes to become pregnant within the year. Her rheumatologist has not discussed this yet. She currently uses condoms for contraception. What should the pharmacist advise?", "options": {"A": "Leflunomide should be stopped at a positive test, and folic acid offsets fetal risk", "B": "Leflunomide is teratogenic with a long half-life, so a cholestyramine washout is needed first", "C": "Leflunomide is acceptable in pregnancy, but the dose should be halved during the first trimester", "D": "Leflunomide can continue until the pregnancy test is positive, since it clears within a few days"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Leflunomide is teratogenic with a long half-life, so a cholestyramine washout is needed first): Leflunomide is teratogenic, and its active metabolite persists for months, so women planning pregnancy need an accelerated washout with cholestyramine and a documented low level before conceiving. Stopping only at a positive test or halving the dose leaves the embryo exposed, and folic acid does not offset the fetal risk."}, {"id": 1197, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 3, "question": "A 3 year old boy has had right ear pain and a fever of 38.0 C for 1 day. The assessing clinician finds unilateral acute otitis media with mild symptoms. He is drinking well and his parents can reach care easily. The pharmacist is asked about management. What is the most appropriate approach?", "options": {"A": "Immediate amoxicillin for 10 days, since all middle ear infections need antibiotic therapy", "B": "Immediate amoxicillin-clavulanate, since it is first-line for uncomplicated middle ear infection in children", "C": "Observation for 48 hours with analgesics, starting antibiotics if no improvement occurs", "D": "Antibiotic ear drops for 7 days, applied into the affected ear canal twice each day"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Observation for 48 hours with analgesics, starting antibiotics if no improvement occurs): Many mild, unilateral cases in children of this age resolve without antibiotics, so observation with pain relief and follow-up is acceptable. Amoxicillin is the first agent if treatment becomes necessary, and amoxicillin-clavulanate is not first-line. Ear drops do not reach the middle ear when the eardrum is intact."}, {"id": 1198, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "EH is a 52 year old woman who started once weekly semaglutide two weeks ago. She calls the pharmacy with severe, persistent abdominal pain that radiates to her back, with vomiting. What should the pharmacist advise? She has no history of pancreatitis or gallstones.", "options": {"A": "take an extra dose of semaglutide to ease the upset, since levels are still building", "B": "wait three days for the pain to settle before seeking care, since most reactions resolve", "C": "stop the medication and seek urgent medical assessment, since pancreatitis is a recognized rare complication", "D": "continue the weekly dose and eat smaller meals, since nausea and pain are expected during titration"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (stop the medication and seek urgent medical assessment, since pancreatitis is a recognized rare complication): Mild nausea is common during GLP-1 titration, but severe persistent abdominal pain radiating to the back suggests pancreatitis, and the drug should be stopped and the patient assessed urgently. Delay or extra dosing could be harmful."}, {"id": 1199, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 3, "question": "SN is a 62 year old woman with nonpurulent cellulitis of the lower leg and no fever. She reports throat swelling and hives after ampicillin 20 years ago. She asks the pharmacist which oral antibiotic is safest and effective for her infection. What should be recommended?", "options": {"A": "Clindamycin 300 mg four times daily, avoiding beta-lactams given her prior anaphylaxis", "B": "Cephalexin 500 mg four times daily, since cross-reactivity with penicillin is rare", "C": "Cloxacillin 500 mg four times daily, because the reaction happened many years ago", "D": "Amoxicillin-clavulanate 875 mg twice daily, since a skin test is not available locally"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Clindamycin 300 mg four times daily, avoiding beta-lactams given her prior anaphylaxis): Clindamycin covers streptococci and methicillin-susceptible staphylococci without involving penicillin allergy pathways. Cephalexin shares an identical side chain with ampicillin, so it carries a real cross-reaction risk. Cloxacillin and amoxicillin-clavulanate are penicillins, and an old reaction does not remove the risk."}, {"id": 1200, "domain": "leadership_and_stewardship", "chapter": 11, "question": "A patient who uses injection drugs returns a bag of used syringes to the pharmacy, and a new staff member is unsure how to accept it safely. The pharmacy runs a needle return program and keeps puncture resistant containers behind the counter. What is the correct handling?", "options": {"A": "Decline unless the syringes are capped, and ask the patient to count them first", "B": "Empty the bag into a sharps bin, sorting the syringes first so that needles face upward", "C": "Accept it, and have the patient recap each needle before staff place it in storage", "D": "Accept it in a puncture resistant container, and do not recap, sort, or count the syringes"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Accept it in a puncture resistant container, and do not recap, sort, or count the syringes): Used syringes should be placed directly into a rigid puncture resistant container by the patient where possible, without recapping, sorting, or counting, because each extra handling step raises the risk of needlestick injury. Requiring recapping or counting discourages returns and increases injury risk. Staff should use tongs or gloves if anything is loose."}, {"id": 1201, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "SV is a 54 year old man with confirmed hypogonadism starting testosterone 1% gel 50 mg daily. He lives with his wife and a three year old granddaughter and asks how to use the gel safely. What should the pharmacist counsel?", "options": {"A": "Apply to clean dry shoulders or upper arms, wash hands after, and keep the site covered", "B": "Allow immediate skin contact with children, since gel transfer to others is not clinically significant", "C": "Apply it to the genital skin for best absorption and let it dry before dressing", "D": "Apply to the lower back and thighs after showering, then rinse the area after five minutes"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Apply to clean dry shoulders or upper arms, wash hands after, and keep the site covered): Testosterone gel can transfer to partners and children by skin contact and cause virilization. The patient should apply to clean dry skin of the shoulders or upper arms, wash hands, cover the site and avoid contact until it dries. Rinsing early would remove unabsorbed drug and reduce delivery."}, {"id": 1202, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 4, "question": "BC is a 66 year old man with type 2 diabetes treated with metformin, who has a chesty cough from a cold and picks a traditional sugar-sweetened cough syrup. He checks his glucose at home each morning. The pharmacist should advise:", "options": {"A": "choosing a product containing pseudoephedrine, since it helps lower glucose during illness", "B": "using the sweetened syrup, since a small volume contains too little sugar to matter", "C": "choosing a sugar-free guaifenesin product and monitoring glucose, since illness can raise glucose levels", "D": "stopping metformin during the cold, since cough syrups interact with it"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (choosing a sugar-free guaifenesin product and monitoring glucose, since illness can raise glucose levels): A sugar-free formulation avoids adding carbohydrate, and illness itself can raise glucose through stress hormones, so closer monitoring makes sense. Sympathomimetic decongestants tend to raise rather than lower glucose, and there is no cough syrup interaction that requires stopping metformin."}, {"id": 1203, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "LD is a 27 year old man started on aripiprazole 15 mg daily one week ago. He cannot sit still, paces the ward, and says he feels an inner restlessness that he cannot control. His examination shows no rigidity, tremor or fever. What should the pharmacist suggest?", "options": {"A": "Increase aripiprazole to 30 mg daily, since restlessness shows the current dose is inadequate for psychosis", "B": "Lower the antipsychotic dose and consider propranolol or a short course of a benzodiazepine", "C": "Stop aripiprazole and begin haloperidol, which is less likely to cause restlessness than aripiprazole", "D": "Add benztropine as first choice, because akathisia responds best to anticholinergics in nearly all patients"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Lower the antipsychotic dose and consider propranolol or a short course of a benzodiazepine): Subjective inner restlessness with pacing soon after starting a partial dopamine agonist is akathisia, a common extrapyramidal effect. First steps are reducing the dose, and a beta blocker such as propranolol or a brief benzodiazepine often helps. Raising the dose worsens it, and haloperidol causes more akathisia than aripiprazole."}, {"id": 1204, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 1, "question": "JC is a 66 year old man with heart failure taking ramipril 10 mg daily and spironolactone 25 mg daily. His physician told him to cut sodium, and he buys a potassium chloride salt substitute at the pharmacy. He asks the pharmacist whether it is a good choice to use at every meal. What is the best answer?", "options": {"A": "Recommend it in small amounts, since heart failure drugs do not affect potassium handling", "B": "Advise against it, as potassium chloride added to his two potassium-raising drugs can cause hyperkalemia", "C": "Recommend it but hold ramipril on days he eats restaurant meals, to limit changes in potassium", "D": "Recommend it, because replacing sodium with potassium lowers blood pressure and improves outcomes in heart failure"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Advise against it, as potassium chloride added to his two potassium-raising drugs can cause hyperkalemia): Salt substitutes are mostly potassium chloride, and with an ACE inhibitor and a mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist the potassium can rise to dangerous levels, especially if kidney function dips. Herbs, lemon and spices are safer ways to flavour food. Adjusting ramipril around meals is not a recognized strategy."}, {"id": 1205, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 7, "question": "TH is a 74 year old woman with chronic kidney disease and a creatinine clearance of 25 mL/min. She asks for milk of magnesia and magnesium containing antacid tablets to treat her occasional constipation and heartburn. What should the pharmacist advise her about these products?", "options": {"A": "Switch to an aluminum hydroxide and magnesium combination, which is safe because aluminum balances the magnesium", "B": "Use magnesium products no more than weekly, since weekly use is unlikely to cause accumulation", "C": "Avoid magnesium products because magnesium accumulates in kidney disease; polyethylene glycol is an alternative", "D": "Magnesium products are acceptable at half the usual dose, since kidneys clear little magnesium"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Avoid magnesium products because magnesium accumulates in kidney disease; polyethylene glycol is an alternative): Magnesium is eliminated by the kidneys, so with a creatinine clearance of 25 mL/min it can build up and cause hypermagnesemia with weakness, low blood pressure and arrhythmias. Halving the dose or using it weekly only slows the problem, and combination antacids still contain magnesium. Polyethylene glycol is a suitable laxative in this setting."}, {"id": 1206, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "CM is the mother of a 6 year old boy with a rare, life-threatening metabolic disorder that has not responded to standard treatment. She asks the community pharmacist whether she can apply to Health Canada herself for access to an unapproved drug her son's specialist mentioned. What should the pharmacist tell her?", "options": {"A": "She can apply directly using the patient request form, provided the specialist signs a supporting statement", "B": "The pharmacist can submit the request for her son once the family signs a consent form", "C": "Only the treating practitioner can submit the request, and the manufacturer must agree to supply", "D": "Any physician may import the drug for him without a request if limited to ninety days"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Only the treating practitioner can submit the request, and the manufacturer must agree to supply): The Special Access Program accepts requests only from practitioners, who must describe the patient's serious condition and why conventional options are inadequate. The manufacturer must also be willing to supply the drug. Patients and pharmacists cannot apply on their own."}, {"id": 1207, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "SD is a 61 year old man with type 2 diabetes taking NPH insulin 24 units before breakfast and 16 units at bedtime. His physician wants to convert him to once daily insulin glargine, and the product monograph advises starting at 80 percent of the total daily NPH dose. What starting glargine dose should the pharmacist verify?", "options": {"A": "32 units once daily, which is 80 percent of his 40 unit total NPH dose", "B": "40 units once daily, because the conversion is made unit for unit with his NPH dose", "C": "24 units once daily, which keeps the larger prebreakfast NPH dose and drops the bedtime dose", "D": "19 units once daily, which is 80 percent of only the 24 unit morning NPH dose"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (32 units once daily, which is 80 percent of his 40 unit total NPH dose): The total daily NPH dose is 24 plus 16, or 40 units. Eighty percent of 40 is 32 units, given once daily. A unit for unit conversion would ignore the reduction advised to limit hypoglycemia, and using only one of the two NPH doses misstates his total insulin."}, {"id": 1208, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 6, "question": "DB is a 52 year old man who has bought a 14 day course of an over the counter omeprazole 20 mg three times in the past eight weeks. His heartburn returns within days of finishing each course. He mentions occasional trouble swallowing dry bread. What should the pharmacist do?", "options": {"A": "Add an over the counter H2 receptor antagonist at bedtime and continue the PPI", "B": "Switch to a different over the counter PPI taken twice daily for four weeks", "C": "Refer him for medical assessment of recurrent symptoms and swallowing difficulty before another course", "D": "Recommend a longer 28 day course of the same product, since 14 days was too short"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Refer him for medical assessment of recurrent symptoms and swallowing difficulty before another course): Over the counter PPIs are meant for a single 14 day course for frequent heartburn, and repeating them within months or having swallowing difficulty needs medical assessment. Dysphagia is an alarm feature that can signal stricture or malignancy. Extending or switching acid suppression delays investigation."}, {"id": 1209, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 7, "question": "GT is a 64 year old man taking esomeprazole 40 mg daily. A recent endoscopy confirmed Barrett esophagus with no dysplasia, and his heartburn is well controlled. At a medication review, the pharmacist is asked whether the acid suppressant can be deprescribed. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Stop now and use calcium carbonate antacids, which protect the esophageal lining equally well", "B": "Change to a bedtime histamine-2 antagonist, since proton pump inhibitors have no role in Barrett esophagus", "C": "Continue the proton pump inhibitor, because Barrett esophagus is a recognized indication for ongoing therapy", "D": "Reduce to 20 mg every second day, then stop once symptoms are controlled for two weeks"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Continue the proton pump inhibitor, because Barrett esophagus is a recognized indication for ongoing therapy): Deprescribing guidance excludes patients with Barrett esophagus, because long term acid suppression is recommended to reduce esophageal injury. Antacids and histamine-2 antagonists are less effective at maintaining healing. Recognizing a valid long term indication is part of safe deprescribing."}, {"id": 1210, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "Among 200 patients with drug-induced liver injury and 200 controls in a case-control study, 30 cases and 10 controls took the suspect antibiotic. A pharmacist preparing a safety bulletin calculates the odds ratio of antibiotic exposure for cases compared with controls.", "options": {"A": "0.15, calculated as the 30 exposed cases divided by all 200 cases in the study", "B": "20, calculated as the 30 exposed cases minus the 10 exposed controls in the table", "C": "3.0, calculated as 30 exposed cases divided by 10 exposed controls from the table", "D": "3.35, calculated as 30 times 190 divided by the product of 170 and 10"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (3.35, calculated as 30 times 190 divided by the product of 170 and 10): In the two by two table, exposed cases are 30, unexposed cases 170, exposed controls 10 and unexposed controls 190. The odds ratio is 30 x 190 divided by 170 x 10, which is 5,700 divided by 1,700, or 3.35. Dividing the exposed counts gives 3.0 and ignores the unexposed groups."}, {"id": 1211, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "KL is a 63 year old woman with type 2 diabetes who has taken liraglutide 1.8 mg daily and metformin for a year. Her A1c is 7.9%. A new prescription for sitagliptin 100 mg daily arrives from a different physician. What should the pharmacist do?", "options": {"A": "Contact the prescriber, because combining a DPP-4 inhibitor with a GLP-1 agonist adds no benefit", "B": "Dispense it, since the two drugs act on different pathways and combine well for lowering A1c", "C": "Dispense it with advice to separate the two drugs by four hours to avoid an interaction", "D": "Dispense it and reduce the liraglutide dose by half to prevent excess insulin release"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Contact the prescriber, because combining a DPP-4 inhibitor with a GLP-1 agonist adds no benefit): Both drug classes act through the incretin pathway, and a GLP-1 receptor agonist already produces much greater receptor stimulation than a DPP-4 inhibitor can add. The combination raises cost and adverse effects without added glucose lowering. The two drugs do not have a timing interaction, and the claim that they combine well is incorrect."}, {"id": 1212, "domain": "communication_and_collaboration", "chapter": 11, "question": "After waiting 45 minutes on Monday evening for a lorazepam refill, a patient raised his voice, demanded the prescription and left without it. The pharmacist is documenting the encounter in his profile, and the record could be reviewed later. Which entry follows good documentation standards?", "options": {"A": "Waited 45 minutes, raised voice, requested the prescription, left at 6:40 pm without it", "B": "Incident occurred; details withheld from the profile to avoid prejudicing future care providers", "C": "Patient was extremely rude and aggressive, clearly under the influence of something and probably seeking drugs", "D": "Difficult patient again, staff felt threatened, recommend the pharmacy refuse him service in future"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Waited 45 minutes, raised voice, requested the prescription, left at 6:40 pm without it): Good documentation is factual, objective and free of speculation or labels. Recording times, observed behaviour and what was said gives later readers an accurate picture. Opinions about motive, sweeping labels and recommendations to refuse service are not facts and could be damaging, while withholding the event leaves an incomplete record."}, {"id": 1213, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "A patient asks the pharmacy for a printed copy of her own dispensing history for the last three years because she is changing providers. The technician says the request must be approved by her physician first. What is the correct position?", "options": {"A": "Only the last year of records may be released, since older history is internal pharmacy data", "B": "The pharmacy owns the records and may release them only with written approval from each prescriber", "C": "Patients may access their own pharmacy records, so the pharmacy should provide the copy", "D": "Records may go to the new provider, but a copy cannot be given to the patient"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Patients may access their own pharmacy records, so the pharmacy should provide the copy): Patients have a right of access to their own personal health information held by the pharmacy, subject to limited exceptions that are not present here. No prescriber's approval is needed, and the right is not limited to one year or to transfers between providers. The pharmacy may verify the patient's identity before releasing the copy."}, {"id": 1214, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 4, "question": "ED is a 45 year old man with schizophrenia taking clozapine 400 mg daily, who has smoked a pack a day for years. He tells the pharmacist he will start a quit attempt tomorrow with varenicline. The pharmacist should notify the prescriber because stopping smoking can:", "options": {"A": "speed clozapine absorption from the gut, which may raise peak levels after each dose", "B": "lower clozapine levels, which may allow psychotic symptoms to return within a few days", "C": "change clozapine protein binding only, which will not require monitoring or a dose review", "D": "raise clozapine blood levels, increasing the risk of sedation, seizures and other toxicity"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (raise clozapine blood levels, increasing the risk of sedation, seizures and other toxicity): Components of tobacco smoke induce CYP1A2, which metabolizes clozapine, so quitting removes the induction and clozapine levels climb over days to weeks. Dose reduction and monitoring by the prescriber are needed to avoid toxicity. The change is a metabolic effect, not an absorption or binding effect."}, {"id": 1215, "domain": "communication_and_collaboration", "chapter": 11, "question": "A woman whose husband has advanced lung cancer and receives subcutaneous morphine at home asks the pharmacist quietly whether the morphine is making his death come sooner. She looks anxious and guilty, and she says the nurses are busy today. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Suggest that she discuss the question with the physician later, because the pharmacist cannot comment", "B": "Acknowledge her worry, explain that carefully titrated morphine treats pain and breathlessness without shortening life", "C": "Reassure her that the dose does not matter and encourage her to avoid asking the nurse", "D": "Explain that morphine will inevitably shorten life but the benefit of comfort justifies the effect"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Acknowledge her worry, explain that carefully titrated morphine treats pain and breathlessness without shortening life): When opioids are titrated to symptoms, evidence does not show that they shorten life, and the main aims are relief of pain and breathlessness. Acknowledging her fear and giving honest information eases guilt. Dismissing the question, overstating a life shortening effect or deferring entirely leaves her distress unaddressed."}, {"id": 1216, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A pharmacist reviewing a paper in the diabetes clinic sees that a paired t-test was used to compare each of 20 patients' fasting glucose before and after a dietary program. Which assumption about the data would justify the use of this test?", "options": {"A": "The glucose values are on a categorical scale with exactly two categories recorded per patient", "B": "The values in the before group and the after group are independent samples from different patients", "C": "The within patient differences in glucose are approximately normally distributed across the sample", "D": "The two sets of values have identical variances and sample sizes greater than one hundred"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The within patient differences in glucose are approximately normally distributed across the sample): A paired t-test analyzes the within patient differences, and it assumes those differences are approximately normally distributed. The two sets of values come from the same patients, so they are related rather than independent. The test is for continuous data and does not require identical variances."}, {"id": 1217, "domain": "leadership_and_stewardship", "chapter": 11, "question": "JG is a 43 year old smoker who starts varenicline today with no other medications. He asks the pharmacist when he should stop smoking and how the dose will change over the next weeks. He has no psychiatric history and normal renal function. Which counselling is correct?", "options": {"A": "Keep 0.5 mg twice daily throughout treatment, and quit on the last day of week one", "B": "Increase to 1 mg twice daily over one week, and quit between days 8 and 35", "C": "Start at 1 mg twice daily on day one, and stop smoking on the same day", "D": "Take 1 mg once daily for twelve weeks, and keep smoking until the supply is used"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Increase to 1 mg twice daily over one week, and quit between days 8 and 35): Varenicline is titrated from 0.5 mg daily for three days to 0.5 mg twice daily for four days, then 1 mg twice daily, with the quit date set between day 8 and day 35. Starting at the full dose increases nausea, and keeping smoking until the end of treatment defeats its purpose. A standard course lasts 12 weeks."}, {"id": 1218, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "Oxycodone 5 mg tablets are prescribed for a 62 year old man by a nurse practitioner at a rural clinic who is registered with the provincial nursing college. The pharmacist has confirmed the practitioner's identity. What determines whether the narcotic prescription may be filled?", "options": {"A": "The clinic must hold a Health Canada exemption number, which the pharmacist records on file", "B": "A nurse practitioner may prescribe narcotics only after the physician co-signs each prescription form", "C": "Federal regulations recognize nurse practitioners, so it is valid when provincial law authorizes the prescribing", "D": "Federal law limits narcotic prescribing to physicians and dentists, so the pharmacist must decline it"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Federal regulations recognize nurse practitioners, so it is valid when provincial law authorizes the prescribing): Federal narcotic regulations now allow nurse practitioners to prescribe narcotics, with the scope set by the provincial regulator. The pharmacist confirms registration and that the order is within provincial authority, as for any narcotic. Physician co-signature and a federal exemption number are not required."}, {"id": 1219, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 3, "question": "BQ is a four week old exclusively breastfed boy brought in by his mother with white plaques on his inner cheeks that do not wipe off easily and poor feeding. His mother has sore, pink, shiny nipples. Which treatment plan should the pharmacist recommend?", "options": {"A": "Give the infant oral fluconazole daily and leave the mother untreated unless symptoms persist", "B": "Treat both together: nystatin for the infant and a topical antifungal for the nipples", "C": "Treat the infant only, since the mother's nipples will clear once the infant's mouth has healed", "D": "Stop breastfeeding for the course of treatment, because the yeast passes between mother and infant"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Treat both together: nystatin for the infant and a topical antifungal for the nipples): Thrush passes back and forth between the breastfed infant's mouth and the mother's nipples, so treating only one partner leads to reinfection. Nystatin suspension is the usual first treatment for the infant's mouth, and a topical antifungal such as miconazole or clotrimazole is applied to the nipples. Breastfeeding can continue, and oral fluconazole is reserved for cases that fail topical care."}, {"id": 1220, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "AW is a 57 year old woman with type 2 diabetes who takes gliclazide MR 60 mg daily and metformin 1000 mg twice daily. Her physician is starting semaglutide for weight and glycemic control. Which proactive step should the pharmacist suggest to the prescriber?", "options": {"A": "Consider lowering the gliclazide dose, since added semaglutide increases the hypoglycemia risk", "B": "Increase the gliclazide dose to match the semaglutide, because both agents are titrated upward together", "C": "Make no changes, because GLP-1 receptor agonists add no hypoglycemia risk when given with sulfonylureas", "D": "Stop metformin before the first injection, since it cannot be combined with GLP-1 agonists"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Consider lowering the gliclazide dose, since added semaglutide increases the hypoglycemia risk): Semaglutide alone seldom causes hypoglycemia, but combined with a sulfonylurea such as gliclazide the risk rises because insulin release is stimulated by two mechanisms. Reducing the sulfonylurea at the start is a common precaution. Metformin is routinely combined with GLP-1 agonists and does not need to be stopped."}, {"id": 1221, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 7, "question": "A patient needs vitamin D3 2000 IU daily. The pharmacy stocks 25 mcg tablets. Given that 1 mcg of vitamin D equals 40 IU, how many tablets should the pharmacist advise per day? He has normal calcium levels and takes no other supplements.", "options": {"A": "Half a tablet daily, since 1 mcg is equivalent to 80 IU of vitamin D", "B": "Two tablets daily, since 2000 IU equals 50 mcg, which is two 25 mcg tablets", "C": "One tablet daily, since 25 mcg is equivalent to 2000 IU of vitamin D", "D": "Four tablets daily, since 1 mcg is equivalent to 20 IU of vitamin D"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Two tablets daily, since 2000 IU equals 50 mcg, which is two 25 mcg tablets): With 40 IU per mcg, 2000 IU equals 50 mcg, so two 25 mcg tablets provide the dose. Using the wrong conversion factor gives one, four or half a tablet. Vitamin D labels may state either unit, so confirm which is shown."}, {"id": 1222, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A hospital team is designing a trial and wants to know which design choice would reduce the number of participants required, while keeping power at 80% and the significance level at 0.05. Which choice would lower the required sample size?", "options": {"A": "Lowering the significance level to 0.01, since a stricter threshold needs less data to reach", "B": "Targeting a larger expected difference between groups, since larger effects need fewer participants", "C": "Increasing the power to 95%, since higher power means fewer participants are needed to find it", "D": "Choosing an outcome with greater variability, since a wide spread makes differences easier to detect"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Targeting a larger expected difference between groups, since larger effects need fewer participants): Sample size grows with variability, with the required power and with a stricter significance level, and it falls as the expected effect gets larger. A very large expected difference is easier to detect, but it should be set on clinical grounds and not simply chosen to shrink the trial."}, {"id": 1223, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 7, "question": "A mother plans to bring her 4 year old son to Costa Rica and asks about insect repellent. The pharmacist recommends a product containing DEET. Which advice follows Health Canada guidance for this age group? He will be outdoors in humid forests for most of each day.", "options": {"A": "Use DEET 30% applied up to three times daily to exposed clothing and skin", "B": "Use repellent infused wristbands as the only protective measure, applied once for the whole day", "C": "Use picaridin 20% on the child's hands so he can spread it on his own face", "D": "Use DEET at 10% or less, applied up to three times daily to exposed skin"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Use DEET at 10% or less, applied up to three times daily to exposed skin): Health Canada advises DEET up to 10% for children aged 2 to 12, applied no more than three times daily, and not to the hands where they can reach the eyes and mouth. Higher concentrations are meant for older children and adults. Wristbands have not been shown to protect adequately."}, {"id": 1224, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "ZH, 83, brings a new prescription for nitrofurantoin 100 mg twice daily for an uncomplicated bladder infection, written by a walk-in clinic physician. She lives alone and has no recent laboratory results on file. During intake, which information should the pharmacist collect before completing the data entry check?", "options": {"A": "Her fasting blood glucose result from the past year, because nitrofurantoin disrupts insulin secretion", "B": "A recent creatinine clearance or eGFR, since reduced kidney function limits its effectiveness and safety", "C": "Her recent blood pressure readings, because nitrofurantoin commonly causes fluid retention and hypertension", "D": "Her latest bone density result, because the drug lowers calcium absorption from the gut"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A recent creatinine clearance or eGFR, since reduced kidney function limits its effectiveness and safety): Nitrofurantoin reaches effective urine concentrations only when kidney function is adequate, and it carries a greater risk of toxicity when function is reduced. Knowing the CrCl or eGFR allows the pharmacist to judge whether it is suitable. The other values do not bear on this drug."}, {"id": 1225, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 1, "question": "BT is a 59 year old man without diabetes who has heart failure with an ejection fraction of 29%. His cardiologist started dapagliflozin 10 mg once daily. He asks why he needs a diabetes medication and what he should know. What should the pharmacist explain?", "options": {"A": "It must be taken twice daily with meals, and genital infections require stopping it right away", "B": "It lowers heart failure admissions without diabetes, and should be held during vomiting or diarrhea", "C": "It works only by lowering blood glucose, so it can be stopped once glucose is normal", "D": "It should cause marked weight loss within weeks, which is its main aim in heart failure"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (It lowers heart failure admissions without diabetes, and should be held during vomiting or diarrhea): SGLT2 inhibitors reduce cardiovascular death and heart failure hospitalization in reduced ejection fraction whether or not diabetes is present. Because they promote fluid loss and can contribute to ketoacidosis, they are paused during dehydrating illness. They are taken once daily, and genital mycotic infection is usually treated rather than a reason to stop."}, {"id": 1226, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A 31 year old man with ulcerative colitis started azathioprine 150 mg daily three weeks ago. His white blood cell count has fallen to 1.9 x 10^9/L. A pharmacist suspects a pharmacogenetic cause. Testing would most likely show deficient activity of:", "options": {"A": "Thiopurine methyltransferase, which normally inactivates thiopurines and limits formation of toxic nucleotides", "B": "Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase, which normally protects red cells from oxidative stress caused by azathioprine metabolites", "C": "UDP glucuronosyltransferase 1A1, which normally conjugates azathioprine so that it can be excreted in bile", "D": "Dihydropyrimidine dehydrogenase, which normally breaks down thiopurines before they are converted into active metabolites"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Thiopurine methyltransferase, which normally inactivates thiopurines and limits formation of toxic nucleotides): TPMT methylates and inactivates mercaptopurine derived from azathioprine, so low activity shunts drug toward cytotoxic thioguanine nucleotides and causes early severe myelosuppression. DPYD is relevant to fluoropyrimidines, not thiopurines. G6PD and UGT1A1 deficiencies cause different drug problems."}, {"id": 1227, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A 44 year old man takes sertraline 100 mg daily. After dental surgery he is prescribed tramadol 50 mg every six hours. Two days later he reports agitation, sweating, tremor and diarrhea. The pharmacist suspects the interaction is mainly pharmacodynamic because both drugs:", "options": {"A": "Displace each other from albumin binding sites, which transiently raises the free fraction of each drug", "B": "Inhibit renal tubular secretion, so that both drugs accumulate in the plasma and cause toxicity", "C": "Increase synaptic serotonin, producing additive effects on the same physiological pathway", "D": "Compete for CYP2C9 metabolism, so plasma concentrations of both drugs rise as the enzyme saturates"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Increase synaptic serotonin, producing additive effects on the same physiological pathway): Both sertraline and tramadol enhance serotonergic transmission, so combining them produces additive serotonin excess, seen as agitation, tremor, sweating and diarrhea. A pharmacodynamic interaction changes the drug effect at the target without necessarily changing concentrations. The other mechanisms describe pharmacokinetic interactions that do not explain this picture."}, {"id": 1228, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 1, "question": "MN is a 49 year old man with a new proximal deep vein thrombosis confirmed in the emergency department today, with normal kidney function and no active cancer. The physician prescribes apixaban. The pharmacist is reviewing the prescription for the correct initial regimen. Which regimen is appropriate?", "options": {"A": "5 mg twice daily from day one, as the higher loading dose applies to pulmonary embolism", "B": "10 mg twice daily for seven days, then 5 mg twice daily to complete treatment", "C": "15 mg twice daily for 21 days, then 5 mg twice daily, as used with rivaroxaban", "D": "2.5 mg twice daily for six months, which is the dose used for extended prevention only"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (10 mg twice daily for seven days, then 5 mg twice daily to complete treatment): Acute venous thromboembolism treatment with apixaban starts at 10 mg twice daily for seven days, then 5 mg twice daily. The 15 mg twice daily for 21 days schedule belongs to rivaroxaban. The 2.5 mg twice daily dose is used only for extended prevention after at least six months of treatment."}, {"id": 1229, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "While picking up lorazepam, ZH, a 36 year old patient, tells pharmacist AT that he has a specific plan to seriously injure a named coworker tomorrow and has obtained a weapon. ZH declines to discuss it further and leaves. Which action by AT is ethically and legally justified?", "options": {"A": "Wait until ZH returns next week and then ask him to explain the plan in detail", "B": "Disclose to police only what is needed to prevent the imminent harm, and document it", "C": "Keep the information confidential because patient trust matters more than any threat made during a visit", "D": "Tell the coworker's employer everything in ZH's medication profile so they can manage the risk"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Disclose to police only what is needed to prevent the imminent harm, and document it): Confidentiality yields when there is a clear, serious, imminent risk of harm to an identifiable person, and disclosure should be limited to what is needed to prevent it. Telling police with minimal detail and documenting the decision is both lawful and ethical. Staying silent, waiting a week, or giving an employer the whole medication profile is either unsafe or an unjustified breach."}, {"id": 1230, "domain": "communication_and_collaboration", "chapter": 11, "question": "SQ is a 55 year old woman who recently immigrated and takes warfarin. During a medication review she mentions that she drinks a herbal tea every day that her family prepares from traditional herbs. How should the pharmacist respond to this information?", "options": {"A": "Tell her that Western medicines work better and herbal products have no real benefit", "B": "Advise her to stop all traditional remedies because they are not proven to be safe", "C": "Explain that herbal teas are natural and do not interact with prescription medications", "D": "Ask what herbs the tea contains and check them for warfarin interactions"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Ask what herbs the tea contains and check them for warfarin interactions): A respectful, curious approach identifies the herbs and allows interaction screening, since some herbal products change the INR. Dismissing or banning traditional remedies damages trust and may hide future use, and calling herbs harmless is wrong."}, {"id": 1231, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A neurology clinic plans a 12 patient study of a new migraine drug in which every patient receives the drug for eight weeks and placebo for eight weeks, in random order. Which design feature is needed to keep the first period from contaminating the second?", "options": {"A": "A washout period between treatments, long enough for the first drug's effect to disappear", "B": "A requirement that every patient starts on the active drug so carryover is equal for everyone", "C": "A larger sample in the second period, so leftover drug effect is diluted by extra participants", "D": "A separate group of patients who stay on one treatment, removing the need to separate periods"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A washout period between treatments, long enough for the first drug's effect to disappear): In a crossover trial, each patient serves as their own control, which is efficient, but a lingering effect from the first treatment can distort the second period. A washout interval sized to the drug's duration of action, together with random order, limits this carryover."}, {"id": 1232, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 7, "question": "A father reports that his 3 year old son swallowed a lithium coin cell battery from a remote control about 20 minutes ago. The boy is playing and comfortable, with no drooling or vomiting. The father asks the pharmacist whether he can wait and watch him. What should the pharmacist advise?", "options": {"A": "Go to emergency care immediately, since waiting for symptoms is not appropriate", "B": "Give fluids and watch for vomiting or drooling over the next day at home", "C": "Schedule a chest X-ray at the next available outpatient appointment this week", "D": "Give a laxative and check his stools over the next two days for the battery"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Go to emergency care immediately, since waiting for symptoms is not appropriate): A swallowed lithium button battery can cause severe esophageal burns within two hours, often before symptoms appear, so urgent X-ray and removal are needed. A child who looks well is not safe to observe at home. Laxatives or delayed imaging allow tissue damage to progress."}, {"id": 1233, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 3, "question": "HW is a 63 year old man prescribed fidaxomicin 200 mg tablets twice daily for 10 days for a C. difficile recurrence. He has no drug allergies. The pharmacist is preparing the prescription and counselling. Which dispensing quantity and instruction is correct?", "options": {"A": "40 tablets, taken one tablet four times daily for 10 days, on an empty stomach", "B": "20 tablets, taken one tablet twice daily for 10 days, with or without food", "C": "28 tablets, taken one tablet twice daily for 14 days, with or without food", "D": "10 tablets, taken one tablet once daily for 10 days, with or without food"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (20 tablets, taken one tablet twice daily for 10 days, with or without food): Twice daily for 10 days is 20 doses, so 20 tablets are needed. Fidaxomicin can be taken with or without food. The other quantities change the frequency or the duration."}, {"id": 1234, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 4, "question": "At follow up, GH reports a sore mouth with small ulcers and persistent fatigue. She says she has taken every medication as directed. Her labs now show white blood cells 2.8 x 10^9/L and platelets 98 x 10^9/L, both well below baseline. Which action should the pharmacist take first?", "options": {"A": "Contact the rheumatologist today, as methotrexate myelosuppression is suspected and the dose may be held", "B": "Recommend she stop naproxen and continue methotrexate, since the NSAID alone explains the low platelet count", "C": "Suggest an oral antifungal for the ulcers and repeat the blood count at her next visit", "D": "Advise increasing her folic acid to daily dosing and repeating the blood count in one month"}, "cluster_id": "CHS6", "cluster_text": "GH is a 57 year old woman with rheumatoid arthritis diagnosed eight months ago. Current medications: methotrexate 20 mg once weekly by subcutaneous injection, folic acid 5 mg once weekly, naproxen 500 mg twice daily as needed and a calcium plus vitamin D supplement. She has no known drug allergies. Baseline labs: AST 24 U/L, ALT 28 U/L, creatinine 70 umol/L, eGFR 85 mL/min/1.73 m2, white blood cells 6.1 x 10^9/L, platelets 240 x 10^9/L. Her disease activity remains moderate, and the rheumatologist is considering adding a biologic.", "cluster_size": 3, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Contact the rheumatologist today, as methotrexate myelosuppression is suspected and the dose may be held): Falling white cells and platelets with mouth ulcers and fatigue point to methotrexate related bone marrow suppression, which needs same day prescriber contact and probably a held dose. Naproxen can raise methotrexate levels but does not explain this alone. Extra folic acid or an antifungal does not treat marrow toxicity."}, {"id": 1235, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 4, "question": "After recovery, the rheumatologist restarts methotrexate at 10 mg once weekly and the pharmacy dispenses 2.5 mg tablets. GH asks how to take them and how many tablets she should take on each dosing day. What should the pharmacist tell her?", "options": {"A": "Take four tablets once weekly on one fixed day shown clearly on the label", "B": "Take two tablets twice a week on separate days, which spreads the same 10 mg total", "C": "Take eight tablets once weekly on a fixed day, as each tablet contains 1.25 mg", "D": "Take one tablet daily for four days each week, so the weekly total remains 10 mg"}, "cluster_id": "CHS6", "cluster_text": "GH is a 57 year old woman with rheumatoid arthritis diagnosed eight months ago. Current medications: methotrexate 20 mg once weekly by subcutaneous injection, folic acid 5 mg once weekly, naproxen 500 mg twice daily as needed and a calcium plus vitamin D supplement. She has no known drug allergies. Baseline labs: AST 24 U/L, ALT 28 U/L, creatinine 70 umol/L, eGFR 85 mL/min/1.73 m2, white blood cells 6.1 x 10^9/L, platelets 240 x 10^9/L. Her disease activity remains moderate, and the rheumatologist is considering adding a biologic.", "cluster_size": 3, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Take four tablets once weekly on one fixed day shown clearly on the label): 10 mg divided by 2.5 mg per tablet equals four tablets, taken together once weekly on a fixed day. Spreading the dose across several days is a known cause of fatal methotrexate errors. Eight tablets would give 20 mg, because each tablet holds 2.5 mg, not 1.25 mg."}, {"id": 1236, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 4, "question": "The rheumatologist adds etanercept 50 mg weekly to GH's methotrexate. Before her first injection, GH asks the pharmacist which vaccines she should have and whether any are unsafe. Her tuberculosis screen was negative, and she has not yet had her influenza or pneumococcal vaccines. What advice is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "All vaccines are safe on etanercept provided injections are separated by at least two weeks", "B": "Influenza vaccine is unsafe, but live vaccines such as MMR are recommended before the biologic starts", "C": "Vaccines should be postponed until after one year of etanercept to avoid blunting the response", "D": "Inactivated vaccines such as influenza and pneumococcal are recommended, but live vaccines are avoided"}, "cluster_id": "CHS6", "cluster_text": "GH is a 57 year old woman with rheumatoid arthritis diagnosed eight months ago. Current medications: methotrexate 20 mg once weekly by subcutaneous injection, folic acid 5 mg once weekly, naproxen 500 mg twice daily as needed and a calcium plus vitamin D supplement. She has no known drug allergies. Baseline labs: AST 24 U/L, ALT 28 U/L, creatinine 70 umol/L, eGFR 85 mL/min/1.73 m2, white blood cells 6.1 x 10^9/L, platelets 240 x 10^9/L. Her disease activity remains moderate, and the rheumatologist is considering adding a biologic.", "cluster_size": 3, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Inactivated vaccines such as influenza and pneumococcal are recommended, but live vaccines are avoided): Patients on biologics and methotrexate should receive inactivated vaccines such as influenza and pneumococcal, ideally before or early in treatment, while live vaccines are avoided because of infection risk. Delaying vaccines for a year leaves her unprotected, and spacing injections does not make a live vaccine safe."}, {"id": 1237, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 7, "question": "CJ is a 14 year old swimmer with ear pain when the outer ear is tugged and a red, narrowed canal. There is no discharge or hearing loss and no history of ear tubes. The pharmacist suspects otitis externa. Which management is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Acidifying ear drops such as acetic acid, keeping the ear dry, and using an analgesic", "B": "Daily ear syringing with warm water to remove debris and trapped moisture", "C": "Oral amoxicillin for ten days since swimmer's ear is a middle ear bacterial infection", "D": "Cotton swab cleaning of the canal after each swim to remove any remaining moisture"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Acidifying ear drops such as acetic acid, keeping the ear dry, and using an analgesic): Mild otitis externa responds to acidifying or antiseptic ear drops, keeping the canal dry and analgesia. Syringing and swabs can injure the inflamed canal skin. Oral antibiotics are not first line because this is an outer ear infection, not otitis media."}, {"id": 1238, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "OL is a 52 year old man with stage 5 CKD on hemodialysis who takes sevelamer carbonate 800 mg three times daily. He often skips it when he eats snacks or misses a meal. How should the pharmacist counsel him on timing his phosphate binder?", "options": {"A": "Take it after dialysis sessions only because the binder replaces phosphate removal by the dialysis machine", "B": "Take all three tablets together at bedtime since binders work best after phosphate has been absorbed", "C": "Take it one hour before meals so the binder reaches the gut before any food arrives", "D": "Take it with each meal or large snack, and skip it when no food is eaten"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Take it with each meal or large snack, and skip it when no food is eaten): Phosphate binders work by binding dietary phosphate in the gut, so they must be present with food. A dose taken without food has nothing to bind. Larger snacks with phosphate content may justify a dose, and timing before or after meals reduces effect."}, {"id": 1239, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "A new pharmacy technician asks how the pharmacy decides whether a drug needs a prescription for federal purposes. The pharmacist points to a Health Canada document. Which document lists the medicinal ingredients that require a prescription for sale under the Food and Drug Regulations?", "options": {"A": "The provincial formulary, which determines which drugs patients may purchase from pharmacies without a prescription", "B": "The Controlled Drugs and Substances Act schedules, which list every drug requiring a practitioner prescription", "C": "The Prescription Drug List, which Health Canada maintains and updates for federal prescription requirements", "D": "The FDA Orange Book, which assigns each marketed drug to prescription or nonprescription status"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The Prescription Drug List, which Health Canada maintains and updates for federal prescription requirements): The Prescription Drug List is the federal list of medicinal ingredients requiring a prescription under the Food and Drug Regulations. A provincial formulary concerns reimbursement, the Orange Book is a United States publication, and the schedules of the Controlled Drugs and Substances Act cover only controlled substances."}, {"id": 1240, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "HJ is a 45 year old man on furosemide with a serum potassium of 3.0 mmol/L. His physician orders potassium chloride oral solution 60 mEq daily in three divided doses. The pharmacy stocks 20 mEq per 15 mL. What volume should each of the three doses contain?", "options": {"A": "20 mL per dose, giving 26.7 mEq each time he takes it", "B": "5 mL per dose, giving 6.7 mEq each time he takes it", "C": "15 mL per dose, giving 20 mEq each time", "D": "45 mL per dose, giving 60 mEq each time he takes it"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (15 mL per dose, giving 20 mEq each time): Sixty milliequivalents divided into three doses is 20 mEq per dose. At 20 mEq per 15 mL, each dose is 15 mL. The 45 mL figure is the whole daily volume, which would deliver the entire day's potassium at once."}, {"id": 1241, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "A pharmacy owner in a mid-sized city wants staff training that responds to the health-related Calls to Action of Canada's Truth and Reconciliation Commission. Which training topic most directly matches what those calls ask of health professionals in their daily practice?", "options": {"A": "Translation of the pharmacy's labels into every Indigenous language spoken in the pharmacy's province", "B": "The history and legacy of residential schools, treaties, and anti-racism skills for working with Indigenous patients", "C": "Marketing strategies for increasing prescription volume among communities that live near the pharmacy location", "D": "Medication therapy for chronic diseases that are listed as the most common among Indigenous patients"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The history and legacy of residential schools, treaties, and anti-racism skills for working with Indigenous patients): The health related Calls to Action ask for training in the history and legacy of residential schools, treaties and Indigenous rights, together with intercultural competency, conflict resolution and anti-racism skills. Marketing and disease lists do not address those aims, and translating labels, while useful in some settings, is not the central training request."}, {"id": 1242, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 1, "question": "NR is a 64 year old woman with heart failure and an ejection fraction of 35%, taking carvedilol, valsartan and furosemide. She asks for a cold product to relieve a blocked nose and sinus pressure. She is looking at a tablet containing pseudoephedrine and ibuprofen. Which recommendation is best?", "options": {"A": "Recommend the combination tablet for three days, since short use carries little cardiac risk", "B": "Suggest saline nasal spray and acetaminophen, avoiding pseudoephedrine and ibuprofen in heart failure", "C": "Recommend phenylephrine tablets instead, because oral phenylephrine has no effect on blood pressure or heart rate", "D": "Recommend oxymetazoline spray for two weeks, since topical products are not absorbed enough to matter"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Suggest saline nasal spray and acetaminophen, avoiding pseudoephedrine and ibuprofen in heart failure): Pseudoephedrine raises blood pressure and heart rate, and NSAIDs cause sodium and water retention, so both are avoided in heart failure. Saline spray and acetaminophen treat the symptoms safely. Oral phenylephrine is also a sympathomimetic, and topical decongestants beyond a few days cause rebound congestion."}, {"id": 1243, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "On her shift, a regulated pharmacy technician notices that the pharmacist often initials final checks within seconds, sometimes before the product has been brought to the verification bench. Two near misses have occurred this month. What is the most appropriate action for the technician?", "options": {"A": "Leave the matter with the pharmacist since accountability for final checks rests only with pharmacists", "B": "Quietly perform a second check on every prescription herself and keep this arrangement private from others", "C": "Raise the concern directly, record the near misses, and escalate to the manager if needed", "D": "Wait for the next scheduled performance review and raise the concern in that formal setting"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Raise the concern directly, record the near misses, and escalate to the manager if needed): Regulated technicians share accountability for safe practice, and near misses are signals that should be raised and recorded so the process can be corrected. Compensating privately hides the weakness, and waiting for a review delays action while errors continue. Accountability for the final check does not remove the technician's duty to speak up."}, {"id": 1244, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "A 34 year old man has started prednisone 40 mg daily for an ulcerative colitis flare. He has been taking it at 8:00 pm and now reports poor sleep and restlessness. He asks the pharmacist whether the timing matters. What is the best advice?", "options": {"A": "Split it into four equal doses across the day, because that lowers total drug exposure", "B": "Take the dose at bedtime with a large meal, because evening dosing prevents adrenal suppression", "C": "Take the dose each morning with food, which fits cortisol rhythm and reduces insomnia", "D": "Take it only before breakfast on an empty stomach, because food blunts its anti-inflammatory effect"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Take the dose each morning with food, which fits cortisol rhythm and reduces insomnia): Natural cortisol peaks in the early morning, and prednisone taken at night can cause insomnia and agitation. A single morning dose with food matches the natural rhythm, lowers the chance of sleep problems, and reduces stomach upset. Splitting or evening dosing offers no advantage for this indication."}, {"id": 1245, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "A technician is about to draw 5 mL from a multiple-dose vial of heparin inside the biological safety cabinet. She wipes the rubber stopper once with a dry gauze square and then inserts the needle. The pharmacist observes the step. What disinfection practice should replace this one?", "options": {"A": "Swab the stopper with sterile 70% isopropyl alcohol and let it air dry before puncture", "B": "Wipe the stopper with a dry sterile gauze square twice, rubbing in one direction only", "C": "Wipe the stopper with 95% ethanol and insert the needle straight away to avoid delays", "D": "Spray the stopper with 70% isopropyl alcohol and puncture it immediately while still wet"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Swab the stopper with sterile 70% isopropyl alcohol and let it air dry before puncture): Vial stoppers must be disinfected with sterile 70% isopropyl alcohol and left to dry so the alcohol has contact time to kill organisms before the needle passes through. Dry gauze only moves contamination around, and puncturing a wet stopper can carry alcohol into the vial. Higher concentrations of ethanol evaporate before they disinfect effectively."}, {"id": 1246, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "A pharmacist is asked how long a 5 mL bottle of timolol 0.5% will last for a patient using one drop in each eye twice daily. Assume 20 drops per mL. How many days should the pharmacist record as the days supply?", "options": {"A": "25 days, since four drops daily from a bottle of 100 drops lasts that long", "B": "13 days, since each daily use takes eight drops from the 100 drop supply", "C": "50 days, since the bottle holds 100 drops and only two drops are used daily", "D": "100 days, since the bottle is dispensed as 100 drops and one drop is used daily"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (25 days, since four drops daily from a bottle of 100 drops lasts that long): A 5 mL bottle at 20 drops per mL holds 100 drops. One drop in each eye twice daily uses 4 drops a day, so 100 / 4 = 25 days. Counting two drops a day gives 50, eight drops gives about 12, and one drop gives 100, none of which matches the directions."}, {"id": 1247, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 1, "question": "TA is an 80 year old man brought to a hospital emergency department after a fall with a head strike. CT shows an intracranial hemorrhage. He takes warfarin for atrial fibrillation and his INR is 4.8. The team asks the pharmacist for the most appropriate reversal. What should the pharmacist recommend?", "options": {"A": "Protamine sulfate given intravenously, which neutralizes warfarin in the same way it neutralizes heparin", "B": "Oral vitamin K 2.5 mg with a repeat INR the next day, given the moderate INR", "C": "Four factor prothrombin complex concentrate together with intravenous vitamin K 5 to 10 mg", "D": "Fresh frozen plasma alone, replacing factors slowly over several hours while warfarin is held"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Four factor prothrombin complex concentrate together with intravenous vitamin K 5 to 10 mg): Life-threatening bleeding on warfarin requires rapid reversal with prothrombin complex concentrate, which replaces vitamin K dependent factors within minutes, plus intravenous vitamin K to sustain the effect. Plasma is slower and needs large volumes, and oral vitamin K is too slow. Protamine reverses heparin, not warfarin."}, {"id": 1248, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "A patient asks the pharmacist for a copy of her own dispensing history from the past two years. She does not want it sent to anyone else. The pharmacy is subject to provincial health privacy law. Which response is correct?", "options": {"A": "Require a letter from her physician before releasing any part of the history to the patient", "B": "Provide access within the legislated time, charging only a reasonable fee where the law allows", "C": "Provide only a verbal summary, since written copies may be released only to another health professional", "D": "Decline, because dispensing records are the property of the pharmacy and cannot be given to patients"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Provide access within the legislated time, charging only a reasonable fee where the law allows): Privacy laws give individuals a right of access to their own records, subject to limited exceptions, and require a response within a set period. A pharmacy may recover reasonable copying costs but cannot refuse access or demand physician approval."}, {"id": 1249, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "A customer collapses at the counter with hives, lip swelling and wheeze minutes after trying a product sample, and is struggling to breathe. No relative is nearby and the customer cannot respond to questions. The pharmacist who is trained to give emergency epinephrine should:", "options": {"A": "Search the customer's wallet for an emergency contact and wait to reach that person for permission", "B": "Wait for paramedics to arrive, because treating without explicit consent exposes the pharmacist to liability", "C": "Give intramuscular epinephrine and call 911, relying on the emergency exception to informed consent", "D": "Give an oral antihistamine and observe, since epinephrine is reserved for patients who can state consent"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Give intramuscular epinephrine and call 911, relying on the emergency exception to informed consent): When a person is incapable and delay would risk serious harm, consent is implied for emergency treatment. Anaphylaxis is treated with epinephrine without delay, and an oral antihistamine is not an adequate substitute."}, {"id": 1250, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "A 39 year old woman in her second month of methimazole therapy for Graves disease has been told that radioactive iodine is planned later. She asks about future pregnancy plans. Which advice should the pharmacist give about radioactive iodine and pregnancy?", "options": {"A": "Pregnancy is safe right after treatment because iodine stays within the thyroid gland alone", "B": "Pregnancy must be avoided only during the dose week, since fetal radiation exposure ends then", "C": "Testing is required only if her period is late, and conception may follow next week", "D": "Pregnancy must be excluded before the dose, and conception delayed for several months afterward"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Pregnancy must be excluded before the dose, and conception delayed for several months afterward): Radioactive iodine crosses the placenta and destroys the fetal thyroid, so a negative pregnancy test is required before treatment. Women are advised to avoid conception for several months afterward, usually about six, while thyroid hormone levels stabilize and radiation clears. Short delays do not provide the needed margin."}, {"id": 1251, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "A pharmacist prepares a 10 mL syringe of fentanyl 10 mcg/mL for an anesthesia team from a vial of fentanyl 50 mcg/mL. The syringe is to be filled to a total of 10 mL with sterile 0.9% sodium chloride in the biological safety cabinet. How much stock fentanyl and diluent should be combined?", "options": {"A": "5 mL of stock fentanyl plus 5 mL of 0.9% sodium chloride, to reach 25 mcg/mL", "B": "0.2 mL of stock fentanyl plus 9.8 mL of 0.9% sodium chloride, to reach 1 mcg/mL", "C": "8 mL of stock fentanyl plus 2 mL of 0.9% sodium chloride, to reach 40 mcg/mL", "D": "2 mL of stock fentanyl plus 8 mL of 0.9% sodium chloride, to reach 10 mcg/mL"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (2 mL of stock fentanyl plus 8 mL of 0.9% sodium chloride, to reach 10 mcg/mL): The final syringe must contain 10 mL at 10 mcg/mL, which is 100 mcg of fentanyl. At 50 mcg/mL stock, 100 mcg requires 2 mL, and the remaining 8 mL is diluent. The other mixtures yield 25, 1 or 40 mcg/mL."}, {"id": 1252, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "CR is a 32 year old man who has just completed induction and starts buprenorphine-naloxone 8/2 mg sublingual tablets once daily at home. He has never used sublingual medication before. He asks the pharmacist exactly how to take each tablet. Which instruction should the pharmacist give?", "options": {"A": "Swallow the tablet whole with water, since it is absorbed through the stomach lining quickly", "B": "Hold the tablet between the cheek and gum only after taking a sip of water", "C": "Let the tablet dissolve under the tongue, without swallowing, chewing, eating or drinking meanwhile", "D": "Chew the tablet thoroughly and then swallow it, which speeds the onset of its effect"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Let the tablet dissolve under the tongue, without swallowing, chewing, eating or drinking meanwhile): Buprenorphine has very low oral bioavailability because of extensive liver metabolism, so it must dissolve under the tongue for absorption through the oral mucosa. Swallowing or chewing the tablet wastes the dose and can leave him with withdrawal. Drinking or eating before it dissolves also reduces absorption."}, {"id": 1253, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 7, "question": "AY is a 28 year old woman who asks for lozenges for a severe sore throat that began yesterday. During the assessment, she speaks in a muffled voice, is drooling, and says swallowing even saliva is extremely painful. What should the pharmacist do?", "options": {"A": "Advise her to rest her voice and drink warm fluids, since viral pharyngitis explains these findings", "B": "Recommend acetaminophen 1000 mg and a throat spray, and advise a throat swab appointment next week", "C": "Direct her to an emergency department now, because these findings may signal airway obstruction", "D": "Suggest benzocaine lozenges and salt water gargles, with physician review if no better in three days"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Direct her to an emergency department now, because these findings may signal airway obstruction): A muffled voice, drooling and inability to swallow saliva suggest epiglottitis or a deep neck infection, which can obstruct the airway rapidly. This is an emergency and not suitable for self-care. Symptom relief products and a delayed swab appointment would put her at serious risk."}, {"id": 1254, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A pharmacist reading a published trial finds that the primary outcome in the paper differs from the primary outcome listed in the trial registry before enrolment began. The paper highlights a significant result for the new outcome. What is the main concern?", "options": {"A": "Selective outcome reporting, since a favourable outcome may be chosen after seeing results", "B": "Type II error, since a switched outcome reduces the power available to detect a real difference", "C": "Recall bias, since trial patients had to remember events that happened before the study began", "D": "Allocation bias, since the enrolled patients were probably chosen after the registry outcome was changed"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Selective outcome reporting, since a favourable outcome may be chosen after seeing results): Prespecified outcomes protect against choosing the most flattering result after seeing the data. A mismatch between registry and publication suggests outcome switching, which inflates the chance of a false positive finding and calls for cautious interpretation."}, {"id": 1255, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 3, "question": "KF is a 72 year old woman weighing 55 kg with serum creatinine 150 umol/L, starting isoniazid, rifampin, pyrazinamide and ethambutol daily for pulmonary tuberculosis. She has no history of liver disease. Using Cockcroft-Gault, which statement about her creatinine clearance and dosing is correct?", "options": {"A": "About 26 mL/min, so ethambutol and pyrazinamide move to three times weekly dosing", "B": "About 26 mL/min, so isoniazid and rifampin need dosing three times weekly while others stay daily", "C": "About 14 mL/min, so rifampin is reduced by half and the other drugs continue unchanged", "D": "About 45 mL/min, so all four drugs are given daily at standard weight based doses"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (About 26 mL/min, so ethambutol and pyrazinamide move to three times weekly dosing): Cockcroft-Gault gives (140 - 72) x 55 x 1.04 / 150 = 25.9 mL/min. When creatinine clearance falls below 30 mL/min, the renally cleared drugs ethambutol and pyrazinamide are given three times weekly. Isoniazid and rifampin are cleared mostly by the liver and need no renal adjustment, so reducing them is wrong."}, {"id": 1256, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "SK is a 44 year old man with gout who asks the pharmacist how lifestyle changes may reduce flares. He drinks four beers on weekends, eats red meat daily and has a BMI of 33 kg/m2. Which counselling advice is most evidence based?", "options": {"A": "Follow a strict purine free diet, because diet changes alone lower urate into the target range", "B": "Fast for several days at a time, since rapid fasting reliably lowers serum urate", "C": "Switch from beer to spirits, since distilled alcohol has little effect on serum urate or flares", "D": "Limit alcohol, especially beer, and work toward gradual weight loss, since both lower serum urate"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Limit alcohol, especially beer, and work toward gradual weight loss, since both lower serum urate): Alcohol, especially beer, and obesity both raise urate, and gradual weight loss lowers it. Diet alone rarely achieves target, and rapid fasting can raise urate and precipitate flares. All alcohol types raise flare risk."}, {"id": 1257, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 4, "question": "RN is a 71 year old woman with COPD on LAMA/LABA/ICS triple therapy. She has had two episodes of pneumonia in the past year and no exacerbations. Her blood eosinophil count is 50 cells/uL. The pharmacist reviews her regimen. What should be recommended?", "options": {"A": "Increase the inhaled corticosteroid dose, because a higher dose is protective against pneumonia", "B": "Continue triple therapy and add an oral corticosteroid taper each month to prevent pneumonia", "C": "Discuss withdrawing the inhaled corticosteroid with the prescriber, given pneumonia and low eosinophils", "D": "Replace the LAMA/LABA component with salbutamol while keeping the inhaled corticosteroid unchanged"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Discuss withdrawing the inhaled corticosteroid with the prescriber, given pneumonia and low eosinophils): Inhaled corticosteroids raise the risk of pneumonia in COPD, and patients with low eosinophils and no exacerbations gain little from them. Stepping down to LAMA/LABA keeps bronchodilation while reducing risk. Higher steroid doses or monthly oral steroids would worsen the problem."}, {"id": 1258, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "Two studies of the same antihypertensive each report a 25% relative risk reduction in stroke. Study X enrolled adults with a baseline five-year stroke risk of 2%; study Y enrolled adults with a baseline risk of 12%. A pharmacist must decide which population to prioritize for a formulary program. Which choice is best supported?", "options": {"A": "The 2% baseline group, because tolerating a drug is easier when the disease risk is low", "B": "Neither group, because identical relative risk reductions mean the benefit is equal in both studies", "C": "Study X adults, since a 25% reduction from 2% yields the lowest residual stroke risk", "D": "Study Y adults, who gain 3 percentage points against 0.5 for study X"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Study Y adults, who gain 3 percentage points against 0.5 for study X): With the same 25% relative reduction, the absolute reduction is 0.5 percentage points when baseline risk is 2% and 3 percentage points when it is 12%. Patients at higher baseline risk gain more per person treated, so they are the sensible priority. Equal relative reductions do not imply equal absolute benefit."}, {"id": 1259, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "A pharmacist reviews a faxed prescription written as Zyprexa 5 mg whose handwriting could also be read as Zyrtec 5 mg, a different drug. The patient is waiting, the clinic is closed, and the patient cannot say what the drug is for. What should the pharmacist do?", "options": {"A": "Withhold dispensing until the prescriber or an on-call colleague clarifies which drug was intended", "B": "Ask the patient which medication they were expecting and dispense whichever one the patient names", "C": "Dispense olanzapine because Zyprexa is the more common order and the handwriting looks closer to it", "D": "Dispense cetirizine since it is the lower risk drug and the patient can return if needed"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Withhold dispensing until the prescriber or an on-call colleague clarifies which drug was intended): An ambiguous order for look-alike names must be clarified with the prescriber, or a covering colleague, before dispensing because the two drugs treat very different conditions. Choosing the lower risk drug, the commoner drug, or the one the patient names substitutes a guess for confirmation. Documenting the clarification also supports later quality review."}, {"id": 1260, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "AB is a 67 year old man with an acute gout flare. He is taking clarithromycin 500 mg twice daily for a chest infection and has an eGFR of 70 mL/min/1.73 m2. His physician orders colchicine 1.2 mg followed by 0.6 mg one hour later. What should the pharmacist recommend?", "options": {"A": "Double the colchicine dose to offset reduced absorption caused by the antibiotic in the gut", "B": "Separate the two drugs by four hours, which removes the interaction by avoiding overlapping peak levels", "C": "Choose another flare treatment, since clarithromycin markedly raises colchicine levels and can cause fatal toxicity", "D": "Give colchicine as ordered since the interaction matters only when eGFR is below 30 mL/min/1.73 m2"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Choose another flare treatment, since clarithromycin markedly raises colchicine levels and can cause fatal toxicity): Clarithromycin inhibits CYP3A4 and P-glycoprotein, sharply raising colchicine exposure and risking myelosuppression, neuromuscular toxicity and death. Separating doses does not help because inhibition persists. A glucocorticoid or another suitable agent should be chosen."}, {"id": 1261, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A pharmacist planning a renal dosing quality project wants to examine whether age (years) is associated with serum creatinine (umol/L) in 60 older adults, with one value of each from a single clinic visit. Both are continuous and roughly normally distributed. Which analysis is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Pearson correlation, which measures the strength of a linear association between two continuous variables", "B": "A one-way ANOVA, which compares the mean outcome across three or more independent patient groups", "C": "An unpaired t-test, which compares the mean values of two separate groups of older patients", "D": "A chi-square test, which compares observed and expected counts between two categorical variables in the sample"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Pearson correlation, which measures the strength of a linear association between two continuous variables): Both age and creatinine are continuous and approximately normal, and the question concerns the strength of a linear association, so Pearson correlation fits. A chi-square test needs categorical variables, a t-test compares group means, and ANOVA compares means across three or more groups."}, {"id": 1262, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "Two prescriptions for a father and son who share the name Wei Chan are filled on the same day, and the finished bags sit side by side at the counter. Which step best prevents the wrong bag from being handed over?", "options": {"A": "Ask each customer to say whether the medication names on the bag look familiar to them", "B": "Hand over the bag nearest the customer after checking the name printed on the label", "C": "Staple the receipts together so the bags are handled as one order at the counter", "D": "Confirm a second identifier such as date of birth at pickup and store the bags apart"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Confirm a second identifier such as date of birth at pickup and store the bags apart): Identical names are a classic cause of wrong-patient errors, so two identifiers are checked at pickup and the bags are physically separated. Asking whether the drug names look familiar shifts the check to the patient, and combining the orders makes a mix-up more likely."}, {"id": 1263, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "A pharmacist wants to create a new master formulation for a 10% urea cream that the pharmacy will prepare repeatedly for patients with dry skin. The pharmacy has no existing record for it. What should be the basis of this formulation under NAPRA non-sterile compounding standards?", "options": {"A": "A published formulation with stability data from a reliable reference, reviewed and approved by the pharmacist", "B": "The method on a supplier's powder label, with the suggested shelf life used as the date", "C": "The pharmacist's own recipe, adjusted by experience, with a conservative expiry date set at one month", "D": "A formulation from an online pharmacist forum that several colleagues report using without problems for years"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A published formulation with stability data from a reliable reference, reviewed and approved by the pharmacist): A master formulation must rest on credible evidence, ideally a published or validated formula with stability data, so that ingredients, procedure and beyond-use date are supported. The pharmacist reviews and approves it before use. Forum posts, personal experience and supplier labels do not provide validated stability or quality."}, {"id": 1264, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 6, "question": "NG is a 36 year old woman with current estrogen receptor positive breast cancer, treated with tamoxifen. She asks about a levonorgestrel intrauterine system to help control irregular bleeding and prevent pregnancy. Which advice should the pharmacist give her? She has no plans to become pregnant.", "options": {"A": "Recommend the etonogestrel implant since progestins alone do not stimulate breast tissue growth in hormone-sensitive disease", "B": "Advise that hormones are not recommended in breast cancer, so a copper IUD is preferred", "C": "Recommend the levonorgestrel IUS because its local action keeps systemic hormone exposure negligible in her case", "D": "Recommend depot medroxyprogesterone since its high dose suppresses ovarian estrogen production without harm to tissue"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Advise that hormones are not recommended in breast cancer, so a copper IUD is preferred): Current breast cancer is a contraindication to all hormonal contraceptives, including progestin-only methods, because hormone-sensitive tumours may be stimulated. The copper IUD is nonhormonal and effective. Tamoxifen is not a contraceptive, and pregnancy during treatment should be avoided."}, {"id": 1265, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "During a routine narcotic count, a pharmacist finds that the physical stock of hydromorphone 2 mg tablets is 24 tablets short of the perpetual inventory record. Recent entries have been rechecked and no error is found. What is the next required step?", "options": {"A": "Adjust the inventory record to match the physical count and note the correction in the register", "B": "Notify the provincial college only, since missing tablets are a professional rather than federal matter", "C": "Order replacement stock and wait for the next monthly audit to decide whether to report it", "D": "Report the unexplained loss to Health Canada's Office of Controlled Substances, and document the investigation"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Report the unexplained loss to Health Canada's Office of Controlled Substances, and document the investigation): Under the Narcotic Control Regulations a pharmacist must report a loss or theft of narcotics to Health Canada's Office of Controlled Substances once an unexplained shortage is confirmed, and the investigation should be documented. Changing the record to hide the gap or waiting for an audit breaches the duty to report. The provincial college is not the only body that must be told."}, {"id": 1266, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "A patient picks up a first prescription for ramipril and says he has no questions and is in a rush. The pharmacy assistant starts to ring it through and asks whether the pharmacist needs to do anything else. What is the pharmacist's obligation at release?", "options": {"A": "No offer is needed, since the patient has said he has no questions about the drug", "B": "Give a leaflet and have the assistant record that counselling was provided in full", "C": "Counsel only if the drug is on the pharmacy's high-alert list, such as warfarin or insulin", "D": "Offer counselling on the new drug, and document that the patient declined if he refuses"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Offer counselling on the new drug, and document that the patient declined if he refuses): For a new prescription the pharmacist must offer counselling and note whether it was accepted. A patient being in a rush does not remove the offer, and the offer applies to new drugs generally, not only high-alert ones. A leaflet handed over by an assistant is not pharmacist counselling."}, {"id": 1267, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "A manufacturer's letter states that a recall of an over-the-counter ibuprofen suspension extends to the consumer level, and the pharmacy sold several bottles of the affected lot through its front store. Which additional step does the consumer-level scope require beyond removing shelf stock?", "options": {"A": "Take reasonable steps to alert purchasers, such as an in-store notice and calls", "B": "Nothing further, because over-the-counter sales are anonymous and the manufacturer alone is responsible for notifying consumers", "C": "Contact only prescribers of patients on the drug, since consumers will see the posting", "D": "Report each sold bottle to the provincial college and wait for written instructions before contacting anyone"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Take reasonable steps to alert purchasers, such as an in-store notice and calls): A consumer-level recall means purchasers must be reached, so the pharmacy should help spread the word with a visible notice and, where records exist, direct contact. The manufacturer does not carry this alone. Prescribers and the college are not the people at risk from a bottle bought off the shelf."}, {"id": 1268, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 1, "question": "ML is a 74 year old woman taking ramipril 10 mg and hydrochlorothiazide 25 mg daily for hypertension. She asks to buy ibuprofen 400 mg three times daily for knee osteoarthritis pain. What is the most appropriate recommendation? Her creatinine is normal and she has no history of ulcers.", "options": {"A": "permit ibuprofen and take it two hours before ramipril, since spacing removes the kidney risk", "B": "permit ibuprofen with meals, since food prevents any effect on kidney blood flow or function", "C": "suggest acetaminophen or a topical NSAID, since oral NSAIDs with her drugs risk kidney injury", "D": "permit ibuprofen and stop hydrochlorothiazide on days she uses it, since only the diuretic interacts"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (suggest acetaminophen or a topical NSAID, since oral NSAIDs with her drugs risk kidney injury): An NSAID combined with an ACE inhibitor and a diuretic reduces renal perfusion and can cause acute kidney injury, the so-called triple whammy. Acetaminophen or a topical NSAID avoids most of that risk. Food and spacing do not prevent the hemodynamic effect."}, {"id": 1269, "domain": "communication_and_collaboration", "chapter": 11, "question": "While counselling a patient who has just picked up a new prescription, a pharmacist refers to JD as 'she', and JD, a non-binary person, quietly says they use 'they' pronouns. A family member is waiting nearby. What is the best way for the pharmacist to respond?", "options": {"A": "Apologize briefly, use the correct pronouns from then on, and continue without drawing attention to it", "B": "Ask JD why they use those pronouns before continuing, so the pharmacist can understand properly", "C": "Avoid using any pronouns again for the rest of the visit to prevent a second mistake", "D": "Explain at length why the error occurred and ask JD to remind staff in future visits"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Apologize briefly, use the correct pronouns from then on, and continue without drawing attention to it): A short apology followed by correct use of pronouns shows respect without making the patient manage the pharmacist's discomfort. Long explanations put the burden on the patient, asking them to justify their identity is intrusive, and avoiding pronouns altogether signals discomfort and can feel evasive."}, {"id": 1270, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "YB is a 52 year old man who drinks four to five beers most nights and strained his back. He asks to take acetaminophen 1,000 mg four times daily for a week. His liver enzymes were normal last year. What is the best advice?", "options": {"A": "Advise avoiding food with each dose, since eating raises acetaminophen levels in the blood", "B": "Allow 6 g daily for short courses, since the liver recovers quickly after each day", "C": "Allow 4 g daily, since normal liver tests last year show he tolerates the full dose", "D": "Advise a lower daily maximum of about 2 g with regular alcohol use, and consider alternatives"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Advise a lower daily maximum of about 2 g with regular alcohol use, and consider alternatives): Regular heavy alcohol use induces CYP2E1 and depletes glutathione, which increases formation of the toxic acetaminophen metabolite, so a lower ceiling of about 2 g daily is advised. A normal enzyme result from the previous year does not predict current tolerance. Daily doses above 4 g are never appropriate, and food does not raise acetaminophen exposure."}, {"id": 1271, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "A prescriber telephones to cancel a prescription for tramadol that was entered for TW, a 52 year old woman, but has not yet been picked up. The pharmacist asks how the pharmacy should handle the cancellation in its records. What is the best approach?", "options": {"A": "Leave the prescription active and place the bag back on the shelf for later use", "B": "Wait for the prescriber to fax written cancellation before deactivating the record in the profile", "C": "Delete the prescription from the system so it no longer appears in the patient's history", "D": "Record the cancellation with date, prescriber and reason, and inactivate the prescription so no fills occur"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Record the cancellation with date, prescriber and reason, and inactivate the prescription so no fills occur): A cancelled prescription remains part of the dispensing record, so it is documented and made inactive rather than deleted. Leaving it active risks an unwanted fill. A written cancellation is not required before the pharmacist acts on a prescriber's direct instruction."}, {"id": 1272, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "A 16 year old is starting fluoxetine for moderate depression after a school counsellor referral. His mother asks the pharmacist what to watch for during the first weeks of treatment, and what to do if she is concerned. What is the most important counselling point?", "options": {"A": "Expect mood to improve within three days, and call the doctor only if it does not", "B": "Watch for new suicidal thoughts or worse agitation, especially in early weeks and after dose changes", "C": "Stop the medication on days he feels well, and restart it only when his mood drops", "D": "Skip all follow-up for the first three months so the medication can work without interruption"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Watch for new suicidal thoughts or worse agitation, especially in early weeks and after dose changes): Health Canada advises close monitoring of children and young adults starting antidepressants because of an increased risk of suicidal thinking and behaviour early in treatment. Benefit takes weeks, not days. Intermittent use is ineffective, and delaying follow-up removes the safety net."}, {"id": 1273, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "During a routine shelf check, a pharmacist finds hydromorphone 4 mg tablets mixed into the bin of 2 mg tablets. Two prescriptions for hydromorphone 2 mg were filled from that bin earlier today by the technician. What is the most appropriate action?", "options": {"A": "Quarantine the stock, contact patients who received those fills, verify the contents, and document the event", "B": "Wait for the patients to report any unusual drowsiness before taking steps to retrieve the supply", "C": "Replace the bin label and ask the technician to recount the tablets at the next audit", "D": "Remove the 4 mg tablets and continue dispensing, since only two fills could have been affected"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Quarantine the stock, contact patients who received those fills, verify the contents, and document the event): Opioid strength errors can cause respiratory depression, so patients who may hold the wrong tablets must be reached promptly and their supply verified or replaced. Quarantining the bin prevents further errors, and documenting the event supports an investigation of how mixing occurred."}, {"id": 1274, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A 34 year old woman takes phenytoin 300 mg daily for epilepsy and has had two breakthrough seizures. Her total level is 8 umol/L (reference 40 to 80). She reports taking every dose, was started on rifampin three weeks ago for tuberculosis, and has a normal albumin. The pharmacist should first:", "options": {"A": "Recognize rifampin induction of phenytoin metabolism and recommend a cautious dose increase with close monitoring", "B": "Recommend doubling the phenytoin dose to 600 mg daily, since linear kinetics predict a proportional rise", "C": "Correct the level for albumin using the Sheiner-Tozer equation and then confirm that it is therapeutic", "D": "Attribute the low level to poor adherence and counsel her before changing the dose"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Recognize rifampin induction of phenytoin metabolism and recommend a cautious dose increase with close monitoring): Rifampin is a strong enzyme inducer that lowers phenytoin concentrations, which fits the timing and the reported adherence. Phenytoin shows saturable kinetics, so doses must be raised in small steps with repeat levels rather than doubled. Albumin is normal, so correction would not change the picture, and blaming adherence ignores the new interacting drug."}, {"id": 1275, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "XB is a 69 year old man whose creatinine clearance is 35 mL/min. A renally cleared antibiotic normally given as 750 mg every 12 hours has a monograph instruction to halve the dose at a clearance between 30 and 50 mL/min while keeping the interval. What new daily total should the pharmacist enter?", "options": {"A": "375 mg per day, given as 375 mg every 24 hours as adjusted", "B": "1500 mg per day, given as 750 mg every 12 hours as before", "C": "1125 mg per day, given as 562 mg every 12 hours as adjusted", "D": "750 mg per day, given as 375 mg every 12 hours"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (750 mg per day, given as 375 mg every 12 hours): The usual daily total is 1500 mg, and halving each dose gives 375 mg every 12 hours, or 750 mg per day. Extending the interval as well would halve the total twice and risk underdosing the infection."}, {"id": 1276, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "CP is a 54 year old man receiving cisplatin chemotherapy, with nausea after each cycle. He now has tremor and cramps but no diarrhea, with potassium 3.0 mmol/L with magnesium 0.5 mmol/L (normal 0.7 to 1.0). Which statement best explains his electrolyte disturbance?", "options": {"A": "Cisplatin raises aldosterone, which drives potassium into cells and lowers magnesium absorption in the small intestine", "B": "Cisplatin causes renal tubular magnesium wasting, and the low magnesium makes potassium loss resistant to replacement", "C": "Antiemetics given with cisplatin bind magnesium in the gut, which explains the low serum levels", "D": "Hydration fluids given with cisplatin dilute serum electrolytes, so values normalize once infusions stop"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Cisplatin causes renal tubular magnesium wasting, and the low magnesium makes potassium loss resistant to replacement): Cisplatin damages the proximal and distal tubule, causing urinary magnesium wasting. Magnesium depletion then increases renal potassium loss, so potassium will not correct until magnesium is replaced. Dilution from fluids does not explain a lasting fall."}, {"id": 1277, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 4, "question": "SK is a 45 year old man using fluticasone 250 mcg two puffs twice daily by metered dose inhaler without a spacer. He is otherwise well. He reports hoarseness and white patches on his tongue, and he rinses with water only occasionally. What should the pharmacist recommend?", "options": {"A": "Stop the inhaled corticosteroid immediately and switch to regular salbutamol, since thrush signals an allergy", "B": "Swallow water after each dose instead of spitting, which treats the candidiasis from inside", "C": "Use a spacer, rinse and spit after each dose, and arrange treatment for oral thrush", "D": "Shake the inhaler harder and inhale more slowly, which removes drug that stays in the mouth"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Use a spacer, rinse and spit after each dose, and arrange treatment for oral thrush): Hoarseness and white tongue patches are typical local effects of an inhaled corticosteroid, with oral candidiasis needing antifungal treatment. A spacer lowers oropharyngeal deposition, and rinsing then spitting washes residual drug away. Thrush is not an allergy, and swallowing the rinse does not clear the mouth."}, {"id": 1278, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 3, "question": "GN is a 50 year old man in week four of daily isoniazid for latent tuberculosis. He reports two days of nausea, dark urine and marked fatigue. His ALT is 190 U/L with an upper limit of normal of 40. The clinic asks what the pharmacist recommends.", "options": {"A": "Continue isoniazid and recheck ALT in two weeks, since the rise is below five times normal", "B": "Reduce isoniazid to half dose with pyridoxine, which usually settles mild hepatitis within several days", "C": "Continue isoniazid and add ursodiol for liver protection, then recheck ALT after the next dose", "D": "Stop isoniazid now and arrange urgent medical assessment for probable drug induced liver injury"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Stop isoniazid now and arrange urgent medical assessment for probable drug induced liver injury): Isoniazid is stopped when ALT exceeds three times the upper limit with symptoms of hepatitis, or five times the limit without symptoms. His nausea, dark urine and fatigue plus ALT near five times normal meet the symptomatic criterion. Halving the dose or adding ursodiol does not protect the liver, and delay risks acute liver failure."}, {"id": 1279, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "An 18 kg child, ZK, has a fever and mild pain, and his mother asks the pharmacist for the correct ibuprofen dose. The label dose is 10 mg/kg per dose, and the suspension is 100 mg per 5 mL. What should the pharmacist tell her?", "options": {"A": "Give 9 mL (180 mg) per dose, repeated no more often than every six hours", "B": "Give 4.5 mL (90 mg) per dose, repeated every hour until the fever settles", "C": "Give 5 mL (100 mg) per dose, repeated every eight hours for children over four", "D": "Give 18 mL (360 mg) per dose, repeated no more often than every six hours"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Give 9 mL (180 mg) per dose, repeated no more often than every six hours): The dose is 18 kg x 10 mg/kg = 180 mg. At 100 mg per 5 mL, 180 mg is 180 / 100 x 5 = 9 mL. Ibuprofen is repeated at intervals of at least six hours, and hourly dosing would risk toxicity. Using 18 mL would double the dose, and 4.5 mL would underdose."}, {"id": 1280, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 4, "question": "A mother asks about giving fluticasone propionate by metered dose inhaler to her 3 year old daughter, who has recurrent wheeze. She plans to use a spacer with a face mask. Which instruction is most appropriate for the pharmacist to give?", "options": {"A": "Let her hold the mask loosely near the face so she is not frightened by it", "B": "Seal the mask snugly on her face and let her take several normal breaths per puff", "C": "Give two puffs into the spacer at once, then remove the mask when she coughs", "D": "Have her inhale one big breath and hold it for ten seconds after each puff"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Seal the mask snugly on her face and let her take several normal breaths per puff): Young children cannot hold their breath, so they take several normal breaths through a spacer with a well sealed mask. Loose masks waste drug in the air, and puffs should be given one at a time. A single deep breath hold is not a realistic technique at age three."}, {"id": 1281, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A hospital drug committee asks the pharmacist to recommend which drug properties make routine serum concentration monitoring worthwhile. A good candidate for routine level monitoring is a drug with a narrow therapeutic index, a clear relationship between concentration and effect, and:", "options": {"A": "A short half life of under one hour so that levels fall rapidly after each dose", "B": "Large pharmacokinetic variability between patients and no simple clinical marker of drug effect", "C": "Predictable absorption and clearance, so that dose adjustments can be calculated from age and weight alone", "D": "A pharmacologic effect such as blood pressure that is easily measured at the bedside"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Large pharmacokinetic variability between patients and no simple clinical marker of drug effect): Monitoring adds value when patients differ widely in how they handle a drug and the effect cannot be read directly, as with aminoglycosides or lithium. If clearance is predictable or the effect is easy to measure, such as blood pressure, levels add little. Half life alone does not determine whether monitoring helps."}, {"id": 1282, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "HV is a 68 year old woman with Parkinson disease who is starting a rotigotine 2 mg/24 h transdermal patch. Her husband will apply it each morning after her shower. He asks the pharmacist how to use the patch correctly. Which instruction is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Apply one patch daily to clean dry skin, rotate sites, and remove the old patch first", "B": "Cut the patch in half on the first week to lower the dose and avoid nausea", "C": "Apply a new patch every second day at the same site to reduce skin reactions", "D": "Apply a heating pad over the patch to speed absorption when stiffness is severe"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Apply one patch daily to clean dry skin, rotate sites, and remove the old patch first): Rotigotine is replaced every 24 hours, with the previous patch removed and a new one placed at a different clean, dry site to limit skin irritation. External heat increases absorption and can cause overdose effects. Patches are not to be cut, and the dosing interval must be daily."}, {"id": 1283, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 7, "question": "BP is a 22 year old man with painful, red shoulders from a beach day yesterday. There are no blisters, he has no fever or dizziness, and he takes no regular medications. He asks the pharmacist what will ease the discomfort. Which recommendation is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Benzocaine spray applied several times daily to numb the skin over the burned areas", "B": "Petroleum jelly applied thickly over the burn, which helps trap heat and speeds the healing", "C": "Hydrogen peroxide soaks twice daily to prevent infection in the burned areas of skin", "D": "Cool compresses and an unscented moisturizer, with oral ibuprofen if he has no contraindication"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Cool compresses and an unscented moisturizer, with oral ibuprofen if he has no contraindication): Superficial sunburn is managed with cooling, moisturizers and an oral NSAID, which can ease pain and inflammation. Thick petrolatum occludes heat and topical anesthetics such as benzocaine can sensitize skin. Peroxide soaks irritate injured skin and do not help."}, {"id": 1284, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "A 35 year old woman presents a handwritten prescription from a walk-in clinic for montelukast 10 mg daily, with the physician's printed name, clinic address and licence number but no signature. The pharmacy cannot reach the clinic this afternoon. What should the pharmacist do?", "options": {"A": "Hold the prescription and obtain confirmation of the order from the prescriber before dispensing", "B": "Dispense the order because the printed name and licence number reliably establish the prescriber's identity", "C": "Ask the patient to sign the prescription so that it becomes a valid order", "D": "Sign the prescription on the prescriber's behalf after recording the patient's identification details"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Hold the prescription and obtain confirmation of the order from the prescriber before dispensing): A prescription must be authorized by the prescriber, and an unsigned handwritten order cannot be assumed to be genuine even when printed details are present. The pharmacist should obtain confirmation from the prescriber, document it, and then dispense. Neither the pharmacist nor the patient can supply the missing authorization."}, {"id": 1285, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 1, "question": "DP is a 77 year old woman on warfarin with a stable INR of 2.4 for months. For hip osteoarthritis, her doctor recommends regular acetaminophen 1 g three times daily. She asks whether this combination is safe because acetaminophen is not a blood thinner. What should the pharmacist advise?", "options": {"A": "Daily acetaminophen at 3 g can raise INR, so check the INR in one week", "B": "It is safe with no monitoring, because acetaminophen has no effect on warfarin at any dose", "C": "It lowers the INR, so she should increase warfarin by 10% when she starts acetaminophen", "D": "It is safe only if she switches to ibuprofen on days when the pain is severe"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Daily acetaminophen at 3 g can raise INR, so check the INR in one week): Regular acetaminophen at about 2 g per day or more can raise the INR, probably through a metabolite that reduces vitamin K dependent clotting factor synthesis. An INR check within a week catches the change. Ibuprofen would add bleeding risk, and the effect is a rise, not a fall, in INR."}, {"id": 1286, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "ZK is a 38 year old woman who recently arrived in Canada as a refugee claimant. She has type 1 diabetes, needs insulin glargine 20 units nightly, and has no provincial health card. She tells you she has no money. Which action should the pharmacist take first to make sure she keeps her insulin?", "options": {"A": "Give a one week supply free and ask her to return with her health card", "B": "Determine whether she is covered under the Interim Federal Health Program and bill it", "C": "Explain that insulin cannot be dispensed without a health card and refer her to a clinic", "D": "Refer her to the emergency department so a hospital can supply the insulin directly"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Determine whether she is covered under the Interim Federal Health Program and bill it): Refugee claimants are generally covered for prescription drugs under the Interim Federal Health Program, so the pharmacist should confirm eligibility and bill it. Refusing or sending her elsewhere interrupts essential insulin therapy, and a free supply does not solve the continuing access problem. Emergency department referral is unnecessary when coverage can be arranged at the pharmacy."}, {"id": 1287, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 6, "question": "HL is a 40 year old with heavy menstrual bleeding who has completed her family and has no contraindication to hormonal therapy. Bleeding soaks through a pad every hour for two days each cycle. She wants a long-term option with the greatest reduction in blood loss. Which should the pharmacist suggest discussing?", "options": {"A": "A levonorgestrel 52 mg intrauterine system, which reduces menstrual blood loss substantially", "B": "Norethindrone 0.35 mg taken for seven days each month, which regulates the lining reliably", "C": "Daily aspirin 81 mg, which thins the blood and decreases the total volume of bleeding", "D": "A copper intrauterine device, which reduces menstrual flow more than hormonal options do"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A levonorgestrel 52 mg intrauterine system, which reduces menstrual blood loss substantially): The levonorgestrel 52 mg intrauterine system thins the endometrium and reduces menstrual blood loss by roughly 70 to 90 percent, making it a first line long-term approach. A copper device can increase bleeding. Intermittent norethindrone is not an effective regimen, and aspirin impairs clotting and can worsen bleeding."}, {"id": 1288, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A trial of a new anticoagulant was stopped early by its data monitoring committee after an interim analysis showed a large benefit based on only 60 events. A pharmacist considers how to interpret the reported effect. What concern is most relevant?", "options": {"A": "Early stopping eliminates random error, because the interim analysis is conducted by an independent committee", "B": "Early stopping removes the need for a confidence interval, because the effect is already proven", "C": "Early stopping ensures that the harms of the drug were fully measured by the interim analysis", "D": "Trials stopped early for benefit tend to overestimate the effect, especially with few events"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Trials stopped early for benefit tend to overestimate the effect, especially with few events): Stopping when an interim result crosses a boundary captures random highs in the estimate, so the effect size tends to be inflated, particularly with few events. Safety data are also usually immature. The result may still be real, but the magnitude should be treated cautiously."}, {"id": 1289, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 6, "question": "DW is a 40 year old man who wants a laxative that will work within an hour because he has an early flight tomorrow morning and has not had a bowel movement for three days. He is otherwise healthy. Which product best matches his need?", "options": {"A": "Lactulose 30 mL taken now, which reliably acts within one hour of the dose", "B": "Bisacodyl 10 mg rectal suppository, which usually produces a bowel movement within an hour", "C": "Psyllium taken with water this morning, producing a bowel movement within the hour", "D": "Bisacodyl 5 mg enteric-coated tablets taken the night before, working within thirty minutes"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Bisacodyl 10 mg rectal suppository, which usually produces a bowel movement within an hour): A bisacodyl suppository stimulates the rectum and acts in roughly 15 to 60 minutes. Oral bisacodyl takes 6 to 12 hours, bulk-forming agents take 12 to 72 hours, and lactulose takes 24 to 48 hours. The suppository is the only one that matches his timeline."}, {"id": 1290, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "A community pharmacist wants to share de-identified medication incident and near miss data so that other pharmacies can learn from errors that occurred in her store. Which national program, supported by ISMP Canada and NAPRA, is designed for this purpose?", "options": {"A": "Health Canada's Canada Vigilance program, which collects adverse reaction reports for marketed drugs", "B": "The Community Pharmacy Incident Reporting program, which collects and analyses incidents and near misses", "C": "The provincial drug plan's claims audit program, which reviews billing accuracy for dispensed items", "D": "Each province's prescription monitoring program, which tracks narcotic dispensing by prescribers and pharmacies"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The Community Pharmacy Incident Reporting program, which collects and analyses incidents and near misses): CPhIR, supported by ISMP Canada, lets community pharmacies record incidents and near misses and learn from aggregated analysis. Canada Vigilance deals with adverse reactions to products, and monitoring and audit programs serve different purposes."}, {"id": 1291, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 1, "question": "FG is a 63 year old woman taking apixaban 5 mg twice daily for atrial fibrillation. She tells the pharmacist she bought St. John's wort to help her mood and plans to start it tomorrow. What should the pharmacist advise?", "options": {"A": "take it, since it has no interaction with anticoagulants of any type", "B": "take it, but double the apixaban dose to offset the herbal effect", "C": "take it at midday only, since timing avoids any enzyme induction", "D": "avoid it, because St. John's wort induces P-glycoprotein and CYP3A4 and lowers apixaban levels"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (avoid it, because St. John's wort induces P-glycoprotein and CYP3A4 and lowers apixaban levels): St. John's wort is a strong inducer of CYP3A4 and P-glycoprotein and can lower apixaban exposure enough to risk stroke. She should not combine them, and her mood symptoms should be assessed by her prescriber. Timing does not prevent induction."}, {"id": 1292, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 3, "question": "WD is a 70 year old man who finished amoxicillin-clavulanate for pneumonia nine days ago. He now has six watery stools daily, lower abdominal cramping and a temperature of 37.9 C. He lives alone and asks for loperamide. What should the pharmacist recommend?", "options": {"A": "Suggest probiotics and loperamide together, and reassess only if the symptoms persist beyond two weeks", "B": "Advise bismuth subsalicylate and a bland diet, with referral only if blood appears", "C": "Avoid loperamide and refer him promptly for assessment, including testing for Clostridioides difficile", "D": "Sell loperamide and advise oral fluids, since antibiotic associated diarrhea usually resolves by itself within days"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Avoid loperamide and refer him promptly for assessment, including testing for Clostridioides difficile): Frequent watery diarrhea with cramping and fever after recent antibiotics in an older adult raises concern for Clostridioides difficile infection. Antimotility agents can worsen outcomes by trapping toxin in the bowel, so loperamide is avoided. He needs testing and treatment, not delayed self care."}, {"id": 1293, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "A pharmacy is writing a standard operating procedure for cleaning and monitoring its medication fridge. The pharmacist-in-charge lists possible elements and asks which belongs in a well-written procedure so any trained staff member can follow it consistently. Which element is most essential?", "options": {"A": "The supplier's marketing brochure describing the fridge's features and the warranty offered with the purchase", "B": "Steps stating who does each task, how often, and the action for out-of-range temperatures", "C": "A general statement of the pharmacy's commitment to quality, signed by the owner and reviewed yearly", "D": "The names of staff who have previously failed to record temperatures, so they are watched closely"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Steps stating who does each task, how often, and the action for out-of-range temperatures): A procedure must be specific enough that any trained person performs the task the same way, which means defining responsibility, frequency and the corrective action when a limit is exceeded. Quality statements, marketing material and lists of staff who made past errors do not guide consistent action. The procedure should also be version controlled and reviewed."}, {"id": 1294, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "A breastfeeding mother, 29 year old ML, uses cannabis oil nightly and asks the pharmacist whether she can continue. Her infant is six weeks old and exclusively breastfed. She reports it helps her anxiety and sleep. What is the best advice?", "options": {"A": "Advise continuing at night only, because THC clears from milk within an hour of each dose", "B": "Advise pumping and discarding milk for one hour after use, which removes THC from the supply", "C": "Advise switching to CBD-only oil, since CBD cannot pass into breast milk or affect the infant", "D": "Advise stopping cannabis while breastfeeding, since THC enters milk, and discuss safer anxiety options"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Advise stopping cannabis while breastfeeding, since THC enters milk, and discuss safer anxiety options): THC passes into breast milk and can affect infant development, so stopping use while breastfeeding is recommended, along with safer treatment for anxiety. Timing doses or pumping and discarding does not reliably remove THC from milk, because THC is fat soluble and persists. CBD products can also contain THC and are not proven safe for infants."}, {"id": 1295, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "A 54 year old woman on levothyroxine 100 mcg daily has no symptoms, but her laboratory report shows TSH below the range and free T4 raised. She has taken biotin 10 mg daily for hair loss for two months. What should the pharmacist suggest?", "options": {"A": "Switch to liothyronine because the report shows she developed resistance to her levothyroxine dose", "B": "Add a beta-blocker for the raised free T4, and keep biotin since assays are unaffected", "C": "Reduce levothyroxine by half immediately, because the result confirms she is taking too much hormone", "D": "Stop biotin for several days before repeating thyroid tests, since assay interference is possible"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Stop biotin for several days before repeating thyroid tests, since assay interference is possible): High-dose biotin can interfere with some immunoassays, producing falsely low TSH and falsely high free T4 that mimic hyperthyroidism. Because she is asymptomatic, the result should be repeated after biotin is held before any dose is changed. Cutting the dose on a possibly spurious result could cause true hypothyroidism."}, {"id": 1296, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 3, "question": "AZ is a 35 year old woman with drug susceptible pulmonary tuberculosis who has completed two months of isoniazid, rifampin, pyrazinamide and ethambutol with a negative culture at two months and no cavities on x-ray. The pharmacist at the TB clinic is asked what the continuation phase should be. Which regimen is correct?", "options": {"A": "Isoniazid, rifampin and pyrazinamide daily for seven more months, completing nine months in total", "B": "Isoniazid and rifampin daily for four more months, completing six months in total", "C": "Isoniazid and rifampin daily for one more month, since the two month culture is negative", "D": "Rifampin alone daily for four more months, since isoniazid is no longer needed after two months"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Isoniazid and rifampin daily for four more months, completing six months in total): Standard treatment for drug susceptible pulmonary tuberculosis is two months of four drugs followed by four months of isoniazid and rifampin, a six month total. Pyrazinamide and ethambutol are stopped after the intensive phase. Extending to nine months is reserved for cavitation with a positive two month culture, and shortening the course leads to relapse."}, {"id": 1297, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 1, "question": "VT is a 45 year old man weighing 80 kg with normal kidney function who has just been diagnosed with an acute pulmonary embolism. The hospital team plans a treatment-dose low molecular weight heparin before discharge. Which enoxaparin regimen is correct?", "options": {"A": "120 mg given subcutaneously every 12 hours, which is reserved for patients above 100 kg", "B": "80 mg given subcutaneously once daily for the acute treatment of the pulmonary embolism", "C": "40 mg given subcutaneously every 12 hours, which is the usual dose used for thromboprophylaxis", "D": "80 mg given subcutaneously every 12 hours for acute treatment of the pulmonary embolism"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (80 mg given subcutaneously every 12 hours for acute treatment of the pulmonary embolism): Treatment of acute VTE with enoxaparin is 1 mg/kg every 12 hours, so 80 mg twice daily for 80 kg. A once daily regimen needs 1.5 mg/kg. The 40 mg dose is for prophylaxis."}, {"id": 1298, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "A pharmacist compounds 100 mL of furosemide oral solution 10 mg/mL for a child, a preparation that is sensitive to light. The technician has several containers on the shelf and asks which to use for dispensing. Which container best protects the product?", "options": {"A": "An amber bottle with a tight closure, to be kept in the carton", "B": "A clear plastic bottle with a tight closure, kept on a windowsill", "C": "A clear glass bottle with a child resistant cap, placed in a paper bag", "D": "A white opaque dropper bottle without a closure that seals tightly"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (An amber bottle with a tight closure, to be kept in the carton): Light sensitive liquids should be dispensed in amber or light resistant containers with a tight closure, which blocks the wavelengths that degrade the drug. Clear containers let light in even if a bag or carton covers them part of the time. Tight closures also limit evaporation and contamination."}, {"id": 1299, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "WL is an 83 year old woman in long-term care on glimepiride and insulin. A nurse treats a reading of 3.4 mmol/L with 15 g of juice and the follow-up reading 15 minutes later is 5.1 mmol/L. Her supper arrives in about two hours. What should the pharmacist suggest next?", "options": {"A": "Give 4 units of rapid-acting insulin with the snack so glucose does not overshoot upward", "B": "Repeat the juice twice more, since sulfonylurea lows tend to return within the hour", "C": "No further treatment is needed, since the glucose is above 4.0 mmol/L and supper is coming", "D": "Offer a carbohydrate and protein snack, since supper is more than an hour away"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Offer a carbohydrate and protein snack, since supper is more than an hour away): After glucose recovers, a snack with carbohydrate and protein is advised when the next meal is more than an hour away, because a sulfonylurea and insulin can cause the low to return. Further juice would overtreat and cause a rebound high. Giving insulin or doing nothing leaves her at risk of a repeat low."}, {"id": 1300, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A pharmacy resident explains that an odds ratio of 4.0 means a patient with the exposure is four times as likely to have the outcome, in a study where the outcome occurred in 40% of unexposed patients. The pharmacist is asked to correct or confirm this statement.", "options": {"A": "Correct, because an odds ratio of 4.0 is interpreted as a relative risk of 4.0", "B": "Incorrect, since the outcome is common and the relative risk is only about 1.8", "C": "Correct, since odds ratios greater than 1 can be read directly as multiples of risk", "D": "Incorrect, since the odds ratio of 4.0 means the absolute risk rises by 4 percentage points"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Incorrect, since the outcome is common and the relative risk is only about 1.8): With a baseline risk of 40% the baseline odds are 0.67, so an odds ratio of 4.0 gives odds of 2.67 and a risk of about 73%. The relative risk is therefore about 1.8, not 4. When outcomes are common, an odds ratio overstates the relative risk, so reading it as a risk ratio is a mistake."}, {"id": 1301, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A systematic review of a topical therapy for chronic wounds searched only one English-language database and did not look at trial registries or conference abstracts. The pooled result shows a strong benefit. A clinician asks a pharmacist what the main concern about this review is. What is the best answer?", "options": {"A": "A missing conference abstract search mainly affects the heterogeneity statistic, not the pooled effect", "B": "Missed unpublished or negative trials could bias the pooled estimate toward overstating the claimed benefit", "C": "Restricting to English reports tends to lower the pooled effect by losing positive trials", "D": "Searching one database ensures the included trials are of higher quality than those found elsewhere online"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Missed unpublished or negative trials could bias the pooled estimate toward overstating the claimed benefit): A narrow search risks missing small, negative or unpublished trials, which pushes the pooled result toward benefit. Comprehensive reviews search multiple databases and registries. A narrow search does not improve quality of the included trials."}, {"id": 1302, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 3, "question": "NV is a 47 year old woman with mild distal onychomycosis affecting one great toenail, under 30% of the nail area, and no matrix involvement. She has cirrhosis from chronic liver disease and wishes to avoid tablets. Which treatment would the pharmacist suggest?", "options": {"A": "Daily topical ciclopirox or efinaconazole for many months, accepting slow regrowth of a clear nail", "B": "Oral fluconazole 150 mg once weekly for six months, which is considered safe in advanced cirrhosis", "C": "Oral itraconazole 200 mg daily for 12 weeks, which is gentler on the liver than terbinafine", "D": "Oral terbinafine 250 mg daily for 12 weeks, the highest cure rate option"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Daily topical ciclopirox or efinaconazole for many months, accepting slow regrowth of a clear nail): For mild distal disease affecting under half of one nail without matrix involvement, topical therapy is reasonable, and it avoids hepatotoxicity concerns in a patient with cirrhosis. Terbinafine and itraconazole both carry liver injury risk and are best avoided in chronic liver disease, and itraconazole is not gentler on the liver. Fluconazole is not a proven safe regimen in cirrhosis either."}, {"id": 1303, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "A 74 year old man takes prednisone 20 mg daily for polymyalgia rheumatica and also uses naproxen 500 mg twice daily for knee osteoarthritis. He has no gastroprotection and has had indigestion this month. What is the pharmacist's best recommendation?", "options": {"A": "Continue both unchanged, because corticosteroids protect the stomach lining from the effects of naproxen", "B": "Take naproxen only at bedtime on an empty stomach, which removes the added prednisone risk", "C": "Switch naproxen to ibuprofen, since ibuprofen carries no bleeding risk when combined with steroids", "D": "Add gastroprotection or use a safer analgesic, since this pair raises gastrointestinal bleeding risk"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Add gastroprotection or use a safer analgesic, since this pair raises gastrointestinal bleeding risk): Corticosteroids combined with a nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug sharply increase the risk of ulcers and bleeding, particularly in older adults. A gastroprotective proton pump inhibitor or replacement with a safer analgesic such as acetaminophen reduces that risk. Switching to another NSAID or changing the timing does not remove the interaction."}, {"id": 1304, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "QF is a 30 year old woman with generalized epilepsy, seizure-free for two years on lamotrigine 300 mg daily. She presents a new prescription from her gynecologist for a combined estrogen and progestin pill. No one has contacted her neurologist. What is the most important concern to raise?", "options": {"A": "Estrogen increases the rash risk from lamotrigine, so it must be stopped before the pill starts", "B": "The pill raises lamotrigine levels and causes toxicity, so her dose must be lowered right now", "C": "No interaction is expected, so the pill can start on the first day of her cycle", "D": "Estrogen can roughly halve lamotrigine levels, so the neurologist should plan monitoring and dosing"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Estrogen can roughly halve lamotrigine levels, so the neurologist should plan monitoring and dosing): Ethinyl estradiol induces glucuronidation of lamotrigine and can lower its levels by about 50 percent, so seizures may recur during active pills, and levels may rise sharply during pill-free weeks. The neurologist should plan dose adjustment and monitoring. Lamotrigine does not require stopping, and the pill does not raise its levels."}, {"id": 1305, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "A 31 year old woman in the obstetrical unit has magnesium sulfate ordered as 20 mmol of magnesium. The pharmacy holds 50% magnesium sulfate heptahydrate, which contains about 2 mmol of magnesium per millilitre. The pharmacist confirms the volume to add to the infusion bag. Which volume is correct?", "options": {"A": "10 mL, which supplies about 5 g of magnesium sulfate", "B": "5 mL, which supplies about 2.5 g of magnesium sulfate", "C": "40 mL, which supplies about 20 g of magnesium sulfate", "D": "20 mL, which supplies about 10 g of magnesium sulfate"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (10 mL, which supplies about 5 g of magnesium sulfate): At 2 mmol per mL, 20 mmol needs 10 mL. A 50% solution holds 0.5 g per mL, so 10 mL is 5 g, which matches the usual 4 to 6 g loading range. Twenty millilitres would deliver 10 g, a dose that risks respiratory depression."}, {"id": 1306, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 3, "question": "A pharmacist verifies a prescription for LR, a 3 year old weighing 15 kg, with acute otitis media. The prescriber orders amoxicillin 80 mg/kg/day divided every 12 hours. The pharmacy stocks a suspension of 250 mg per 5 mL. What volume should each dose be?", "options": {"A": "6 mL of the 250 mg per 5 mL suspension, given every 12 hours", "B": "10 mL of the 250 mg per 5 mL suspension, given every 12 hours", "C": "12 mL of the 250 mg per 5 mL suspension, given every 12 hours", "D": "24 mL of the 250 mg per 5 mL suspension, given every 12 hours"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (12 mL of the 250 mg per 5 mL suspension, given every 12 hours): The daily dose is 80 mg/kg x 15 kg = 1200 mg, so each of two doses is 600 mg. At 250 mg per 5 mL the volume is 600 / 250 x 5 = 12 mL. The 6 mL and 24 mL figures are halving and doubling errors, and 10 mL gives 500 mg."}, {"id": 1307, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 4, "question": "Twenty minutes after her first dose of cephalexin, a 38 year old woman in the pharmacy waiting area develops widespread hives, lip swelling and wheezing. Her blood pressure is falling and she looks pale. What is the pharmacist's first action?", "options": {"A": "Sit her upright, give a salbutamol inhaler, and call 911 only if wheezing does not ease", "B": "Give epinephrine 0.3 mg into the shoulder muscle, then observe her ten minutes before calling", "C": "Give diphenhydramine 50 mg by mouth first, and call 911 if symptoms persist after thirty minutes", "D": "Give epinephrine 0.3 mg intramuscularly into the outer thigh, then call 911 immediately"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Give epinephrine 0.3 mg intramuscularly into the outer thigh, then call 911 immediately): Hives, swelling, wheeze and hypotension after a new drug indicate anaphylaxis, for which intramuscular epinephrine into the anterolateral thigh is the first-line treatment, with emergency services activated at once. Antihistamines act too slowly and do not treat airway or circulatory compromise. Delay increases the risk of death."}, {"id": 1308, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "An 80 year old woman with newly diagnosed osteoporosis, weighing 58 kg, has a serum creatinine of 110 umol/L and is being considered for alendronate. Using the Cockcroft-Gault equation, which statement about her calculated creatinine clearance and alendronate suitability is correct?", "options": {"A": "CrCl is about 39 mL/min, above the 35 mL/min cutoff, so alendronate may be used", "B": "CrCl is about 33 mL/min, under the 35 cutoff, so alendronate is not advised", "C": "CrCl is about 21 mL/min, so alendronate is advised only at half the weekly dose", "D": "CrCl is about 62 mL/min, far above the 35 cutoff, so alendronate may be used"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (CrCl is about 33 mL/min, under the 35 cutoff, so alendronate is not advised): For a woman the calculation is (140 minus 80) times 58 times 1.04 divided by 110, which gives about 33 mL/min. Alendronate is not recommended below about 35 mL/min. Using the male factor of 1.23 would wrongly give about 39 mL/min and suggest it is acceptable."}, {"id": 1309, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 4, "question": "A family of four shares one home, and one member has just been diagnosed with scabies by their doctor. The pharmacist is asked how to manage the household. Two other family members are itching and one is not. Which advice is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Throw away all bedding and clothing in the home, and treat only the first diagnosed person", "B": "Wash recent bedding, towels and clothing in hot water, and treat every household member together", "C": "Seal the bedding in plastic bags for ten minutes, and treat others only when burrows appear", "D": "Wash only the affected person's pyjamas, and treat just the itching family members after one week"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Wash recent bedding, towels and clothing in hot water, and treat every household member together): Mites survive away from skin for only a few days, so hot washing of recent items plus simultaneous treatment of everyone in close contact prevents reinfestation. Symptoms can be delayed for weeks, so waiting for itching to appear allows spread. Discarding all items is unnecessary."}, {"id": 1310, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 4, "question": "GD is a 72 year old man, 68 kg, with serum creatinine 220 umol/L, starting varenicline for smoking cessation. Using the Cockcroft-Gault equation, his creatinine clearance is about 26 mL/min. For severe renal impairment, the pharmacist should recommend a starting dose of:", "options": {"A": "1 mg once daily, with a maximum of 1 mg twice daily after one week", "B": "0.5 mg twice daily from day one, titrated up to 1 mg twice daily", "C": "0.5 mg once daily, with a maximum of 0.5 mg twice daily", "D": "1 mg twice daily, since varenicline needs no adjustment with reduced renal function"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (0.5 mg once daily, with a maximum of 0.5 mg twice daily): Varenicline is cleared by the kidneys, and with a clearance under 30 mL/min the labelled dose is 0.5 mg once daily, increased if needed to a maximum of 0.5 mg twice daily. His clearance is (140 - 72) x 68 x 1.23 / 220, about 26 mL/min. Standard titration to 1 mg twice daily would cause accumulation."}, {"id": 1311, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "Methotrexate 2.5 mg tablets, four tablets daily, quantity 120, is prescribed for a 58 year old man with rheumatoid arthritis. The pharmacy profile shows no previous methotrexate, and the pharmacist suspects the frequency is a prescribing error. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Contact the prescriber to confirm weekly dosing, because daily methotrexate for arthritis risks serious toxicity", "B": "Dispense as written and counsel him to watch for mouth sores and unusual bruising", "C": "Reduce the quantity to 30 tablets, since a smaller supply limits the risk of an overdose", "D": "Dispense one week of tablets and ask the prescriber to confirm the frequency later"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Contact the prescriber to confirm weekly dosing, because daily methotrexate for arthritis risks serious toxicity): Methotrexate for rheumatoid arthritis is taken once weekly, and daily dosing has caused fatal toxicity, so a daily order must be clarified before any tablets are dispensed. Counselling cannot make a dangerous order safe, and a short or reduced supply still exposes him to harm. The pharmacist should document the call and the corrected directions."}, {"id": 1312, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "HH is a 52 year old man who has just started orlistat 120 mg three times daily with meals. He asks what to do on days when he skips lunch or eats a meal with almost no fat. What should the pharmacist advise?", "options": {"A": "Skip the orlistat dose when a meal is missed or contains almost no fat", "B": "Take the dose at the next snack instead, because missed capsules must be made up", "C": "Take the capsule on an empty stomach, since it acts in the gut without food", "D": "Double the next dose with dinner, because a missed dose measurably reduces weight loss"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Skip the orlistat dose when a meal is missed or contains almost no fat): Orlistat blocks lipases that digest dietary fat, so it works only while fat from a meal is present in the gut. Taking it without a fat-containing meal gives no benefit, and doubling doses does not add effect but can worsen oily stools. The instruction is to take it with, or within an hour of, a meal containing fat."}, {"id": 1313, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "A prescriber authorizes a 7 day carry of buprenorphine and naloxone for a patient taking 12 mg per day. The pharmacy stocks 8 mg/2 mg and 2 mg/0.5 mg tablets. How many tablets of each strength are needed to dispense the 7 day carry?", "options": {"A": "7 tablets of 8 mg/2 mg and 28 tablets of 2 mg/0.5 mg for the week", "B": "7 tablets of 8 mg/2 mg and 7 tablets of 2 mg/0.5 mg for the week", "C": "14 tablets of 8 mg/2 mg and 14 tablets of 2 mg/0.5 mg for the week", "D": "7 tablets of 8 mg/2 mg and 14 tablets of 2 mg/0.5 mg for the week"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (7 tablets of 8 mg/2 mg and 14 tablets of 2 mg/0.5 mg for the week): A 12 mg daily dose is one 8 mg tablet plus two 2 mg tablets (8 plus 4). Over 7 days that is 7 of the 8 mg tablets and 14 of the 2 mg tablets. The other combinations give 8 mg, 16 mg or 20 mg per day, which are wrong doses."}, {"id": 1314, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "NH, a 70 year old woman, has a prescription for lisinopril 5 mg once daily for 30 days. The pharmacy stocks only 10 mg scored tablets, and NH is agreeable to splitting them. The pharmacist prepares the label and quantity. Which label and quantity are correct?", "options": {"A": "Take half a 10 mg tablet daily; dispense 15 tablets for the full course", "B": "Take one 10 mg tablet once daily; dispense 30 tablets for the full course", "C": "Take half a 10 mg tablet once daily; dispense 30 tablets for the full course", "D": "Take two 5 mg tablets once daily; dispense 60 tablets for the full course"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Take half a 10 mg tablet daily; dispense 15 tablets for the full course): The 5 mg dose from a scored 10 mg tablet is half a tablet, so 30 days needs 15 tablets. One whole tablet would double the dose, dispensing 30 tablets would give a 60 day supply, and 5 mg tablets are not the product in stock."}, {"id": 1315, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 1, "question": "RK is a 57 year old man who started amiodarone three months ago. He is a landscaper who works outdoors and reports that his forearms burn after short sun exposure. What counselling is most appropriate? He takes no other medications and his thyroid tests are normal.", "options": {"A": "use broad-spectrum sunscreen and cover exposed skin, because amiodarone causes marked photosensitivity", "B": "take a morning dose instead of an evening dose, since timing removes the sun reaction", "C": "stop amiodarone at once and restart it only in winter, since the reaction is seasonal", "D": "use a tanning bed first to build tolerance, since gradual exposure reduces the reaction"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (use broad-spectrum sunscreen and cover exposed skin, because amiodarone causes marked photosensitivity): Amiodarone causes photosensitivity in many patients, and some develop a blue-grey skin discolouration. Sun protection is the main measure, and the drug is not stopped without cardiology review. Tanning does not build tolerance."}, {"id": 1316, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "WS is a 59 year old man with a BMI of 36 kg/m2 who asks for zopiclone because he wakes unrefreshed. His wife reports loud snoring and pauses in his breathing, and he falls asleep at work. Which pharmacist response is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Suggest diphenhydramine 50 mg at bedtime, which has no breathing effects and is safe in obesity", "B": "Refer him for sleep apnea assessment before any hypnotic, since sedatives can worsen breathing", "C": "Dispense a short course of zopiclone 3.75 mg, since his symptoms are typical of simple insomnia", "D": "Recommend melatonin 10 mg and weight loss, since apnea testing is needed only in severe cases"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Refer him for sleep apnea assessment before any hypnotic, since sedatives can worsen breathing): Loud snoring, witnessed apneas, obesity, and daytime sleepiness strongly suggest obstructive sleep apnea, which needs diagnosis and treatment. Sedative-hypnotics and sedating antihistamines can depress breathing and worsen apnea, so they should be avoided until it is assessed. Melatonin and weight loss alone do not replace testing."}, {"id": 1317, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 4, "question": "KA is a 33 year old woman visiting her sister's home, where there are cats. She gets sneezing and itchy eyes within an hour of exposure and has cetirizine 10 mg tablets at home. She asks when to take a tablet for this visit. The pharmacist should advise taking it:", "options": {"A": "about one hour before arriving, since cetirizine takes roughly an hour to start working", "B": "only after symptoms begin, because the tablet works within about five minutes of swallowing", "C": "at bedtime the night before, since a tablet at night acts best the next day", "D": "right on arrival, since taking it earlier would use up the dose before exposure"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (about one hour before arriving, since cetirizine takes roughly an hour to start working): Oral second generation antihistamines reach effect in about an hour, so taking one before a predictable exposure prevents symptoms. Waiting for symptoms means a delay before relief, and a dose the night before may be less reliable, because the effect is not timed to the exposure and may be fading by the time she arrives."}, {"id": 1318, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "A pharmacist realizes during the following shift that she dispensed the wrong strength of warfarin the previous evening and calls the patient before he takes a dose. The pharmacy owner is angry and suggests keeping the incident private. What should the pharmacist do about documentation and reporting?", "options": {"A": "Report only to the pharmacy owner and let the owner decide on further reporting", "B": "Document the event only in the owner's private notes so the patient profile stays clean", "C": "Document the event and report it through the pharmacy's incident program, as professional standards require", "D": "Keep the matter private since the patient was contacted before any dose and no harm followed"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Document the event and report it through the pharmacy's incident program, as professional standards require): Pharmacists are professionally accountable for documenting and reporting medication incidents, including errors caught before a dose is taken, so that the pharmacy can learn from them. An owner's wish for privacy does not change this duty. Keeping notes separate from the record or limiting reports to the owner defeats quality improvement."}, {"id": 1319, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "A 40 year old patient requests an early refill of hydromorphone 4 mg, 14 days after receiving 30 tablets. The provincial monitoring program shows two other prescribers and two other pharmacies in the last month for the same drug. The patient says tablets were lost. What should the pharmacist do?", "options": {"A": "Refuse and tell the patient to use another pharmacy, without contacting the prescriber or anyone else", "B": "Decline the early refill, tell the prescriber about the pattern, and offer the patient support", "C": "Dispense a seven day supply only and report the patient to police for drug trafficking", "D": "Fill the early refill, since lost medication is a legitimate reason, and document the patient's explanation"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Decline the early refill, tell the prescriber about the pattern, and offer the patient support): The monitoring record shows overlapping supplies from several sources, which raises concern for misuse or diversion and for patient harm. The pharmacist declines the early supply and shares the information with the prescriber so the patient's care can be reassessed with support offered. Filling the refill ignores the pattern, and sending the patient away or calling police does not help her."}, {"id": 1320, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "A 62 year old woman stable on warfarin for atrial fibrillation, with INR consistently 2.4, has just begun methimazole for hyperthyroidism. Over the next six weeks her thyroid function normalizes. Which change in her warfarin response should the pharmacist anticipate?", "options": {"A": "Warfarin must be stopped during methimazole therapy because the drugs cannot be combined safely", "B": "Her INR will stay unchanged, because thyroid status does not influence how warfarin is handled", "C": "Warfarin sensitivity will rise as she becomes euthyroid, so a considerably lower dose is needed", "D": "Warfarin sensitivity will fall as she becomes euthyroid, so more warfarin may be needed"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Warfarin sensitivity will fall as she becomes euthyroid, so more warfarin may be needed): Hyperthyroidism speeds breakdown of vitamin K-dependent clotting factors, which makes patients more sensitive to warfarin. As thyroid function returns to normal, that catabolism slows and the same dose produces a lower INR, so the dose often has to rise. INR monitoring should be frequent during the transition."}, {"id": 1321, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 4, "question": "ZC is a 29 year old woman prescribed budesonide-formoterol dry powder inhaler, 200/6 mcg, two inhalations twice daily for asthma. The inhaler contains 120 doses and has a dose counter. The pharmacist is writing the days supply. Assuming no priming doses, the inhaler lasts approximately:", "options": {"A": "60 days, because each pair of inhalations counts as a single dose on the counter", "B": "30 days, since she uses four inhalations each day from the 120 available", "C": "15 days, because every inhalation is counted twice on the counter during use", "D": "40 days, because the dose counter is based on three inhalations each day of use"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (30 days, since she uses four inhalations each day from the 120 available): Each inhalation uses one counter dose, and two inhalations twice daily means four per day. Dividing 120 by 4 gives 30 days. The other values treat paired inhalations as one dose or assume a different daily count."}, {"id": 1322, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "IP is a 69 year old woman with eGFR 40 mL/min/1.73 m2 scheduled for a contrast enhanced CT scan tomorrow. She takes ramipril, furosemide and celecoxib for osteoarthritis. The radiology nurse asks the pharmacist what medication advice to give for the procedure. What should be recommended?", "options": {"A": "Hold celecoxib around the scan, ensure hydration, and recheck kidney function a few days afterwards", "B": "Stop furosemide only and continue celecoxib, because loop diuretics are the main cause of contrast nephropathy", "C": "Continue all medications and start N-acetylcysteine, which prevents contrast nephropathy at eGFR below 45 mL/min/1.73 m2", "D": "Withhold ramipril for one week before the scan, since ACE inhibitors cause tubular injury from contrast"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Hold celecoxib around the scan, ensure hydration, and recheck kidney function a few days afterwards): Risk of contrast nephropathy rises with eGFR below 45, so nephrotoxic NSAIDs should be held and volume status optimized. Hydration is the best supported preventive measure, with kidney function rechecked afterwards. N-acetylcysteine has not shown reliable benefit, and a week off ramipril has no supporting evidence."}, {"id": 1323, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "A community pharmacy is preparing to put a new automated tablet-packaging machine into service. The owner wants to meet professional standards before the first patient order is processed. Which step should the pharmacy complete before routine use of the machine?", "options": {"A": "Register the machine with Health Canada as a medical device before using it for packaging", "B": "Begin with live patient orders and correct any problems found by pharmacists during checks", "C": "Obtain verbal approval from the machine supplier and begin routine packaging the next day", "D": "Validate it with test runs, document the results and train the staff who operate it"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Validate it with test runs, document the results and train the staff who operate it): Automation must be validated, with documented test runs and staff training, before it is used for patient orders. Testing on live orders exposes patients to errors, supplier approval does not replace the pharmacy's own validation, and a packaging machine is not registered with Health Canada as a medical device."}, {"id": 1324, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 3, "question": "HG is a 47 year old woman with confirmed group A streptococcal pharyngitis. The prescriber orders penicillin V 600 mg twice daily for 10 days. The pharmacy only has penicillin V 300 mg tablets in stock. What should the pharmacist dispense and label?", "options": {"A": "Dispense 40 tablets, taken as 2 tablets twice daily for the full 10 days", "B": "Dispense 60 tablets, taken as 2 tablets three times daily for the full 10 days", "C": "Dispense 40 tablets, taken as 2 tablets twice daily and stopped once her pain settles", "D": "Dispense 20 tablets, taken as 1 tablet twice daily for the full 10 days"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Dispense 40 tablets, taken as 2 tablets twice daily for the full 10 days): A 600 mg dose from 300 mg tablets is 2 tablets. Twice daily for 10 days is 20 doses, so 40 tablets are needed. The course should be finished to eradicate the organism and prevent rheumatic fever."}, {"id": 1325, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "An assistant at a small compounding pharmacy prepares a batch of 100 capsules of a compounded drug, weighs each ingredient, and labels the container. The pharmacy is busy, and the assistant suggests releasing the batch directly to the patient since she followed the master formulation. Who must verify the preparation before release?", "options": {"A": "The patient verifies the capsules on receipt and signs the label to confirm the contents match", "B": "A second assistant may check only the labels, since the formulation record already shows calculations", "C": "The assistant's own check suffices when she has completed training and followed the master formulation", "D": "A pharmacist must check calculations, ingredients, weights, appearance and label before the preparation is released"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A pharmacist must check calculations, ingredients, weights, appearance and label before the preparation is released): The pharmacist is accountable for each compounded preparation and must verify the calculations, ingredients and amounts, the physical appearance, and the label before the product leaves the pharmacy. Following a master formulation does not remove the need for an independent check, and a patient cannot judge the contents on receipt."}, {"id": 1326, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "A patient reports that his unopened insulin pens, kept in a cooler bag, stayed in a parked car on a summer day and the bag thermometer peaked at 38 degrees Celsius for about five hours. The monograph says not to expose insulin to temperatures above 30 degrees. The pens look clear. What should the pharmacist advise?", "options": {"A": "Use the pens only for the morning dose and discard them if glucose rises", "B": "Discard the pens, since exposure above the labelled limit cannot be judged by appearance", "C": "Keep using the pens because insulin that looks clear and colourless is still potent", "D": "Refrigerate the pens for two days to recover potency, then use them as usual"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Discard the pens, since exposure above the labelled limit cannot be judged by appearance): Heat above the labelled limit degrades insulin, and loss of potency cannot be seen, since degraded insulin can still look clear. Refrigerating the pens afterward does not restore the lost activity. Using pens of uncertain potency risks high glucose, so they should be replaced."}, {"id": 1327, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 4, "question": "VL is a 36 year old woman with no seizure history who is prescribed bupropion SR 150 mg for smoking cessation and plans to quit on the 20th of the month. She asks when to begin the tablets so they will work in time. The pharmacist should advise starting:", "options": {"A": "the day after her quit date, at 150 mg daily for six weeks only", "B": "one to two weeks before the quit date, at 150 mg daily then twice daily", "C": "on the quit date itself, at 300 mg daily starting with the first tablet", "D": "four weeks before the quit date, at 150 mg twice daily from the initial day"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (one to two weeks before the quit date, at 150 mg daily then twice daily): Bupropion needs time to reach steady state, so it is begun one to two weeks before the quit date at 150 mg daily, then raised to 150 mg twice daily after several days. Starting at the full dose on the quit date, or starting weeks early, does not follow the labelled approach and raises the risk of adverse effects such as insomnia."}, {"id": 1328, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "CY is a 38 year old woman who injects insulin glargine 26 units once daily and primes the pen with 2 units before each injection. Her pen holds 300 units. The pharmacist is calculating how many complete days one pen will last for the dispensing record. What is the correct figure?", "options": {"A": "10 days, because each day uses 28 units including the 2 unit priming dose", "B": "11 days, because each day uses 26 units excluding the 2 unit priming dose", "C": "15 days, because each day uses 20 units, leaving out the 2 unit priming dose", "D": "12 days, because each day uses 24 units excluding the 2 unit priming dose"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (10 days, because each day uses 28 units including the 2 unit priming dose): Each day uses 26 units to inject plus 2 units to prime, a total of 28. Three hundred units divided by 28 is 10.7, so 10 complete days. Leaving out the priming dose gives 11 days and would cause an early run-out."}, {"id": 1329, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 4, "question": "HJ is a 77 year old woman with COPD on day two of an exacerbation. Her daughter calls the pharmacy to report severe breathlessness at rest, new confusion, blue lips, and that she cannot finish sentences. The daughter asks whether to increase inhaler doses at home and wait. What should the pharmacist advise?", "options": {"A": "Start her emergency prednisone supply and visit her family doctor in the morning", "B": "Double her reliever doses and recheck breathing in two hours before deciding next steps", "C": "Call 911 or arrange emergency transport now, as these signs suggest acute respiratory failure", "D": "Have her lie flat and rest an hour, then call back if she remains blue"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Call 911 or arrange emergency transport now, as these signs suggest acute respiratory failure): Breathlessness at rest, confusion, cyanosis and inability to speak sentences are signs of life threatening hypoxemia or hypercapnia and need emergency care immediately. Home measures delay treatment such as oxygen and ventilatory support. Lying flat worsens breathing in advanced COPD."}, {"id": 1330, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 6, "question": "EN is a 19 year old with painful periods who cannot take NSAIDs because ibuprofen triggered wheezing and hives last year. Acetaminophen gives little relief and she also wants contraception. The pharmacist considers which option addresses both concerns. What should be suggested?", "options": {"A": "A hormonal contraceptive, which lowers prostaglandin production and reduces menstrual cramping pain", "B": "Naproxen 250 mg with an antihistamine, since the antihistamine prevents the wheezing and hives", "C": "A copper intrauterine device, which reduces prostaglandin production and cramping within a few months", "D": "Acetaminophen 1 g every four hours, which is the maximum dose and works best for cramps"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A hormonal contraceptive, which lowers prostaglandin production and reduces menstrual cramping pain): Hormonal contraceptives suppress ovulation and thin the endometrium, lowering prostaglandin release and cramping while providing contraception. A copper device can increase cramping and bleeding. Combining an NSAID with an antihistamine does not prevent a true reaction, and acetaminophen every four hours exceeds the daily maximum."}, {"id": 1331, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "UA is an 82 year old woman weighing 54 kg with a serum creatinine of 96 umol/L. A pharmacist reviewing her new prescription for a renally cleared antibiotic uses Cockcroft-Gault with actual weight to estimate her creatinine clearance. Which value is correct?", "options": {"A": "About 44 mL/min, found by entering her age as 65 years", "B": "About 40 mL/min, using the male constant of 1.23 instead", "C": "About 24 mL/min, found by entering her weight as 38 kg", "D": "About 34 mL/min, using the female constant of 1.04"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (About 34 mL/min, using the female constant of 1.04): (140 - 82) x 54 x 1.04 / 96 = 33.9 mL/min. Using the male constant would raise the estimate by 18%, and entering a younger age would make her kidneys look healthier than they are, both leading to antibiotic overdosing."}, {"id": 1332, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "A pharmacy owner, UB, who holds a conscientious objection, decides the whole pharmacy will not stock or dispense hormonal contraceptives or give referral information. A patient, KE, 24, asks about the pill. Which statement best reflects professional expectations about this policy?", "options": {"A": "A pharmacy policy is valid when posted clearly, even if patients lose access to time-sensitive services", "B": "Referral information may be withheld, since giving it would be taking part in the objected service", "C": "Conscience rights are personal, and patients must still receive timely information and referral", "D": "Owners may restrict services across the whole pharmacy because the property and licence belong to them"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Conscience rights are personal, and patients must still receive timely information and referral): Conscience rights belong to individual pharmacists, not to a pharmacy as a business. The pharmacy must still make sure that patients get timely information and referral. A blanket policy that leaves patients with no access, or withholds referral information, does not meet professional expectations."}, {"id": 1333, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "A regulated technician is entering a refill for diazepam 5 mg for MK, a 76 year old woman, and notices the profile also shows lorazepam 1 mg filled six days ago from a different prescriber. The pharmacist is counselling another patient. What should the technician do?", "options": {"A": "Complete the refill and add a profile note for the pharmacist to review later", "B": "Contact the second prescriber to confirm that the lorazepam prescription was truly intended for this patient", "C": "Pause processing, flag the possible duplication, and refer it to the pharmacist for clinical assessment", "D": "Telephone the patient to ask whether she is still taking the lorazepam, then decide"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Pause processing, flag the possible duplication, and refer it to the pharmacist for clinical assessment): Possible therapeutic duplication of two benzodiazepines in an older adult is a clinical issue, and assessing it is a pharmacist responsibility. The technician's role is to recognize the concern and refer it promptly. Completing the fill, or contacting the patient or prescriber, is outside the technician's role."}, {"id": 1334, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "A wholesaler's tote arrives with its tamper seal intact. The invoice lists five bottles of oxycodone 5 mg (100 tablets each), but the tote holds four. The delivery driver has already left. The pharmacist is checking the shipment into inventory. What should the pharmacist do?", "options": {"A": "Add the missing bottle to inventory as received since it was invoiced and the pharmacy paid", "B": "Discard the paperwork and absorb the loss so the audit trail stays simple", "C": "Accept the delivery and mention the difference at the next quarterly count of narcotics", "D": "Record the shortage on the invoice, notify the wholesaler the same day, and report if unresolved"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Record the shortage on the invoice, notify the wholesaler the same day, and report if unresolved): Receipts must match what is physically received. The shortage is documented immediately, the wholesaler is told so the shipment can be traced, and a loss is reported if it cannot be resolved. Recording stock that is not present falsifies the perpetual inventory, and delaying the discrepancy allows evidence to disappear."}, {"id": 1335, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A 80 year old man in long-term care takes warfarin 4 mg daily with a stable INR of 2.5. After a five day course of metronidazole, his INR is 5.8 with no bleeding. The pharmacist identifies the likely mechanism and recommends:", "options": {"A": "Reduced vitamin K from gut flora loss; give intravenous vitamin K 10 mg and continue warfarin", "B": "Displacement from albumin binding sites; stop warfarin for two weeks and restart at half the dose", "C": "CYP2C9 inhibition slows warfarin clearance; hold one or two doses and recheck the INR", "D": "Induction of warfarin metabolism by CYP3A4; increase the warfarin dose by 20% and recheck the INR"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (CYP2C9 inhibition slows warfarin clearance; hold one or two doses and recheck the INR): Metronidazole inhibits CYP2C9, the main route of S-warfarin clearance, so the INR climbs. For an INR between 4.5 and 10 without bleeding, holding a dose or two and rechecking is usual, and routine vitamin K is not needed. Induction would lower the INR, and a two week stop would leave him unprotected."}, {"id": 1336, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 6, "question": "LF is a 29 year old breastfeeding her ten week old and has a painful, red, wedge-shaped area on her right breast. Her temperature is 38.9 C and she has chills and aches since yesterday. She has no drug allergies and asks if she must stop nursing. What is the best advice?", "options": {"A": "Keep nursing or expressing, use ibuprofen, and arrange an antibiotic compatible with breastfeeding", "B": "Start ciprofloxacin 500 mg twice daily, because it penetrates breast tissue better than other antibiotics", "C": "Stop nursing from the affected breast for one week to let the infection settle down", "D": "Apply heat and wait, since mastitis is rarely infectious and resolves without antibiotics in most women"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Keep nursing or expressing, use ibuprofen, and arrange an antibiotic compatible with breastfeeding): Frequent milk removal helps resolve mastitis, and the milk is safe for the infant. Ibuprofen relieves pain and inflammation, and fever with systemic symptoms calls for an antibiotic such as cephalexin or cloxacillin that is compatible with lactation. Stopping nursing worsens engorgement, and fluoroquinolones are not a first agent in lactation."}, {"id": 1337, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "A 33 year old woman is in week three of lamotrigine titration for bipolar II disorder. She develops a mild, non-itchy red rash on her torso and wonders whether she can safely ignore it. She takes no other medicines. What should the pharmacist advise?", "options": {"A": "Skip one dose, then resume at the same dose when the rash fades, avoiding the reaction", "B": "Continue the titration schedule and apply hydrocortisone cream, since mild rashes fade without other treatment", "C": "Take an antihistamine and double the next dose, since the rash shows that titration is working", "D": "Stop lamotrigine and call the prescriber today, since a rash may precede a serious reaction"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Stop lamotrigine and call the prescriber today, since a rash may precede a serious reaction): Rash during lamotrigine titration can be the first sign of Stevens-Johnson syndrome, so the drug is held and the patient assessed promptly. Continuing or accelerating the dose risks a serious reaction. If lamotrigine is later restarted after a gap, it must be re-titrated from the start."}, {"id": 1338, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "EL is a 10 year old girl who has taken guanfacine extended-release 3 mg daily for ADHD. Her family ran out and she missed doses for two days. She now has a pounding headache and her blood pressure is 140/90 mm Hg. Her father asks whether stopping suddenly caused this. What should the pharmacist explain?", "options": {"A": "Stopping cannot cause this, because guanfacine has no withdrawal effects and the headache is a coincidence", "B": "This is a delayed reaction to guanfacine itself, so she should drink fluids and restart it", "C": "This is a stimulant withdrawal reaction that fades in two days, so no treatment is needed", "D": "Sudden stopping can cause rebound high blood pressure, so she needs prompt medical review"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Sudden stopping can cause rebound high blood pressure, so she needs prompt medical review): Guanfacine and clonidine can cause rebound hypertension, and sometimes tachycardia and anxiety, when stopped suddenly, so doses are tapered by no more than 1 mg every three to seven days. A pounding headache with raised pressure after missed doses fits that pattern and needs prompt assessment. Guanfacine is not a stimulant, so stimulant withdrawal does not explain it."}, {"id": 1339, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A pharmacist assesses a rash in a 34 year old woman using the WHO-UMC causality categories. The rash began four days after starting a new antibiotic, resolved after stopping it, and has no plausible alternative explanation. No rechallenge has been attempted. The best category is:", "options": {"A": "Probable or likely, because the timing and dechallenge response fit and no other cause is evident", "B": "Possible, because the reaction could also be explained by the underlying illness or other concurrent drugs", "C": "Certain, because the reaction resolved after the drug was stopped and no alternative cause exists", "D": "Unlikely, because the rash began after several days rather than within the first hour"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Probable or likely, because the timing and dechallenge response fit and no other cause is evident): A plausible time relationship, improvement on withdrawal and no better explanation meet the criteria for probable. Certain requires a pharmacologically convincing event with a positive rechallenge, which was not done. Possible applies when other explanations remain, and a delay of days does not make a rash unlikely."}, {"id": 1340, "domain": "communication_and_collaboration", "chapter": 11, "question": "A pharmacist is writing instructions for a patient who takes an oral antibiotic and wants to avoid medical jargon. The patient should take the antibiotic with food to reduce stomach upset. Which wording is the clearest for a patient with limited health literacy?", "options": {"A": "Take this antibiotic concurrently with a meal to mitigate gastric adverse effects of therapy", "B": "Take this medicine with a meal or snack to help prevent an upset stomach", "C": "Take this medicine with food to minimize gastrointestinal irritation and the potential for emesis", "D": "Administer this agent with food intake to reduce nausea, dyspepsia and associated discomfort"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Take this medicine with a meal or snack to help prevent an upset stomach): Plain language uses familiar words such as meal, snack and upset stomach. The other wordings use terms like gastrointestinal, emesis, concurrently and dyspepsia that many patients do not recognize."}, {"id": 1341, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "A pharmacist dispenses warfarin 5 mg tablets for a 71 year old man whose dose is 7.5 mg on Mondays and Thursdays and 5 mg on all other days. The prescription is for 60 tablets. How many full days does this quantity supply, assuming he starts on a Monday?", "options": {"A": "60 days, since 60 tablets at the base dose of one tablet daily last that long", "B": "52 days, since seven weeks use 56 tablets and the remaining four cover three more days", "C": "49 days, since each week requires eight tablets and only seven complete weeks can be filled", "D": "56 days, since seven full weeks use 56 tablets and the leftover tablets are not used"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (52 days, since seven weeks use 56 tablets and the remaining four cover three more days): A week uses 1.5 + 1.5 + 5 = 8 tablets. Seven weeks use 56 tablets, covering 49 days, and 4 tablets remain. Monday takes 1.5, Tuesday 1 and Wednesday 1, leaving 0.5 tablet, which cannot complete Thursday's 7.5 mg dose, so the supply lasts 52 full days. Counting whole weeks only gives 49, and assuming one tablet daily gives 60."}, {"id": 1342, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "A pharmacist compounds a 2% topical cream from commercial ingredients for AB, a 9 year old girl, and prepares the label. In addition to the usual prescription information, which element should appear on the label of a compounded preparation according to NAPRA standards?", "options": {"A": "The pharmacist's licence number and the cost of each ingredient used", "B": "The name of the supplier of each ingredient and the lot numbers", "C": "The compounding method steps followed by the technician who prepared it", "D": "The beyond-use date and storage conditions for the compounded preparation"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The beyond-use date and storage conditions for the compounded preparation): NAPRA's non-sterile compounding standards call for the beyond-use date and storage conditions on the label so the patient knows how long and how to keep the product. Supplier names, lot numbers and cost belong in compounding records, not on the patient's label."}, {"id": 1343, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "RJ has been stable on lithium for six years, and his family doctor started hydrochlorothiazide 25 mg daily last week for hypertension. Considering this recent medication change, which explanation best accounts for the tremor, nausea and thirst that RJ now reports to the pharmacist at the counter today?", "options": {"A": "A relapse of mania that requires doubling the lithium dose and adding a benzodiazepine for sleep", "B": "Reduced renal lithium clearance caused by the thiazide diuretic, raising his serum lithium level", "C": "An allergic reaction to hydrochlorothiazide that needs an antihistamine and a switch to a different salt", "D": "Low lithium levels caused by increased urination from the diuretic, which require a higher lithium dose"}, "cluster_id": "CHS7", "cluster_text": "RJ is a 41 year old man with bipolar I disorder, stable for six years on lithium carbonate 300 mg three times daily. He also has hypertension. Last week his family doctor started hydrochlorothiazide 25 mg daily. Two months ago his serum lithium was 0.7 mmol/L, creatinine 88 umol/L. Today he reports hand tremor, nausea and unusual thirst. Allergies: none.", "cluster_size": 3, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Reduced renal lithium clearance caused by the thiazide diuretic, raising his serum lithium level): Thiazides reduce lithium excretion by increasing proximal tubular reabsorption, often raising levels by 25 to 40 percent within days to weeks. Tremor, nausea and thirst are early signs of lithium toxicity. Increasing the dose would worsen the problem."}, {"id": 1344, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "RJ's tremor, nausea and thirst began about a week after the new hydrochlorothiazide was added to his lithium. His prescriber has not yet been contacted about any change, and his last lithium level was two months ago. Which action should the pharmacist recommend first for RJ today?", "options": {"A": "Recommend stopping lithium for good and starting valproate, since symptoms show he cannot tolerate lithium", "B": "Arrange a 12 hour post-dose lithium level and kidney function today, and contact the prescriber", "C": "Take the next lithium dose with extra salt and wait a week for symptoms to settle", "D": "Double the hydrochlorothiazide dose to flush out fluid, and recheck his lithium level again next month"}, "cluster_id": "CHS7", "cluster_text": "RJ is a 41 year old man with bipolar I disorder, stable for six years on lithium carbonate 300 mg three times daily. He also has hypertension. Last week his family doctor started hydrochlorothiazide 25 mg daily. Two months ago his serum lithium was 0.7 mmol/L, creatinine 88 umol/L. Today he reports hand tremor, nausea and unusual thirst. Allergies: none.", "cluster_size": 3, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Arrange a 12 hour post-dose lithium level and kidney function today, and contact the prescriber): New neurological and GI symptoms after starting an interacting drug warrant a prompt lithium level and renal function, plus prescriber contact to change the antihypertensive. Waiting a week risks progression to toxicity, and more diuretic would increase lithium retention."}, {"id": 1345, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "The prescriber stops the thiazide and wants a replacement antihypertensive that interacts least with lithium, given that his blood pressure still needs control. Which agent is most suitable for RJ, a 41 year old man with stable lithium levels and mild hypertension?", "options": {"A": "Amlodipine, a dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker that does not meaningfully change renal lithium clearance", "B": "Ramipril, because ACE inhibitors increase lithium excretion and lower the risk of toxicity in stable patients", "C": "Furosemide, because loop diuretics have no effect on lithium handling and are widely used with lithium", "D": "Losartan, because angiotensin receptor blockers are known to lower serum lithium concentrations over several weeks"}, "cluster_id": "CHS7", "cluster_text": "RJ is a 41 year old man with bipolar I disorder, stable for six years on lithium carbonate 300 mg three times daily. He also has hypertension. Last week his family doctor started hydrochlorothiazide 25 mg daily. Two months ago his serum lithium was 0.7 mmol/L, creatinine 88 umol/L. Today he reports hand tremor, nausea and unusual thirst. Allergies: none.", "cluster_size": 3, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Amlodipine, a dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker that does not meaningfully change renal lithium clearance): ACE inhibitors, ARBs and diuretics including loop agents can reduce lithium clearance and raise levels, so they need close monitoring or are avoided. Dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers have minimal effect on lithium levels and are a suitable agent."}, {"id": 1346, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A laboratory lowers the cutoff for a positive troponin result so that fewer myocardial infarctions are missed. A pharmacist on the cardiac unit is asked how this change will most likely affect the test's characteristics. Which pair of effects is expected?", "options": {"A": "Specificity increases while sensitivity decreases, producing more false negative results among the patients tested", "B": "Both sensitivity and specificity increase because the test now detects more of the disease", "C": "Sensitivity increases while specificity decreases, so more false positive results are produced", "D": "Sensitivity increases and specificity is unaffected, since the cutoff only changes how disease is defined"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Sensitivity increases while specificity decreases, so more false positive results are produced): Lowering the cutoff flags more people as positive, so more truly diseased patients are caught, which raises sensitivity. The same change also flags more healthy people, lowering specificity and creating more false positives. Sensitivity and specificity trade off against each other as the cutoff moves."}, {"id": 1347, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 3, "question": "FA is a 58 year old man prescribed itraconazole capsules 200 mg daily for 12 weeks for toenail onychomycosis after terbinafine was not tolerated. He takes omeprazole 40 mg daily for reflux. The pharmacist is concerned about reduced absorption. What is the best advice?", "options": {"A": "Take the capsules with the omeprazole dose, since proton pump inhibitors boost the absorption of itraconazole", "B": "Take the capsules right after a full meal, with an acidic drink such as cola", "C": "Double the capsule dose to 400 mg daily so that blood levels match the usual range", "D": "Take the capsules on an empty stomach at bedtime with an antacid to speed up dissolution"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Take the capsules right after a full meal, with an acidic drink such as cola): Itraconazole capsules need gastric acid to dissolve, so acid suppression can lower blood levels substantially. Taking them right after a full meal and with an acidic drink such as cola improves absorption in this setting. Antacids and omeprazole reduce rather than enhance absorption, and doubling the dose adds toxicity and interaction risk without a predictable benefit."}, {"id": 1348, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "After a workflow change, a pharmacy's dashboard shows average prescription wait time fell from 22 to 12 minutes. Over the same period, complaints about rushed counselling rose from 2 to 9 per month and two wrong-strength near misses were reported. How should the manager interpret these results?", "options": {"A": "Wait time should be dropped as an indicator because reducing it causes errors and rushed counselling", "B": "The change succeeded because wait time improved, and the other results are probably normal variation", "C": "The faster waits came with safety and quality costs, so the change should be reviewed", "D": "Only the complaints matter because patient satisfaction is the only measure of service quality"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The faster waits came with safety and quality costs, so the change should be reviewed): Improving one measure can create unintended effects elsewhere, which is why a balancing set of measures is monitored. Rising complaints and near misses suggest the faster process may be compromising counselling and safety, so the team should study the cause and adapt the change. Declaring success on one indicator or abandoning the indicator both overlook the trade off."}, {"id": 1349, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "After preparing intravenous methotrexate, a technician finishes the task and carries the empty vials, used syringes, gown, outer gloves and contaminated wipes toward the regular pharmacy garbage container. The pharmacist stops her. Which disposal method is appropriate for this waste?", "options": {"A": "Place items in regular garbage once empty vials have been rinsed three times with clean water", "B": "Flush residues from the syringes and vials down the sink, then discard containers in regular garbage", "C": "Place items in a regular biohazard bag and send it out with the general infectious waste", "D": "Place items in labelled hazardous drug waste containers, with needles in puncture-resistant hazardous sharps containers"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Place items in labelled hazardous drug waste containers, with needles in puncture-resistant hazardous sharps containers): Items that contacted a hazardous drug carry residue, so they go into designated, clearly labelled, leak-proof hazardous waste containers, and needles go into puncture-resistant sharps containers made for hazardous drugs. Rinsing vials or flushing residues spreads contamination into the environment and water supply. Hazardous waste is handled by a licensed disposal contractor."}, {"id": 1350, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A hospital wants to measure how often patients starting a new proton pump inhibitor develop Clostridioides difficile infection. It plans to follow 5,000 new users and 5,000 nonusers forward for one year. Which design is this, and which measure of association does it allow?", "options": {"A": "Cross-sectional study; the prevalence in each group permits calculation of an odds ratio for causation", "B": "Randomized trial; the allocation of users permits calculation of a hazard ratio free from confounding", "C": "Case-control study; the odds of exposure in each group permit calculation of an incidence rate difference", "D": "Prospective cohort study; incidence in each group permits calculation of a relative risk"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Prospective cohort study; incidence in each group permits calculation of a relative risk): Following exposed and unexposed groups forward to observe new events is a prospective cohort design. Because the number at risk is known, incidence can be measured in each group and compared as a relative risk. Without random assignment, confounding remains possible."}, {"id": 1351, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "PL, a 62 year old man, keeps all of his tablets and a tube of cream in the bathroom cabinet beside the shower because it is convenient. He asks the pharmacist whether this is fine. What is the most appropriate counselling about storing his medications?", "options": {"A": "Keep them in the bathroom cabinet because the closed door keeps them dry and dark", "B": "Store them in a cool, dry place away from the bathroom, in their original containers", "C": "Keep them in the kitchen cupboard above the stove where they remain warm and dry", "D": "Keep them in the bathroom but move them to the bottom shelf for cooler air"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Store them in a cool, dry place away from the bathroom, in their original containers): Heat and humidity speed the breakdown of many drugs, and a bathroom is warm and humid after every shower. Storage in a cool, dry room away from heat sources is best, and original containers protect the contents from moisture and light. Cupboards above the stove are also exposed to heat."}, {"id": 1352, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 1, "question": "BV is a 64 year old woman two weeks after an NSTEMI who started ticagrelor 90 mg twice daily. She phones the pharmacy because she feels mildly short of breath when climbing stairs. She has no chest pain, swelling, fever or wheeze, and a follow-up ECG was normal. What should the pharmacist advise?", "options": {"A": "Mild breathlessness is a common early ticagrelor effect, so continue it and report worsening", "B": "Take extra acetylsalicylic acid 325 mg daily, which counteracts the breathlessness caused by ticagrelor", "C": "Switch to prasugrel tomorrow without a gap, since this breathlessness occurs with every P2Y12 inhibitor", "D": "Stop ticagrelor today, as breathlessness signals heart failure caused directly by the new antiplatelet drug"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Mild breathlessness is a common early ticagrelor effect, so continue it and report worsening): Dyspnea occurs in roughly one in seven patients on ticagrelor, usually in the first weeks, and is generally mild and self-limited. Her normal ECG and absence of other symptoms make a cardiac cause unlikely, so stopping an essential antiplatelet is unwise. Higher acetylsalicylic acid doses would also reduce ticagrelor's benefit."}, {"id": 1353, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "A prescriber writes lorazepam 0.5 mg one tablet twice daily, quantity 28 tablets, with instructions to dispense in portions of seven days' supply. The pharmacist plans how to fill the prescription. How many tablets does the pharmacist dispense in each portion?", "options": {"A": "Fourteen tablets per portion, giving two portions that cover the full fourteen day supply", "B": "Four tablets per portion, giving seven portions across the prescribed fourteen day period of therapy", "C": "Seven tablets per portion, giving four portions over the full prescribed quantity of the prescription", "D": "Twenty eight tablets in the first portion, with the remainder dispensed at a later pharmacy visit"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Fourteen tablets per portion, giving two portions that cover the full fourteen day supply): Two tablets a day for seven days is 14 tablets per portion. The 28 tablets therefore make two portions that cover 14 days. The other answers confuse tablets per day with days per portion."}, {"id": 1354, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "VH is a 27 year old man with type 1 diabetes using basal-bolus insulin. He plans to drink several beers at a party tonight and asks about low blood sugar. He usually has no problems when he drinks only a little. What counselling should the pharmacist give?", "options": {"A": "Choose sweet mixed drinks, which supply carbohydrate and make lows after drinking unlikely tonight", "B": "Skip his bedtime basal insulin after drinking, since alcohol already lowers glucose enough overnight", "C": "Drink on an empty stomach so alcohol raises glucose, and check again in the morning", "D": "Eat while drinking, check glucose at bedtime, and watch for lows through the next day"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Eat while drinking, check glucose at bedtime, and watch for lows through the next day): Alcohol blocks glucose release from the liver, so hypoglycemia can occur during drinking and for many hours afterward, including the next day. Eating, testing at bedtime and staying alert for delayed lows reduce the risk. Skipping basal insulin invites ketoacidosis, and drinking without food or relying on sweet drinks does not protect him."}, {"id": 1355, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A guideline recommends a blood pressure regimen on the basis of trials that enrolled adults aged 18 to 55. The pharmacist is reviewing the plan for an 82 year old woman with frailty and recurrent falls. Which GRADE concept best describes the main limitation in applying the recommendation to her?", "options": {"A": "Indirectness, because the trial population differs from the patient and lowers certainty for this application", "B": "Publication bias, because trials with younger participants are more often published than other trials", "C": "Imprecision, because the trial confidence intervals were wide and probably included both benefit and harm", "D": "Inconsistency, because the trials gave different results in different age groups and cannot be combined easily"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Indirectness, because the trial population differs from the patient and lowers certainty for this application): Indirectness arises when the studied population, intervention or outcome differs from the clinical question. A woman aged 82 with frailty differs substantially from trial participants aged 18 to 55, so certainty about benefit and harm is lower for her. The other domains concern the trials themselves, not their fit to this patient."}, {"id": 1356, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 7, "question": "RB is an 82 year old man taking digoxin 0.25 mg daily for atrial fibrillation. His creatinine clearance has fallen to 28 mL/min, and he reports nausea and yellow vision. His digoxin level is 2.4 nmol/L (target 0.6 to 1.2 nmol/L). What should the pharmacist recommend?", "options": {"A": "Hold digoxin, check an ECG and potassium, and restart later at a lower dose", "B": "Increase to 0.5 mg daily, since renal impairment reduces digoxin's effect on heart rate", "C": "Give potassium chloride supplements and keep the dose, because low potassium alone causes his symptoms", "D": "Continue 0.25 mg daily and recheck the level in a month, since vision changes are common"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Hold digoxin, check an ECG and potassium, and restart later at a lower dose): Digoxin is cleared by the kidney, so falling clearance has led to accumulation and toxicity, shown by the high level and the nausea and visual changes. The drug should be held while an ECG and electrolytes are checked, then resumed at a lower dose guided by renal function. Raising the dose or continuing unchanged would worsen toxicity."}, {"id": 1357, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 1, "question": "VW is a 70 year old man with coronary disease on simvastatin 40 mg nightly. He is hospitalized for recurrent atrial fibrillation and his cardiologist starts amiodarone 200 mg daily. What is the best recommendation about the statin? His LDL is at target.", "options": {"A": "stop all lipid therapy for the duration, since statins cannot be taken with antiarrhythmics", "B": "increase simvastatin to 80 mg, since amiodarone reduces its absorption from the gut", "C": "limit simvastatin to 20 mg daily or change to another statin, since amiodarone raises simvastatin levels", "D": "keep simvastatin 40 mg unchanged, since amiodarone induces its clearance and lowers statin exposure"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (limit simvastatin to 20 mg daily or change to another statin, since amiodarone raises simvastatin levels): Amiodarone inhibits CYP3A4 and P-glycoprotein, raising simvastatin concentrations, and the simvastatin dose is capped at 20 mg daily with it. Another statin with less interaction potential is a reasonable alternative."}, {"id": 1358, "domain": "communication_and_collaboration", "chapter": 11, "question": "A man collecting a new antiretroviral prescription hesitates to speak when other customers queue close behind him at the counter. The pharmacist needs to counsel him about the dosing, interactions and adherence. Which action best protects his privacy while still allowing complete counselling?", "options": {"A": "Counsel at the counter but turn the computer screen away and avoid naming the medication", "B": "Lower the voice and continue counselling at the counter while customers wait behind him", "C": "Offer to move to the private counselling room and continue the discussion there", "D": "Shorten the counselling to the dose instructions and provide written material to read at home"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Offer to move to the private counselling room and continue the discussion there): Health information is confidential under PIPEDA and provincial privacy law, and a private space lets the pharmacist counsel fully and openly. Lowering the voice or omitting the drug name still leaves the conversation exposed or incomplete."}, {"id": 1359, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "A 60 year old man takes oxycodone 20 mg daily for chronic back pain. His physician has also prescribed lorazepam 1 mg twice daily for anxiety. The pharmacist reviews the new prescription at the counter. What is the most important concern to raise?", "options": {"A": "The combination is safe because the drugs act on different receptors and do not add up", "B": "The combination mainly raises blood pressure, so only monthly blood pressure and pulse checks are needed", "C": "The combination makes both drugs less effective, so each dose should be raised to control symptoms", "D": "Combining them raises the risk of profound sedation and respiratory depression, so call the prescriber"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Combining them raises the risk of profound sedation and respiratory depression, so call the prescriber): Both drug classes depress the central nervous system, and concurrent use is associated with overdose death and respiratory depression, so it is generally avoided or used with great caution. The effects add up rather than cancel, and the main danger is not hypertension."}, {"id": 1360, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 3, "question": "A 60 year old man phones the pharmacy with a severe sore throat for 3 days, drooling, a muffled voice and difficulty opening his mouth. He is unable to swallow his saliva and has a fever of 39.2 C. He asks which over-the-counter product to try. What is the best advice?", "options": {"A": "Provide a delayed prescription of amoxicillin and advise him to fill it tomorrow morning if worse", "B": "Recommend throat lozenges and salt water gargles, with a recheck in 3 days at the pharmacy", "C": "Send him to the emergency department now, as these signs suggest a deep neck infection", "D": "Suggest ibuprofen and fluids, and arrange a routine clinic visit sometime within the week"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Send him to the emergency department now, as these signs suggest a deep neck infection): Drooling, trismus, a muffled voice and inability to swallow saliva suggest a peritonsillar abscess, epiglottitis or deep neck infection that can compromise the airway. Home measures and delayed prescriptions waste critical time. Emergency assessment is needed."}, {"id": 1361, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "A patient with asthma has an empty pressurized metered dose inhaler and a canister that is still partly full. She asks whether to put them in the recycling box at the curb. What should the pharmacist advise about the disposal of inhalers?", "options": {"A": "Place them in curbside recycling after removing the plastic holder from the metal canister", "B": "Puncture the canister to release the pressure and put it in household garbage", "C": "Return the inhalers to the pharmacy, since canisters and remaining drug need special handling", "D": "Rinse the inhaler and dispose of it with the paper recycling to reduce waste"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Return the inhalers to the pharmacy, since canisters and remaining drug need special handling): Pressurized canisters contain propellant and residual drug and can burst or pollute if mishandled, so they should be returned to the pharmacy take-back program. Puncturing them is hazardous, and household recycling streams are not set up for medication containers of this type."}, {"id": 1362, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "IG is a 35 year old woman who has difficulty swallowing capsules and takes venlafaxine extended-release capsules 150 mg once daily for anxiety. A pharmacy assistant asks the pharmacist whether she may open the capsule to make it easier to take. What is the correct information to give?", "options": {"A": "The capsule may be opened and half taken in the morning and half at bedtime tonight", "B": "The capsule may be opened and the beads crushed to speed absorption before swallowing", "C": "The capsule must not be opened, because the beads dissolve quickly when exposed to the air", "D": "The capsule may be opened and the contents sprinkled on applesauce, then swallowed without chewing"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The capsule may be opened and the contents sprinkled on applesauce, then swallowed without chewing): Venlafaxine extended-release capsules contain coated beads that can be sprinkled on a small amount of applesauce and swallowed without chewing. Crushing the beads would destroy the controlled release. Splitting the contents between two times of day alters the dosing."}, {"id": 1363, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 3, "question": "SH is a 70 year old woman taking warfarin 3 mg daily for atrial fibrillation, with a stable INR of 2.5. She asks for a three day miconazole vaginal ovule for typical yeast symptoms, having used one before without trouble. How should the pharmacist respond?", "options": {"A": "Sell it without comment, since vaginal products are absorbed too little to affect warfarin therapy", "B": "Advise holding warfarin for three days while the ovule is used, then restarting the usual dose", "C": "Warn that miconazole may raise her INR, and arrange an INR check this week", "D": "Recommend oral fluconazole 150 mg instead, since it has no recognized interaction with warfarin therapy"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Warn that miconazole may raise her INR, and arrange an INR check this week): Miconazole, even when applied vaginally, can be absorbed enough to inhibit CYP2C9 and raise the INR, and bleeding cases have been reported. A short INR recheck lets the dose be adjusted. Fluconazole interacts with warfarin even more strongly, and stopping warfarin leaves her unprotected against stroke."}, {"id": 1364, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 7, "question": "His mother asks which warning signs would mean DW should be taken to an emergency department rather than managed at home with fluids and acetaminophen. Which sign should the pharmacist identify as the most urgent for a 14 month old with bronchiolitis?", "options": {"A": "A temperature that stays above 38 C for the first two days of the illness", "B": "Refusing solid foods while still accepting small sips of fluid frequently between his coughs", "C": "A cough that is worse at night and produces clear nasal drainage", "D": "Chest wall indrawing with grunting, or pauses in his breathing"}, "cluster_id": "CHS8", "cluster_text": "DW is a 14 month old boy weighing 11 kg. He has had a runny nose and cough for three days and now has a temperature of 38.9 C with fast, noisy breathing. He attends daycare where other children have bronchiolitis. He is drinking about half his usual fluids but still has wet diapers, and he is alert and playful between coughs. He has no allergies and takes no regular medications. His mother has acetaminophen suspension 160 mg per 5 mL.", "cluster_size": 3, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Chest wall indrawing with grunting, or pauses in his breathing): Indrawing of the chest wall, grunting and pauses in breathing signal respiratory distress and impending failure, which need emergency care. Reduced appetite, a fever lasting a couple of days and a nighttime cough are common in bronchiolitis and can be managed at home with monitoring. Fluid intake and wet diapers are the key hydration checks."}, {"id": 1365, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 7, "question": "DW weighs 11 kg and has a fever of 38.9 C. His mother asks for the volume of acetaminophen suspension that gives a dose closest to 15 mg/kg. Which volume of the 160 mg per 5 mL product should the pharmacist recommend for a single dose today?", "options": {"A": "About 5 mL, which delivers 160 mg and is close to 15 mg/kg", "B": "About 10 mL, which delivers 320 mg and doubles the intended dose", "C": "About 2.5 mL, which delivers 80 mg and falls below the intended dose", "D": "About 7.5 mL, which delivers 240 mg and exceeds the intended dose"}, "cluster_id": "CHS8", "cluster_text": "DW is a 14 month old boy weighing 11 kg. He has had a runny nose and cough for three days and now has a temperature of 38.9 C with fast, noisy breathing. He attends daycare where other children have bronchiolitis. He is drinking about half his usual fluids but still has wet diapers, and he is alert and playful between coughs. He has no allergies and takes no regular medications. His mother has acetaminophen suspension 160 mg per 5 mL.", "cluster_size": 3, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (About 5 mL, which delivers 160 mg and is close to 15 mg/kg): A 15 mg/kg dose for 11 kg is 165 mg, which is 5.2 mL of a 32 mg per mL suspension. Giving 5 mL provides 160 mg, or about 14.5 mg/kg, which is accurate and practical to measure. The other volumes give about 7, 22 and 29 mg/kg."}, {"id": 1366, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 7, "question": "DW's mother also asks for a cough syrup and a decongestant from the shelf to help his breathing and sleep tonight, since he is 14 months old and has a fever with a cough. What should the pharmacist recommend instead for his congestion and cough?", "options": {"A": "An inhaled salbutamol trial every four hours at home, since it helps most infants with bronchiolitis", "B": "A guaifenesin liquid measured with a syringe, given four times daily to loosen chest secretions", "C": "Saline drops with gentle nasal suctioning before feeds and sleep, plus fluids", "D": "A dextromethorphan syrup at half the adult dose, with a pseudoephedrine drop to relieve congestion"}, "cluster_id": "CHS8", "cluster_text": "DW is a 14 month old boy weighing 11 kg. He has had a runny nose and cough for three days and now has a temperature of 38.9 C with fast, noisy breathing. He attends daycare where other children have bronchiolitis. He is drinking about half his usual fluids but still has wet diapers, and he is alert and playful between coughs. He has no allergies and takes no regular medications. His mother has acetaminophen suspension 160 mg per 5 mL.", "cluster_size": 3, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Saline drops with gentle nasal suctioning before feeds and sleep, plus fluids): Saline drops with suction clear the nose so he can feed and sleep, and fluids support hydration. Health Canada advises against cough and cold products in children under six, and guaifenesin is not recommended either. Bronchodilators have not been shown to help most infants with bronchiolitis."}, {"id": 1367, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "In the intensive care unit, a 29 year old patient with cerebral edema is receiving hypertonic saline. The pharmacist is asked how much sodium chloride is contained in one 250 mL bag labelled 3% w/v before it is released from the central pharmacy. What is the correct content?", "options": {"A": "30 g of sodium chloride, since 3% w/v equals 12 g per 100 mL", "B": "0.75 g of sodium chloride, since 3% w/v equals 0.3 g per 100 mL", "C": "7.5 g of sodium chloride, since 3% w/v equals 3 g per 100 mL", "D": "75 g of sodium chloride, since 3% w/v equals 30 g per 100 mL"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (7.5 g of sodium chloride, since 3% w/v equals 3 g per 100 mL): A 3% w/v solution has 3 g in each 100 mL, so 250 mL holds 2.5 x 3 = 7.5 g. That is about 128 mmol of sodium. A tenfold error here would give a dangerous hypertonic load."}, {"id": 1368, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "DK is a 26 year old man with type 1 diabetes who wears a continuous glucose monitor. Before supper his sensor reads 6.5 mmol/L with a double downward arrow, and he plans to eat 50 g of carbohydrate with a standard lispro bolus. What approach is most sensible for the bolus?", "options": {"A": "Add a correction bolus on top of the meal dose to prepare for the carbohydrate rise", "B": "Take the full standard bolus, since arrows describe trends and not insulin needs", "C": "Treat with 15 g carbohydrate and skip the bolus altogether, because 6.5 mmol/L counts as low", "D": "Reduce the bolus or delay it until glucose stabilizes, and confirm with a fingerstick"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Reduce the bolus or delay it until glucose stabilizes, and confirm with a fingerstick): A double downward arrow means glucose is falling quickly, so a full bolus given now risks a low before the meal is absorbed. Reducing or delaying the bolus allows for the trend, and a fingerstick confirms the value. Adding a correction would compound the risk, while skipping the bolus altogether would leave the meal uncovered."}, {"id": 1369, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 6, "question": "BJ is a 38 year old woman who had a copper T380A intrauterine device inserted seven years ago. She has no problems and asks whether she needs to replace it soon or if it still protects her from pregnancy. The pharmacist should advise that:", "options": {"A": "All intrauterine devices must be replaced after five years to remain contraceptive", "B": "It remains effective, since copper T380A devices provide contraception for up to ten years", "C": "It has lost effectiveness because copper ions are depleted after three years", "D": "It is effective only if she adds condoms during the last two years of use"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (It remains effective, since copper T380A devices provide contraception for up to ten years): The copper T380A device is approved for up to ten years of use and remains effective throughout. Five years is the labelled duration for some older hormonal systems, not for this device. Copper ions are not depleted in a way that reduces effect before ten years, and backup is not needed."}, {"id": 1370, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "DL, 34, takes levothyroxine 100 mcg daily for hypothyroidism and is planning a pregnancy. Her drug plan now requires a different interchangeable generic brand starting next month. She asks the pharmacist what she should do after the change. The pharmacist's best advice is to:", "options": {"A": "take the new tablets with breakfast and coffee to compensate for possible lower absorption", "B": "report new symptoms and have TSH rechecked about six to eight weeks later", "C": "double the first week's dose so that blood levels settle before the next planned laboratory test", "D": "expect no change and skip any thyroid testing because interchangeable products are identical in every patient"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (report new symptoms and have TSH rechecked about six to eight weeks later): Levothyroxine has a narrow therapeutic range, and small exposure changes can shift TSH, which matters before and during pregnancy. A TSH check about six to eight weeks after a product change is a reasonable safeguard. Changing the dose or timing without direction is not appropriate."}, {"id": 1371, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 1, "question": "AN is an 80 year old woman who has had a digoxin level drawn two hours after her morning dose. The result is 2.0 nmol/L, and she has no symptoms of toxicity. How should the pharmacist advise the prescriber to interpret this result?", "options": {"A": "treat it as accurate, since digoxin has already distributed by two hours after oral dosing", "B": "double the dose, since a level drawn early understates the real concentration in the blood", "C": "treat it as true toxicity, since any value above 1.2 nmol/L requires an immediate dose reduction", "D": "interpret it cautiously, because levels taken under six hours after a dose overestimate steady state"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (interpret it cautiously, because levels taken under six hours after a dose overestimate steady state): Digoxin takes several hours to distribute into tissues, so a sample drawn soon after a dose is falsely high. Levels should be drawn at least six to eight hours after the dose, or just before the next one, and clinical status guides decisions."}, {"id": 1372, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 6, "question": "NA is a 71 year old man with osteoarthritis and a gastric ulcer that bled two years ago. He needs a daily NSAID for knee pain, has stable hypertension and no coronary disease. Which strategy lowers his risk of recurrent upper gastrointestinal bleeding the most?", "options": {"A": "Naproxen taken with food, which gives sufficient protection for a patient with prior bleeding", "B": "Ibuprofen at the lowest dose with an H2 receptor antagonist at bedtime for protection", "C": "Enteric coated diclofenac, since the coating prevents gastric injury throughout systemic exposure", "D": "A COX-2 selective NSAID such as celecoxib combined with a daily PPI"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A COX-2 selective NSAID such as celecoxib combined with a daily PPI): For patients with a prior ulcer bleed who still need an NSAID, the lowest risk strategy is a COX-2 selective agent plus a PPI. Enteric coating does not prevent injury caused by systemic prostaglandin inhibition, and food or a bedtime H2 blocker gives incomplete protection."}, {"id": 1373, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 4, "question": "MJ is a 62 year old woman with psoriatic arthritis who will start an interleukin-17 inhibitor in three weeks. She asks the pharmacist whether she can get the recombinant zoster vaccine before treatment starts. She is in good health today with no fever. Which response is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Yes, the recombinant vaccine is non-live and ideally given before immunosuppression, in two doses", "B": "Yes, but only after six months on the biologic, once her immune response has stabilized", "C": "No, all zoster vaccines contain live virus and are contraindicated for anyone planning a biologic", "D": "No, shingles vaccination must wait until the biologic course is complete and she is off treatment"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Yes, the recombinant vaccine is non-live and ideally given before immunosuppression, in two doses): The recombinant zoster vaccine is not a live vaccine, so it can be given to people on or about to start immunosuppressive therapy, and responses are best before treatment begins. The schedule has two doses. Waiting for months or until after treatment leaves her at higher risk of shingles."}, {"id": 1374, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "NA is a staff pharmacist at a chain store and assumes the employer's commercial liability policy protects everything. At renewal, NA is asked to confirm personal professional liability coverage. The college's by-laws require it for all practising members. What is the most appropriate response by NA?", "options": {"A": "Skip the question, because liability insurance is the responsibility of the pharmacy owner or licensed manager", "B": "Confirm coverage by referring to the employer's policy, since employees are insured under the corporate plan", "C": "Arrange personal coverage, usually through the provincial pharmacists' association, and keep proof for the college", "D": "Purchase coverage only for work outside the pharmacy, such as vaccination clinics and home visits"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Arrange personal coverage, usually through the provincial pharmacists' association, and keep proof for the college): When the regulator requires each registrant to hold liability coverage, an employer's policy may not meet the requirement or may not cover the pharmacist personally. Personal coverage through the pharmacists' association is the usual route, and the proof must be available if the college asks."}, {"id": 1375, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "Pharmacy manager LB wants a routine so the dispensary never misses a drug recall. One technician suggests relying on patients to mention recalls, while another proposes using the wholesaler's emails only. Which approach best meets professional expectations for a pharmacy in Canada?", "options": {"A": "Review recalls only during annual inventory counts, when each product is already handled and examined", "B": "Rely on wholesaler emails alone, since wholesalers carry the legal duty to inform every pharmacy", "C": "Check the Health Canada recalls database regularly and also act on wholesaler notices", "D": "Wait for the manufacturer to mail a printed notice to the pharmacy before removing affected stock"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Check the Health Canada recalls database regularly and also act on wholesaler notices): A pharmacy should combine sources: the Health Canada recalls and safety alerts listing and the notices sent by suppliers. A single channel can fail, and waiting for mail or an annual count delays removal of stock that may harm patients."}, {"id": 1376, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "A new order for ramipril 5 mg daily arrives for HA, 69, who has hypertension and no kidney disease. The profile shows losartan 100 mg daily dispensed three weeks ago from the same practice group, and the alert reads therapeutic duplication. What is the most appropriate action?", "options": {"A": "Dispense both and ask HA to take ramipril in the morning and losartan at night", "B": "Contact the prescriber, since combining an ACE inhibitor and an ARB adds risk without benefit", "C": "Dispense ramipril and tell HA to stop losartan by himself once the new supply is open", "D": "Dispense both after confirming HA's blood pressure is high on a single reading at the counter"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Contact the prescriber, since combining an ACE inhibitor and an ARB adds risk without benefit): Dual blockade of the renin-angiotensin system increases hyperkalemia, hypotension and kidney injury without proven outcome benefit in most patients. The prescriber may have intended a switch or made an entry error, so clarification is needed before dispensing."}, {"id": 1377, "domain": "communication_and_collaboration", "chapter": 11, "question": "A pharmacist asks a man with COPD who smokes whether he is thinking about quitting. He replies that he has set a quit date next week and has bought nicotine patches. According to the stages of change model, in which stage is he?", "options": {"A": "Preparation, because he has a plan and has taken steps toward quitting", "B": "Contemplation, because he is weighing the advantages and disadvantages of quitting", "C": "Action, because he has already purchased nicotine patches for his attempt", "D": "Maintenance, because he has formed firm intentions about stopping smoking"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Preparation, because he has a plan and has taken steps toward quitting): Preparation involves intending to act soon and taking small steps such as setting a date. Contemplation lacks a plan, action means the behaviour has already changed, and maintenance means the change has been sustained for months."}, {"id": 1378, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "A resident wants to give a renally cleared drug every 24 hours instead of every 12 hours for convenience in PH, an 80 year old woman, keeping the same 100 mg dose. The drug has a half-life of 12 hours. How would the average steady-state concentration change?", "options": {"A": "It would be unchanged, because the half-life of 12 hours sets the average concentration", "B": "It would fall to a quarter, because both the interval and the dose affect the concentration", "C": "It would double, because the longer interval allows more drug to accumulate between the doses", "D": "It would be roughly halved, because the daily dose is reduced to half the original"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (It would be roughly halved, because the daily dose is reduced to half the original): The average steady-state concentration is proportional to dose divided by interval. Moving from 100 mg every 12 hours to 100 mg every 24 hours halves the daily dose, so the average concentration also halves and peak to trough variation widens."}, {"id": 1379, "domain": "leadership_and_stewardship", "chapter": 11, "question": "FL is 29 years old and 30 weeks pregnant. She asks for a pertussis containing vaccine at the pharmacy and says her last tetanus booster was four years ago. She has no fever and no history of vaccine reactions. What should the pharmacist advise?", "options": {"A": "Wait until after delivery, since vaccines containing pertussis are avoided during the third trimester", "B": "Give Tdap only after a pertussis exposure, since routine vaccination is not advised in pregnancy", "C": "Give tetanus diphtheria vaccine alone now, because pertussis components are not recommended before delivery", "D": "Give Tdap now, since NACI advises it in every pregnancy, at 27 to 32 weeks"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Give Tdap now, since NACI advises it in every pregnancy, at 27 to 32 weeks): NACI recommends a dose of Tdap in every pregnancy, ideally at 27 to 32 weeks, regardless of previous vaccination, because maternal antibodies cross the placenta and protect newborns who are too young to be vaccinated. Waiting until after delivery loses that protection. Tdap is not limited to pertussis exposure."}, {"id": 1380, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "A pharmacy manager audits 60 randomly chosen narcotic prescriptions and finds that 9 lack a documented patient identification check. After a process change, a repeat audit of another 60 prescriptions finds 3 lacking documentation. By how many percentage points did the noncompliance rate fall?", "options": {"A": "12 percentage points, from 20% to 8% noncompliance after the process change was made", "B": "10 percentage points, because noncompliance fell from 15% in the first audit to 5%", "C": "6 percentage points, from 9% to 3% noncompliance in the sampled prescriptions", "D": "15 percentage points, since the first audit alone found 15% noncompliance in the sample"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (10 percentage points, because noncompliance fell from 15% in the first audit to 5%): The initial audit found 9 of 60, or 15%, without documentation, and the repeat audit found 3 of 60, or 5%. The absolute change is 15% minus 5%, which is 10 percentage points. Reporting the counts as percentages, or using a wrong baseline, gives different figures."}, {"id": 1381, "domain": "communication_and_collaboration", "chapter": 11, "question": "A patient with glaucoma has been prescribed latanoprost 0.005% and timolol 0.5% eye drops for the same eye, both once daily. The pharmacist demonstrates how to use them. Which instruction about timing and technique is correct for this regimen at home?", "options": {"A": "Place the drop onto the eyelashes so it runs in gently over the cornea", "B": "Instill both drops together within seconds so that they mix well on the surface", "C": "Squeeze the bottle firmly and blink repeatedly to spread each drop across the tear film", "D": "Wait about five minutes between drops and avoid touching the dropper tip to the eye"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Wait about five minutes between drops and avoid touching the dropper tip to the eye): Different eye drops need about five minutes between them so that the second does not wash out the first. The head is tilted back, the drop goes into the lower conjunctival sac, and the dropper tip must not touch the eye to prevent contamination."}, {"id": 1382, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 4, "question": "BN is a 36 year old woman with nasal congestion who bought a saline irrigation bottle with salt packets and asks what water to use. Tap water is available at home, and a household filter jug sits on her counter. The pharmacist should advise using:", "options": {"A": "water from a household filter jug, since filtration removes the organisms of concern", "B": "distilled water, or tap water that was boiled then cooled, mixed with the saline packet", "C": "cold tap water straight from the faucet, since the saline kills any organisms present", "D": "warm tap water after letting it run for a minute, then adding the saline packet"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (distilled water, or tap water that was boiled then cooled, mixed with the saline packet): Untreated tap water can carry organisms such as amoeba that are harmless when swallowed but dangerous in the nose, so irrigation uses distilled water or water that was boiled and cooled. Saline does not disinfect the water, and household filter jugs are not designed to remove these organisms."}, {"id": 1383, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 7, "question": "A grandmother who keeps her blood pressure and diabetes pills in her handbag will care for her 2 year old grandson this weekend. She asks the pharmacist how to prevent an accidental poisoning during the visit. Which advice is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Keep all medicines, including those in bags, locked or stored high out of reach", "B": "Keep only the diabetes pills out of reach, since blood pressure drugs are less harmful", "C": "Keep the pills in child resistant vials on a low shelf, since the caps block entry", "D": "Keep the pills in a weekly organizer on the counter, since it hides the labels"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Keep all medicines, including those in bags, locked or stored high out of reach): Handbags and purses are a common source of toddler poisonings, and medicines should be locked or stored high regardless of the container. Child resistant caps slow children but are not child proof, and organizers are often easy to open. Many adult medicines, including blood pressure drugs, can be dangerous to a toddler."}, {"id": 1384, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 7, "question": "A pharmacist reviews clarithromycin 250 mg twice daily prescribed for a 7 year old boy weighing 24 kg with pneumonia. The reference states 15 mg/kg/day divided every 12 hours, with a maximum of 1 g daily. What should the pharmacist conclude about this prescription?", "options": {"A": "The dose is acceptable, since 500 mg daily remains well below the 1 g daily maximum", "B": "The dose is too low, since 15 mg/kg/day should be given with each single dose", "C": "The dose of 500 mg daily exceeds the 360 mg calculated, so contact the prescriber", "D": "The dose is acceptable, since children over six years receive the usual adult dose"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The dose of 500 mg daily exceeds the 360 mg calculated, so contact the prescriber): The weight based dose is 15 x 24 = 360 mg per day, or 180 mg every 12 hours, while the prescription provides 500 mg daily. Staying under the 1 g ceiling does not make a dose correct when it exceeds the weight based range. The prescriber should be contacted to confirm or adjust."}, {"id": 1385, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "A pharmacy technician preparing the monthly audit asks which federal regulation governs record keeping and refills for a prescription for temazepam 15 mg at bedtime. The patient is a 77 year old woman in a retirement home. What should the pharmacist tell the technician?", "options": {"A": "Temazepam is a benzodiazepine, so it falls under the Benzodiazepines and Other Targeted Substances Regulations", "B": "Temazepam is a controlled drug in Part G of the Food and Drug Regulations, like amphetamines", "C": "Temazepam is a narcotic, so the Narcotic Control Regulations apply, including the ban on refills", "D": "Temazepam is an unscheduled prescription drug, so only the provincial pharmacy act applies to its refills"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Temazepam is a benzodiazepine, so it falls under the Benzodiazepines and Other Targeted Substances Regulations): Benzodiazepines, including temazepam, are targeted substances regulated under the Benzodiazepines and Other Targeted Substances Regulations, which set record and refill requirements. Narcotics fall under the Narcotic Control Regulations, and Part G drugs such as amphetamines and methylphenidate are a separate class. Provincial law applies as well, but it does not replace the federal rules for these drugs."}, {"id": 1386, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "While discarding a syringe after preparing a cytotoxic infusion, a pharmacist (TG) splashes a few drops of drug solution onto the bare skin of her forearm between the glove cuff and the gown sleeve. She feels no burning. A colleague suggests she simply finish her shift. What should happen first?", "options": {"A": "Wipe the skin with 70% isopropyl alcohol, continue working and record the incident at shift end", "B": "Wait an hour to see whether symptoms develop before washing, so the exposure is clearly documented", "C": "Apply antiseptic cream to the area and monitor for redness over the next two days", "D": "Remove contaminated clothing and gloves, wash the skin with soap and water, then report the exposure"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Remove contaminated clothing and gloves, wash the skin with soap and water, then report the exposure): Skin exposure to a cytotoxic drug requires immediate decontamination: remove affected clothing and gloves and wash the skin with soap and water, then seek assessment and report the incident for documentation. Alcohol and creams do not remove the drug and may increase absorption. The absence of burning does not mean there is no absorption, so waiting is unsafe."}, {"id": 1387, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 7, "question": "Based on RT's weight of 92 kg, the 60 hours that have passed since unprotected intercourse on cycle day 14, and her plan to start a combined oral contraceptive pill afterward, which oral emergency contraceptive is most appropriate at this visit, given that she declines a copper IUD?", "options": {"A": "Levonorgestrel 3 mg once, doubled for weight, which is the standard approved Canadian dose", "B": "Levonorgestrel 1.5 mg once, since it is more effective than ulipristal beyond seventy two hours", "C": "Ulipristal acetate 30 mg once, which keeps better efficacy than levonorgestrel at her weight", "D": "The Yuzpe regimen of combined pills, since it is preferred in women with higher body weight"}, "cluster_id": "CHS9", "cluster_text": "RT, 24, requests emergency contraception 60 hours after unprotected intercourse. Her weight is 92 kg (BMI 33). Her last menstrual period began 14 days ago and her cycle is usually 28 days. She has no medical conditions, allergies or regular medications. She has not vomited and wants to start a combined oral contraceptive pill afterward.", "cluster_size": 3, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Ulipristal acetate 30 mg once, which keeps better efficacy than levonorgestrel at her weight): Levonorgestrel emergency contraception is less effective at a body weight above about 70 kg, and she is also at 60 hours. Ulipristal retains efficacy through five days and is less affected by weight. Doubling levonorgestrel is not an approved Canadian dose, and the Yuzpe regimen is less effective than either single agent. Her combined pill should be delayed for several days after ulipristal, with a barrier method used meanwhile."}, {"id": 1388, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 7, "question": "RT plans to start her combined oral contraceptive after taking ulipristal today for emergency contraception, and she asks when to begin the pill. What should the pharmacist advise about when to start the pill and what protection she needs in the meantime?", "options": {"A": "Start the pill today with the ulipristal, and no backup method is needed", "B": "Take the pill with ulipristal today, then rely on the pill from the second day", "C": "Start the pill at her next period, with no other protection needed before then", "D": "Wait five days before starting it, and use condoms until seven days after starting"}, "cluster_id": "CHS9", "cluster_text": "RT, 24, requests emergency contraception 60 hours after unprotected intercourse. Her weight is 92 kg (BMI 33). Her last menstrual period began 14 days ago and her cycle is usually 28 days. She has no medical conditions, allergies or regular medications. She has not vomited and wants to start a combined oral contraceptive pill afterward.", "cluster_size": 3, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Wait five days before starting it, and use condoms until seven days after starting): Hormonal contraceptives may reduce the effect of ulipristal because both act on the progesterone receptor, so the pill is delayed for five days and a barrier method is used until the pill has been taken for seven days. Starting immediately weakens emergency contraception."}, {"id": 1389, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 7, "question": "RT, who took emergency contraception 60 hours after unprotected intercourse, asks how she will know whether it worked and when she should take a pregnancy test. Her cycle is usually 28 days and her last period began 14 days ago. Which advice should the pharmacist give about follow up?", "options": {"A": "Take a test if her next period is more than seven days late or unusually light", "B": "No test is needed, because emergency contraception reliably prevents every pregnancy that might occur", "C": "Take a test within one hour, since hormone levels are accurate immediately after intercourse", "D": "Take a test if no period occurs within three months, since sooner results are not reliable"}, "cluster_id": "CHS9", "cluster_text": "RT, 24, requests emergency contraception 60 hours after unprotected intercourse. Her weight is 92 kg (BMI 33). Her last menstrual period began 14 days ago and her cycle is usually 28 days. She has no medical conditions, allergies or regular medications. She has not vomited and wants to start a combined oral contraceptive pill afterward.", "cluster_size": 3, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Take a test if her next period is more than seven days late or unusually light): No emergency contraceptive is fully effective. A period more than a week late or unusually light and short may indicate pregnancy and a test is advised, with medical review if positive. Testing within hours is not reliable."}, {"id": 1390, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "A pharmacist is explaining to a pharmacy student how drug shortages are reported in Canada. The student asks who has a legal obligation to report an anticipated or actual shortage of a prescription drug to Health Canada. Who is required to report?", "options": {"A": "Provincial colleges of pharmacists, who must submit a shortage report for each unavailable drug", "B": "Community pharmacists, who must report each unfilled prescription to Health Canada within five days", "C": "Drug manufacturers, who must post shortage reports on the Drug Shortages Canada website", "D": "Patients, who must file a report with Health Canada before the pharmacy can order an alternative"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Drug manufacturers, who must post shortage reports on the Drug Shortages Canada website): Under federal regulations, manufacturers are required to report anticipated and actual shortages, and the reports are published on Drug Shortages Canada. Pharmacists, patients and colleges may use that site, but they have no duty to file individual shortage reports."}, {"id": 1391, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 4, "question": "PT is a 57 year old woman with well controlled asthma on low dose budesonide, and her last exacerbation was three years ago. Her ophthalmologist has just started timolol 0.5% eye drops twice daily for glaucoma. She reports no respiratory symptoms yet. What should the pharmacist do?", "options": {"A": "Continue the timolol and double her budesonide dose as preventive protection against possible bronchospasm", "B": "Contact the ophthalmologist to suggest an alternative, since timolol can trigger bronchospasm even as eye drops", "C": "Counsel her that eye drops are not absorbed into the blood and cannot affect the lungs", "D": "Advise her to apply the drops only at night, which prevents any lung effect"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Contact the ophthalmologist to suggest an alternative, since timolol can trigger bronchospasm even as eye drops): Timolol is a nonselective beta blocker, and enough is absorbed through the nasolacrimal mucosa to cause bronchospasm in asthma, sometimes fatally. Another class of glaucoma drops should be requested. Raising the inhaled steroid or changing the timing does not block the beta receptor effect."}, {"id": 1392, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 4, "question": "A woman who is 20 weeks pregnant has been diagnosed with scabies and asks the pharmacist which treatment is both safe and effective for her. She has no known drug allergies and no other health problems. What is the most appropriate recommendation?", "options": {"A": "Oral ivermectin 200 mcg/kg as a single dose, since it is the preferred option in pregnancy", "B": "A topical corticosteroid alone daily until burrows disappear, since it avoids exposing the fetus to antiparasitics", "C": "Lindane 1% lotion left on for eight hours, since it is safe in the second trimester", "D": "Permethrin 5% cream applied from the neck down, considered the preferred treatment during pregnancy"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Permethrin 5% cream applied from the neck down, considered the preferred treatment during pregnancy): Topical permethrin has minimal systemic absorption and the most reassuring safety data in pregnancy. Oral ivermectin is generally avoided in pregnancy because of limited data. Lindane is neurotoxic and should not be used, and a corticosteroid does not kill mites."}, {"id": 1393, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "TW, a pharmacy technician, tells the pharmacist that the vaccine refrigerator reading was minus 2 degrees Celsius on the morning check, and the stock includes hepatitis B vaccine. She asks why freezing matters since the vaccines look normal. What is the best explanation?", "options": {"A": "Freezing is a problem only if the vaccine has been frozen for longer than one week", "B": "Freezing affects only live vaccines, so adjuvanted vaccines like hepatitis B are not harmed", "C": "Freezing permanently damages the aluminum adjuvant in vaccines such as hepatitis B, reducing potency", "D": "Freezing only changes the colour of the liquid and the vaccine regains potency after thawing"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Freezing permanently damages the aluminum adjuvant in vaccines such as hepatitis B, reducing potency): Aluminum adjuvanted vaccines, including the hepatitis vaccine, lose potency irreversibly when frozen, even though appearance may seem normal. The loss cannot be reversed by thawing, so the stock must be isolated and assessed. Live vaccines have their own temperature limits, but adjuvanted products are not exempt."}, {"id": 1394, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "A pharmacist is counselling an Inuit elder in a northern community clinic about a new inhaler. The elder says little and avoids eye contact. A colleague suggests the patient is not interested in the information. Which approach best reflects culturally safe practice?", "options": {"A": "Shorten the counselling to the three key points and move on to the next waiting patient", "B": "Slow the pace, ask how the elder prefers to receive information, and confirm understanding with teach-back", "C": "Explain that eye contact is expected during counselling and ask the elder to maintain it", "D": "Conclude that the elder prefers no counselling and provide a written leaflet in English only"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Slow the pace, ask how the elder prefers to receive information, and confirm understanding with teach-back): Culturally safe practice avoids assumptions based on stereotypes, such as reading limited eye contact as disinterest, and instead gives the patient control over how information is shared and checks understanding respectfully. Insisting on eye contact imposes the provider's norms, and shortening counselling or leaving only a leaflet reduces the information and respect the patient receives."}, {"id": 1395, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A trial coordinator is setting up a multicentre trial of a new diuretic in heart failure and is choosing a method to protect allocation concealment at each of the 12 participating sites, where clinic staff enroll patients. Which feature best protects allocation concealment?", "options": {"A": "Placebo tablets that look identical to the active diuretic, preventing patients from knowing their treatment", "B": "Analysis of all participants in their assigned groups, preserving the balance created by the randomization", "C": "Assignment by alternating admission order, which is simple for staff at each site to follow", "D": "Central web based randomization releasing each assignment only after the patient is enrolled"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Central web based randomization releasing each assignment only after the patient is enrolled): Concealment means that people enrolling patients cannot know the next assignment. A central system that discloses allocation only after enrolment achieves this. Alternation is predictable, identical placebos concern blinding after allocation, and intention to treat concerns analysis."}, {"id": 1396, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "OP is a 48 year old man newly started on NPH insulin vials twice daily. He shakes the vial vigorously for half a minute before each dose because he believes this mixes it best. The vial looks foamy. What counselling is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Shake it harder and longer, since NPH crystals settle quickly and need strong agitation", "B": "Gently roll the vial between the palms until evenly cloudy, and avoid vigorous shaking", "C": "Let the vial warm up and settle for an hour, then draw up the clear layer", "D": "Draw up the dose from the top of the vial, where the insulin is most concentrated"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Gently roll the vial between the palms until evenly cloudy, and avoid vigorous shaking): NPH is a suspension, so the crystals must be evenly dispersed by gentle rolling or inversion. Vigorous shaking produces bubbles that can cause inaccurate doses and may damage the insulin. Letting it settle and drawing from one layer would give a dose with the wrong concentration."}, {"id": 1397, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 1, "question": "LC is a 29 year old woman taking warfarin 5 mg daily for a mechanical aortic valve. She comes to the pharmacy for refills and mentions that her period is two weeks late. A home pregnancy test this morning was positive. She has not yet seen her physician about it. What should the pharmacist do?", "options": {"A": "Dispense the refill and reassure her that warfarin is safe once the first trimester ends", "B": "Dispense the refill and advise folic acid 5 mg, which prevents warfarin embryopathy", "C": "Contact her prescriber today, since warfarin is teratogenic and the anticoagulant plan must change", "D": "Tell her to stop warfarin right away and wait until her next appointment to discuss alternatives"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Contact her prescriber today, since warfarin is teratogenic and the anticoagulant plan must change): Warfarin crosses the placenta and causes embryopathy, especially between weeks 6 and 12, so she needs same day review to move to a heparin based or adjusted strategy with a specialist. Stopping with no replacement exposes her mechanical valve to thrombosis. Folic acid does not prevent warfarin embryopathy."}, {"id": 1398, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "QX is a 38 year old man with type 1 diabetes with influenza, fever and poor appetite. His glucose is 13.2 mmol/L and blood ketones are 0.3 mmol/L. He asks how to manage insulin and fluids at home. What advice should the pharmacist give?", "options": {"A": "Continue basal insulin, check glucose and ketones every four hours, and drink fluids steadily", "B": "Stop all insulin until he eats again, and check glucose twice a day as illness settles", "C": "Take only mealtime insulin when he eats, and limit fluids to avoid worsening his nausea", "D": "Halve the basal insulin and check glucose once daily, since fever lowers his insulin needs"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Continue basal insulin, check glucose and ketones every four hours, and drink fluids steadily): In type 1 diabetes basal insulin must continue during illness to prevent ketoacidosis, and fever usually raises rather than lowers insulin need. Frequent glucose and ketone checks and steady fluids prevent dehydration. Stopping or halving insulin and restricting fluids are the opposite of sick day guidance."}, {"id": 1399, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "An oncology pharmacist is verifying cisplatin 75 mg/m2 for the next treatment cycle for a woman who is 150 cm tall and weighs 60 kg. Using the Mosteller formula, her body surface area is calculated first. What is the correct cisplatin dose?", "options": {"A": "About 135 mg, calculated from the standard adult body surface area of 1.8 m2", "B": "About 119 mg, calculated from a body surface area of 1.58 m2", "C": "About 188 mg, calculated from a body surface area of 2.5 m2", "D": "About 75 mg, which is the labelled dose and is not adjusted to body size"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (About 119 mg, calculated from a body surface area of 1.58 m2): Mosteller body surface area is the square root of (150 x 60 / 3600), which is the square root of 2.5, or 1.58 m2. The dose is 75 mg/m2 x 1.58 m2, about 119 mg. Skipping the square root gives 2.5 m2 and 188 mg, and using a fixed adult surface area or no adjustment ignores her smaller size."}, {"id": 1400, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "ZM is a 30 year old man picking up a naloxone kit. He says he hesitates to call 911 when friends overdose because he often carries a small amount of drugs. He asks the pharmacist whether he could face charges. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "The Act protects only the person who overdosed, and any caller remains liable for possession charges", "B": "The Good Samaritan Drug Overdose Act protects people at an overdose scene from simple possession charges", "C": "Protection applies only when the caller waits for police and surrenders the drugs on request", "D": "The Act covers every drug offence, including trafficking, if the caller phones within five minutes"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The Good Samaritan Drug Overdose Act protects people at an overdose scene from simple possession charges): The federal Good Samaritan Drug Overdose Act protects anyone who seeks emergency help for an overdose, the person who overdosed, and others at the scene from charges of simple possession of a controlled substance. It does not cover trafficking offences. Fear of police involvement should not delay calling for help."}, {"id": 1401, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "A 28 year old woman with depression and a past history of bulimia asks about bupropion, which she heard also helps with smoking cessation. She takes no other medications and has never had a seizure. What is the main concern the pharmacist should raise?", "options": {"A": "Bupropion causes serotonin syndrome when used alone, so she should avoid it unless taking an SSRI", "B": "Bupropion commonly causes marked weight gain and sedation, so another drug should be chosen for her", "C": "Bupropion cannot be used by patients who smoke, since nicotine blocks absorption of the drug", "D": "Bupropion lowers the seizure threshold and is contraindicated in eating disorders, so use another"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Bupropion lowers the seizure threshold and is contraindicated in eating disorders, so use another): Bupropion increases seizure risk, and purging, restriction and electrolyte disturbance in bulimia or anorexia magnify that risk, so it is contraindicated. It tends to cause weight loss, not gain, and is itself used for smoking cessation. It is not strongly serotonergic."}, {"id": 1402, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 6, "question": "WR is a 27 year old woman who wants to start norethindrone 0.35 mg progestin-only pills today, day 9 of her menstrual cycle. Her period began nine days ago and she has had no intercourse since. She is reasonably certain she is not pregnant. How long should she use backup contraception?", "options": {"A": "No backup is needed because she is not at risk of pregnancy this cycle", "B": "Use condoms or abstain for the first two days after starting the pill", "C": "Use condoms or abstain for the first fourteen days after starting the pill", "D": "Use condoms or abstain for the first seven days after starting the pill"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Use condoms or abstain for the first two days after starting the pill): A progestin-only pill started after day 5 of the cycle needs 48 hours of backup contraception while cervical mucus thickens. The seven-day rule applies to combined hormonal methods. Because she could be fertile now, and cycles vary, going without backup is not advised."}, {"id": 1403, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "A 72 year old woman received her first zoledronic acid 5 mg infusion yesterday for osteoporosis. This morning she has a temperature of 38.2 C, muscle aches and headache. She is worried about an allergy. What is the best advice from the pharmacist?", "options": {"A": "This signals a severe allergy, so she avoids future infusions and carries an epinephrine auto-injector", "B": "This indicates kidney failure, so she should stop all medications and attend a dialysis centre", "C": "Expect an acute phase reaction, use acetaminophen, and note it lessens with later doses", "D": "It will worsen with each infusion, so she should switch to a daily oral bisphosphonate"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Expect an acute phase reaction, use acetaminophen, and note it lessens with later doses): Flu-like symptoms within one to three days of the first intravenous bisphosphonate are an acute phase response and usually resolve in a few days. Acetaminophen manages the discomfort, and the reaction becomes less frequent with later doses. It is not an allergy and does not predict kidney failure."}, {"id": 1404, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "A patient on methadone with weekend carries reports that his take-home bottle for Sunday was stolen from his car on Saturday evening. He asks the pharmacist to replace it right away. The pharmacist has the usual daily dose in stock. What is the most appropriate response?", "options": {"A": "Refuse any further carries from the pharmacy and notify the college on the next working day", "B": "Replace it once the patient pays and signs a note saying that the dose is lost", "C": "Replace the dose immediately, since a missed dose risks withdrawal and a relapse into use", "D": "Document the report and contact the prescriber, who decides whether and how to replace it"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Document the report and contact the prescriber, who decides whether and how to replace it): Replacing a lost or stolen carry is a prescriber decision, since it involves an extra supply of an opioid and possible diversion. The pharmacist records what the patient says and calls the prescriber. Immediate replacement skips that judgement, and cutting off carries is a punitive step beyond the pharmacist's role."}, {"id": 1405, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 6, "question": "KS weighs 84 kg and has Crohn disease. The gastroenterologist orders infliximab 5 mg/kg intravenously for induction. The hospital pharmacist is checking the order before the infusion is prepared. What dose in milligrams should the verified order show for this patient?", "options": {"A": "168 mg, calculated as 2 mg/kg multiplied by 84 kg of actual body weight", "B": "420 mg, calculated as 5 mg/kg multiplied by the patient weight of 84 kg", "C": "500 mg, calculated as five whole vials regardless of the body weight of the patient", "D": "840 mg, calculated as 10 mg/kg multiplied by 84 kg of actual body weight"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (420 mg, calculated as 5 mg/kg multiplied by the patient weight of 84 kg): The dose is 5 mg/kg x 84 kg = 420 mg. A dose of 10 mg/kg is used only in selected cases with a documented plan, and a flat five vials ignores the weight-based order. Verifying the arithmetic prevents a twofold dosing error."}, {"id": 1406, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "PS is a 49 year old man with type 2 diabetes for eight years, whose blood pressure is normal and who takes metformin only. His urine albumin to creatinine ratio today is 4.2 mg/mmol, and eGFR is 82 mL/min/1.73 m2. How should the pharmacist interpret this finding?", "options": {"A": "It is normal for diabetes, because only ratios above 20 mg/mmol indicate early kidney damage", "B": "It is a lab artifact, so no repeat is needed until his next annual screening visit", "C": "It confirms advanced kidney failure, so metformin must be stopped and dialysis planning started", "D": "It suggests albuminuria and needs confirming with repeat samples within about three months"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (It suggests albuminuria and needs confirming with repeat samples within about three months): A ratio of 2.0 mg/mmol or more is abnormal in diabetes, and because values vary day to day, two of three samples over three months confirm persistent albuminuria. His eGFR of 82 is preserved, so there is no advanced kidney failure or reason to stop metformin. Dismissing the result delays treatment that slows kidney disease."}, {"id": 1407, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "Compared with warfarin over 18 months of follow-up, a new anticoagulant had a hazard ratio of 1.25 (95% confidence interval 0.90 to 1.74) for major bleeding in a trial. A pharmacist presenting to the thrombosis committee must state what the result supports.", "options": {"A": "The anticoagulant is safer than warfarin because 0.90 is below 1 at the lower confidence limit", "B": "Bleeding was proven to be equal between drugs, because a hazard ratio near 1 shows equivalence", "C": "A difference in bleeding was not shown, since the interval includes the value 1", "D": "The anticoagulant causes 25% more major bleeding than warfarin, confirmed by the reported hazard ratio"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A difference in bleeding was not shown, since the interval includes the value 1): The hazard ratio of 1.25 is a point estimate, but the interval 0.90 to 1.74 includes 1, so the data are compatible with less, equal or more bleeding. A hazard ratio near 1 does not prove equivalence. The result neither confirms excess bleeding nor shows safety."}, {"id": 1408, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 3, "question": "PS is a 29 year old woman starting doxycycline 100 mg twice daily for 7 days for chlamydia. She takes calcium carbonate antacid tablets for heartburn and spends most days outdoors this summer. Which counselling points are most important for her?", "options": {"A": "Take doxycycline lying down after meals and skip sunscreen, which reduces drug absorption", "B": "Take doxycycline on an empty stomach with milk to improve its absorption each time", "C": "Separate doxycycline from the antacid by 2 to 3 hours, and use sunscreen outdoors", "D": "Take the antacid and doxycycline together to reduce stomach upset; sunscreen is optional"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Separate doxycycline from the antacid by 2 to 3 hours, and use sunscreen outdoors): Calcium in antacids and milk binds doxycycline and reduces absorption, so doses should be separated. Doxycycline causes photosensitivity, so sun protection is needed. It should be taken upright with water to prevent esophageal irritation."}, {"id": 1409, "domain": "leadership_and_stewardship", "chapter": 11, "question": "Five minutes after receiving a vaccine at the pharmacy, a 45 year old man weighing 70 kg reports an itchy throat, develops hives on his chest, has audible wheeze, and says he feels faint. His blood pressure is falling. What should the pharmacist do immediately?", "options": {"A": "Give diphenhydramine 50 mg by mouth, then reassess breathing and blood pressure in 15 minutes", "B": "Inject epinephrine 0.5 mg subcutaneously in the deltoid and observe him for 30 minutes before calling", "C": "Lay him flat, start oxygen, and give epinephrine only if hypotension persists after 10 minutes", "D": "Inject epinephrine 0.5 mg intramuscularly into the anterolateral thigh and call 911"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Inject epinephrine 0.5 mg intramuscularly into the anterolateral thigh and call 911): Hives with wheeze and falling blood pressure signal anaphylaxis, for which intramuscular epinephrine in the anterolateral thigh is the standard treatment and should not be delayed. Emergency services are called at the same time. Antihistamines act too slowly, and subcutaneous or deltoid injection gives slower absorption than the thigh."}, {"id": 1410, "domain": "leadership_and_stewardship", "chapter": 11, "question": "A hospitalized 71 year old man with community acquired pneumonia has received piperacillin tazobactam for three days. Blood cultures grow penicillin susceptible Streptococcus pneumoniae, and he is improving clinically with normal renal function and no allergies. As the stewardship pharmacist, what should you recommend?", "options": {"A": "Add vancomycin to the regimen to cover possible resistant organisms not yet detected on culture", "B": "Narrow therapy to penicillin or amoxicillin based on the culture result and susceptibility", "C": "Continue the broad spectrum therapy until the course ends, since narrowing may allow resistance to emerge", "D": "Change to a fluoroquinolone, because oral broad spectrum agents are preferred for all pneumonia step downs"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Narrow therapy to penicillin or amoxicillin based on the culture result and susceptibility): Once culture and susceptibility results identify a narrow-spectrum susceptible organism, therapy should be de-escalated to the narrowest effective agent, which lowers adverse effects, Clostridioides difficile risk, and resistance selection. Continuing broad coverage or adding vancomycin offers no benefit against a susceptible pathogen. Fluoroquinolones should be reserved for situations without a narrower alternative."}, {"id": 1411, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 7, "question": "EM is a 36 year old woman with blistering rash and swelling of both eyelids that began a day after she used a new hair dye. She breathes comfortably and swallows normally. She asks for hydrocortisone cream. What should the pharmacist do?", "options": {"A": "Recommend hydrocortisone 1% cream to the face for seven days with a cool compress", "B": "Recommend an oral antihistamine and plan to repeat the dye test within one week", "C": "Suggest petroleum jelly to the eyelids and ask her to monitor the swelling for one week", "D": "Refer her the same day for assessment of severe allergic contact dermatitis needing oral corticosteroid"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Refer her the same day for assessment of severe allergic contact dermatitis needing oral corticosteroid): Facial and eyelid swelling with blistering after hair dye is a severe allergic contact reaction that usually needs a short course of oral corticosteroid. Topical hydrocortisone is too weak and eyelid use raises concerns. Repeat exposure to the dye should be avoided."}, {"id": 1412, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "The pharmacist is writing the drug class review and must summarize the pooled result for the composite of heart failure hospitalization or cardiovascular death. Using the pooled hazard ratio, its confidence interval, and the heterogeneity and funnel plot findings from this meta-analysis, which statement best interprets the overall result?", "options": {"A": "The drug is proven to reduce cardiovascular death alone, because the pooled result is significant", "B": "The drug probably lowers combined events by about 18%, and the interval excludes no effect", "C": "The drug lowers combined events by about 18%, but publication bias may explain the result", "D": "The drug lowers the combined events by 82%, and the interval is too wide to conclude"}, "cluster_id": "CHS10", "cluster_text": "A pharmacist is preparing a drug class review of an SGLT2 inhibitor for heart failure with preserved ejection fraction. A meta-analysis of six randomized trials (9,800 patients) reports a pooled hazard ratio for heart failure hospitalization or cardiovascular death of 0.82 (95% CI 0.74 to 0.91), using a random-effects model, with an I-squared of 12%. The two-year event rate in the control arms was 20%. The funnel plot shows no obvious asymmetry.", "cluster_size": 3, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The drug probably lowers combined events by about 18%, and the interval excludes no effect): A hazard ratio of 0.82 corresponds to about an 18% relative reduction in the composite. The interval of 0.74 to 0.91 does not include 1.0, so the effect is statistically significant. The result applies to the composite, not to death alone, and the symmetric funnel plot argues against publication bias."}, {"id": 1413, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "What does the I-squared value of 12% suggest about how consistent the six randomized trials of the SGLT2 inhibitor in heart failure included in this meta-analysis were with one another, and what follows for pooling their results with a random-effects model?", "options": {"A": "Little inconsistency exists between the trials beyond chance, so pooling their results is reasonable", "B": "The trials had a twelve percent chance of reporting a benefit that was due to chance", "C": "Substantial heterogeneity exists between the trials, so the pooled result should not be used for decisions", "D": "Only twelve percent of participants were included, so the pooled sample is probably too small"}, "cluster_id": "CHS10", "cluster_text": "A pharmacist is preparing a drug class review of an SGLT2 inhibitor for heart failure with preserved ejection fraction. A meta-analysis of six randomized trials (9,800 patients) reports a pooled hazard ratio for heart failure hospitalization or cardiovascular death of 0.82 (95% CI 0.74 to 0.91), using a random-effects model, with an I-squared of 12%. The two-year event rate in the control arms was 20%. The funnel plot shows no obvious asymmetry.", "cluster_size": 3, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Little inconsistency exists between the trials beyond chance, so pooling their results is reasonable): I-squared estimates how much of the variability between trials goes beyond chance. A low value of 12% indicates that the trials give consistent results, which supports combining them. It does not describe the sample included or the probability of a chance finding."}, {"id": 1414, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A pharmacist is preparing a drug class review and treats the pooled hazard ratio of 0.82 as a relative risk. Using the two year control event rate of 20 percent in the trials, about how many patients need treatment for two years to prevent one event?", "options": {"A": "About 6 patients need treatment for two years, because the relative risk reduction is 18 percent", "B": "About 5 patients need treatment for two years, because the control event rate is 20 percent", "C": "About 56 patients need treatment for two years, because the absolute benefit is 1.8 percent", "D": "About 28 patients need treatment for two years, because the absolute benefit is 3.6 percent"}, "cluster_id": "CHS10", "cluster_text": "A pharmacist is preparing a drug class review of an SGLT2 inhibitor for heart failure with preserved ejection fraction. A meta-analysis of six randomized trials (9,800 patients) reports a pooled hazard ratio for heart failure hospitalization or cardiovascular death of 0.82 (95% CI 0.74 to 0.91), using a random-effects model, with an I-squared of 12%. The two-year event rate in the control arms was 20%. The funnel plot shows no obvious asymmetry.", "cluster_size": 3, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (About 28 patients need treatment for two years, because the absolute benefit is 3.6 percent): The relative risk reduction is 18%, so the absolute risk reduction is 20% times 0.18, or 3.6%. The number needed to treat is 1 divided by 0.036, which is about 28. Using only the relative reduction or the control rate ignores one of the two inputs."}, {"id": 1415, "domain": "communication_and_collaboration", "chapter": 11, "question": "TC, a 55 year old man with type 2 diabetes, has an A1c of 8.6% on metformin and says he wants to feel less tired. The pharmacist is writing a care plan goal with him. Which goal best meets SMART criteria and reflects his priorities?", "options": {"A": "Take metformin as prescribed, and the pharmacist will count his tablets at each visit to confirm", "B": "Reach an A1c of 7.5% or less in three months by walking daily after supper", "C": "Normalize blood sugar and avoid all diabetes complications for the rest of his life", "D": "Improve diabetes control and feel better over the coming months with help from the pharmacy"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Reach an A1c of 7.5% or less in three months by walking daily after supper): A SMART goal is specific, measurable, achievable, relevant and time bound. The correct goal names a target value, a timeframe and a concrete behaviour he can carry out. Vague or unrealistic goals cannot be measured, and a goal driven by pill counting ignores what the patient wants."}, {"id": 1416, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "CP is an 81 year old man with a creatinine clearance of 25 mL/min who presents a prescription for a renally cleared antibiotic at the usual full adult dose for a urinary infection. The prescriber is unreachable for the day. In most provinces, what may the pharmacist do?", "options": {"A": "Change to a different antibiotic class chosen on the pharmacist's own judgment, then tell her", "B": "Adapt the dose or interval for renal function, document the reasoning, and notify the prescriber", "C": "Refuse to dispense and ask the patient to attend an emergency department that day", "D": "Dispense the full dose as written, since a pharmacist cannot change a dose without the prescriber"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Adapt the dose or interval for renal function, document the reasoning, and notify the prescriber): Adjusting dose or interval for renal function falls under adaptation in most provinces when it serves the patient, with documentation and notification. Dispensing a full dose risks accumulation and toxicity in severe renal impairment. Switching classes is a different authority and is not needed."}, {"id": 1417, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "A pharmacy technician finds a regular customer, a 44 year old man with type 1 diabetes, slumped and unresponsive in the waiting area. His wife says he took his usual insulin an hour ago and has a glucagon kit in her purse. What is the most appropriate response by the pharmacist?", "options": {"A": "Position him on his side, give glucagon by injection, and call emergency services", "B": "Give oral glucose gel under his tongue, using glucagon only if he fails to improve", "C": "Lay him flat, check his glucose with a meter, and wait for the result before acting", "D": "Pour juice into his mouth to raise his glucose, then wait for him to wake"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Position him on his side, give glucagon by injection, and call emergency services): An unresponsive patient on insulin must be treated as severely hypoglycemic, with glucagon given without delay and emergency services called. Placing him on his side protects his airway. Putting liquid or gel in the mouth of an unresponsive person risks aspiration, and delaying treatment for a meter reading wastes critical time."}, {"id": 1418, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "Amoxicillin 250 mg/5 mL suspension is unavailable. The prescriber agrees to substitute amoxicillin 400 mg/5 mL for DP, a 4 year old girl who needs 250 mg three times daily for 7 days. What dose volume should the label state, rounded to the nearest 0.1 mL?", "options": {"A": "5.0 mL three times daily, because the volume stays the same as the earlier strength", "B": "8.0 mL three times daily, because the stronger concentration requires a larger volume", "C": "3.1 mL three times daily, because the stronger product contains 80 mg in each millilitre", "D": "1.6 mL three times daily, because the higher concentration means the dose can be halved"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (3.1 mL three times daily, because the stronger product contains 80 mg in each millilitre): The 400 mg/5 mL product contains 80 mg/mL, so 250 mg needs 250 / 80 = 3.125 mL, which rounds to 3.1 mL. Keeping 5 mL would give 400 mg, a stronger concentration needs a smaller volume rather than a larger one, and halving the volume again would give only about 125 mg."}, {"id": 1419, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "On a routine walk-through, the pharmacist finds a recycling bin behind the pharmacy containing unshredded labels and prescription printouts with patient names, drugs and addresses. The bin sits in an unlocked outdoor enclosure and is emptied by a municipal truck tomorrow morning. What should the pharmacist do?", "options": {"A": "Move the bin indoors for later shredding and mention it at the next staff meeting", "B": "Black out patient names with a marker, then return the printouts to the recycling bin", "C": "Retrieve and shred them now, then assess the exposure as a possible reportable privacy breach", "D": "Let the municipal collection proceed, since recycled paper is processed in bulk and identification is unlikely"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Retrieve and shred them now, then assess the exposure as a possible reportable privacy breach): Documents with identifiable health information must be destroyed securely, and papers left in an unlocked outdoor bin may already have been accessed. The pharmacist should secure and shred them now and assess whether a privacy breach occurred and who must be notified. Delaying shredding, relying on bulk processing or marker redaction leaves the information at risk."}, {"id": 1420, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 3, "question": "JN is a 41 year old woman starting a 14 day H. pylori regimen that includes esomeprazole 20 mg twice daily. She tells the pharmacist she plans to take both tablets in the morning with her coffee, so that she can forget about them for the day. How should the pharmacist advise her?", "options": {"A": "Take each dose with a full meal, since food raises absorption of the PPI tablet", "B": "Take one dose 30 minutes before breakfast and the second 30 minutes before supper", "C": "Take both doses together before breakfast, since acid suppression from one dose lasts a day", "D": "Take each dose at bedtime, since nocturnal acid secretion is the main target of treatment"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Take one dose 30 minutes before breakfast and the second 30 minutes before supper): Proton pump inhibitors work best when taken about 30 minutes before a meal, when the acid pumps are being activated by food, and twice daily dosing in eradication regimens is split before breakfast and supper. Taking both together in the morning leaves acid unsuppressed in the evening, which can lower eradication rates. Food reduces rather than improves the effect of most PPIs."}, {"id": 1421, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 1, "question": "OD is a 61 year old man who takes rosuvastatin 20 mg daily with an LDL of 2.4 mmol/L and triglycerides of 5.8 mmol/L. His doctor orders gemfibrozil 600 mg twice daily. What should the pharmacist advise? His liver and kidney function are normal.", "options": {"A": "suggest fenofibrate as the fibrate, since gemfibrozil raises statin levels and increases myopathy risk", "B": "separate the doses by 12 hours, since this removes the interaction between gemfibrozil and the statin", "C": "accept gemfibrozil and add vitamin E daily, since vitamin E prevents statin muscle damage", "D": "accept gemfibrozil, since the combination with rosuvastatin carries a lower myopathy risk than with other statins"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (suggest fenofibrate as the fibrate, since gemfibrozil raises statin levels and increases myopathy risk): Gemfibrozil inhibits statin glucuronidation and OATP transporters, which raises statin levels and increases rhabdomyolysis risk. Fenofibrate has little effect on statin pharmacokinetics. Spacing the doses does not remove the interaction."}, {"id": 1422, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "In an observational study, the crude risk ratio for an exposure and an outcome is 1.8. When the data are split by sex, the risk ratio is 1.0 in men and 1.0 in women. What do these stratified results indicate about the crude association?", "options": {"A": "The crude ratio is correct, because stratifying by sex reduces the precision of the estimate", "B": "Sex confounds the crude association, since both stratum specific ratios are equal to one", "C": "The exposure causes the outcome in both sexes, because the two stratified ratios agree", "D": "Sex is an effect modifier, because the association differs between men and women in the strata"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Sex confounds the crude association, since both stratum specific ratios are equal to one): Stratum-specific ratios of 1.0 show no association within each sex, so the crude 1.8 arose because sex is related to both the exposure and the outcome. If the ratios had differed between strata, effect modification would be suggested instead."}, {"id": 1423, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "DM is a 68 year old postmenopausal woman with type 2 diabetes, osteoporosis and a prior wrist fracture. Her A1c is 8.1% on metformin. Her physician is considering adding pioglitazone or a DPP-4 inhibitor. Which concern should the pharmacist raise about pioglitazone for her?", "options": {"A": "It lowers calcium absorption, so a calcium supplement must be added to prevent fractures", "B": "It blocks parathyroid hormone release, which lowers calcium and weakens bone over several years", "C": "It causes severe hypoglycemia in postmenopausal women, so it needs a lower starting dose than usual", "D": "It raises fracture risk in women, making it a poor choice given her bone history"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (It raises fracture risk in women, making it a poor choice given her bone history): Pioglitazone is associated with increased fractures, particularly in women, because it affects bone formation. With osteoporosis and a previous fracture, a DPP-4 inhibitor is the safer comparison. Pioglitazone alone rarely causes hypoglycemia and its bone effect does not depend on combination or calcium absorption."}, {"id": 1424, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 7, "question": "HB is a 29 year old woman, 8 weeks pregnant, with nausea and vomiting for five days. She cannot keep fluids down for over 24 hours, has lost 3 kg and feels dizzy on standing. She asks for a prescription for the nausea. What should the pharmacist do?", "options": {"A": "Prescribe dimenhydrinate every four hours as needed with telephone review in two weeks", "B": "Prescribe doxylamine-pyridoxine at the usual starting dose and reassess in one week", "C": "Recommend ginger and small frequent meals only, with a follow up in two weeks", "D": "Refer her for same day medical assessment for possible hyperemesis gravidarum rather than prescribing"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Refer her for same day medical assessment for possible hyperemesis gravidarum rather than prescribing): Inability to keep down fluids for 24 hours, weight loss and orthostatic dizziness suggest dehydration and possible hyperemesis gravidarum, which may need intravenous fluids and specialist care. Outpatient prescribing and delayed follow up would be unsafe."}, {"id": 1425, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 7, "question": "CW is a 41 year old who will fly east across seven time zones next week and asks about melatonin for jet lag. She takes no medications. Which statement best reflects evidence and dosing? She wants to arrive rested for a work conference.", "options": {"A": "Melatonin is unsafe for adults and should be replaced by a nightly sedative-hypnotic", "B": "Low dose melatonin at destination bedtime has reasonable evidence for reducing jet lag after eastward travel", "C": "Melatonin has been proven to cure jet lag when taken before departure", "D": "High dose melatonin, 10 mg or more at midday, is the best supported regimen"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Low dose melatonin at destination bedtime has reasonable evidence for reducing jet lag after eastward travel): Trials support melatonin 0.5 to 5 mg at the new local bedtime for several days to shorten jet lag, especially when crossing several time zones. It does not eliminate symptoms and timing is key. Sedative hypnotics are not preferred and high midday dosing is not supported."}, {"id": 1426, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 3, "question": "CM is a 78 year old woman who wears her upper denture day and night and has a red sore palate beneath it. Her dentist diagnosed Candida denture stomatitis and asks the pharmacist to advise alongside nystatin suspension. Which advice is most important for clearing the infection?", "options": {"A": "Keep wearing the denture day and night so nystatin stays in contact with the infected palate", "B": "Leave the denture out overnight and disinfect it daily, since it harbours the yeast", "C": "Use an alcohol based mouthwash twice daily, since it removes the yeast from the denture surface", "D": "Soak the denture in dilute bleach overnight and rinse it briefly before wearing it each morning"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Leave the denture out overnight and disinfect it daily, since it harbours the yeast): Dentures worn through the night create a warm, moist, low oxygen surface where Candida grows, so leaving the denture out overnight and disinfecting it daily is central to clearing and preventing infection. Antifungal treatment fails if the reservoir on the denture is left untouched. Soaking in bleach can damage the denture and irritate tissue, and mouthwash does not clear yeast embedded in the acrylic."}, {"id": 1427, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 7, "question": "TD is a 30 year old traveller with diarrhea who took loperamide 4 mg initially, then 2 mg after each of two loose stools, a total of 8 mg today. He has a third loose stool and asks whether to take another capsule. What should the pharmacist advise?", "options": {"A": "Yes, take 4 mg more now, since a double dose is needed for the third stool", "B": "No, loperamide should be stopped for good and replaced with an antibiotic taken without assessment", "C": "No, 8 mg is the nonprescription daily limit, so use fluids and seek care if needed", "D": "Yes, take one more 2 mg capsule, since the maximum nonprescription daily dose is 12 mg"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (No, 8 mg is the nonprescription daily limit, so use fluids and seek care if needed): The recommended maximum for nonprescription loperamide is 8 mg per day, which he has already taken. Oral rehydration remains the priority, and persistent or worsening symptoms, fever or blood in stool need medical assessment. Antibiotics should not be self started without assessment."}, {"id": 1428, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "VB, a 66 year old man taking warfarin for atrial fibrillation, asks a community pharmacist whether a turmeric and black pepper supplement could change his INR. The pharmacist wants evidence-graded interaction information on this supplement. Which approach is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Tell VB natural ingredients do not interact with warfarin, as they are not regulated like drugs", "B": "Search the product monograph database, since supplements are covered by the same monograph system as drugs", "C": "Check the Natural Medicines interaction tool, then discuss monitoring and INR follow-up with the patient", "D": "Rely on the supplement label alone, since manufacturers must list the known drug interactions in Canada"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Check the Natural Medicines interaction tool, then discuss monitoring and INR follow-up with the patient): Natural Medicines grades interaction evidence for supplement ingredients, including bleeding and INR effects reported with turmeric. Labels are not a complete source of interaction data, and natural ingredients can certainly interact with warfarin. Because the evidence is limited, INR monitoring is still prudent."}, {"id": 1429, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 4, "question": "CW is a 45 year old man who completed treatment for latent tuberculosis and now receives infliximab infusions every eight weeks for Crohn disease. He asks which symptoms between infusions should prompt him to call his doctor promptly. Which list is most appropriate for the pharmacist to give?", "options": {"A": "Persistent cough, fevers, night sweats, weight loss, or any signs of infection should be reported", "B": "Only high fevers above 40 C need reporting, because mild infections are expected on infliximab", "C": "Mild headache, nasal congestion and a brief sore throat after each infusion are the main signs", "D": "Nothing needs reporting between visits, since infection monitoring is done with blood tests alone"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Persistent cough, fevers, night sweats, weight loss, or any signs of infection should be reported): Patients on tumour necrosis factor inhibitors are at risk for serious infection and tuberculosis reactivation, so persistent cough, fevers, night sweats, weight loss or other infection signs need prompt reporting. Waiting for a very high fever or the next visit delays treatment, and mild post-infusion symptoms are not the key warning signs."}, {"id": 1430, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 1, "question": "BR is a 47 year old man who developed a calf deep vein thrombosis four weeks after hip replacement surgery. He has no cancer, no family history and no prior clots, and his surgeon calls the clot provoked by the operation. How long should anticoagulation usually continue?", "options": {"A": "three months, after which treatment is stopped and recurrence risk is low", "B": "six weeks only, since a calf thrombosis tied to surgery needs no longer course", "C": "twelve months, since provoked thromboses need the same duration as unprovoked events", "D": "lifelong, because any thrombosis after major surgery is likely to come back"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (three months, after which treatment is stopped and recurrence risk is low): A DVT provoked by a major transient risk factor such as surgery carries a low recurrence risk, so three months of anticoagulation is enough. Lifelong therapy is reserved for persistent risk factors or selected unprovoked events."}, {"id": 1431, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "A manufacturer informs pharmacies that one lot of an injectable product is being recalled because use of the product has a reasonable probability of causing serious adverse health consequences or death. A pharmacy technician asks how Health Canada classifies a recall at this level of risk. Which classification applies?", "options": {"A": "Type II recall, the category used when exposure may cause temporary or medically reversible adverse effects", "B": "Type III recall, the category used when use is unlikely to cause adverse health consequences", "C": "Type I recall, the category used when the product could cause serious harm or death", "D": "Type IV notice, a voluntary category used when the product label contains a minor printing error"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Type I recall, the category used when the product could cause serious harm or death): Health Canada uses three recall types. Type I applies when there is a reasonable probability of serious adverse health consequences or death. Type II covers temporary or reversible effects, and Type III covers products unlikely to cause harm. There is no Type IV category."}, {"id": 1432, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 1, "question": "OT is a 62 year old man with type 2 diabetes taking rosuvastatin 20 mg daily. His fasting triglycerides are 6.4 mmol/L despite lifestyle changes, and his physician has prescribed gemfibrozil 600 mg twice daily. The pharmacist considers the combination. Which recommendation is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Dispense as written, because rosuvastatin is not affected by gemfibrozil unlike the other statins", "B": "Dispense as written and separate the two drugs by four hours to prevent the interaction", "C": "Stop rosuvastatin while gemfibrozil is taken, then restart it after triglycerides fall below 5 mmol/L", "D": "Suggest fenofibrate in place of gemfibrozil, because it interacts far less with statins"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Suggest fenofibrate in place of gemfibrozil, because it interacts far less with statins): Gemfibrozil inhibits statin glucuronidation and hepatic uptake, raising statin levels and the risk of myopathy and rhabdomyolysis. Fenofibrate does not share this interaction to the same degree, so it is the fibrate used with a statin when needed. Spacing the doses does not prevent the effect because the mechanism involves metabolism and transport."}, {"id": 1433, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "HR is a 26 year old woman who started lamotrigine 25 mg daily nine days ago for epilepsy, titrated per the schedule. She calls the pharmacy to say she has developed a faint red rash on her trunk and a sore throat. What should the pharmacist advise?", "options": {"A": "Apply hydrocortisone cream and continue the titration, since most lamotrigine rashes are harmless and fade soon", "B": "Wait one week to see whether the rash spreads, and only then contact her prescriber", "C": "Take an antihistamine and double the next dose to protect against seizures during the rash", "D": "Get medical assessment promptly, since a lamotrigine rash may be a serious skin reaction"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Get medical assessment promptly, since a lamotrigine rash may be a serious skin reaction): Most serious lamotrigine rashes, including Stevens-Johnson syndrome, appear in the first 2 to 8 weeks, and a rash with fever, sore throat, or mucosal involvement needs urgent assessment and usually drug withdrawal. Treating it or waiting risks progression. Doubling the dose would make the reaction worse."}, {"id": 1434, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 6, "question": "KP is 34 weeks pregnant and has aching knees and mild back pain that disturb her sleep. She has no kidney or liver disease and asks which over the counter pain reliever is the safest choice for her. What should the pharmacist recommend and why?", "options": {"A": "Acetaminophen, because NSAIDs late in pregnancy can close the fetal ductus arteriosus early", "B": "Naproxen, because it is the only analgesic that has been shown safe throughout pregnancy", "C": "Ibuprofen, because acetaminophen causes fetal liver damage when taken at usual doses in the third trimester", "D": "Aspirin 650 mg, because it is cleared by the placenta and spares the fetal circulation"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Acetaminophen, because NSAIDs late in pregnancy can close the fetal ductus arteriosus early): Acetaminophen at usual doses is the preferred analgesic in pregnancy. NSAIDs taken in the third trimester can constrict the fetal ductus arteriosus and reduce amniotic fluid, so they are avoided. Analgesic doses of aspirin carry similar risks, and acetaminophen does not harm the fetal liver at recommended doses."}, {"id": 1435, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 3, "question": "DQ is a 41 year old man taking once weekly isoniazid and rifapentine for 12 weeks under direct observation to treat latent tuberculosis infection. After his third dose he calls the pharmacy reporting fever, chills, dizziness and flushing within hours of the dose. What is the most appropriate advice?", "options": {"A": "Switch to daily isoniazid, since flushing proves the rifapentine dose was too high for his weight", "B": "Premedicate with acetaminophen before future doses, since these effects are expected and settle by week four", "C": "Take the next dose with a large meal, which prevents the reaction by slowing drug absorption", "D": "Hold further doses and seek prompt medical assessment for a possible rifapentine systemic reaction"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Hold further doses and seek prompt medical assessment for a possible rifapentine systemic reaction): Isoniazid with rifapentine can cause a systemic drug reaction with flu-like symptoms, flushing, dizziness and sometimes low blood pressure, usually within hours of a dose. Further doses are held until a clinician assesses him, and the regimen is often stopped or changed. Premedication, food and dose changes do not reliably prevent it."}, {"id": 1436, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A 50 year old kidney transplant recipient takes tacrolimus 4 mg twice daily. The transplant clinic wants a trough level. The patient takes her morning dose at 0800 and arrives at clinic at 1000 for a blood draw, having already taken her morning dose. The pharmacist should advise that:", "options": {"A": "The level is a valid peak, so it can be interpreted using the trough target range", "B": "This is not a trough, so redraw it just before the next morning dose", "C": "The level is a valid trough because it was drawn within three hours of the dose", "D": "The level can be converted to a trough by subtracting the expected rise from that dose"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (This is not a trough, so redraw it just before the next morning dose): Tacrolimus targets apply to a trough drawn about 12 hours after the previous dose and just before the next one. A sample two hours after dosing is near the peak and would read much higher, which could lead to an unnecessary and risky dose cut. Back calculating a trough from a peak is unreliable, so the sample should be repeated at the correct time."}, {"id": 1437, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 4, "question": "HA is a 57 year old man who has had an anaphylactic reaction at a pharmacy vaccination clinic and has received epinephrine. He is pale, dizzy and sweaty but is breathing comfortably and not vomiting. A staff member asks whether to help him stand. The pharmacist should have him:", "options": {"A": "stand with support, so that his breathing remains easier until help arrives from the ambulance", "B": "lie flat with his legs raised, and avoid sitting or standing up suddenly", "C": "sit upright in a chair with his head between his knees, then stand slowly", "D": "walk slowly around the room, since movement improves circulation after an epinephrine injection"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (lie flat with his legs raised, and avoid sitting or standing up suddenly): With low blood pressure from anaphylaxis, lying flat with the legs raised keeps blood flowing to vital organs, and sudden sitting or standing has caused fatal collapse. Those with breathing difficulty may need to sit, and those who vomit should lie on their side, but neither applies here."}, {"id": 1438, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "A patient receiving oxycodone for chronic pain moves to a new town and asks the new pharmacy to transfer his remaining oxycodone prescription from his former pharmacy. The prescription was written three weeks ago and has instructions to dispense in portions. Under the federal Narcotic Control Regulations, what should the receiving pharmacist do?", "options": {"A": "Accept the transfer if the former pharmacy confirms the portions already dispensed and the remaining quantity", "B": "Accept the transfer by phone once and record the former pharmacist's name and licence number", "C": "Decline the transfer and ask the prescriber to send a new prescription", "D": "Accept the transfer in writing from the former pharmacist, provided the patient signs an authorization form"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Decline the transfer and ask the prescriber to send a new prescription): Narcotic prescriptions cannot be transferred between pharmacies under the Narcotic Control Regulations. The patient needs a new prescription from the prescriber. A one time transfer, written authorization or confirmation of the portions already dispensed does not create the authority to transfer."}, {"id": 1439, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 6, "question": "WK is a 35 year old man prescribed a scopolamine 1.5 mg transdermal patch for a three day cruise. He has never used a patch for motion sickness. Which counselling should the pharmacist give him about applying and using it?", "options": {"A": "Apply over the abdomen with a heat pad to speed absorption of the drug", "B": "Apply behind the ear after nausea starts, since it works within five minutes", "C": "Apply behind one ear at least four hours before departure and wash hands afterward", "D": "Apply to the chest immediately at embarkation, changing it every twenty-four hours until arrival"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Apply behind one ear at least four hours before departure and wash hands afterward): The scopolamine patch is placed behind the ear several hours before travel because onset is slow, and one patch lasts about 72 hours. Hands are washed afterward to avoid transferring drug to the eyes, which can cause blurred vision and a dilated pupil. Heat increases absorption and the risk of adverse effects."}, {"id": 1440, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "TH is a 76 year old man weighing 70 kg with serum creatinine 130 umol/L. He needs a drug whose monograph requires dose reduction below a CrCl of 50 mL/min. Using the Cockcroft-Gault equation, what is his estimated creatinine clearance, and what action follows?", "options": {"A": "About 61 mL/min, so no adjustment is needed when creatinine is under 150 umol/L in men", "B": "About 42 mL/min, so the reduced dose recommended for clearance below 50 mL/min should be used", "C": "About 85 mL/min, so the full dose is appropriate because clearance exceeds the 50 mL/min threshold", "D": "About 34 mL/min, which still calls for dose reduction, since no factor applies for male sex"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (About 42 mL/min, so the reduced dose recommended for clearance below 50 mL/min should be used): CrCl = (140 - 76) x 70 x 1.23 / 130 = 4480 x 1.23 / 130, which is about 42 mL/min. This is below 50, so the reduced dose applies. Omitting the 1.23 male factor gives an incorrect 34, and no creatinine cut off removes the need to calculate clearance."}, {"id": 1441, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 7, "question": "VR is a 40 year old man leaving for rural India in three days and has no hepatitis A protection. He is concerned that three days is too short to be protected. What should the pharmacist tell him about hepatitis A vaccine?", "options": {"A": "Hepatitis A vaccine is not recommended for adults, who are protected by childhood infections", "B": "A single dose before departure gives protection, with a booster later to complete the series", "C": "Protection only begins after two doses, so he should delay the trip until both are given", "D": "The vaccine must be given at least four weeks before departure to work"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A single dose before departure gives protection, with a booster later to complete the series): Hepatitis A vaccine provides protective antibodies in most adults within two to four weeks, and a single dose before departure is recommended even when travel is imminent. A booster at six to twelve months provides long term protection. Delaying travel is not necessary."}, {"id": 1442, "domain": "communication_and_collaboration", "chapter": 11, "question": "A pharmacist phones a prescriber's office and speaks with the clinic nurse, who relays that the physician approves changing a patient's amoxicillin to azithromycin because of a rash. The pharmacist is documenting this verbal order. What must the record include to be complete?", "options": {"A": "The order as received and the pharmacist's initials, since the phone system records the call details", "B": "Only the new prescription details, because reasons for a verbal change belong in the prescriber's chart", "C": "A note that the physician approved the change, with the rash reason added by the clinic", "D": "Date, time, who gave the order, the exact order received, and the pharmacist's initials"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Date, time, who gave the order, the exact order received, and the pharmacist's initials): A verbal order needs enough detail to be traced and verified: who gave it, the date and time, the exact content and who received it. The pharmacy phone system does not replace the pharmacist's own record, and relying on the prescriber's chart leaves the pharmacy record incomplete. A note without the actual order or its source cannot be checked later."}, {"id": 1443, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "ER is a 35 year old woman with hypothyroidism who takes levothyroxine 88 mcg each morning at 7:00 with a glass of water and eats breakfast at 7:15. Her TSH is 7.2 mIU/L despite full adherence and no change in dose. Which adjustment is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Take it with breakfast so food improves absorption, then recheck her TSH in two weeks", "B": "Split the tablet into halves at breakfast and lunch, and recheck TSH in six months", "C": "Switch to desiccated extract at the same milligram strength, and recheck TSH in one year", "D": "Take it 30 to 60 minutes before breakfast, then repeat TSH in six weeks"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Take it 30 to 60 minutes before breakfast, then repeat TSH in six weeks): Food shortly after levothyroxine reduces absorption, so the tablet should be taken 30 to 60 minutes before eating. TSH reaches a new steady state in six to eight weeks, so earlier rechecks are not informative. Desiccated extract is not preferred and has variable hormone content."}, {"id": 1444, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "A pharmacy technician (RD) who prepares intravenous admixtures arrives for her shift with a runny nose, frequent sneezing and a mild sore throat. She says she feels well enough to work and will wear an extra mask. The pharmacist-in-charge reviews the sterile compounding personnel policy. What is the best decision?", "options": {"A": "Allow her to compound with a second mask and an extra pair of sterile gloves", "B": "Reassign her to non-sterile duties until symptoms resolve, as her illness could contaminate sterile preparations", "C": "Allow her to prepare only low-risk preparations, since they carry the shortest beyond-use dates", "D": "Allow her to work if she takes an antihistamine and washes her hands twice before gloving"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Reassign her to non-sterile duties until symptoms resolve, as her illness could contaminate sterile preparations): Staff with respiratory infections shed microorganisms from the nose, mouth and skin, and an extra mask or gloves cannot reliably contain them in a cleanroom. Personnel policies require staff with symptoms to avoid sterile compounding until recovered, and the pharmacist can reassign her to non-sterile tasks. Medication that suppresses sneezing does not remove the infection."}, {"id": 1445, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 7, "question": "BM is a 4 year old girl with mild ear pain in one ear and no ear discharge, a temperature of 38.0 C and a new diagnosis of acute otitis media. She is otherwise healthy and the prescriber offered observation. Which plan is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Give a decongestant and antihistamine combination for five days to drain the middle ear fluid", "B": "Start amoxicillin 90 mg/kg/day at once, since observation is unsafe in children under five years", "C": "Start ear drops containing an antibiotic and corticosteroid to treat the middle ear infection", "D": "Treat pain with acetaminophen and observe for 48 hours before using antibiotics"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Treat pain with acetaminophen and observe for 48 hours before using antibiotics): In a healthy child over six months with mild unilateral otitis media, observation for 48 hours with analgesia is an accepted approach because many infections resolve on their own. Antibiotics are started if symptoms worsen or persist. Decongestants and antihistamines are not effective, and ear drops do not penetrate an intact eardrum."}, {"id": 1446, "domain": "leadership_and_stewardship", "chapter": 11, "question": "A pharmacy manager is writing a disaster plan after a flood closed neighbouring stores. She wants the plan to protect patient access to medications and records during an extended closure. Which element is most important to include in the plan?", "options": {"A": "A tested plan for offsite access to patient profiles and a backup dispensing location", "B": "A stockpile of sixty days of every drug on the formulary, kept in the basement", "C": "A policy of closing for the duration and referring all patients to the nearest emergency department", "D": "A paper binder of patient profiles stored in the dispensary and updated once each year"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A tested plan for offsite access to patient profiles and a backup dispensing location): Continuity depends on being able to reach patient profiles and dispense from another site when the premises are unusable, and the plan must be tested to be trusted. Closing and redirecting patients to emergency departments overloads hospitals. A paper binder in the same building is lost in the same event, and stockpiling in a basement is impractical and vulnerable to flooding."}, {"id": 1447, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "A rural pharmacy runs short of morphine 10 mg tablets on a Friday afternoon, and a patient with cancer pain needs a supply for the weekend. The pharmacist asks a nearby pharmacy to sell some stock. Under the Narcotic Control Regulations, what must happen for the sale to be lawful?", "options": {"A": "A telephone call to the provincial college, which then authorizes the narcotic stock transfer between pharmacies", "B": "A verbal agreement between the two pharmacists, then a written order once the stock has arrived", "C": "A letter from the prescriber confirming the regimen, with no record kept by the supplier", "D": "A signed written order from the requesting pharmacist, with both pharmacies recording the transaction"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A signed written order from the requesting pharmacist, with both pharmacies recording the transaction): A pharmacist who supplies narcotics to another pharmacist needs a signed written order from the requester, and both pharmacies must record the transaction so that stock can be accounted for. A verbal arrangement or approval from the college does not meet the requirement."}, {"id": 1448, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A pharmacist is asked by a long-term care nurse whether a liquid antibiotic suspension contains lactose or gluten for a resident with celiac disease. The pharmacist needs Canadian-specific, up-to-date ingredient information on a particular marketed product. Which source is best suited to answer this?", "options": {"A": "The Natural Medicines database, which provides ingredient evidence for natural health products and not for drugs", "B": "The Canadian product monograph through e-CPS, or the Health Canada Drug Product Database listing", "C": "A tertiary dosing reference that is based mainly on American products and their labelling texts", "D": "A systematic review of antibiotic efficacy in older adults living in long-term care homes in Canada"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The Canadian product monograph through e-CPS, or the Health Canada Drug Product Database listing): Non-medicinal ingredients such as lactose are listed in the Canadian product monograph and in Health Canada's Drug Product Database. American references may describe products with different formulations. Efficacy reviews do not list excipients."}, {"id": 1449, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "On Monday morning a pharmacy receives three notices at once: a Type III recall of calcium tablets for a labelling error, a Type I recall of a heparin flush lot also used in the clinic upstairs, and a Type II recall of a cough syrup. Staffing is limited. What is the best order of action?", "options": {"A": "Start with the cough syrup recall because it affects the most patients seen in a week", "B": "Act on the Type I heparin flush recall first, then process the other two", "C": "Process the notices in the order they arrived, since all recalls require the same response", "D": "Start with the calcium recall since it is the simplest and can be closed quickly"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Act on the Type I heparin flush recall first, then process the other two): Recall classes signal risk. A Type I recall carries a reasonable probability of serious harm or death and, with the product also in clinical use, comes first. The Type II and Type III notices still need action afterwards, but arrival order and simplicity should not outrank patient safety."}, {"id": 1450, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A trial of a new drug for atrial fibrillation reports a neutral primary composite of stroke, bleeding and death. On review, deaths rose from 2% to 3% while hospitalizations fell. A pharmacist is asked how to interpret the neutral composite. Which approach is best?", "options": {"A": "Accept the neutral composite as proof of safety, because a combined result includes all the harms", "B": "Disregard the death result, because components of a composite are too uncommon to interpret alone", "C": "Conclude the drug lowers mortality, since fewer hospitalizations indicate that fewer patients are dying overall", "D": "Examine each component, because a neutral composite can hide more deaths offset by fewer hospitalizations"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Examine each component, because a neutral composite can hide more deaths offset by fewer hospitalizations): A neutral composite can result from effects in opposite directions on components of different importance. A rise in deaths matters far more than a fall in hospitalizations, so each component needs to be read separately. A composite does not prove safety for any one component."}, {"id": 1451, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 4, "question": "A 34 year old developed an itchy flat red rash on her trunk eight days after starting amoxicillin-clavulanate for sinusitis. She has no fever, no mouth sores, no swelling and no trouble breathing. She asks what to do. What is the best advice?", "options": {"A": "Take the next dose with an antihistamine and finish the course, since drug rashes resolve alone", "B": "Apply a topical antifungal cream, since an itchy rash during antibiotics is usually a yeast infection", "C": "Stop the antibiotic and have the prescriber assess it, since this is a mild late reaction", "D": "Go to emergency by ambulance now, since any rash after an antibiotic is a life-threatening reaction"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Stop the antibiotic and have the prescriber assess it, since this is a mild late reaction): A mild morbilliform rash several days into a penicillin course without systemic or mucosal features is typically a benign delayed reaction, though the drug should be stopped and the reaction assessed. Immediate emergency transport is not needed without warning features. Continuing the drug could allow progression."}, {"id": 1452, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 3, "question": "ZP is a 58 year old woman in hospital with a remote history of urticaria after penicillin. She now needs meropenem for an intra-abdominal infection that has not responded to other treatment. The nurse asks whether it is safe to give. What should the pharmacist advise?", "options": {"A": "Meropenem should be replaced by ceftazidime, since cephalosporins are a safer choice after penicillin allergy", "B": "Meropenem is acceptable only if she first takes an antihistamine every day for a week", "C": "Meropenem must be avoided, because carbapenems cross react with penicillins in most allergic patients", "D": "Meropenem can be given with usual monitoring, since cross reactivity with penicillins is below one percent"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Meropenem can be given with usual monitoring, since cross reactivity with penicillins is below one percent): Cross reactivity between penicillins and carbapenems is low, under one percent, so meropenem can be given at full dose with ordinary observation. Pretreating with antihistamines does not prevent true allergy. Ceftazidime is not clearly safer and is a poorer fit for this infection."}, {"id": 1453, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "Before a busy influenza clinic, a technician proposes drawing up 40 doses into syringes in the morning so the line moves faster, and refrigerating them until patients arrive. The product monograph does not address pre drawn doses. What should the pharmacist decide?", "options": {"A": "Allow pre drawn doses if they are labelled with the time and used within eight hours", "B": "Allow pre drawn doses if the syringes are stored in the vaccine refrigerator all day", "C": "Allow pre drawn doses only for the first twenty patients, then switch to single dosing", "D": "Draw each dose just before it is given, because stability of pre drawn doses is unsupported"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Draw each dose just before it is given, because stability of pre drawn doses is unsupported): Without manufacturer data, there is no assurance that a vaccine stays potent and sterile in a syringe held for hours, and labelling errors are more likely when many syringes look alike. Drawing the dose immediately before giving it avoids both risks. Labelling or limiting the batch does not supply missing stability evidence."}, {"id": 1454, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A cohort study finds that patients who took more than 80% of their statin doses had fewer injuries from falls and fewer motor vehicle crashes than patients who took less. The pharmacist is asked whether the statin prevents injuries. What is the best explanation for the pattern?", "options": {"A": "Healthy adherer bias, because consistent adherence reflects generally healthier and more careful behaviours", "B": "The statin protects against injury, because the link between adherence and injuries is clearly causal", "C": "Recall bias, because adherent patients remember their statin doses more accurately than nonadherent patients", "D": "Publication bias, because studies finding fewer injuries are more likely to be selected for review"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Healthy adherer bias, because consistent adherence reflects generally healthier and more careful behaviours): Statins have no plausible mechanism for preventing crashes. Patients who take medicines consistently also tend to follow other safe habits, so adherence marks a healthier lifestyle. A benefit seen for unrelated outcomes is a clue to this bias rather than to a drug effect."}, {"id": 1455, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A heparin protocol reduces postoperative venous thromboembolism by a relative risk reduction of 50% in a ward where the baseline rate is 4% without prophylaxis. A pharmacist estimates the number needed to treat for local practice from these two values. What is it?", "options": {"A": "50, because the absolute reduction is 2% and 1 divided by 0.02 equals 50", "B": "2, because half of the 4% baseline rate is 2 and the NNT equals that value", "C": "25, because 1 divided by the 4% baseline rate gives the number needed to treat", "D": "200, because 1 divided by the 50% reduction is 2 and is then multiplied by 100"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (50, because the absolute reduction is 2% and 1 divided by 0.02 equals 50): The absolute risk reduction is the relative risk reduction times the baseline risk: 0.50 times 0.04 equals 0.02. The NNT is 1 divided by 0.02, or 50. Using the baseline rate alone gives 25, and the other values do not follow from either figure."}, {"id": 1456, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 6, "question": "NM is a 35 year old taking omeprazole 40 mg daily for reflux who requests emergency contraception 80 hours after unprotected intercourse. She weighs 74 kg, is not pregnant, and is not breastfeeding. The pharmacist knows ulipristal absorption depends on stomach acidity. What is the best plan?", "options": {"A": "Give ulipristal 30 mg as usual, since proton pump inhibitors have no effect on its absorption", "B": "Give ulipristal 30 mg and tell her to stop omeprazole for the following five days", "C": "Give levonorgestrel 1.5 mg because it is licensed up to 120 hours and unaffected by weight", "D": "Arrange copper IUD insertion, since omeprazole may reduce ulipristal absorption and levonorgestrel is weaker"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Arrange copper IUD insertion, since omeprazole may reduce ulipristal absorption and levonorgestrel is weaker): Ulipristal dissolves best in an acidic stomach, so acid suppression may lower its effect. At 80 hours levonorgestrel is beyond its labelled 72 hour window and is generally less effective than ulipristal or a copper device. A copper device is not affected by either issue, so it is the most reliable method."}, {"id": 1457, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "A pharmacy introduces barcode scanning at the verification station. A pharmacist, checking an order for ZK, a 45 year old woman, sees a green scan confirmation for apixaban 5 mg tablets. The prescription reads apixaban 2.5 mg twice daily. How should the pharmacist interpret the scan?", "options": {"A": "It confirms the product is in date, but strength must be checked by the technician", "B": "It replaces the independent check, so the pharmacist signs off and releases the order", "C": "It confirms the picked product matches stock, not that the strength matches the prescription", "D": "It confirms the strength matches the prescription, so manual comparison is only needed for controlled drugs"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (It confirms the picked product matches stock, not that the strength matches the prescription): The barcode scan confirms the identity of the product that was picked against the stock record, and a pharmacist must still compare drug, strength and directions with the original prescription. Scanning supports the check but does not replace it, and strength verification is not a technician-only task."}, {"id": 1458, "domain": "communication_and_collaboration", "chapter": 11, "question": "A pharmacist needs to quickly confirm whether a patient who developed hives after amoxicillin also reacted to penicillin V in the past. The patient is in the middle of a busy afternoon and the history must be recorded accurately. Which question type best suits this specific need?", "options": {"A": "A leading question suggesting that penicillin V is probably safe for someone who tolerated other antibiotics", "B": "A closed question that asks directly whether she also reacted to penicillin V", "C": "An open-ended question inviting her to describe every drug reaction she has had", "D": "A hypothetical question asking how she would feel about trying penicillin V again"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A closed question that asks directly whether she also reacted to penicillin V): Closed questions give brief, specific answers and are appropriate when a single fact must be confirmed. Open-ended questions suit exploring beliefs and experiences, while leading and hypothetical questions bias or blur the history."}, {"id": 1459, "domain": "communication_and_collaboration", "chapter": 11, "question": "An Elder from a nearby First Nation is picking up medications for diabetes. During counselling, the pharmacist asks about her blood sugar readings and she pauses for a long time before answering. The pharmacist feels uncomfortable with the silence. What is the most respectful approach?", "options": {"A": "Ask a family member standing nearby to answer on her behalf and then continue", "B": "Allow the silence, avoid interrupting, and give her time to respond in her own way", "C": "Move on to the next topic quickly and assume that she has nothing more to say", "D": "Fill the pause by rephrasing the question several times in simpler words to help her answer"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Allow the silence, avoid interrupting, and give her time to respond in her own way): In many Indigenous communities, silence is a normal part of respectful conversation and shows thoughtfulness, not confusion. Giving time lets her answer fully. Rephrasing repeatedly, moving on, or asking someone else to answer implies she is unable or unwilling to speak."}, {"id": 1460, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 6, "question": "VB is a 31 year old with epilepsy treated with valproic acid who plans to conceive in about three months. She currently takes no supplements. Her prescriber asks the pharmacist to recommend a folic acid dose and timing for a woman taking this anticonvulsant. Which advice is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Folic acid 0.4 mg daily, starting once a missed period has confirmed the pregnancy", "B": "Folic acid 4 to 5 mg daily, starting about three months before conception", "C": "Folic acid 1 mg daily, which is the usual upper limit before pregnancy", "D": "Folic acid 10 mg daily, starting only during the second trimester to prevent cleft defects"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Folic acid 4 to 5 mg daily, starting about three months before conception): Women taking anticonvulsants such as valproic acid carry a higher risk of neural tube defects, so Canadian guidance advises 4 to 5 mg of folic acid daily beginning about three months before conception and continuing into early pregnancy. Low doses suit women at low risk. Starting after a missed period comes too late, since the neural tube closes within the first weeks of gestation."}, {"id": 1461, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "NP is a 38 year old man who takes ibuprofen 400 mg on about 20 days each month for headaches that have worsened over five months. He says the pain returns as each dose wears off. He asks the pharmacist for a stronger OTC product. What should the pharmacist advise?", "options": {"A": "Continue present use, since overuse headache occurs only with opioids and triptans", "B": "Switch to daily acetaminophen with codeine, which does not contribute to overuse headache", "C": "Increase ibuprofen to 800 mg per dose so that each headache resolves faster and sooner", "D": "Limit simple analgesics to under 15 days monthly, and discuss prevention with the prescriber"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Limit simple analgesics to under 15 days monthly, and discuss prevention with the prescriber): Using a simple analgesic on 15 or more days a month for over three months can cause medication overuse headache, and the rebound pain he describes fits. Cutting back below 15 days, with a preventive plan supervised by the prescriber, is the standard approach. Higher doses and codeine products only deepen the cycle."}, {"id": 1462, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "A community pharmacist receives a new prescription for carbamazepine 200 mg twice daily for mood stabilization for UC, a 47 year old man. The profile shows he has taken clozapine 300 mg daily for four years, with regular blood counts. The prescriber is a locum who did not see the profile. What should the pharmacist do?", "options": {"A": "Dispense the carbamazepine and halve the clozapine dose, since carbamazepine will raise clozapine blood levels", "B": "Hold the fill and call the prescriber, since the combination raises bone marrow risk", "C": "Dispense the carbamazepine and increase the clozapine blood count schedule to weekly, which is sufficient protection", "D": "Dispense the carbamazepine once he confirms he feels well, since long-term clozapine users tolerate new drugs"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Hold the fill and call the prescriber, since the combination raises bone marrow risk): Carbamazepine can cause bone marrow suppression, so combining it with clozapine raises the risk of agranulocytosis, and carbamazepine also induces clozapine metabolism and lowers its levels. The combination is generally avoided, so the prescriber should be contacted before dispensing. More frequent counts do not remove the risk, and halving clozapine would move in the wrong direction."}, {"id": 1463, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "A pharmacy's dispensing system fails and the vendor says the last backup was ten weeks ago. Patient profiles and prescription records created since then cannot be restored. The pharmacist-manager must decide how to meet record obligations. What is the most appropriate response?", "options": {"A": "Ask patients to bring in old labels and recreate profiles only from what they recall", "B": "Rebuild records from original prescriptions and claims, document the loss, and notify the college", "C": "Resume with a new blank database and treat the earlier ten weeks of records as lost", "D": "Have the vendor rebuild data from claims and tell the college only if asked"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Rebuild records from original prescriptions and claims, document the loss, and notify the college): The pharmacy must still keep complete and accurate records, so it should reconstruct what it can from original prescriptions and claim submissions and clearly document what was lost and when. Notifying the college is part of transparent handling of a record keeping failure. Treating the period as lost or relying on patient memory leaves unsafe gaps in medication histories."}, {"id": 1464, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 7, "question": "GH is a 45 year old gardener who cut his hand on a rusty garden tool twelve hours ago. The 1 cm wound is clean, has stopped bleeding and shows no redness. His last tetanus containing vaccine was twelve years ago. What advice should the pharmacist give?", "options": {"A": "No booster is needed for a clean wound until fifteen years after the last dose", "B": "Clean the wound and receive tetanus immune globulin only, since boosters are meant for deep wounds", "C": "Wash the wound with running water and advise a tetanus booster since ten years have passed", "D": "A booster is needed only if the wound is deeper than five centimetres or visibly infected"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Wash the wound with running water and advise a tetanus booster since ten years have passed): For a clean minor wound, a tetanus booster is recommended when more than ten years have passed since the last dose. Immune globulin is reserved for dirty or high risk wounds in people with uncertain or incomplete immunization. Wound size alone does not set the booster rule."}, {"id": 1465, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "CW is a 55 year old woman with an eGFR of 52 mL/min/1.73 m2 and urine ACR of 4 mg/mmol, both stable over six months. She has no other kidney damage markers. She asks what stage of chronic kidney disease this represents. How should the pharmacist classify her kidney function?", "options": {"A": "No chronic kidney disease, because eGFR must fall below 45 mL/min/1.73 m2 before the diagnosis applies", "B": "Stage G2 with normal albuminuria, since eGFR above 45 mL/min/1.73 m2 is classified as mildly reduced", "C": "Stage G3a with moderately increased albuminuria, which meets the definition of chronic kidney disease", "D": "Stage G3b with severely increased albuminuria, because an ACR above 3 mg/mmol is classed as severe"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Stage G3a with moderately increased albuminuria, which meets the definition of chronic kidney disease): An eGFR of 45 to 59 is category G3a, and an ACR of 3 to 30 mg/mmol is moderately increased (A2). Persistence beyond three months with eGFR below 60 or albuminuria meets the definition of CKD. Severely increased albuminuria begins above 30 mg/mmol."}, {"id": 1466, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A 62 year old woman with unstable angina who has had percutaneous coronary intervention is a CYP2C19 *2/*2 poor metabolizer. Her cardiologist wants an antiplatelet with a reliable effect independent of CYP2C19. Which agent is most appropriate to pair with aspirin?", "options": {"A": "Clopidogrel 150 mg daily, a doubled maintenance dose that fully overcomes loss of CYP2C19 activity", "B": "Warfarin with a target INR of 2.0 to 3.0, which replaces dual therapy after stent placement", "C": "Dipyridamole, which acts on phosphodiesterase and is preferred over thienopyridines in poor metabolizers of CYP2C19", "D": "Ticagrelor, which is active without metabolic activation and is not affected by CYP2C19 genotype"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Ticagrelor, which is active without metabolic activation and is not affected by CYP2C19 genotype): Ticagrelor is given in active form and its antiplatelet effect does not depend on CYP2C19, making it a standard alternative for poor metabolizers after stenting. Higher clopidogrel doses only partly improve response in these patients. Warfarin and dipyridamole do not replace a P2Y12 inhibitor after a stent."}, {"id": 1467, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A research team analyzing a negative trial of a new antidepressant tests the effect in 20 different subgroups and finds a significant benefit (p = 0.03) in one, women aged 40 to 50. Why should this finding be treated cautiously?", "options": {"A": "Multiple comparisons raise the chance of a false positive, so the finding only generates hypotheses", "B": "Subgroup findings are more reliable than overall results, because participants within a subgroup are more similar", "C": "The significance level must be raised for subgroups, because each has fewer patients than the sample", "D": "A p value of 0.03 indicates a type II error when it appears in one subgroup"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Multiple comparisons raise the chance of a false positive, so the finding only generates hypotheses): With 20 tests at the 0.05 level, about one false positive is expected by chance alone. A subgroup result that was not prespecified and not corrected for multiple testing should prompt a new confirmatory study, not a change in practice."}, {"id": 1468, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "In a province without mandatory adult abuse reporting, a pharmacist suspects that an older patient is being financially exploited by a relative who collects all of her prescriptions and cheques. The patient seems mentally capable and speaks clearly. What is the best approach?", "options": {"A": "Take no action, because only a court may decide whether a capable adult is being exploited", "B": "Speak with her, offer resources, and involve adult protection if risk or incapacity emerges", "C": "Report to adult protection at once without speaking to the patient, since suspicion must be reported", "D": "Tell the relative about the concern and ask how the cheques and prescriptions were handled"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Speak with her, offer resources, and involve adult protection if risk or incapacity emerges): A capable adult has the right to make her own choices, so the pharmacist should speak with her first, offer support, and escalate when she is at risk or lacks capacity. Confronting the relative may increase her risk."}, {"id": 1469, "domain": "communication_and_collaboration", "chapter": 11, "question": "During teach-back, DH, a 68 year old man, says he will take his new prednisone 40 mg tablets only when his joints hurt. The prescription is for 40 mg each morning for five days for a gout flare. What is the best response from the pharmacist?", "options": {"A": "Repeat the original explanation again, slowly, and write the directions in the margin of the leaflet", "B": "Accept his response and add a note to his profile about poor adherence", "C": "Tell him that he has misunderstood and that he must take the tablets daily", "D": "Re-explain in different words, accept responsibility for the confusion, and repeat teach-back"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Re-explain in different words, accept responsibility for the confusion, and repeat teach-back): A teach-back error means the explanation needs to change, so the pharmacist rephrases, takes ownership and checks again until he can state the correct plan. Correcting him bluntly or repeating the same words does not address the misunderstanding."}, {"id": 1470, "domain": "communication_and_collaboration", "chapter": 11, "question": "A caller says she is a physiotherapist at a nearby clinic and asks for the current medication list of a mutual patient, HJ, a 62 year old man recovering from knee surgery. The pharmacist does not recognize her. What should the pharmacist do before disclosing any information?", "options": {"A": "Give the list over the phone, because physiotherapists form part of the circle of care", "B": "Refuse any information and tell her to ask HJ to bring a printed list himself", "C": "Fax the list to the clinic's general number so a record of the disclosure exists", "D": "Verify identity by calling back the clinic's listed number and confirm she is treating HJ"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Verify identity by calling back the clinic's listed number and confirm she is treating HJ): Even for providers in the circle of care, the pharmacist must be confident of who is asking and that the person is involved in the patient's care. Calling back a clinic number found independently defeats impersonation. Assuming a profession is included in the circle of care is not enough, and faxing to a general line risks a misdirected disclosure."}, {"id": 1471, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "CL is a 26 year old woman at 12 weeks of pregnancy with opioid use disorder, currently using fentanyl daily. She tells the pharmacist she wants to stop all opioids immediately to protect the baby. What is the most appropriate advice?", "options": {"A": "Opioid agonist treatment with methadone or buprenorphine is recommended, and withdrawal alone should be avoided", "B": "Stop at once under home detoxification, since fetal exposure falls quickly and risks end", "C": "Taper off fentanyl over two days using diphenhydramine, which controls all withdrawal symptoms", "D": "Wait until after delivery before any treatment, since medications are riskier than ongoing fentanyl use"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Opioid agonist treatment with methadone or buprenorphine is recommended, and withdrawal alone should be avoided): Withdrawal in pregnancy is associated with relapse, fetal stress and preterm labour. Methadone or buprenorphine stabilizes both mother and fetus and is the recommended treatment, with neonatal monitoring after birth. Rapid detoxification and delaying treatment both leave her at higher risk than treatment would."}, {"id": 1472, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "Out of concern for her well-being, QN, a hospital pharmacist, looks up the medication profile of SV, a 45 year old coworker on sick leave. SV did not consent, and QN has no role in her care. How should this action be characterized?", "options": {"A": "Acceptable if QN documents the reason in the profile and notifies SV by email afterward", "B": "A privacy breach, because access without a care role or consent is not permitted", "C": "A minor policy issue, because no information was shared outside the hospital with other people", "D": "Acceptable, since pharmacists may review any profile in the hospital system when acting out of concern"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A privacy breach, because access without a care role or consent is not permitted): Access to personal health information requires a care relationship or consent, and concern for a coworker creates neither. The access is a privacy breach even though no information left the hospital. Documenting a reason or emailing SV later does not authorize it."}, {"id": 1473, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "TK, a pharmacist at a regional poison centre, collects details on 12 adolescents who developed seizures after using the same energy supplement. She reports their ages, doses and clinical course, with no comparison group. Which design describes her report, and what is its main limitation?", "options": {"A": "Cross-sectional study; it measures prevalence but cannot identify which product patients used before seizures", "B": "Case-control study; it identifies cases but cannot give an odds ratio without recruiting healthy controls", "C": "Case series; with no comparison group it cannot show that the supplement caused seizures", "D": "Retrospective cohort study; it estimates incidence but requires follow-up of all adolescents using the supplement"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Case series; with no comparison group it cannot show that the supplement caused seizures): Describing a handful of affected patients without controls is a case series. It is useful for generating hypotheses and recognizing new harms, but without a comparison group it cannot show that the product, rather than something else, caused the seizures."}, {"id": 1474, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "A prescription for a high-cost cancer drug that the provincial plan does not cover is presented by TY, a 55 year old patient who cannot afford it. TY quietly asks pharmacist BE to compound a cheaper copy of the patented product. How should BE respond ethically and legally?", "options": {"A": "Compound the copy since TY has consented and the compounded version contains the identical active ingredient", "B": "Substitute another cancer drug of her choosing and inform the oncologist at the next visit", "C": "Decline the copy because it is not permitted, and explore assistance programs or alternatives", "D": "Refuse any involvement and tell TY that cost is not a pharmacist concern in drug access"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Decline the copy because it is not permitted, and explore assistance programs or alternatives): Compounding a copy of a commercially available product is not permitted under NAPRA compounding standards, so the request cannot be met. The pharmacist's duty to the patient is still served by exploring assistance programs, compassionate access, or therapeutic alternatives. Substituting another cancer drug independently exceeds the pharmacist's role."}, {"id": 1475, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A hospital introduced an antimicrobial stewardship program and a pharmacist plots monthly fluoroquinolone use for 24 months before and 24 months after. No comparison hospital is available. Which design is this, and what is the main threat to concluding that the program caused the change?", "options": {"A": "Interrupted time series; concurrent events or secular trends may also explain the change", "B": "Prospective cohort study; patients given fluoroquinolones differed from others before the program started", "C": "Randomized trial; the sequence of months was not concealed from the pharmacists reviewing prescribing", "D": "Cross-sectional study; use was measured once so the order of events cannot be determined"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Interrupted time series; concurrent events or secular trends may also explain the change): Repeated measurements before and after an intervention, with the trend modelled, make an interrupted time series. Without a control hospital, a concurrent guideline change, new product or shifting trend could produce the same pattern, so causation is less certain than with a controlled design."}, {"id": 1476, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "A technician uses a min-max system. For one product, the minimum is 10 units and the maximum is 30 units. A count shows 8 units on hand, and 6 units are already on order from the wholesaler. The system triggers an order only when the inventory position falls to the minimum or below. How many units should be ordered now?", "options": {"A": "None, since the 14 units on hand and on order are above the minimum", "B": "22 units, since 30 minus 8 units on hand brings the stock to maximum", "C": "2 units, since only the shortfall below the minimum of 10 needs replacing", "D": "16 units, since 30 minus the 14 units on hand and on order reaches maximum"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (None, since the 14 units on hand and on order are above the minimum): The inventory position is units on hand plus units on order, which is 8 + 6 = 14. That is above the minimum of 10, so no new order is triggered. Ordering up to maximum would apply only if the position had fallen to the minimum or below."}, {"id": 1477, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "QJ is a 75 kg, 63 year old man on a heparin infusion at 14 units/kg/h from a bag of 25 000 units in 500 mL. The aPTT is below target and the nomogram says to increase the rate by 2 units/kg/h. What new pump rate should the pharmacist verify?", "options": {"A": "21 mL per hour, equal to 1050 units per hour from this bag", "B": "24 mL per hour, equal to 1200 units per hour from this bag", "C": "48 mL per hour, equal to 2400 units per hour from this bag", "D": "26 mL per hour, equal to 1300 units per hour from this bag"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (24 mL per hour, equal to 1200 units per hour from this bag): The new dose is 16 x 75 = 1200 units per hour. The bag holds 50 units per mL, so the rate is 1200 / 50 = 24 mL per hour. The 21 mL per hour figure is the old rate, 14 x 75 / 50, which would leave him subtherapeutic."}, {"id": 1478, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A pharmacy and therapeutics committee considers replacing a drug with a cheaper one on the basis of a trial claiming non-inferiority for in-hospital mortality. The prespecified margin was an absolute difference of 8 percentage points, while the observed mortality with the established drug was only 6%. What is the main concern?", "options": {"A": "The trial needs a placebo arm because non-inferiority cannot be judged against an established drug", "B": "The margin is too small for the baseline rate, so power to detect harm is low", "C": "Using an absolute difference is invalid because margins must be expressed as hazard ratios", "D": "The margin exceeds the baseline event rate, so almost any result could be declared non-inferior"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The margin exceeds the baseline event rate, so almost any result could be declared non-inferior): A margin of 8 percentage points is larger than the entire baseline mortality of 6%, so even a doubling or tripling of deaths could fall within it. The committee should not rely on such a margin to justify a switch. Margins may be absolute or relative and active comparators are acceptable."}, {"id": 1479, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "KP is a 41 year old woman with fibromyalgia who asks for oxycodone because widespread pain disturbs her sleep. She has tried no medications so far and is physically inactive. Which approach should the pharmacist recommend as the foundation of treatment?", "options": {"A": "Rest and activity avoidance until pain settles, since exercise tends to worsen fibromyalgia symptoms", "B": "Graded aerobic exercise with sleep hygiene, adding duloxetine or low dose amitriptyline if needed", "C": "Short term oxycodone at the lowest dose, with exercise introduced only after her pain improves", "D": "Daily ibuprofen at full dose, which treats the underlying muscle inflammation behind fibromyalgia pain"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Graded aerobic exercise with sleep hygiene, adding duloxetine or low dose amitriptyline if needed): Guidelines make regular graded aerobic exercise and sleep measures the foundation of fibromyalgia care, with duloxetine, pregabalin or low dose amitriptyline added when needed. Opioids lack evidence of benefit and carry harm. NSAIDs do not work because fibromyalgia is not an inflammatory condition, and rest worsens deconditioning."}, {"id": 1480, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A trial reports a hazard ratio of 0.85 for death with a new drug, with a 95% confidence interval from 0.70 to 1.04. A pharmacist is asked how to interpret the interval for the committee reviewing the drug at a formulary meeting.", "options": {"A": "The interval proves the drug has no effect, because its upper limit is higher than 1.0", "B": "The interval shows that 95% of patients had a hazard between 0.70 and 1.04 during follow-up", "C": "The interval proves the drug works, because the point estimate of 0.85 lies below 1.0", "D": "The interval includes 1.0, so the data fit benefit, no effect or slight harm"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The interval includes 1.0, so the data fit benefit, no effect or slight harm): A confidence interval spanning 1.0 means the trial cannot exclude no difference at the 5% level, yet it also fails to exclude a meaningful benefit of up to 30%. This is an inconclusive result, not evidence of absence of effect."}, {"id": 1481, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "After declining to fill a prescription because of a conscientious objection, LK, a pharmacist, referred the patient to a nearby pharmacy but did not record the event. The patient later complains that she was refused without explanation. What should LK have done?", "options": {"A": "Give a detailed account of LK's moral reasoning so the patient understands the refusal fully", "B": "Tell the patient that the prescriber made an error and that the prescription is not valid", "C": "Avoid mentioning the objection and state only that the drug could not be filled today", "D": "Explain the objection respectfully, confirm access elsewhere, and document what was discussed and referred"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Explain the objection respectfully, confirm access elsewhere, and document what was discussed and referred): Colleges expect an objecting pharmacist to explain respectfully that the objection exists, confirm that the patient can obtain the drug elsewhere, and record what happened. A short, honest statement avoids both a vague refusal and a lengthy moral argument. Blaming the prescriber is dishonest."}, {"id": 1482, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "BO is a 48 year old man recovering from a dental extraction who takes two tablets of acetaminophen 300 mg with codeine 30 mg four times daily. The pharmacist calculates his morphine milligram equivalents with a codeine factor of 0.15 and also checks the daily acetaminophen total. Which statement is correct?", "options": {"A": "He takes 240 MME and 2400 mg of acetaminophen each day", "B": "He takes 36 MME and 2400 mg of acetaminophen per day", "C": "He takes 9 MME and 1200 mg of acetaminophen each day, below the maximum", "D": "He takes 36 MME and 4800 mg of acetaminophen each day, above the maximum"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (He takes 36 MME and 2400 mg of acetaminophen per day): Eight tablets daily give 240 mg of codeine and 2400 mg of acetaminophen. Codeine 240 x 0.15 = 36 MME. The acetaminophen total is under the 4 g daily maximum, though close to the 3 g limit used with liver risk."}, {"id": 1483, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 6, "question": "CS is a 44 year old woman with heart failure who takes ramipril 10 mg and spironolactone 25 mg daily. Her serum potassium is 5.2 mmol/L. She asks for a drospirenone-only contraceptive pill. What should the pharmacist advise? Her blood pressure is stable and she has no known allergies.", "options": {"A": "Start it and stop spironolactone, which is the only interacting drug of concern", "B": "Start it and recheck her potassium in one year, since the progestin dose is small", "C": "Avoid drospirenone, since its antimineralocorticoid effect adds hyperkalemia risk to her current drugs", "D": "Start it with a potassium supplement to offset its effect on her electrolytes"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Avoid drospirenone, since its antimineralocorticoid effect adds hyperkalemia risk to her current drugs): Drospirenone has antimineralocorticoid activity similar to spironolactone and can raise potassium, particularly with an ACE inhibitor and a baseline potassium already above normal. A potassium supplement would worsen the problem. A different progestin or a nonhormonal method is a safer approach."}, {"id": 1484, "domain": "communication_and_collaboration", "chapter": 11, "question": "A pharmacist receives a prescription for alprazolam 0.5 mg, 60 tablets, for a patient whose provincial drug profile shows 90 tablets of the same drug filled elsewhere five days ago. The prescriber cannot be reached. What is the most appropriate action?", "options": {"A": "Fill half the quantity today and ask the patient to explain the earlier prescription to you", "B": "Report the patient to police before speaking with the prescriber or the patient", "C": "Fill the prescription as written, because the patient has a valid order and signature", "D": "Hold the fill, explain the concern openly and keep trying to reach the prescriber"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Hold the fill, explain the concern openly and keep trying to reach the prescriber): Overlapping benzodiazepine supplies raise concern about duplication or misuse, so the pharmacist holds the fill, explains openly and continues to seek clarification. Dispensing without clarification is unsafe, and involving police first is disproportionate."}, {"id": 1485, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A trial comparing mean pain scores reports a p value of 0.03 for a two sample t-test between a new analgesic and placebo. A pharmacist is asked what the p value means when presenting to nurses on the unit. Which statement is correct?", "options": {"A": "The difference between the groups is clinically important because the p value is below 0.05", "B": "There is a 3% probability that the analgesic has no effect on the pain scores", "C": "With no true difference, results this extreme would occur about 3% of the time", "D": "The analgesic reduces pain in 97% of patients who take it compared with those given placebo"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (With no true difference, results this extreme would occur about 3% of the time): A p value is the probability of results at least as extreme as those observed if the null hypothesis of no difference were true. It is not the probability that the drug is ineffective, not the percentage of patients who benefit, and not a measure of clinical importance."}, {"id": 1486, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 6, "question": "TY is a 40 year old man receiving bismuth subsalicylate as part of H. pylori quadruple therapy. After two days he calls worried about black stools and a dark tongue. He has no abdominal pain, dizziness or vomiting. What should the pharmacist explain?", "options": {"A": "Bismuth commonly darkens stool and tongue, which is harmless, unless pain or dizziness develops", "B": "Black stools mean ulcer bleeding, so he should stop everything and go to emergency", "C": "Dark stools signal toxicity from metronidazole, so the metronidazole should be stopped and replaced", "D": "The tongue discoloration shows an allergic reaction to tetracycline, which requires an antihistamine"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Bismuth commonly darkens stool and tongue, which is harmless, unless pain or dizziness develops): Bismuth forms black bismuth sulfide in the gut, so dark stools and a temporary black tongue are common and harmless. He has no signs of bleeding such as dizziness, pain or vomiting, though tarry stools with those symptoms would need urgent review. Metronidazole and tetracycline do not cause this colour change."}, {"id": 1487, "domain": "communication_and_collaboration", "chapter": 11, "question": "QJ is a 52 year old woman who collects a new prescription for tamoxifen and tearfully says she feels overwhelmed by her breast cancer diagnosis. The pharmacist wants to respond with empathy before beginning the counselling session. Which response is most appropriate in this situation at the counter?", "options": {"A": "Many patients feel that way, and the leaflet explains each side effect in detail", "B": "Try not to worry, most women do well on this medication and you will be fine", "C": "I understand how you feel, so let me quickly go through the counselling points", "D": "That sounds like a lot to take in; would it help to go over this together?"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (That sounds like a lot to take in; would it help to go over this together?): Empathy names and validates the feeling and offers support, which builds trust before information is given. Premature reassurance dismisses her concern, and moving quickly to the counselling points or the leaflet shifts away from her emotional state."}, {"id": 1488, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A pharmacist is asked to find the best evidence about whether a newly marketed drug causes a serious adverse reaction occurring in roughly 1 in 5,000 users. Randomized trials enrolled about 3,000 patients in total. Which source is most likely to answer the question reliably?", "options": {"A": "The manufacturer's marketing summary, because it combines all available safety information in one place", "B": "The existing randomized trials, because randomization ensures that rare harms appear at their true frequency", "C": "A single case report, because it provides the strongest evidence on the true rate of harm", "D": "Large observational studies or pharmacovigilance data, because trials were too small to detect rare events"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Large observational studies or pharmacovigilance data, because trials were too small to detect rare events): A reaction at 1 in 5,000 is unlikely to appear in a trial of 3,000 patients. Large cohort studies, registries and spontaneous reporting systems cover many more patients and can detect rare harms. A single case report cannot estimate frequency."}, {"id": 1489, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 6, "question": "TA is a 21 year old woman with an etonogestrel implant inserted four months ago. She reports six weeks of prolonged light bleeding that bothers her. A pregnancy test was negative and she has no pain or fever. Which management is most appropriate for the pharmacist to offer?", "options": {"A": "Advise removal of the implant immediately because prolonged bleeding signals contraceptive failure", "B": "Ibuprofen 400 mg three times daily with food for five days, with reassurance", "C": "Recommend tranexamic acid 1 g four times daily for six weeks to stop her bleeding", "D": "Advise inserting a second implant in the other arm to stabilize her bleeding pattern"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Ibuprofen 400 mg three times daily with food for five days, with reassurance): Irregular bleeding is the most common reason for implant removal, and a short course of a nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug often settles it. Bleeding does not indicate failure of contraception, and a second implant is not an accepted treatment. Long courses of tranexamic acid are not recommended for this problem."}, {"id": 1490, "domain": "communication_and_collaboration", "chapter": 11, "question": "AM is a 31 year old woman with asthma who has not refilled her budesonide inhaler for four months and uses salbutamol daily. She says that steroids 'are dangerous and weaken the body'. What should the pharmacist do first in this conversation at the counter?", "options": {"A": "Refer her back to the prescriber for a different controller without discussing her concerns", "B": "Explain that inhaled steroids are safe at the low doses prescribed and she should restart them", "C": "Encourage her to keep using salbutamol as needed, since it relieves her symptoms quickly", "D": "Ask what she has heard about steroid inhalers, validate her worry, then give facts"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Ask what she has heard about steroid inhalers, validate her worry, then give facts): Beliefs about medicines drive non-adherence, so the pharmacist elicits her concerns and then addresses them respectfully. Simply instructing her ignores the belief, and daily rescue use signals poor control that needs a controller."}, {"id": 1491, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "An investigator comparing two drugs measured five different laboratory outcomes and ran five separate unpaired t-tests at the 0.05 level, finding one significant result (p = 0.04). A pharmacist reviewing the paper critiques the analysis. Which concern is most important?", "options": {"A": "Multiple tests inflate the false positive risk, so the one result may be chance", "B": "Five tests is too few, because the analysis needs at least ten comparisons to be reliable", "C": "A p value of 0.04 is above the usual threshold, so the result is not significant", "D": "A t-test cannot be used for laboratory outcomes, which must be analyzed with a chi-square test"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Multiple tests inflate the false positive risk, so the one result may be chance): Five separate tests at the 0.05 level give roughly a 23% chance of at least one false positive when no true differences exist, so a single p of 0.04 may be chance. A correction for multiple comparisons or a prespecified primary outcome is needed. The test selected suits continuous outcomes, and 0.04 is below 0.05."}, {"id": 1492, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 1, "question": "GW is a 55 year old man with newly confirmed hypertension and an average pressure of 168/102 mmHg, which is more than 20 mmHg systolic above his treatment target. He has no diabetes or compelling indication. The prescriber is deciding between a single drug and a combination. What should the pharmacist suggest?", "options": {"A": "Monotherapy with a thiazide at the lowest dose, adding a second drug only after six months", "B": "A combination of an ACE inhibitor and an ARB, to block the renin angiotensin system fully", "C": "A combination of a beta-blocker and verapamil, to lower pressure and heart rate together", "D": "A single pill combination, such as an ACE inhibitor with amlodipine, to improve adherence"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A single pill combination, such as an ACE inhibitor with amlodipine, to improve adherence): Hypertension Canada supports starting a two drug combination, preferably as a single pill, when pressure is more than 20/10 mmHg above target, because it lowers pressure faster and improves adherence. Combinations should pair complementary classes such as an ACE inhibitor or ARB with a calcium channel blocker or diuretic. Two renin angiotensin blockers or a beta-blocker with verapamil are harmful pairings."}, {"id": 1493, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "MK, 70, takes amlodipine for hypertension. His prescription has no refills left and his physician is on leave for two weeks. MK has two days of tablets remaining. In a province where pharmacists may renew prescriptions, what is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Tell MK to take half of his usual dose until his physician returns from leave", "B": "Assess his stability, renew within provincial authority, document, and notify the prescriber", "C": "Refuse to dispense and refer MK to an emergency department for a new prescription", "D": "Dispense a 90 day supply without documentation because the drug is a common antihypertensive"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Assess his stability, renew within provincial authority, document, and notify the prescriber): Where provincial scope allows, a pharmacist can renew a prescription for a stable chronic condition after assessment, then document the decision and inform the prescriber. Stopping therapy or reducing the dose risks uncontrolled blood pressure."}, {"id": 1494, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 3, "question": "FJ is a 33 year old woman, 6 months postpartum and exclusively breastfeeding a healthy infant, with dysuria and frequency for 2 days and no fever. A pharmacy dipstick shows positive nitrites and leukocyte esterase. She has no allergies and no kidney disease. Which treatment is suitable while she is breastfeeding?", "options": {"A": "Moxifloxacin 400 mg once daily for 5 days, which also treats most urinary organisms", "B": "Amoxicillin 500 mg three times daily for 7 days, as a reliable choice for E. coli", "C": "Nitrofurantoin 100 mg twice daily for 5 days, compatible with her infant's age", "D": "Ciprofloxacin 500 mg twice daily for 7 days, since it is safe in nursing infants"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Nitrofurantoin 100 mg twice daily for 5 days, compatible with her infant's age): Nitrofurantoin passes into milk in small amounts and is acceptable with a healthy infant beyond the first month. Fluoroquinolones are generally avoided in lactation when alternatives exist. Amoxicillin has high resistance rates for community E. coli, so it is not a reliable cystitis treatment."}, {"id": 1495, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "A patient picks up two of three prescriptions because the pharmacy is out of the third, a 30 day supply of metoprolol 50 mg tablets. The pharmacist will order it for delivery tomorrow. Which step best documents and protects the patient?", "options": {"A": "Tell the patient verbally and add a sticky note to the unfilled prescription bag", "B": "Record the balance owing, give the patient a written note, and arrange delivery tomorrow", "C": "Fill a smaller quantity from another strength and tell the patient to take extra tablets", "D": "Release the two prescriptions and remind the patient to return in a few days"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Record the balance owing, give the patient a written note, and arrange delivery tomorrow): A partial release needs a record of what is still owed and clear information to the patient so therapy is not interrupted. A verbal reminder alone is easily forgotten, and combining strengths so the patient takes extra tablets creates a dosing error risk without prescriber or pharmacist planning."}, {"id": 1496, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "DR, who has type 2 diabetes with painful neuropathy and takes gabapentin 600 mg three times daily, asks what to expect when she starts the cannabis her physician authorized. Considering her current medications and history, which counselling point is most appropriate to give her first?", "options": {"A": "Choose a high-potency inhaled product for rapid effect, since inhalation avoids sedation and any drug interactions", "B": "Begin with the full 10 mg dose for faster pain control, since gabapentin offsets sedation", "C": "Use it only at bedtime and expect that it will let her stop metformin and gabapentin", "D": "Start with a low THC dose, increase slowly, and expect added sedation with gabapentin"}, "cluster_id": "CHS11", "cluster_text": "DR is a 58 year old woman with type 2 diabetes and painful diabetic neuropathy. Her medications are metformin 1000 mg twice daily, gabapentin 600 mg three times daily, rosuvastatin 10 mg daily, and escitalopram 10 mg daily. Her eGFR is 72 mL/min/1.73 m2. She has never used cannabis, but her physician has provided a medical document for dried cannabis with a plan of 5 to 10 mg THC daily. She lives alone, drives to the grocery store twice weekly, and asks the pharmacist for counselling.", "cluster_size": 3, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Start with a low THC dose, increase slowly, and expect added sedation with gabapentin): THC can cause dizziness and sedation, and gabapentin adds to the effect, so a low starting dose with slow increases is safest. Starting at the full dose raises the risk of falls and impairment. Cannabis is not a replacement for diabetes or neuropathy medications, and inhaled products still interact."}, {"id": 1497, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "DR decides to take a 10 mg THC edible at bedtime instead of dried cannabis, and she plans to take the dose around 10 pm. She asks whether she may drive to the grocery store the next morning. What should the pharmacist advise?", "options": {"A": "Wait at least eight hours after ingesting it, and longer if she still feels any impairment", "B": "Drive normally after eight hours of sleep, since sleep clears impairment from a bedtime dose", "C": "Wait at least thirty minutes after waking, because impairment lasts only while THC is felt", "D": "Wait two hours after the edible, because ingested THC clears faster than inhaled THC"}, "cluster_id": "CHS11", "cluster_text": "DR is a 58 year old woman with type 2 diabetes and painful diabetic neuropathy. Her medications are metformin 1000 mg twice daily, gabapentin 600 mg three times daily, rosuvastatin 10 mg daily, and escitalopram 10 mg daily. Her eGFR is 72 mL/min/1.73 m2. She has never used cannabis, but her physician has provided a medical document for dried cannabis with a plan of 5 to 10 mg THC daily. She lives alone, drives to the grocery store twice weekly, and asks the pharmacist for counselling.", "cluster_size": 3, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Wait at least eight hours after ingesting it, and longer if she still feels any impairment): Health Canada advises waiting at least eight hours after eating or drinking cannabis before driving, and longer if effects persist, because edible effects last longer than inhaled effects. Two hours or a night of sleep does not guarantee that impairment has ended. Impairment is not limited to the period when the effect is felt."}, {"id": 1498, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "DR returns in six weeks after starting dried cannabis under her physician's plan. She reports better sleep but new episodes of rapid heartbeat, dry mouth, and light-headedness on standing. Her blood pressure is 102/60 mmHg sitting. Which assessment and action is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Recommend a daily dose of an over-the-counter antihistamine to treat the dry mouth and rapid heartbeat", "B": "Reassure her these effects will fade soon and advise increasing the dose to build tolerance", "C": "Suspect THC-related tachycardia and orthostatic hypotension, advise a lower dose, and notify the physician", "D": "Attribute the symptoms to metformin and advise stopping it today to avoid worsening low blood pressure"}, "cluster_id": "CHS11", "cluster_text": "DR is a 58 year old woman with type 2 diabetes and painful diabetic neuropathy. Her medications are metformin 1000 mg twice daily, gabapentin 600 mg three times daily, rosuvastatin 10 mg daily, and escitalopram 10 mg daily. Her eGFR is 72 mL/min/1.73 m2. She has never used cannabis, but her physician has provided a medical document for dried cannabis with a plan of 5 to 10 mg THC daily. She lives alone, drives to the grocery store twice weekly, and asks the pharmacist for counselling.", "cluster_size": 3, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Suspect THC-related tachycardia and orthostatic hypotension, advise a lower dose, and notify the physician): Rapid heartbeat, dry mouth, and light-headedness on standing are typical effects of THC, which can raise heart rate and lower blood pressure on standing. A lower dose and notification of the physician is appropriate. Metformin does not cause tachycardia, a higher dose would worsen the effects, and antihistamines worsen dry mouth."}, {"id": 1499, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 3, "question": "WK is a 70 year old man, 68 kg, with a serum creatinine of 130 umol/L, treated for a febrile urinary tract infection with trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole double strength twice daily for 14 days. After calculating his Cockcroft-Gault clearance, what should the pharmacist conclude?", "options": {"A": "Clearance is about 72 mL/min, so the regimen should be changed to ciprofloxacin instead", "B": "Clearance is about 45 mL/min, so the dose should be halved and given once daily", "C": "Clearance is about 45 mL/min, so no dose reduction is needed for this regimen", "D": "Clearance is about 28 mL/min, so the dose should be halved for the full course"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Clearance is about 45 mL/min, so no dose reduction is needed for this regimen): Clearance is (140 - 70) x 68 x 1.23 / 130 = about 45 mL/min. Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole is usually reduced only when clearance falls below 30 mL/min. He still needs monitoring of potassium and creatinine during therapy."}, {"id": 1500, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "In an open-label trial of a new wound dressing, nurses were aware of which patients were assigned to the new dressing and gave those patients more frequent assessments and extra advice. Healing was faster in that group. Which bias is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Selection bias, because patients who expected faster healing were allowed to choose the new dressing", "B": "Performance bias, because care differed between the groups in ways beyond the dressing itself", "C": "Lead time bias, because the new dressing group was assessed earlier in wound healing", "D": "Recall bias, because nurses remembered the earlier dressing changes of the new dressing group better"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Performance bias, because care differed between the groups in ways beyond the dressing itself): When staff know the assignment, they may treat the groups differently in ways that influence outcomes. The faster healing may reflect the extra attention rather than the dressing. Blinding of care providers, or standardized co-interventions, reduces this bias."}, {"id": 1501, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A formulary committee notes that randomized trials of a new biologic lasted 12 weeks and enrolled 600 patients. A pharmacist is asked why such trials are poorly suited to detecting rare or delayed adverse effects. What is the best explanation to give?", "options": {"A": "They are usually too small and too short to capture rare or late harms", "B": "They rely on retrospective recall of adverse effects, which misses events from the distant past", "C": "They allocate treatment at random, which prevents any harm from being linked to the study drug", "D": "They exclude patients who take other medications, so only interactions can be detected"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (They are usually too small and too short to capture rare or late harms): Rare events need thousands of exposed patients to appear, and late events need years of observation. Typical premarketing trials are sized for efficacy over a short period. Postmarketing cohort studies and surveillance are therefore needed to detect uncommon or delayed harms."}, {"id": 1502, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 4, "question": "JR is a 5 year old boy weighing 18 kg with a fever from a viral illness. His physician has recommended acetaminophen 15 mg/kg per dose. The pharmacy stocks a children's liquid of 160 mg per 5 mL. How many mL should the pharmacist tell the parent to give for one dose?", "options": {"A": "5.6 mL, which delivers a dose of about 180 mg of acetaminophen", "B": "8.4 mL, which delivers a dose of about 270 mg of acetaminophen", "C": "1.7 mL, which delivers a dose of about 54 mg of acetaminophen", "D": "16.9 mL, which delivers a dose of about 540 mg of acetaminophen"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (8.4 mL, which delivers a dose of about 270 mg of acetaminophen): The dose is 18 kg x 15 mg/kg = 270 mg. At 160 mg per 5 mL, the volume is 270 / 160 x 5 = 8.4 mL. The other volumes result from using 10 mg/kg, from using the wrong concentration, or from inverting the calculation."}, {"id": 1503, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 6, "question": "IM is a 28 year old who is five weeks postpartum and exclusively breastfeeding. She had two standard glasses of wine with dinner and asks when it is safe to nurse again. She feels fully alert and has no other medications. What should the pharmacist advise?", "options": {"A": "No wait is needed if she nurses within the first hour, when milk levels stay low", "B": "Pump and discard milk for twenty-four hours, since alcohol stays in milk after blood levels fall", "C": "Wait about twelve hours per drink, since alcohol is eliminated slowly from breast milk", "D": "Wait about two hours per drink, since milk alcohol follows her blood alcohol level"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Wait about two hours per drink, since milk alcohol follows her blood alcohol level): Alcohol passes freely into milk and the level mirrors maternal blood alcohol, so it falls as she metabolizes it, about one standard drink every two hours. After two drinks she should wait about four hours. Pumping and discarding does not speed clearance, and milk levels peak, not stay low, in the first hour."}, {"id": 1504, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 1, "question": "RS is a 64 year old man starting isosorbide mononitrate ER 30 mg each morning for stable angina. Four days later he phones the pharmacy because he has a throbbing headache every morning, and he asks whether he should stop the drug. His pressure and pulse at home are normal. What is the best advice?", "options": {"A": "Double the dose for three days, because this will build tolerance to the headache faster", "B": "Take the tablet with strong coffee, which blocks the headache by constricting the blood vessels", "C": "Headache is common early and usually fades within days, so continue and use acetaminophen", "D": "Stop the drug now, because headache shows that the dose has damaged his blood vessels"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Headache is common early and usually fades within days, so continue and use acetaminophen): Nitrate-induced headache results from cerebral vasodilation and typically lessens over the first week as tolerance develops. Acetaminophen is a reasonable aid, and continuing therapy keeps his angina protected. Doubling the dose would worsen headache and hypotension, and stopping the drug is unnecessary."}, {"id": 1505, "domain": "communication_and_collaboration", "chapter": 11, "question": "BN, a 78 year old woman with a creatinine clearance of 28 mL/min, receives a nurse practitioner's new prescription for gabapentin 600 mg three times daily for neuropathic pain. The pharmacist is concerned about the dose. Which action best supports respectful collaboration and patient safety?", "options": {"A": "Return the prescription to BN and tell her to ask a physician to rewrite it", "B": "Call the nurse practitioner, share the clearance value, and recommend a renally adjusted lower daily dose", "C": "Lower the dose on the label using the pharmacist's judgment and tell BN afterward", "D": "Fill the prescription as written because the nurse practitioner has prescribing authority within her scope"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Call the nurse practitioner, share the clearance value, and recommend a renally adjusted lower daily dose): Gabapentin is renally cleared, and the stated dose is well above the usual range for a clearance of 28 mL/min, so there is a risk of sedation, falls and toxicity. The pharmacist should contact the prescriber, give the data and propose a specific adjustment. Prescribing authority does not remove the duty to question a dose, and changing it unilaterally or deflecting the problem to the patient is not collaborative."}, {"id": 1506, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "A 27 year old man with newly diagnosed bipolar I disorder is starting valproate. His prescriber asks the pharmacist which monitoring is most important at baseline and during ongoing treatment, given his young age and no other conditions. Which recommendation is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "TSH and creatinine every month, since valproate is cleared mainly by the kidneys and affects thyroid", "B": "Serum potassium and an ECG every three months, since valproate commonly causes serious arrhythmias", "C": "Liver function tests and a platelet count at baseline and periodically, along with weight", "D": "A serum valproate level two hours after each dose, since peak levels guide dose adjustment"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Liver function tests and a platelet count at baseline and periodically, along with weight): Valproate can cause hepatotoxicity, thrombocytopenia and weight gain, so liver enzymes, platelets and weight are followed. Renal and thyroid monitoring is central to lithium rather than valproate. Levels, when used, are trough samples rather than peaks."}, {"id": 1507, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "A 59 year old man took dexamethasone 4 mg daily for brain metastases for eight weeks and stopped it suddenly last week because he felt well. He now has marked fatigue, nausea, dizziness on standing and poor appetite. What should the pharmacist recommend?", "options": {"A": "Contact his prescriber urgently, as adrenal insufficiency is possible and replacement may be needed", "B": "Reassure him the symptoms are minor viral effects, and advise rest and fluids this week", "C": "Advise an over-the-counter multivitamin with iron, since the symptoms suggest a mild nutrient shortage", "D": "Suggest he restart half of the old dose after a week without contacting his prescriber"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Contact his prescriber urgently, as adrenal insufficiency is possible and replacement may be needed): Eight weeks of dexamethasone suppresses the adrenal axis, and sudden cessation can cause adrenal insufficiency with fatigue, nausea, orthostatic dizziness and weakness. This needs prompt medical assessment because cortisol replacement may be required and crisis can develop. Treating it as a viral illness delays recognition of a potentially dangerous problem."}, {"id": 1508, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 7, "question": "NM is a 78 year old woman in a retirement residence who has taken quetiapine 25 mg at bedtime for insomnia for eight months. She has no psychosis or bipolar disorder and has gained 4 kg. Staff ask the pharmacist for a deprescribing recommendation. What is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Increase to 50 mg for one week so the sedative effect can be tested first", "B": "Replace quetiapine with lorazepam 0.5 mg nightly, since benzodiazepines are preferred sleep aids for older adults", "C": "Continue quetiapine, because stopping any antipsychotic leads to relapse of psychosis within a few days", "D": "Halve the dose for two weeks, then stop, and add sleep hygiene measures"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Halve the dose for two weeks, then stop, and add sleep hygiene measures): Low dose quetiapine for insomnia lacks good evidence and adds metabolic and fall risks, so a gradual reduction with non-drug sleep measures is appropriate. She has no psychotic disorder, so relapse of psychosis is not the concern. Benzodiazepines are also avoided in older adults, and raising the dose adds harm."}, {"id": 1509, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "YN is a 64 year old man with type 2 diabetes taking dapagliflozin 10 mg daily and metformin. He is scheduled for elective knee replacement surgery in one week. What should the pharmacist advise about dapagliflozin? His kidney function is normal and his A1c is 7.2%.", "options": {"A": "hold it three days before the procedure to lower euglycemic ketoacidosis risk", "B": "stop it only on the day before surgery, since one missed dose clears the drug", "C": "increase the dose the week before surgery, since extra glucose lowering protects wound healing", "D": "continue it right up to the morning of surgery, since stopping it causes rebound hyperglycemia afterward"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (hold it three days before the procedure to lower euglycemic ketoacidosis risk): SGLT2 inhibitors are held about three days before planned surgery because perioperative fasting and stress raise the risk of euglycemic diabetic ketoacidosis, and the drug effect lasts several days. Doses are restarted when the patient is eating normally."}, {"id": 1510, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 1, "question": "CY is a 72 year old man transferred to a long-term care home with heart failure and an ejection fraction of 30%. His list includes metoprolol tartrate 50 mg twice daily, ramipril and furosemide, started by his former physician. The pharmacist is doing the admission medication review. Which recommendation is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Stop the beta-blocker, as long-term care residents with reduced ejection fraction do not benefit from it", "B": "Continue metoprolol tartrate, as it improves survival in heart failure just like the extended release form", "C": "Change to atenolol, since a more beta-1 selective drug is better tolerated in heart failure", "D": "Suggest bisoprolol or carvedilol, the beta-blockers with proven benefit in heart failure"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Suggest bisoprolol or carvedilol, the beta-blockers with proven benefit in heart failure): Only bisoprolol, carvedilol and sustained release metoprolol succinate (not marketed in Canada) have mortality data in heart failure with reduced ejection fraction. Immediate release metoprolol tartrate and atenolol were not shown to give the same benefit. Older adults still benefit when the drug is titrated carefully."}, {"id": 1511, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 3, "question": "GN is a 76 year old man brought from a long term care home with pneumonia. Assessment shows confusion, respiratory rate 32 per minute, urea 9.2 mmol/L and blood pressure 88/50 mmHg. Based on severity scoring, where should he be treated, and what does the score indicate?", "options": {"A": "Short stay ward admission, because a score of 3 reflects moderate pneumonia that needs brief observation", "B": "Hospital, likely intensive care, since a CURB-65 score of 5 indicates severe pneumonia with high mortality", "C": "Home with oral antibiotics since a CURB-65 score of 1 or 2 permits outpatient care", "D": "Home with close follow up by telephone, because nursing staff monitor residents closely"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Hospital, likely intensive care, since a CURB-65 score of 5 indicates severe pneumonia with high mortality): He scores one point each for confusion, urea above 7 mmol/L, respiratory rate 30 or more, low blood pressure and age 65 or older, for a total of 5. A score of 3 to 5 indicates severe disease needing hospital care, with 4 to 5 often requiring intensive care. Care home residence does not substitute for acute treatment."}, {"id": 1512, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "A pharmacist is sorting the narcotic and controlled substance cabinet. An assistant says methylphenidate, used for ADHD in a 12 year old patient, falls under the Benzodiazepines and Other Targeted Substances Regulations. How should the pharmacist respond to the assistant?", "options": {"A": "Methylphenidate is a controlled drug under Part G of the Food and Drug Regulations", "B": "Correct, because every drug that can be abused is listed under the targeted substances regulations federally", "C": "Methylphenidate is a narcotic and follows the Narcotic Control Regulations, including the ban on refills", "D": "Correct, because stimulants used for ADHD are classed as targeted substances along with benzodiazepines"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Methylphenidate is a controlled drug under Part G of the Food and Drug Regulations): Methylphenidate is a controlled drug regulated under Part G of the Food and Drug Regulations, which has its own prescription and record keeping rules. The targeted substances regulations cover benzodiazepines and a defined list of other drugs. It is not classified as a narcotic."}, {"id": 1513, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 3, "question": "RB is a 38 year old woman whose chart lists penicillin allergy. She explains that when she took penicillin years ago she had nausea and vomiting but no rash, swelling or breathing difficulty. The pharmacist is updating her record. How should this reaction be documented?", "options": {"A": "As an intolerance, because nausea and vomiting are side effects rather than allergic reactions", "B": "As a possible cephalosporin allergy, because gastrointestinal symptoms indicate cross reactivity between these drug classes", "C": "As a delayed hypersensitivity reaction that should prevent any future use of beta-lactam antibiotics", "D": "As a severe penicillin allergy, since gastrointestinal reactions can precede an anaphylactic response later"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (As an intolerance, because nausea and vomiting are side effects rather than allergic reactions): Nausea and vomiting are predictable, non-immune adverse effects, so they are recorded as an intolerance. Calling them an allergy wrongly removes penicillins and sometimes cephalosporins from her future antibiotic use. Gastrointestinal upset does not signal cross reactivity or later anaphylaxis."}, {"id": 1514, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A new lipid-modifying drug raises HDL cholesterol markedly in phase 2 trials. A patient, 57 year old DK with a prior myocardial infarction, asks the pharmacist at a cardiac rehabilitation clinic whether it will prevent another heart attack. What is the most accurate response?", "options": {"A": "The drug should start now because higher HDL has cut cardiovascular risk in all earlier trials", "B": "The HDL rise is a clinical outcome, so the drug is known to prevent heart attacks", "C": "Surrogate results from phase 2 trials are more reliable than outcome trials, being measured sooner", "D": "HDL raising is a surrogate, and cardiovascular benefit is unproven until outcome trials report"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (HDL raising is a surrogate, and cardiovascular benefit is unproven until outcome trials report): HDL cholesterol is a laboratory marker. Drugs that raise HDL, including niacin and CETP inhibitors, have not consistently reduced cardiovascular events in outcome trials. Until such a trial reports, the patient should be told that benefit is unproven."}, {"id": 1515, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 7, "question": "GL is a 45 year old man taking sertraline 100 mg daily who asks to buy St John's wort capsules for persistent low mood. He has no other medical conditions. What should the pharmacist advise? He drinks little alcohol and has had no previous reactions to medicines.", "options": {"A": "It is acceptable if taken twelve hours apart to avoid competition in the gut", "B": "The combination is acceptable if his sertraline dose is lowered by half while taking it", "C": "Avoid the combination, since additive serotonergic effects increase the risk of serotonin syndrome", "D": "It is safe because St John's wort acts only on dopamine receptors in the brain"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Avoid the combination, since additive serotonergic effects increase the risk of serotonin syndrome): St John's wort has serotonergic activity, so combining it with an SSRI can cause serotonin syndrome. Dose spacing does not prevent a pharmacodynamic interaction. He should discuss his persistent low mood with his prescriber rather than self treat."}, {"id": 1516, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "AB is a 57 year old man starting glimepiride 2 mg daily for type 2 diabetes. He usually skips breakfast, has a large lunch, and works night shifts twice a week. He asks when to take the tablet and what to do on days he eats late. What counselling is best?", "options": {"A": "Take it at bedtime each night, eating a snack if his glucose falls below 4 mmol/L", "B": "Take it at the same time daily, even when he skips a meal, for steady levels", "C": "Take it with his first main meal, and omit it if no meal is eaten", "D": "Double the dose on days he eats a large meal, so glucose after lunch stays controlled"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Take it with his first main meal, and omit it if no meal is eaten): Sulfonylureas stimulate insulin release with or without food, so they should be linked to a meal and left out when no meal is eaten to avoid hypoglycemia. Fixed clock timing or bedtime dosing ignores his irregular eating. Doubling the dose for larger meals increases the risk of severe lows."}, {"id": 1517, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "ND is a 60 year old man who started allopurinol 100 mg daily four weeks ago for gout with tophi. His serum urate is now 450 umol/L, eGFR is 60 mL/min/1.73 m2 and he has had no rash or flare. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Keep the 100 mg dose since targets apply only after six months at one dose", "B": "Stop allopurinol and switch to daily colchicine since urate lowering failed after four weeks of treatment", "C": "Jump to 600 mg daily since larger steps do not raise hypersensitivity risk", "D": "Increase allopurinol by 100 mg increments every few weeks until urate falls below 360 umol/L"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Increase allopurinol by 100 mg increments every few weeks until urate falls below 360 umol/L): A urate above target on a starting dose calls for stepwise titration, typically every 2 to 5 weeks, until urate is below 360 umol/L (below 300 with tophi). Slow increases keep hypersensitivity risk low. Colchicine prevents flares but does not lower urate."}, {"id": 1518, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "RP, 55, has a profile entry reading 'sulfa allergy: rash with sulfamethoxazole'. A new prescription for furosemide 40 mg daily for ankle swelling is entered and a drug allergy alert fires. The technician asks whether to cancel the order. What is the best response by the pharmacist?", "options": {"A": "Hold the prescription and ask the prescriber to change to a drug without a sulfonamide group", "B": "Delete the allergy from the profile because sulfonamide antibiotics and diuretics are the same class", "C": "Cross-reactivity is unlikely with this non-antibiotic sulfonamide, so document the review and proceed with filling", "D": "Dispense but advise RP to stop furosemide if any skin change occurs after the first week"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Cross-reactivity is unlikely with this non-antibiotic sulfonamide, so document the review and proceed with filling): Allergy to a sulfonamide antibiotic does not reliably predict reaction to non-antibiotic sulfonamides such as furosemide, because the structures differ. Reviewing the history and documenting the decision is appropriate, and the allergy entry should remain on the profile."}, {"id": 1519, "domain": "leadership_and_stewardship", "chapter": 11, "question": "During a norovirus outbreak at a care home, the pharmacist is asked how to prepare 2 L of a 0.1 percent chlorine disinfectant from household bleach containing 5 percent sodium hypochlorite. How much bleach should be added to water to prepare the solution?", "options": {"A": "100 mL of bleach, with water added to reach a total volume of 2 L", "B": "200 mL of bleach, with water added to reach a total volume of 2 L", "C": "40 mL of bleach, with water added to reach a total volume of 2 L", "D": "4 mL of bleach, with water added to reach a total volume of 2 L"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (40 mL of bleach, with water added to reach a total volume of 2 L): Using C1 x V1 = C2 x V2, the volume of bleach needed is 0.1 x 2000 mL divided by 5, which equals 40 mL. This is a 1 in 50 dilution. Four millilitres would give only 0.01 percent, a tenfold slip that is far too weak for outbreak disinfection, while 100 or 200 mL would give 0.25 or 0.5 percent and corrode surfaces and irritate staff."}, {"id": 1520, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 7, "question": "RV is a 76 year old man with a previous myocardial infarction who takes ramipril and metoprolol only. A pharmacist applying START criteria compares his list with his history. His LDL cholesterol is 3.4 mmol/L and he has no bleeding history. What omission should be flagged?", "options": {"A": "Missing a benzodiazepine, since anxiety after a heart attack is common and should be treated routinely", "B": "Missing statin and antiplatelet therapy, to be raised with the prescriber after reviewing goals", "C": "Missing a daily NSAID, since long term analgesia is routinely needed after a heart attack", "D": "Missing a second beta-blocker, since combining two agents improves survival after myocardial infarction"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Missing statin and antiplatelet therapy, to be raised with the prescriber after reviewing goals): START criteria identify beneficial drugs that are missing, and secondary prevention after myocardial infarction normally includes a statin and an antiplatelet agent. His LDL is above target and he has no bleeding history that would argue against them. The decision still depends on his goals of care and life expectancy, so it is raised with the prescriber."}, {"id": 1521, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "CF, an 83 year old woman, returns to a community pharmacy with her daughter. The daughter reports that the label on the new bottle of glyburide says take 2 tablets twice daily, while the prescription was take 1 tablet twice daily. CF has taken two doses so far today. What is the best first action?", "options": {"A": "Replace the bottle with a correctly labelled one and ask the daughter to monitor at home", "B": "Document the discrepancy and file an incident report before contacting the patient or prescriber", "C": "Take back the bottle, correct the label and tell the daughter to give less tomorrow", "D": "Assess CF for hypoglycemia symptoms and check glucose now, then notify the prescriber"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Assess CF for hypoglycemia symptoms and check glucose now, then notify the prescriber): An older adult who has taken double the intended sulfonylurea dose is at real risk of prolonged hypoglycemia, so assessment and glucose checking come first, followed by prescriber notification and then correction, disclosure and reporting. Paperwork before assessment, or a corrected bottle with no assessment, delays recognition of harm."}, {"id": 1522, "domain": "communication_and_collaboration", "chapter": 11, "question": "A community pharmacist has time for one comprehensive medication review this afternoon and wants to choose the patient who will benefit most. The pharmacist is guided by risk factors for medication related harm and wants to prevent readmission. Which patient should be reviewed first?", "options": {"A": "A woman who takes one statin at a stable dose and collects it punctually each month", "B": "A man discharged two days ago on twelve medications from four prescribers, with several changes", "C": "A man who started a single new antibiotic today for a short course of treatment", "D": "A young woman who asks for emergency contraception and has no regular medications to report"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A man discharged two days ago on twelve medications from four prescribers, with several changes): Risk of medication related harm rises with the number of drugs, the number of prescribers and recent transitions of care or changes. A recent discharge on many medications from several prescribers carries the highest risk of discrepancies and adverse events. The other patients have simple regimens, short courses or stable therapy."}, {"id": 1523, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "PB notices a pharmacy assistant opening the profile of a neighbour who is not being served and is not the assistant's patient. The assistant says it was only curiosity and nothing was shared with anyone. How should PB treat the situation?", "options": {"A": "Give a verbal warning and take no further action, since the assistant is trained on privacy", "B": "Treat it as an unauthorized access breach, stop it, and follow the pharmacy's breach procedure", "C": "Ask the neighbour to sign a form agreeing the assistant may view the profile in future", "D": "Treat it as harmless, since no information left the building and the assistant gave no details"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Treat it as an unauthorized access breach, stop it, and follow the pharmacy's breach procedure): Viewing a record without a need related to care is itself an unauthorized use, even when nothing is disclosed. The pharmacy must contain it, document it, assess notification duties, and apply its disciplinary and training processes."}, {"id": 1524, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "DK, 81, takes citalopram 40 mg daily. A new prescription for azithromycin 500 mg on day one, then 250 mg daily for four days, is entered and a QT prolongation alert appears. No recent ECG is on file. What should the pharmacist do?", "options": {"A": "Dispense azithromycin and advise DK to take it at least four hours apart from citalopram", "B": "Dispense azithromycin and tell DK to halve the citalopram dose until the antibiotic is finished", "C": "Call the prescriber about another antibiotic and the citalopram dose above the older adult maximum", "D": "Dispense azithromycin because the QT effect of a short course is too small to matter"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Call the prescriber about another antibiotic and the citalopram dose above the older adult maximum): Citalopram dose-dependently prolongs the QT interval, and Health Canada advises a maximum of 20 mg daily in patients over 65. Adding azithromycin raises risk further, and separating the doses does not remove the pharmacodynamic effect. The prescriber should review both issues."}, {"id": 1525, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "The pharmacist prepares 500 mL of a 1:10,000 w/v potassium permanganate solution for a patient's foot soak. Potassium permanganate crystals are weighed on a balance that can measure accurately to 10 mg. How much potassium permanganate is required for the final volume?", "options": {"A": "500 mg, because a 1:10,000 w/v strength means 1 g in each 1,000 mL", "B": "50 mg, because a 1:10,000 w/v strength means 1 g in each 10,000 mL", "C": "5 g, because a 1:10,000 w/v strength means 1 g in each 100 mL", "D": "5 mg, because a 1:10,000 w/v strength means 1 g in each 100,000 mL"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (50 mg, because a 1:10,000 w/v strength means 1 g in each 10,000 mL): The ratio 1:10,000 w/v means 1 g in 10,000 mL, so 500 mL needs 500 / 10,000 g, which is 0.05 g or 50 mg. This amount is 5 times the balance sensitivity of 10 mg, so it can be weighed directly. The other amounts come from misreading the ratio by factors of ten."}, {"id": 1526, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 7, "question": "MB is a 33 year old who returned from Ghana ten days ago and now has a temperature of 39.2 C, headache and chills. She took her malaria tablets irregularly. She asks for acetaminophen. What should the pharmacist advise? She has shaking chills and has not eaten today.", "options": {"A": "Seek same day emergency assessment, with urgent malaria blood film testing", "B": "Restart the leftover malaria tablets at the usual dose and watch for improvement", "C": "Rest and drink oral rehydration solution, since fever after travel usually reflects a viral illness", "D": "Take acetaminophen and fluids, then see a doctor if fever persists beyond a week"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Seek same day emergency assessment, with urgent malaria blood film testing): Fever after travel to a malaria area is malaria until proven otherwise, and falciparum malaria can progress rapidly. Diagnosis needs urgent blood testing, and leftover prophylaxis tablets are not treatment doses. Delay risks severe disease."}, {"id": 1527, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "A hospital pharmacist is asked about treatment of severe neuroleptic malignant syndrome in a 42 year old man with a temperature of 40.1 C, marked rigidity and rising creatine kinase. The antipsychotic has been stopped and fluids started. Which drug is most appropriate to add for severe rigidity?", "options": {"A": "Cyproheptadine, which antagonizes the serotonin receptors responsible for rigidity and fever in this condition", "B": "Dantrolene or bromocriptine, which counter muscle rigidity and dopamine blockade in severe cases", "C": "Haloperidol at a lower dose to control agitation and hallucinations during recovery", "D": "Metoclopramide, which relieves nausea and increases dopamine transmission in the basal ganglia"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Dantrolene or bromocriptine, which counter muscle rigidity and dopamine blockade in severe cases): Neuroleptic malignant syndrome results from central dopamine blockade, and severe cases beyond stopping the drug and cooling use dantrolene to relax muscle or bromocriptine to restore dopamine tone. Antipsychotics and dopamine antagonists such as metoclopramide worsen the syndrome. Cyproheptadine is for serotonin syndrome."}, {"id": 1528, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "A pharmacy assistant reconstitutes a 150 mL bottle of amoxicillin powder for oral suspension with the volume of water printed on the manufacturer's label, and makes no other change. The pharmacist asks how the beyond-use date should be assigned. Which approach applies to this simple reconstitution?", "options": {"A": "Use the storage time and temperature stated in the manufacturer's labelling for the reconstituted product", "B": "Assign the dry powder's printed expiry date, since adding water does not change its stability profile", "C": "Assign seven days refrigerated as the default for any liquid antibiotic prepared by a pharmacy", "D": "Assign 30 days from reconstitution, the general limit for water-based liquids prepared in a community pharmacy"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Use the storage time and temperature stated in the manufacturer's labelling for the reconstituted product): When a commercial product is reconstituted exactly as the manufacturer directs, the manufacturer's data on stability after reconstitution govern the dating, including the storage temperature. The general dating tables for compounded preparations apply only when the pharmacy changes or combines products. The powder's printed expiry applies to the dry product only."}, {"id": 1529, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "HG, a 45 year old man with psoriasis, has a prescription for 500 g of 3% coal tar ointment. The pharmacy stocks only 5% coal tar ointment and 1% coal tar ointment in the same base. The pharmacist decides to mix the two strengths. Which combination gives exactly 3% in the final 500 g?", "options": {"A": "Mix 250 g of the 5% ointment with 250 g of the 1% ointment, then stir", "B": "Mix 300 g of the 5% ointment with 200 g of the 1% ointment, then stir", "C": "Mix 150 g of the 5% ointment with 350 g of the 1% ointment, then stir", "D": "Mix 200 g of the 5% ointment with 300 g of the 1% ointment, then stir"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Mix 250 g of the 5% ointment with 250 g of the 1% ointment, then stir): Alligation gives the parts of each stock as the difference between the target and the other strength: 2 parts of 5% and 2 parts of 1%, so equal amounts. Half of 500 g is 250 g of each, and the check is (250 x 5 + 250 x 1) / 500, which equals 3%. Other splits give weaker or stronger products than ordered."}, {"id": 1530, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 6, "question": "GP is a 67 year old man who had a myocardial infarction with a stent six weeks ago and takes aspirin 81 mg and ticagrelor 90 mg twice daily. Endoscopy for dyspepsia shows a small duodenal ulcer without bleeding. His physician asks whether to stop aspirin. What should the pharmacist recommend?", "options": {"A": "Stop both antiplatelets now and restart them once the ulcer has fully healed", "B": "Continue antiplatelet therapy, add a PPI, and test for and treat H. pylori", "C": "Stop aspirin and continue ticagrelor alone while an H2 receptor antagonist heals the ulcer", "D": "Switch aspirin to enteric coated aspirin, since this removes the ulcer risk from antiplatelet therapy"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Continue antiplatelet therapy, add a PPI, and test for and treat H. pylori): Stopping antiplatelet therapy six weeks after a stent places him at high risk of stent thrombosis, which outweighs the risk from a non bleeding ulcer. The ulcer should be treated with a PPI and H. pylori should be tested for and eradicated if present. Enteric coating does not prevent aspirin ulcers, and H2 blockers are less effective."}, {"id": 1531, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "MB is a 68 year old man who has taken metformin for seven years. He now reports numbness and tingling in both feet, and his hemoglobin shows a mild macrocytic anemia. His eGFR is normal. Which test should the pharmacist suggest?", "options": {"A": "a thyroid stimulating hormone level, since metformin commonly causes hypothyroidism after years of use", "B": "a serum vitamin B12 level, since long-term metformin can reduce vitamin B12 absorption", "C": "a fasting insulin level, since metformin commonly leads to pancreatic failure over time", "D": "a serum lactate level, since metformin neuropathy comes from chronic lactic acid build-up"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (a serum vitamin B12 level, since long-term metformin can reduce vitamin B12 absorption): Long-term metformin is associated with vitamin B12 deficiency, which can cause neuropathy and macrocytic anemia, and periodic monitoring is recommended. Lactic acidosis is an acute risk and does not produce a chronic neuropathy."}, {"id": 1532, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "A pharmacist is asked to prepare 60 g of hydrocortisone 2.5% cream for a 6 year old (AJ) with eczema. The pharmacy stocks only 1% and 5% hydrocortisone creams in the same cream base. How much of each commercial cream should be mixed to make the 60 g?", "options": {"A": "37.5 g of the 5% cream with 22.5 g of the 1% cream, giving about 3.5%", "B": "15 g of the 5% cream with 45 g of the 1% cream, giving about 2%", "C": "30 g of the 5% cream and 30 g of 1% cream, which averages to 3%", "D": "22.5 g of the 5% cream mixed with 37.5 g of the 1% cream"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (22.5 g of the 5% cream mixed with 37.5 g of the 1% cream): By alligation, the difference between the target and the weaker cream gives 1.5 parts of the 5% cream, and the difference between the stronger cream and the target gives 2.5 parts of the 1% cream, for 4 parts in total. Sixty grams split in that ratio gives 22.5 g and 37.5 g. Check: 1.125 g plus 0.375 g is 1.5 g in 60 g, which is 2.5%."}, {"id": 1533, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 1, "question": "UN is a 59 year old woman with type 2 diabetes, an A1c of 7.1% and an albumin to creatinine ratio of 12 mg/mmol on repeat testing. Her pressure averages 142/86 mmHg and she takes only metformin. Her eGFR is 78 mL/min. The prescriber asks the pharmacist to recommend the most appropriate first antihypertensive.", "options": {"A": "A dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker, because it reduces albuminuria more than other drug classes", "B": "An ACE inhibitor or ARB, because both reduce albuminuria and slow kidney disease progression", "C": "A beta-blocker such as atenolol, because it protects the kidneys and the heart in diabetes", "D": "A thiazide diuretic, because it lowers pressure effectively and does not affect glucose levels"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (An ACE inhibitor or ARB, because both reduce albuminuria and slow kidney disease progression): In diabetes with albuminuria, an ACE inhibitor or ARB is the preferred first-line agent because it lowers pressure and reduces albuminuria and kidney disease progression. Thiazides can modestly raise glucose, atenolol offers no renal protection, and dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers do not reduce albuminuria meaningfully."}, {"id": 1534, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 6, "question": "HV is a 68 year old woman discharged ten days ago after clindamycin therapy for a leg infection. She now has eight watery stools daily, abdominal cramping and a low-grade fever, and she asks for loperamide to stop the diarrhea. The pharmacist should recommend:", "options": {"A": "Take loperamide 4 mg, then 2 mg after each stool, and recheck after two days", "B": "Start oral rehydration and a probiotic for one week, then see her physician if no better", "C": "Avoid loperamide and arrange prompt medical assessment, including stool testing for Clostridioides difficile", "D": "Use bismuth subsalicylate 30 mL every hour until her stools have clearly improved"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Avoid loperamide and arrange prompt medical assessment, including stool testing for Clostridioides difficile): Severe diarrhea with fever after clindamycin raises strong suspicion of C. difficile infection. Antimotility drugs can trap toxin and risk toxic megacolon, so they should be avoided until infection is excluded. Delaying assessment for a week is unsafe."}, {"id": 1535, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A medication safety bulletin states that switching to a new protocol 'reduced falls by 40%' in long-term care. Residents falling fell from 5 per 100 to 3 per 100 over a year. The pharmacist is asked whether the 40% figure is accurate and how to describe it to families.", "options": {"A": "It is inaccurate because the relative reduction should be 60%, calculated from the final fall rate", "B": "It is accurate, and families should be told that 40 of every 100 residents avoid falling", "C": "It is inaccurate because the true reduction is 2%, which is the only valid summary", "D": "It is a correct relative reduction, with an absolute reduction of 2 per 100"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (It is a correct relative reduction, with an absolute reduction of 2 per 100): A fall from 5 to 3 per 100 is a relative reduction of 40%, so the bulletin figure is correct, but the absolute reduction is only 2 per 100 residents. Families are best served by hearing both figures. The relative figure alone suggests 40 in 100 residents benefit, which is not what happened."}, {"id": 1536, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "AD is a 64 year old woman with type 2 diabetes and peripheral neuropathy who finds a small blister on her heel from new shoes. It is not painful and has no drainage. She asks whether she can pop it and carry on. What should the pharmacist advise?", "options": {"A": "Pop it with a sterilized needle, apply antibiotic ointment, and keep wearing the shoes with padding", "B": "Leave it intact, cover it, stop wearing those shoes, and have it checked soon", "C": "Apply a strong adhesive bandage and continue walking normally, since it causes her no pain", "D": "Soak the foot in hot water with Epsom salts daily until the blister dries up"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Leave it intact, cover it, stop wearing those shoes, and have it checked soon): Neuropathy hides pain, so a minor friction blister can progress to an ulcer without warning. The roof of the blister protects the skin underneath, the shoe causing the pressure must be removed, and a clinician should review it promptly. Popping it, hot soaks or continued walking raise the risk of infection or burns."}, {"id": 1537, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "A hospital pharmacy committee is selecting safeguards for high-alert medications such as insulin, opioids and anticoagulants in its inpatient dispensing area, after a review of local near misses involving dose calculation errors. Which strategy is best supported by safe medication practice guidance?", "options": {"A": "Verbal confirmation between two staff who read the order aloud together before release", "B": "Independent double checks of dose and calculations by a second person before release", "C": "Posted reminders in the work area asking staff to be extra careful with these drugs", "D": "A single check by the most experienced pharmacist on shift to speed up verification"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Independent double checks of dose and calculations by a second person before release): An independent check, in which a second person performs the calculation or verification without seeing the original result, catches errors that one person misses. Reminders and shared verbal confirmations depend on vigilance and lose the independence that makes the check effective."}, {"id": 1538, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "SW is a 60 year old man who takes carvedilol 12.5 mg, two tablets in the morning and one in the evening. The provincial plan covers up to a 90 day supply per claim. How many tablets should the pharmacist dispense for one 90 day claim?", "options": {"A": "270 tablets, since the patient uses three tablets each day for 90 days", "B": "360 tablets, since the patient uses four tablets each day for 90 days", "C": "135 tablets, since the patient uses one and a half tablets each day for 90 days", "D": "180 tablets, since the patient uses two tablets each day for 90 days"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (270 tablets, since the patient uses three tablets each day for 90 days): Daily use is 2 + 1 = 3 tablets, so 90 days requires 3 x 90 = 270 tablets. Counting only the morning dose or treating each dose as a single tablet gives a quantity that does not match the stated regimen."}, {"id": 1539, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "After a dispensing error reaches a patient and causes mild harm, a pharmacist worries that disclosure will lead to a claim against the pharmacy. The pharmacist's liability policy requires prompt notice of any incident, and the patient has phoned the pharmacy to report symptoms. What should the pharmacist do?", "options": {"A": "Make sure the patient is safe, disclose the error, and notify the liability insurer promptly", "B": "Contact the insurer and disclose to the patient only after the insurer approves the message wording", "C": "Record the error internally and wait for a complaint before involving the insurer, to avoid alarm", "D": "Disclose to the patient, then offer a personal payment for costs so the matter settles quietly"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Make sure the patient is safe, disclose the error, and notify the liability insurer promptly): Patient safety and honest disclosure come before concerns about liability, and most policies require early notice so that coverage is not jeopardized. Settling privately or delaying disclosure undermines the patient's trust and the insurer's ability to assist."}, {"id": 1540, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "The mother of PE, a 6 year old boy with epilepsy, receives a prescription for midazolam as a seizure rescue medication. She asks the pharmacist when she should give it and what she should do afterward. What is the best counselling?", "options": {"A": "Give it at the first twitch, and skip the emergency call if the seizure stops", "B": "Give it for a seizure beyond five minutes, then call 911 and monitor his breathing", "C": "Give it only after 20 minutes of seizure, because earlier use can cause breathing to stop", "D": "Give it daily before school to prevent seizures, and repeat it every four hours as needed"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Give it for a seizure beyond five minutes, then call 911 and monitor his breathing): Rescue benzodiazepines are used to stop a prolonged convulsive seizure, commonly one lasting longer than five minutes, with emergency services called and breathing monitored because respiratory depression can occur. They are not a daily preventive, and giving them for brief twitching exposes the child to needless sedation. Waiting twenty minutes delays treatment of status epilepticus."}, {"id": 1541, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 3, "question": "CG is a 55 year old woman who receives a prescription for a single 3 g fosfomycin sachet for uncomplicated cystitis. She has no allergies and normal kidney function. She asks the pharmacist how to take it. Which counselling is correct?", "options": {"A": "Swallow the granules dry, then repeat one sachet every day for 3 days", "B": "Dissolve it in hot tea and repeat the dose if symptoms persist after 24 hours", "C": "Take one sachet twice daily with meals for 5 days until the course finishes", "D": "Dissolve the sachet in half a glass of water and drink it once"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Dissolve the sachet in half a glass of water and drink it once): Fosfomycin trometamol granules are mixed in about 90 to 120 mL of water and taken as one dose, ideally on an empty stomach and before bed after emptying the bladder. Dry granules are not swallowed, and the product is not repeated daily or twice daily. Symptoms may take a couple of days to settle even though therapy is complete."}, {"id": 1542, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 1, "question": "NK is a 54 year old man with an office pressure of 142/88 mmHg confirmed by home readings. His physician wants a trial of lifestyle changes first. NK asks about salt, saying he already avoids the salt shaker at the table. Which dietary sodium advice best matches Hypertension Canada recommendations?", "options": {"A": "Aim for about 2500 mg sodium daily, mainly by using sea salt instead of table salt", "B": "Aim for about 2000 mg sodium daily by reducing processed and restaurant foods", "C": "Aim for about 3500 mg sodium daily, as long as he avoids adding salt during cooking", "D": "Aim for about 1000 mg sodium daily using salt substitutes made with potassium chloride"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Aim for about 2000 mg sodium daily by reducing processed and restaurant foods): Hypertension Canada recommends a sodium intake of about 2000 mg daily, and most dietary sodium comes from processed and restaurant foods rather than the shaker. Sea salt has the same sodium content as table salt. Very low targets using potassium based substitutes are not advised without checking kidney function and medications."}, {"id": 1543, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "AB is a 50 year old man with a nasogastric tube who receives continuous enteral feeding and phenytoin suspension 100 mg three times daily. His total phenytoin level has fallen to 25 umol/L from a stable 55 umol/L. What should the pharmacist recommend?", "options": {"A": "Hold feeds about two hours around each phenytoin dose and recheck the level", "B": "Mix the phenytoin directly into the feeding bag so it is delivered slowly across the day", "C": "Halve each phenytoin dose, since the low level shows the drug is accumulating in his tissues", "D": "Give phenytoin sodium injection through the tube, which is absorbed the same as the suspension"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Hold feeds about two hours around each phenytoin dose and recheck the level): Enteral feeds bind phenytoin in the tube and gut and reduce absorption, so feeds are usually held about two hours before and after each dose, and levels are rechecked. A low level reflects poor absorption, not accumulation, so halving the dose would worsen seizure risk. Injectable phenytoin is not absorbed enterally in the same way."}, {"id": 1544, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "Acetaminophen 120 mg supp, 1 pr q6h prn fever, max 4 doses/24h, is written for a 4 year old boy with a fever from an ear infection. His father asks how to use it. Which counselling statement correctly reflects the directions?", "options": {"A": "Give one suppository by mouth every six hours as needed for fever, up to four doses", "B": "Insert one suppository rectally every six hours on a fixed schedule, up to four doses", "C": "Insert one suppository rectally every six hours as needed for fever, up to four doses", "D": "Insert one suppository rectally every four hours as needed for fever, up to six doses"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Insert one suppository rectally every six hours as needed for fever, up to four doses): Supp means suppository, pr means per rectum, q6h means every six hours and prn means as needed, with a stated limit of four doses in 24 hours. A fixed schedule drops prn, giving the suppository by mouth ignores pr, and every four hours misreads q6h."}, {"id": 1545, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "PA, a pharmacist, learns that a pharmacy technician has been completing final checks on prescriptions at night when no pharmacist is in the store. The technician says the manager approved the arrangement months ago to cut costs, and several patients have already received medications this way. What should PA do?", "options": {"A": "Ask the technician to stop and take no further action, since it will not be repeated", "B": "Report the matter to the pharmacy's insurer only, since the risk concerns coverage rather than regulation", "C": "Accept the manager's approval and keep quiet, since the manager holds responsibility for all staffing decisions", "D": "Stop the practice, make sure patients are safe, and report the situation to the college"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Stop the practice, make sure patients are safe, and report the situation to the college): Final checks are a pharmacist's responsibility, and unsupervised release of prescriptions endangers patients. A manager's approval cannot authorize a practice that breaks the standards, and the regulator needs to know of the unsafe practice."}, {"id": 1546, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 7, "question": "WK is a 59 year old man who stopped pantoprazole 40 mg daily two weeks ago after four years of use. He now has heartburn most nights and thinks the original problem has returned. He asks the pharmacist whether he must restart the medicine. What is the best advice?", "options": {"A": "Rebound acid symptoms can last several weeks, so use antacids as needed and delay restarting", "B": "Restart pantoprazole 40 mg daily now, because these symptoms prove the original disease has returned", "C": "Symptoms after stopping are due to Helicobacter pylori, so he should start an eradication regimen", "D": "Take a double dose of pantoprazole for three days to reset acid production, then stop again"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Rebound acid symptoms can last several weeks, so use antacids as needed and delay restarting): Long term acid suppression raises gastrin and parietal cell mass, so stopping can cause rebound hypersecretion for two to four weeks. Antacids or an on-demand approach can bridge this period before deciding if therapy is truly needed. The symptoms do not prove relapse or infection, and a double dose would simply restart the dependency."}, {"id": 1547, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "ZR is a 43 year old man starting disulfiram 250 mg daily after a supervised withdrawal. He is motivated and says he will stay away from beer and spirits. He also uses a mouthwash and cough syrup regularly. What counselling should the pharmacist emphasize?", "options": {"A": "Avoid mouthwash and cough syrups containing alcohol, since small amounts can trigger a reaction", "B": "Alcohol may be consumed in small amounts after the first week once the drug has stabilized", "C": "The reaction occurs only with beer and spirits, so liquid medicines are not a concern", "D": "Take the tablet only after drinking, since it neutralizes alcohol within an hour of intake"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Avoid mouthwash and cough syrups containing alcohol, since small amounts can trigger a reaction): Disulfiram blocks aldehyde dehydrogenase, so even small amounts of alcohol in mouthwash or cough syrup can trigger flushing, palpitations and vomiting, and the sensitivity can last up to two weeks after the last dose. Taking it after drinking does not protect against anything. Patients should read labels for hidden alcohol."}, {"id": 1548, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 7, "question": "The pharmacist is asked to compound 30 g of hydrocortisone 0.5% cream for a patient with mild dermatitis, using hydrocortisone 2.5% cream and a bland cream base. Which quantities should be combined to prepare it? The patient has no known allergies and will apply it twice daily to the neck.", "options": {"A": "5 g of the 2.5% cream and 25 g of bland base, rounded for easier weighing", "B": "12 g of the 2.5% cream and 18 g of bland base for adequate potency", "C": "6 g of the 2.5% cream mixed with 24 g of the bland cream base", "D": "10 g of the 2.5% cream and 20 g of bland base for quick preparation"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (6 g of the 2.5% cream mixed with 24 g of the bland cream base): The amount of hydrocortisone needed is 30 g x 0.5 percent, which is 0.15 g. At 2.5 percent, 6 g of cream supplies that amount, and the remaining 24 g is base. The other mixes give concentrations of about 0.42, 0.83 and 1.0 percent."}, {"id": 1549, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "MZ is a 69 year old man with type 2 diabetes, an A1c of 7.8% on metformin, and a recent hospital admission for heart failure. His physician plans to add a DPP-4 inhibitor. Which agent should the pharmacist identify as needing the most caution in this patient?", "options": {"A": "Saxagliptin, because trials showed more heart failure hospitalizations with it than placebo", "B": "Linagliptin, because it is cleared by the kidneys and accumulates when heart failure worsens", "C": "Every agent in the class, because heart failure risk is identical across all DPP-4 inhibitors", "D": "Sitagliptin, because it causes fluid retention through the same mechanism as pioglitazone"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Saxagliptin, because trials showed more heart failure hospitalizations with it than placebo): The saxagliptin cardiovascular outcome trial showed an increase in heart failure hospitalization, and the product carries a caution. Sitagliptin and linagliptin did not show this signal in their outcome trials. Linagliptin is eliminated mainly through the bile rather than the kidney, and DPP-4 inhibitors do not cause fluid retention like thiazolidinediones."}, {"id": 1550, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "A pharmacist wearing spill kit protective equipment has absorbed a small 10 mL spill of a hazardous drug solution in the pharmacy preparation area using absorbent pads. The visible liquid is gone. What cleaning sequence should the pharmacist follow next on the contaminated surface?", "options": {"A": "Clean with detergent, rinse with clean water, then disinfect, working from least to most contaminated", "B": "Rinse the surface with plenty of water, then dry it and leave the area uncovered", "C": "Wipe once with 70% isopropyl alcohol and allow it to evaporate, which deactivates the drug residue", "D": "Disinfect with 70% isopropyl alcohol first, then clean with detergent, working from most to least contaminated"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Clean with detergent, rinse with clean water, then disinfect, working from least to most contaminated): Detergent physically removes drug residue, and a clean water rinse takes away the detergent and dissolved drug, followed by disinfection. Cleaning proceeds from the least to the most contaminated area so residue is not spread outward. Alcohol alone does not deactivate or remove most hazardous drugs, and water alone leaves residue behind."}, {"id": 1551, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A generic manufacturer reports a bioequivalence study of an oral tablet. The geometric mean ratio of AUC (test to reference) was 0.97, with a 90% confidence interval of 0.89 to 1.06. The acceptance range is 0.80 to 1.25. A pharmacist is asked whether the product meets the standard for AUC. What is the best conclusion?", "options": {"A": "Bioequivalence cannot be judged until a 95% confidence interval is calculated using the same data", "B": "Bioequivalence is supported only if the point estimate lies outside the interval, showing a real difference", "C": "Bioequivalence for AUC is supported because the whole interval lies within 0.80 to 1.25", "D": "Bioequivalence is not supported because the point estimate of 0.97 differs from 1.00 in the reference"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Bioequivalence for AUC is supported because the whole interval lies within 0.80 to 1.25): Bioequivalence is concluded when the 90% confidence interval of the geometric mean ratio falls entirely within the acceptance range. The interval of 0.89 to 1.06 sits well inside 0.80 to 1.25. A point estimate slightly below 1.00 is expected and does not on its own indicate a difference."}, {"id": 1552, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "WN is a 74 year old man with Parkinson disease taking carbidopa-levodopa and ropinirole, who also takes tamsulosin. He reports dizziness on standing. His blood pressure is 128/76 mmHg sitting and 98/60 mmHg standing. What is the most appropriate pharmacist recommendation?", "options": {"A": "Review contributing drugs such as tamsulosin and ropinirole, and advise slow rising with adequate fluids", "B": "Take double doses of carbidopa-levodopa on waking, since low blood pressure signals underdosing of levodopa", "C": "Stop all fluids after noon, since fluid intake worsens orthostatic symptoms in Parkinson disease", "D": "Add an over-the-counter decongestant each morning to raise blood pressure, as the first measure"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Review contributing drugs such as tamsulosin and ropinirole, and advise slow rising with adequate fluids): A fall of 30 mmHg systolic on standing is orthostatic hypotension, which can be caused by Parkinson disease itself and worsened by alpha-blockers and dopamine agonists. Reviewing and adjusting contributing drugs and using non-drug measures should lead. Doubling levodopa would add to the hypotension, and fluid restriction or decongestants are inappropriate."}, {"id": 1553, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "The pharmacist will compound DK's suspension from the tablets on hand, using the prescribed concentration of 25 mg per 5 mL. How many 25 mg spironolactone tablets must be crushed to prepare the entire 150 mL, assuming no overage is added and every tablet contributes its full labelled strength?", "options": {"A": "15 tablets, since the suspension needs only 2.5 mg of drug in each millilitre", "B": "6 tablets, since 150 mL of liquid corresponds to 150 mg of drug in total", "C": "150 tablets, since each millilitre of the suspension needs 25 mg of spironolactone", "D": "30 tablets, since 150 mL at 5 mg/mL needs 750 mg of spironolactone in total"}, "cluster_id": "CHS12", "cluster_text": "DK is a 5 year old girl who weighs 18 kg and has a prescription for spironolactone oral suspension 25 mg per 5 mL, 150 mL, to be taken once daily. No commercial liquid is available. The pharmacy stocks spironolactone 25 mg tablets, glycerin, and a commercial suspending vehicle. A published stability study supports a 28 day beyond use date for this preparation when refrigerated and protected from light. The pharmacist will compound the full 150 mL now.", "cluster_size": 3, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (30 tablets, since 150 mL at 5 mg/mL needs 750 mg of spironolactone in total): The strength is 25 mg per 5 mL, which is 5 mg/mL, so 150 mL needs 150 x 5, or 750 mg of drug. Dividing 750 mg by 25 mg gives 30 tablets. Using the dose per 5 mL as the dose per millilitre would overstate the tablets fivefold."}, {"id": 1554, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "DK is a 5 year old girl who needs spironolactone 25 mg per 5 mL suspension compounded from 25 mg tablets, 150 mL in total. After crushing the tablets to a fine powder in a mortar, what should the pharmacist do next to prepare a smooth, uniform suspension?", "options": {"A": "Wet the powder with glycerin to form a paste, then add the vehicle in portions", "B": "Dissolve the powder in purified water, filter the liquid, and then add the vehicle", "C": "Add the full volume of vehicle at once and shake the bottle for one minute", "D": "Heat the vehicle to 60 degrees Celsius and stir the powder into it quickly"}, "cluster_id": "CHS12", "cluster_text": "DK is a 5 year old girl who weighs 18 kg and has a prescription for spironolactone oral suspension 25 mg per 5 mL, 150 mL, to be taken once daily. No commercial liquid is available. The pharmacy stocks spironolactone 25 mg tablets, glycerin, and a commercial suspending vehicle. A published stability study supports a 28 day beyond use date for this preparation when refrigerated and protected from light. The pharmacist will compound the full 150 mL now.", "cluster_size": 3, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Wet the powder with glycerin to form a paste, then add the vehicle in portions): Crushed tablet powder is poorly wettable and clumps if liquid is added all at once. Glycerin wets the particles into a smooth paste, and adding the vehicle in portions lets the paste thin evenly. Filtering would remove undissolved drug, and heating the vehicle is unnecessary and risks degrading the drug."}, {"id": 1555, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "The pharmacist labels the finished spironolactone suspension for DK's family before it is dispensed. The preparation was compounded today from tablets and a commercial vehicle, and the stability study supports a limited period. Which storage and dating instructions should appear on the label for this particular compounded preparation?", "options": {"A": "Store in the refrigerator, protect from light, shake well, and discard after 28 days", "B": "Store in the refrigerator, no shaking is needed, and discard after the tablet expiry date", "C": "Store in the freezer between doses and discard one year after the preparation date", "D": "Store at room temperature, shake well before use, and discard after 90 days as for tablets"}, "cluster_id": "CHS12", "cluster_text": "DK is a 5 year old girl who weighs 18 kg and has a prescription for spironolactone oral suspension 25 mg per 5 mL, 150 mL, to be taken once daily. No commercial liquid is available. The pharmacy stocks spironolactone 25 mg tablets, glycerin, and a commercial suspending vehicle. A published stability study supports a 28 day beyond use date for this preparation when refrigerated and protected from light. The pharmacist will compound the full 150 mL now.", "cluster_size": 3, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Store in the refrigerator, protect from light, shake well, and discard after 28 days): Compounded liquids are supported only for the period shown by their stability data, here 28 days refrigerated and protected from light. Suspensions settle, so the bottle must be shaken to deliver an even dose. Using the tablet expiry date or a long room temperature period would exceed the supported stability."}, {"id": 1556, "domain": "leadership_and_stewardship", "chapter": 11, "question": "A customer who buys a naloxone kit mentions he is afraid to call 911 during an overdose because he carries a small amount of cannabis and unprescribed pills. The pharmacist explains the federal Good Samaritan Drug Overdose Act. What should the pharmacist say?", "options": {"A": "Protection applies only to the person overdosing, not to bystanders who call 911 for help", "B": "Protection applies only if the caller stays to give police a written statement", "C": "People who seek help for an overdose are protected from simple possession charges", "D": "Protection covers all drug offences including trafficking whenever emergency services are contacted"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (People who seek help for an overdose are protected from simple possession charges): The federal Good Samaritan Drug Overdose Act protects the person who calls for help, the person overdosing, and others at the scene from charges of simple possession and breaches of related conditions. It does not cover trafficking or other serious offences. There is no requirement to give a statement, so fear of police should not delay the call."}, {"id": 1557, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "OR is a 21 year old man with type 1 diabetes who plans to run before work. His fasting glucose is 15.2 mmol/L and his blood ketone meter reads 1.8 mmol/L. He took his usual insulin last night. What should the pharmacist advise about exercising now?", "options": {"A": "Run only a short distance and recheck ketones afterwards, since light exercise is safe with ketones", "B": "Run at an easy pace, since exercise will lower the glucose and clear the ketones quickly", "C": "Eat extra carbohydrate and run, since the ketones show his body needs more fuel for activity", "D": "Postpone exercise and follow his ketone plan with extra rapid-acting insulin and fluids"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Postpone exercise and follow his ketone plan with extra rapid-acting insulin and fluids): A ketone level above 1.5 mmol/L with a high glucose signals insulin deficiency, and exercise can push glucose and ketones higher. He should delay activity and treat with insulin and fluids until ketones fall. Eating more or running lightly does not correct the underlying insulin shortage."}, {"id": 1558, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "A pharmacy receives a vaccine shipment, and the temperature indicator inside the box shows that the contents were above 8 degrees Celsius during transit. The courier is waiting for a signature, and clinic is scheduled for the next morning. What should the pharmacist do?", "options": {"A": "Use the vaccine at the clinic the next morning, since the vial itself appears unchanged", "B": "Return the whole shipment to the courier and cancel all future orders from that supplier", "C": "Do not use it, keep it at 2 to 8 degrees, and notify the supplier", "D": "Sign for the shipment and put the vaccine into the fridge with the other stock"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Do not use it, keep it at 2 to 8 degrees, and notify the supplier): Because the indicator tripped, the product left its allowed range, so the vaccine cannot be assumed to be potent. It should be quarantined at 2 to 8 degrees and kept apart from usable stock while the supplier or manufacturer advises. Mixing it with good stock risks accidental use, and the appearance of the vial shows nothing about potency."}, {"id": 1559, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "RH is a 74 year old man in a hospice receiving oral morphine 120 mg daily and is moved to subcutaneous dosing because of vomiting. Using 2 mg oral morphine equals 1 mg subcutaneous, and a breakthrough dose of 10% of the daily dose, which subcutaneous breakthrough dose is correct?", "options": {"A": "1.2 mg subcutaneously, which is 1% of 120 mg per day", "B": "12 mg subcutaneously, which is 10% of 120 mg per day", "C": "6 mg subcutaneously, which is 10% of 60 mg per day", "D": "3 mg subcutaneously, which is 10% of 30 mg per day"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (6 mg subcutaneously, which is 10% of 60 mg per day): Converting 120 mg oral to subcutaneous at 2 to 1 gives 60 mg per 24 hours. Ten percent of that is 6 mg. Using 12 mg would apply the oral figure to a route that is twice as potent, doubling the breakthrough dose."}, {"id": 1560, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "The owner of a small town pharmacy wants to make 500 mL of 70% ethanol for surface disinfection from a 95% ethanol stock held in the dispensary. A new graduate is asked to confirm the volume of stock needed. Which preparation is correct?", "options": {"A": "Measure about 368 mL of the 95% stock and add purified water to 500 mL", "B": "Measure about 263 mL of the 95% stock and add purified water to 500 mL", "C": "Measure about 475 mL of the 95% stock and add 25 mL of purified water", "D": "Measure about 350 mL of the 95% stock and add 150 mL of purified water"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Measure about 368 mL of the 95% stock and add purified water to 500 mL): C1V1 = C2V2 gives V1 = 70 x 500 / 95 = 368 mL of stock, brought to 500 mL with purified water. Simply taking 350 mL ignores the fact that the stock is not 100% ethanol, so the final product would be only about 66%."}, {"id": 1561, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 3, "question": "CJ is a 70 year old man with type 2 diabetes and a foot ulcer. Today he has a fever of 38.9 C, heart rate of 112, foul-smelling drainage and redness spreading up to his ankle. He asks the pharmacist for an antibiotic that might be at home. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Recommend soaking the foot in warm saltwater and a topical antibiotic ointment daily", "B": "Refer him to the emergency department now, as systemic signs suggest severe foot infection", "C": "Suggest dressing changes and acetaminophen, with a clinic visit the following week or so", "D": "Start oral cephalexin for 7 days and review the foot at the clinic in one week"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Refer him to the emergency department now, as systemic signs suggest severe foot infection): Fever, tachycardia and spreading infection show a severe diabetic foot infection, which may involve deep tissue or bone and needs urgent assessment, imaging and intravenous therapy. Oral therapy or topical care at home would delay treatment and risk limb loss."}, {"id": 1562, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 3, "question": "NM is a 66 year old woman starting bismuth quadruple therapy with tetracycline 500 mg four times daily. She takes calcium carbonate 500 mg with each meal for osteopenia and has dairy products at breakfast and supper. Which advice best protects the absorption of tetracycline?", "options": {"A": "Switch tetracycline to doxycycline 100 mg twice daily since its absorption is unaffected by calcium products", "B": "Take tetracycline one hour after each calcium tablet, which gives adequate separation from the supplement", "C": "Pause the calcium supplement for 14 days and separate tetracycline from dairy products", "D": "Take tetracycline together with the calcium and dairy, since food buffers stomach upset from the antibiotic"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Pause the calcium supplement for 14 days and separate tetracycline from dairy products): Calcium, dairy and other polyvalent cations chelate tetracycline in the gut and sharply reduce absorption, which could lead to treatment failure. Pausing the supplement for the two weeks and separating the antibiotic from dairy preserves the dose. One hour of separation is too short, and doxycycline is also chelated by calcium."}, {"id": 1563, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 1, "question": "KW is a 52 year old woman taking warfarin 6 mg daily for a mechanical mitral valve, with a stable INR of 3.1 and a target of 2.5 to 3.5. She tells the pharmacist she plans to start St John's wort from a health food store for low mood. What should the pharmacist advise?", "options": {"A": "Use it, because natural health products are tested and do not alter prescription drug effects", "B": "Avoid it, since it induces warfarin metabolism and can lower the INR below target", "C": "Use it only with food, since taking it with meals blocks its interaction with warfarin", "D": "Use it, but expect the INR to rise and reduce the warfarin dose by a quarter"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Avoid it, since it induces warfarin metabolism and can lower the INR below target): St John's wort induces CYP enzymes and P-glycoprotein, which lowers warfarin exposure and can drop the INR into a range that leaves a mechanical valve unprotected. Taking it with food does not block the interaction, and pre-emptively lowering the dose would worsen the effect. She should discuss mood treatment with her doctor."}, {"id": 1564, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 1, "question": "CP is a 62 year old woman who takes metoprolol tartrate 50 mg twice daily for stable angina. Her chest pain has been absent for two months, so she plans to stop her tablets today because she feels cured. She asks the pharmacist if that is acceptable. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "She can halve the dose for one day then stop, as that is a sufficient taper", "B": "Stopping is acceptable, since her angina has resolved and metoprolol is only taken for symptom relief", "C": "She should switch to taking it only on days when she expects physical exertion or stress", "D": "Stopping suddenly can cause rebound angina, so reduction should be tapered by her prescriber"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Stopping suddenly can cause rebound angina, so reduction should be tapered by her prescriber): Abrupt beta-blocker withdrawal can cause rebound tachycardia, angina and even myocardial infarction, so doses are tapered over one to two weeks. Her angina control reflects the drug working rather than a cure. Using the drug only on demand does not work because it needs steady levels to prevent angina."}, {"id": 1565, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 4, "question": "UL is a 38 year old woman weighing 72 kg who receives infliximab 5 mg/kg at week 6 for ulcerative colitis. The hospital pharmacy supplies 100 mg vials. The pharmacist prepares the infusion order and rounds up to whole vials according to policy. How many vials are needed?", "options": {"A": "Three vials, because the 360 mg dose is rounded down to the closest 300 mg amount", "B": "Five vials, because the 360 mg dose is followed by one spare vial for safety", "C": "Eight vials, because 5 mg/kg was applied to her weight in pounds, about 158 lb", "D": "Four vials, because the 360 mg dose rounds up to 400 mg in whole vials"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Four vials, because the 360 mg dose rounds up to 400 mg in whole vials): 5 mg/kg x 72 kg = 360 mg, which needs 3.6 vials, so the policy of rounding up gives four vials. Rounding down would under-dose, adding a spare vial is not part of the calculation, and applying a per kilogram dose to pounds overstates the dose more than twofold."}, {"id": 1566, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "KA is a 55 year old woman with depression-related insomnia. Her prescriber orders trazodone 25 mg at bedtime for 30 nights, and the pharmacy stocks only trazodone 50 mg scored tablets. How many tablets should the pharmacist dispense, and how should they be labelled?", "options": {"A": "30 tablets, labelled to take half a tablet nightly, with the unused halves returned to stock", "B": "8 tablets, labelled to take half a tablet at bedtime every night until they are gone", "C": "15 tablets, labelled to take half a tablet at bedtime each night", "D": "30 tablets, labelled to take one tablet at bedtime on alternate nights for 60 nights"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (15 tablets, labelled to take half a tablet at bedtime each night): A 25 mg dose is half of a 50 mg scored tablet, so 30 nights needs 30 halves, which is 15 whole tablets. The label should state the half-tablet dose clearly. Dispensing 30 tablets would double the supply, and 8 tablets would cover only 16 nights."}, {"id": 1567, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 7, "question": "GN is a 55 year old man who asks for something for burning urination and frequency that began two days ago. He has no fever or flank pain. He takes no regular medications and says he has never had urinary problems before. Which response is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Recommend cranberry capsules and extra fluids for three days, then refer if symptoms persist", "B": "Suggest an alkalinizing sodium citrate sachet and review by phone in two days to confirm improvement", "C": "Recommend phenazopyridine for three days to relieve burning, since it also treats the infection itself", "D": "Refer him for assessment and urine testing, since urinary infection in men is complicated"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Refer him for assessment and urine testing, since urinary infection in men is complicated): Urinary symptoms in men are usually considered complicated and may involve the prostate, so they need a medical assessment with urine testing. Cranberry products do not treat an established infection. Phenazopyridine only relieves discomfort and does not treat infection, and delaying care for a phone review is not appropriate."}, {"id": 1568, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "A 69 year old woman with cancer pain says she lost two of her ten fentanyl 25 mcg/h patches while travelling, and asks for a replacement supply today. Her last fill was six days ago. What is the most appropriate action?", "options": {"A": "Dispense two patches after she signs a statement describing the loss and how it occurred", "B": "Contact the prescriber, who must issue a new prescription for any replacement, and record the loss", "C": "Refer her to an emergency department, since pharmacies cannot supply narcotics once a prescription is filled", "D": "Dispense two replacement patches from the original prescription, since she has not exceeded the prescribed total"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Contact the prescriber, who must issue a new prescription for any replacement, and record the loss): Narcotic prescriptions cannot be refilled, and a replacement for lost medication is a new supply that needs a new prescription from the prescriber. The pharmacist should record the reported loss and let the prescriber judge the circumstances and the pain plan. A signed statement does not authorize dispensing, and sending her to an emergency department is not necessary."}, {"id": 1569, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 6, "question": "TK is a 21 year old with severe nodular acne whose dermatologist prescribes isotretinoin 40 mg daily. Her pregnancy test today is negative and she is sexually active with a male partner. She asks what contraception rules apply to her treatment. Which requirement should the pharmacist explain?", "options": {"A": "Contraception for one week after the last dose, because the half-life is very short", "B": "Effective contraception for one month before, during, and for one month after treatment", "C": "Contraception for three years after the final dose, as required for this retinoid medication", "D": "Contraception only during the weeks of active treatment, since the drug clears within a day"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Effective contraception for one month before, during, and for one month after treatment): Isotretinoin is highly teratogenic, so effective contraception is required starting one month before treatment, throughout it, and for one month after the last dose. The three year rule applies to acitretin, which is stored in fat. A one week period is not enough to clear the drug and its metabolites."}, {"id": 1570, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "A 30 year old violinist gets a pounding heartbeat and hand tremor only before concerts, never during daily practice. She has no asthma and no heart disease. She asks about taking propranolol before performances at her next recital this month. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Take a daily SSRI only on concert days, since it starts working within an hour", "B": "Take lorazepam 2 mg four hours before each concert, since sedation will improve her playing", "C": "Take buspirone 10 mg thirty minutes before performing, since it gives immediate relief of physical symptoms", "D": "A single propranolol dose 30 to 60 minutes before performing can reduce tremor and palpitations"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A single propranolol dose 30 to 60 minutes before performing can reduce tremor and palpitations): Beta-blockers blunt the physical symptoms of performance anxiety, and a dose taken shortly before an event is a recognized approach in patients without asthma or conduction disease. SSRIs and buspirone need weeks of regular dosing, and benzodiazepines may impair fine motor performance."}, {"id": 1571, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "A 76 year old woman started escitalopram three weeks ago. She now has nausea, lethargy and new confusion, and a blood test shows sodium of 123 mmol/L. She also takes hydrochlorothiazide. Her daughter asks the pharmacist what is going on. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Advise drinking three litres of water daily to correct sodium, then recheck in a month", "B": "Suspect low sodium from the SSRI plus the thiazide, and contact the prescriber urgently", "C": "Increase escitalopram to 20 mg, since confusion in depression means the current dose is too low", "D": "Add a sedative at bedtime, since lethargy and confusion show the depression is uncontrolled"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Suspect low sodium from the SSRI plus the thiazide, and contact the prescriber urgently): SSRIs can cause SIADH, especially in older women taking thiazide diuretics, leading to hyponatremia with confusion and nausea. The prescriber needs to review and likely change therapy. Extra water would worsen dilutional hyponatremia, and a sedative or higher dose would add risk."}, {"id": 1572, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 4, "question": "HM is a 61 year old woman using a fluticasone-salmeterol dry powder inhaler twice daily for COPD. The pharmacist watches her slide the lever, then exhale directly into the mouthpiece before inhaling. She says it clears the powder from the device. The pharmacist should teach her to exhale:", "options": {"A": "into the device only once, then close the cover to protect the powder dose", "B": "away from the device, then inhale slowly and gently as with a metered dose inhaler", "C": "into the mouthpiece to warm the powder, then inhale slowly and deeply over ten seconds", "D": "away from the device before placing her lips around the mouthpiece and inhaling quickly"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (away from the device before placing her lips around the mouthpiece and inhaling quickly): Breathing out into a dry powder inhaler introduces moisture and can blow the loaded dose out of the device. She should exhale away from it, then take a quick, forceful breath in to disperse the powder. A slow inhalation is the technique for pressurized inhalers and gives poor delivery from powder devices."}, {"id": 1573, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "HD is a 63 year old man with a 55 mL prostate who started dutasteride 0.5 mg daily three weeks ago and asks why his urine flow has not improved. He also has mild lower urinary tract symptoms. What should the pharmacist tell him about the expected response?", "options": {"A": "Early worsening is expected for two months, so he should stop the drug if urgency increases", "B": "Benefit should appear within one week, so no response by now means switching to tamsulosin", "C": "Symptom improvement usually takes several months, because the drug shrinks the prostate gradually over time", "D": "Flow improves only when the dose is doubled because 0.5 mg does not reach prostate tissue"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Symptom improvement usually takes several months, because the drug shrinks the prostate gradually over time): Five alpha reductase inhibitors reduce prostate volume slowly, with symptom benefit taking about 3 to 6 months. Three weeks is too early to judge. An alpha blocker acts within days if faster relief is wanted."}, {"id": 1574, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 7, "question": "A 9 year old girl weighing 24 kg will take atovaquone-proguanil pediatric tablets, two daily at her weight. Her family travels for 14 days in the risk area, with dosing starting one day before arrival and continuing seven days after leaving. How many tablets are required?", "options": {"A": "30 pediatric tablets, which covers two tablets daily for fifteen days only", "B": "22 pediatric tablets, which covers one tablet daily for twenty two days", "C": "66 pediatric tablets, which covers three tablets daily for twenty two days", "D": "44 pediatric tablets, which covers two tablets daily for twenty two days"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (44 pediatric tablets, which covers two tablets daily for twenty two days): Prophylaxis runs 1 day before, 14 days in the area and 7 days after, which totals 22 days. At two tablets daily she needs 44 tablets. The other numbers come from a wrong daily dose or by leaving out the post-travel days."}, {"id": 1575, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 4, "question": "A 45 year old man describes a round, dusky purple patch on his thigh that appears at the same spot hours after taking trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole, fading with leftover pigment. It has recurred with each use over the past year. What should the pharmacist conclude?", "options": {"A": "This fits a fixed drug eruption, so avoid the drug in future and document the reaction", "B": "This fits a viral rash, so he should keep taking the antibiotic and use a moisturizer", "C": "This fits contact dermatitis from laundry detergent, so he should change detergents and continue the antibiotic", "D": "This fits a bruise from tight clothing, so the drug can be reused without any change"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (This fits a fixed drug eruption, so avoid the drug in future and document the reaction): A well-defined round patch recurring at the same site after each exposure, often healing with pigmentation, is the hallmark of a fixed drug eruption. Sulfonamides are common causes. Repeated exposure keeps producing the lesion, so the agent should be avoided and recorded in his profile."}, {"id": 1576, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A pharmacist treats a patient who developed marked drowsiness and confusion after combining a licensed natural health product with a prescription sedative. The patient asks whether reporting to Health Canada applies only to prescription drugs. The pharmacist explains that Canada Vigilance accepts reports of suspected reactions to:", "options": {"A": "Prescription and non-prescription drugs only, because natural products are reported through provincial poison control centres", "B": "Prescription and non-prescription drugs, natural health products, biologics and radiopharmaceuticals", "C": "Prescription drugs only, because natural products are not regulated as health products in Canada", "D": "Prescription drugs and biologics, because natural health products are reported to a separate agency"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Prescription and non-prescription drugs, natural health products, biologics and radiopharmaceuticals): Canada Vigilance covers marketed health products broadly, including natural health products, which are licensed and regulated by Health Canada. Poison control centres manage exposures but are not the reporting route for reactions. Excluding natural products would miss interactions like this one."}, {"id": 1577, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 7, "question": "RB is a 58 year old nearsighted man who asks for lubricating drops because he has seen flashes of light and a growing dark curtain over part of the vision in his right eye since this morning. The eye is not red or painful. What should the pharmacist do?", "options": {"A": "Arrange an emergency eye assessment today for suspected retinal detachment and advise against driving", "B": "Recommend lubricating drops and rest, and advise an eye appointment within two weeks", "C": "Recommend an omega-3 supplement and screen breaks, since the symptoms suggest digital eye strain", "D": "Suggest antihistamine eye drops and recheck him in the pharmacy tomorrow if the shadow persists"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Arrange an emergency eye assessment today for suspected retinal detachment and advise against driving): Flashes and a spreading curtain of vision loss are classic symptoms of retinal detachment, which is a sight threatening emergency in a nearsighted adult. Prompt repair gives the best outcome. Lubricants or supplements would miss this diagnosis."}, {"id": 1578, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 1, "question": "VD is a 71 year old man taking apixaban 5 mg twice daily for atrial fibrillation. His blood pressure averages 162/94 mmHg, he takes naproxen 500 mg twice daily for arthritis and drinks about five alcoholic drinks daily. He asks what could make his anticoagulant safer. Which advice is the most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Switch to aspirin 81 mg daily, which carries a lower bleeding risk than apixaban", "B": "Lower apixaban to 2.5 mg twice daily, since bleeding risk factors justify the dose reduction", "C": "Stop the anticoagulant until the blood pressure is controlled and his alcohol intake has fallen", "D": "Treat the hypertension, replace naproxen with acetaminophen, and cut back alcohol use"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Treat the hypertension, replace naproxen with acetaminophen, and cut back alcohol use): Uncontrolled hypertension, NSAID use and heavy alcohol intake are modifiable bleeding risk factors identified by HAS-BLED. Correcting them lowers bleeding risk while keeping stroke protection. Dose reduction without meeting the dose criteria and a switch to aspirin leave him less protected without making treatment safer, and stopping exposes him to stroke."}, {"id": 1579, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 1, "question": "LC is a 66 year old woman admitted to hospital and started on sotalol 80 mg twice daily. Her admission ECG shows a QTc of 480 ms, her potassium is 3.2 mmol/L and her magnesium is low. What is the most appropriate pharmacist recommendation?", "options": {"A": "give the first dose now and correct electrolytes later, since sotalol corrects the QT interval", "B": "increase sotalol to 160 mg twice daily, since higher doses shorten repolarization", "C": "hold sotalol and correct potassium and magnesium first, because QT prolongation raises torsades risk", "D": "add azithromycin for infection cover, since macrolides lower the risk of arrhythmia with sotalol"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (hold sotalol and correct potassium and magnesium first, because QT prolongation raises torsades risk): Sotalol prolongs repolarization, and a baseline QTc near 480 ms with low potassium and magnesium sets up torsades de pointes. Electrolytes should be corrected and the QTc reassessed before sotalol is given. Macrolides further prolong the QT interval."}, {"id": 1580, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "MN is a 35 year old man who took a large amount of diphenhydramine sleep aid tablets. He is agitated with dilated pupils, hot dry flushed skin, urinary retention and absent bowel sounds. His reflexes are normal. Which finding best separates his condition from serotonin syndrome?", "options": {"A": "Dry skin and absent bowel sounds, whereas serotonin syndrome causes sweating and hyperactive bowel sounds", "B": "Tachycardia, since serotonin syndrome typically lowers heart rate and blood pressure rather than raising them", "C": "Agitation, since serotonin syndrome produces somnolence and low mood rather than excitation", "D": "Dilated pupils, since mydriasis does not occur in serotonin syndrome in adults"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Dry skin and absent bowel sounds, whereas serotonin syndrome causes sweating and hyperactive bowel sounds): Anticholinergic toxicity produces hot, dry skin, urinary retention and decreased bowel sounds, whereas serotonin syndrome causes diaphoresis and hyperactive bowel sounds with clonus and hyperreflexia. Both can cause agitation, mydriasis and tachycardia, so those do not separate them. Normal reflexes in this patient also point away from serotonin toxicity."}, {"id": 1581, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "The master formula for 120 mL of an oral solution lists drug 1.5 g, sorbitol 70% solution 18 mL, flavour 0.6 mL, and purified water to 120 mL. A prescription requires 480 mL. A technician must write the worksheet for the pharmacist to check. Which worksheet is correct?", "options": {"A": "Drug 6 g, sorbitol solution 72 mL, flavour 0.6 mL, and purified water to 480 mL", "B": "Drug 4.5 g, sorbitol solution 54 mL, flavour 1.8 mL, and purified water to 480 mL", "C": "Drug 6 g, sorbitol solution 72 mL, flavour 2.4 mL, purified water to 480 mL", "D": "Drug 6 g, sorbitol solution 72 mL, flavour 2.4 mL, purified water up to 360 mL"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Drug 6 g, sorbitol solution 72 mL, flavour 2.4 mL, purified water to 480 mL): The enlarging factor is 480 divided by 120, which is 4, and every ingredient is multiplied by 4. That gives 6 g of drug, 72 mL of sorbitol solution and 2.4 mL of flavour. Water is added to the final volume of 480 mL, and leaving the flavour unchanged would weaken the taste of the product."}, {"id": 1582, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "AF is a 27 year old woman who took rizatriptan 10 mg ODT for her first migraine this morning. She calls the pharmacy worried because about twenty minutes later she felt tightness in her neck and chest that settled within five minutes. She feels well now. What should the pharmacist advise?", "options": {"A": "Repeat the dose with an antacid, since the sensation is caused by stomach acid reflux", "B": "Take a second dose now, since the tightness shows the first dose was too small", "C": "Chest or neck tightness is common and brief, but severe or lasting pain needs urgent care", "D": "Stop rizatriptan for good and see a cardiologist, since any chest symptom proves heart damage occurred"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Chest or neck tightness is common and brief, but severe or lasting pain needs urgent care): Brief sensations of tightness in the chest, neck or throat are common with triptans and usually reflect vasoconstriction without heart injury. She should seek urgent care if pain is severe, spreads, lasts, or comes with shortness of breath or sweating. Redosing is not appropriate, and the symptom is not reflux."}, {"id": 1583, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "ID is an 84 year old woman in long-term care with a gravity infusion of 500 mL of lactated Ringer's ordered over 4 hours. The facility has no pumps and uses tubing delivering 10 drops per mL. The nurse asks the pharmacist to calculate the drip rate in drops per minute. Which rate is correct?", "options": {"A": "About 10 drops per minute, rounded to a whole drop count", "B": "About 21 drops per minute, rounded to a whole drop count", "C": "About 42 drops per minute, rounded to a whole drop count", "D": "About 125 drops per minute, rounded to a whole drop count"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (About 21 drops per minute, rounded to a whole drop count): 500 mL x 10 drops per mL = 5000 drops over 240 minutes, or 20.8 drops per minute, rounded to 21. A rate of 42 would apply to 20 drop tubing, and 125 is the mL per hour figure, which would infuse the bag in under two hours with 10 drop tubing."}, {"id": 1584, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "NJ is a 52 year old woman with type 2 diabetes who has injected insulin for years using 12.7 mm pen needles pinching a skin fold. She has a body mass index of 31 kg/m2 and wonders whether a different needle is better. What should the pharmacist recommend, based on Diabetes Canada advice?", "options": {"A": "An 8 mm needle at 45 degrees, with a skin fold lifted for each injection", "B": "A 6 mm needle at 45 degrees into the arm, because longer needles reduce injection pain", "C": "Keep the 12.7 mm needle but inject into muscle in the thigh to speed absorption", "D": "A 4 mm needle at 90 degrees, with no skin fold needed for most adults"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A 4 mm needle at 90 degrees, with no skin fold needed for most adults): Diabetes Canada supports 4 mm pen needles inserted at 90 degrees for adults of all body sizes, usually without pinching a skin fold. Shorter needles reduce the chance of accidental intramuscular injection, which speeds absorption unpredictably. Longer needles raise that risk and are not less painful."}, {"id": 1585, "domain": "communication_and_collaboration", "chapter": 11, "question": "A community pharmacy wants to redesign its prescription labels so that they are easier to read for older adults, many of whom have reduced vision. Which change is consistent with accepted patient-centred label guidance for community pharmacy practice in Canada?", "options": {"A": "Use a larger sans serif font and put the drug name and directions first", "B": "Print the directions in capital letters and abbreviate the units to save label space", "C": "Use a decorative font and place the pharmacy logo and store promotions at the top", "D": "Place the prescriber name, address and refill history ahead of the directions for use"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Use a larger sans serif font and put the drug name and directions first): Patient-centred labels put the most important information, the drug name and directions, first in a large plain font. Decorative fonts, extra administrative details above the directions, and capital letters with abbreviations all make the label harder to read."}, {"id": 1586, "domain": "leadership_and_stewardship", "chapter": 11, "question": "During a heat warning, a community pharmacist reviews KP, a 79 year old who takes furosemide 40 mg, ramipril 10 mg, and lithium 600 mg twice daily. She lives alone, reports poor fluid intake, and says she has been dizzy and shaky today. What is the main concern and best action?", "options": {"A": "Risk of dehydration and lithium toxicity, so contact her prescriber the same day for assessment", "B": "Risk of low blood sugar, so advise extra carbohydrate and a recheck in 15 minutes", "C": "A minor inconvenience, so recommend lighter clothing and drinking only when she feels thirsty", "D": "Risk of high blood pressure from heat, so suggest an extra ramipril dose if dizzy"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Risk of dehydration and lithium toxicity, so contact her prescriber the same day for assessment): Heat, poor intake, a loop diuretic, and an ACE inhibitor lower fluid volume and kidney function, which reduces lithium clearance and can raise levels into the toxic range, causing tremor, dizziness, and confusion. She needs same day assessment, with lithium level and kidney testing, and possible temporary dose changes by her prescriber. Extra ramipril would worsen kidney function and volume depletion."}, {"id": 1587, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "A 64 year old woman started pregabalin 75 mg twice daily for generalized anxiety disorder two days ago. She drives to work each morning by herself and has a glass of wine most evenings. What counselling should the pharmacist give her?", "options": {"A": "Pregabalin does not affect alertness, so driving and alcohol can continue as before without any change", "B": "Pregabalin should be taken with grapefruit juice each morning, since it improves absorption and speeds relief", "C": "Dizziness and drowsiness are common early, so avoid driving until response is known and limit alcohol", "D": "Pregabalin works best taken only on anxious days, so she should skip doses on calm days"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Dizziness and drowsiness are common early, so avoid driving until response is known and limit alcohol): Pregabalin commonly causes dizziness, somnolence and impaired coordination, particularly early in treatment, and alcohol adds to central nervous system depression. Regular dosing is needed for anxiolytic effect, and grapefruit juice is not relevant to pregabalin absorption."}, {"id": 1588, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 3, "question": "VK is a 40 year old man with HIV, well controlled on a single tablet of bictegravir, emtricitabine and tenofovir alafenamide. He tells the pharmacist his mood has been low for weeks and asks to buy St. John's wort tablets from the natural health shelf. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Allow it if taken four hours apart from the antiretroviral tablet, which prevents the interaction", "B": "Allow it, since bictegravir is cleared by the kidneys and is not affected by herbal products", "C": "Advise against it, since it lowers antiretroviral levels, and refer him for mood assessment", "D": "Allow it at half the labelled dose since the enzyme effect depends on the amount taken"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Advise against it, since it lowers antiretroviral levels, and refer him for mood assessment): St. John's wort induces CYP3A4, UGT1A1 and P-glycoprotein, which lowers levels of bictegravir and tenofovir alafenamide and can cause virologic failure with resistance. Halving the dose does not avoid the problem, and spacing the doses does not help since induction persists. A mood assessment addresses the underlying concern safely."}, {"id": 1589, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "NB is a 38 year old woman who pours two large glasses of wine most nights to fall asleep. She wakes at 3 am and cannot return to sleep, and her partner says she snores. She asks whether the wine is helping. What is the most accurate response from the pharmacist?", "options": {"A": "Wine improves deep sleep and prevents night waking, so the 3 am waking has another cause", "B": "Alcohol speeds sleep onset but fragments later sleep and can worsen snoring", "C": "Her snoring and waking come from low exercise, and a sedating antihistamine would solve both", "D": "Wine is a reasonable long-term sleep aid, provided she limits it to two glasses on weeknights"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Alcohol speeds sleep onset but fragments later sleep and can worsen snoring): Alcohol is sedating at first but disrupts REM and causes fragmented, lighter sleep in the second half of the night, and it relaxes upper airway muscles, which worsens snoring and sleep apnea. It also builds tolerance. Using it as a sleep aid is discouraged, and sedating antihistamines add their own next-day effects."}, {"id": 1590, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 6, "question": "YT is a 57 year old woman with troublesome hot flashes who had a deep vein thrombosis three years ago. Her prescriber has ruled out systemic hormone therapy and asks the pharmacist about a non-hormonal drug with evidence for vasomotor symptoms. Which option is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Raloxifene, because it reduces hot flashes and also lowers fracture risk after menopause", "B": "Soy isoflavone supplements, since trials show they work as well as estrogen for vasomotor symptoms", "C": "Venlafaxine extended release, an SNRI shown to reduce the frequency of hot flashes", "D": "Vaginal estradiol cream, which relieves hot flashes through its steady absorption into the whole bloodstream"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Venlafaxine extended release, an SNRI shown to reduce the frequency of hot flashes): Venlafaxine and some other SSRIs and SNRIs, as well as gabapentin, reduce hot flashes and carry no estrogen related clot risk. Raloxifene can worsen hot flashes and raises venous thromboembolism risk. Vaginal estrogen treats local symptoms only, and soy products have not matched estrogen in trials."}, {"id": 1591, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "A pharmacy technician (ZH) who has prepared cytotoxic drugs for two years tells the pharmacist she is eight weeks pregnant. She feels capable and wants to keep her usual rotation so she does not lose income. The pharmacy has a hazardous drug policy. What should the pharmacist do?", "options": {"A": "Let her continue her rotation, since two years of safe practice shows her technique prevents exposure", "B": "Limit her to oral hazardous drugs only, since these carry a lower exposure risk overall", "C": "Offer reassignment away from hazardous drug handling during pregnancy, keeping her pay and hours where possible", "D": "Let her continue if she wears a respirator and a third pair of gloves each time"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Offer reassignment away from hazardous drug handling during pregnancy, keeping her pay and hours where possible): Many hazardous drugs are reproductive hazards, and standard guidance, including NIOSH and NAPRA-aligned policies, supports allowing staff who are pregnant or trying to conceive to avoid handling them. Experience and extra personal protective equipment reduce but do not remove exposure, and oral drugs still release dust. The pharmacist should offer alternative duties rather than leaving the choice to the individual."}, {"id": 1592, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 1, "question": "SL is an 83 year old woman with nonvalvular atrial fibrillation. She weighs 58 kg and her serum creatinine is 110 umol/L. Her physician prescribes apixaban 5 mg twice daily. The pharmacist reviews the dose against the product monograph criteria. What is the appropriate recommendation?", "options": {"A": "Give 2.5 mg twice daily, as two reduction criteria are met: age and weight", "B": "Change to 2.5 mg once daily, since patients over 80 need once daily dosing", "C": "Keep 5 mg twice daily, since reduction requires all three of age, weight and creatinine", "D": "Keep 5 mg twice daily, since the dose is reduced only when creatinine exceeds 133 umol/L"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Give 2.5 mg twice daily, as two reduction criteria are met: age and weight): Apixaban is reduced to 2.5 mg twice daily when at least two of three criteria are present: age 80 or older, weight 60 kg or less, creatinine 133 umol/L or higher. She is 83 and weighs 58 kg, so two are met even though creatinine is below the threshold. The drug stays twice daily."}, {"id": 1593, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 1, "question": "CJ is a 70 year old man taking rivaroxaban 20 mg once daily with his 6 pm dinner for atrial fibrillation. He calls the pharmacy at 10 pm, realizing he forgot today's dose. He has no bleeding. What should the pharmacist tell him?", "options": {"A": "Skip today's dose, because the missed dose cannot be taken after the scheduled time has passed", "B": "Skip today's dose and take two tablets tomorrow evening to make up for the missed anticoagulant", "C": "Take the missed dose now with food, then return to the usual time tomorrow", "D": "Take half a tablet now and half tomorrow, so the daily total stays near 20 mg"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Take the missed dose now with food, then return to the usual time tomorrow): For a once daily direct oral anticoagulant, a missed dose is taken as soon as remembered on the same day, and the next dose returns to the normal schedule. Doubling the next dose raises bleeding risk. Splitting a tablet is not recommended, and skipping leaves a long gap in protection."}, {"id": 1594, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "A hospital is designing a non-hazardous sterile compounding suite with an anteroom, a buffer room and a corridor. The pharmacist reviews the planned air pressure arrangement with the engineer. Which pressure relationship best protects the compounding environment from contamination entering from less clean areas?", "options": {"A": "The anteroom has the lowest pressure, so air is drawn out of the buffer room", "B": "All three rooms are kept at identical pressure, so air movement stays minimal inside the suite", "C": "The buffer room has the highest pressure, the anteroom less, and the corridor the lowest", "D": "The corridor has the highest pressure, so air flows from the corridor into the buffer room"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The buffer room has the highest pressure, the anteroom less, and the corridor the lowest): Positive pressure cascades from the cleanest room outward, so air flows from the buffer room into the anteroom and from the anteroom into the corridor, carrying contaminants away from the critical area. If the corridor or anteroom were at higher pressure, or all pressures equal, unfiltered air could enter the buffer room. Hazardous drug suites use the opposite design in the buffer room."}, {"id": 1595, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "A patient asks for private counselling on a new vaginal estrogen cream and says that for religious reasons she prefers a female pharmacist. Only a male pharmacist is on duty until a female colleague arrives in 40 minutes. What is the best approach?", "options": {"A": "Explain that care does not depend on pharmacist gender, then counsel her now privately", "B": "Ask her to explain her religious beliefs so the pharmacy can judge the request", "C": "Dispense the cream with a written leaflet and skip counselling, since her preference cannot be met", "D": "Offer her the choice of waiting for the female pharmacist or private counselling now"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Offer her the choice of waiting for the female pharmacist or private counselling now): A culturally safe response respects the patient's reasonable preference and gives her the choice between waiting for a female pharmacist and being counselled promptly in private by the pharmacist on duty. Overriding the request, skipping counselling or requiring the patient to justify her beliefs disregards her autonomy and leaves a safety gap, since a new vaginal estrogen product needs proper counselling."}, {"id": 1596, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "A grandmother brings her 6 year old grandson to the pharmacy for a scheduled vaccine, saying his parents are travelling this week. She has no written authorization. The pharmacist knows she is a regular customer. What is the appropriate step?", "options": {"A": "Get consent from a parent or guardian, by documented phone call if permitted, before vaccinating", "B": "Offer a different vaccine that is routinely given without parental consent to young children under seven", "C": "Vaccinate the child and ask the grandmother to sign the consent form instead of the parents", "D": "Vaccinate the child, since a grandparent bringing him is accepted as proof of the family's consent"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Get consent from a parent or guardian, by documented phone call if permitted, before vaccinating): A young child cannot consent, so a parent or legal guardian must do so unless the law gives someone else authority. A relative's signature does not substitute, though a documented phone consent may be acceptable under the pharmacy's policy."}, {"id": 1597, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "GT is a 72 year old man with Parkinson disease who is starting entacapone 200 mg with each carbidopa-levodopa dose, taken four times daily. At pickup, his daughter, who manages his pills, asks the pharmacist what changes and side effects to expect. Which counselling statement is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "Stool will turn black and signals bleeding, so entacapone should be stopped and a doctor called", "B": "Urine may turn brown-orange and is harmless, but dyskinesia can increase and levodopa may need lowering", "C": "Entacapone must be taken alone on an empty stomach, apart from his carbidopa-levodopa doses", "D": "Entacapone replaces carbidopa, so the carbidopa component of his levodopa tablets can be removed"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Urine may turn brown-orange and is harmless, but dyskinesia can increase and levodopa may need lowering): Entacapone inhibits COMT, which extends levodopa effect, and it commonly discolours urine harmlessly. Greater levodopa availability can increase dyskinesia, in which case the prescriber lowers the levodopa dose. It is taken together with each levodopa dose and does not replace carbidopa, which blocks a different enzyme."}, {"id": 1598, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "On its public social media page, a pharmacy receives a post from a follower whose son, 4 years old, has a fever of 39.5 C. She asks how much children's ibuprofen to give. The pharmacist on duty wants to respond helpfully. Which reply best meets professional standards?", "options": {"A": "Reply publicly with the standard ibuprofen dose per kilogram and advise care if he worsens", "B": "Say the label dose is safe and ask her to message you her son's weight", "C": "Invite her to call or visit privately so her son can be assessed properly", "D": "Delete her post because pharmacists may not respond to clinical questions on social media platforms"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Invite her to call or visit privately so her son can be assessed properly): A child with a high fever needs an individual assessment, including weight, age and warning signs, which cannot be done safely in a public thread. Moving the conversation to a private phone call or visit protects confidentiality and lets the pharmacist give a proper dose. Posting a dose publicly or deleting the post without helping both fall short of professional standards."}, {"id": 1599, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 1, "question": "ZK is a 69 year old man with type 2 diabetes, an A1c of 8.4% and heart failure with an ejection fraction of 30% and NYHA class III symptoms. His prescriber writes for pioglitazone 30 mg daily in addition to metformin. What should the pharmacist recommend?", "options": {"A": "Suggest glyburide, since sulfonylureas have no effect on fluid balance and lower A1c reliably", "B": "Suggest an SGLT2 inhibitor, since pioglitazone causes fluid retention and should be avoided here", "C": "Start pioglitazone at 15 mg daily and add a thiazide to offset the expected fluid gain", "D": "Suggest saxagliptin, since dipeptidyl peptidase-4 inhibitors improve both heart failure outcomes and glucose control"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Suggest an SGLT2 inhibitor, since pioglitazone causes fluid retention and should be avoided here): Thiazolidinediones such as pioglitazone cause sodium and fluid retention and Thiazolidinediones such as pioglitazone cause sodium and fluid retention and are contraindicated in heart failure (the Canadian monograph lists NYHA class I to IV).. SGLT2 inhibitors lower A1c modestly and improve heart failure outcomes. Saxagliptin has been linked to more heart failure hospitalization, and sulfonylureas do nothing for the heart failure itself."}, {"id": 1600, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 3, "question": "A pharmacist reviewing long-term care and prenatal clinic lists finds four patients with positive urine cultures and no urinary symptoms, no fever and no other signs of infection. Each patient has otherwise been stable for several weeks. Which patient should receive an antibiotic for asymptomatic bacteriuria?", "options": {"A": "A woman 12 weeks pregnant whose bacteriuria was found during routine prenatal screening", "B": "A 45 year old woman with type 2 diabetes and good control found on screening", "C": "A 66 year old man with a long-term indwelling urinary catheter and cloudy urine", "D": "A 79 year old woman in long-term care whose urine smells strongly and looks cloudy"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A woman 12 weeks pregnant whose bacteriuria was found during routine prenatal screening): Pregnancy is a main indication for treating asymptomatic bacteriuria, since it raises risk of pyelonephritis and preterm birth. Smell, cloudiness, catheters and diabetes alone do not justify treatment without symptoms, and treating them promotes resistance and C. difficile."}, {"id": 1601, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "SE is a 22 year old man who received his first haloperidol 5 mg dose in the emergency department six hours ago. He now presents to the community pharmacy with painful neck twisting and an upward eye deviation that began an hour ago. He is frightened. What should the pharmacist recommend?", "options": {"A": "A call to the poison centre, since the reaction signals haloperidol overdose needing activated charcoal", "B": "Reassurance that the spasm will pass, with an extra haloperidol dose to relax the muscles", "C": "A trial of oral acetaminophen and a heating pad, because the neck spasm is muscular strain", "D": "Urgent medical assessment for acute dystonia, which responds to an anticholinergic such as benztropine"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Urgent medical assessment for acute dystonia, which responds to an anticholinergic such as benztropine): Acute dystonia is a common early extrapyramidal reaction to high-potency antipsychotics such as haloperidol, appearing within hours to days. It is treated with an anticholinergic such as benztropine or diphenhydramine and needs prompt medical assessment. Extra haloperidol would worsen it, and it is not a muscle strain or an overdose."}, {"id": 1602, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A 63 year old man with stable angina uses nitroglycerin spray as needed and takes metoprolol and rosuvastatin. He presents a new prescription for sildenafil 50 mg to be used tonight and mentions he sprayed nitroglycerin this morning for chest tightness. The pharmacist's priority is to:", "options": {"A": "Dispense sildenafil at a reduced dose of 25 mg because metoprolol and rosuvastatin raise sildenafil levels", "B": "Dispense sildenafil with advice to wait at least 12 hours after the nitroglycerin spray before use", "C": "Withhold sildenafil and contact the prescriber, since nitrates and sildenafil together can cause severe hypotension", "D": "Dispense sildenafil and advise using the nitroglycerin spray only while seated if chest pain recurs"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Withhold sildenafil and contact the prescriber, since nitrates and sildenafil together can cause severe hypotension): Phosphodiesterase-5 inhibitors potentiate nitrate induced vasodilation through cyclic GMP, and the combination can cause profound hypotension and ischemia. A patient with active angina who needs nitrates should not receive sildenafil at all. A short wait or a lower dose does not make the combination safe, and the prescriber needs to be informed."}, {"id": 1603, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A resident asks a ward pharmacist for the usual dose adjustment of an antibiotic in a patient with a creatinine clearance of 25 mL/min. The pharmacist needs a quick, reasonably authoritative answer with citations. Which resource is the most efficient first choice?", "options": {"A": "A tertiary reference such as Lexicomp, which summarizes renal dosing recommendations with supporting citations", "B": "A primary literature search for pharmacokinetic trials, which provides original data but needs time to appraise", "C": "A call to the manufacturer, which gives tailored dosing advice but is slower than a reference", "D": "A general medicine textbook published years ago, which describes disease management more than drug dosing"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A tertiary reference such as Lexicomp, which summarizes renal dosing recommendations with supporting citations): Tertiary references condense and cite renal dosing recommendations, making them the fastest reliable starting point for a routine question. Primary searches and manufacturer calls are slower and reserved for gaps. The pharmacist should still confirm against the Canadian monograph when labelling differs."}, {"id": 1604, "domain": "communication_and_collaboration", "chapter": 11, "question": "At a long-term care team meeting, nurses report that a resident, 86 year old FW, has fallen three times this month. Her medications include zopiclone 7.5 mg at bedtime, tamsulosin, ramipril, sertraline and oxybutynin. The pharmacist is asked for input. What is the most helpful contribution?", "options": {"A": "Suggest increasing tamsulosin to improve urinary symptoms at night so she wakes less often", "B": "Advise that falls are expected at her age and suggest restricting her mobility on the unit", "C": "Identify fall-risk medications and propose a stepwise deprescribing plan to the physician", "D": "Recommend adding vitamin D and calcium supplements and a hip protector order instead of changing drugs"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Identify fall-risk medications and propose a stepwise deprescribing plan to the physician): Sedative hypnotics, anticholinergics such as oxybutynin and alpha blockers contribute to falls and orthostasis in older adults. A pharmacist's medication review with a concrete deprescribing proposal gives the team a practical action. Supplements may help bone health but do not remove the cause, and restricting mobility harms function."}, {"id": 1605, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 3, "question": "MD is a 24 year old man with urethral discharge and dysuria for 3 days. A Gram stain at the clinic shows gram-negative intracellular diplococci. He has no allergies and is otherwise healthy. Which treatment follows current Canadian guidance for uncomplicated urogenital gonorrhea?", "options": {"A": "A single oral dose of ciprofloxacin 500 mg, chosen because it is inexpensive and convenient", "B": "Oral azithromycin 1 g alone as a single dose, avoiding the need for an injection", "C": "Oral doxycycline 100 mg twice daily for 7 days, which covers gonorrhea reliably", "D": "A single intramuscular dose of ceftriaxone, with testing for other sexually transmitted infections"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A single intramuscular dose of ceftriaxone, with testing for other sexually transmitted infections): Ceftriaxone given by injection is the recommended backbone for gonorrhea because resistance to older oral agents is widespread. Ciprofloxacin and azithromycin alone, and doxycycline, are no longer reliable. Patients should also be offered testing for chlamydia, HIV and syphilis."}, {"id": 1606, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "WK is an 80 year old woman with CKD (CrCl 25 mL/min) who takes gabapentin 600 mg three times daily for neuropathic pain. Her family reports sedation, unsteadiness and myoclonic jerks over the past week. Which action should the pharmacist suggest?", "options": {"A": "Switch to pregabalin at the same milligram dose, because pregabalin is cleared by the liver", "B": "Reduce the gabapentin dose to match her clearance, since accumulation causes the neurologic toxicity she shows", "C": "Continue the current dose and recheck in one month, since effects resolve as the body adapts", "D": "Increase to 900 mg three times daily, since uncontrolled pain probably explains her agitation and jerks"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Reduce the gabapentin dose to match her clearance, since accumulation causes the neurologic toxicity she shows): Gabapentin is eliminated unchanged by the kidney, and 1800 mg per day far exceeds the limit for a clearance of 25 mL/min. Sedation, ataxia and myoclonus are signs of accumulation. Pregabalin is also renally cleared and needs equivalent reduction."}, {"id": 1607, "domain": "communication_and_collaboration", "chapter": 11, "question": "A prescriber has ordered an antibiotic for an 82 year old woman weighing 55 kg with a serum creatinine of 120 umol/L, and the pharmacist needs her creatinine clearance for the call. Which value should the pharmacist quote using the Cockcroft-Gault equation with actual body weight?", "options": {"A": "About 28 mL/min, using the female factor of 1.04", "B": "About 33 mL/min, using the male factor of 1.23", "C": "About 35 mL/min, using the female factor and a weight of 70 kg", "D": "About 21 mL/min, using the female factor and a creatinine of 160 umol/L"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (About 28 mL/min, using the female factor of 1.04): Cockcroft-Gault gives (140 - 82) x 55 x 1.04 / 120, which is 58 x 55 x 1.04 = 3318, then divided by 120 equals about 27.6 mL/min. Using the male factor or an incorrect weight overstates clearance."}, {"id": 1608, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 4, "question": "OA is a 51 year old kidney transplant recipient taking tacrolimus 4 mg twice daily at 08:00 and 20:00. She arrives for a routine trough blood test at 09:00 after taking her morning dose at 08:00. The lab is ready to draw the sample. What should the pharmacist advise?", "options": {"A": "A trough sample is drawn just before the dose, so this result would be misleading", "B": "Skip the evening dose and draw the sample tomorrow morning so that the timing is exact", "C": "The sample is valid because tacrolimus reaches a steady plateau within one hour of every dose", "D": "Draw it at 14:00 because tacrolimus troughs are measured six hours after the morning dose"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A trough sample is drawn just before the dose, so this result would be misleading): A trough reflects the lowest level just before the next dose, usually about 12 hours after the previous one. A sample one hour after a dose is near the peak and would overstate exposure, risking an unneeded dose cut. The sample should be rebooked for before a dose, without skipping any dose."}, {"id": 1609, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 6, "question": "ZP is an 18 year old who has just taken levonorgestrel 1.5 mg after unprotected intercourse 24 hours ago. She asks what to expect and what to do next. Her last period was two weeks ago, and she has no other medical issues. What should the pharmacist tell her about her next period?", "options": {"A": "It will be heavier than usual, so she should seek care if it lasts three days", "B": "It will be skipped this cycle, so she should expect no bleeding until the following month", "C": "It may arrive early or late, so she should test if over seven days late", "D": "It will come on time, and any delay of even one day confirms the method failed"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (It may arrive early or late, so she should test if over seven days late): Levonorgestrel can shift ovulation, so the next period may come earlier or later than expected. A pregnancy test is advised if it is more than about a week late. A one day delay does not show failure, and the period is not skipped or reliably heavy."}, {"id": 1610, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 6, "question": "NZ is a 32 year old at 14 weeks gestation with chronic hypertension. Her blood pressure is 152/98 mmHg on enalapril 10 mg daily, and she has asthma treated with budesonide and salbutamol. Her obstetrician asks the pharmacist for a suitable pregnancy-compatible replacement. Which option is best?", "options": {"A": "Labetalol, because it is the only pregnancy-compatible agent and asthma is no concern with it", "B": "Losartan, because angiotensin receptor blockers are preferred when an ACE inhibitor has been poorly tolerated", "C": "Atenolol, because beta blockers have the strongest evidence for fetal growth and perinatal outcomes", "D": "Extended-release nifedipine, which suits pregnancy and avoids the bronchospasm risk of beta blockers"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Extended-release nifedipine, which suits pregnancy and avoids the bronchospasm risk of beta blockers): Extended-release nifedipine is an accepted pregnancy antihypertensive and does not worsen asthma. Labetalol is also used in pregnancy but its beta blockade can provoke bronchospasm, so it is a poorer fit for her. Losartan carries the same fetotoxicity as enalapril, and atenolol is associated with poor fetal growth."}, {"id": 1611, "domain": "communication_and_collaboration", "chapter": 11, "question": "A pharmacist is explaining the benefit of a daily statin to a patient with limited health literacy who reads slowly and wants to avoid jargon. The patient's ten year heart attack risk falls from 10 percent to 6 percent with treatment. Which wording is the clearest way to explain this benefit?", "options": {"A": "It reduces your relative risk of a heart attack by 40 percent compared with taking nothing", "B": "Your heart attack risk is multiplied by a factor of 0.6 compared with having no statin", "C": "The number needed to treat to prevent one heart attack over ten years is twenty five", "D": "Out of 100 people like you, 10 have heart attacks untreated and 6 when treated"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Out of 100 people like you, 10 have heart attacks untreated and 6 when treated): Natural frequencies out of 100 people are easier for patients with limited numeracy to grasp than relative risk, ratios or number needed to treat. The other statements are mathematically consistent with the same figures, but only this wording communicates the absolute benefit plainly."}, {"id": 1612, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 3, "question": "HQ is a 67 year old man with atrial fibrillation taking apixaban 5 mg twice daily who is about to start a rifampin containing regimen for active tuberculosis. He asks whether he can remain on his current anticoagulant for stroke prevention. What should the pharmacist advise?", "options": {"A": "Avoid apixaban with rifampin and change to warfarin, with frequent INR monitoring during induction", "B": "Switch to dabigatran, which is not metabolized by cytochrome enzymes and is unaffected by rifampin", "C": "Continue apixaban 5 mg twice daily, since its dual clearance route protects it from enzyme induction", "D": "Increase apixaban to 10 mg twice daily during rifampin to compensate for the lower drug levels"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Avoid apixaban with rifampin and change to warfarin, with frequent INR monitoring during induction): Rifampin induces CYP3A4 and P-glycoprotein, which cuts apixaban exposure to the point where stroke protection is unreliable, and the same applies to dabigatran, rivaroxaban and edoxaban. Warfarin remains workable because the INR can be followed and the dose raised, though requirements may double. Raising the apixaban dose is not an established strategy."}, {"id": 1613, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "A pharmacy grades incidents with the NCC MERP index. Category C means the error reached the patient with no harm and no extra monitoring, D means it reached the patient and required monitoring, and E means temporary harm needing intervention. A wrong-strength tablet reached a patient, who needed extra blood pressure checks but was unharmed. Which category applies?", "options": {"A": "Category E, because the wrong strength required a nurse to intervene with a temporary treatment change", "B": "Category C, because the wrong-strength tablet reached the patient and ultimately no harm occurred", "C": "Category B, because the error was detected before the patient was affected by the tablet", "D": "Category D, because monitoring was required to confirm that the error caused no harm"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Category D, because monitoring was required to confirm that the error caused no harm): Category C applies when an error reaches the patient without harm and without any need for extra monitoring, while category D applies when it reaches the patient and monitoring is needed to confirm no harm resulted. Here the extra blood pressure checks make it category D. Category E requires actual temporary harm, and an error that reached the patient cannot be a category B event."}, {"id": 1614, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "A technician finds 6 bottles of an expensive drug that no patient has purchased in 14 months, and 2 of the bottles expire in 5 months. The pharmacy owner asks the pharmacist how to manage this dead stock. What is the best approach?", "options": {"A": "Reorder it in the same quantity so the shelf stays fully stocked in case of demand", "B": "Dispose of all the bottles through household waste to free up shelf space", "C": "Review usage, return eligible bottles to the wholesaler, and adjust the minimum order level", "D": "Keep the stock and extend the expiry dates to match the wholesaler's later shipments"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Review usage, return eligible bottles to the wholesaler, and adjust the minimum order level): Slow-moving stock ties up money and risks expiry, so the pharmacy reviews usage, returns what the wholesaler will accept, and lowers the stocking level. Reordering repeats the problem, expiry dates cannot be changed by the pharmacy, and pharmaceutical waste must go through a proper disposal program rather than household garbage."}, {"id": 1615, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "LB is an 86 year old man with Alzheimer disease who uses a rivastigmine 9.5 mg per 24 hours patch. His caregiver admits she sometimes leaves the old patch on while adding a new one to be safe. What should the pharmacist counsel about patch use?", "options": {"A": "Remove the old patch before applying one new patch daily, and rotate the site", "B": "Apply the new patch to the same area each day, since one site gives steady absorption", "C": "Overlapping two patches is acceptable for a day, since it ensures steady drug levels during replacement", "D": "Cut the patch in half if nausea occurs, and place both halves on the chest"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Remove the old patch before applying one new patch daily, and rotate the site): A rivastigmine patch is changed every 24 hours: the old patch is removed before the new one is applied, and sites are rotated, with at least 14 days before the same spot is reused, to limit skin reactions. Wearing two patches delivers a double dose and raises the risk of vomiting, bradycardia, and weight loss. Cutting a patch alters drug release."}, {"id": 1616, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 1, "question": "GM is a 63 year old man with symptomatic paroxysmal atrial fibrillation who had an anterior myocardial infarction three years ago. His ejection fraction is 40%. A physician orders flecainide 100 mg twice daily to maintain sinus rhythm, and he already takes bisoprolol and apixaban. How should the pharmacist respond?", "options": {"A": "Dispense it at a lower dose of 50 mg twice daily, which removes the proarrhythmic risk", "B": "Dispense it and stop bisoprolol, because beta-blockers prevent flecainide from restoring sinus rhythm", "C": "Dispense it, since class Ic drugs are preferred for rhythm control when ejection fraction is reduced", "D": "Call the prescriber, since flecainide is contraindicated after myocardial infarction or structural heart disease"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Call the prescriber, since flecainide is contraindicated after myocardial infarction or structural heart disease): Flecainide increased mortality after myocardial infarction in the CAST trial and is contraindicated in structural heart disease. A lower dose does not remove the proarrhythmic risk. Amiodarone or sotalol are more suitable rhythm control alternatives here, and the beta-blocker should be continued."}, {"id": 1617, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 4, "question": "LS is a 29 year old woman with seasonal allergic rhinitis who has taken loratadine 10 mg daily for three weeks. She still has constant nasal congestion and sneezing that disturb her sleep, and her symptoms are moderate to severe. She asks about a stronger antihistamine. The pharmacist should recommend:", "options": {"A": "adding an intranasal corticosteroid, which works better than antihistamines for moderate to severe symptoms", "B": "replacing loratadine with diphenhydramine at bedtime, which is more effective for nasal congestion", "C": "doubling the loratadine dose to 20 mg, since higher doses improve nasal congestion", "D": "adding a short course of oral pseudoephedrine for several weeks to control the congestion"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (adding an intranasal corticosteroid, which works better than antihistamines for moderate to severe symptoms): Intranasal corticosteroids are the most effective single therapy for moderate to severe allergic rhinitis, particularly for congestion, which antihistamines treat poorly. Doubling the antihistamine dose adds side effects without clear benefit, and diphenhydramine is sedating and no better for congestion. Oral decongestants are for brief use only."}, {"id": 1618, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "A 38 year old man presents a new prescription for oxycodone 10 mg, quantity 60, from a walk-in clinic physician. The provincial monitoring system shows an oxycodone fill from another prescriber six days ago and a lorazepam fill last week. What is the most appropriate action?", "options": {"A": "Decline to dispense and tell him to return with a prescription from his regular physician", "B": "Dispense half of the quantity and tell him to return for the remainder next week", "C": "Dispense the full quantity and report the walk-in clinic physician to the provincial college immediately", "D": "Hold the prescription, discuss the overlapping fills with him, and contact both prescribers before deciding"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Hold the prescription, discuss the overlapping fills with him, and contact both prescribers before deciding): A prescription monitoring system alert is a drug therapy problem that must be assessed before dispensing, especially with an opioid plus a benzodiazepine. The pharmacist should talk with the patient and contact the prescribers to confirm the plan, then decide using professional judgment and document it. Dispensing without assessment or turning him away without exploring the cause both fall short of that standard."}, {"id": 1619, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 3, "question": "WP is a 68 year old woman with community-acquired pneumonia treated at home. She takes simvastatin 40 mg nightly and amlodipine 5 mg daily. The prescriber has ordered clarithromycin 500 mg twice daily with amoxicillin. What should the pharmacist advise?", "options": {"A": "Continue as ordered and counsel her to take simvastatin in the morning, which avoids any overlap", "B": "Reduce clarithromycin to 250 mg twice daily since the interaction depends mostly on the antibiotic dose", "C": "Replace amoxicillin with cefuroxime, since the penicillin raises simvastatin concentrations through P-glycoprotein inhibition", "D": "Replace clarithromycin with doxycycline, because clarithromycin inhibits CYP3A4 and raises simvastatin levels with myopathy risk"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Replace clarithromycin with doxycycline, because clarithromycin inhibits CYP3A4 and raises simvastatin levels with myopathy risk): Clarithromycin is a strong CYP3A4 inhibitor that markedly raises simvastatin exposure and risks rhabdomyolysis, and timing changes do not prevent it. Doxycycline provides atypical coverage without the interaction. Amoxicillin does not affect simvastatin."}, {"id": 1620, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 1, "question": "PW is a 70 year old woman with heart failure who takes furosemide 40 mg each morning. Her weight has risen from 71 kg to 74 kg over eight days, with new ankle edema and two pillows needed to sleep. Her blood pressure and creatinine are stable. What should the pharmacist suggest?", "options": {"A": "Recommend increasing furosemide, for example to 40 mg twice daily, with a recheck of creatinine", "B": "Advise a fluid restriction of 500 mL daily and keep the same furosemide dose for now", "C": "Switch furosemide to evening dosing, since the timing of the dose explains the weight gain", "D": "Add metolazone 5 mg daily straight away, since a thiazide is needed each time edema returns"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Recommend increasing furosemide, for example to 40 mg twice daily, with a recheck of creatinine): A gain of 3 kg in a week with edema and orthopnea shows fluid overload that the current loop diuretic dose does not control, so the loop diuretic is increased or divided. Fluid restriction alone is rarely enough, and a thiazide is added only if a higher loop dose fails. Timing of the dose does not explain the accumulation of fluid."}, {"id": 1621, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "A nurse practitioner in a rural clinic sends a prescription for clonazepam 0.5 mg to a community pharmacy for a 47 year old patient with panic disorder. The pharmacist has not received prescriptions from nurse practitioners for targeted substances before and asks whether the prescription is valid. What is the correct assessment?", "options": {"A": "It is invalid because only physicians and dentists may prescribe targeted substances under federal law", "B": "It is valid only when a physician countersigns the prescription within seventy two hours of issue", "C": "It is invalid unless the nurse practitioner holds a separate federal licence from Health Canada", "D": "It is valid when the province authorizes nurse practitioners to prescribe this targeted substance"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (It is valid when the province authorizes nurse practitioners to prescribe this targeted substance): Federal law recognizes practitioners who are authorized by their province to prescribe the drug, and many provinces authorize nurse practitioners to prescribe targeted substances. No federal licence or physician countersignature is required."}, {"id": 1622, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 6, "question": "HJ is a 30 year old breastfeeding her eight week old infant and has seasonal allergic rhinitis with sneezing and itchy eyes. She was using diphenhydramine 50 mg at bedtime and found her supply seemed lower and her infant sleepy. Which antihistamine should the pharmacist suggest instead?", "options": {"A": "Chlorpheniramine 4 mg every six hours, because first-generation agents are the safest class while nursing", "B": "Loratadine 10 mg daily, because it has low milk transfer and little sedating effect", "C": "Hydroxyzine 25 mg at bedtime, because it is sedating but leaves milk supply unchanged", "D": "Diphenhydramine 25 mg at bedtime, because halving the dose removes any effect on the infant"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Loratadine 10 mg daily, because it has low milk transfer and little sedating effect): Second-generation antihistamines such as loratadine pass into milk in small amounts and cause little sedation, so they are preferred. Sedating first-generation drugs can cause infant drowsiness and may lower milk supply. Halving the dose of diphenhydramine reduces but does not remove those effects."}, {"id": 1623, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "PW is a 45 year old wheelchair user who has been counselled about her new lamotrigine at the open dispensary counter, because the only private counselling room has a step and a narrow door. She asks for privacy. Which response best fulfils the pharmacy's professional obligations?", "options": {"A": "Ask her to send a family member to the counselling room and relay the information", "B": "Provide an accessible private space for counselling now and plan a permanent accessibility modification", "C": "Provide written handouts only, because the existing room cannot be changed in the short term", "D": "Continue at the counter but lower your voice and ask nearby customers to step back"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Provide an accessible private space for counselling now and plan a permanent accessibility modification): Patients with disabilities are entitled to the same privacy for counselling as others, so the pharmacy should find an accessible private space immediately and plan lasting changes. Lowering one's voice at an open counter does not protect confidentiality. Handouts or a relative acting as a go-between substitute for her own counselling and shift the barrier onto her."}, {"id": 1624, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 6, "question": "A pharmacy student asks how to tell candidiasis from bacterial vaginosis by history alone. FW, a 45 year old woman, describes thick white cottage cheese like discharge, vulvar itching and redness, and no odour. Which feature best supports vulvovaginal candidiasis rather than bacterial vaginosis in this presentation?", "options": {"A": "Frothy yellow green discharge with an elevated vaginal pH above 5.0 and prominent urinary symptoms", "B": "Mucopurulent discharge with cervical friability and a vaginal pH above 5.0 after recent intercourse", "C": "Thin homogeneous grey discharge with a positive whiff test after adding potassium hydroxide to the sample", "D": "Curdy discharge with pruritus and a normal vaginal pH of 4.0 to 4.5"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Curdy discharge with pruritus and a normal vaginal pH of 4.0 to 4.5): Candidiasis typically causes thick white discharge with itching and erythema while the vaginal pH stays in the normal range of 4.0 to 4.5. An elevated pH and a positive whiff test point to bacterial vaginosis, and frothy discharge with urinary symptoms points to trichomoniasis."}, {"id": 1625, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "A 68 year old woman weighing 72 kg with a complicated urinary tract infection is prescribed gentamicin 5 mg/kg intravenously every 24 hours. Her renal function is normal. The pharmacy stocks gentamicin 40 mg/mL vials. What volume should be drawn up for one dose?", "options": {"A": "18 mL, which delivers 720 mg based on 10 mg/kg dosing", "B": "4.5 mL, which delivers 180 mg based on 2.5 mg/kg dosing", "C": "9 mL, which delivers 360 mg for her 72 kg body weight", "D": "1.8 mL, which delivers 72 mg and is based on one milligram per kilogram"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (9 mL, which delivers 360 mg for her 72 kg body weight): The dose is 5 mg/kg x 72 kg = 360 mg, and 360 mg / 40 mg per mL = 9 mL. The other volumes correspond to 1 mg/kg, 10 mg/kg and 2.5 mg/kg. Weight-based aminoglycoside orders should be checked against actual body weight before any volume is measured."}, {"id": 1626, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "OL is a 65 year old woman on carbidopa-levodopa 100/25 mg four times daily. She feels stiffness return about thirty minutes before each dose, and her neurologist asks the pharmacist which add-on would best extend the benefit of each levodopa dose. What is the best suggestion?", "options": {"A": "Add a second levodopa dose at once to each existing dose, which lengthens the daily regimen", "B": "Add a proton pump inhibitor, since reduced stomach acid prolongs the duration of levodopa action", "C": "Add entacapone with each levodopa dose, because COMT inhibition lengthens the effect of every dose", "D": "Add benztropine at bedtime, because anticholinergics extend levodopa action and reduce wearing off"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Add entacapone with each levodopa dose, because COMT inhibition lengthens the effect of every dose): End-of-dose wearing off arises because levodopa has a short half-life. COMT inhibitors such as entacapone, and MAO-B inhibitors, extend its effect. Anticholinergics treat tremor but not wearing off, and doubling each dose adds dyskinesia risk. Gastric acid suppression does not extend levodopa action."}, {"id": 1627, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "Shares in a company that makes an over-the-counter nasal spray are held by pharmacist DX. HL, a 44 year old patient, asks which spray is best for allergies, and a cheaper competing brand has equivalent evidence. How should DX handle the recommendation?", "options": {"A": "Disclose the shareholding and present both products fairly, including the cheaper equivalent", "B": "Recommend the shareholder product and keep the shares private, since the evidence supports both sprays equally", "C": "Recommend the cheaper spray without explanation and avoid discussing the shares to prevent any confusion", "D": "Refer HL to another pharmacist, because owning shares bars DX from advising on any allergy product"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Disclose the shareholding and present both products fairly, including the cheaper equivalent): Holding shares in a manufacturer is a conflict that must be disclosed. Presenting both products fairly, including the cheaper equivalent, lets HL choose with full information. Hiding the interest or steering to the cheaper brand without explanation withholds relevant facts, and owning shares does not bar a pharmacist from advising."}, {"id": 1628, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "TG is an 86 year old woman weighing 42 kg, frail with little muscle mass, whose serum creatinine is 70 umol/L, reported as within the laboratory normal range. A student suggests her kidney function is therefore normal and no dose changes are needed. What should the pharmacist explain?", "options": {"A": "Serum creatinine reflects kidney function only, so a normal value means the doses are fine", "B": "Normal creatinine in older adults means kidney function is preserved, whatever the muscle mass", "C": "Low muscle mass lowers creatinine production, so a normal value can overestimate her kidney function", "D": "Frailty raises creatinine production, so the normal value suggests better kidney function"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Low muscle mass lowers creatinine production, so a normal value can overestimate her kidney function): Creatinine comes from muscle, so a frail patient produces little and the serum value can look normal despite reduced filtration. Cockcroft-Gault with her low weight and age gives a clearance near 30 mL/min, so doses of renally cleared drugs may need adjusting."}, {"id": 1629, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 4, "question": "WD is a 28 year old woman who is 12 weeks pregnant with worsening inflammatory acne on her cheeks. She used tretinoin cream before pregnancy and asks about safe options. Her physician is not available today. Which product is most appropriate for the pharmacist to suggest?", "options": {"A": "Topical tretinoin 0.01% gel, because the low strength is considered safe throughout the whole pregnancy period", "B": "Topical azelaic acid or benzoyl peroxide, which are generally regarded as acceptable in pregnancy", "C": "Isotretinoin 10 mg daily at a reduced dose, since lower doses carry a low fetal risk", "D": "Oral doxycycline 100 mg twice daily for six weeks, which is the preferred inflammatory acne option"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Topical azelaic acid or benzoyl peroxide, which are generally regarded as acceptable in pregnancy): Azelaic acid and benzoyl peroxide have little systemic absorption and are generally regarded as acceptable options in pregnancy. Doxycycline is avoided because tetracyclines affect fetal bone and teeth, isotretinoin is a potent teratogen at any dose, and topical retinoids are generally avoided as a precaution."}, {"id": 1630, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "A pharmacy launches an online store. A Schedule III product is listed with an add to cart button and no link to contact a pharmacist. The owner asks the pharmacist whether the site meets the requirements for sale of this schedule. Which requirement is the site missing?", "options": {"A": "A requirement that the pharmacy post a lower price than the shelf price in the store", "B": "A requirement that every online buyer upload a prescription issued by a practitioner for the product", "C": "A way for customers to consult a pharmacist before purchasing, equivalent to in-store availability", "D": "A requirement that the product be shipped only by a courier licensed as a pharmacy"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A way for customers to consult a pharmacist before purchasing, equivalent to in-store availability): Schedule III products need no prescription but depend on a pharmacist being available to help patients choose. An online listing must offer an equivalent means of contacting a pharmacist before purchase. Prescriptions, pricing rules and licensed couriers are not part of the schedule's conditions."}, {"id": 1631, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "JY is a 51 year old man with a private drug plan that uses a formulary. His new prescription is rejected as not a listed benefit, and the pharmacist wants to help him find affordable alternatives. What is the best first step?", "options": {"A": "Tell him the drug is unsafe because the plan did not approve it for payment", "B": "Check the plan's formulary for a covered alternative in the same class and contact the prescriber", "C": "Resubmit the claim with a different DIN until the plan accepts it for payment", "D": "Dispense a smaller quantity so that the claim falls under the plan's yearly dollar limit"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Check the plan's formulary for a covered alternative in the same class and contact the prescriber): A non-formulary rejection is a coverage issue, not a safety judgment. The pharmacist looks for a listed alternative and works with the prescriber to select one. Changing the DIN to force payment would be a false claim."}, {"id": 1632, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "VT is an 88 year old woman who received a 500 mL sodium chloride 0.9% bolus for dehydration, and the nephrology team is tracking her sodium load. Normal saline contains 154 mmol of sodium per litre. How much sodium did VT receive from this single bolus?", "options": {"A": "38.5 mmol of sodium from the bag, which is also 38.5 mEq", "B": "308 mmol of sodium from the bag, which is also 308 mEq", "C": "154 mmol of sodium from the bag, which is also 154 mEq", "D": "77 mmol of sodium, which is also 77 mEq"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (77 mmol of sodium, which is also 77 mEq): Half a litre holds half of 154 mmol, which is 77 mmol. Sodium is monovalent, so mmol and mEq are numerically equal. The 154 figure is the content of a full litre."}, {"id": 1633, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "AB asks which medication could reduce his craving and heavy drinking days after his recent withdrawal management. Considering his liver enzymes, his preference for once daily dosing, his missed midday doses and his current medications, which agent should the pharmacist recommend to the prescriber?", "options": {"A": "Lorazepam 1 mg three times daily, since benzodiazepines reduce craving during early abstinence", "B": "Naltrexone 50 mg daily, since it suits his routine and his enzymes are under twice normal", "C": "Disulfiram 250 mg daily, since it deters drinking and his liver enzymes are only mildly raised", "D": "Acamprosate 666 mg three times daily, since it needs no liver monitoring"}, "cluster_id": "CHS13", "cluster_text": "AB is a 52 year old man, 84 kg, who completed outpatient withdrawal management one week ago. He drank about ten standard drinks daily for eight years and wants help staying abstinent. He misses midday doses and prefers a once daily tablet. ALT is 62 U/L and AST 70 U/L (upper normal limit 40 U/L), bilirubin and creatinine are normal, and creatinine clearance is 85 mL/min. He takes lisinopril 10 mg daily and pantoprazole 40 mg daily, uses no opioids, and has no known allergies.", "cluster_size": 3, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Naltrexone 50 mg daily, since it suits his routine and his enzymes are under twice normal): Naltrexone once daily fits his preference for simple dosing, and mild transaminase elevation under twice the upper limit of normal does not preclude it. He uses no opioids, so there is no blockade problem. Acamprosate is a valid drug but needs three daily doses, which conflicts with his missed midday doses. Disulfiram is hepatotoxic, and benzodiazepines do not prevent relapse."}, {"id": 1634, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "AB, 52, completed withdrawal management a week ago after about ten drinks daily for eight years, and starts naltrexone 50 mg once daily. He asks what to expect and what he must tell other health care providers. Which counselling point should the pharmacist include?", "options": {"A": "Stop it if he drinks, and restart only after a week of abstinence", "B": "Expect drowsiness and avoid driving for the first month, since naltrexone is strongly sedating", "C": "Expect a flushing and nausea reaction if he drinks alcohol while taking the tablets", "D": "Carry a card stating he takes naltrexone, as opioid pain medicines will be blocked"}, "cluster_id": "CHS13", "cluster_text": "AB is a 52 year old man, 84 kg, who completed outpatient withdrawal management one week ago. He drank about ten standard drinks daily for eight years and wants help staying abstinent. He misses midday doses and prefers a once daily tablet. ALT is 62 U/L and AST 70 U/L (upper normal limit 40 U/L), bilirubin and creatinine are normal, and creatinine clearance is 85 mL/min. He takes lisinopril 10 mg daily and pantoprazole 40 mg daily, uses no opioids, and has no known allergies.", "cluster_size": 3, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Carry a card stating he takes naltrexone, as opioid pain medicines will be blocked): Naltrexone blocks opioid receptors, so opioid analgesics may not work and emergency teams need to know he takes it. It is not strongly sedating, it is meant to continue through a lapse rather than being stopped, and the flushing reaction belongs to disulfiram."}, {"id": 1635, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "Six months later AB is taking oral naltrexone once daily and remains abstinent. He now needs elective hernia surgery that will require opioid analgesia afterward, and his surgeon asks the pharmacist how the naltrexone should be handled around the operation. What should the pharmacist advise?", "options": {"A": "Stop naltrexone for good, since opioids are unsafe after any alcohol use disorder", "B": "Continue naltrexone through surgery and give higher opioid doses to overcome the receptor blockade", "C": "Hold naltrexone about three days before surgery and restart once opioids are finished", "D": "Hold naltrexone only on the morning of surgery, since its blockade wears off within hours"}, "cluster_id": "CHS13", "cluster_text": "AB is a 52 year old man, 84 kg, who completed outpatient withdrawal management one week ago. He drank about ten standard drinks daily for eight years and wants help staying abstinent. He misses midday doses and prefers a once daily tablet. ALT is 62 U/L and AST 70 U/L (upper normal limit 40 U/L), bilirubin and creatinine are normal, and creatinine clearance is 85 mL/min. He takes lisinopril 10 mg daily and pantoprazole 40 mg daily, uses no opioids, and has no known allergies.", "cluster_size": 3, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Hold naltrexone about three days before surgery and restart once opioids are finished): Oral naltrexone has an effect lasting about one to three days, so it is held roughly 72 hours before planned opioid use and restarted when opioids are stopped. Overriding the blockade with high opioid doses risks respiratory depression once the blockade wanes. Stopping it for good is unnecessary."}, {"id": 1636, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 1, "question": "GP is a 55 year old man who started warfarin three days ago for a new pulmonary embolism, with enoxaparin 1 mg/kg every 12 hours. Today his INR is 2.3. The ward pharmacist is asked whether the enoxaparin can be stopped. What is the best recommendation?", "options": {"A": "continue it until INR is 2.0 or higher for 24 hours and five days of overlap", "B": "stop it today and give aspirin 81 mg daily until the INR becomes stable above 2.0", "C": "continue it for a full month, since warfarin takes four weeks to reach a stable effect", "D": "stop it today, since a single INR above 2.0 means the warfarin is now protective"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (continue it until INR is 2.0 or higher for 24 hours and five days of overlap): Warfarin lowers clotting factor activity gradually, so an INR of 2.0 does not yet mean full protection. Parenteral anticoagulation overlaps for at least five days and continues until the INR is at least 2.0 for 24 hours."}, {"id": 1637, "domain": "communication_and_collaboration", "chapter": 11, "question": "After a dispensing error was disclosed, the patient asks the pharmacist who made the mistake and says he plans to file a complaint with the provincial college. He is upset but listening. The pharmacist wants to communicate honestly and professionally. Which response is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Name the technician who prepared the prescription so he knows who was responsible for the error", "B": "Take responsibility for the pharmacy, explain what happened, and describe how to file a complaint", "C": "Say the error was minor and that a complaint would not change anything about the outcome", "D": "Ask him to hold off on complaining until the pharmacy has completed its own internal review"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Take responsibility for the pharmacy, explain what happened, and describe how to file a complaint): Disclosure focuses on the system and the pharmacy's responsibility rather than naming or blaming an individual. Patients retain the right to complain and should be told how, and discouraging or minimizing the concern damages trust. Honest explanation of events and corrective steps is the professional standard."}, {"id": 1638, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 1, "question": "OL is a 60 year old man with newly diagnosed nonvalvular atrial fibrillation. He had a transient ischemic attack with brief left arm weakness eight months ago. He has no hypertension, diabetes or heart failure and takes only atorvastatin. His physician asks the pharmacist whether he needs anticoagulation. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Yes, a direct oral anticoagulant is advised since his prior transient ischemic attack qualifies him", "B": "Aspirin 81 mg daily is adequate, because the transient attack resolved fully without any lasting deficit", "C": "No, he is under 65 with no other risk factors, so no antithrombotic therapy is needed", "D": "Anticoagulation is needed only if he reaches age 65 or has a second transient ischemic attack"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Yes, a direct oral anticoagulant is advised since his prior transient ischemic attack qualifies him): Under CHADS-65, a prior stroke or transient ischemic attack qualifies a patient for oral anticoagulation at any age, and it carries a high recurrent stroke risk. Complete resolution of symptoms does not lower that risk. Aspirin is not adequate protection against cardioembolic stroke."}, {"id": 1639, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "CP is a 62 year old woman whose prescriptions are fully paid by a provincial drug benefit program. She asks why her pharmacy loyalty card earns points on her shampoo and vitamins but not on her prescriptions. What is the most accurate explanation the pharmacist can give?", "options": {"A": "Points are awarded only on drugs sold over the counter because prescriptions are legally not sales", "B": "Prescription transactions are excluded because pharmacy software cannot record purchases that involve a dispensing fee", "C": "Many provinces bar rewards on publicly funded prescriptions because points would act as rebates", "D": "The loyalty company declines points on any product that carries a drug identification number"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Many provinces bar rewards on publicly funded prescriptions because points would act as rebates): Public drug programs restrict inducements, and rewards on publicly funded prescriptions could operate as a rebate that influences where patients fill them. The exclusion is therefore a regulatory rule rather than a software limit. Over the counter products with a DIN can earn points, and prescriptions are sales that are simply subject to these restrictions."}, {"id": 1640, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "LM is a 70 year old man with gout, a recent myocardial infarction and prior allopurinol hypersensitivity (a severe rash a year ago). His urate is 540 umol/L and eGFR is 85 mL/min/1.73 m2. Which urate lowering recommendation is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Start febuxostat 40 mg daily, since it is preferred after allopurinol reactions even with heart disease", "B": "Use daily colchicine alone, since it lowers serum urate below target when taken long term", "C": "Consider probenecid with good hydration, since allopurinol must be avoided and febuxostat carries cardiovascular risk", "D": "Rechallenge allopurinol at full dose under observation, because severe rashes seldom recur with a second exposure"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Consider probenecid with good hydration, since allopurinol must be avoided and febuxostat carries cardiovascular risk): A prior severe cutaneous reaction means allopurinol should not be rechallenged. Febuxostat has a warning for increased cardiovascular death in patients with established cardiovascular disease. With adequate kidney function a uricosuric such as probenecid is a reasonable option."}, {"id": 1641, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 6, "question": "NB is a 45 year old man with Crohn disease about to begin adalimumab. His gastroenterologist has ordered a CBC and liver enzymes. Which additional baseline screening should the pharmacist confirm has been completed before the first injection is given at the clinic next week?", "options": {"A": "Pulmonary function testing and an ECG, because anti-TNF drugs commonly cause cardiopulmonary toxicity early", "B": "Tuberculosis screening and hepatitis B serology, because biologic therapy can reactivate latent infection", "C": "HLA-B27 genotyping and a bone density scan, because treatment response depends on both results", "D": "Thiopurine methyltransferase activity, because adalimumab is metabolized by that enzyme in the liver"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Tuberculosis screening and hepatitis B serology, because biologic therapy can reactivate latent infection): Anti-TNF drugs impair the immune control of latent tuberculosis and hepatitis B, so screening is done before the first dose. TPMT testing applies to azathioprine, not to adalimumab. The other tests are not part of routine pre-biologic screening."}, {"id": 1642, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "Researchers comparing two oral anticoagulants in a large insurance claims database use propensity score matching to analyze bleeding outcomes. A pharmacist reviewing the paper wants to explain the purpose of this method to a student. What does the technique accomplish?", "options": {"A": "It converts the observational comparison into a trial, so intention to treat analysis can be applied", "B": "It pairs patients with similar estimated probabilities of receiving the drug, using measured characteristics", "C": "It randomly assigns patients to the two anticoagulants after the study ends and data are analyzed", "D": "It removes all confounding, including unmeasured factors, by creating pairs of otherwise identical patients"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (It pairs patients with similar estimated probabilities of receiving the drug, using measured characteristics): A propensity score summarizes measured covariates into the probability of treatment. Matching on it creates groups with similar measured characteristics, mimicking some balance of randomization. It cannot account for unmeasured confounders, so residual confounding remains possible."}, {"id": 1643, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 6, "question": "PD, 49, has irregular cycles, frequent night-time hot flashes and an intact uterus, and her mother developed breast cancer at age 72. She asks whether menopausal hormone therapy is an option, given her wish for good sleep. Which statement best describes her situation?", "options": {"A": "Therapy is appropriate only after twelve full months without menses, so she must wait longer", "B": "Her age makes progestogen unnecessary, since her uterus is already shrinking during the menopausal transition", "C": "She has no contraindication, so low dose therapy is reasonable after discussing risks", "D": "Her mother's breast cancer is an absolute contraindication to any form of systemic estrogen therapy"}, "cluster_id": "CHS14", "cluster_text": "PD is a 49 year old woman with cycles that have been irregular for 18 months and frequent hot flashes that wake her several times nightly. She has an intact uterus, a BMI of 27, blood pressure of 118/74 mmHg, and does not smoke. She has no history of clots, stroke, or breast cancer, although her mother developed breast cancer at age 72. A mammogram last year was normal. She takes no regular medications and has no drug allergies.", "cluster_size": 3, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (She has no contraindication, so low dose therapy is reasonable after discussing risks): A family history of breast cancer in an older relative is not a contraindication, and PD has no personal history of clots, stroke, or breast cancer. At 49 with disruptive symptoms, therapy can be started at a low dose after a shared decision. Symptoms during the transition can be treated before twelve months, and her uterus still needs progestogen if she takes estrogen."}, {"id": 1644, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 6, "question": "The prescriber plans systemic therapy for PD to relieve her night sweats and hot flashes. The pharmacist is asked which regimen suits a woman with an intact uterus who prefers lower clot risk and has no contraindications. Which regimen should be recommended?", "options": {"A": "Oral estradiol alone, since a progestogen is needed only after a hysterectomy has been done", "B": "Transdermal estradiol alone, because patch delivery does not stimulate the endometrium in women with a uterus", "C": "Transdermal estradiol with oral micronized progesterone, protecting the endometrium while limiting clot risk", "D": "Oral conjugated estrogen with testosterone, because androgens protect the endometrium and relieve night sweats"}, "cluster_id": "CHS14", "cluster_text": "PD is a 49 year old woman with cycles that have been irregular for 18 months and frequent hot flashes that wake her several times nightly. She has an intact uterus, a BMI of 27, blood pressure of 118/74 mmHg, and does not smoke. She has no history of clots, stroke, or breast cancer, although her mother developed breast cancer at age 72. A mammogram last year was normal. She takes no regular medications and has no drug allergies.", "cluster_size": 3, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Transdermal estradiol with oral micronized progesterone, protecting the endometrium while limiting clot risk): Transdermal estradiol avoids first pass liver metabolism and carries a lower clot risk than oral estrogen. Because PD has a uterus, a progestogen such as micronized progesterone is needed to prevent endometrial hyperplasia, regardless of the estrogen route. Testosterone does not protect the endometrium."}, {"id": 1645, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 6, "question": "PD starts menopausal hormone therapy and asks how long she will need to take it. She wants to know the plan for stopping and for review of her symptoms and risks. Which counselling point should the pharmacist give about duration?", "options": {"A": "Stop after two weeks if relief is incomplete, since longer use of therapy is considered unsafe", "B": "Use the lowest effective dose for the shortest time, with yearly review", "C": "Continue for at least ten years, since stopping sooner raises her heart disease risk", "D": "Continue until age seventy five, since the dose cannot be changed after therapy begins"}, "cluster_id": "CHS14", "cluster_text": "PD is a 49 year old woman with cycles that have been irregular for 18 months and frequent hot flashes that wake her several times nightly. She has an intact uterus, a BMI of 27, blood pressure of 118/74 mmHg, and does not smoke. She has no history of clots, stroke, or breast cancer, although her mother developed breast cancer at age 72. A mammogram last year was normal. She takes no regular medications and has no drug allergies.", "cluster_size": 3, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Use the lowest effective dose for the shortest time, with yearly review): Menopausal hormone therapy is used at the lowest effective dose for as short a time as needed to control symptoms, with periodic reassessment of benefit and risk. There is no fixed ten year course and stopping does not raise heart risk. Doses can be adjusted, and relief often takes several weeks."}, {"id": 1646, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A very large trial of 50,000 patients reports that a new lipid drug reduced a composite event outcome with a hazard ratio of 0.98 (95% CI 0.96 to 0.99, p less than 0.001). A guideline committee asks whether this should change prescribing. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "The result is unreliable, because trials with over 10,000 participants cannot produce valid confidence intervals", "B": "The result shows no benefit, because the confidence interval does not extend below 0.90", "C": "Practice should change at once, because a p value below 0.001 shows a very large effect", "D": "Statistically significant, but a 2% relative reduction is too small to matter for most patients"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Statistically significant, but a 2% relative reduction is too small to matter for most patients): With enough participants, trivial differences become statistically significant. The effect size and interval show a relative reduction of only 1% to 4%, so clinical importance, cost and harms need to be weighed, not the p value alone."}, {"id": 1647, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 3, "question": "A 28 year old woman has had 2 days of sore throat, cough, runny nose and a temperature of 37.4 C. There is no tonsillar exudate and no neck node tenderness. She asks the pharmacist for amoxicillin because she did well with it last winter. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Recommend azithromycin for 3 days since it is convenient and covers atypical throat pathogens", "B": "Symptoms point to a viral cause, so recommend analgesics, fluids and lozenges, not antibiotics", "C": "Request a rapid strep test first and start amoxicillin while waiting for the result", "D": "Suggest amoxicillin for 5 days since her sore throat has already lasted two days"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Symptoms point to a viral cause, so recommend analgesics, fluids and lozenges, not antibiotics): Cough and rhinorrhea with no fever, exudate or tender nodes make a viral pharyngitis far more likely than group A strep. Antibiotics offer no benefit and bring side effects and resistance. Testing is for patients with higher scores, and starting a drug before results defeats the purpose."}, {"id": 1648, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "An employer instructs pharmacist CU to recommend a high-margin herbal supplement to every patient on warfarin. CU knows it may raise bleeding risk. A patient, 63 year old FP, on warfarin asks about it. What should CU do at the counter this afternoon?", "options": {"A": "Recommend it as instructed and suggest FP tell the prescriber, since the employer sets sales policy", "B": "Say little about it and let FP read the label, since no interaction is proven", "C": "Sell the supplement but record a note that FP was told the employer recommended it", "D": "Warn FP about the bleeding risk and advise against it, putting patient safety first"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Warn FP about the bleeding risk and advise against it, putting patient safety first): The pharmacist's first duty is to the patient, and a supplement that may raise bleeding risk with warfarin should not be promoted. Warning FP and advising against it puts safety ahead of an employer's sales instruction. Staying quiet or selling it with a note does not protect FP."}, {"id": 1649, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 3, "question": "RB, a 35 year old man, received benzathine penicillin G for secondary syphilis this morning. Six hours later he calls the pharmacy with fever, chills, headache and a worsened rash, but no breathing difficulty or swelling of the lips or throat. What is the most likely explanation?", "options": {"A": "An anaphylactic reaction to penicillin, requiring epinephrine and emergency transport right away", "B": "Treatment failure of the injection, requiring a second injection given within the next 24 hours", "C": "A Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction, expected within 24 hours and managed with acetaminophen and fluids", "D": "A drug fever from penicillin, requiring a switch to doxycycline for the rest of therapy"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction, expected within 24 hours and managed with acetaminophen and fluids): Rapid killing of spirochetes releases inflammatory products that cause fever, chills and rash flare within hours, called the Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction. It settles in about a day and is treated symptomatically. The absence of airway or circulatory signs argues against anaphylaxis, and treatment failure cannot be judged this early."}, {"id": 1650, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 7, "question": "PK is a 60 year old man and long-term smoker who asks for a cough syrup. He has had a cough for five weeks, and this week noticed blood streaks in his sputum and has lost weight. Which action is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Recommend dextromethorphan syrup and a humidifier, with a return visit if the cough lasts a week", "B": "Refer promptly for medical assessment, since a persistent cough with blood needs investigation", "C": "Recommend guaifenesin to loosen the sputum and suggest he stop smoking, then review in two weeks", "D": "Suggest an inhaled bronchodilator from the shelf, since smokers' cough usually causes streaked sputum"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Refer promptly for medical assessment, since a persistent cough with blood needs investigation): A cough lasting more than three weeks with hemoptysis and weight loss in a smoker is a red flag for lung cancer or serious infection. He needs prompt imaging and assessment. Cough suppressants and expectorants would only delay the work up, and smokers' cough does not usually cause bloody sputum."}, {"id": 1651, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "LC, a staff pharmacist, is told by head office to release refill prescriptions without pharmacist review during busy periods to keep wait times short. LC believes this conflicts with the college's standards of practice. If a patient is harmed, LC's responsibility to the college would be:", "options": {"A": "Absent, since the college regulates only the pharmacy as a business and not individual employee pharmacists", "B": "Personal, because following an employer directive does not remove a pharmacist's accountability for meeting practice standards", "C": "Limited to the pharmacy manager, because managers set workflow policies that staff must follow", "D": "Shared with head office, since a written corporate policy transfers part of the regulatory responsibility"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Personal, because following an employer directive does not remove a pharmacist's accountability for meeting practice standards): Registrants are individually accountable to their regulator for meeting standards, and an employer's instruction is not a defence. LC should raise the concern and refuse to follow a policy that breaches the standards."}, {"id": 1652, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 1, "question": "TR is a 48 year old man with a mid-arm circumference of 41 cm attending a pharmacy blood pressure clinic. Only a standard adult cuff, designed for arms up to 33 cm, is available, and it reads 152/94 mmHg. The pharmacist considers how to interpret and respond to this reading. What should be done?", "options": {"A": "Remeasure with a large adult cuff, because an undersized cuff falsely raises the reading", "B": "Record 152/94 mmHg as accurate, because cuff size affects readings only in very thin arms", "C": "Place the standard cuff over the forearm instead, since forearm readings match upper arm values", "D": "Subtract 10 mmHg from each value to adjust for the cuff, then record the corrected result"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Remeasure with a large adult cuff, because an undersized cuff falsely raises the reading): A cuff that is too small for the arm circumference overestimates blood pressure, so a large adult cuff should be used for an arm of this size. Subtracting a guessed correction is not valid, and forearm values differ from upper arm values. The repeat reading should use the correct cuff before any conclusion is drawn."}, {"id": 1653, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A 45 year old man with a kidney infection is receiving gentamicin 7 mg/kg once daily by the hospital's extended interval protocol. The pharmacist wants to individualize the dosing interval using a nomogram. The best time to draw the single serum sample, relative to the dose, is:", "options": {"A": "After three days of therapy, once steady state has been reached after five half-lives", "B": "Thirty minutes after the end of the infusion, to capture the true peak concentration", "C": "Immediately before the second dose, to measure the trough that reflects the full dosing interval", "D": "Between 6 and 14 hours after the start of the first infusion"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Between 6 and 14 hours after the start of the first infusion): Extended interval nomograms such as the Hartford nomogram plot a single level taken 6 to 14 hours after the start of the infusion to select the dosing interval. A peak or trough sample does not fit the nomogram, and waiting days delays dose individualization for an aminoglycoside that should be adjusted early."}, {"id": 1654, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "A Canadian resident returns from a long stay in Spain with a prescription drug that is not sold in Canada, in its original container. At the community pharmacy she asks how much medication Health Canada allows an individual to bring across the border for personal use. What should the pharmacist answer?", "options": {"A": "Up to one full year of therapy if a copy of the foreign prescription accompanies it", "B": "A supply of up to thirty days, and only when a Canadian prescriber approved the trip", "C": "A supply of up to one hundred eighty days if the foreign prescriber provides a letter", "D": "A supply of no more than ninety days, in original packaging, for personal use"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A supply of no more than ninety days, in original packaging, for personal use): Health Canada allows individuals to import a prescription drug for personal use up to a ninety day supply, in its original packaging. Larger quantities or later supply must come through authorized Canadian channels. The other limits are not part of the personal importation policy."}, {"id": 1655, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "ND is a 39 year old man who has taken clozapine 350 mg daily for two years. He asks for something for constipation, saying he has passed little stool for five days and now has a swollen, painful abdomen with vomiting. What should the pharmacist advise?", "options": {"A": "Same-day emergency assessment, because clozapine can cause bowel obstruction that needs urgent medical treatment", "B": "Use a bulk-forming fibre supplement twice daily and hold clozapine until the next bowel movement occurs", "C": "Use a glycerin suppository now, since clozapine constipation is minor and responds to simple measures", "D": "Take a stimulant laxative such as bisacodyl tonight and increase fluids, then reassess in two days"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Same-day emergency assessment, because clozapine can cause bowel obstruction that needs urgent medical treatment): Clozapine slows gut motility and can cause severe constipation progressing to ileus or obstruction, which can be fatal. Five days without stool with distension, pain, and vomiting are red flags requiring emergency assessment, not self-treatment. Laxatives could be dangerous if there is an obstruction, and holding clozapine abruptly adds risk."}, {"id": 1656, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "In the private immunization room, EH is about to give an intramuscular vaccine to LM, an anxious 19 year old patient wearing a fitted sweater. EH needs to expose the deltoid. EH is a pharmacist authorized to inject. Which approach best maintains professional boundaries?", "options": {"A": "Pull up the sleeve quickly to save time, since vaccination is a routine procedure patients expect", "B": "Explain the need, ask permission, let LM expose the arm, and offer a chaperone", "C": "Ask LM to remove the sweater and wait in a gown until the vaccine is ready", "D": "Give the vaccine through the sweater fabric to avoid any exposure or discomfort for LM"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Explain the need, ask permission, let LM expose the arm, and offer a chaperone): Respect for the patient's body and dignity means explaining the need, asking permission, letting LM expose the arm herself, and offering a chaperone. Pulling up the sleeve without asking, requiring a gown, or injecting through clothing is either intrusive or unsafe. A correct injection requires proper exposure of the site."}, {"id": 1657, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "BF is a 63 year old man who finished a hydromorphone taper last month and still has 40 tablets of 2 mg at home. His wife asks the pharmacist how to dispose of them safely. What is the best advice?", "options": {"A": "Place the tablets loose in the garbage, since they cannot be retrieved after trash collection", "B": "Keep the tablets for future flares of pain, stored in an unlocked bathroom cabinet", "C": "Flush the tablets down the toilet, since this prevents anyone else from ever finding them", "D": "Return unused tablets to a pharmacy for safe destruction, not flushing or discarding them"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Return unused tablets to a pharmacy for safe destruction, not flushing or discarding them): Returning unused medication to a pharmacy ensures it is destroyed safely and prevents diversion, accidental ingestion by children, and environmental harm. Flushing contaminates water systems, and household trash can be searched. Keeping leftover opioids for later pain invites unsupervised use and theft."}, {"id": 1658, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "QM, 71, can name his warfarin as a blood thinner but insists he cannot bleed because he feels strong and has never had a bruise. Which element of decision making capacity is most in question for QM when he refuses regular INR testing and dose adjustments?", "options": {"A": "The ability to communicate a choice, since he states a clear preference about the testing", "B": "The ability to appreciate how the information applies to him and likely outcomes", "C": "The ability to recall the information, which he showed by naming the drug and its class", "D": "The ability to perform the test himself, which depends on his dexterity and eyesight"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The ability to appreciate how the information applies to him and likely outcomes): Capacity requires both understanding the facts and appreciating how they apply to oneself. QM remembers the facts and states a clear preference, but he denies that bleeding applies to him, which is a failure of appreciation."}, {"id": 1659, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 7, "question": "SN is an 85 year old frail woman who receives the same loading dose of a water soluble drug as a younger adult. Her plasma concentration after the dose is higher than predicted. A pharmacist considers age related changes in drug distribution. Which change best explains the higher concentration?", "options": {"A": "A larger volume of distribution, since body fat rises and holds water soluble drugs", "B": "A smaller volume of distribution, since lean mass and total body water fall with age", "C": "Higher serum albumin, which binds more drug in the plasma and prevents distribution into the tissues", "D": "Faster hepatic blood flow, which reduces the amount of drug reaching tissues after a single dose"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A smaller volume of distribution, since lean mass and total body water fall with age): Total body water and lean mass decline with age, so water soluble drugs distribute into a smaller space and reach higher plasma concentrations from the same loading dose. Body fat rises with age, which affects lipophilic drugs instead. Serum albumin tends to fall, not rise, and hepatic blood flow decreases with age."}, {"id": 1660, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "LS is the father of a 4 year old girl with a cough and runny nose. He asks a pharmacy assistant for a Schedule II cough and cold syrup to give her tonight. The pharmacist notes that the product label includes a dosing table for children aged two to five. What should the pharmacist do?", "options": {"A": "Advise against use under age six because risks outweigh benefits, and suggest supportive care", "B": "Allow the sale if LS agrees to give only one dose and to watch for drowsiness", "C": "Suggest alternating the syrup with a second nonprescription cough product to keep each dose lower", "D": "Recommend the syrup at the lowest listed dose for the girl's weight, using a measured syringe"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Advise against use under age six because risks outweigh benefits, and suggest supportive care): Health Canada advises that nonprescription cough and cold products should not be used in children under six because the risks outweigh the benefits. The pharmacist should recommend supportive care such as fluids, honey for children over one, saline and rest. A dosing table on an older label does not override that advice."}, {"id": 1661, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 6, "question": "TG is a 50 year old woman who has taken mesalamine 4.8 g daily for ulcerative colitis for three years. She has no complaints and her symptoms are controlled. She asks whether any routine laboratory checks are needed while she continues this drug. Which monitoring should the pharmacist suggest?", "options": {"A": "Periodic serum creatinine and urinalysis, because 5-ASA products can rarely cause interstitial nephritis", "B": "An annual eye examination, because mesalamine commonly causes retinal toxicity over time", "C": "Regular INR measurement, because mesalamine raises bleeding risk through vitamin K antagonism", "D": "Yearly bone marrow assessment, because mesalamine suppresses blood cell production at higher doses"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Periodic serum creatinine and urinalysis, because 5-ASA products can rarely cause interstitial nephritis): Mesalamine is associated with rare interstitial nephritis, so renal function and urinalysis are checked before treatment and periodically afterward. It does not interact with vitamin K, harm the retina, or routinely suppress the marrow. Early detection of kidney injury allows the drug to be stopped before damage progresses."}, {"id": 1662, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "DM, a 49 year old man, checks his refrigerator with a thermometer that reads only in degrees Fahrenheit, and it shows 46 degrees. His insulin must stay between 2 and 8 degrees Celsius. The pharmacist converts the reading using the formula (F minus 32) x 5/9. What does the pharmacist tell him?", "options": {"A": "About 25.2 degrees Celsius, which is far above the acceptable range for his insulin", "B": "About 25.6 degrees Celsius, which is far above the acceptable range for his insulin", "C": "About 14.0 degrees Celsius, which is above the acceptable range for his insulin", "D": "About 7.8 degrees Celsius, which is within the acceptable range for his insulin"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (About 7.8 degrees Celsius, which is within the acceptable range for his insulin): Subtracting 32 from 46 gives 14, and 14 x 5/9 gives 7.8 degrees Celsius, which is inside the 2 to 8 degree range. Skipping the multiplication gives 14, and multiplying by 5/9 without subtracting gives 25.6, both unsafe looking but wrong. The value is close to the top of the range, so he should be advised to keep the fridge at a slightly colder setting."}, {"id": 1663, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "A hospital patient weighing 80 kg with a pulmonary embolism is started on a heparin infusion at 18 units/kg/hour after the initial bolus. The bag contains 25,000 units in 250 mL (100 units/mL). At what rate in mL/hour should the pump be set?", "options": {"A": "18 mL/hour, which delivers 1800 units per hour for an 80 kg patient", "B": "1.44 mL/hour, which delivers 144 units per hour for an 80 kg patient", "C": "144 mL/hour, which delivers 14,400 units per hour for an 80 kg patient", "D": "14.4 mL/hour, which delivers 1440 units per hour for an 80 kg patient"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (14.4 mL/hour, which delivers 1440 units per hour for an 80 kg patient): The dose is 18 units/kg/hour x 80 kg = 1440 units per hour. At 100 units/mL, 1440 / 100 = 14.4 mL/hour. The other rates are ten times too high, ten times too low or use the per kilogram figure as the rate directly, and a tenfold heparin error is dangerous."}, {"id": 1664, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "A 28 year old woman with Graves disease has palpitations and a heart rate of 108 beats per minute, and takes propranolol 20 mg four times daily with no asthma. She asks how long she will need the propranolol. What is the best answer?", "options": {"A": "Continue until the antithyroid drug works, usually several weeks, then taper it off", "B": "Stop within three days, since the symptoms come from anxiety rather than thyroid hormone", "C": "Continue only until her next period, because it blocks hormone production by the thyroid gland", "D": "Continue for life, because the beta-blocker treats the thyroid disease and cannot be stopped safely"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Continue until the antithyroid drug works, usually several weeks, then taper it off): Beta-blockers relieve adrenergic symptoms such as tremor and rapid pulse but do not treat the disease. They bridge the weeks until methimazole lowers thyroid hormone levels, after which they are tapered. Stopping too early leaves her symptomatic, while lifelong use is unnecessary."}, {"id": 1665, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "The marketing coordinator of a pharmacy chain plans to send a promotional text about its flu shot clinic to every patient in the dispensing database, including patients who gave their number only for prescription-ready alerts and were told it would not be used for marketing. The pharmacist-manager is asked to approve. What is the correct response?", "options": {"A": "Limit it to patients who agreed to marketing, since other numbers were given for care", "B": "Approve it, since every patient has an ongoing relationship and health promotion is part of care", "C": "Approve it for patients over 65 only, since seniors face the highest influenza risk", "D": "Approve it if the text names the pharmacy and offers an unsubscribe link for every recipient"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Limit it to patients who agreed to marketing, since other numbers were given for care): Contact details collected for prescription alerts, with an assurance they would not be used for marketing, cannot be repurposed for promotion without consent under privacy law. Including an unsubscribe link or restricting by age does not create consent that was never given. Messages should go only to patients who agreed to receive marketing."}, {"id": 1666, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 1, "question": "SJ is a 77 year old man who takes ramipril 5 mg daily and potassium chloride 20 mmol daily prescribed years ago for a loop diuretic that was stopped. His cardiologist now adds spironolactone 25 mg daily for heart failure. What should the pharmacist recommend?", "options": {"A": "double the potassium chloride dose, since spironolactone lowers potassium by increasing urinary loss", "B": "continue all three drugs and recheck potassium in six months, since spironolactone is potassium neutral", "C": "stop the potassium chloride supplement and check potassium and creatinine within about one week", "D": "stop ramipril and keep the other two, since ACE inhibitors reduce potassium in heart failure"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (stop the potassium chloride supplement and check potassium and creatinine within about one week): Spironolactone and an ACE inhibitor both raise potassium, and a supplement on top creates a serious hyperkalemia risk. The supplement is stopped and potassium and creatinine are measured within a week of the change."}, {"id": 1667, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "A mother asks for a printout of the medications her 16 year old daughter has filled. The daughter is a capable patient who has asked the pharmacist to keep her prescriptions confidential from her parents. What should the pharmacist do?", "options": {"A": "Give the printout but remove the oral contraceptive entry, since only that drug is sensitive", "B": "Decline without the daughter's consent, since a capable minor controls her own health information", "C": "Give the printout once the mother shows identification proving that she is the daughter's legal parent", "D": "Give the printout, because parents are entitled to the records of any child under eighteen"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Decline without the daughter's consent, since a capable minor controls her own health information): A capable adolescent holds the right to consent to disclosure of her own health information, and parental status alone does not override it. Removing one entry would still disclose information and misrepresent the record."}, {"id": 1668, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A trial reports that a new inhaler reduced asthma exacerbations compared with standard therapy, with a rate ratio of 0.85 and a 95% confidence interval of 0.70 to 1.04. A respiratory therapist asks a pharmacist whether the inhaler has been shown to be superior. Which statement is best?", "options": {"A": "Superiority was demonstrated because the point estimate of 0.85 shows a fifteen percent reduction in exacerbations", "B": "Superiority was demonstrated because most of the confidence interval lies below 1.0 in a large trial", "C": "The inhaler is proven equivalent because the interval includes 1.0, so the treatments do not differ", "D": "Superiority was not demonstrated because the interval includes 1.0, although some benefit remains possible"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Superiority was not demonstrated because the interval includes 1.0, although some benefit remains possible): Superiority requires the confidence interval to exclude no effect, which for a rate ratio means the upper limit must be below 1.0. Here the limit is 1.04, so a chance finding cannot be excluded. Failing to show superiority does not prove the two inhalers are equivalent."}, {"id": 1669, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "ZM is a 72 year old man with severe renal impairment who needs a drug that is eliminated mainly by the kidneys and has a normal volume of distribution. The prescriber asks how renal impairment should affect the loading dose compared with the maintenance dose. What is the best advice?", "options": {"A": "Increase the loading dose, since impaired kidneys cause lower early concentrations of the drug", "B": "Keep the usual loading dose, since it depends on volume of distribution, and reduce maintenance", "C": "Reduce both doses in proportion to the creatinine clearance, because clearance changes both of them", "D": "Reduce the loading dose only and keep the usual maintenance dose to avoid delay in effect"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Keep the usual loading dose, since it depends on volume of distribution, and reduce maintenance): The loading dose is determined by volume of distribution and target concentration, which are unchanged here. Maintenance dosing depends on clearance, which is reduced, so it is the maintenance dose or interval that must be adjusted."}, {"id": 1670, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 4, "question": "QR is a 22 year old man who has used salbutamol 100 mcg about five times a week for three months, wakes with wheezing twice weekly, and has no controller medication. He asks for a refill of his reliever inhaler. The pharmacist should:", "options": {"A": "provide the refill and reassure him that this reliever use is normal for mild asthma", "B": "decline the refill until he has seen his prescriber, since reliever overuse is dangerous", "C": "provide the refill and recommend he see his prescriber about starting a daily inhaled corticosteroid", "D": "provide the refill and suggest a higher number of puffs before exercise and at night"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (provide the refill and recommend he see his prescriber about starting a daily inhaled corticosteroid): Reliever use more than twice weekly and night symptoms indicate uncontrolled asthma that needs a daily inhaled corticosteroid. He should still receive the reliever, since he needs rescue therapy now. Increasing puffs does not treat the inflammation, and reassurance misses the opportunity to improve control."}, {"id": 1671, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "VB is a 31 year old woman taking risperidone 4 mg daily for schizophrenia for five months. She tells the pharmacist that her periods stopped three months ago and she has milk-like nipple discharge. Her pregnancy test was negative. What should the pharmacist suggest?", "options": {"A": "Advise her to stop risperidone for one month to see whether her periods and discharge return", "B": "Recommend a repeat pregnancy test every month, since these findings usually signal an early pregnancy", "C": "Report probable raised prolactin to the prescriber and ask about switching to aripiprazole", "D": "Start a combined oral contraceptive to restore regular periods and suppress the nipple discharge she describes"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Report probable raised prolactin to the prescriber and ask about switching to aripiprazole): Risperidone blocks dopamine in the pituitary pathway that normally suppresses prolactin, so amenorrhea and galactorrhea are common. The prescriber can consider switching to a prolactin-sparing agent such as aripiprazole. Stopping on her own risks relapse, and contraceptives do not address the cause."}, {"id": 1672, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "JF is a 16 year old girl with type 1 diabetes on glargine at bedtime and lispro with meals. She tells the pharmacist she skips her glargine when she feels too sick to eat, believing she needs no insulin without food. What should the pharmacist explain?", "options": {"A": "Basal insulin is needed even without food, and skipping it risks ketoacidosis during illness", "B": "Basal insulin is optional during illness, as long as she takes lispro with any food eaten", "C": "Basal insulin can be halved on sick days, because less food intake lowers her insulin need", "D": "Basal insulin can be skipped if glucose is under 10 mmol/L, which prevents lows"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Basal insulin is needed even without food, and skipping it risks ketoacidosis during illness): Basal insulin controls glucose production by the liver and ketone formation, so it is required even when she is not eating, and illness often raises insulin needs. Omitting or halving it can lead to ketoacidosis within hours. A glucose below 10 mmol/L does not remove the need for basal insulin."}, {"id": 1673, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 6, "question": "QZ is a 56 year old man whose endoscopy shows a 1.5 cm gastric ulcer. Biopsies are negative for Helicobacter pylori and he takes no NSAIDs. His physician prescribes pantoprazole 40 mg daily. For how long should the pharmacist expect standard acid suppression to be given to heal this ulcer?", "options": {"A": "Four weeks, which is the standard duration for both duodenal and gastric ulcers", "B": "Two weeks, since PPIs heal gastric ulcers rapidly and longer therapy adds only risk", "C": "About eight weeks, with endoscopy to confirm healing of the gastric ulcer", "D": "Six months, since gastric ulcers recur quickly unless acid suppression continues for half a year"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (About eight weeks, with endoscopy to confirm healing of the gastric ulcer): Duodenal ulcers usually heal with about four weeks of PPI therapy, whereas gastric ulcers need about eight weeks and repeat endoscopy to exclude malignancy. Two weeks is inadequate for healing. Six months is not routine for an uncomplicated ulcer without ongoing risk factors."}, {"id": 1674, "domain": "communication_and_collaboration", "chapter": 11, "question": "CN is a 59 year old man with limited literacy who is being discharged on eight new medications including warfarin and insulin. The pharmacist has only ten minutes before his ride arrives. How should the counselling be prioritized in the time available?", "options": {"A": "Give a printed list of all eight medications and ask him to call with questions", "B": "Review all eight medications in the order listed on the discharge summary, one at a time", "C": "Focus first on the highest risk medications and a few key messages, then follow up", "D": "Teach the lowest risk medications first to build confidence and finish with warfarin"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Focus first on the highest risk medications and a few key messages, then follow up): With limited time, the pharmacist teaches the high-alert drugs such as warfarin and insulin first, limits the number of messages and arranges a follow-up. Going down the list risks running out of time before the dangerous drugs are covered."}, {"id": 1675, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "A pharmacy technician in the hospital sterile compounding room is asked to prepare 1000 mL of dextrose 10% for the neonatal unit from dextrose 50% injection and sterile water. The pharmacist checks the calculation before compounding begins. How much dextrose 50% is required?", "options": {"A": "500 mL of dextrose 50% plus sterile water to make 1000 mL", "B": "200 mL of dextrose 50% plus sterile water to make 1000 mL", "C": "100 mL of dextrose 50% plus sterile water to make 1000 mL", "D": "250 mL of dextrose 50% plus sterile water to make 1000 mL"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (200 mL of dextrose 50% plus sterile water to make 1000 mL): Using C1V1 = C2V2, 10% x 1000 mL divided by 50% gives 200 mL of stock. The remainder, 800 mL, is sterile water. Using 250 mL would give 12.5%, and 500 mL would give 25%, both hyperosmolar for a neonate."}, {"id": 1676, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 3, "question": "TS is a 72 year old man with confirmed C. difficile infection. He now has blood pressure of 80/50 mmHg, a distended abdomen with absent bowel sounds, and a white blood cell count of 28 x 10^9/L. The team asks the pharmacist to recommend therapy for this fulminant infection.", "options": {"A": "Oral metronidazole 500 mg three times daily alone, with intravenous fluid support only", "B": "Vancomycin 500 mg orally four times daily plus intravenous metronidazole 500 mg every 8 hours", "C": "Intravenous vancomycin 500 mg every 6 hours alone, because it reaches the colon lumen", "D": "Vancomycin 125 mg orally four times daily alone, as for a typical non-severe episode in adults"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Vancomycin 500 mg orally four times daily plus intravenous metronidazole 500 mg every 8 hours): Hypotension and ileus define fulminant disease, which needs a higher oral vancomycin dose and intravenous metronidazole to reach the colon from the bloodstream when the gut is not moving. The standard dose and metronidazole alone are too weak. Intravenous vancomycin does not enter the colon lumen."}, {"id": 1677, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "A pharmacy that plans to give vaccines at its counter has a household bar refrigerator in the back room and a purpose built pharmacy grade refrigerator on order. The assistant asks if vaccines can be stored in the bar refrigerator until the new one arrives. What should the pharmacist say?", "options": {"A": "Store them in the bar refrigerator, but place a thermometer on top of the vaccine boxes", "B": "Store them in the bar refrigerator, because its small size keeps the internal temperature stable", "C": "Store them in the bar refrigerator beside a frozen gel pack to hold the temperature", "D": "Store them only in a purpose built or pharmacy grade refrigerator holding 2 to 8 degrees"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Store them only in a purpose built or pharmacy grade refrigerator holding 2 to 8 degrees): Household and bar refrigerators cycle widely in temperature and often freeze items near the cooling element, so they are not recommended for vaccine storage. Purpose built or pharmacy grade units hold the 2 to 8 degree range more reliably. Placing a frozen pack near the vaccine increases the chance of freezing damage."}, {"id": 1678, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "A 50 year old man with hypertension controlled on amlodipine 5 mg daily is starting venlafaxine 150 mg daily for generalized anxiety disorder. He asks whether anything needs to be monitored once he starts the new drug. What should the pharmacist advise?", "options": {"A": "No monitoring is needed, since SNRIs have no effect on the cardiovascular system at any dose", "B": "Blood pressure will fall on venlafaxine, so his amlodipine dose can usually be cut within weeks", "C": "Blood pressure should be checked regularly, since venlafaxine can raise it in a dose-related way", "D": "Only his thyroid function needs monitoring, since SNRIs commonly cause changes in thyroid hormone production"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Blood pressure should be checked regularly, since venlafaxine can raise it in a dose-related way): Venlafaxine inhibits norepinephrine reuptake and can increase blood pressure, particularly at higher doses, so periodic measurement is advised, more so in patients who already have hypertension. The drug does not lower blood pressure, and thyroid testing is not routinely needed."}, {"id": 1679, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 6, "question": "SK is a 29 year old woman at 28 weeks gestation with heartburn after meals several times per week. She has already raised the head of her bed and eats smaller meals. She takes only a prenatal vitamin. Which pharmacotherapy should the pharmacist recommend as the first choice?", "options": {"A": "Omeprazole 40 mg twice daily started immediately, since PPIs are preferred over antacids in pregnancy", "B": "Sodium bicarbonate tablets dissolved in water after each meal, since they relieve symptoms fastest", "C": "Misoprostol 200 mcg after meals, since it protects the stomach lining without affecting the pregnancy", "D": "Calcium carbonate or an alginate antacid taken after meals and at bedtime as needed"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Calcium carbonate or an alginate antacid taken after meals and at bedtime as needed): In pregnancy, heartburn is managed stepwise, with antacids or alginates first, then an H2 receptor antagonist, and a PPI only if symptoms persist. Sodium bicarbonate adds a sodium and fluid load, and misoprostol is contraindicated because it can cause uterine contractions and miscarriage."}, {"id": 1680, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "A customer holds two products from the natural health aisle: a vitamin D supplement showing an NPN and a homeopathic remedy showing a DIN-HM. She asks the pharmacist what these numbers mean. Which explanation about the numbers on natural health product labels is accurate?", "options": {"A": "Each number shows Health Canada licensed the product after assessing safety, quality and evidence", "B": "Both numbers are assigned by the manufacturer for inventory tracking and carry no regulatory approval", "C": "An NPN means efficacy was proven in clinical trials, while a DIN-HM means no review occurred", "D": "The numbers show that provincial colleges approved each product for sale only inside licensed pharmacies"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Each number shows Health Canada licensed the product after assessing safety, quality and evidence): Natural health products receive a Natural Product Number and homeopathic medicines a DIN-HM only after Health Canada licenses them, which involves review of safety, quality and the evidence supporting the stated use. The numbers are not manufacturer codes, and licensing is a federal decision rather than a provincial college one."}, {"id": 1681, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "SG is a 45 year old woman with trigeminal neuralgia whose oxcarbazepine was increased from 600 to 1200 mg daily two weeks ago. She now has headache and nausea with sodium 126 mmol/L and normal volume status. What should the pharmacist suggest?", "options": {"A": "Give a high salt diet and continue the same dose, since sodium wasting explains the level", "B": "Add a thiazide diuretic to remove excess fluid, because diuretics correct euvolemic hyponatremia from drug effects", "C": "Reduce or change the oxcarbazepine and restrict fluids, since the drug causes water retention through SIADH", "D": "Increase water intake to prevent dehydration from the antiepileptic drug and recheck sodium next month"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Reduce or change the oxcarbazepine and restrict fluids, since the drug causes water retention through SIADH): Oxcarbazepine and carbamazepine enhance renal water reabsorption, causing euvolemic SIADH related hyponatremia that is dose related. Management is dose reduction or substitution and fluid restriction. Thiazides and extra water would worsen the hyponatremia."}, {"id": 1682, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 7, "question": "A pharmacist assesses a patient for a minor ailment but decides not to prescribe because the symptoms fall outside protocol. Which action best fits professional expectations in most provinces? The patient is frustrated and wants a prescription from the pharmacy today.", "options": {"A": "Dispense a nonprescription product and avoid documenting, since no prescription was issued", "B": "Issue a prescription anyway, noting scope limits only in the pharmacy's internal log", "C": "Tell the patient to return when symptoms worsen, with no referral and no record needed", "D": "Explain the reason, refer to another provider, and document the decision and referral"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Explain the reason, refer to another provider, and document the decision and referral): When a patient is outside scope or protocol, the pharmacist should explain the reasoning, make or facilitate a referral, and record the assessment and outcome. Prescribing outside scope breaches regulatory standards, and not recording the encounter weakens continuity and accountability."}, {"id": 1683, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 3, "question": "A 38 year old healthy nonsmoker has had a cough with yellow sputum for 6 days. He has no fever, normal breathing and normal lung sounds on examination. He asks for an antibiotic. What should the pharmacist explain about antibiotics for his acute bronchitis?", "options": {"A": "Antibiotics do not shorten the cough from acute bronchitis in healthy adults and add risk", "B": "Antibiotics are advised whenever the cough produces coloured sputum for longer than 3 days", "C": "Antibiotics prevent pneumonia in healthy adults and should be given when a cough lasts 5 days", "D": "Antibiotics shorten the cough by about one week, so a short course is usually reasonable"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Antibiotics do not shorten the cough from acute bronchitis in healthy adults and add risk): Acute bronchitis is almost always viral, and trials show antibiotics do not meaningfully shorten cough in healthy adults while causing adverse effects. Sputum colour reflects inflammatory cells, not bacterial infection. Routine treatment does not prevent pneumonia."}, {"id": 1684, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "A community pharmacist renews a patient's expired prescription for rosuvastatin 10 mg to maintain continuity of care for PL, a 58 year old woman. The prescriber's office is closed until Monday and the patient has no tablets left at home. Which record-keeping step is essential after the renewal?", "options": {"A": "Record only the quantity dispensed, because the rationale is kept in the prescriber's own chart", "B": "Send the patient to the prescriber to sign the renewed prescription before it is dispensed", "C": "Document the assessment and rationale, and notify the original prescriber of the renewal promptly", "D": "Keep the record private, since the prescriber is told only when a complaint is made"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Document the assessment and rationale, and notify the original prescriber of the renewal promptly): A pharmacist who renews a prescription is accountable for the decision and must document the assessment and rationale. The original prescriber is notified so the chart stays current. Leaving the prescriber uninformed undermines continuity of care."}, {"id": 1685, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "A new pharmacy assistant, working a busy Saturday shift, asks why every prescription claim submitted to a provincial drug plan needs the product's DIN entered correctly before the claim is accepted. What is the best explanation of what the drug identification number tells the plan?", "options": {"A": "It identifies the specific product and manufacturer authorized for sale in Canada, so pricing is correct", "B": "It shows the plan the patient's coverage level and deductible for that particular product", "C": "It certifies that the plan has approved the product for coverage on its formulary list", "D": "It identifies the prescriber who wrote the order so the plan can verify their licence status"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (It identifies the specific product and manufacturer authorized for sale in Canada, so pricing is correct): A drug identification number is assigned by Health Canada to a specific drug product from a specific manufacturer, including strength and form, so the plan can price and track it. It says nothing about the prescriber or the patient's coverage, and having a DIN does not guarantee that a plan lists the product."}, {"id": 1686, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "Three days after dispensing, a pharmacist discovers she gave a patient metformin 1000 mg tablets instead of the prescribed 500 mg. The patient, BA, is a 68 year old man whose creatinine clearance is 55 mL/min and who has reported mild dizziness. What should the pharmacist do first?", "options": {"A": "Contact the prescriber only, and leave the decision about telling BA to the prescriber's judgment", "B": "Wait for BA's next refill to replace the tablets, and record the correction in the file", "C": "Contact BA to assess for harm, disclose the error, and report it through incident reporting", "D": "Ask BA to return the bottle and record the event only if he reports symptoms"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Contact BA to assess for harm, disclose the error, and report it through incident reporting): Professional standards require prompt disclosure of a dispensing error to the patient, an assessment of any harm, correction of the supply and documentation through the pharmacy's incident reporting system. Hiding the event or delaying until a refill leaves a symptomatic patient at risk."}, {"id": 1687, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "After a six hour online workshop on diabetes, a pharmacist wants her new storefront sign and website to read 'Certified Diabetes Specialist Pharmacist.' She holds no formal recognized specialty designation. The pharmacy owner supports the idea. What is the best advice?", "options": {"A": "Use the title, since it reflects extra education and the owner controls the pharmacy's signage", "B": "State the training accurately, such as workshop completion, without implying a recognized specialty", "C": "Use the shorter title 'Diabetes Specialist' without the word certified, because that avoids implying any examination", "D": "Use the title but add small print noting that she holds no formal specialty designation yet"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (State the training accurately, such as workshop completion, without implying a recognized specialty): Advertising of credentials must be accurate and not suggest a recognized specialty or certification that the pharmacist does not hold. Describing the workshop truthfully informs the public without misleading them. Small print does not correct a headline claim, and dropping one word still implies a specialist status that she has not earned."}, {"id": 1688, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 4, "question": "DL, the mother of a 3 year old with asthma, asks how to clean her child's plastic valved holding chamber. She usually wipes the inside with a dry cloth after each use and has noticed the medication seems to cling to the walls. The pharmacist should advise cleaning it with:", "options": {"A": "warm water and mild dish detergent, then air drying without rubbing, to limit static", "B": "boiling water for five minutes weekly, which sterilizes the plastic and prevents static buildup", "C": "hot soapy water, rinsing well and drying the inside with a clean towel afterward", "D": "an alcohol wipe on the inside surface weekly, followed by wiping dry with a cloth"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (warm water and mild dish detergent, then air drying without rubbing, to limit static): Detergent washing leaves a thin coating that reduces static charge, which otherwise attracts drug particles to the plastic. Rubbing or towel drying recreates static, so the chamber is left to drip dry. Alcohol and boiling water can damage or deform the plastic and do not address static."}, {"id": 1689, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 1, "question": "JD is a 44 year old man with type 2 diabetes for three years, a normal blood pressure and no known vascular disease. His LDL cholesterol is 2.4 mmol/L and he does not take a statin. He asks whether he needs one because his cholesterol seems low. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "No, a statin is considered only after he has had diabetes for at least ten years", "B": "Yes, statin therapy is recommended for adults with diabetes aged 40 or older", "C": "No, statins are recommended in diabetes only when LDL cholesterol exceeds 3.5 mmol/L at diagnosis", "D": "No, but he should start a statin when his A1c rises above 8.0% on treatment"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Yes, statin therapy is recommended for adults with diabetes aged 40 or older): The Canadian Cardiovascular Society recommends a statin for adults with diabetes aged 40 or older, even when LDL is modest, because diabetes raises vascular risk substantially. An LDL of 2.4 mmol/L does not remove that indication. The aim is roughly a 30 to 50% reduction in LDL."}, {"id": 1690, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "WL is a 49 year old man who took a single dose of a drug with a half-life of 6 hours and has normal clearance. A clinician asks the pharmacist after about how long roughly 97 percent of the dose will have been eliminated from the body. What should the pharmacist answer?", "options": {"A": "About 12 hours, which equals two half-lives and removes most of the dose from the body", "B": "About 18 hours, which equals three half-lives and removes nearly all of the dose", "C": "About 60 hours, which equals ten half-lives and removes the whole dose from the body", "D": "About 30 hours, which equals five half-lives of the drug in the body"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (About 30 hours, which equals five half-lives of the drug in the body): After five half-lives about 97 percent of a dose is eliminated, and 5 x 6 hours is 30 hours. Two half-lives remove only about 75 percent and three about 87.5 percent."}, {"id": 1691, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 6, "question": "FD is a 66 year old woman, 15 years past menopause, who asks to start an estradiol patch for hot flashes that returned this winter. She has hypertension and a 10 year cardiovascular risk of 18 percent. How should the pharmacist describe the risk of starting therapy now?", "options": {"A": "Risk applies only to oral products, so any patch can be used safely at her age", "B": "Risk is identical at every age, so starting now carries the same risk as at fifty", "C": "Risk is higher when started after age 60 or 10 years past menopause", "D": "Risk is lowest when therapy starts late, because older arteries are protected against estrogen effects"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Risk is higher when started after age 60 or 10 years past menopause): Starting hormone therapy more than 10 years after menopause or after age 60 is associated with a less favourable balance, including more cardiovascular events and stroke. With her added cardiovascular risk, benefit should be weighed carefully and non-hormonal drugs considered. Transdermal products carry less clot risk than oral ones, but that does not make them risk free at her age."}, {"id": 1692, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 1, "question": "AB is a 66 year old man with stable angina and difficulty swallowing large tablets. He takes ranolazine extended-release 500 mg twice daily and asks the pharmacist if he can crush the tablets and mix them in applesauce. What should the pharmacist advise?", "options": {"A": "Crushing is acceptable if he takes the powder within thirty minutes of mixing it with food", "B": "Chewing the tablets is acceptable, because the active drug is absorbed in the mouth", "C": "The tablets must be swallowed whole, so ask the prescriber about another antianginal", "D": "Splitting each tablet in half is acceptable, but the halves must be swallowed together"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The tablets must be swallowed whole, so ask the prescriber about another antianginal): Ranolazine extended-release tablets are formulated to release the drug slowly, and crushing, chewing or breaking them can cause rapid release and higher peak levels. They must be swallowed whole. If swallowing is the barrier, the prescriber should consider another antianginal that can be given in a different form."}, {"id": 1693, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 7, "question": "A 2 year old boy weighing 12 kg may have swallowed up to five ferrous fumarate tablets, each containing 100 mg of elemental iron. The poison centre guidance states that ingestions of 20 mg/kg elemental iron or less are usually mild, and above 20 mg/kg may cause toxicity. What is the estimated dose and action?", "options": {"A": "About 8 mg/kg, which is within the mild range, so home observation is enough", "B": "About 42 mg/kg, which is above the mild range, so seek emergency assessment", "C": "About 17 mg/kg, which is within the mild range, so home observation is enough", "D": "About 125 mg/kg, which reflects the total tablet weight, so seek advice only if he vomits"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (About 42 mg/kg, which is above the mild range, so seek emergency assessment): Five tablets contain 500 mg of elemental iron, and 500 divided by 12 kg is about 42 mg/kg, which is above the 20 mg/kg threshold for possible toxicity. This needs emergency assessment and poison centre contact. The other estimates come from miscounting tablets or using the wrong iron content."}, {"id": 1694, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 3, "question": "JP is a 64 year old man with blood cultures growing methicillin-susceptible Staphylococcus aureus on day two of vancomycin therapy. He is clinically stable with normal renal function, and echocardiography is pending. The team asks the pharmacist whether the antibiotic should change. What is the best recommendation?", "options": {"A": "Continue vancomycin for the full course, since it is equally effective against susceptible strains", "B": "Switch to cefazolin or cloxacillin, which work faster than vancomycin against susceptible strains", "C": "Switch to oral cephalexin, because step down to oral therapy is appropriate on day two", "D": "Add gentamicin to vancomycin for the full course to improve bacterial killing"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Switch to cefazolin or cloxacillin, which work faster than vancomycin against susceptible strains): For methicillin-susceptible Staphylococcus aureus bacteremia, beta-lactams such as cefazolin or cloxacillin are more rapidly bactericidal and give better outcomes than vancomycin. Gentamicin adds nephrotoxicity without benefit. Oral step down is premature on day two of bacteremia before the source and endocarditis are assessed."}, {"id": 1695, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "A hospital pharmacy manager reviews a quarterly controlled substances audit covering four staff members who handle narcotic stock. The audit compared each person's records with the dispensing system, patient charts and ward orders over three months. Which finding would raise the strongest concern for possible diversion?", "options": {"A": "One pharmacist took the most vacation days, so her narcotic reconciliations were done by other colleagues", "B": "One technician handled the highest narcotic volume because she covered the busiest ward all quarter", "C": "One pharmacist's counts were corrected twice by a second person after a documented transcription error", "D": "One technician logged frequent unwitnessed narcotic waste entries, some for patients without matching orders"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (One technician logged frequent unwitnessed narcotic waste entries, some for patients without matching orders): Frequent wastage without a witness, particularly for patients who have no matching order, is a recognized diversion pattern because the drug leaves stock without a clinical record to support it. Corrected counts after a documented error show a working control. High volume on a busy ward and reconciliations done by others during leave are explained by workload and scheduling."}, {"id": 1696, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "A pharmacist who has just moved from Alberta to Ontario notices that one nonprescription product sits in a different schedule than in the previous province. She asks the pharmacy manager why the schedules differ if NAPRA publishes the national drug schedules. What is the most accurate explanation?", "options": {"A": "NAPRA publishes a national model, and each province decides how to adopt or modify it", "B": "The schedules are reviewed by Health Canada each year and then applied unchanged in every province", "C": "Provinces may add products to the schedules but cannot move any to a less restrictive category", "D": "The NAPRA schedules are binding federal law, so any difference must be a printing error"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (NAPRA publishes a national model, and each province decides how to adopt or modify it): The NAPRA national drug schedules are a model that provinces and territories adopt through their own legislation or regulations, and some modify them. Federal law sets the Prescription Drug List, but the pharmacy conditions of sale come from provincial adoption."}, {"id": 1697, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "A pharmacist who supplies a long-term care home learns that a resident has requested medical assistance in dying on site. The pharmacy has no written policy on handling these requests. Which action best prepares the pharmacy to respond lawfully and consistently?", "options": {"A": "Tell the home the pharmacy will not handle such prescriptions and that residents must transfer elsewhere", "B": "Ask the family to obtain the drugs from a hospital so the pharmacy is not involved", "C": "Develop a written procedure covering dispensing, referral if staff object, and return of unused drugs", "D": "Wait until a prescription arrives and then decide individually whether each staff member will take part"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Develop a written procedure covering dispensing, referral if staff object, and return of unused drugs): A written procedure prepares the pharmacy for lawful dispensing, effective referral where individual staff object, and safe return of unused drugs. Case by case improvisation risks delays for the patient, and refusing to serve the home or asking families to find drugs elsewhere shifts the burden onto the patient."}, {"id": 1698, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A screening program reports that five year survival after cancer diagnosis rose from 60% to 80%, but deaths from the cancer per 100,000 people did not change. Tumours are now found earlier in the same disease course. Which bias best explains the apparent survival gain?", "options": {"A": "Lead time bias, because earlier detection lengthens measured survival without delaying the time of death", "B": "Attrition bias, because screened patients were more likely to be lost before the five year mark", "C": "Publication bias, because programs with improved survival are more likely to report their results", "D": "Recall bias, because screened patients remember their symptoms better than patients diagnosed clinically"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Lead time bias, because earlier detection lengthens measured survival without delaying the time of death): Earlier diagnosis starts the survival clock sooner, so patients live longer from diagnosis even when death occurs at the same age. Population mortality, which was unchanged, is the fair measure of whether screening helps. Survival after diagnosis is vulnerable to lead time and length biases."}, {"id": 1699, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "A drug previously listed as Schedule I is reclassified by NAPRA as Schedule III after a safety review. A pharmacy owner asks the pharmacist what changes at the point of sale once the province adopts the new classification. Which change occurs?", "options": {"A": "A prescription is no longer required, and the product may move to the self-selection area", "B": "A prescription is still required, but the pharmacy assistant may now complete the sale unsupervised", "C": "The product may be sold in any retail outlet if the label includes full counselling instructions", "D": "A pharmacist must counsel every purchaser in a private area before the sale is completed"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A prescription is no longer required, and the product may move to the self-selection area): Schedule III products are sold without a prescription from a self-selection area inside the pharmacy, with a pharmacist available to help. They cannot be sold outside the pharmacy, and counselling at every sale is the Schedule II requirement. Removing the prescription requirement is the main change from Schedule I."}, {"id": 1700, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "A pharmacist suspects that a toddler is neglected, after a caregiver repeatedly delays filling an antibiotic and the child looks underweight and listless. The pharmacist plans to ask the pharmacy manager to make the report instead. Which statement is correct?", "options": {"A": "The duty applies only to physicians and nurses, so pharmacists may report at their discretion", "B": "The report is required only after a second pharmacist independently confirms the same concern in writing", "C": "The duty is personal to the pharmacist and cannot be handed off to a manager", "D": "The manager may report on behalf of the pharmacy, and the pharmacist's duty is then met"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The duty is personal to the pharmacist and cannot be handed off to a manager): Child protection statutes impose the duty on any person who has reasonable grounds to suspect, and telling a supervisor does not discharge it. No second opinion or professional hierarchy is needed."}, {"id": 1701, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 6, "question": "UL is a 33 year old woman prescribed ciprofloxacin 500 mg twice daily for pyelonephritis. She also takes aluminum magnesium hydroxide antacid tablets several times daily for heartburn and wants to keep taking them. What timing instructions should the pharmacist give for ciprofloxacin?", "options": {"A": "Take ciprofloxacin together with the antacid, which protects her stomach from antibiotic irritation", "B": "Take ciprofloxacin at least six hours before or two hours after each antacid dose", "C": "Take ciprofloxacin thirty minutes before each antacid dose, which is enough to avoid binding", "D": "Take ciprofloxacin at least two hours before or six hours after each antacid dose"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Take ciprofloxacin at least two hours before or six hours after each antacid dose): Aluminum and magnesium cations chelate fluoroquinolones in the gut and sharply reduce absorption. Ciprofloxacin should be taken at least two hours before or six hours after antacids. A 30 minute gap is too short, and swapping the intervals would leave the antacid still present in the gut."}, {"id": 1702, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "Calling from another pharmacy, a colleague asks to transfer a patient's active prescription for rosuvastatin 10 mg daily, which has three refills remaining. The patient is moving to a new city. What is the correct way for the transferring pharmacist to complete the transfer?", "options": {"A": "Fax the original prescription to the new pharmacy and keep the refills active at both locations", "B": "Tell the caller that transfers require a new prescription from the physician for the rosuvastatin", "C": "Hand the original prescription to the patient so she can present it at the new pharmacy", "D": "Give the details pharmacist to pharmacist, record the transfer, and cancel the remaining refills here"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Give the details pharmacist to pharmacist, record the transfer, and cancel the remaining refills here): A prescription for a non-controlled drug can be transferred by direct communication between pharmacists, with the transfer recorded and the remaining refills cancelled so they cannot be filled twice. Faxing the original, keeping active refills at both sites, or handing out the original creates a risk of duplicate fills. A new prescription from the physician is not needed."}, {"id": 1703, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 3, "question": "KP is a 6 year old boy with a drained 4 cm skin abscess on his buttock, now with fever of 38.6 C and spreading redness. Culture results are pending. Which oral agent would a prescriber reasonably choose for presumed MRSA in this child?", "options": {"A": "Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole, dosed by weight, since doxycycline is generally avoided in young children", "B": "Ciprofloxacin, dosed by weight, because it covers both staphylococci and streptococci", "C": "Cephalexin alone, dosed by weight, since it reliably covers community MRSA strains", "D": "Doxycycline 100 mg twice daily, as it is the usual first choice for children"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole, dosed by weight, since doxycycline is generally avoided in young children): Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole has reliable activity against community MRSA and is used in children. Doxycycline is avoided in children under 8 because of tooth staining, and the adult dose would be wrong anyway. Beta-lactams do not cover MRSA, and fluoroquinolones are avoided in children."}, {"id": 1704, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 7, "question": "MQ is a 69 year old man who takes clopidogrel 75 mg daily after a coronary stent. He has frequent heartburn and asks for omeprazole 20 mg, which his friend uses. He has no alarm symptoms. The pharmacist wants acid suppression without reducing his antiplatelet effect. What is the best recommendation?", "options": {"A": "Recommend esomeprazole 20 mg daily, since it is the proton pump inhibitor safest with clopidogrel", "B": "Recommend omeprazole 20 mg taken at bedtime, since separating it from clopidogrel removes the interaction", "C": "Recommend stopping clopidogrel while he uses any acid suppressant, and restart it once heartburn resolves", "D": "Recommend pantoprazole 20 mg daily, which has less effect on clopidogrel activation than omeprazole"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Recommend pantoprazole 20 mg daily, which has less effect on clopidogrel activation than omeprazole): Omeprazole and esomeprazole inhibit CYP2C19, which activates clopidogrel, so they can reduce its antiplatelet effect. Pantoprazole has minimal CYP2C19 inhibition and is the preferred proton pump inhibitor with clopidogrel. Separating the dose times does not solve the interaction, and stopping clopidogrel after a stent is dangerous."}, {"id": 1705, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 1, "question": "DK is a 68 year old man with heart failure and an ejection fraction of 27%, taking carvedilol 12.5 mg twice daily, sacubitril/valsartan 49/51 mg twice daily, spironolactone, dapagliflozin and furosemide 40 mg daily. He is euvolemic, but blood pressure is 92/58 mmHg and he feels lightheaded when standing. Which action does the pharmacist suggest?", "options": {"A": "Stop sacubitril/valsartan and restart ramipril, because ACE inhibitors cause less hypotension than the newer combination product", "B": "Reduce the furosemide dose and stagger dosing times of the other blood pressure lowering drugs", "C": "Stop carvedilol now, since the beta-blocker is the drug most likely to cause the low pressure", "D": "Add midodrine to raise his pressure so all four drug classes can continue"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Reduce the furosemide dose and stagger dosing times of the other blood pressure lowering drugs): Mild hypotension in a euvolemic patient on guideline therapy is managed by trimming the diuretic and separating dose times, which preserves the drugs that lower mortality. Stopping a prognostic agent or adding a pressor undermines treatment. Switching back to an ACE inhibitor does not meaningfully reduce hypotension."}, {"id": 1706, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 1, "question": "WK is a 68 year old man with stable symptomatic peripheral arterial disease, no recent bleeding and no kidney impairment. His vascular surgeon mentions rivaroxaban 2.5 mg twice daily plus aspirin 81 mg daily. Which statement should the pharmacist make?", "options": {"A": "this combination is for acute limb ischemia only and has no role in stable disease", "B": "this combination is meant to replace statin therapy once the dual regimen is established", "C": "this combination can lower cardiovascular and limb events, but bleeding risk needs counselling", "D": "this combination is safe with ibuprofen for joint pain and carries no added bleeding risk"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (this combination can lower cardiovascular and limb events, but bleeding risk needs counselling): Low dose rivaroxaban plus aspirin reduced major cardiovascular and limb events in stable PAD in large trials, with more major bleeding, so patients are selected and counselled. It does not replace a statin, and adding NSAIDs would increase bleeding."}, {"id": 1707, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A 62 year old woman takes atorvastatin 80 mg daily and enjoys a large glass of grapefruit juice with breakfast every day. She has no muscle pain. She asks the pharmacist whether this matters. The pharmacist explains that grapefruit juice mainly affects atorvastatin by:", "options": {"A": "Blocking renal tubular secretion, which delays elimination of atorvastatin and its active metabolites", "B": "Inhibiting intestinal CYP3A4, which increases the amount of drug reaching the circulation", "C": "Inducing intestinal CYP3A4, which lowers the amount of atorvastatin that reaches the circulation", "D": "Increasing gastric pH, which dissolves the tablet faster and speeds the onset of the drug"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Inhibiting intestinal CYP3A4, which increases the amount of drug reaching the circulation): Furanocoumarins in grapefruit irreversibly inhibit CYP3A4 in the gut wall, reducing gut wall metabolism and raising systemic exposure of substrates such as atorvastatin. The effect is greater with large daily quantities and high doses. It is not an induction, renal or gastric pH effect."}, {"id": 1708, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 4, "question": "RC is a 34 year old man whose asthma action plan lists a personal best peak flow of 500 L/min. This morning he has chest tightness and a reading of 340 L/min. He asks what to do under his plan, which uses the standard colour zones. What should the pharmacist advise?", "options": {"A": "This is the yellow zone, so he should wait one week before changing his treatment", "B": "This is the yellow zone, so use the reliever and start the plan's step up", "C": "This is the red zone, so he should skip the reliever and call an ambulance", "D": "This is the green zone, so he should continue his usual medicines and recheck tomorrow"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (This is the yellow zone, so use the reliever and start the plan's step up): 340 divided by 500 is 68% of his personal best, which falls in the yellow zone of 50 to 80%. The plan directs him to use his reliever and start the step up in treatment, and to seek care if he does not improve. Green is above 80%, and red is below 50%."}, {"id": 1709, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 3, "question": "OG is a 52 year old woman with chronic hepatitis B and compensated cirrhosis starting entecavir 0.5 mg once daily. Her CrCl is 80 mL/min. She usually takes her pills with breakfast at seven and asks about the best way to take this one. How should the pharmacist counsel her?", "options": {"A": "Take it with an antacid at bedtime, since stomach acid destroys the drug before absorption", "B": "Take it with breakfast, because food increases absorption and improves antiviral effect in cirrhosis", "C": "Take it on an empty stomach, at least two hours from any meal", "D": "Take it only when liver enzymes are raised, using the lowest dose that controls ALT"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Take it on an empty stomach, at least two hours from any meal): Food reduces entecavir absorption, so it is taken on an empty stomach, at least two hours after and two hours before a meal. Antivirals for hepatitis B are taken continuously, since stopping can trigger flares that are dangerous in cirrhosis. Antacids and fatty meals do not improve absorption."}, {"id": 1710, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "The pharmacist at a small pharmacy is arranging a new narcotic cabinet after a renovation of the dispensary. The owner asks which arrangement is consistent with the federal requirement to keep narcotic stock secure from theft. Which choice is best?", "options": {"A": "A cabinet in the staff room, since the key is held by whoever opens the store", "B": "An unlocked shelf behind the dispensary counter, since the dispensary is closed to the public", "C": "A locked drawer in the front store, since staff in the front store watch it", "D": "A locked, secured cabinet or safe in the dispensary, accessible only to authorized staff"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A locked, secured cabinet or safe in the dispensary, accessible only to authorized staff): Narcotics must be stored where only authorized staff can reach them, which means a locked cabinet or safe inside the controlled dispensary area. A closed dispensary alone is not enough, and a front-store drawer or shared staff-room key widens access beyond the people accountable for the stock."}, {"id": 1711, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "SN is an 8 year old boy who started methylphenidate extended-release 27 mg each morning three weeks ago. His mother says he now skips lunch and refuses dinner, and she worries about his growth. His weight has dropped slightly. What practical advice should the pharmacist give her?", "options": {"A": "Give it after breakfast and offer nutritious snacks in the evening when appetite returns", "B": "Require him to finish lunch and dinner using rewards, and allow no snacks between meals", "C": "Stop the medication at once because appetite loss shows it is unsafe, and request a nonstimulant", "D": "Move the dose to bedtime so appetite is unaffected during the day, then monitor weight"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Give it after breakfast and offer nutritious snacks in the evening when appetite returns): Stimulants suppress appetite in the middle of the day, so giving the dose with or after breakfast and offering calorie-dense snacks when the effect wears off supports intake. Moving the dose to bedtime would disrupt sleep and lose daytime benefit. Appetite loss is common and manageable, and growth is followed by the prescriber rather than by stopping at once."}, {"id": 1712, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A patient reports hives and wheezing about 20 minutes after the first dose of a generic levothyroxine from a newly stocked supplier. She recovered after emergency treatment and kept the bottle. The pharmacist suspects a possible product quality problem. In addition to supporting the patient, the pharmacist should:", "options": {"A": "Wait for the manufacturer to confirm a manufacturing defect before contacting Health Canada about the product", "B": "Switch the patient to another generic manufacturer and document the event in the pharmacy file only", "C": "Quarantine the remaining stock of that lot and report the reaction and suspected quality problem", "D": "Return the lot to the supplier for credit and report only if other patients complain"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Quarantine the remaining stock of that lot and report the reaction and suspected quality problem): A serious reaction with a possible quality defect should be reported promptly, and removing the suspect lot from sale protects other patients. Keeping the bottle preserves evidence for investigation. Waiting for confirmation, or quietly returning stock, delays signal detection and can leave patients exposed."}, {"id": 1713, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 7, "question": "A customer holds a bottle of homeopathic cough syrup that shows a DIN-HM on the label. She asks whether it is the same as a Natural Product Number. What should the pharmacist explain? She is shopping for her toddler and asks whether it is regulated like other natural products.", "options": {"A": "It is a manufacturer's batch code that confirms the strength of each bottle", "B": "It identifies an authorized homeopathic medicine, which is licensed separately from products carrying an NPN", "C": "It shows the same standard as an NPN and the two numbers are interchangeable", "D": "It means the product is a prescription drug that must be dispensed through the pharmacy"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (It identifies an authorized homeopathic medicine, which is licensed separately from products carrying an NPN): A DIN-HM identifies a homeopathic medicine licensed by Health Canada under the natural health product framework with its own evidence requirements, while other natural health products carry an NPN. Neither is a lot number, and neither indicates prescription status."}, {"id": 1714, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "Citing a long relationship, a patient asks the pharmacist to change the date and add two extra items on a receipt for an extended health benefits claim, explaining the plan is large and will not notice. She has always been courteous. What should the pharmacist do?", "options": {"A": "Refuse and report her to the insurer immediately, since the request alone proves she intends fraud", "B": "Alter the receipt but record the change in the pharmacy file so an audit trail exists", "C": "Change the date only, because adjusting a date is less serious than adding items not sold", "D": "Decline, explain that receipts must show what was actually dispensed, and offer an accurate copy"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Decline, explain that receipts must show what was actually dispensed, and offer an accurate copy): A receipt is a record of what was dispensed and sold, and altering it to support a claim would make the pharmacist a party to insurance fraud. Documenting the alteration does not make it acceptable, and changing only the date still falsifies the record. The appropriate step is to refuse politely and offer a genuine duplicate, without assuming criminal intent."}, {"id": 1715, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "An insurance adjuster phones the pharmacy requesting a claimant's dispensing history for a motor vehicle accident claim. The claimant has not signed an authorization. The adjuster says the claim will be delayed without the records and asks for them today. The pharmacist should:", "options": {"A": "Fax the full history, since insurers are entitled to health information needed to settle a claim", "B": "Fax the history if the adjuster supplies a claim number and the date of the accident", "C": "Confirm the claimant's drugs verbally, since a phone conversation is not a formal disclosure of records", "D": "Ask for the claimant's signed authorization or a legal order, then release only relevant information"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Ask for the claimant's signed authorization or a legal order, then release only relevant information): A third party such as an insurer needs the patient's consent or legal authority before receiving health information, and the release should be limited to what the purpose requires. A claim number and verbal disclosure do not change that."}, {"id": 1716, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "Authorized in her province to receive verbal prescriptions for non-controlled drugs, a regulated technician takes a call from a clinic nurse for cephalexin 500 mg capsules for a 30 year old woman. The nurse gives the quantity but cannot recall the frequency or duration. What should the technician do?", "options": {"A": "Mark the prescription incomplete and ask the patient to obtain the directions from the clinic", "B": "Obtain the missing directions from the prescriber, rather than assuming a standard regimen", "C": "Record the usual directions, one capsule four times daily for seven days, and tell the nurse", "D": "Ask the patient what the clinic told her and enter whatever she reports as the directions"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Obtain the missing directions from the prescriber, rather than assuming a standard regimen): A verbal prescription must contain complete directions from the prescriber, and a technician cannot supply missing dosing information based on a typical regimen. The pharmacy should obtain the frequency and duration from the prescriber or the prescriber's office. Relying on the patient's recall or sending her to the clinic adds delay and risk of error."}, {"id": 1717, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 4, "question": "RE is a 47 year old lung transplant recipient taking tacrolimus 5 mg twice daily with a trough of 9 mcg/L. Her team starts voriconazole 200 mg twice daily for a fungal infection. The prescriber has not mentioned any change in tacrolimus. What is the pharmacist's best recommendation?", "options": {"A": "Suggest increasing tacrolimus by one third, since azole antifungals induce its metabolism and lower levels", "B": "Suggest no change because voriconazole does not affect CYP3A4, and recheck the trough next month", "C": "Suggest changing tacrolimus to once daily dosing with the same total daily dose during the infection", "D": "Suggest cutting tacrolimus to about one third of the dose and monitoring troughs closely"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Suggest cutting tacrolimus to about one third of the dose and monitoring troughs closely): Voriconazole is a potent CYP3A4 inhibitor and markedly raises tacrolimus exposure, so labelling advises reducing the tacrolimus dose to about one third and monitoring troughs frequently. Increasing the dose or waiting a month risks nephrotoxicity and neurotoxicity, and a formulation change does not address the interaction."}, {"id": 1718, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 1, "question": "AW is a 77 year old man with permanent atrial fibrillation who takes digoxin 0.125 mg daily and apixaban. His cardiologist adds amiodarone 200 mg daily for fast ventricular rates. His digoxin level before the change was 1.0 nmol/L and his creatinine clearance is 48 mL/min. What should the pharmacist recommend about digoxin?", "options": {"A": "Stop digoxin on the day amiodarone starts and restart after the loading period of amiodarone ends", "B": "Continue digoxin unchanged, since the two drugs act at different sites and do not interact", "C": "Halve the digoxin dose, then monitor the digoxin level and heart rate closely", "D": "Double the digoxin dose, because amiodarone induces digoxin clearance and lowers the serum level"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Halve the digoxin dose, then monitor the digoxin level and heart rate closely): Amiodarone inhibits P-glycoprotein and reduces renal and nonrenal digoxin clearance, which can double the digoxin concentration. Halving the dose and following the level and heart rate prevents toxicity. Amiodarone does not induce digoxin clearance, and stopping digoxin altogether is unnecessary."}, {"id": 1719, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "The pharmacist at a hospital outpatient pharmacy receives a call from a nurse practitioner arranging medications for a patient who qualifies for medical assistance in dying. The nurse practitioner asks whether the pharmacist is permitted to dispense. Under the Criminal Code, which statement is accurate about a pharmacist's role?", "options": {"A": "A pharmacist informed of the purpose may dispense on a prescription without committing an offence", "B": "The pharmacist may dispense only after the provincial college issues a special permit for each case", "C": "A pharmacist who dispenses must attend the administration to witness the procedure as a legal safeguard", "D": "Only hospital pharmacists are covered by the exemption, and community pharmacists may not dispense these drugs"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A pharmacist informed of the purpose may dispense on a prescription without committing an offence): The Criminal Code exempts a pharmacist who dispenses a substance on a prescription for medical assistance in dying when the pharmacist knows the purpose. No special college permit is required, the pharmacist does not need to attend the administration, and the exemption is not limited to hospital practice."}, {"id": 1720, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "RS, 79, wants his medications synchronized to one pickup date. His list includes levothyroxine, atorvastatin and ramipril, plus a new 10 day course of cefuroxime for bronchitis that was started today. The pharmacist plans the sync date. Which approach is best?", "options": {"A": "Include the antibiotic and shorten its course so it ends on the sync date", "B": "Exclude the short antibiotic course and synchronize only the long-term chronic medications", "C": "Include the antibiotic and dispense a 30 day supply to match the other medications", "D": "Synchronize levothyroxine alone, since only one drug can be aligned at a time"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Exclude the short antibiotic course and synchronize only the long-term chronic medications): A short antibiotic course has a fixed end and should not be extended or shortened to fit a refill schedule. Chronic medications are good candidates for synchronization, and aligning them reduces trips and missed doses."}, {"id": 1721, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "BP is a 40 year old woman with type 1 diabetes whose carbohydrate ratio is 1 unit per 8 g. A cereal label lists 40 g of carbohydrate per serving and she eats 2.5 servings, with a pre-meal glucose in the target range. How many units of rapid-acting insulin should she dose for the meal?", "options": {"A": "12.5 units, because 100 g of carbohydrate divided by 8 g per unit gives 12.5", "B": "20.0 units, because the 2.5 servings are multiplied by the 8 g in her ratio", "C": "5.0 units, because the 40 g serving is divided by the ratio of 8 g", "D": "10.0 units, because the 2.5 servings are rounded down to 2 before dividing by the ratio"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (12.5 units, because 100 g of carbohydrate divided by 8 g per unit gives 12.5): Total carbohydrate is 40 g times 2.5 servings, which is 100 g. Dividing by 8 g per unit gives 12.5 units. Using only one serving, multiplying by the ratio, or rounding the servings gives a dose that does not match what she eats."}, {"id": 1722, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "PH is a 49 year old woman taking alprazolam 1 mg three times daily for three years. She asks for an early refill, saying her bag with the medication was stolen on a trip. Her last fill was 12 days ago, and the prescriber is unavailable until Monday. What should the pharmacist do?", "options": {"A": "Refill the prescription now on her word alone, since the regulations require trusting patient reports", "B": "Dispense a seven day supply using the remaining refill authorization already in the pharmacy's computer system", "C": "Refuse supply until she brings a police report, and tell her to skip doses until Monday", "D": "Verify the loss, contact the prescriber, and supply once authorized to avoid abrupt withdrawal"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Verify the loss, contact the prescriber, and supply once authorized to avoid abrupt withdrawal): A reported loss of a targeted substance needs verification and prescriber authorization before an early supply, since early refills fall outside the written interval. Alprazolam taken for years should not be stopped abruptly because withdrawal can be severe, so the pharmacist should work promptly to arrange authorized supply."}, {"id": 1723, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 7, "question": "ZP, a 9 month old weighing 8 kg, has a fever and her mother wants to know the most acetaminophen she may receive in one day. The reference maximum is 75 mg/kg/day, and the suspension is 160 mg per 5 mL. What is the maximum daily volume?", "options": {"A": "No more than 37.5 mL in 24 hours, which corresponds to 150 mg/kg/day", "B": "No more than 18.75 mL in 24 hours, equal to 600 mg", "C": "No more than 9.4 mL in 24 hours, which corresponds to 37.5 mg/kg/day", "D": "No more than 25 mL in 24 hours, which corresponds to 100 mg/kg/day"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (No more than 18.75 mL in 24 hours, equal to 600 mg): The daily maximum is 75 x 8 = 600 mg. At 32 mg per mL this is 18.75 mL over 24 hours. The other volumes correspond to double, half or a third higher than the stated limit."}, {"id": 1724, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "In a rural emergency department, the physician asks the pharmacist whether calcium chloride 10% in a 10 mL syringe delivers more elemental calcium than calcium gluconate 10% in a 10 mL vial for a 73 year old man with hyperkalemia. Which statement is accurate?", "options": {"A": "Both give the same elemental calcium because both are 10% solutions of calcium salts", "B": "Calcium gluconate gives about three times as much elemental calcium per 10 mL", "C": "Calcium gluconate gives about double the elemental calcium because the gluconate salt is larger", "D": "Calcium chloride gives about three times as much elemental calcium per 10 mL"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Calcium chloride gives about three times as much elemental calcium per 10 mL): Calcium chloride 10% in 10 mL provides about 273 mg of elemental calcium, while calcium gluconate 10% in 10 mL provides about 93 mg. A percentage describes the salt, not the calcium, so equal percentages do not mean equal calcium. Chloride is also more irritating to veins."}, {"id": 1725, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "A patient brings back 20 unused lorazepam 1 mg tablets from a prescription that the prescriber discontinued. She asks the pharmacist to give them to her neighbour, who has a similar prescription. The tablets are still in their original, labelled pharmacy container. What should the pharmacist do?", "options": {"A": "Give them to the neighbour once the pharmacist checks that the neighbour's prescribed strength matches", "B": "Accept them for destruction under the pharmacy's policy and do not return them to stock", "C": "Return them to the shelf after counting and checking the tablets against the original label", "D": "Mail them back to the manufacturer for credit and record the return as a sale"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Accept them for destruction under the pharmacy's policy and do not return them to stock): Medications returned by patients must not be dispensed again because their storage and handling cannot be verified, so they are destroyed under the pharmacy's procedures. Sharing them with a neighbour would be an unauthorized supply of a targeted substance."}, {"id": 1726, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 1, "question": "TP is a 72 year old woman with heart failure and an ejection fraction of 25%. Two years ago she had lip and tongue swelling attributed to lisinopril, and she has taken no ACE inhibitor since. Her cardiologist writes a prescription for sacubitril/valsartan 24/26 mg twice daily. What should the pharmacist do?", "options": {"A": "Dispense it after a 36 hour washout, which is enough to remove the angioedema risk", "B": "Dispense it, since the swelling followed an ACE inhibitor and this product contains an ARB", "C": "Dispense it but add a daily antihistamine to prevent recurrent lip and tongue swelling", "D": "Contact the cardiologist, because sacubitril/valsartan is contraindicated after angioedema linked to an ACE inhibitor"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Contact the cardiologist, because sacubitril/valsartan is contraindicated after angioedema linked to an ACE inhibitor): Sacubitril/valsartan raises bradykinin through neprilysin inhibition, so it is contraindicated with a history of angioedema related to an ACE inhibitor or an ARNI. The ARB component does not remove that risk, and the washout rule concerns current ACE inhibitor use rather than a past reaction. Antihistamines do not reliably prevent bradykinin-mediated swelling."}, {"id": 1727, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "KO, a 64 kg, 66 year old woman admitted with acute heart failure, has dobutamine ordered at 2.5 mcg/kg/min. The bag contains 250 mg in 250 mL. The nurse on the cardiac unit asks the pharmacist to confirm the pump setting in mL per hour. What rate is correct?", "options": {"A": "2.5 mL per hour, using the 1 mg per mL bag concentration as written", "B": "9.6 mL per hour, using the 1 mg per mL bag concentration", "C": "0.16 mL per hour, using the 1 mg per mL bag concentration as written", "D": "96 mL per hour, using the 1 mg per mL bag concentration as written"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (9.6 mL per hour, using the 1 mg per mL bag concentration): The dose is 2.5 x 64 = 160 mcg per minute, or 9600 mcg per hour, which is 9.6 mg per hour. At 1 mg per mL the rate is 9.6 mL per hour. The 96 figure comes from a decimal slip and would deliver ten times the intended dose."}, {"id": 1728, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 7, "question": "PD is a 74 year old woman who has taken zopiclone 7.5 mg nightly for five years. She is worried about falling but is anxious about stopping. The pharmacist wants to use an approach shown to help older adults discontinue sedative hypnotics. What should the pharmacist do first?", "options": {"A": "Replace zopiclone with diphenhydramine 50 mg nightly, since it is an easier drug to stop later", "B": "Give an educational brochure on sedative risks and tapering, then set a follow up date", "C": "Wait until her next physician visit before discussing options, since pharmacists cannot raise deprescribing with patients", "D": "Tell her to stop tonight and call if withdrawal symptoms occur, limiting the time at risk"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Give an educational brochure on sedative risks and tapering, then set a follow up date): Patient education about the risks of sedative hypnotics, with tapering plans, has been shown to help older adults reduce or stop these drugs. A follow up date supports adherence to the taper. Stopping suddenly risks withdrawal, diphenhydramine is a poorer drug for older adults, and pharmacists are well placed to start the conversation."}, {"id": 1729, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 7, "question": "WB is a 62 year old man who asks for a hemorrhoid cream. He describes itching and bright red blood on the tissue for the past week, and this is the first time he has noticed bleeding. He has not had colon cancer screening. What should the pharmacist do?", "options": {"A": "Refer him for assessment, since new rectal bleeding at age 62 needs investigation first", "B": "Recommend a stool softener and sitz baths, and review the bleeding at his next routine visit", "C": "Sell a hemorrhoid cream and refer only if the bleeding continues beyond a week", "D": "Advise that bright red blood on tissue indicates hemorrhoids, so treatment can begin right away"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Refer him for assessment, since new rectal bleeding at age 62 needs investigation first): New rectal bleeding in an adult over 50 who has not been screened can signal colorectal cancer, even if hemorrhoids are the likely cause. Assessment should come before self-treatment. Treating first risks masking symptoms and delaying diagnosis."}, {"id": 1730, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "During an antimicrobial stewardship review, a hospital pharmacist moves a validated MRSA screening swab from a ward with 2% MRSA prevalence to a ward with 20% prevalence. Which characteristic of the test is expected to remain essentially unchanged between the two wards?", "options": {"A": "Its negative predictive value, which stays fixed because the test result is read the same way", "B": "Its positive predictive value, which is determined by the swab technique rather than the prevalence", "C": "Its sensitivity and specificity, which describe the test itself rather than the population being screened", "D": "Its false positive count per 100 swabs, which depends only on the specificity of the test"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Its sensitivity and specificity, which describe the test itself rather than the population being screened): Sensitivity and specificity describe how the swab performs in diseased and non-diseased people and are expected to stay similar across settings. Predictive values and the number of false positives per 100 swabs change with prevalence. The false positive count depends on both specificity and prevalence."}, {"id": 1731, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "CN is a 69 year old man with heart failure and diabetic neuropathy who started pregabalin 75 mg twice daily two weeks ago. His ankles are newly swollen and his weight is up 2.5 kg, with no new dyspnea. Furosemide dose is unchanged. What should the pharmacist recommend?", "options": {"A": "Consider pregabalin related edema and ask the prescriber about reducing the dose or changing therapy", "B": "Increase furosemide only, since swelling with heart failure is treated with a larger diuretic dose", "C": "Reassure him that swelling from pregabalin is harmless and has no relevance to heart failure control", "D": "Stop pregabalin abruptly today and start it again at the same dose once swelling has resolved"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Consider pregabalin related edema and ask the prescriber about reducing the dose or changing therapy): Pregabalin and gabapentin commonly cause peripheral edema and weight gain, which can be confused with or worsen heart failure. The timing after starting pregabalin with an unchanged diuretic points to the drug, so dose reduction or a different agent should be considered. Simply adding diuretic treats the symptom, and abrupt stopping risks withdrawal."}, {"id": 1732, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 7, "question": "EV is an 83 year old woman weighing 54 kg with a serum creatinine of 118 umol/L. Using the Cockcroft-Gault equation with her actual body weight, the pharmacist estimates her creatinine clearance before reviewing her home medications for renal dose adjustment. Which value is closest?", "options": {"A": "About 32 mL/min, which is a moderate reduction needing only minor dose changes", "B": "About 27 mL/min, which is a severe reduction in renal function for dose adjustment", "C": "About 44 mL/min, which is a mild reduction that needs no change in drug doses", "D": "About 21 mL/min, which reflects kidney failure and requires stopping all renally cleared drugs"}, "cluster_id": "CHS15", "cluster_text": "EV is an 83 year old woman admitted to hospital with pneumonia. She weighs 54 kg and her serum creatinine is 118 umol/L. At home she takes gabapentin 300 mg three times daily for postherpetic neuralgia, acetaminophen 500 mg as needed, and calcium with vitamin D. She has had two falls in the past year and has no known drug allergies.", "cluster_size": 3, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (About 27 mL/min, which is a severe reduction in renal function for dose adjustment): For a woman, CrCl = (140 - 83) x 54 x 1.04 / 118 = 3201 / 118, which is about 27 mL/min. This is a marked reduction that affects dosing of renally cleared drugs such as gabapentin. Using the male factor would give about 32 mL/min and understate the problem."}, {"id": 1733, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 7, "question": "Her gabapentin 300 mg three times daily was continued at admission. The pharmacist checks the product monograph, which lists 200 to 700 mg once daily for a creatinine clearance of 15 to 29 mL/min, and compares it with her estimated clearance of about 27 mL/min. Which regimen should the pharmacist recommend?", "options": {"A": "Continue 300 mg three times daily, because hospital doses are not adjusted during acute infections", "B": "Increase to 600 mg three times daily, because pneumonia has made her pain harder to control", "C": "Change to 400 mg twice daily, since twice daily dosing suits this level of kidney function", "D": "Gabapentin 300 mg once daily, adjusted afterward according to pain relief and tolerability"}, "cluster_id": "CHS15", "cluster_text": "EV is an 83 year old woman admitted to hospital with pneumonia. She weighs 54 kg and her serum creatinine is 118 umol/L. At home she takes gabapentin 300 mg three times daily for postherpetic neuralgia, acetaminophen 500 mg as needed, and calcium with vitamin D. She has had two falls in the past year and has no known drug allergies.", "cluster_size": 3, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Gabapentin 300 mg once daily, adjusted afterward according to pain relief and tolerability): With a clearance of about 27 mL/min she falls in the 15 to 29 mL/min band, where gabapentin is given as a single daily dose in the 200 to 700 mg range. Her current 900 mg per day is too high and raises the risk of sedation and falls. Acute illness does not remove the need for renal adjustment, and a twice daily schedule belongs to a higher clearance band."}, {"id": 1734, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 7, "question": "After the dose is lowered, the pharmacist counsels EV and her daughter about monitoring in hospital and at home. Which advice about adverse effects is most important given her age, renal function, weight of 54 kg and history of two falls in the past year?", "options": {"A": "Check her INR weekly, because gabapentin increases the anticoagulant effect of other drugs she may take", "B": "Watch for drowsiness, dizziness and unsteadiness, and report any new falls or confusion promptly", "C": "Watch for gum overgrowth and rash, which are the usual adverse effects of gabapentin", "D": "Monitor for low blood sugar, tremor and sweating, because gabapentin is known to lower blood glucose"}, "cluster_id": "CHS15", "cluster_text": "EV is an 83 year old woman admitted to hospital with pneumonia. She weighs 54 kg and her serum creatinine is 118 umol/L. At home she takes gabapentin 300 mg three times daily for postherpetic neuralgia, acetaminophen 500 mg as needed, and calcium with vitamin D. She has had two falls in the past year and has no known drug allergies.", "cluster_size": 3, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Watch for drowsiness, dizziness and unsteadiness, and report any new falls or confusion promptly): Gabapentin commonly causes sedation, dizziness and ataxia, and these effects are amplified when renal clearance is low and in someone who has already fallen. Early reporting allows the dose to be reduced. Gum overgrowth is linked to phenytoin, and gabapentin does not affect the INR or lower blood glucose."}, {"id": 1735, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 6, "question": "ER is a 78 year old woman with nocturnal heartburn taking famotidine 20 mg twice daily. Her estimated creatinine clearance is 28 mL/min and she has had some confusion this month. She also takes ramipril and a statin, and her serum creatinine has been stable. Which change should the pharmacist suggest?", "options": {"A": "Switch to cimetidine 400 mg twice daily, which is safer in renal impairment", "B": "Reduce the dose by half to 20 mg once daily, reflecting reduced renal clearance", "C": "Increase to 40 mg twice daily, since confusion suggests acid control is inadequate overnight", "D": "Continue the same dose, since famotidine is cleared by the liver and needs no adjustment"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Reduce the dose by half to 20 mg once daily, reflecting reduced renal clearance): Famotidine is eliminated mainly by the kidneys, and with a clearance below 50 mL/min the dose should be halved or the interval doubled because accumulation can cause confusion in older adults. Increasing the dose would worsen toxicity. Cimetidine has more central nervous system effects and interactions, so it is not safer."}, {"id": 1736, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 4, "question": "PD is a 55 year old man who takes moclobemide for depression and asks for a cough syrup with dextromethorphan for a dry cough. He has had a cough for four days and wants a product that works overnight. The pharmacist should advise against it because the combination can cause:", "options": {"A": "serotonin toxicity, with agitation, fever, tremor and a rapid heart rate", "B": "bleeding, because dextromethorphan inhibits platelet function when combined with antidepressant therapy", "C": "profound hypotension, because both drugs lower blood pressure through the same receptor mechanism", "D": "severe liver injury, because moclobemide strongly increases dextromethorphan metabolism in the liver"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (serotonin toxicity, with agitation, fever, tremor and a rapid heart rate): Dextromethorphan has serotonergic activity, and combining it with a monoamine oxidase inhibitor such as moclobemide risks serotonin syndrome. Moclobemide would slow, not speed, dextromethorphan metabolism, and neither drug is notable for platelet effects. An alternative such as honey or a non-serotonergic approach is safer."}, {"id": 1737, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "A hospital pharmacy receives a shipment that includes vials of carboplatin and a liquid antineoplastic in a corrugated cardboard carton. The carton looks intact, but the receiving clerk is unsure how to open it. The pharmacist reviews the receiving procedure for hazardous drugs. Which practice is appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Open the carton at the shared receiving counter with ordinary examination gloves, since it looks intact", "B": "Wear chemotherapy gloves, open the carton in a designated area, and inspect for breakage or leaks", "C": "Store the sealed carton in the stockroom and open it only when the drugs are needed", "D": "Ask the courier to open the carton and report breakage before the pharmacy accepts the delivery"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Wear chemotherapy gloves, open the carton in a designated area, and inspect for breakage or leaks): Shipping cartons of hazardous drugs may hide leaks or broken vials, so staff wear chemotherapy gloves, and a gown if damage is suspected, and unpack the shipment in a designated area away from general traffic while inspecting each item. Opening them at a shared counter or storing them unopened leaves any contamination undetected."}, {"id": 1738, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "IC is an 82 year old woman in long-term care with type 2 diabetes, an A1c of 6.4% and two hypoglycemic episodes this month. She takes glyburide 5 mg twice daily. Which change is most appropriate? Her kidney function is mildly reduced and she eats poorly.", "options": {"A": "switch to glimepiride at the same dose, since all sulfonylureas have identical hypoglycemia risk", "B": "replace glyburide with a DPP-4 inhibitor and relax her A1c target", "C": "keep glyburide and reduce her meals, since lower intake prevents glucose swings", "D": "increase glyburide to 10 mg twice daily, since higher doses reduce nocturnal hypoglycemia"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (replace glyburide with a DPP-4 inhibitor and relax her A1c target): Glyburide has a long active-metabolite half-life and carries the highest hypoglycemia risk among sulfonylureas, which is especially dangerous in frail older adults. An A1c near 6.4% is lower than needed, so a safer agent and a relaxed target are appropriate."}, {"id": 1739, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A 40 year old man newly diagnosed with HIV is to start a regimen that includes abacavir. His physician asks the pharmacist which pretreatment test is required to lower the risk of a serious hypersensitivity reaction. The pharmacist recommends testing for the allele:", "options": {"A": "CYP2B6*6, because poor metabolizers accumulate abacavir and then develop serious hypersensitivity reactions", "B": "HLA-B*15:02, and abacavir should be started at half dose if the result is positive", "C": "HLA-B*58:01, which predicts abacavir hypersensitivity and requires a lower dose when the result is positive", "D": "HLA-B*57:01, and abacavir should not be started if the result is positive"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (HLA-B*57:01, and abacavir should not be started if the result is positive): HLA-B*57:01 strongly predicts abacavir hypersensitivity, and testing before starting is standard care, with abacavir avoided in carriers. HLA-B*15:02 relates to carbamazepine and HLA-B*58:01 to allopurinol. Dose reduction does not prevent an immune mediated reaction."}, {"id": 1740, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "A 40 year old on venlafaxine 150 mg daily took tramadol for back pain yesterday. He now has agitation, diarrhea, tremor, inducible clonus and a temperature of 38.9 degrees C. His wife phones the pharmacy. What should the pharmacist advise?", "options": {"A": "Take acetaminophen for the fever and continue venlafaxine, since symptoms from tramadol resolve on their own", "B": "Treat it as stomach flu, drink fluids, and take another tramadol dose for his original pain", "C": "Halve each drug and watch the symptoms at home for two days before deciding anything further", "D": "Stop both serotonergic drugs and go to the emergency department urgently for suspected serotonin syndrome"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Stop both serotonergic drugs and go to the emergency department urgently for suspected serotonin syndrome): Tremor, clonus, agitation, diarrhea and fever after adding tramadol to an SNRI strongly suggest serotonin syndrome, which can progress rapidly. Stopping the drugs and urgent assessment are needed. Reducing doses at home or taking more tramadol would prolong exposure."}, {"id": 1741, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "A pharmacist wants to confirm whether a newly compounded white cream is an oil in water or a water in oil emulsion. A technician places a small drop of the cream on the surface of a beaker of water and the cream disperses readily. What can the pharmacist conclude about the emulsion?", "options": {"A": "It is an oil in water emulsion, because the aqueous external phase mixes with water", "B": "It is a water in oil emulsion, because it feels greasy when rubbed on the skin", "C": "It is a water in oil emulsion, because the oily external phase spreads across water", "D": "It is an oil in water emulsion only if the cream also dissolves in oil"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (It is an oil in water emulsion, because the aqueous external phase mixes with water): An emulsion disperses readily in a liquid that matches its external phase, so a cream that mixes with water has water as the external phase. That makes it an oil in water emulsion, which is also washable from skin. Water in oil emulsions would stay as a separate film on the water surface."}, {"id": 1742, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "At the counter, a home-care nurse hands the pharmacist a bag of loose needles and syringes mixed with unused tablets, because the patient has died. Some needles are uncapped. The pharmacist has a returns bin and a sharps container nearby. What should the pharmacist do first?", "options": {"A": "Ask the nurse to separate everything and then take the sharps away for disposal elsewhere", "B": "Pour the bag into the returns bin and sort the sharps from the tablets later", "C": "Use tongs or a scoop to move the sharps into a sharps container without touching them", "D": "Recap each needle one at a time, then place the capped needles in the returns bin"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Use tongs or a scoop to move the sharps into a sharps container without touching them): Loose uncapped needles are the immediate injury risk, so they are moved into a puncture-resistant container with a tool rather than by hand. Pouring or sorting by hand and recapping both create needle-stick exposure, and sending the nurse away leaves the unsafe bag at the pharmacy."}, {"id": 1743, "domain": "leadership_and_stewardship", "chapter": 11, "question": "HR is a 63 year old woman taking amlodipine 5 mg daily who measures her home blood pressure twice daily for seven days, averaging 128/78 mmHg. At the pharmacy her seated office average is 146/90 mmHg, and she says she feels anxious at appointments. Which interpretation is correct?", "options": {"A": "Office readings are more reliable than home values, so the amlodipine dose should be doubled today", "B": "Home and office readings must match to be valid, so discard both and repeat", "C": "Her pressure is uncontrolled, so add a thiazide diuretic and recheck the office pressure", "D": "Home readings show control, so continue therapy and tell her physician about the white coat effect"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Home readings show control, so continue therapy and tell her physician about the white coat effect): Hypertension Canada treats a home average below 130/80 mmHg as controlled, and a higher office reading with normal home values suggests a white coat effect. Increasing therapy based on the office value alone risks hypotension and adverse effects. The two settings do not need to match, and the physician should receive both sets of values."}, {"id": 1744, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 6, "question": "BA is a 57 year old woman with type 2 diabetes and an A1C of 9.1%, taking metformin and empagliflozin 10 mg daily. She has had three vulvovaginal yeast infections this year and asks for another antifungal. Which drug therapy problem should the pharmacist prioritize in addition to treating this episode?", "options": {"A": "Metformin raises genital mycotic infection risk, so it should be replaced with a sulfonylurea", "B": "Empagliflozin raises genital mycotic infection risk, so therapy should be reassessed with the prescriber", "C": "Recurrent infection signals antifungal resistance, so she needs a longer course of oral terbinafine", "D": "Her infections reflect hygiene habits, so counselling on washing is enough while both drugs continue"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Empagliflozin raises genital mycotic infection risk, so therapy should be reassessed with the prescriber): SGLT2 inhibitors cause glycosuria, which promotes genital yeast infections, and her high A1C adds to the risk. The pharmacist should treat this episode and discuss with the prescriber whether to continue the drug or improve control in another way. Metformin does not cause these infections, and terbinafine is not used for vaginal candidiasis."}, {"id": 1745, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "A pharmacy delivers prescriptions to a long-term care home using its own driver. A staff member suggests leaving the sealed bags at the nursing station without any sign-off to save time. Which practice best protects the integrity and confidentiality of the medications?", "options": {"A": "Give the driver a general list of resident names and ask the driver to match them", "B": "Leave the bags unsigned at the nursing station and trust staff to put them away", "C": "Send a text message to the home each time a delivery has been left", "D": "Obtain a signature from the facility nurse who receives the delivery and keep the record"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Obtain a signature from the facility nurse who receives the delivery and keep the record): A signed handover from a responsible nurse shows who accepted the drugs and when, protecting patients and the pharmacy. Unsigned drop-offs and text messages leave no proof, and giving a driver a resident list spreads private information beyond the people who need it."}, {"id": 1746, "domain": "leadership_and_stewardship", "chapter": 11, "question": "At a diabetes screening event, a 52 year old man who is 1.75 m tall and weighs 98 kg has a waist circumference of 112 cm and a sibling with type 2 diabetes. The pharmacist calculates his body mass index. Which statement about his risk is correct?", "options": {"A": "His BMI is 36 kg/m2, so he should start metformin without further testing", "B": "His BMI is 28 kg/m2, so routine screening again in three years is suitable", "C": "His BMI is 32 kg/m2, so he should have a laboratory glucose or A1c test", "D": "His BMI is 32 kg/m2, but his waist circumference lowers the need for any testing"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (His BMI is 32 kg/m2, so he should have a laboratory glucose or A1c test): BMI is weight divided by height squared, which is 98 divided by 3.0625, or 32 kg/m2. Together with a large waist circumference and a sibling with diabetes, this places him at high risk and warrants a laboratory A1c or fasting glucose. Metformin is not started on a screening estimate, and a wide waist increases rather than lowers risk."}, {"id": 1747, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "HN, a 4 year old, weighs 33 lb at the clinic. A prescription for acetaminophen suspension is written as 15 mg/kg per dose every six hours as needed for fever. The pharmacist converts the weight to kilograms for data entry and dose checking. Which single dose is correct?", "options": {"A": "150 mg per dose, based on a weight of about 10 kg", "B": "225 mg per dose, based on a weight of 15 kg", "C": "495 mg per dose, using the weight in pounds directly in the calculation", "D": "75 mg per dose, based on a weight of about 5 kg"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (225 mg per dose, based on a weight of 15 kg): 33 lb divided by 2.2 gives 15 kg. At 15 mg/kg, the dose is 15 x 15 = 225 mg. Using pounds directly would more than double the dose, and the other weights come from conversion errors."}, {"id": 1748, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 7, "question": "The pharmacist is advising ZN, who will spend seven days on a beach trip and plans to apply sunscreen to her whole body twice daily. She uses 30 mL per application and each bottle contains 200 mL. How many bottles should she bring?", "options": {"A": "One bottle, since a 200 mL bottle is enough for a full week of use", "B": "Five bottles, since each application needs an extra 30 mL for reapplication after swimming", "C": "Three bottles, since the week requires 420 mL and two bottles hold only 400 mL", "D": "Two bottles, since 400 mL covers the planned application schedule with room to spare"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Three bottles, since the week requires 420 mL and two bottles hold only 400 mL): Seven days x two applications x 30 mL equals 420 mL, which is more than two bottles but fits within three bottles (600 mL). One or two bottles would lead to under application and lower protection. Five bottles overestimates the need."}, {"id": 1749, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "KR is a 72 year old man with BPH on tamsulosin and a history of urinary retention requiring a catheter last year. He has nasal congestion for two days and asks for a nonprescription cold product. Which product should the pharmacist recommend?", "options": {"A": "Saline nasal spray, with an intranasal steroid if needed, avoiding oral decongestants and antihistamines", "B": "Phenylephrine with chlorpheniramine combination product, since both ingredients are safe in men with prostate enlargement", "C": "Diphenhydramine 25 mg at bedtime, since sedating antihistamines relax the bladder and improve urine flow", "D": "Pseudoephedrine 60 mg every six hours, because oral decongestants affect the nose and not bladder neck"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Saline nasal spray, with an intranasal steroid if needed, avoiding oral decongestants and antihistamines): Oral sympathomimetic decongestants tighten the bladder neck and first generation antihistamines have anticholinergic effects, and either can precipitate retention in men with BPH. Saline and intranasal steroids act locally. Combination cold products carry both risks."}, {"id": 1750, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "ML is a 55 year old man with chronic pain who takes tramadol 50 mg twice daily and has never used strong opioids. A physician faxes a prescription for a fentanyl 50 mcg/h patch to the community pharmacy. The pharmacist explains why this starting plan is a problem, citing which requirement?", "options": {"A": "Patches are restricted to cancer pain, so a chronic non-cancer indication is not allowed", "B": "Patches should begin at 50 mcg/h for opioid-naive patients, so the strength is correct", "C": "Patches need a documented trial of oxycodone first, since tramadol users cannot convert to fentanyl", "D": "Patches suit only opioid-tolerant patients taking at least 60 mg oral morphine daily for a week"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Patches suit only opioid-tolerant patients taking at least 60 mg oral morphine daily for a week): Fentanyl patches are indicated only for opioid-tolerant patients, generally those taking at least 60 mg of oral morphine or equivalent daily for a week or longer. His tramadol is about 20 MME, so a 50 mcg/h patch, near 120 MME, could cause fatal respiratory depression. The product monograph does not limit patches to cancer pain."}, {"id": 1751, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 4, "question": "HZ is a 38 year old woman who wears a 21 mg nicotine patch and admits she still smoked four cigarettes yesterday. She is afraid that smoking while wearing it is dangerous and wants to stop using the patch. The pharmacist should advise her to:", "options": {"A": "remove the patch for the day, then restart it only when she has stopped smoking", "B": "double up with a second 21 mg patch to remove all urges to smoke", "C": "switch to a lower strength patch immediately, as continued smoking raises nicotine levels too high", "D": "keep wearing the patch, since smoking while using it is not dangerous"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (keep wearing the patch, since smoking while using it is not dangerous): Smoking a few cigarettes while wearing a patch is not dangerous and is a common slip, and removing the patch would leave her without cover for cravings. She should be encouraged to continue and strengthen the plan. Doubling the patch or lowering the strength is not guided by this event."}, {"id": 1752, "domain": "communication_and_collaboration", "chapter": 11, "question": "HG is a 70 year old man who takes levothyroxine daily and often forgets it. He wakes at 6:30 and eats breakfast at 8:00. The pharmacist wants a reminder strategy that fits the drug's administration requirements. Which advice is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Take it with breakfast at 8:00 each day, since eating at the same time aids memory", "B": "Keep it at the bedside, take it on waking, and eat 30 to 60 minutes later", "C": "Take it whenever he remembers during the day and double the next dose if missed", "D": "Take it with his calcium and iron supplements so one cue covers all of them"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Keep it at the bedside, take it on waking, and eat 30 to 60 minutes later): Levothyroxine is best taken on an empty stomach with water, 30 to 60 minutes before food, and linking it to waking gives a reliable cue. Food, calcium and iron reduce its absorption, and doubling doses is not recommended."}, {"id": 1753, "domain": "leadership_and_stewardship", "chapter": 11, "question": "Public health asks a pharmacy to give MMR vaccine to contacts of a measles case after a school exposure 48 hours ago. Four contacts arrive: a healthy 8 year old with two documented doses, a 19 year old with no records or history of disease, a 4 month old, and a pregnant woman without records. Who should the pharmacist vaccinate?", "options": {"A": "The 8 year old, because school contacts should all receive an additional dose after exposure", "B": "The 4 month old, because infants are most vulnerable and the vaccine works at any age", "C": "The 19 year old, since adult contacts without records can be protected within 72 hours", "D": "The pregnant woman, because measles in pregnancy carries higher risk and live vaccine is recommended there"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The 19 year old, since adult contacts without records can be protected within 72 hours): MMR given within 72 hours of exposure can prevent or lessen disease in susceptible contacts, and an adult without records or history is such a contact. The 8 year old has two documented doses and is considered protected. Live vaccine is contraindicated in pregnancy, and a 4 month old is too young for MMR and needs a different post-exposure approach through public health."}, {"id": 1754, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "MN is a 63 year old woman with type 2 diabetes and an A1c of 8.6 percent after maximal metformin plus a sulfonylurea. The prescriber writes for a newer diabetes drug listed as Limited Use, covered after failure of metformin and one other agent. The claim rejects. What should the pharmacist do?", "options": {"A": "Tell her the drug cannot be covered since her A1c remains above the target level", "B": "Bill the plan for the sulfonylurea instead so the newer drug is paid under it", "C": "Submit the request on the prescriber's behalf and sign it as the person prescribing", "D": "Provide the criteria to the prescriber, who can submit the coverage request with her treatment history"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Provide the criteria to the prescriber, who can submit the coverage request with her treatment history): MN appears to meet the stated criteria because she has failed metformin and one other agent, but the request must come from the prescriber with her history. A pharmacist cannot sign as the prescriber, and billing a different drug misrepresents the claim."}, {"id": 1755, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A trial of a statin in 1,200 older adults found 36 cardiovascular events among 600 on the statin and 54 events among 600 on placebo over five years. A pharmacist preparing a journal club presentation is asked to report the relative risk.", "options": {"A": "0.33, the proportion of events prevented relative to the placebo group over the five years", "B": "0.67, the statin group risk of 6% divided by the placebo risk of 9%", "C": "3%, the difference between the two event rates over the five years of follow-up", "D": "1.50, found by dividing the placebo group event rate by the statin group event rate"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (0.67, the statin group risk of 6% divided by the placebo risk of 9%): Risk on the statin is 36 of 600, or 6%, and on placebo is 54 of 600, or 9%. Relative risk is the treated risk divided by the control risk, 0.06 divided by 0.09, which is about 0.67. The 3% figure is the absolute difference, and inverting the ratio gives 1.5, which describes control relative to treatment."}, {"id": 1756, "domain": "leadership_and_stewardship", "chapter": 11, "question": "A healthy 66 year old man who has never received a pneumococcal vaccine asks the pharmacist what is recommended for him. He has no chronic illness, does not smoke, and takes no regular medications. Using current NACI guidance, which approach is appropriate for him?", "options": {"A": "Pneumococcal vaccine is advised only for those with chronic illness, so none is recommended for him", "B": "Pneumococcal polysaccharide 23 valent vaccine alone, repeated every five years until age 75", "C": "Two doses of 20 valent conjugate vaccine, given eight weeks apart to complete the series", "D": "A single dose of 20 valent pneumococcal conjugate vaccine, with no further doses needed"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A single dose of 20 valent pneumococcal conjugate vaccine, with no further doses needed): NACI recommends a single dose of a higher valency pneumococcal conjugate vaccine such as the 20 valent product for adults 65 years and older who have not been vaccinated, regardless of other health conditions. Repeating the polysaccharide vaccine every five years is outdated, and an added conjugate dose is not needed."}, {"id": 1757, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "ZK must earn 24 learning credits in each two year cycle, and at least half must come from accredited programs. After fourteen months, ZK has recorded 9 accredited credits and 5 non-accredited credits. What is the minimum ZK still needs to complete before the cycle ends?", "options": {"A": "Three more accredited credits and seven more credits of any kind, for ten in total", "B": "Ten more accredited credits, because non-accredited credits already recorded cannot be applied toward the total", "C": "Twelve more accredited credits, since the half requirement applies separately to the credits remaining", "D": "Seven more accredited credits, because only the accredited credits count toward reaching half of the cycle"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Three more accredited credits and seven more credits of any kind, for ten in total): The cycle needs 24 credits, so 24 minus 14 recorded leaves 10 more. Half of 24 is 12 accredited, and ZK holds 9, so 3 of the remaining 10 must be accredited and the other 7 can be of any type."}, {"id": 1758, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "A hospital pharmacy wants a key performance indicator that reflects whether its discharge medication reconciliation service improves results and safety for patients going home to community care. Which of these indicators is an outcome measure rather than a process measure?", "options": {"A": "Percentage of discharged patients readmitted within 30 days for a medication-related problem", "B": "Average number of minutes the pharmacist spends completing each discharge reconciliation form per patient", "C": "Percentage of discharged patients who received a reconciliation interview before leaving the hospital", "D": "Number of discharge reconciliation forms completed by pharmacy staff during each month of the year"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Percentage of discharged patients readmitted within 30 days for a medication-related problem): An outcome measure shows the result experienced by patients, such as readmission for a medication related problem. The interview rate, forms completed and minutes per form describe whether and how the service was delivered, which makes them process measures. Outcome measures are harder to move but reveal whether the process actually works."}, {"id": 1759, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "An opioid agonist therapy patient asks the pharmacist directly for weekend take-home doses of methadone, saying the prescriber agreed last month during a visit. The prescription on file shows daily witnessed dosing with no carries. What should the pharmacist do?", "options": {"A": "Decline to dispense any methadone until the patient returns with written confirmation from the prescriber", "B": "Give a single extra dose as a carry and let the patient arrange the rest later", "C": "Dispense the weekend carries now, since the patient says the prescriber agreed during a visit", "D": "Dispense only as written, and contact the prescriber to confirm and obtain a new prescription"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Dispense only as written, and contact the prescriber to confirm and obtain a new prescription): Carries are authorized by the prescriber, so a verbal report from the patient is not enough. The pharmacist continues the dosing as written, contacts the prescriber to clarify, and obtains an amended prescription before releasing any take-home doses. Refusing all dosing would also needlessly interrupt therapy."}, {"id": 1760, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A pharmacy student on a community placement asks how reports of suspected adverse reactions submitted by Canadian health professionals are used by Health Canada. The preceptor explains that the Canada Vigilance Program, which collects these reports for marketed products, primarily serves to:", "options": {"A": "Compensate patients who have been harmed by a marketed product, once causality has been established", "B": "Estimate the exact incidence of each adverse reaction among all patients who take a marketed product", "C": "Detect safety signals after market authorization so that labelling or regulatory action can be taken", "D": "Determine whether individual prescribers acted negligently when they selected a particular drug for a patient"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Detect safety signals after market authorization so that labelling or regulatory action can be taken): Spontaneous reports are pooled and reviewed to find new or changing risks, which can lead to updated monographs, advisories or withdrawals. Because reporting is incomplete and the number of exposed patients is unknown, the data cannot give true incidence. The program does not compensate patients or judge prescribers."}, {"id": 1761, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "CW is a 17 year old girl diagnosed with type 1 diabetes at age 12, with no eye symptoms. Her mother asks when screening for diabetic retinopathy should be started and how often it should continue. What should the pharmacist tell them?", "options": {"A": "She is due now, five years after diagnosis, then every one to two years", "B": "She should wait until age 25, then be screened once every five years afterwards", "C": "She is due now, then every five years if the first examination is normal", "D": "She should be screened only if symptoms appear, then have yearly eye pressure checks"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (She is due now, five years after diagnosis, then every one to two years): In type 1 diabetes screening begins five years after diagnosis for patients aged 15 or older, and repeats every one to two years. She meets both criteria now. Waiting for symptoms or stretching the interval to five years would miss early retinopathy, which is usually silent."}, {"id": 1762, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 3, "question": "A pharmacist working with a community clinic reviews four patients who each present with a different skin infection, noting the examination findings and exposure history for every one of them. Which patient most strongly suggests the need for antibiotic coverage that includes MRSA?", "options": {"A": "A patient with sharply demarcated nonpurulent leg redness and no recent hospital contact", "B": "A patient with a clean surgical wound that is red around the edges but not draining", "C": "A patient with a purulent abscess and a household member with a recent MRSA infection", "D": "A patient with facial erysipelas and a fever, with no draining wound anywhere"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A patient with a purulent abscess and a household member with a recent MRSA infection): Purulence and household MRSA exposure are classic indicators of community MRSA. Nonpurulent cellulitis and erysipelas are usually streptococcal, and a clean red surgical wound without drainage does not point to MRSA."}, {"id": 1763, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "A 46 year old woman has taken semaglutide 0.25 mg once weekly for four weeks for weight management and tolerated it well. Her physician asks the pharmacist what the next step in the standard dose escalation should be. Which is correct?", "options": {"A": "Increase to 1.0 mg weekly now, then to 2.4 mg after a single further week", "B": "Stay on 0.25 mg indefinitely, because the starting dose is already the intended maintenance dose", "C": "Increase to 0.5 mg weekly for four weeks, then continue stepwise escalation", "D": "Jump to the 2.4 mg maintenance dose now, because she tolerated the starting dose well"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Increase to 0.5 mg weekly for four weeks, then continue stepwise escalation): Semaglutide for weight management is escalated in four-week steps from 0.25 mg to 0.5 mg, 1 mg, 1.7 mg and then 2.4 mg to limit gastrointestinal effects. The starting dose is only for tolerability and is not effective for sustained weight loss. Skipping steps raises the chance of severe nausea and vomiting."}, {"id": 1764, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "A 50 year old man has taken propylthiouracil 100 mg three times daily for ten weeks for Graves disease. He phones the pharmacy to report dark urine, yellow eyes and loss of appetite over three days. What is the pharmacist's best advice?", "options": {"A": "Reduce to 50 mg three times daily and recheck his liver enzymes in one month", "B": "Stop propylthiouracil and arrange urgent liver testing because severe hepatotoxicity is a recognized risk", "C": "Increase to 200 mg three times daily, since symptoms suggest his thyroid disease is worsening", "D": "Continue the drug and drink more fluids, since dark urine is a harmless treatment effect"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Stop propylthiouracil and arrange urgent liver testing because severe hepatotoxicity is a recognized risk): Propylthiouracil carries a boxed warning for severe, sometimes fatal liver injury, and jaundice with dark urine and anorexia are the warning signs. The drug must be stopped and liver function tested urgently. Lowering the dose or continuing it while waiting risks progression to liver failure."}, {"id": 1765, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 6, "question": "An 18 kg child with vomiting from gastroenteritis is prescribed a single dose of ondansetron 0.15 mg/kg. The pharmacy stocks ondansetron oral solution 4 mg per 5 mL. How much of the solution should the pharmacist measure for this dose?", "options": {"A": "5.0 mL of the 4 mg/5 mL solution as a single dose", "B": "6.8 mL of the 4 mg/5 mL solution as a single dose", "C": "2.7 mL of the 4 mg/5 mL solution as a single dose", "D": "3.4 mL of the 4 mg/5 mL solution as a single dose"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (3.4 mL of the 4 mg/5 mL solution as a single dose): The dose is 0.15 mg/kg x 18 kg = 2.7 mg. At 4 mg per 5 mL, the volume is 2.7 / 4 x 5 = 3.4 mL. Giving 2.7 mL confuses milligrams with millilitres, and 6.8 mL would double the intended dose."}, {"id": 1766, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "A 57 year old man transferred from a US hospital arrives with a lab printout showing serum creatinine 1.2 mg/dL. The Canadian pharmacist needs the value in umol/L to apply the Cockcroft-Gault equation, knowing that 1 mg/dL equals 88.4 umol/L. What is the converted value?", "options": {"A": "About 74 umol/L, found by dividing 88.4 by 1.2 here", "B": "About 10.6 umol/L, found by multiplying 1.2 by 8.84", "C": "About 0.014 umol/L, found by dividing 1.2 by 88.4 here", "D": "About 106 umol/L, found by multiplying 1.2 by 88.4"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (About 106 umol/L, found by multiplying 1.2 by 88.4): Converting from mg/dL to umol/L means multiplying by 88.4, so 1.2 x 88.4 = 106 umol/L. Entering 1.2 directly into the umol/L version of the equation would overestimate clearance about 88 times."}, {"id": 1767, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 3, "question": "BM is a 62 year old man in intensive care receiving piperacillin-tazobactam and vancomycin together for severe sepsis. His baseline renal function is normal. The pharmacist is setting up monitoring. Which adverse effect risk is increased by combining these two antibiotics, and what monitoring should be done?", "options": {"A": "Hearing loss from the combination, so audiometry should be performed before every dose", "B": "Acute kidney injury, so serum creatinine and urine output should be monitored daily", "C": "Bone marrow suppression, so a complete blood count should be repeated every six hours", "D": "Hepatotoxicity from the combination, so liver enzymes should be measured twice weekly"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Acute kidney injury, so serum creatinine and urine output should be monitored daily): Combining vancomycin with piperacillin-tazobactam is associated with a higher incidence of acute kidney injury than either alone, so renal function and urine output are watched closely. Ototoxicity is tied to aminoglycosides, and liver or marrow toxicity are not the main concern with this pairing."}, {"id": 1768, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "ZF is a 15 year old with plantar warts. The prescription is for 150 g of salicylic acid 3% ointment, and the pharmacy has a 10% salicylic acid ointment and a plain ointment base. How should the pharmacist prepare the product, mixing the two ingredients by weight?", "options": {"A": "50 g of the 10% ointment with 100 g of plain ointment base, mixed well", "B": "15 g of the 10% ointment with 135 g of plain ointment base, mixed well", "C": "30 g of the 10% ointment with 120 g of plain ointment base, mixed well", "D": "45 g of the 10% ointment with 105 g of plain ointment base, mixed well"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (45 g of the 10% ointment with 105 g of plain ointment base, mixed well): The 150 g product needs 3% x 150 = 4.5 g of salicylic acid. A 10% ointment supplies 0.1 g per gram, so 45 g provides 4.5 g. The remaining 105 g is plain base. Fifty grams of stock would give a 3.3% product, a strength that matters for irritation in a teenager."}, {"id": 1769, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "In a hospital ward, a pharmacist is asked about a 73 year old man with potassium 6.9 mmol/L and peaked T waves on ECG. He takes spironolactone and has acute kidney injury. Which should be given first to protect the heart?", "options": {"A": "Regular insulin with dextrose first, since moving potassium into cells must precede membrane stabilization", "B": "Intravenous calcium gluconate, which stabilizes cardiac membranes within minutes without lowering serum potassium", "C": "Intravenous furosemide alone, since urinary losses will lower potassium faster than other therapy", "D": "Oral sodium polystyrene sulfonate, which removes potassium quickly enough to protect the myocardium within minutes"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Intravenous calcium gluconate, which stabilizes cardiac membranes within minutes without lowering serum potassium): Calcium antagonizes the membrane effect of potassium on the myocardium within minutes and is given first when ECG changes are present. Insulin with dextrose and then a binder or dialysis lower the potassium itself. Binders act over hours and furosemide depends on adequate kidney function."}, {"id": 1770, "domain": "leadership_and_stewardship", "chapter": 11, "question": "A 24 year old student has a two day sore throat with temperature 38.2 C, tonsillar exudate, tender anterior cervical nodes, and no cough. He asks the pharmacist whether he needs an antibiotic. Using the Centor criteria, what should the pharmacist recommend?", "options": {"A": "Advise waiting seven days and filling an antibiotic only if symptoms worsen, with no test", "B": "Refer for rapid strep testing or throat culture before any antibiotic is started", "C": "Recommend lozenges and acetaminophen only, because most sore throats with exudate are viral", "D": "Start amoxicillin immediately, since a score of four confirms group A streptococcal infection"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Refer for rapid strep testing or throat culture before any antibiotic is started): He meets all four Centor criteria, which raises the chance of group A streptococcus but does not confirm it, so testing is needed before an antibiotic is prescribed. Testing protects him from unnecessary antibiotics if the result is negative and ensures treatment if it is positive. Symptom care alone or an untested delayed prescription would not establish the diagnosis."}, {"id": 1771, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 3, "question": "SO is a 70 year old man who developed a painful vesicular rash on his right flank eight days ago. New blisters are still appearing today, and he has not yet seen a physician. He has normal renal function. He asks if antiviral treatment is still possible. What should the pharmacist advise?", "options": {"A": "Antivirals no longer help beyond 72 hours, so use only analgesics and calamine lotion now", "B": "Antivirals help only if started with oral corticosteroids, so wait for a steroid prescription first", "C": "Treatment is useful only for eye involvement, so no antiviral is needed for flank zoster", "D": "Antiviral treatment can still help since new lesions are forming, so arrange prompt assessment"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Antiviral treatment can still help since new lesions are forming, so arrange prompt assessment): The 72 hour window is a guide, and antivirals are still recommended beyond it when new lesions continue to appear, since viral replication is ongoing. Older age also raises the risk of postherpetic neuralgia, which supports treating. Corticosteroids are not a prerequisite, and treatment is not limited to ophthalmic zoster."}, {"id": 1772, "domain": "leadership_and_stewardship", "chapter": 11, "question": "A 36 year old man buying cigarettes says he enjoys smoking, is not ready to quit, but does not mind the pharmacist asking. The pharmacist wants to apply a brief motivational approach to tobacco use. Which response best fits this approach?", "options": {"A": "List the health risks of smoking in detail so he understands the urgency of quitting now", "B": "End the conversation and avoid raising smoking again, because pressure will damage the relationship", "C": "Offer brief personally relevant information, keep the door open, and ask again later", "D": "Provide a prescription cessation product anyway, so that he has it when he decides to quit"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Offer brief personally relevant information, keep the door open, and ask again later): For a person who is not ready to quit, brief motivational counselling links smoking to what matters to him, respects his autonomy, and invites future discussion, which raises later quit attempts. Detailed lecturing tends to increase resistance, and giving a prescription product without readiness wastes it. Dropping the topic entirely misses repeated opportunities to prompt change."}, {"id": 1773, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "RD is a 67 year old man whose pharmacist is deciding how to respond to a request about his current medications. In most provinces, a pharmacist may adapt an existing prescription. Which change would be an adaptation rather than prescribing a new drug?", "options": {"A": "Changing the tablet strength and number of tablets per dose, keeping the same total daily dose", "B": "Adding a new drug to treat a side effect caused by the current regimen he takes", "C": "Prescribing a drug for a new condition that he has just described to the pharmacist", "D": "Replacing the drug with a different drug from another class for the same condition he has"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Changing the tablet strength and number of tablets per dose, keeping the same total daily dose): Adaptation modifies an existing prescription, such as its strength, dosage form, regimen or quantity, for the same drug and the same purpose. Switching drugs, adding drugs or treating a new condition are separate prescribing authorities with their own requirements."}, {"id": 1774, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 4, "question": "A 26 year old woman has fever, skin pain and erosions in her mouth. Her medications are lamotrigine started two weeks ago, levothyroxine for five years, ibuprofen as needed and a daily multivitamin. Which drug is the most likely culprit?", "options": {"A": "Ibuprofen, since any as-needed drug causes more reactions than one started two weeks ago", "B": "Levothyroxine, since thyroid hormone replacement is a common cause of blistering skin and mucosal eruptions", "C": "The multivitamin, since daily supplements commonly cause fever and erosions of the mouth and skin", "D": "Lamotrigine, because severe skin reactions typically appear within eight weeks of starting therapy"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Lamotrigine, because severe skin reactions typically appear within eight weeks of starting therapy): Lamotrigine is a well-known cause of Stevens-Johnson syndrome, with onset usually within two to eight weeks of starting or after a rapid dose increase. Long-term stable drugs such as levothyroxine are very unlikely culprits. The timing and mucosal involvement make a recent new drug the focus."}, {"id": 1775, "domain": "leadership_and_stewardship", "chapter": 11, "question": "A pharmacy staff member asks how to recognize an opioid overdose after a customer collapsed outside the store. She wants to know which combination of findings most strongly suggests an opioid overdose that needs naloxone and an urgent call for emergency help.", "options": {"A": "Sleepiness that improves when the person is shaken, with normal breathing and regular speech", "B": "Chest pain, sweating, and jaw discomfort in a person who also reports recent drug use", "C": "Unresponsive, slow or absent breathing, blue or grey lips, and pinpoint pupils", "D": "Agitation, rapid breathing, dilated pupils, and flushed warm skin after recent drug use"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Unresponsive, slow or absent breathing, blue or grey lips, and pinpoint pupils): Opioids depress breathing and consciousness, producing unresponsiveness, slow or absent respirations, cyanosis or grey skin, and small pupils. Agitation with dilated pupils suggests stimulant toxicity, and chest pain points to a cardiac event. A person who rouses and breathes normally is sedated but not in overdose."}, {"id": 1776, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "ST is a 48 year old woman who asks for a renewal of her ramipril 10 mg, which expired last week. Her pharmacy blood pressure reading is 192/118 mmHg, and she reports a severe headache and blurred vision since this morning. What is the pharmacist's best action?", "options": {"A": "Renew for a short supply and schedule a follow-up visit with her doctor next week", "B": "Refer her to emergency care now for possible hypertensive emergency instead of completing a routine renewal", "C": "Renew the ramipril for six months and advise her to recheck her pressure at home tonight", "D": "Renew and increase to 20 mg daily to bring the pressure down faster before bedtime"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Refer her to emergency care now for possible hypertensive emergency instead of completing a routine renewal): A very high blood pressure with headache and visual changes suggests end-organ involvement and needs urgent assessment. Renewing the medication does not address the emergency, and a dose increase cannot be monitored safely at home. The refill can be dealt with after she is assessed."}, {"id": 1777, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "FD, a 58 year old man, arrives to refill his regular prescription for a medication now on national shortage. The pharmacy has a limited supply. He asks why he cannot get his usual 90 day supply. What should the pharmacist say?", "options": {"A": "Dispense the full 90 days to him because he is an established patient of the pharmacy", "B": "Refer him to another pharmacy instead of dispensing the limited stock held in your pharmacy", "C": "Tell him the pharmacy has chosen not to stock the product because of the manufacturer's price", "D": "Explain the shortage and dispense a shorter supply so stock is shared fairly"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Explain the shortage and dispense a shorter supply so stock is shared fairly): Honest explanation of the shortage and a reduced supply let more patients receive therapy and keep him informed about when he will need another fill. Giving a false reason is dishonest, giving one patient the full supply disadvantages others, and sending him away when stock is available abandons care."}, {"id": 1778, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 1, "question": "AL is a 49 year old woman who comes to the pharmacy clinic at 3 pm for a cholesterol check ordered by her physician as part of cardiovascular screening. She ate lunch two hours ago and asks whether the sample will be useless. She has no history of very high triglycerides. How should the pharmacist respond?", "options": {"A": "A non-fasting sample is acceptable for screening and risk assessment, so testing can proceed", "B": "Non-fasting results overestimate HDL, so only the triglyceride value can be trusted from this sample", "C": "The result will be valid only if she drinks water and avoids all fat until testing", "D": "Fasting for twelve hours is needed to measure LDL accurately, so she should return tomorrow morning"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A non-fasting sample is acceptable for screening and risk assessment, so testing can proceed): The Canadian Cardiovascular Society accepts non-fasting lipid samples for screening and risk assessment, because LDL, HDL and total cholesterol change little after a meal. Fasting is requested mainly when triglycerides are very high, and a repeat fasting test can follow then. Asking her to return would only delay assessment."}, {"id": 1779, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "A 66 year old man with glaucoma is prescribed dorzolamide 2% ophthalmic solution with the directions 'i gtt OD tid'. The pharmacy technician prepares the label and asks the pharmacist to verify the meaning of the directions. Which label wording is correct?", "options": {"A": "Instil one drop into the left eye three times a day, spaced evenly through the day", "B": "Instil one drop into both eyes three times a day, spaced evenly through the day", "C": "Instil one drop into the right eye once a day, since OD means once daily", "D": "Instil one drop into the right eye three times a day, spaced evenly"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Instil one drop into the right eye three times a day, spaced evenly): OD means the right eye (oculus dexter), gtt means drop and tid means three times daily. OS is the left eye and OU is both eyes. OD is itself a risky abbreviation because it can be misread as once daily, so writing right eye in full on the label is safest."}, {"id": 1780, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "TR is a 63 year old man who wants to confirm that his new pharmacist is licensed and learn whether any discipline findings exist. He asks the pharmacy assistant where to look. Where should the assistant direct him to check registration status and findings of professional misconduct?", "options": {"A": "The provincial pharmacists' association membership directory, which lists disciplinary decisions for each member", "B": "The provincial college's online public register of pharmacists, pharmacies and discipline decisions", "C": "The Health Canada Drug Product Database, which includes registration details for each practising pharmacist", "D": "The pharmacy's own business licence posted at the dispensary, which shows each pharmacist's discipline record"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The provincial college's online public register of pharmacists, pharmacies and discipline decisions): Colleges maintain a public register showing each member's registration status and conditions, and many include discipline outcomes. Associations are voluntary membership groups, the Drug Product Database lists drugs, and a business licence concerns the premises."}, {"id": 1781, "domain": "communication_and_collaboration", "chapter": 11, "question": "A patient with hypertension says, 'I know I should take the pills, but I hate how tired they make me.' The pharmacist wants to use a reflective listening statement in response. Which is the best example of a reflection in motivational interviewing?", "options": {"A": "You must keep taking them because the risk of stroke is much higher without them", "B": "Have you thought about taking the tablets at bedtime so the tiredness bothers you less?", "C": "Tiredness is a common effect, and most patients find that it settles within two weeks", "D": "You want to look after your blood pressure, but the tiredness is hard to live with"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (You want to look after your blood pressure, but the tiredness is hard to live with): A reflection restates both sides of the patient's ambivalence without advice or judgement. The question offers a solution, the third is a directive, and the last gives reassurance that dismisses the feeling."}, {"id": 1782, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "Reports from a trial of a new antidepressant give an NNH of 12 for nausea and an NNH of 150 for seizures. A pharmacist counselling a 26 year old man starting the drug wants to describe which adverse effect is more likely. Which statement is correct?", "options": {"A": "Nausea is more likely, since a smaller NNH means harm occurs in more patients", "B": "Seizures are more likely because rarer events are listed with the larger number needed to harm", "C": "Both are equally likely because an NNH describes the severity of harm rather than frequency", "D": "Seizures are more likely, since a larger NNH means that harm occurs in more patients"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Nausea is more likely, since a smaller NNH means harm occurs in more patients): A smaller NNH means fewer patients need to be treated before one is harmed, so the event is more common. Nausea at 12 is far more common than seizures at 150. A larger NNH signals rarer harm, and an NNH describes frequency, not severity."}, {"id": 1783, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "A prescription for a fluticasone and salmeterol dry powder inhaler arrives for PT, 12. The pharmacy stocks another manufacturer's product that contains the same drugs but uses a different inhaler device. The software lists the device-based product as not interchangeable. What should the pharmacist do?", "options": {"A": "Substitute the other product and tell PT to use the old technique with the new device", "B": "Contact the prescriber, since a different device needs a new order and teaching", "C": "Dispense the other product and ask the school nurse to check technique at the next term", "D": "Dispense a different strength of the original product and adjust the number of puffs"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Contact the prescriber, since a different device needs a new order and teaching): A different device changes how the dose is delivered and requires a different inhalation technique, so it is not a simple substitution when the product is not listed as interchangeable. The pharmacist seeks a new order and teaches the new device to the child and parent. Changing the strength or the number of puffs would alter the prescribed regimen without authority."}, {"id": 1784, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "RM has been on methadone 80 mg daily and has not attended for 4 consecutive days because of a hospital admission for another reason. He arrives and asks for his usual 80 mg now, saying he feels fine. No prescriber is on site. What should the pharmacist do?", "options": {"A": "Dispense the full 80 mg because he tolerated it before and says that he feels fine", "B": "Dispense his usual dose and ask him to return tomorrow to see his prescriber", "C": "Dispense half of the dose and observe him for an hour before he goes home", "D": "Hold the dose and contact the prescriber for reassessment, since tolerance may have fallen"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Hold the dose and contact the prescriber for reassessment, since tolerance may have fallen): After several missed days opioid tolerance can fall, so the previous dose may cause overdose. The prescriber should reassess before dosing resumes. Feeling well does not show tolerance, and an arbitrary half dose or giving the full dose and following up later lacks prescriber direction."}, {"id": 1785, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "PD is a 77 year old woman who has received a special authorization drug for two years, and the approval expired yesterday. The claim for her monthly supply rejects, and the pharmacy closes soon before the weekend. The drug controls a serious chronic condition. What should the pharmacist do?", "options": {"A": "Dispense one dose only, since the pharmacy cannot supply without an active authorization on file", "B": "Send her home without the drug until the authorization is renewed next week by the plan", "C": "Contact the prescriber about renewing the authorization, and arrange an emergency supply to continue therapy", "D": "Rebill the claim under a similar covered drug's DIN so that payment is accepted"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Contact the prescriber about renewing the authorization, and arrange an emergency supply to continue therapy): An expired authorization needs a renewal request from the prescriber, but a lapse in therapy for a serious condition may harm the patient, so the pharmacist provides a bridging supply. Billing another DIN is fraudulent, and sending her home leaves her untreated."}, {"id": 1786, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 3, "question": "SK is a 66 year old woman recovering from a first C. difficile episode. She takes omeprazole 20 mg daily for mild heartburn, with no history of ulcers, bleeding or Barrett esophagus. She asks how to lower her chance of another episode. What should the pharmacist recommend?", "options": {"A": "Add a probiotic and continue the proton pump inhibitor, since probiotics prevent recurrence reliably", "B": "Increase the proton pump inhibitor dose to protect her stomach during any further antibiotic therapy", "C": "Switch to a high dose H2 blocker, since H2 blockers carry no C. difficile risk", "D": "Review whether the proton pump inhibitor is still needed, and stop it if no indication"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Review whether the proton pump inhibitor is still needed, and stop it if no indication): Acid suppression is associated with a higher risk of C. difficile and recurrence, so stopping an unneeded proton pump inhibitor is a sound step. Raising the dose adds risk. Probiotics lack proven benefit for preventing recurrence, and H2 blockers have been linked with some risk as well."}, {"id": 1787, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "In a two year smoking cessation trial, most participants who were lost to follow-up were still smoking, and the analysis included only those who answered the final telephone survey. Which bias is most likely to inflate the reported quit rate?", "options": {"A": "Publication bias, because trials with high quit rates are more likely to be accepted by journals", "B": "Attrition bias, because those lost to follow-up differed systematically from those who remained in the study", "C": "Performance bias, because staff provided different counselling to participants depending on their smoking status", "D": "Recall bias, because participants who quit remember their smoking history more accurately than those who continued"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Attrition bias, because those lost to follow-up differed systematically from those who remained in the study): When the participants who drop out have different outcomes from those who remain, analyzing only completers overstates success. Here continuing smokers were less likely to answer, so the observed quit rate is too high. Methods such as intention to treat with sensitivity analyses help address this."}, {"id": 1788, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 6, "question": "BQ is a 78 year old woman with chronic kidney disease and an eGFR of 22 mL/min/1.73 m2 who asks for a laxative for occasional constipation. She has been using magnesium hydroxide 30 mL daily for several weeks. Which advice is best?", "options": {"A": "Continue magnesium hydroxide but take it with more water to prevent accumulation of the ion", "B": "Use mineral oil each morning, a stool lubricant with no renal clearance concerns", "C": "Change to a sodium phosphate enema daily, which is cleared easily by damaged kidneys", "D": "Switch to polyethylene glycol, because magnesium can accumulate when kidney function is reduced"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Switch to polyethylene glycol, because magnesium can accumulate when kidney function is reduced): Magnesium is renally cleared and can cause hypermagnesemia in advanced kidney disease, so it should be avoided. Polyethylene glycol is not absorbed and is a safer osmotic laxative. Sodium phosphate products can cause phosphate nephropathy, and mineral oil carries a risk of aspiration."}, {"id": 1789, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 4, "question": "JD is a 44 year old liver transplant recipient taking tacrolimus immediate release 2.5 mg twice daily with stable troughs. His team converts him to the once daily extended release capsule to improve adherence. The usual starting conversion is one to one by total daily milligrams. What daily dose does the pharmacist expect in the order?", "options": {"A": "7.5 mg once daily, because extended release products need a 50% higher dose to match exposure", "B": "2.5 mg once daily, because each dose is converted separately and only the morning dose continues", "C": "3.75 mg once daily, because extended release products are converted at a 25% lower dose", "D": "5 mg once daily, because the total daily milligram dose stays the same at first"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (5 mg once daily, because the total daily milligram dose stays the same at first): The conversion is made milligram for milligram on total daily dose, so 2.5 mg twice daily becomes 5 mg once daily. Troughs are then rechecked within about a week and adjusted. A reduction or increase at conversion is not standard, and half the daily dose would underexpose the graft."}, {"id": 1790, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "A technician must measure 8 mL of glycerin for a compounded mouthwash. The pharmacy has graduated cylinders of 10 mL, 25 mL, 50 mL and 100 mL. The technician reaches for the 100 mL cylinder because it is clean and nearest the sink. The pharmacist reviews the plan for accuracy of the measurement. Which cylinder should be used?", "options": {"A": "The 100 mL cylinder, since its wide base steadies viscous liquids and eases reading the meniscus", "B": "The 25 mL cylinder, since a larger cylinder leaves room to mix and avoids glycerin spills", "C": "The 10 mL cylinder, since the smallest cylinder that holds the volume limits measurement error", "D": "The 50 mL cylinder, because graduated cylinders are accurate to one percent of their full capacity"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The 10 mL cylinder, since the smallest cylinder that holds the volume limits measurement error): A graduated cylinder is most accurate when the measured volume falls within the lower part of its range, so the smallest cylinder that can hold 8 mL is the 10 mL one. Error is roughly proportional to the size of the graduations, which are much coarser on a 100 mL cylinder. Viscous liquids such as glycerin should also be allowed to drain fully."}, {"id": 1791, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "PJ, a 34 year old woman, has a prednisone 5 mg tablet prescription: 60 mg daily for 5 days, then reduce by 10 mg every 3 days until stopped, with a last dose of 10 mg. During verification, how many tablets are required for the full course?", "options": {"A": "150 tablets, because the full course adds up to 750 mg", "B": "210 tablets, because every step including 60 mg lasts five days", "C": "126 tablets, because every step including 60 mg lasts three days", "D": "90 tablets, because the taper steps alone add up to 450 mg"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (150 tablets, because the full course adds up to 750 mg): The first phase uses 60 mg for 5 days, which is 300 mg. The taper then uses 50, 40, 30, 20 and 10 mg for 3 days each, which is 150 mg x 3 = 450 mg. The total of 750 mg is 150 tablets of 5 mg."}, {"id": 1792, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 4, "question": "JU is a 50 year old man using a 21 mg nicotine patch daily in week two. He reports vivid dreams and trouble sleeping but has no skin reaction and is not smoking. He asks what to do because he does not want to stop the patch. The pharmacist should recommend:", "options": {"A": "removing the patch at bedtime and applying a fresh one each morning as usual", "B": "applying the patch at bedtime instead, which keeps nicotine levels highest during sleep", "C": "moving to the 7 mg patch now, because sleep disturbance shows the dose is too high", "D": "stopping the patch for two nights, then restarting at the same 21 mg strength"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (removing the patch at bedtime and applying a fresh one each morning as usual): Vivid dreams and insomnia from a 24 hour patch are common, and removing it at bedtime lessens them while keeping daytime protection against cravings. Jumping to a lower strength risks relapse since he is still in the initial step. Applying it at night would worsen the problem."}, {"id": 1793, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A pharmacist is classifying the evidence for a drug's effect on mortality using the GRADE approach for a hospital formulary monograph. A colleague asks how certainty is rated at the start and how it can then change. Which statement is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "Systematic reviews start at high certainty irrespective of the quality of the trials they pool", "B": "Randomized trials start at high certainty, and ratings fall for problems such as inconsistency", "C": "All evidence starts at moderate certainty and moves up or down by the journal's impact factor", "D": "Observational studies start at high certainty when samples are large, and ratings fall only for bias"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Randomized trials start at high certainty, and ratings fall for problems such as inconsistency): Under GRADE, randomized trials begin at high certainty and observational studies begin at low certainty. Certainty can be lowered for risk of bias, inconsistency, indirectness, imprecision or publication bias. A review inherits the certainty of the studies it pools, not a separate starting level."}, {"id": 1794, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "Direct-sales wellness products are sold by pharmacist ZB, who invites patients, including GR, a 70 year old on a limited income, to home sales parties at ZB's house. GR feels obliged to buy to keep ZB's goodwill. What boundary concern does this raise?", "options": {"A": "Pharmacists cannot sell non-drug products to patients unless the college approves each sale in writing", "B": "Using the relationship for personal financial gain exploits the power imbalance of vulnerable patients", "C": "The home party is a customer service matter for a pharmacist, not a professional boundary concern", "D": "Selling products outside the pharmacy is a licence violation only if the products require a prescription"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Using the relationship for personal financial gain exploits the power imbalance of vulnerable patients): Selling products to patients through personal social events uses a position of trust for financial gain, and a patient with limited income may feel unable to refuse. That exploits the power imbalance and is a boundary problem. Pharmacists may sell non-drug products, so a licence rule is not the issue."}, {"id": 1795, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A small open-label trial reports that a new analgesic reduced patient-reported pain scores more than usual care, with no blinding of participants or assessors. A pharmacist is asked how much weight to give this result when comparing treatments for chronic low back pain in a 52 year old man. Which appraisal is best?", "options": {"A": "Subjective outcomes in unblinded trials are prone to bias, so the benefit needs cautious interpretation", "B": "Patient-reported pain scores are hard endpoints, so the lack of blinding does not affect reliability", "C": "The result is a surrogate finding, so mortality data are needed before drawing a firm conclusion", "D": "The result is reliable because an open-label design ensures that participants know which treatment they received"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Subjective outcomes in unblinded trials are prone to bias, so the benefit needs cautious interpretation): Pain scores are subjective, and when participants and assessors know the treatment, expectations can shift reported scores toward the new drug. Blinding protects against this bias, so an unblinded benefit deserves caution. Pain relief is a legitimate patient outcome rather than a surrogate, but it is vulnerable to bias in this design."}, {"id": 1796, "domain": "communication_and_collaboration", "chapter": 11, "question": "A pharmacist counsels a blind patient who has just started a new tablet. The patient lives alone and wants to identify each container without help from others. Which method would be most helpful for safe independent use at home each day?", "options": {"A": "Ask a neighbour to check the containers each morning and read the labels aloud to him", "B": "Use tactile markings such as rubber bands or braille, plus audio labels if available", "C": "Provide a larger printed label with high contrast colours on every container", "D": "Send a list of the medications by email for him to review on a screen"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Use tactile markings such as rubber bands or braille, plus audio labels if available): Tactile and audio identifiers let a blind patient identify containers independently. Large print and contrast help only people with low vision, and relying on others reduces independence and privacy."}, {"id": 1797, "domain": "communication_and_collaboration", "chapter": 11, "question": "A busy pharmacy manager asks pharmacists to complete tomorrow's vaccination consent and counselling notes in advance for the booked appointments so that patients can be processed faster. The notes would state that risks and benefits were discussed. How should the pharmacist respond?", "options": {"A": "Decline, explaining that notes must record only what actually happened at the time of the encounter", "B": "Agree, because the notes can be edited tomorrow if anything differs from the planned counselling", "C": "Agree for patients who have been vaccinated before, since the counselling is expected to be identical", "D": "Agree only if each patient signs a paper consent in advance confirming that counselling will occur"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Decline, explaining that notes must record only what actually happened at the time of the encounter): A health record must be an accurate, contemporaneous account of care actually provided. Documenting counselling that has not yet occurred is falsification, even if edits are planned later, and it could hide a missed discussion. Prior vaccination or advance signatures do not make a statement about a future conversation true."}, {"id": 1798, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 4, "question": "JW is a 40 year old cook with chronic hand dermatitis who washes his hands many times each shift. He uses betamethasone valerate 0.1% ointment during flares. He asks how to prevent relapses between courses and which measure is most useful. What should the pharmacist recommend?", "options": {"A": "Hot water hand washing with an antibacterial soap to reduce skin bacteria load on the hands", "B": "Continuing the potent steroid daily as maintenance to prevent any flare from returning at work", "C": "Frequent emollient use, including after every hand wash, with protective gloves for wet tasks", "D": "Wearing occlusive latex gloves for the entire shift, which keeps the hands warm and moist"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Frequent emollient use, including after every hand wash, with protective gloves for wet tasks): Irritant hand dermatitis is driven by barrier damage from wet work, so frequent emollient use and protective gloves for wet tasks prevent relapse. Hot water and antibacterial soaps strip the barrier, daily potent steroid causes atrophy, and gloves worn for a full shift trap moisture and add irritation."}, {"id": 1799, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 1, "question": "ZP is a 67 year old man with peripheral artery disease and a known bilateral renal artery stenosis found on imaging. His pressure is 164/90 mmHg and his creatinine is 118 umol/L. His physician prescribes enalapril 5 mg daily. The pharmacist reviews the choice before dispensing. What is the best recommendation?", "options": {"A": "Change enalapril to an ARB such as losartan, which spares kidney function in this setting", "B": "Suggest a dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker instead, since enalapril may sharply reduce kidney function", "C": "Dispense enalapril and recheck creatinine in six months, as renal artery stenosis does not alter safety", "D": "Change enalapril to a beta-blocker alone, since it is the best class for vascular disease"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Suggest a dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker instead, since enalapril may sharply reduce kidney function): In bilateral renal artery stenosis, glomerular filtration depends on angiotensin II constriction of the efferent arteriole, so ACE inhibitors and ARBs can precipitate acute kidney injury. Both classes share the risk, so switching between them does not help. A dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker lowers pressure without this effect."}, {"id": 1800, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "In a one year trial of an anticoagulant, major bleeding occurred in 3.0% of patients on the drug and 1.5% of patients on placebo. A pharmacist wants to express the harm as the number needed to harm. What is the NNH?", "options": {"A": "About 44, from 1 divided by the average of the two event rates (2.25 percent)", "B": "About 33, from the reciprocal of the drug group bleeding rate of 3.0 percent alone", "C": "About 67, from an absolute risk increase of 1.5 percentage points over one year", "D": "About 2, from the ratio of the two bleeding rates (3.0 divided by 1.5)"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (About 67, from an absolute risk increase of 1.5 percentage points over one year): The absolute risk increase is 3.0% minus 1.5%, or 1.5 percentage points. The NNH is 1 divided by 0.015, which is about 67 patients treated for a year for one additional major bleed. The ratio of 2 is the relative risk, not a number of patients."}, {"id": 1801, "domain": "communication_and_collaboration", "chapter": 11, "question": "ZH, a 70 year old man, has just been shown how to dial and inject a dose of insulin glargine 14 units with a pen. He nods and says he understands. The pharmacist wants to confirm understanding. Which approach best uses the teach-back method?", "options": {"A": "Give him a printed pamphlet with pictures and ask him to read it before he leaves", "B": "Repeat the full instructions a second time more slowly and give him a printed copy", "C": "Ask ZH to demonstrate priming, dialling 14 units and injecting using a training pen", "D": "Ask ZH whether he has any questions and accept his answer if he says no"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Ask ZH to demonstrate priming, dialling 14 units and injecting using a training pen): Teach-back asks the patient to show or explain the information in his own words, so the pharmacist can find and correct gaps. Having him perform the steps with a training pen confirms practical skill. Asking about questions, repeating the same explanation or handing out a pamphlet does not verify understanding."}, {"id": 1802, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "RQ is a 67 year old man with Parkinson disease taking rasagiline 1 mg daily. He comes to the pharmacy with a cough and picks up a cough syrup containing dextromethorphan. The pharmacist notes the interaction. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Advise taking rasagiline at night and the syrup in the morning to avoid contact between them", "B": "Allow the syrup but halve the dose, since toxicity develops only with higher quantities", "C": "Allow the syrup, since selective MAO-B inhibitors do not interact with any cough or cold ingredient", "D": "Avoid the dextromethorphan product, as combining it with an MAO-B inhibitor may cause serotonin syndrome"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Avoid the dextromethorphan product, as combining it with an MAO-B inhibitor may cause serotonin syndrome): Rasagiline labelling advises against combining it with dextromethorphan because of the risk of serotonin syndrome and other serious reactions. Lower doses and spacing the drugs apart do not remove the pharmacologic interaction. A cough remedy without dextromethorphan, such as honey or a different suppressant chosen with the prescriber, is safer."}, {"id": 1803, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 3, "question": "TL is a 36 year old woman with genital herpes who has had seven recurrences in the past year, each lasting about a week. She has normal renal function and is not pregnant. She asks about reducing the number of outbreaks. Which regimen should the pharmacist suggest to her physician?", "options": {"A": "Valacyclovir 500 mg twice daily for three days at the start of each outbreak only", "B": "Acyclovir 200 mg once daily taken during outbreaks and stopped when lesions have healed", "C": "Valacyclovir 500 mg once daily as suppressive therapy, with reassessment after about a year", "D": "Valacyclovir 2 g twice daily for one day, taken at each outbreak and stopped after"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Valacyclovir 500 mg once daily as suppressive therapy, with reassessment after about a year): Six or more recurrences a year justify daily suppressive therapy, which cuts recurrences by most patients. Valacyclovir 500 mg once daily is a standard suppressive dose, reviewed yearly. The other regimens are episodic and treat single outbreaks without lowering how often they occur."}, {"id": 1804, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "AF was dispensed 28 capsules of doxycycline 100 mg from a recalled lot, to take twice daily starting the morning of day 1. The pharmacist phones on the evening of day 3, after both of that day's doses were taken, and asks how many capsules remain to be returned. How many does AF have left?", "options": {"A": "20 capsules, counting a fourth day of twice daily doses that has not happened yet", "B": "24 capsules, counting only two full days of twice daily doses as already taken", "C": "26 capsules, counting only one dose taken on each of the first two days", "D": "22 capsules, because six doses have been taken over the three days"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (22 capsules, because six doses have been taken over the three days): Twice daily for three full days is six capsules taken, so 28 minus 6 leaves 22. The other totals come from counting too many days or too few doses. Knowing the true remaining count helps the pharmacist confirm the patient returns every capsule of the recalled lot."}, {"id": 1805, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 4, "question": "LK is a 42 year old heart transplant recipient taking cyclosporine and mycophenolate. Feeling low for two weeks, she began St. John's wort 300 mg three times daily from a health food store and asks whether it is acceptable. Her latest cyclosporine trough was within range. What should the pharmacist advise?", "options": {"A": "Stop it because it lowers cyclosporine levels and risks rejection, then arrange a mood assessment", "B": "Switch to a 150 mg strength three times daily, which is the dose with fewer interactions", "C": "Continue it, and recheck the cyclosporine trough only if she reports tremor or kidney symptoms", "D": "Continue it but reduce the cyclosporine dose by half, since St. John's wort raises its level"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Stop it because it lowers cyclosporine levels and risks rejection, then arrange a mood assessment): St. John's wort induces CYP3A4 and P-glycoprotein, lowering cyclosporine levels and causing rejection, including fatal graft loss. It should be stopped and her low mood assessed by the team for a safer treatment. Halving cyclosporine would worsen underexposure, and a lower strength still induces enzymes."}, {"id": 1806, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "A 77 year old woman has taken alendronate for seven years. For the past three weeks she has had a dull, aching pain in her left thigh, with no injury. She asks the pharmacist if it is just arthritis. What is the best advice?", "options": {"A": "Double her alendronate dose for a month, because the pain shows her bone is unprotected", "B": "Treat it as arthritis with acetaminophen and heat, and mention it at the next checkup", "C": "Take extra calcium and vitamin D for two months, since low intake usually causes it", "D": "Report thigh pain promptly, as it may signal an atypical femur fracture needing imaging"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Report thigh pain promptly, as it may signal an atypical femur fracture needing imaging): Long-term bisphosphonate use is linked to atypical femur fractures, which often announce themselves with weeks of dull thigh or groin pain before a break occurs. Prompt imaging allows treatment to be stopped and the bone protected. Dismissing it as arthritis or increasing the dose could worsen the injury."}, {"id": 1807, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 1, "question": "HT is a 73 year old man whose ramipril was increased from 5 mg to 10 mg daily one week ago for heart failure. Baseline creatinine was 100 umol/L and potassium 4.6 mmol/L. Today creatinine is 125 umol/L and potassium is 4.9 mmol/L. He feels well with a blood pressure of 118/72 mmHg. What is the appropriate recommendation?", "options": {"A": "Hold ramipril for one week then restart at 2.5 mg, since potassium is above 4.5 mmol/L", "B": "Return to ramipril 5 mg and stop the increase, since any creatinine rise signals kidney damage", "C": "Stop ramipril and avoid renin angiotensin system drugs because of the 25% rise in creatinine", "D": "Continue ramipril unchanged and recheck creatinine and potassium in one to two weeks"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Continue ramipril unchanged and recheck creatinine and potassium in one to two weeks): A creatinine rise of up to 30% and potassium up to 5.5 mmol/L after starting or increasing an ACE inhibitor is expected and acceptable, reflecting lower glomerular pressure. Stopping or reducing the drug removes mortality benefit without cause. A larger rise or higher potassium would call for a dose review."}, {"id": 1808, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 3, "question": "AB is a 57 year old man prescribed pantoprazole 40 mg, clarithromycin 500 mg and amoxicillin 1 g, each twice daily for 14 days, for H. pylori. He also takes simvastatin 40 mg at bedtime for dyslipidemia. What is the pharmacist's best action?", "options": {"A": "Switch the PPI to omeprazole, since pantoprazole is the drug that interacts with simvastatin", "B": "Reduce clarithromycin to 250 mg twice daily, which removes the interaction with simvastatin and still eradicates", "C": "Hold the simvastatin during clarithromycin therapy, or ask the prescriber for a different regimen", "D": "Dispense as written, since a 14 day course is too short to raise simvastatin levels"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Hold the simvastatin during clarithromycin therapy, or ask the prescriber for a different regimen): Clarithromycin is a strong CYP3A4 inhibitor, and simvastatin with it raises statin exposure and the risk of myopathy and rhabdomyolysis. The combination is contraindicated, so the statin is paused for the short course or a bismuth based regimen is requested instead. A short course does not remove the risk, and lowering the clarithromycin dose does not reliably avoid the interaction."}, {"id": 1809, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A study of herbal supplement use and kidney injury relies on pharmacy dispensing records, which do not capture supplements bought over the counter. Many participants used supplements that are absent from the records. Which problem is most likely to affect the exposure data?", "options": {"A": "Publication bias, because dispensing records are summarized only in studies reporting a positive association", "B": "Confounding by indication, because supplements are chosen according to the severity of the kidney disease", "C": "Information bias from exposure misclassification, because over the counter use is missing from the records", "D": "Selection bias from loss to follow-up, because participants who bought supplements elsewhere left the study early"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Information bias from exposure misclassification, because over the counter use is missing from the records): Exposure measured from a source that misses real use classifies some exposed people as unexposed. This is information bias. If the misclassification is unrelated to outcome it usually pulls the estimate toward no effect, which can hide a real association."}, {"id": 1810, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "Ibuprofen 10 mg/kg per dose every six to eight hours as needed is ordered for a 6 year old boy who weighs 44 lb and has a fever. The pharmacy stocks ibuprofen 100 mg/5 mL suspension. Using 1 kg = 2.2 lb, what volume should be measured for one dose?", "options": {"A": "5 mL per dose, which delivers 100 mg as the usual fixed pediatric amount", "B": "20 mL per dose, which delivers 400 mg by using 20 mg/kg dosing for the child", "C": "22 mL per dose, which delivers 440 mg when 44 lb is treated as 44 kg", "D": "10 mL per dose, which delivers 200 mg from a 20 kg weight"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (10 mL per dose, which delivers 200 mg from a 20 kg weight): 44 lb divided by 2.2 is 20 kg, so one dose is 10 mg/kg x 20 kg = 200 mg. At 100 mg per 5 mL, 200 mg is 10 mL. Treating pounds as kilograms gives 22 mL, using 20 mg/kg gives 20 mL, and a fixed 5 mL does not follow the weight-based order."}, {"id": 1811, "domain": "leadership_and_stewardship", "chapter": 11, "question": "RC is a 52 year old who starts oxycodone 10 mg every 12 hours for chronic back pain. When the pharmacist offers a naloxone kit, he says he does not need one because he takes his medication exactly as prescribed and is not an addict. How should the pharmacist respond?", "options": {"A": "Accept his view, because naloxone kits are meant only for people who use opioids outside prescriptions", "B": "Offer a kit only if his dose rises above 90 morphine milligram equivalents per day", "C": "Explain that prescribed opioids can cause overdose, and offer a kit for his household", "D": "Give the kit only to his spouse, since the patient cannot use it when unconscious"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Explain that prescribed opioids can cause overdose, and offer a kit for his household): Respiratory depression can happen with prescribed opioids, particularly when other sedatives or alcohol are used or when kidney function changes, so offering naloxone is recommended for people starting opioids at any dose. Others in the home can recognize an overdose and give naloxone, so the kit should be available to the household, not only to him."}, {"id": 1812, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 3, "question": "FG is a 52 year old man with a positive COVID-19 test who is prescribed nirmatrelvir/ritonavir. He has normal kidney function. He tells the pharmacist he takes St John's wort daily for low mood and has taken it for months. His other medicines are unchanged. What should the pharmacist advise?", "options": {"A": "Continue it and take both products at least four hours apart to avoid the interaction", "B": "Continue it but reduce the nirmatrelvir dose by half to offset the inducing effect", "C": "St John's wort is safe to continue, since it only interacts with antidepressants, not antivirals", "D": "St John's wort lowers antiviral levels, so it should not be combined with this treatment"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (St John's wort lowers antiviral levels, so it should not be combined with this treatment): St John's wort strongly induces CYP3A4 and P-glycoprotein, which can drop nirmatrelvir and ritonavir levels enough to cause treatment failure and resistance. Its use is avoided with this antiviral. Spacing the doses or cutting the antiviral dose does not overcome the induction."}, {"id": 1813, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 6, "question": "CD is a 33 year old six weeks postpartum with moderate depression. She is exclusively breastfeeding her term infant and wants to start an antidepressant while continuing to nurse. She asks the pharmacist which choice is generally preferred in lactation. Which option should the pharmacist suggest?", "options": {"A": "Sertraline, because infant serum levels are usually very low or undetectable with it", "B": "No antidepressant, because every drug of this class reaches milk at unsafe infant concentrations", "C": "Doxepin, because tricyclic antidepressants are sedating and therefore help the infant sleep better at night", "D": "Fluoxetine, because its long half-life gives the infant steadier and safer drug exposure"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Sertraline, because infant serum levels are usually very low or undetectable with it): Sertraline has low milk transfer and infant levels are usually negligible, so it is a common first agent in breastfeeding. Fluoxetine has a long half-life and active metabolite that can accumulate in infants. Doxepin has been linked to infant sedation, and untreated depression is itself a risk, so avoiding all antidepressants is incorrect."}, {"id": 1814, "domain": "communication_and_collaboration", "chapter": 11, "question": "A woman who has waited 40 minutes for her father's heart medications tells the pharmacist the service is unacceptable and says she will complain to the college. The pharmacy was short staffed after a technician called in sick. The prescriptions are now ready. Which opening response is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Apologize for the wait, thank her for raising it, and tell her the medications are ready", "B": "Explain that the pharmacy was short staffed and that the delay was outside the pharmacist's control", "C": "Hand over the medications and offer a small discount in return for her patience with staff", "D": "Tell her complaints should be directed to the pharmacy manager and offer a complaint form"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Apologize for the wait, thank her for raising it, and tell her the medications are ready): A sincere apology and acknowledgement of her concern respond to the emotion first and then solve the immediate need. Explaining the cause before apologizing sounds like an excuse, and redirecting to a form can feel like a brush off. A discount does not address the concern and may suggest the pharmacy is trying to avoid a complaint."}, {"id": 1815, "domain": "leadership_and_stewardship", "chapter": 11, "question": "A pharmacist prepares to give an intramuscular influenza vaccine to a 62 year old man weighing 84 kg. He has no bleeding disorder and wears a short sleeved shirt. The pharmacist reviews the injection technique with a student. Which technique is appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Gluteal muscle, 38 mm needle, inserted at 90 degrees after the patient lies down", "B": "Deltoid muscle, 25 mm needle, inserted at a 90 degree angle to the skin", "C": "Subcutaneous tissue of the upper arm, 16 mm needle, inserted at a 45 degree angle", "D": "Deltoid muscle, 16 mm needle, inserted at a 45 degree angle to reduce bone contact"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Deltoid muscle, 25 mm needle, inserted at a 90 degree angle to the skin): Intramuscular vaccines in adults are given into the deltoid at a 90 degree angle with a needle long enough to reach muscle, usually 25 mm for an adult of this size. Shorter needles risk subcutaneous deposition, which reduces response and increases local reactions. The gluteal site is not preferred in adults because of fat thickness and nerve risk."}, {"id": 1816, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 1, "question": "DR is a 55 year old man with heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia and a prior myocardial infarction. He takes rosuvastatin 40 mg and ezetimibe 10 mg daily with confirmed adherence. His LDL cholesterol remains 2.9 mmol/L. His specialist is considering a next step. Which agent and schedule is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Fenofibrate 145 mg daily, which lowers LDL cholesterol more than ezetimibe or a PCSK9 inhibitor", "B": "Evolocumab 140 mg by subcutaneous injection every two weeks, added to current therapy", "C": "Colestipol granules twice daily, which lowers LDL more than a PCSK9 inhibitor in this setting", "D": "Nicotinic acid 2 g nightly, which has proven to reduce events when added to statins"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Evolocumab 140 mg by subcutaneous injection every two weeks, added to current therapy): For patients with established atherosclerotic disease or familial hypercholesterolemia whose LDL stays above target on maximally tolerated statin plus ezetimibe, a PCSK9 inhibitor is the recommended next step. Evolocumab is given as 140 mg every two weeks or 420 mg monthly. Bile acid sequestrants, fibrates and niacin lower LDL much less or lack outcome benefit."}, {"id": 1817, "domain": "leadership_and_stewardship", "chapter": 11, "question": "KP is a 77 year old woman with uncomplicated cystitis and a creatinine clearance of 28 mL/min. The prescriber has ordered nitrofurantoin 100 mg twice daily for five days. The pharmacist reviews the prescription for appropriateness. What should the pharmacist do?", "options": {"A": "Dispense as written, since short courses of nitrofurantoin are not affected by reduced renal clearance", "B": "Dispense with advice to extend the course to ten days to offset reduced urinary drug levels", "C": "Contact the prescriber to suggest another agent, since nitrofurantoin is ineffective at this clearance", "D": "Dispense and halve the dose to 50 mg twice daily to match her renal function"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Contact the prescriber to suggest another agent, since nitrofurantoin is ineffective at this clearance): Nitrofurantoin depends on renal excretion to reach effective urinary concentrations, and at a clearance below about 30 mL/min it is less effective while serum levels and neuropathy risk rise. The prescriber should be asked to choose another agent. Halving or lengthening the course does not correct the problem."}, {"id": 1818, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 3, "question": "UF is a 40 year old man whose immigration screening shows HBsAg positive, anti-HBs negative, total anti-HBc positive and anti-HBc IgM negative on two tests six months apart. His ALT is normal. His physician asks the pharmacist to interpret these results. Which interpretation is correct?", "options": {"A": "Chronic hepatitis B infection, so HBV DNA testing and liver assessment are needed next", "B": "Vaccine induced immunity because the surface antibody is negative and the core antibody is positive", "C": "Acute hepatitis B infection that will resolve, so the tests should be repeated in a month", "D": "Resolved past infection with natural immunity, so no further follow-up or vaccination is needed now"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Chronic hepatitis B infection, so HBV DNA testing and liver assessment are needed next): HBsAg that persists for more than six months with negative IgM anti-HBc and positive total anti-HBc defines chronic infection. Next steps are HBV DNA, ALT trends and fibrosis assessment to decide on antiviral therapy. Vaccine immunity would show anti-HBs positive only, and past resolved infection would show HBsAg negative."}, {"id": 1819, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "DE is a 70 year old man with atrial fibrillation and a CHADS-65 indication, prescribed apixaban 5 mg twice daily. His refill history shows he is using the supply over twice as long. He admits he takes one dose daily because of the copay on his fixed income. What should the pharmacist do?", "options": {"A": "Explain the stroke risk of skipping doses, then explore coverage and alternatives with the prescriber", "B": "Switch him to warfarin without consulting the prescriber, because it is cheaper and still prevents stroke", "C": "Respect his financial choice and document that he has declined the full prescribed dose regimen", "D": "Tell him once daily apixaban still gives adequate protection and review again in six months"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Explain the stroke risk of skipping doses, then explore coverage and alternatives with the prescriber): Taking half the prescribed dose of a twice daily anticoagulant leaves him unprotected against stroke, so the risk must be explained and the cost barrier addressed. Coverage programs, copay assistance or a prescriber-directed alternative can fix access. Changing drugs without the prescriber or endorsing an unproven dose does not."}, {"id": 1820, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "A patient (EN) who travels often asks the pharmacist for a six month supply of a compounded 0.5 mg/mL oral liquid clonidine solution to take on a long trip. The pharmacist has a validated beyond-use date of 30 days refrigerated. EN says the pharmacy can simply write a longer date. What should the pharmacist do?", "options": {"A": "Dispense six months with a 90 day date, since a traveller needs a longer supply", "B": "Dispense six months of liquid with a 30 day date and tell EN to discard it", "C": "Decline the longer date, supply only 30 days, and discuss alternative arrangements for the trip", "D": "Add a preservative and assign a six month date, noting the change in the compounding record"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Decline the longer date, supply only 30 days, and discuss alternative arrangements for the trip): A beyond-use date is based on stability and sterility evidence and cannot be extended by request or convenience. The pharmacist can supply a quantity that will be used within 30 days and help EN plan, for example by compounding a later batch, arranging a pharmacy at the destination, or considering a commercial dosage form. Adding a preservative would require new validated data."}, {"id": 1821, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 4, "question": "OP is a 24 year old woman with moderate inflammatory acne who wants to start tretinoin 0.025% cream. She is not pregnant and uses an oral contraceptive. She asks when to apply it and how to limit irritation. Which counselling advice is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Apply a pea sized amount at bedtime, start every second night and use daily sunscreen", "B": "Apply it twice daily to the whole face from day one so the skin adapts rapidly", "C": "Apply a thick layer each morning before makeup, since stronger coverage clears lesions more quickly", "D": "Apply a pea sized amount at bedtime and tan lightly, because ultraviolet light improves retinoid action"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Apply a pea sized amount at bedtime, start every second night and use daily sunscreen): A pea sized amount at bedtime, introduced every second night, limits the irritation seen with topical retinoids, and daily sunscreen is needed because the skin is more photosensitive. Thick layers and twice daily use increase irritation, and ultraviolet exposure does not help the retinoid."}, {"id": 1822, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A tertiary reference rates an interaction between a patient's two medications as major, while a second tertiary reference rates it as moderate. The patient, 63 year old LP, is stable on both drugs. Which approach is best for the pharmacist?", "options": {"A": "Adopt the higher severity rating and stop one medication immediately to remove the possibility of harm", "B": "Adopt the lower severity rating, since it avoids unnecessary changes to a stable drug regimen", "C": "Review the cited evidence and patient factors, use judgment, and consult the prescriber if needed", "D": "Average the two ratings and treat the interaction as minor, then document that it was assessed"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Review the cited evidence and patient factors, use judgment, and consult the prescriber if needed): Severity ratings differ because editors weigh evidence differently. The pharmacist should read the underlying references, consider dose, renal function and monitoring alternatives, and communicate with the prescriber when uncertainty remains. Choosing either rating automatically or averaging them is not sound reasoning."}, {"id": 1823, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "A physician offers a pharmacist, JO, a monthly fee for sending the clinic's patients' prescriptions to JO's pharmacy and for promoting the clinic's services to customers. The physician is well respected locally. JO's pharmacy would gain about 40 new patients monthly. What should JO do?", "options": {"A": "Decline, because fees for steering prescriptions undermine patient choice and violate kickback standards", "B": "Accept the fee but report it as income to the college each year as required", "C": "Accept the fee if the patients are told they may choose any pharmacy they prefer instead", "D": "Negotiate a lower fee so the arrangement is considered a fair-market payment for services"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Decline, because fees for steering prescriptions undermine patient choice and violate kickback standards): Payments for directing prescriptions are kickbacks that compromise patient choice and are prohibited by pharmacy practice standards. Informing patients of their choice, reporting the income, or negotiating the fee does not make the payment acceptable. The only appropriate response is to decline the arrangement."}, {"id": 1824, "domain": "communication_and_collaboration", "chapter": 11, "question": "The sons of OB, an 84 year old man with capacity, ask the pharmacist not to tell him that his new medication is for prostate cancer, saying it would crush his spirit. OB has asked the pharmacist what the medication is for. What should the pharmacist do?", "options": {"A": "Follow the sons' request and describe it as a general hormone treatment, since they know him", "B": "Refuse to discuss the medication until the physician has spoken to OB and the sons", "C": "Tell OB the full diagnosis and prognosis immediately in front of the sons, as law requires", "D": "Ask OB privately how much he wants to know and answer his question honestly"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Ask OB privately how much he wants to know and answer his question honestly): A patient with capacity has the right to information about his own care and to decide how much he wants to hear. Asking him privately lets the pharmacist respect his wishes and avoid deception, while taking the sons' concern seriously. Withholding or misrepresenting the purpose is not acceptable, and forcing full disclosure ignores his preferences."}, {"id": 1825, "domain": "communication_and_collaboration", "chapter": 11, "question": "BT is a 66 year old woman taking simvastatin 40 mg nightly who is prescribed clarithromycin 500 mg twice daily for pneumonia. The pharmacist recommends azithromycin, but the prescriber is busy and insists the order stand. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Refuse to dispense and tell the patient that the prescriber made an unsafe decision", "B": "Dispense as written since the prescriber has accepted responsibility and the course is only short", "C": "Restate the risk of myopathy, propose holding simvastatin during therapy, and document it", "D": "Dispense clarithromycin and advise the patient to stop simvastatin on her own initiative"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Restate the risk of myopathy, propose holding simvastatin during therapy, and document it): Clarithromycin strongly inhibits CYP3A4 and raises simvastatin levels, risking myopathy and rhabdomyolysis. Offering a workable alternative such as holding the statin keeps collaboration while protecting the patient, and the pharmacist documents it. Criticizing the prescriber to the patient is unprofessional."}, {"id": 1826, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "A hospitalized resident's family returns two unused weeks of blister cards to the pharmacy, still sealed, and asks that the pharmacy give the tablets to another resident with the same prescriptions. The cards were never opened or removed from the facility's locked room. What should the pharmacist do?", "options": {"A": "Accept the cards and reuse them after repackaging into new cards with a new label", "B": "Accept the cards and reuse the tablets only if they are within their expiry dates", "C": "Decline, and dispose of the returned cards through the medication return program", "D": "Accept the cards and reuse the tablets for the other resident after checking each imprint"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Decline, and dispose of the returned cards through the medication return program): Medications that have left the pharmacy's control cannot be reliably shown to have been stored properly, so returned products are not re dispensed. This applies even when the cards look intact. They are collected for destruction through the medication return program."}, {"id": 1827, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 6, "question": "FW is a 22 year old who took ulipristal 30 mg yesterday after a condom broke. She takes a combined oral contraceptive and wants to restart her pill packet tonight. She asks the pharmacist when to restart it and what protection to use. What should the pharmacist advise?", "options": {"A": "Wait five days after ulipristal, then restart and use condoms for seven days", "B": "Stop the pill and wait until her next period begins before starting a new packet", "C": "Restart tomorrow morning and use condoms for only the following two days, then rely on pills", "D": "Restart tonight, because the two products work independently and need no waiting period between them"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Wait five days after ulipristal, then restart and use condoms for seven days): Progestins in contraceptives can blunt the effect of ulipristal, so hormonal contraception is delayed at least five days after the dose. Barrier protection is then needed until the pill has been taken for seven days. Restarting immediately or using only two days of backup leaves a gap."}, {"id": 1828, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 4, "question": "FK is a 47 year old man prescribed salbutamol 100 mcg, two puffs every four to six hours as needed, by metered dose inhaler without a spacer. He sprays both puffs one right after the other without shaking between them. The pharmacist should counsel him to:", "options": {"A": "shake once, then spray both puffs quickly so the second puff reaches the lungs deeper", "B": "shake before each puff and wait about thirty seconds between the two puffs", "C": "avoid shaking after the initial use, since further shaking wastes propellant and medication", "D": "wait about five minutes between puffs, because drug from one puff blocks the next puff"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (shake before each puff and wait about thirty seconds between the two puffs): The suspension separates between actuations, so each puff needs shaking, and a pause of roughly 30 seconds lets the next puff be inhaled as a separate, well-formed dose. Firing puffs back to back reduces lung deposition. A five minute gap is not needed for salbutamol, and the puffs do not block each other."}, {"id": 1829, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "A pharmacist is preparing one batch of glycerin gelatin suppositories and another batch with a cocoa butter base. The technician asks whether the stainless steel molds should be lubricated before pouring each batch of molten base. Which statement about mold preparation is correct?", "options": {"A": "Lubricate the molds with mineral oil for glycerin gelatin, but cocoa butter usually needs none", "B": "Leave the molds dry for both bases and tap them firmly to release the product", "C": "Lubricate the molds with mineral oil for both bases, since both stick firmly once chilled", "D": "Lubricate the molds with mineral oil for cocoa butter, but glycerin gelatin usually needs no lubricant"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Lubricate the molds with mineral oil for glycerin gelatin, but cocoa butter usually needs none): Glycerin gelatin adheres to the mold and needs a thin film of mineral oil so it can be released. Cocoa butter contracts as it cools, which frees the suppositories from the mold without lubricant. Oil on a cocoa butter mold is not needed and can alter the finished product."}, {"id": 1830, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "YC, a 38 year old parent, asks pharmacist JB about the HPV vaccine for her 13 year old son. The vaccine is publicly funded in their province. JB personally opposes the vaccine and wants to tell her it is unsafe and unnecessary. Which response meets professional obligations?", "options": {"A": "Decline to answer any vaccine questions and tell YC that vaccines are a matter for physicians", "B": "Provide accurate evidence-based information and refer her onward if JB cannot support the service", "C": "Answer neutrally by saying the evidence is unsettled, so YC must decide without pharmacist advice", "D": "Share JB's view that the vaccine is unsafe, since pharmacists may express their personal beliefs freely"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Provide accurate evidence-based information and refer her onward if JB cannot support the service): A conscientious objection concerns what the pharmacist will personally provide, and it is not a licence to give misinformation. JB should give accurate, evidence-based information about the HPV vaccine and refer YC onward if JB cannot support the service. Sharing personal opinion as fact or refusing all vaccine questions fails the patient."}, {"id": 1831, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "A pharmacist is making an ointment by fusion using beeswax (melting point 62 degrees Celsius), white petrolatum (melting point 40 degrees) and mineral oil (liquid at room temperature). The technician asks how to combine them on the hot plate. Which method is correct?", "options": {"A": "Melt the petrolatum first, then add the beeswax last so it does not overheat", "B": "Melt the beeswax first, then add the others by decreasing melting point while stirring", "C": "Melt all three together on high heat and pour the mixture while it is still boiling", "D": "Melt the mineral oil first, then add the others in order of increasing melting point"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Melt the beeswax first, then add the others by decreasing melting point while stirring): The ingredient with the highest melting point is melted first, then the heat is reduced and the others are added in order of decreasing melting point, which avoids overheating the lower melting ones. The mixture is stirred until it congeals so it does not separate. Boiling the mixture can degrade the ingredients and cause uneven texture."}, {"id": 1832, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 1, "question": "KM is a 63 year old woman who walks into the pharmacy with a pounding headache, blurred vision and chest tightness that started an hour ago. The pharmacy blood pressure device reads 196/118 mmHg on two measurements. She takes no regular medication and asks if she can just go home and rest. What should the pharmacist do?", "options": {"A": "Give her a sublingual nifedipine capsule to lower pressure quickly before she leaves the pharmacy", "B": "Recommend rest for thirty minutes, then repeat the reading and send her home if it falls", "C": "Arrange urgent emergency care, because these symptoms with severe elevation suggest acute end-organ damage", "D": "Advise her to see her family physician within three days and start a low-salt diet"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Arrange urgent emergency care, because these symptoms with severe elevation suggest acute end-organ damage): Severe hypertension with headache, visual change and chest tightness suggests a hypertensive emergency with possible end-organ damage, which needs immediate emergency assessment and monitored treatment. Observation at home or a routine appointment delays care. Rapid lowering with sublingual nifedipine is discouraged because it can cause abrupt, dangerous drops in pressure."}, {"id": 1833, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "IA, a pharmacist with a conscientious objection, receives a lawful prescription for a medication to be used in medical assistance in dying. The patient's family is waiting, and the prescriber is available by phone. Which step reflects typical professional expectations?", "options": {"A": "Return the prescription to the family and say the pharmacy does not stock the drug", "B": "Inform the patient and prescriber promptly, and help transfer the prescription to a willing pharmacist", "C": "Dispense the medication and let a technician hand it over, so IA is not directly involved", "D": "Keep the prescription at the pharmacy until the college has reviewed IA's objection in detail"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Inform the patient and prescriber promptly, and help transfer the prescription to a willing pharmacist): A pharmacist with a conscientious objection must tell the patient and prescriber promptly and help ensure the prescription reaches a willing pharmacist without delay. Holding the prescription for a college review or having a technician hand it over does not respect the objection or the patient's timing. Implying the drug is not stocked misleads the family."}, {"id": 1834, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 4, "question": "OT is a 58 year old man with mild COPD who smokes a pack of cigarettes daily and asks which single intervention would most slow his lung function decline. He is not interested in pulmonary rehabilitation at this time. What should the pharmacist emphasize?", "options": {"A": "Take a daily oral mucolytic, because it reduces airway inflammation and halts the decline", "B": "Begin long term oxygen at home overnight, which preserves lung tissue over time", "C": "Quit smoking, supported by counselling and medication such as varenicline or nicotine replacement", "D": "Start an inhaled corticosteroid, which slows the yearly decline in lung function more than quitting"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Quit smoking, supported by counselling and medication such as varenicline or nicotine replacement): Smoking cessation is the only intervention shown to slow the accelerated decline in lung function in COPD, and pharmacotherapy with counselling roughly doubles quit rates. Inhaled corticosteroids and mucolytics do not alter long term decline, and oxygen is reserved for severe resting hypoxemia."}, {"id": 1835, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "A capable adult, 29, tells the pharmacist that her partner hit her last night. She does not want police involved and asks the pharmacist to keep the matter secret. No children live in the home. What should the pharmacist do?", "options": {"A": "Respect her autonomy, offer safety information and referrals, and keep the disclosure confidential", "B": "Call the police anyway, since violence against a partner must be reported by any health professional", "C": "Contact her partner to warn him that the pharmacist knows and will report any further incident", "D": "Refuse to continue counselling until she agrees to a formal police report on the incident"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Respect her autonomy, offer safety information and referrals, and keep the disclosure confidential): There is generally no mandatory duty to report adult partner violence when a capable adult declines and no child or imminent threat is involved. Supporting her with resources and keeping confidence preserves trust and safety."}, {"id": 1836, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "A prescription written on March 1 for sertraline 50 mg, 30 tablets with five refills, was filled on March 1 and refilled on April 1 and May 1. On June 5, JH asks to transfer it to another pharmacy. How many refills remain?", "options": {"A": "Four refills, since only the most recent refill on May 1 counts against the five", "B": "Two refills, since the original fill and both refills count against the five authorized", "C": "Five refills, since a transfer resets the authorization to the full original amount", "D": "Three refills, since two of the five authorized refills have been used by the pharmacy"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Three refills, since two of the five authorized refills have been used by the pharmacy): The March 1 dispensing was the original fill, and April 1 and May 1 were refills one and two. With five authorized, 5 - 2 = 3 remain. The original fill is not counted as a refill, and a transfer does not reset the authorization."}, {"id": 1837, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "ME is a 17 year old girl who started an insulin pump last month. She has left the same infusion set in place for six days because it still seems to work, and she has unexplained glucose readings above 14 mmol/L. What should the pharmacist advise about infusion set use?", "options": {"A": "Change it every two to three days with site rotation, and sooner for unexplained highs", "B": "Keep it until it falls off, and give extra boluses whenever she sees a high", "C": "Change it only when the pump alarms, since modern cannulas are monitored by the device", "D": "Change it every seven days, since cannulas stay reliable for a full week before absorption slows"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Change it every two to three days with site rotation, and sooner for unexplained highs): Infusion sets should be changed every two to three days because absorption worsens and infection and occlusion risk rise with longer wear. Unexplained highs are a prompt to replace the set early. Pumps do not detect all cannula problems, and extra boluses through a failing set do not deliver reliably."}, {"id": 1838, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "A pharmacy technician unpacks a delivery of sterile vials and diluents at the pharmacy receiving counter and starts to carry the outer cardboard shipping cartons, with their packing paper and sealed vials, straight into the ISO Class 7 buffer room. What should the pharmacist instruct her to do instead?", "options": {"A": "Carry the cartons in and open them at the cabinet edge to save handling time", "B": "Remove outer cartons in the anteroom, then wipe each item with sterile 70% isopropyl alcohol", "C": "Place the cartons on a cart in the buffer room, covered with a sterile drape", "D": "Spray the cartons with 70% alcohol at the buffer room entrance, then carry them straight inside"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Remove outer cartons in the anteroom, then wipe each item with sterile 70% isopropyl alcohol): Cardboard and packing paper shed fibres and carry dirt and microbial spores, so outer cartons are removed in the anteroom or a lower-class area and the individual items are disinfected before entering the buffer room. Spraying or wiping cartons is not enough because the material itself generates particles. Paper and cardboard should stay out of classified rooms."}, {"id": 1839, "domain": "communication_and_collaboration", "chapter": 11, "question": "GD, a 31 year old woman at 26 weeks of pregnancy, was diagnosed with gestational diabetes and asks the pharmacist to create a detailed daily meal plan. She takes only a prenatal vitamin. The pharmacist wants to use the team approach appropriately. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Hold the plan until she starts insulin, because nutrition advice is only relevant to insulin dosing", "B": "Tell her meal planning is outside pharmacy practice and suggest an internet search for sample plans", "C": "Offer general advice, then refer GD to a registered dietitian and confirm her physician is informed", "D": "Create the complete meal plan, since pharmacists receive training in carbohydrate counting during their degree"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Offer general advice, then refer GD to a registered dietitian and confirm her physician is informed): A registered dietitian has the training to individualize nutrition therapy in pregnancy, and the pharmacist's role is to support, refer and keep the team informed. The pharmacist can still give general advice. Refusing to engage or sending her to unvetted websites leaves a gap, and nutrition matters whether or not insulin is used."}, {"id": 1840, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 7, "question": "ZK, a 14 month old, weighs 22 lb. A prescriber orders ibuprofen 10 mg/kg per dose for fever. The suspension is 100 mg per 5 mL, and the mother asks the pharmacist to confirm how many millilitres to give for each dose. Which volume is correct?", "options": {"A": "11 mL per dose, which results from using pounds without converting to kilograms", "B": "5 mL per dose, because 22 lb is 10 kg and the dose is 100 mg", "C": "2.5 mL per dose, which results from using a dose of 5 mg/kg, not 10 mg/kg", "D": "10 mL per dose, which results from using a dose of 20 mg/kg, not 10 mg/kg"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (5 mL per dose, because 22 lb is 10 kg and the dose is 100 mg): 22 lb divided by 2.2 is 10 kg, so a 10 mg/kg dose is 100 mg. At 100 mg per 5 mL that is 5 mL. Skipping the pound to kilogram conversion would more than double the dose."}, {"id": 1841, "domain": "leadership_and_stewardship", "chapter": 11, "question": "During a measles outbreak in the region, a mother phones the pharmacy to say her son has fever and a rash, and she plans to bring him in to pick up a prescription. The pharmacist wants to limit exposure to others. What should the pharmacist advise?", "options": {"A": "Have her bring him in so the pharmacist can assess the rash and fever", "B": "Advise her to take him to a busy walk in clinic, where he is seen sooner", "C": "Keep him out of public areas, call public health, and arrange delivery or curbside pickup", "D": "Bring him to the counter wearing a mask, since masks fully prevent measles spread"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Keep him out of public areas, call public health, and arrange delivery or curbside pickup): Measles is airborne and highly contagious, spreading in shared air for up to two hours after an infected person leaves, so a suspected case should avoid public spaces and waiting rooms. Public health must be notified and testing arranged with advance warning to the facility. Delivery or curbside pickup protects other patients, including infants and pregnant women."}, {"id": 1842, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 6, "question": "FW is a 35 year old man with diarrhea-predominant irritable bowel syndrome triggered by stress and large meals. He has already adjusted his diet. He asks for something he can take before social events to prevent urgent diarrhea. The pharmacist should suggest:", "options": {"A": "Psyllium 20 g taken once as a stool bulking dose just before the event", "B": "Lactulose 15 mL twice daily to reduce his stool frequency and cramping", "C": "Loperamide 2 mg taken as needed before the event, within the labelled daily maximum", "D": "Daily bisacodyl 5 mg at bedtime to normalize stool consistency and gut transit"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Loperamide 2 mg taken as needed before the event, within the labelled daily maximum): Loperamide slows gut transit and is useful on an as-needed basis for IBS with diarrhea. Bisacodyl and lactulose are laxatives and would worsen diarrhea. A single large dose of psyllium would not prevent urgency and may increase bloating."}, {"id": 1843, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 6, "question": "GL is a 66 year old man with Child-Pugh class C cirrhosis and cancer pain who has been prescribed oral morphine. His pharmacist knows that drugs with high first pass extraction reach the systemic circulation more readily in cirrhosis. Which approach to starting morphine is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Start with a reduced dose at longer intervals and titrate slowly to response", "B": "Start with a standard dose at longer intervals, since cirrhosis shortens morphine half life", "C": "Start with the usual dose, since morphine clearance depends mainly on the kidneys, not the liver", "D": "Start with the usual adult dose and monitor for sedation before making any changes later"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Start with a reduced dose at longer intervals and titrate slowly to response): Cirrhosis reduces first pass metabolism and hepatic clearance, so morphine bioavailability rises and the half life is prolonged. A lower starting dose with longer intervals and slow titration lowers the risk of oversedation and encephalopathy. Morphine is conjugated in the liver, and cirrhosis lengthens rather than shortens its effect."}, {"id": 1844, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "On a hospital ward, a pharmacist verifies a patient-controlled analgesia syringe for KM, a 66 year old opioid-naive woman after hip surgery. The order is hydromorphone 0.2 mg/mL, but the prepared syringe label reads 2 mg/mL, and the vial used was 10 mg/mL. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Send it to the ward with a warning to monitor sedation closely for the first hour", "B": "Relabel the syringe as 0.2 mg/mL after confirming the volume matches the order", "C": "Quarantine the syringe, prepare a new one from the order, and report the event", "D": "Dilute the syringe contents with saline to reach 0.2 mg/mL and relabel it"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Quarantine the syringe, prepare a new one from the order, and report the event): The label, vial and order disagree, so the contents cannot be trusted and the syringe must be removed from use. Making a new syringe is prepared from the original order, and the event is reported so the cause can be found. Relabelling or diluting an uncertain product, or sending a high-alert opioid with a warning, risks serious overdose."}, {"id": 1845, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "A community pharmacist receives a new prescription for zopiclone 7.5 mg at bedtime for YT, a 78 year old woman with mild kidney impairment who lives alone and has no history of sleeping pill use. What should the pharmacist do?", "options": {"A": "Dispense 7.5 mg and advise her to take it with food, which reduces next-day drowsiness", "B": "Ask the prescriber about starting at 3.75 mg, the recommended dose for older adults", "C": "Change the prescription to 15 mg at bedtime, since older adults metabolize zopiclone faster than younger", "D": "Dispense 7.5 mg as written, because the adult dose is appropriate when kidney impairment is mild"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Ask the prescriber about starting at 3.75 mg, the recommended dose for older adults): In older adults and those with renal or hepatic impairment, the recommended starting dose of zopiclone is 3.75 mg because clearance is reduced and sensitivity to sedation is higher. The pharmacist should recommend this to the prescriber rather than dispensing the usual dose. Taking it with food slows absorption and does not make the dose safer."}, {"id": 1846, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 6, "question": "IL is 12 weeks pregnant and was diagnosed with a deep vein thrombosis yesterday. Her hospital discharge medication list shows warfarin 5 mg daily, started before pregnancy was known. She asks the pharmacist whether this is suitable. Which anticoagulant is the preferred choice for her during pregnancy?", "options": {"A": "No anticoagulant, because treatment should be delayed until delivery to protect the developing fetus", "B": "Low molecular weight heparin, because it does not cross the placenta and avoids embryopathy", "C": "Rivaroxaban, because direct oral anticoagulants are the preferred agents throughout every trimester of pregnancy", "D": "Continue warfarin, because it does not cross the placenta and has a long safety record"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Low molecular weight heparin, because it does not cross the placenta and avoids embryopathy): Warfarin crosses the placenta and can cause embryopathy, especially with exposure in the first trimester, so it is replaced. Low molecular weight heparin does not cross the placenta and is the standard treatment for venous thromboembolism in pregnancy. Direct oral anticoagulants are not recommended because data are limited, and untreated thrombosis is dangerous."}, {"id": 1847, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 6, "question": "KJ is a 24 year old with polycystic ovary syndrome who has had only three periods in the past year. She does not want pregnancy now, declines metformin, and has no contraindication to hormones. Her endometrial thickness on ultrasound is normal. What should the pharmacist recommend to protect her endometrium?", "options": {"A": "Metformin alone, which restores regular shedding of the lining in nearly every woman with the syndrome", "B": "No treatment, since infrequent periods mean the endometrium is exposed to less estrogen than normal", "C": "A combined hormonal contraceptive or cyclic progestin, to prevent hyperplasia from unopposed estrogen exposure", "D": "Estrogen only therapy, which thickens the lining and supports a regular and predictable monthly period"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A combined hormonal contraceptive or cyclic progestin, to prevent hyperplasia from unopposed estrogen exposure): Anovulation leaves the endometrium exposed to estrogen without progesterone, raising the risk of hyperplasia and cancer. A combined contraceptive or a cyclic progestin induces regular shedding and protects the lining. Estrogen alone makes the problem worse, and metformin does not reliably restore cycles."}, {"id": 1848, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 6, "question": "KP is a 72 year old woman in long term care who has taken nitrofurantoin 100 mg twice daily for eight months to prevent recurrent urinary infections. She has new fatigue and her ALT is five times the upper limit of normal with a positive ANA. What should the pharmacist recommend?", "options": {"A": "Reduce the nitrofurantoin dose to 50 mg twice daily and recheck liver enzymes in one month", "B": "Stop nitrofurantoin, notify the prescriber and request an alternative prevention strategy", "C": "Continue nitrofurantoin and add milk thistle to protect her liver during ongoing therapy", "D": "Switch to a macrocrystal nitrofurantoin product to lessen liver exposure and watch symptoms"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Stop nitrofurantoin, notify the prescriber and request an alternative prevention strategy): Long term nitrofurantoin can cause an autoimmune like chronic hepatitis, particularly in older women, shown by raised enzymes and autoantibodies. The drug is the cause, so it should be stopped and the prescriber notified so a different prevention plan can be chosen with liver follow up. Dose reduction, a herbal product or another formulation of the same drug does not remove the cause."}, {"id": 1849, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 4, "question": "GA is a 69 year old man with COPD who uses salbutamol by metered dose inhaler with a spacer. He sprays two puffs into the spacer together, then takes one breath. The pharmacist demonstrates the correct technique. What should he do instead?", "options": {"A": "Spray one puff, wait ten minutes, then take the second puff to limit absorption", "B": "Spray both puffs together, then inhale quickly to push the drug deep into the lungs", "C": "Spray one puff at a time, inhale slowly through the spacer, then repeat", "D": "Spray both puffs into the spacer and let it sit for a minute before inhaling"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Spray one puff at a time, inhale slowly through the spacer, then repeat): Each puff should be delivered into the spacer separately, followed by slow breathing in, so the drug is carried into the airways. Multiple puffs together cause the drug to collide and stick to the walls. Waiting long or letting the spacer sit wastes drug, and fast inhalation increases throat deposition."}, {"id": 1850, "domain": "communication_and_collaboration", "chapter": 11, "question": "During a hypertension follow-up, WP, a 58 year old man, tells the pharmacist that he gets dizzy when he stands up quickly and that his home readings are around 128/76 mmHg. The pharmacist is writing a SOAP note. Where should the statement about dizziness be recorded?", "options": {"A": "Under Objective, since the dizziness could be confirmed by measuring his standing blood pressure later", "B": "Under Assessment, because dizziness is the pharmacist's conclusion about a possible drug therapy problem", "C": "Under Plan, because dizziness will determine which monitoring steps the pharmacist arranges next", "D": "Under Subjective, because it is a symptom reported by the patient in his own words"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Under Subjective, because it is a symptom reported by the patient in his own words): SOAP separates what the patient says (Subjective) from what is measured or observed (Objective), then the pharmacist's judgment (Assessment) and the actions (Plan). A reported symptom is Subjective even if it could later be measured. The pharmacist's own interpretation of it belongs in the Assessment."}, {"id": 1851, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 7, "question": "JB is an 81 year old man who has fallen once this winter. He takes atorvastatin, metformin, ramipril and zolpidem 10 mg at bedtime, which he has used for years. The Canadian product monograph recommends a lower maximum zolpidem dose for older adults. What should the pharmacist advise?", "options": {"A": "Ten milligrams exceeds the older adult maximum of 5 mg and raises his fall risk", "B": "Zolpidem is safe at 10 mg only if taken with food, which lowers its sedating effect", "C": "The dose is appropriate, since older adults clear zolpidem faster and need more to sleep", "D": "Zolpidem should be switched to lorazepam 1 mg, which carries a lower fall risk than zolpidem"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Ten milligrams exceeds the older adult maximum of 5 mg and raises his fall risk): Older adults are more sensitive to zolpidem, so the recommended maximum is 5 mg, and 10 mg increases next day impairment, confusion and falls. Food slows absorption but does not make the higher dose safe. Lorazepam is not a lower risk alternative, since all sedative hypnotics increase fall risk in this age group."}, {"id": 1852, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 6, "question": "ZN is a 62 year old woman taking warfarin 4 mg daily with a stable INR of 2.4. She asks for oral fluconazole 150 mg once for what she believes is a vaginal yeast infection, because it is convenient. What should the pharmacist recommend?", "options": {"A": "Oral ketoconazole 200 mg daily for five days, which has a smaller effect on warfarin", "B": "A topical azole for several days, since fluconazole can raise the INR significantly", "C": "Oral fluconazole 150 mg once, after halving her warfarin dose for the next three days", "D": "Oral fluconazole 150 mg once, then recheck her INR in two weeks as a routine precaution"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A topical azole for several days, since fluconazole can raise the INR significantly): Fluconazole inhibits CYP2C9 and can raise the INR and bleeding risk in patients taking warfarin, so a topical azole avoids the interaction. Empirically halving the warfarin dose is not a safe plan. Ketoconazole also inhibits warfarin metabolism and is not used for this purpose."}, {"id": 1853, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "A 66 year old woman with osteoporosis takes omeprazole 20 mg daily and calcium carbonate 500 mg with a snack. She reports constipation and bloating. Her physician asks the pharmacist for a calcium recommendation. What is the most appropriate change?", "options": {"A": "Switch to calcium citrate, which absorbs well without stomach acid, with or without food", "B": "Stop calcium supplements, since proton pump inhibitor use means diet alone meets her bone needs", "C": "Take the carbonate with omeprazole each morning, because the two combine to improve calcium solubility", "D": "Switch to a higher carbonate dose at bedtime, because acid suppression improves absorption overnight"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Switch to calcium citrate, which absorbs well without stomach acid, with or without food): Calcium carbonate needs stomach acid to dissolve, so a proton pump inhibitor reduces its absorption, whereas calcium citrate does not depend on acid. Citrate is also often better tolerated, with less constipation. Taking carbonate at bedtime or alongside the acid suppressor does not solve the absorption problem."}, {"id": 1854, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 6, "question": "NH is a 52 year old man with cirrhosis. His assigned Child-Pugh points are bilirubin 2, albumin 2, INR 2, ascites 2 and encephalopathy 1. A prescriber asks the pharmacist for the class and dosing implication, given that class A is 5 to 6 points, class B 7 to 9 and class C 10 to 15. What is the response?", "options": {"A": "Score 11, class C, so avoid all drugs cleared by the liver and choose renal agents", "B": "Score 9, class A, so usual starting doses of liver cleared drugs are appropriate to use", "C": "Score 9, class B, so use reduced starting doses of drugs cleared by the liver", "D": "Score 8, class C, so reduce liver cleared drug doses by half and monitor closely"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Score 9, class B, so use reduced starting doses of drugs cleared by the liver): The points add up to 2 + 2 + 2 + 2 + 1 = 9, which falls in class B (7 to 9). Class B calls for caution with reduced starting doses and careful titration of drugs cleared by the liver. A total of 9 is not class A or class C."}, {"id": 1855, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 6, "question": "A prescriber orders metronidazole 2 g as a single dose for trichomoniasis in HG, a 29 year old patient. The pharmacy stocks only 500 mg tablets. HG asks how many tablets to take and how to take them. What should the pharmacist dispense and advise?", "options": {"A": "Two 500 mg tablets together as one dose with food, then two more tomorrow", "B": "Four 500 mg tablets together as one dose with food to reduce nausea", "C": "Four 500 mg tablets taken together on an empty stomach to improve absorption of the dose", "D": "Eight 500 mg tablets divided across the day with meals to reduce nausea and vomiting"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Four 500 mg tablets together as one dose with food to reduce nausea): A 2 g dose equals four 500 mg tablets, and taking them together as a single dose is the ordered regimen. Food reduces the nausea and stomach upset that metronidazole commonly causes. Splitting the dose or taking it on an empty stomach does not match the prescription or improve tolerability."}, {"id": 1856, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "RM is a 72 year old man weighing 70 kg with a serum creatinine of 130 umol/L who is starting levetiracetam. The pharmacist estimates his creatinine clearance using the Cockcroft-Gault equation to check the monograph dosing band. What is his estimated clearance?", "options": {"A": "About 90 mL/min, which is normal renal function and requires no levetiracetam dose adjustment for him", "B": "About 37 mL/min, which falls in the moderate renal impairment dosing band for older men", "C": "About 45 mL/min, which falls in the moderate renal impairment dosing band", "D": "About 56 mL/min, which falls in the mild renal impairment dosing band for older men"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (About 45 mL/min, which falls in the moderate renal impairment dosing band): Cockcroft-Gault gives (140 minus 72) x 70 x 1.23 / 130, which is 5855 / 130, or about 45 mL/min. This falls in the 30 to 50 mL/min band where levetiracetam doses are reduced. Leaving out the 1.23 male factor gives about 37 mL/min and underestimates clearance."}, {"id": 1857, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 7, "question": "ST is an 85 year old woman admitted with pneumonia who has taken lorazepam 2 mg nightly for six years. The resident wrote to stop lorazepam on admission because it is a Beers criteria drug, and the nurse asks the pharmacist whether this is appropriate. What should the pharmacist advise?", "options": {"A": "Switch to zopiclone 7.5 mg nightly, because it can be stopped without tapering", "B": "Continue the usual dose during the acute illness and plan a gradual outpatient taper later", "C": "Stop lorazepam today and monitor closely, since the Beers criteria require immediate discontinuation of benzodiazepines", "D": "Halve the dose to 1 mg tonight and stop in three days to limit sedation"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Continue the usual dose during the acute illness and plan a gradual outpatient taper later): After years of nightly use, abrupt discontinuation in hospital risks withdrawal, delirium and seizures on top of her pneumonia. The Beers criteria advise avoiding starting these drugs and support a slow taper, not an immediate stop. Substituting another hypnotic only shifts the problem, and a rapid three day taper is too fast."}, {"id": 1858, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 6, "question": "A mother asks for advice about her 3 year old son, who weighs 14 kg and has had watery diarrhea for two days with mild dehydration. The pediatrician has recommended oral rehydration solution at 50 mL/kg over four hours. What volume should the pharmacist tell her to give?", "options": {"A": "350 mL over four hours, given in small frequent sips", "B": "700 mL over four hours, given in small frequent sips", "C": "280 mL over four hours, given in small frequent sips", "D": "1400 mL over four hours, given in small frequent sips"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (700 mL over four hours, given in small frequent sips): The volume is 50 mL/kg x 14 kg = 700 mL over four hours. Giving small, frequent sips reduces the chance of vomiting. A figure of 1400 mL would apply to a 100 mL/kg plan for moderate dehydration, and the lower volumes would under-replace fluid losses."}, {"id": 1859, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 1, "question": "KL is a 45 year old woman of Chinese ancestry with type 2 diabetes of seven years and an LDL cholesterol of 3.6 mmol/L. Her physician plans to start rosuvastatin and asks the pharmacist about the starting dose. She has normal liver and renal function. Which starting dose is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "10 mg daily, which is the standard dose, taken only in the evening for better absorption", "B": "20 mg daily, which is the standard starting dose for adults with diabetes in practice", "C": "5 mg daily, because people of Asian ancestry have higher rosuvastatin blood levels", "D": "40 mg daily, because her diabetes places her in the highest risk group at the start"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (5 mg daily, because people of Asian ancestry have higher rosuvastatin blood levels): Patients of Asian ancestry have roughly twice the rosuvastatin exposure of other populations, so the monograph recommends a starting dose of 5 mg daily with careful titration. Higher starting doses raise the risk of myopathy. Rosuvastatin has a long half-life and can be taken at any time of day, so evening dosing adds nothing."}, {"id": 1860, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "WB is a 70 year old man with postherpetic neuralgia and narrow angle glaucoma, treated with timolol eye drops. His physician proposes nortriptyline 10 mg at bedtime. He also has mild prostate enlargement with a weak urinary stream. What should the pharmacist advise?", "options": {"A": "Switch to amitriptyline, because it has weaker anticholinergic effects than nortriptyline", "B": "Suggest a non anticholinergic option, such as renally adjusted low dose gabapentin or topical lidocaine", "C": "Start nortriptyline as planned, since the timolol drops prevent anticholinergic effects on the eye and bladder", "D": "Start nortriptyline at 10 mg and increase weekly since glaucoma risk appears only above 100 mg"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Suggest a non anticholinergic option, such as renally adjusted low dose gabapentin or topical lidocaine): Tricyclic antidepressants are anticholinergic and can precipitate angle closure and urinary retention, so narrow angle glaucoma and prostate enlargement make them poor choices. Timolol does not block these effects. Amitriptyline is actually more anticholinergic than nortriptyline. Low dose renally adjusted gabapentin or topical lidocaine avoids this problem."}, {"id": 1861, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "RC is a 31 year old woman admitted with moderate serotonin syndrome after taking extra sertraline and linezolid. Her temperature is 38.4 C, with clonus and agitation. Serotonergic drugs are stopped and benzodiazepines are given. The team asks what initial oral cyproheptadine dose the pharmacist suggests.", "options": {"A": "1 mg orally every eight hours, which avoids sedation and anticholinergic effects in most patients", "B": "50 mg intravenously over ten minutes, repeated every hour until the fever has resolved", "C": "12 mg orally, then 2 mg every two hours while symptoms continue", "D": "4 mg orally once daily at bedtime, titrated up weekly to a maximum of 32 mg"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (12 mg orally, then 2 mg every two hours while symptoms continue): Cyproheptadine is given as a 12 mg oral loading dose followed by 2 mg every two hours while symptoms persist, then about 8 mg every six hours for maintenance. It is available only orally, so it can be crushed through a nasogastric tube. Small allergy sized doses are inadequate, and sedation is an expected effect."}, {"id": 1862, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 3, "question": "CN is a 58 year old woman admitted with a temperature of 39.1 C and an absolute neutrophil count of 300 cells/uL after chemotherapy. She is hemodynamically stable with no clear source of infection. The team asks the pharmacist for an appropriate empiric antibiotic. Which choice is most suitable?", "options": {"A": "Oral amoxicillin-clavulanate, since it is acceptable for febrile neutropenia with a stable blood pressure", "B": "Vancomycin alone, because gram positive coverage is the main priority in all neutropenic patients", "C": "Ceftriaxone alone, as it covers Pseudomonas aeruginosa and is the usual choice for neutropenia", "D": "Piperacillin-tazobactam given intravenously, chosen for its antipseudomonal activity in febrile neutropenia"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Piperacillin-tazobactam given intravenously, chosen for its antipseudomonal activity in febrile neutropenia): Febrile neutropenia requires prompt intravenous therapy with an agent active against Pseudomonas aeruginosa, such as piperacillin-tazobactam. Ceftriaxone lacks antipseudomonal activity. Vancomycin alone leaves gram negatives untreated, and oral amoxicillin-clavulanate is too narrow for a high risk patient."}, {"id": 1863, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 7, "question": "UM is a 76 year old man with Parkinson disease taking levodopa and carbidopa. A hospital physician ordered metoclopramide 10 mg three times daily for nausea, and by day three his tremor and stiffness have clearly worsened. What should the pharmacist recommend?", "options": {"A": "Stop metoclopramide because it blocks dopamine receptors and can worsen his parkinsonism", "B": "Give metoclopramide only after meals, because the timing relative to food explains the worsening stiffness", "C": "Change metoclopramide to prochlorperazine, which is a safer antiemetic for people with Parkinson disease", "D": "Increase the levodopa dose to overcome the dopamine blockade and continue metoclopramide for nausea"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Stop metoclopramide because it blocks dopamine receptors and can worsen his parkinsonism): Metoclopramide antagonizes dopamine D2 receptors in the brain, which directly opposes levodopa and can worsen motor symptoms. Prochlorperazine blocks the same receptors, so it is not a safer substitute. Raising the levodopa dose chases a drug effect that can be avoided by stopping the offending drug, and meal timing does not change it."}, {"id": 1864, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 3, "question": "ED is a 66 year old man with COPD who recovered from pneumonia last month and has never received a pneumococcal vaccine. He asks the pharmacist which vaccine is recommended for him under Canadian immunization guidance. What is the most appropriate recommendation?", "options": {"A": "Two doses of PPSV23 eight weeks apart, since conjugate vaccines are not approved after age 60", "B": "The seasonal influenza vaccine alone, since it also prevents pneumococcal disease in adults with lung disease", "C": "One dose of conjugate pneumococcal vaccine PCV20, which is sufficient for adults aged 65 and older", "D": "Annual pneumococcal polysaccharide vaccine each autumn, because immunity wanes within twelve months after each dose"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (One dose of conjugate pneumococcal vaccine PCV20, which is sufficient for adults aged 65 and older): NACI recommends a single dose of a higher valency conjugate pneumococcal vaccine such as PCV20 for adults 65 and older and for younger adults with chronic lung disease. Polysaccharide vaccine is not given yearly. Influenza vaccine is recommended each season but does not protect against pneumococcus."}, {"id": 1865, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "A hospital's annual review shows that the latest certification report for the biological safety cabinet used for IV admixtures documented particle counts above the ISO Class 5 limit during the airflow test. The report arrived two days ago, and 30 IV preparations are scheduled for today. What should the pharmacist do?", "options": {"A": "Clean the cabinet with sterile 70% isopropyl alcohol and resume use if visible dust is absent", "B": "Take the cabinet out of service, arrange repair and recertification, and obtain the doses elsewhere", "C": "Continue using the cabinet for today's preparations and schedule repair for the next planned maintenance week", "D": "Use the cabinet only for doses given within one hour, with a shortened beyond-use date"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Take the cabinet out of service, arrange repair and recertification, and obtain the doses elsewhere): A primary engineering control that fails certification cannot assure ISO Class 5 conditions, so it must not be used for sterile compounding until it is repaired and passes recertification. Cleaning does not correct a filter or airflow fault. Shortening the beyond-use date does not reduce the risk at the moment of preparation, so the doses should be obtained from a qualified source meanwhile."}, {"id": 1866, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "The pharmacy has one remaining vial of an injectable antibiotic that cannot be replaced for three weeks. Two patients present prescriptions: AK, a 68 year old with a resistant infection and no alternatives, and BL, a 40 year old with a mild infection who could use an oral alternative. What is the most appropriate approach?", "options": {"A": "Split the vial between both patients so each receives a partial course of therapy", "B": "Fill whichever prescription arrived first, and advise the other patient to try a different pharmacy", "C": "Allocate it to AK, who has no alternative, and ask BL's prescriber about oral options", "D": "Allocate it to BL, since a healthier patient is more likely to complete the whole course"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Allocate it to AK, who has no alternative, and ask BL's prescriber about oral options): When supply is limited, stock goes first to the patient with the greatest clinical need and fewest alternatives, while the other patient is offered a suitable substitute through the prescriber. Order of arrival, partial courses and favouring the healthier patient do not serve patient need."}, {"id": 1867, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "CP is a 19 year old man stable on atomoxetine 80 mg each morning for ADHD. A new prescription arrives from his family doctor for fluoxetine 20 mg daily for depressive symptoms. The pharmacist checks his profile and sees no dose adjustment for atomoxetine. What is the key issue to communicate?", "options": {"A": "Fluoxetine induces atomoxetine metabolism, so atomoxetine levels fall and the ADHD dose should be increased soon", "B": "The two drugs share no pathway, so only a mild increase in nausea is expected", "C": "Fluoxetine inhibits CYP2D6 and raises atomoxetine exposure, so the dose needs prescriber review", "D": "Fluoxetine blocks atomoxetine at the receptor, so he should take them 12 hours apart"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Fluoxetine inhibits CYP2D6 and raises atomoxetine exposure, so the dose needs prescriber review): Atomoxetine is metabolized mainly by CYP2D6, and strong inhibitors such as fluoxetine or paroxetine can multiply its exposure several-fold, increasing adverse effects such as palpitations and mood changes. The labelled approach is a lower dose or slower titration with monitoring. Spacing the doses does not change a metabolic interaction."}, {"id": 1868, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "During a cardiac arrest in a community hospital, a resident asks the pharmacist how much epinephrine is in the prefilled 10 mL syringe labelled 1:10 000 for a 76 year old patient. A 1:1000 ampoule also sits on the crash cart tray. What is the total epinephrine content of the 10 mL syringe?", "options": {"A": "1 mg in total, since 1:10 000 equals 0.1 mg per mL", "B": "10 mg in total, since 1:10 000 equals 1 mg per mL of solution", "C": "0.1 mg in total, since 1:10 000 equals 0.01 mg per mL of solution", "D": "100 mg in total, since 1:10 000 equals 10 mg per mL of solution"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (1 mg in total, since 1:10 000 equals 0.1 mg per mL): A ratio of 1:10 000 means 1 g in 10 000 mL, which is 0.1 mg per mL. Ten millilitres therefore hold 1 mg. The 1:1000 ampoule is ten times stronger at 1 mg per mL, so mixing up the two products gives a tenfold error."}, {"id": 1869, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 7, "question": "OL is a 79 year old woman with a creatinine clearance of 40 mL/min who is prescribed meperidine 50 mg orally every six hours for pain after a fracture. The pharmacist notices she is also taking sertraline. What should the pharmacist recommend to the prescriber?", "options": {"A": "Replace meperidine, because its neurotoxic metabolite accumulates in older adults and it interacts with sertraline", "B": "Continue meperidine but lengthen the interval to every twelve hours, since only the dose frequency matters", "C": "Add a naloxone prescription and continue meperidine, since sertraline only interacts with other antidepressants", "D": "Switch to codeine 30 mg every four hours, since codeine is unaffected by aging"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Replace meperidine, because its neurotoxic metabolite accumulates in older adults and it interacts with sertraline): Meperidine is converted to normeperidine, which accumulates with reduced renal function and can cause tremor, confusion and seizures. It also has serotonergic activity, so combining it with an SSRI raises the risk of serotonin syndrome. Codeine is affected by age and renal function as well, and naloxone does not address either problem."}, {"id": 1870, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "A technician has prepared insulin glargine for DK, a 63 year old man, with a label reading 30 units at bedtime. The prescription specifies Lantus 100 units/mL, but the pen selected from stock is Toujeo 300 units/mL. What should the pharmacist do during the final check?", "options": {"A": "Dispense the pen and tell the patient to reduce the dose to 10 units daily", "B": "Ask the patient which pen he has used before and release whichever product he names", "C": "Keep the pen, since the unit dose is the same, and add a label note", "D": "Replace the pen with the prescribed 100 units/mL product and reprepare the label"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Replace the pen with the prescribed 100 units/mL product and reprepare the label): The dispensed product must match the prescribed concentration because insulin concentrations are not interchangeable at the point of dispensing without prescriber involvement. Adding a label note or a patient-calculated dose adds risk, and relying on the patient's recall of earlier pens does not verify what was prescribed."}, {"id": 1871, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "A hospital physician phones a community pharmacy on a weeknight and asks for a patient's current medication list before surgery tomorrow morning. The patient has not been asked about sharing it. The pharmacist is satisfied the call is genuine. What is the appropriate action?", "options": {"A": "Verify the caller, then share the list, because care providers can rely on implied consent", "B": "Fax the list to the hospital's main number tomorrow, since the information is not needed urgently", "C": "Refuse until the patient signs a written release, since consent must be expressed for all hospitals", "D": "Provide the list together with the complete dispensing history for the past ten years for completeness"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Verify the caller, then share the list, because care providers can rely on implied consent): Health privacy laws allow information to be shared within the circle of care on implied consent when it is needed for care. The pharmacist should confirm the caller's identity and share what is needed, not the whole history."}, {"id": 1872, "domain": "communication_and_collaboration", "chapter": 11, "question": "SE, a 66 year old woman, runs out of her atorvastatin 40 mg before her physician appointment. The pharmacist, authorized by provincial regulation to renew prescriptions, provides a one-month renewal to avoid a gap in therapy. What should the pharmacist do afterward to support collaboration with the prescriber?", "options": {"A": "Notify the prescriber of the renewal and the reason, following provincial communication standards", "B": "File the renewal record in the pharmacy and let SE tell her physician at the appointment", "C": "Wait for the prescriber to contact the pharmacy, since pharmacists need not initiate communication about renewals", "D": "Send the prescriber a request for approval before the renewal can be considered valid for billing"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Notify the prescriber of the renewal and the reason, following provincial communication standards): When pharmacists renew or adapt prescriptions under provincial authority, they are expected to inform the original prescriber so the record and care plan stay aligned. Relying on the patient to relay the information is unreliable. Approval is not required after the fact, so the communication is notification rather than permission."}, {"id": 1873, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A pharmacist helping to appraise a planned trial notes that the investigators intend to halve the non-inferiority margin for the primary outcome from 5 percentage points to 2.5 percentage points, with all other assumptions unchanged. How would this change affect the sample size required?", "options": {"A": "It would remain the same, because sample size depends only on event rate and significance level", "B": "It would increase about twofold, because sample size varies with the inverse of the margin", "C": "It would decrease by about half, because a smaller margin makes the difference easier to detect", "D": "It would increase about fourfold, because sample size varies with the inverse square of margin"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (It would increase about fourfold, because sample size varies with the inverse square of margin): Required sample size is proportional to one over the margin squared. Halving the margin therefore multiplies the sample size by about four. A tighter margin asks the trial to rule out smaller losses of efficacy, which needs far more participants."}, {"id": 1874, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 4, "question": "FB is a 62 year old man with scalp psoriasis and thick scale, and asks for a product that is easy to wash out of hair. The pharmacist is comparing a betamethasone valerate scalp lotion with an ointment. Which vehicle is generally preferred for hair bearing areas of the scalp?", "options": {"A": "A lotion, solution or foam, which spreads easily through hair and rinses out cleanly", "B": "A cream, because its water content makes it the only vehicle suited to scaly plaques", "C": "A paste, because its stiff base stays in place and is easy to remove from hair", "D": "A greasy ointment, because occlusion improves penetration and hair bearing skin is thin and fragile"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A lotion, solution or foam, which spreads easily through hair and rinses out cleanly): Hair bearing areas are best treated with lotions, solutions or foams, which spread through hair and wash out without leaving residue. Ointments and pastes are greasy and hard to remove from hair. Creams are not the only suitable vehicle, and thick scale is better softened with a keratolytic."}, {"id": 1875, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "NK is a 31 year old woman at 10 weeks of pregnancy who has moderate migraine attacks with nausea. She takes no regular medication and asks the pharmacist what she can use for her next attack. What is the best advice?", "options": {"A": "Use ibuprofen 600 mg every six hours, since NSAIDs are preferred during early pregnancy", "B": "Use sumatriptan 100 mg at the start of each attack, since triptans are the preferred class", "C": "Acetaminophen is preferred, and metoclopramide may be added for nausea if medically needed", "D": "Use ergotamine at onset of the attack, since it is safer in pregnancy than acetaminophen"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Acetaminophen is preferred, and metoclopramide may be added for nausea if medically needed): Acetaminophen is the preferred analgesic for migraine in pregnancy, and metoclopramide has reasonable safety data for nausea. Ergotamine is contraindicated because it causes uterine contraction and vasoconstriction. Triptans are used only when benefit clearly outweighs risk, and routine NSAID use is avoided in pregnancy."}, {"id": 1876, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 3, "question": "PB is a 58 year old woman with hepatitis C genotype 3 and decompensated cirrhosis (Child-Pugh class C, with recent ascites and encephalopathy). A specialist has written glecaprevir/pibrentasvir for eight weeks. The pharmacist reviewing the order before dispensing should identify which problem?", "options": {"A": "Glecaprevir is a protease inhibitor contraindicated in decompensated cirrhosis, so another regimen is needed", "B": "Encephalopathy rules out direct acting antiviral therapy, so treatment should wait until the cirrhosis improves", "C": "The regimen needs a dose reduction to half strength, since reduced liver function slows drug clearance", "D": "Eight weeks is too short for genotype 3, so the course should extend to 24 weeks"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Glecaprevir is a protease inhibitor contraindicated in decompensated cirrhosis, so another regimen is needed): Protease inhibitor containing regimens such as glecaprevir/pibrentasvir are contraindicated in Child-Pugh B and C cirrhosis because drug exposure rises and hepatic events have occurred. Sofosbuvir/velpatasvir, usually with ribavirin, is a standard option for decompensated disease. Genotype 3 without cirrhosis can use eight weeks, and dose reduction is not the solution."}, {"id": 1877, "domain": "leadership_and_stewardship", "chapter": 11, "question": "A father asks to use leftover amoxicillin suspension from a course his other child finished last month for his three year old daughter's new ear pain. She has a temperature of 39.1 C, is pulling at her ear, and has been irritable since last night. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Advise against using it, and arrange prompt assessment for a possible infection needing a prescription", "B": "Allow it because the drug worked before, and measure the dose from the old label", "C": "Recommend adding ibuprofen and giving the leftover antibiotic at half the labelled dose", "D": "Allow use for two days only, and recommend assessment if she is not better afterward"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Advise against using it, and arrange prompt assessment for a possible infection needing a prescription): Leftover antibiotics were dosed for another child's weight and condition, may be expired or incomplete, and treat an uncertain diagnosis. A young child with high fever and ear pain needs assessment to confirm otitis media and choose the right drug and dose. Pain control can be offered meanwhile, but a half dose promotes resistance without treating the infection."}, {"id": 1878, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "A patient asks the pharmacist why expired hormone and antibiotic tablets should go to a pharmacy take-back program rather than down the toilet, since the water gets treated anyway at the municipal plant. The pharmacist wants to give an accurate and brief explanation. Which explanation is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "Plants remove most residues, but pharmacies need the drugs returned to prevent misuse by other patients", "B": "Residues in water do not harm fish but can be traced back to the patient", "C": "Flushing is acceptable for antibiotics only, because bacteria in water break down those drugs quickly", "D": "Treatment plants are not designed to remove most drug residues, which can then harm aquatic life"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Treatment plants are not designed to remove most drug residues, which can then harm aquatic life): Wastewater plants are built to handle organic waste, not pharmaceuticals, so residues pass through into rivers and lakes where they can affect fish and other life. Take-back programs avoid that route. The other explanations misstate how treatment works or invent a rule about antibiotics."}, {"id": 1879, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 3, "question": "JW has primary syphilis with a painless chancre and is to receive a single 2.4 million unit dose of benzathine penicillin G. The clinic supplies the product at 600,000 units/mL. The nurse asks the pharmacist how to give the dose. What should be advised?", "options": {"A": "Give 8 mL, divided into two injections of 4 mL, in separate sites", "B": "Give 4 mL intravenously over 5 minutes to speed the effect of the antibiotic", "C": "Give 4 mL, divided into two injections of 2 mL in separate gluteal sites", "D": "Give 2 mL as a single injection into the deltoid muscle"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Give 4 mL, divided into two injections of 2 mL in separate gluteal sites): 2.4 million units at 600,000 units/mL is 4 mL, usually split into two deep intramuscular injections to limit pain. Benzathine penicillin must never be given intravenously because of the risk of cardiopulmonary arrest. The other volumes underdose or overdose the product."}, {"id": 1880, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 1, "question": "CM is a 59 year old woman on apixaban 5 mg twice daily for a pulmonary embolism. She usually takes it at 8 a.m. and 8 p.m. and telephones the pharmacy at 3 p.m. because she forgot her morning dose. What should she be told?", "options": {"A": "skip the morning dose and take only the evening dose at the usual time", "B": "wait until tomorrow morning, then restart with a 10 mg dose to restore anticoagulation", "C": "take the missed dose now and a double dose this evening to make up for it", "D": "take the missed dose now, then resume the usual twice daily schedule without doubling"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (take the missed dose now, then resume the usual twice daily schedule without doubling): For a missed apixaban dose, the patient takes it as soon as remembered on the same day and then resumes the regular schedule. She does not double the next dose, because extra dosing increases bleeding risk."}, {"id": 1881, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 7, "question": "A mother planning a long car trip asks for diphenhydramine to help her 4 year old son sleep, saying a parenting website recommended a small dose. He is healthy and has no allergies. What should the pharmacist advise about this plan?", "options": {"A": "It should not be used for sleep, since children may become agitated or overdose", "B": "It is reasonable for a single trip, since one dose does not cause side effects", "C": "It is safe for sleep at half the adult dose, since children under six respond predictably", "D": "It is acceptable if given with a dose of acetaminophen, since this reduces drowsiness effects"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (It should not be used for sleep, since children may become agitated or overdose): Diphenhydramine is not recommended as a sleep aid in young children because it can cause paradoxical agitation, hallucinations, rapid heart rate and serious overdose. Dose reduction does not make the effect predictable. Combining it with acetaminophen does not change the risk."}, {"id": 1882, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 1, "question": "BQ is a 58 year old woman with atrial fibrillation who had a mechanical mitral valve replacement five years ago. She takes warfarin and her INR is 2.7. A new hospital resident writes a discharge prescription changing warfarin to apixaban 5 mg twice daily. What should the pharmacist do?", "options": {"A": "Dispense apixaban, because direct oral anticoagulants are preferred over warfarin in all patients with atrial fibrillation", "B": "Dispense apixaban at 2.5 mg twice daily, because her valve type calls for a lower dose", "C": "Dispense apixaban after a one week overlap with warfarin until the INR falls below 2.0", "D": "Call the resident, since mechanical valves require warfarin and direct oral anticoagulants are contraindicated"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Call the resident, since mechanical valves require warfarin and direct oral anticoagulants are contraindicated): Trials of dabigatran in patients with mechanical valves showed more thromboembolism and bleeding than warfarin, so direct oral anticoagulants are contraindicated with mechanical valves. Warfarin is the preferred anticoagulant, and a dose reduction or overlap strategy does not make a direct oral anticoagulant acceptable."}, {"id": 1883, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "A caller says she is a nurse at a nearby clinic and urgently needs a patient's medication history for a visit in progress. The pharmacist cannot verify her identity and the caller ID is blocked. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Share the list after asking her to name two of the patient's current medications from memory", "B": "Refuse to share by phone and ask the clinic physician to send a written request instead", "C": "Share the full history now, since delay could harm the patient and nurses are caregivers", "D": "Call back through the clinic's published number, then share only what is needed for care"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Call back through the clinic's published number, then share only what is needed for care): Information can be shared with the care team, but the pharmacist must be reasonably sure of the caller's identity. Calling back through an independently listed number confirms the caller, and sharing only what is needed limits the risk."}, {"id": 1884, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "A prescription for a fentanyl 25 mcg/h transdermal patch directs the patient to change one patch every 72 hours, and the prescriber has authorized a supply of 10 patches. The pharmacist is completing the label and records for this narcotic. For how many days will the supply last?", "options": {"A": "Thirty days, since each of the ten patches is changed every seventy two hours", "B": "Ten days, treating each patch as a single day of therapy for the patient", "C": "Twenty four days, assuming each patch is changed after fifty eight hours of wear", "D": "Twenty days, calculating one patch every two days for each patch dispensed in the quantity"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Thirty days, since each of the ten patches is changed every seventy two hours): Each patch lasts 72 hours, which is 3 days. Ten patches therefore provide 10 times 3, or 30 days of therapy. The other durations assume the wrong wear time per patch."}, {"id": 1885, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 3, "question": "AP is a healthy 30 year old man with no chronic conditions who has had mild influenza symptoms, a low fever and cough, for four days. He is improving and not short of breath. He asks the pharmacist whether he should get oseltamivir. What advice is best?", "options": {"A": "Start oseltamivir at double dose, since late treatment needs higher exposure to be useful", "B": "Oseltamivir is recommended for everyone with influenza, so start it today for five days", "C": "Use oseltamivir at the standard dose to prevent him from spreading influenza to household contacts", "D": "Antiviral therapy is not expected to help much now, so use symptom relief and rest"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Antiviral therapy is not expected to help much now, so use symptom relief and rest): Oseltamivir gives only a modest benefit and works best within 48 hours in people who are severely ill or at high risk. A healthy adult with mild, improving illness on day four is unlikely to benefit. Higher doses are not studied for late treatment, and treating to limit spread is not an indication."}, {"id": 1886, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 4, "question": "VR is a 19 year old man weighing 65 kg with severe nodular acne. His dermatologist orders isotretinoin 1 mg/kg daily and aims for a cumulative dose of 150 mg/kg. He asks how many days the course should last if every dose is taken as scheduled. Which estimate is correct?", "options": {"A": "300 days, because the target is divided by an assumed average dose of 0.5 mg/kg daily", "B": "65 days, because the 65 kg body weight sets the days at one for each kilogram", "C": "225 days, because the target is divided by a daily dose of 0.67 mg/kg", "D": "150 days, because the target in mg/kg divided by the daily mg/kg dose gives days"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (150 days, because the target in mg/kg divided by the daily mg/kg dose gives days): Both quantities are per kilogram, so the weight cancels: 150 mg/kg divided by 1 mg/kg daily equals 150 days. Using body weight as a day count, or dividing by a daily dose different from the ordered 1 mg/kg, gives the other results."}, {"id": 1887, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "A pharmacy team ran a two week trial of automated refill reminder calls with the aim of raising on-time refills from 60% to 70%. The trial recorded 63% on-time refills. The team now meets to examine the data. Which discussion belongs in the Study stage of the PDSA cycle?", "options": {"A": "Deciding to roll out the reminder calls to every location because some improvement was achieved", "B": "Comparing 63% with the 70% aim and exploring why the gain was smaller than predicted", "C": "Writing a prediction of the expected refill rate and selecting the measure for the next test", "D": "Training technicians on the call script and starting the reminder calls on the first trial day"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Comparing 63% with the 70% aim and exploring why the gain was smaller than predicted): The Study stage analyzes the data against the prediction and aim, and asks what was learned and why results differed. Deciding to roll out or adapt belongs to Act, writing predictions and measures belongs to Plan, and starting calls after training is part of Do."}, {"id": 1888, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "OA is a 26 year old man who started olanzapine 15 mg daily three months ago. His baseline weight was 80 kg, and the clinic scale shows 86 kg today. His fasting glucose is unchanged. What percentage of baseline weight has he gained, and what does it signify?", "options": {"A": "7.5 percent, which exceeds the 7 percent mark that should prompt a metabolic intervention", "B": "10 percent, calculated against an ideal weight of 60 kg, which signals a need for intervention", "C": "6 percent, because the gain in kilograms equals the percentage and is below the 7 percent", "D": "6.98 percent, found by dividing the gain by his current weight, which is below 7 percent"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (7.5 percent, which exceeds the 7 percent mark that should prompt a metabolic intervention): Percent change is calculated against baseline weight: 6 kg divided by 80 kg equals 7.5 percent. A gain of 7 percent or more from baseline is a commonly used trigger for lifestyle intervention, metformin consideration, or a switch to a weight-neutral agent. Dividing by current weight gives 6.98 percent and understates the change."}, {"id": 1889, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A urine pregnancy test is evaluated in a clinic population of 1,000 women. Of the 200 who test positive, 190 are truly pregnant. Of the 800 who test negative, 780 are truly not pregnant. A pharmacist calculates the positive predictive value and the negative predictive value. Which pair of values results?", "options": {"A": "Positive predictive value is 95% and negative predictive value is 97.5% in this clinic", "B": "Sensitivity is 95% and specificity is 97.5%, since both use the same denominators in this table", "C": "Positive predictive value is 19% and negative predictive value is 78% when using all women", "D": "Positive predictive value is 97.5% and negative predictive value is 95% in this clinic sample"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Positive predictive value is 95% and negative predictive value is 97.5% in this clinic): PPV is the proportion of positive tests that are true: 190 of 200, or 95%. NPV is the proportion of negative tests that are true: 780 of 800, or 97.5%. Sensitivity and specificity use the disease status columns, not the test result rows, and the 19% and 78% values divide by the wrong total."}, {"id": 1890, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "The charge nurse at a long-term care home telephones the pharmacy to relay the attending physician's verbal order for furosemide 20 mg daily for a resident with leg edema. Provincial standards permit an agent to relay verbal orders for non-controlled drugs. What must the pharmacist do to accept the order?", "options": {"A": "Record only the drug, dose and the nurse's name as the order's source", "B": "Dispense a one month supply with refills, since long-term care orders carry standing refill authority", "C": "Record the prescriber, date and order details, then have the nurse read it back", "D": "Wait for the physician's signed written order before dispensing any quantity of furosemide"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Record the prescriber, date and order details, then have the nurse read it back): A verbal order for a non-controlled drug can be accepted when the pharmacist records who prescribed it, the date and the full order details, and confirms the content by having the person relaying it read it back. The prescriber's identity, not only the relaying nurse's name, belongs in the record. A signed written order and standing refills are not required by the standard described."}, {"id": 1891, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "PJ is a 60 year old woman with cancer pain taking hydromorphone 24 mg daily with poor relief and side effects. The prescriber faxes an order to switch to methadone using a fixed one to one ratio of milligrams. What should the pharmacist do?", "options": {"A": "Halve the methadone dose herself and dispense, since methadone is twice as potent", "B": "Contact the prescriber, because methadone conversion ratios vary with dose and need cautious expert supervision", "C": "Dispense as written but advise taking it every 4 hours, as with other opioids", "D": "Dispense as written, because a fixed ratio is reliable across all hydromorphone doses"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Contact the prescriber, because methadone conversion ratios vary with dose and need cautious expert supervision): Methadone conversion ratios change with the prior opioid dose, and methadone has a long and variable half-life, so accumulation can cause delayed respiratory depression and QT prolongation. A fixed one to one ratio is not valid, and the pharmacist should seek a supervised conversion plan before dispensing."}, {"id": 1892, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "A technician made 20 powder papers by triturating 100 mg of a potent drug with lactose to a total of 1,000 mg, then weighing 500 mg of that trituration into each paper. Each paper was meant to contain 5 mg of drug. The technician asks the pharmacist to check them. What should the pharmacist do?", "options": {"A": "Stop, discard the papers, and remake them with 50 mg of trituration in each", "B": "Release the papers with a warning label advising the patient to take half of each one", "C": "Remove half of the powder from each paper and release them once the weights match", "D": "Release the papers because the trituration was prepared correctly, then label them with dosing directions"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Stop, discard the papers, and remake them with 50 mg of trituration in each): The trituration is 10% drug, so 5 mg of drug requires 50 mg of trituration, not 500 mg. Each paper as made contains 50 mg of the potent drug, ten times the intended dose. The papers cannot be corrected by trimming or labelling, so they must be discarded and remade."}, {"id": 1893, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 3, "question": "On a surgical ward, FH is a 66 year old man on vancomycin for MRSA bacteremia. His serum creatinine has risen from 90 to 150 umol/L over two days, and a level drawn before the fourth dose is 24 mg/L. The next dose is due in one hour. What should the pharmacist recommend?", "options": {"A": "Switch to gentamicin because it clears faster in renal impairment and does not accumulate", "B": "Hold the dose and reassess using a repeat level and renal function before restarting", "C": "Give half of the usual dose now and double the interval, without repeating any level", "D": "Give the usual dose on time and recheck the creatinine in one week"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Hold the dose and reassess using a repeat level and renal function before restarting): A rising creatinine with a high pre-dose level shows that vancomycin is accumulating and the kidney is already stressed. Holding the dose and re-measuring prevents more toxicity while the infection is still treated. Gentamicin is nephrotoxic and accumulates in renal impairment, so it is no safer."}, {"id": 1894, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A company designs a non-inferiority trial of a cheaper oral antibiotic for uncomplicated urinary tract infection in women, using clinical cure at 7 days as the outcome. A formulary pharmacist is asked which approach to choosing the margin would make the trial most credible. Which approach is best?", "options": {"A": "Pick the largest margin that keeps the sample size small, because a smaller trial is cheaper", "B": "Set the margin equal to the observed difference between the two drugs in the pilot study", "C": "Preserve a fraction of the comparator's proven benefit over placebo, using a clinically justified loss", "D": "Use a margin of one percentage point whatever loss of cure rate patients would accept"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Preserve a fraction of the comparator's proven benefit over placebo, using a clinically justified loss): A credible margin is based on the established effect of the comparator over placebo and on the largest loss of efficacy that clinicians and patients would still accept. Choosing a margin for convenience or sample size defeats the purpose, and a pilot difference gives no clinical justification."}, {"id": 1895, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "JM is a 66 year old man with painful diabetic neuropathy taking gabapentin 600 mg three times daily and oxycodone 10 mg every six hours for back pain. His wife says he has been unusually drowsy and slow to respond since the oxycodone was increased last week. What should the pharmacist do?", "options": {"A": "Reassure the family that drowsiness is expected and will settle without any change to doses", "B": "Recommend adding a stimulant such as caffeine tablets to keep him alert during the day", "C": "Assess breathing and alertness, then ask the prescriber to lower the opioid or gabapentin dose", "D": "Advise him to take gabapentin only at night and keep the oxycodone schedule the same"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Assess breathing and alertness, then ask the prescriber to lower the opioid or gabapentin dose): Gabapentinoids and opioids both depress the central nervous system, and their combination raises the risk of sedation and respiratory depression, especially after a recent opioid increase. New drowsiness needs assessment and a dose reduction of one or both agents. Waiting it out or adding a stimulant leaves the interaction in place."}, {"id": 1896, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "OT is a 60 year old man who takes metformin immediate-release 500 mg twice daily with meals and reports stomach upset and loose stools. The prescriber considers metformin extended-release at the same total daily amount, to be taken once a day. Which regimen matches his current daily dose?", "options": {"A": "Metformin extended-release 500 mg once daily, taken with the morning meal after breakfast", "B": "Metformin extended-release 1000 mg twice daily, taken with the two main meals each day", "C": "Metformin extended-release 2000 mg once daily, taken with the evening meal and a snack", "D": "Metformin extended-release 1000 mg once daily, taken with the evening meal each day"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Metformin extended-release 1000 mg once daily, taken with the evening meal each day): His current total is 500 mg x 2 = 1000 mg per day, which is given as one extended-release dose with the evening meal. Half that amount would under-treat him, and doubling or quadrupling the exposure exceeds the intended dose."}, {"id": 1897, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 1, "question": "LF is a 67 year old woman three months after an acute coronary syndrome who takes ticagrelor 90 mg twice daily with acetylsalicylic acid 81 mg. She develops a fungal infection and her doctor prescribes oral ketoconazole 200 mg daily. The pharmacist reviews her profile. Which action should be taken?", "options": {"A": "Dispense ketoconazole and advise taking it twelve hours apart from ticagrelor to avoid the interaction", "B": "Dispense ketoconazole and replace acetylsalicylic acid with clopidogrel for the length of the antifungal course", "C": "Suggest terbinafine instead, because strong CYP3A inhibitors greatly raise ticagrelor levels and bleeding risk", "D": "Dispense ketoconazole and lower ticagrelor to 60 mg twice daily during treatment to offset exposure"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Suggest terbinafine instead, because strong CYP3A inhibitors greatly raise ticagrelor levels and bleeding risk): Ketoconazole is a strong CYP3A4 inhibitor, and combining it with ticagrelor increases ticagrelor exposure several fold, which raises bleeding risk and is to be avoided. Terbinafine does not inhibit CYP3A4. Dose spacing does not remove an inhibition that lasts for days, and swapping the acetylsalicylic acid does not address the problem."}, {"id": 1898, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "PQ is a 74 year old man who has taken oxycodone 10 mg every 6 hours for several months. A different prescriber has sent a new prescription for lorazepam 1 mg three times daily for anxiety. The pharmacy assistant says both prescriptions are valid and ready to fill. What should the pharmacist do?", "options": {"A": "Contact the prescriber about additive respiratory depression, and counsel PQ about sedation and falls", "B": "Fill the prescription because each prescription is valid and the prescribers are responsible for the combination", "C": "Fill the prescription at half the written dose until the next appointment with the new prescriber", "D": "Fill the prescription and ask PQ to stop the oxycodone until his family doctor reviews it"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Contact the prescriber about additive respiratory depression, and counsel PQ about sedation and falls): Combining an opioid and a benzodiazepine increases the risk of respiratory depression, sedation and falls, especially in older adults, so a valid prescription still needs pharmacist assessment. The pharmacist should discuss it with the prescriber and counsel the patient. Halving a dose or stopping the opioid without the prescriber is outside the pharmacist's role."}, {"id": 1899, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "DK is a 49 year old man with asthma whose potassium is 3.0 mmol/L after repeated high dose salbutamol nebulizers in the emergency department. He has no ECG changes and takes no diuretic. What is the most likely mechanism and appropriate response?", "options": {"A": "Salbutamol increases urinary potassium loss through the kidney so a large intravenous potassium bolus is needed", "B": "Beta agonists shift potassium into cells, so monitor and replace only if levels stay low", "C": "The low value reflects dilution from nebulizer saline so no monitoring or treatment is needed", "D": "Salbutamol causes magnesium loss so magnesium sulfate must be given before potassium is replaced"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Beta agonists shift potassium into cells, so monitor and replace only if levels stay low): Beta-2 agonists stimulate the sodium potassium pump and move potassium into cells, producing a transient fall in serum potassium without a true body deficit. Aggressive replacement risks rebound hyperkalemia. Levels are rechecked and replaced cautiously if they remain low."}, {"id": 1900, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 1, "question": "ET is an 86 year old woman weighing 48 kg with an estimated creatinine clearance of 28 mL/min. She has atrial fibrillation with heart failure, and her physician plans to start digoxin. Which starting maintenance dose is most appropriate? She takes furosemide and no other interacting drugs.", "options": {"A": "0.25 mg once daily, since this is the standard adult maintenance dose in atrial fibrillation", "B": "0.0625 to 0.125 mg once daily, with a level checked after steady state is reached", "C": "0.25 mg twice daily, since kidney impairment lowers the drug's volume of distribution", "D": "0.5 mg once daily, since a higher dose is needed to control rate in heart failure"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (0.0625 to 0.125 mg once daily, with a level checked after steady state is reached): Digoxin is cleared mainly by the kidneys, and older, low-weight patients with reduced clearance need lower doses to avoid toxicity. Standard 0.25 mg or higher doses would likely produce toxic concentrations in her."}, {"id": 1901, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "A prescriber orders a prednisone taper using 5 mg tablets: 40 mg daily for 3 days, then 30 mg for 3 days, then 20 mg for 3 days, then 10 mg for 3 days, then stop. How many tablets should the pharmacist dispense for the whole taper?", "options": {"A": "20 tablets, found by adding the tablets for each step but ignoring the three days", "B": "96 tablets, found by counting all twelve days at the full 40 mg dose", "C": "63 tablets, found by adding a final 5 mg step that was not ordered", "D": "60 tablets, found by summing daily tablets across the four three-day steps"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (60 tablets, found by summing daily tablets across the four three-day steps): Each day uses 8, 6, 4 and 2 tablets across the four steps, a total of 20 tablets per set of days. Each step lasts three days, so 20 multiplied by 3 gives 60 tablets. Forgetting the three-day duration gives 20, and treating every day as 40 mg wrongly gives 96."}, {"id": 1902, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 4, "question": "RB is a 50 year old man whose dermatitis involves the face and neck. His physician orders desonide 0.05% cream once daily for 7 days. The pharmacist knows that the face and neck of an adult takes 2.5 fingertip units per application, and one fingertip unit is 0.5 g. What quantity is needed for the course?", "options": {"A": "About 17.5 g, treating each of the 2.5 fingertip units as a full gram per day", "B": "About 8.75 g, from 1.25 g per application applied once daily for seven days", "C": "About 12.6 g, calculated from 1.8 g per daily application for seven days", "D": "About 4.4 g, calculated from half the face and neck area for the full seven days"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (About 8.75 g, from 1.25 g per application applied once daily for seven days): Each application needs 2.5 x 0.5 g = 1.25 g. Once daily for seven days gives 1.25 x 7 = 8.75 g. Counting units as grams, halving the area or using an invented per application amount produce the other totals."}, {"id": 1903, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "A patient on apixaban asks the regulated pharmacy technician at the counter whether she can take ibuprofen for her knee pain. She is 72 years old and mentions occasional nosebleeds. The pharmacist is available in the dispensary. Which response is appropriate within the technician's role?", "options": {"A": "Recommend a low-dose acetaminophen product, since technicians may select products for common minor pain complaints", "B": "Refer her to the pharmacist for assessment and advice on safe pain relief", "C": "Read her the warnings in the apixaban monograph and let her decide whether to buy ibuprofen", "D": "Advise her that occasional ibuprofen is fine, as cautions apply mainly to daily long-term use"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Refer her to the pharmacist for assessment and advice on safe pain relief): Choosing pain relief for a patient on an anticoagulant who has nosebleeds requires an assessment of bleeding risk, which is a pharmacist function. The technician's role is to recognize this and bring in the pharmacist. Giving advice, reading the monograph and leaving the decision to the patient, or selecting a product are all clinical decisions outside a technician's scope."}, {"id": 1904, "domain": "leadership_and_stewardship", "chapter": 11, "question": "A pharmacist performs blood pressure screening on a 60 year old woman in the pharmacy aisle. She just walked in from the parking lot and has her sleeve rolled tightly above the elbow. Which step best improves the accuracy of the reading?", "options": {"A": "Measure at once with the cuff over the rolled sleeve, since delay lowers the reading", "B": "Take one reading after she explains her symptoms, using a small cuff for comfort", "C": "Measure on the arm with the lower reading expected, resting on her lap below heart level", "D": "Seat her for five minutes, back supported, feet flat, with the arm at heart level"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Seat her for five minutes, back supported, feet flat, with the arm at heart level): Accurate readings need five minutes of quiet seated rest, back supported, feet flat, no talking, and the arm supported at heart level on a bare upper arm with a correctly sized cuff. A tight sleeve can act as a tourniquet and raise the reading. An arm below heart level and a cuff that is too small both overestimate pressure."}, {"id": 1905, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "A pharmacy chain offers its pharmacists a monthly bonus based on the number of prescriptions transferred from competitors and the number of vaccination appointments booked. Pharmacist KY worries this may encourage unneeded recommendations to patients. Which principle best addresses this concern?", "options": {"A": "Incentives are acceptable when they reward only vaccination, because vaccines are a preventive public health service", "B": "Bonus programs are prohibited by the Food and Drugs Act, so the offer is unlawful", "C": "Judgment must stay independent of incentives, and recommendations must rest on patient need alone", "D": "Pharmacists may follow any employer bonus structure provided the patient signs a form acknowledging the incentive"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Judgment must stay independent of incentives, and recommendations must rest on patient need alone): Professional judgment must stay independent of financial incentives, and recommendations should rest on patient need. A signed acknowledgement does not cure an incentive that pushes unneeded recommendations. Performance bonuses are not banned by the Food and Drugs Act, and rewarding vaccination alone does not remove the pressure to over-recommend."}, {"id": 1906, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 3, "question": "KQ is a 4 year old boy weighing 16 kg with community-acquired pneumonia treated at home. The physician orders amoxicillin 90 mg/kg/day divided into two doses. The suspension strength is 250 mg per 5 mL. What volume should each dose be?", "options": {"A": "Give 28.8 mL of suspension once daily, which provides 1440 mg in a single dose", "B": "Give 14.4 mL of the suspension twice daily, which provides 720 mg per dose", "C": "Give 7.2 mL of the suspension twice daily, which provides 360 mg per dose", "D": "Give 9.6 mL of the suspension three times daily, which provides 480 mg with each dose"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Give 14.4 mL of the suspension twice daily, which provides 720 mg per dose): Daily dose = 90 x 16 = 1440 mg, so each of two doses is 720 mg. Volume = 720 mg / 250 mg x 5 mL = 14.4 mL. Giving the whole day in one dose or in three doses changes the ordered schedule, and 7.2 mL delivers half of the intended dose."}, {"id": 1907, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 1, "question": "RM is a 63 year old man with type 2 diabetes and hypertension who takes ramipril 10 mg daily, with a pressure of 146/88 mmHg and a creatinine of 98 umol/L. His physician writes a new prescription for valsartan 80 mg daily to add to his current therapy for extra pressure lowering. How should the pharmacist respond?", "options": {"A": "Dispense valsartan, and check potassium and creatinine in one week to monitor the dual therapy", "B": "Dispense valsartan and reduce ramipril to 2.5 mg, which makes the combination safe in diabetes", "C": "Replace ramipril with valsartan 160 mg, because an ARB provides superior protection in diabetic patients", "D": "Suggest amlodipine instead, since an ACE inhibitor plus an ARB adds harm without benefit"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Suggest amlodipine instead, since an ACE inhibitor plus an ARB adds harm without benefit): ACE inhibitor plus ARB combinations raise the risk of hyperkalemia, hypotension and acute kidney injury without improving cardiovascular or renal outcomes, so they should be avoided. A calcium channel blocker or thiazide-type diuretic is the sensible add-on. Monitoring or dose reduction does not remove the added harm."}, {"id": 1908, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 7, "question": "A mother of a 9 month old boy with a cough and runny nose asks whether a teaspoon of honey after dinner would help, as her aunt suggested. He has no allergies and is feeding well. What should the pharmacist advise?", "options": {"A": "Honey is acceptable if first heated, since cooking destroys the spores that cause illness", "B": "Honey is acceptable from six months of age, with half a teaspoon before bedtime only", "C": "Honey is acceptable if it is pasteurized, since processing removes the infant botulism risk", "D": "Honey must be avoided before 12 months because of infant botulism risk"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Honey must be avoided before 12 months because of infant botulism risk): Honey can contain Clostridium botulinum spores, which can germinate in an infant's immature gut and cause infant botulism, so honey is avoided before 12 months. Heating or pasteurizing does not destroy the spores. It may be considered for cough in older children."}, {"id": 1909, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A pharmacist is researching whether a rarely used antifungal can cause hearing loss for a physician's patient on a hospital ward. The only available evidence is two published case reports and no controlled studies. When formulating the response, the pharmacist should:", "options": {"A": "Decline to answer until randomized trials are published, and advise the physician to monitor weekly", "B": "Describe the limited quality of the evidence and its uncertainty along with a practical recommendation", "C": "State that no relationship exists because controlled studies have not been published for the drug", "D": "State that the drug causes hearing loss because published reports document the event in patients"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Describe the limited quality of the evidence and its uncertainty along with a practical recommendation): Case reports can raise a signal but cannot prove or exclude causation, so the response should state how weak the evidence is and still give a usable recommendation, such as monitoring or an alternative. Declaring a definite link or no link goes beyond the data. Refusing to answer does not help the physician."}, {"id": 1910, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "A 5 year old boy with impetigo is prescribed cephalexin 250 mg orally four times daily for 7 days. The pharmacy stocks cephalexin 250 mg/5 mL suspension. The prescriber has confirmed the dose suits his 18 kg weight. What total volume must be dispensed so the course can be completed?", "options": {"A": "105 mL, which is 5 mL per dose, three doses daily, for seven days", "B": "280 mL, which is 10 mL per dose, four doses daily, for seven days of therapy", "C": "140 mL, which is 5 mL per dose, four doses daily, for seven days", "D": "70 mL, which is 5 mL per dose, two doses daily, for seven days"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (140 mL, which is 5 mL per dose, four doses daily, for seven days): A 250 mg dose from 250 mg per 5 mL is 5 mL. Four doses daily is 20 mL, and seven days is 140 mL. Using three doses gives 105 mL, two doses gives 70 mL, and 10 mL per dose doubles the volume of each dose."}, {"id": 1911, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A pharmacist reads that a new anticoagulant produced a relative risk reduction of 30% for stroke in atrial fibrillation. Before recommending it to a prescriber, the pharmacist wants a statistic that reflects the real size of benefit for an average patient. Which additional figure is most useful?", "options": {"A": "The p value, because a smaller value shows that the size of benefit is larger", "B": "The 95% confidence interval width, because a narrower interval means a larger absolute benefit", "C": "The relative risk, because it is calculated independently of the baseline stroke rate in the trial", "D": "The absolute risk reduction, which reflects the baseline stroke rate seen in controls"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The absolute risk reduction, which reflects the baseline stroke rate seen in controls): A relative risk reduction does not show how many patients benefit because it ignores baseline risk. The absolute risk reduction applies the relative effect to the control group's stroke rate and allows the NNT to be derived. A p value reflects statistical certainty, not effect size, and interval width does not measure benefit."}, {"id": 1912, "domain": "communication_and_collaboration", "chapter": 11, "question": "RP is a 78 year old man who is hard of hearing and wears hearing aids. His daughter attends. He has been dispensed a new anticoagulant and changes to three other medicines, and he agrees with everything without asking questions. How should the pharmacist proceed?", "options": {"A": "Speak louder and quickly cover all four changes since he agrees with the plan", "B": "Direct the counselling to the daughter, who can hear well, and give her the written changes", "C": "Mail a written summary of the changes and telephone him next week with questions", "D": "Counsel him directly in a quiet room with written key points and teach-back"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Counsel him directly in a quiet room with written key points and teach-back): The patient is the person taking the medicines, so he should be counselled directly in a quiet setting, with written points and teach-back to verify understanding. Agreement alone does not show he heard, and phone follow-up is difficult for a person with hearing loss."}, {"id": 1913, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "HK is a 66 year old woman who receives intravenous gentamicin every 24 hours. A trough was ordered, but the nurse drew the sample 30 minutes after the infusion ended, and the level reads above the toxic threshold. The resident wants to hold all further doses. What should the pharmacist recommend?", "options": {"A": "Hold all further doses and switch to another antibiotic, since the toxic result is reliable", "B": "Recognize the sample was timed as a peak, redraw a true trough before the next dose", "C": "Halve the dose and keep the 24 hour interval, since the sample proves excess exposure", "D": "Give the next dose as planned and draw a peak after it, ignoring the current value"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Recognize the sample was timed as a peak, redraw a true trough before the next dose): A level drawn shortly after an infusion reflects peak concentration, which is naturally high and cannot be compared with a trough threshold. A correctly timed trough before the next dose answers the real question of accumulation. Acting on a mistimed sample could stop effective therapy."}, {"id": 1914, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 4, "question": "NO is a 71 year old woman with plaque psoriasis on her legs. She has used calcipotriol and betamethasone dipropionate ointment daily for 9 weeks, and the product monograph limits continuous use to about 4 weeks. She asks for a refill. Which action is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Hold the refill, explain the risks of prolonged use, and refer her to the prescriber", "B": "Dispense a smaller tube and tell her to apply it every second day instead of daily", "C": "Switch to a more potent steroid ointment for the legs and continue it for a month", "D": "Refill it, since psoriasis is chronic, and tell her to stop if the skin turns thin"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Hold the refill, explain the risks of prolonged use, and refer her to the prescriber): Use beyond the monograph limit raises the risk of skin atrophy, steroid effects and calcium disturbance from the vitamin D component. The pharmacist should withhold the refill and direct her to the prescriber for a maintenance plan. Switching to a stronger steroid or simply spacing applications keeps the problem going."}, {"id": 1915, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 6, "question": "VY is a 49 year old woman who takes kava capsules daily for anxiety and a green tea extract for weight loss. She reports fatigue, nausea and mild yellowing of her eyes. Her ALT is six times the upper limit of normal. What is the most appropriate advice?", "options": {"A": "Stop the kava only and continue the green tea extract, since it is a safe antioxidant", "B": "Stop both supplements and see her physician promptly for liver assessment", "C": "Switch to valerian root and a different weight loss supplement before liver tests are repeated", "D": "Continue both supplements at half the dose and repeat her liver tests in one month"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Stop both supplements and see her physician promptly for liver assessment): Both kava and concentrated green tea extract have been linked to hepatotoxicity, and Health Canada has issued advisories about kava. Jaundice with a marked ALT rise needs the suspected agents stopped and medical evaluation. Reducing the dose or swapping to another herbal product leaves her exposed to risk."}, {"id": 1916, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 4, "question": "KP is a 15 year old girl just prescribed an epinephrine auto-injector with a blue safety cap and an orange tip. At the pharmacy she practises with a trainer device and asks the pharmacist to confirm each step so that she can use it correctly in an emergency. Which sequence should the pharmacist teach?", "options": {"A": "Press the orange tip into the thigh, then pull off the blue cap to release it", "B": "Pull off the orange cap, press the blue end into the thigh, then count to ten", "C": "Pull off the blue cap, press the orange tip firmly into the outer thigh", "D": "Remove the blue cap, press the orange tip into the upper arm, and rub the site"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Pull off the blue cap, press the orange tip firmly into the outer thigh): The safety cap is removed before the device is pushed firmly against the outer thigh, which triggers the injection and produces a click. The needle end is the orange tip, so it must not be handled or placed against the skin until the cap is off. The upper arm is not the recommended site, and rubbing is not part of the steps."}, {"id": 1917, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 4, "question": "RS is a 76 year old woman weighing 58 kg with rheumatoid arthritis, newly prescribed methotrexate 15 mg weekly. Her serum creatinine is 110 umol/L. The pharmacist estimates her creatinine clearance with the Cockcroft-Gault equation before dispensing. Which result and conclusion are correct?", "options": {"A": "About 70 mL/min, so the usual 15 mg weekly dose is appropriate for her renal function", "B": "About 35 mL/min, so the pharmacist should discuss a lower methotrexate dose with the prescriber", "C": "About 3.5 mL/min, so methotrexate is contraindicated and must not be dispensed at any dose today", "D": "About 35 mL/min, but methotrexate is not renally cleared, so the dose needs no adjustment"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (About 35 mL/min, so the pharmacist should discuss a lower methotrexate dose with the prescriber): CrCl = (140 - 76) x 58 x 1.04 / 110 = 3860 / 110, about 35 mL/min. Methotrexate is mainly renally cleared, so reduced clearance raises toxicity risk and a lower dose should be discussed. A tenfold decimal slip or an assumption that the drug is not renally cleared leads to the wrong conclusion."}, {"id": 1918, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "A hospital pharmacist is asked by a daughter, the substitute decision maker, to start an antibiotic for her unconscious mother. The mother had clearly said, while capable, that she would refuse antibiotics at the end of life. How should the daughter decide?", "options": {"A": "Choose what the daughter personally believes is best, since a substitute replaces the patient's earlier views", "B": "Follow the wishes of the oldest family member present, since families decide jointly in most hospitals", "C": "Follow the mother's known prior capable wishes, using best interests only if none are known", "D": "Defer to the pharmacist's recommendation, because substitute decision makers lack authority to decline treatment"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Follow the mother's known prior capable wishes, using best interests only if none are known): A substitute decision maker must apply the patient's prior capable wishes that apply to the situation. Best interests become the standard only when no applicable wish is known, and the substitute may refuse treatment on the patient's behalf."}, {"id": 1919, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 4, "question": "TW is a 24 year old woman with a history of anaphylaxis to cashews who carries an epinephrine auto-injector. Her physician has just prescribed propranolol 40 mg twice daily for migraine prevention. The pharmacist should contact the prescriber because beta-blockers:", "options": {"A": "interact with the epinephrine preservative and shorten the shelf life of the device in storage", "B": "increase mast cell release of histamine, which makes food allergy reactions more frequent", "C": "block absorption of intramuscular epinephrine from the thigh and delay its onset by hours", "D": "may blunt the response to epinephrine, making anaphylaxis harder to treat and more severe"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (may blunt the response to epinephrine, making anaphylaxis harder to treat and more severe): Beta-blockers, especially non-selective ones, can make anaphylaxis more severe and reduce the response to epinephrine, so alternatives are usually chosen in patients at risk. They do not act on mast cells directly or alter epinephrine absorption or stability."}, {"id": 1920, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "During a monthly review of compounding records, a pharmacist finds that a 2% hydroquinone cream dispensed to a patient (CT) two days ago was prepared using 20 g of hydroquinone instead of 2 g per 100 g. CT has not yet reported any problem. The technician who prepared it is upset. What should the pharmacist do first?", "options": {"A": "Correct the compounding record and the master formulation first, then plan a staff training session", "B": "Contact CT promptly to stop using the cream and arrange its return, then inform the prescriber", "C": "Wait for CT to report a reaction before acting, since no harm has yet been reported", "D": "Prepare a correct replacement cream and deliver it at CT's next scheduled visit, explaining the delay"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Contact CT promptly to stop using the cream and arrange its return, then inform the prescriber): Patient safety is the priority. A tenfold overdose of hydroquinone can cause serious skin irritation, so the pharmacist should reach CT right away, retrieve the cream and tell the prescriber. Documentation, a corrected master record, incident reporting and retraining then follow, but none of them should delay protecting the patient."}, {"id": 1921, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "Digoxin .125 mg po daily, quantity 30, is written for a 78 year old woman with atrial fibrillation. The pharmacist notes that the dose could be misread as 125 mg. What is the best way to correct and record the dose?", "options": {"A": "Write 0.125 mg as 0.1250 mg to show the exact strength of the digoxin tablet", "B": "Write 0.125 mg with a leading zero, or 125 mcg, after prescriber confirmation", "C": "Write .125 mg in larger print so that the decimal point stands out on the label", "D": "Write 125 mg on the label, since the decimal point can be safely dropped"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Write 0.125 mg with a leading zero, or 125 mcg, after prescriber confirmation): A decimal with no zero before it can be missed, so .125 mg may be read as 125 mg, a 1000-fold overdose of digoxin. ISMP Canada recommends a leading zero (0.125 mg), or expressing the dose as 125 mcg, after confirming with the prescriber. A trailing zero, larger print or dropping the decimal does not remove the hazard."}, {"id": 1922, "domain": "leadership_and_stewardship", "chapter": 11, "question": "SM is a 58 year old woman who has taken oxycodone for chronic knee pain for four years and now uses 110 morphine milligram equivalents per day with no improvement in function. She asks the pharmacist about options. Which recommendation is consistent with current Canadian opioid guidance?", "options": {"A": "Stop the oxycodone today to avoid further dependence, and treat any withdrawal symptoms afterward", "B": "Increase the dose gradually until function improves, because the current dose is still within guidance", "C": "Switch to a long-acting opioid at the same dose, which carries less overdose risk", "D": "Discuss a gradual taper with her prescriber, since benefit is unproven and risk rises"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Discuss a gradual taper with her prescriber, since benefit is unproven and risk rises): Canadian guidance advises keeping chronic non-cancer pain doses below 50 and ideally never above 90 morphine milligram equivalents, and recommends tapering when function has not improved. Tapering should be gradual and collaborative to limit withdrawal and loss of trust. Abrupt discontinuation is harmful, and switching to a long-acting product at the same dose does not lower risk."}, {"id": 1923, "domain": "communication_and_collaboration", "chapter": 11, "question": "PC, a 49 year old man, says he lost his hydromorphone and demands an early replacement supply, becoming loud when told it is not possible without prescriber involvement. His prescription was filled six days ago for 14 days. What is the most appropriate approach?", "options": {"A": "Tell him the matter is closed and ask him to leave before he disturbs other customers", "B": "Dispense a few days' supply as an emergency measure so he does not go into withdrawal", "C": "Call the police at once because loss claims for opioids indicate diversion in most cases", "D": "Stay calm, explain that replacement needs prescriber authorization, offer to call, and document"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Stay calm, explain that replacement needs prescriber authorization, offer to call, and document): Lost controlled drugs cannot be replaced without a new valid prescription, and the safe approach is to stay calm, state the rule and the way forward, and involve the prescriber. Documenting the event protects both parties. Emergency supplies for narcotics are not available without prescriber authorization, and immediate police involvement or ending the conversation escalates the situation."}, {"id": 1924, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "In a province where it is permitted, a pharmacist delegates the checking of incoming narcotic shipments to a regulated technician. A discrepancy of 100 tablets goes unnoticed for a month, and the college opens an investigation. Which principle will most likely apply?", "options": {"A": "Only the technician is responsible, since the duties become hers once the pharmacist has assigned them", "B": "The pharmacist remains responsible for oversight, and the technician answers for her own work", "C": "Only the pharmacist is responsible, since a regulated technician has no personal accountability to the college", "D": "Responsibility rests with the pharmacy owner, since stock discrepancies are a business matter for the corporation"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The pharmacist remains responsible for oversight, and the technician answers for her own work): Delegation does not remove the pharmacist's accountability for supervising and for choosing a competent delegate, while a regulated technician is also accountable to the college for her own performance. Neither professional can hand the whole responsibility to the other. Stock discrepancies involving narcotics are a matter of professional regulation, not only an internal business issue."}, {"id": 1925, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "At a hospital outpatient pharmacy, a patient named EF, 36 years old, asks to transfer a prescription for oxycodone 5 mg tablets from a pharmacy in another city, saying the doctor there retired last month. The pharmacist is reviewing what can legally be done. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Call the other pharmacy and transfer the prescription as with any other drug that has refills", "B": "Explain that narcotics cannot be transferred, and obtain a new prescription from a prescriber", "C": "Transfer the prescription once only, since a single transfer is allowed for any controlled drug", "D": "Accept a photocopy of the old prescription and dispense the remaining quantity from that copy"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Explain that narcotics cannot be transferred, and obtain a new prescription from a prescriber): Federal narcotic rules prohibit transferring a narcotic prescription between pharmacies, and a copy cannot replace the original authorization. A new prescription from a current prescriber is needed. The single-transfer allowance applies to some other controlled substances, not narcotics."}, {"id": 1926, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "UK is a 70 year old man with type 2 diabetes taking acarbose 100 mg three times daily with meals plus insulin glargine. He becomes shaky with a glucose of 3.3 mmol/L at lunch and has table sugar and glucose tablets available. Which treatment should the pharmacist recommend?", "options": {"A": "Glucose tablets or dextrose, because acarbose delays the digestion of ordinary table sugar", "B": "A chocolate bar with nuts, because the fat prevents a rebound drop in glucose later", "C": "Table sugar dissolved in water, because it is absorbed as quickly as glucose with acarbose", "D": "A glass of milk with a slice of toast, because protein and starch give lasting rises"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Glucose tablets or dextrose, because acarbose delays the digestion of ordinary table sugar): Acarbose blocks alpha-glucosidase, so sucrose and starch are broken down slowly and cannot rescue a low in time. Pure glucose does not need digestion and is absorbed directly. Milk, toast and chocolate contain slow or digested components and delay the glucose rise."}, {"id": 1927, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "A pharmacist facing a college inquiry argues that a published standard of practice on prescription verification is only guidance and cannot be the basis of a finding against him. The college's lawyer disagrees. What best describes the status of college standards of practice?", "options": {"A": "Standards are suggestions that a discipline committee may consider only when a patient suffers physical harm", "B": "Standards describe minimum expected practice, and breaching them can support a finding of professional misconduct", "C": "Standards apply only to pharmacists with fewer than five years of practice after initial registration", "D": "Standards bind pharmacy owners only, since the college regulates premises rather than individual pharmacists"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Standards describe minimum expected practice, and breaching them can support a finding of professional misconduct): Standards of practice set out the minimum expected conduct and competence of registrants, and a breach can ground a finding of professional misconduct whether or not harm occurred. They apply to every registrant, not just new graduates, and they are not limited to cases of harm."}, {"id": 1928, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 1, "question": "EV is a 60 year old woman with stable angina and asthma who needed oral corticosteroids for an exacerbation two months ago. Her family physician writes a new prescription for propranolol 40 mg twice daily to prevent angina. The pharmacist reviews the profile before dispensing. What is the best recommendation?", "options": {"A": "Dispense a half dose of propranolol and increase it slowly if no wheezing develops", "B": "Recommend a long-acting dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker, because propranolol can trigger bronchospasm in asthma", "C": "Dispense propranolol and advise using her reliever inhaler before each dose to prevent wheezing episodes", "D": "Change propranolol to bisoprolol, since cardioselective beta-blockers have no effect on the airways of asthma patients"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Recommend a long-acting dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker, because propranolol can trigger bronchospasm in asthma): Nonselective beta-blockers such as propranolol block beta2 receptors in the airways and can precipitate bronchospasm, particularly in patients with recent exacerbations. A dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker treats angina without this risk. Cardioselective agents are used cautiously in mild asthma but still keep some airway effect, so a claim of no effect is wrong."}, {"id": 1929, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 4, "question": "A 33 year old developed hives and facial flushing after taking ibuprofen for a headache. She asks which pain reliever is least likely to cause the same reaction. She has no history of asthma or sinus disease. What should the pharmacist recommend?", "options": {"A": "Naproxen, since propionic acid derivatives differ enough from ibuprofen to avoid the same reaction pattern", "B": "Ketorolac, since a stronger NSAID gives pain relief with less cross reaction risk than ibuprofen", "C": "Aspirin, since salicylates act through a different pathway from the one behind her reaction", "D": "Acetaminophen at usual doses, which is generally tolerated in patients with NSAID-induced urticaria"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Acetaminophen at usual doses, which is generally tolerated in patients with NSAID-induced urticaria): NSAID-induced urticaria is usually due to COX-1 inhibition, which is shared by most NSAIDs, so cross-reactivity is common. Acetaminophen is a weak COX inhibitor and is usually tolerated. Naproxen, aspirin and ketorolac would be expected to trigger the same reaction."}, {"id": 1930, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "At a busy counter, PL starts counselling a patient about a new HIV medication while four other customers queue within earshot. The patient lowers his voice, glances at the line, and looks uncomfortable. A private counselling room is available a few steps away. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Move to the private counselling area or a quieter spot, and continue the discussion there", "B": "Ask the waiting customers to step back and continue, since the topic is a general one", "C": "Continue speaking quietly at the counter, since counselling must finish before the next customer is served", "D": "Hand the patient written information and end counselling, since a private discussion takes too long"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Move to the private counselling area or a quieter spot, and continue the discussion there): Reasonable safeguards include a setting where sensitive counselling cannot be overheard. Written material can supplement but not replace counselling that the patient needs, and asking people to step back does not create privacy."}, {"id": 1931, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "Clonazepam 0.5 mg twice daily, quantity 60, was prescribed for a 45 year old woman with two refills, each to be dispensed at 30 day intervals. She returns on day 21 saying she is travelling and asks for the first refill. What should the pharmacist do?", "options": {"A": "Contact the prescriber for approval to dispense the refill early, and document the decision", "B": "Dispense the refill now, since 21 days is within a week of the stated interval", "C": "Dispense the refill after the patient signs an acknowledgement that it is being given early", "D": "Decline, since the interval is fixed by law and no prescriber approval can change it"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Contact the prescriber for approval to dispense the refill early, and document the decision): When a prescriber specifies dispensing intervals for refills of a targeted substance, the pharmacist follows them. Travel is a reason to ask the prescriber to authorize an earlier refill, not a reason for the pharmacist to override the interval. A patient acknowledgement does not create authority, and the interval is set by the prescriber rather than fixed in law."}, {"id": 1932, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "OT is a 59 year old man with years of heavy drinking brought to the emergency department with unsteady gait, horizontal nystagmus and confusion. His blood glucose is 3.6 mmol/L. Wernicke encephalopathy is suspected. Which initial treatment plan should the pharmacist recommend?", "options": {"A": "Thiamine 100 mg orally once daily, with the glucose given after his confusion has cleared", "B": "Intravenous glucose first to correct hypoglycemia, then thiamine 100 mg orally once daily afterward", "C": "Folic acid 5 mg daily and a multivitamin, with thiamine added only if symptoms persist", "D": "Thiamine 500 mg intravenously three times daily, given before or with the glucose"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Thiamine 500 mg intravenously three times daily, given before or with the glucose): Ataxia, nystagmus and confusion in a heavy drinker are the classic features of Wernicke encephalopathy, which needs high dose parenteral thiamine, often 500 mg intravenously three times daily for the first days. Thiamine goes in before or with glucose because glucose metabolism consumes thiamine and can worsen the injury. Low dose oral thiamine is inadequate for treatment."}, {"id": 1933, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "An unencrypted laptop holding a pharmacy's patient list, including names, phone numbers and drug names, is stolen from a car. The business is subject to PIPEDA for this data. Which set of obligations best describes what the pharmacy must do?", "options": {"A": "Notify the police only, since theft is a crime and the Commissioner handles only deliberate misuse", "B": "Assess the risk of significant harm, notify the Commissioner and patients if met, and record", "C": "Notify patients only if the laptop held credit card numbers, since health data is less sensitive", "D": "Wait thirty days to see whether the information is misused before deciding if notification is needed"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Assess the risk of significant harm, notify the Commissioner and patients if met, and record): PIPEDA requires organizations to report breaches that pose a real risk of significant harm to the Privacy Commissioner and affected individuals, and to keep a record of all breaches. A list of drug names is sensitive, and waiting for evidence of misuse is not permitted."}, {"id": 1934, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 3, "question": "A 21 year old woman who is not pregnant was treated with doxycycline for chlamydia. She took every dose, her symptoms resolved and her partner was treated. She asks the pharmacist whether she needs a test of cure. What is the correct advice?", "options": {"A": "A test of cure is not routine, but retest in about 3 to 6 months", "B": "A repeat test in 2 weeks is needed to confirm that the infection is gone", "C": "A test of cure at 1 week is needed, together with a repeat course of antibiotics", "D": "No further testing is needed unless symptoms return, since reinfection is unlikely after treatment"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A test of cure is not routine, but retest in about 3 to 6 months): Test of cure is reserved for pregnancy, poor adherence, persistent symptoms or certain regimens. Nucleic acid tests can stay positive from dead organisms for weeks, so early repeat testing misleads. Reinfection is common, so retesting at about 3 to 6 months is advised."}, {"id": 1935, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "LW is a 33 year old man with type 1 diabetes on basal-bolus insulin. He plans a 60 minute moderate cycling session two hours after lunch, when his lispro is still active. His pre-exercise glucose is 6.8 mmol/L. What advice should the pharmacist give to reduce his hypoglycemia risk?", "options": {"A": "Take extra carbohydrate before starting and test glucose partway through the session", "B": "Skip his bedtime basal insulin that night, since exercise lowers glucose until the next morning", "C": "Take a larger lispro dose with a pre-exercise snack so the carbohydrate is fully covered", "D": "Start the session unchanged, since moderate cycling does not lower glucose when lispro is active"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Take extra carbohydrate before starting and test glucose partway through the session): Exercise with active rapid-acting insulin on board markedly increases hypoglycemia risk, and a starting value of 6.8 mmol/L is acceptable, but active rapid-acting insulin and a 60 minute session mean glucose can fall, so carbohydrate should be available and glucose checked partway through. Extra carbohydrate beforehand and monitoring during activity are standard measures. Skipping basal insulin risks ketoacidosis, and adding more rapid insulin worsens the risk."}, {"id": 1936, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "Invited to join a provincial formulary advisory committee, OW, a hospital pharmacist, also consults for two drug manufacturers on a drug class the committee will review next month. Both consulting contracts pay annual fees. Which step best manages the conflict of interest?", "options": {"A": "Join the committee and vote normally, since consulting income is separate from public formulary decisions", "B": "Terminate one consulting contract so only a single manufacturer remains linked to the drug class", "C": "Disclose both contracts and step out of discussions and votes on that drug class", "D": "Disclose the contracts but participate fully in votes, because disclosure alone removes the conflict of interest"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Disclose both contracts and step out of discussions and votes on that drug class): Consulting contracts with manufacturers create a conflict for a formulary committee reviewing that drug class. Disclosure plus stepping out of discussion and voting protects the integrity of the decision. Disclosure alone does not remove the conflict, and ending one contract leaves the other."}, {"id": 1937, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "A pharmacy employs an unregulated assistant, a regulated technician and two pharmacists. The pharmacy manager is assigning tasks for the day shift. Which task is appropriate to assign to the unregulated assistant under pharmacist supervision, according to the usual provincial approach to delegation?", "options": {"A": "Transferring a prescription to another pharmacy by telephone with the prescriber's details", "B": "Performing the final technical check on completed prescriptions before they go to pickup", "C": "Retrieving stock and preparing the vial with the label for later technical checking", "D": "Accepting a verbal new prescription from the prescriber's office and entering it"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Retrieving stock and preparing the vial with the label for later technical checking): An unregulated assistant can do support tasks such as retrieving stock and preparing vials, which are then checked by a regulated professional. Receiving or transferring prescriptions and performing a check are regulated activities that require a licensed pharmacist or, where permitted, a regulated technician."}, {"id": 1938, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 7, "question": "MP is a 74 year old woman who takes amitriptyline 50 mg nightly for diabetic neuropathic pain. She reports dry mouth, constipation and dizziness when standing. Her creatinine clearance is 65 mL/min. Which change would the pharmacist suggest to the prescriber for safer pain control?", "options": {"A": "Switch to doxepin 100 mg nightly, which is a safe sedating antidepressant with fewer side effects", "B": "Taper amitriptyline and start low dose gabapentin, titrating slowly while watching for sedation", "C": "Increase amitriptyline to 75 mg nightly, since dry mouth usually fades as the dose rises", "D": "Change amitriptyline to imipramine, which carries a lower anticholinergic burden and less orthostatic hypotension"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Taper amitriptyline and start low dose gabapentin, titrating slowly while watching for sedation): Tricyclic antidepressants with strong anticholinergic effects cause dry mouth, constipation and orthostatic dizziness, and they raise fall risk in older adults. A low dose of gabapentin titrated slowly offers neuropathic pain relief without those effects, provided sedation is watched. Imipramine is also anticholinergic, and higher doses of any tricyclic would worsen the problem."}, {"id": 1939, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 7, "question": "A mother brings her two year old son to the pharmacy. He has had watery diarrhea for two days, is drinking small amounts, and has slightly dry lips but still passes urine and is playful. There is no blood or high fever. What should the pharmacist recommend?", "options": {"A": "Give undiluted apple juice and sports drinks for fluids, and withhold solid food for a day", "B": "Advise emergency department care, since a toddler with diarrhea for two days needs intravenous fluids", "C": "Offer oral rehydration solution in small, frequent amounts and continue his usual feeding", "D": "Start loperamide at the pediatric dose to slow the stool output, and offer water only"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Offer oral rehydration solution in small, frequent amounts and continue his usual feeding): Mild dehydration in a toddler who is alert and passing urine is managed with oral rehydration solution and continued age appropriate feeding. Undiluted juice and sports drinks have the wrong sugar and salt balance and can worsen diarrhea. Loperamide is not recommended in young children, and intravenous fluids are reserved for more severe dehydration."}, {"id": 1940, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A pharmacist reads a meta-analysis that pools nine trials of different statin doses in patient groups ranging from healthy adults to people with prior stroke. The authors must choose a statistical model for the pooled estimate. Which choice and rationale is most appropriate for this review?", "options": {"A": "A random-effects model, which allows true effects to differ between trials and widens the interval", "B": "A fixed-effect model, because pooling trials of different doses is valid only when heterogeneity is zero", "C": "No pooling, because trials enrolling different populations cannot be combined in a meta-analysis safely", "D": "A fixed-effect model, which assumes each trial estimates one true effect and gives the narrowest interval"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A random-effects model, which allows true effects to differ between trials and widens the interval): The trials differ in dose and population, so their true effects are unlikely to be identical. A random-effects model accounts for that between-trial variation and gives a more honest, wider interval. A fixed-effect model assumes one common effect and would overstate precision here."}, {"id": 1941, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "A pharmacist is assigning work to an unregulated pharmacy assistant for a busy Monday under direct supervision. Twelve patients are waiting, three phone messages need returning, and a wholesaler delivery has arrived. In most provinces, which task is appropriate to delegate to the assistant?", "options": {"A": "Perform the final check of repeat prescriptions that a pharmacist already verified once before this week", "B": "Unpack and shelve the wholesaler delivery, with the pharmacist verifying any controlled drugs received", "C": "Take a patient's question about whether two prescribed drugs interact and answer it from the database", "D": "Assess a patient's early refill request and decide whether to approve it from the profile history"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Unpack and shelve the wholesaler delivery, with the pharmacist verifying any controlled drugs received): Unpacking and shelving stock is a non-clinical task that an assistant may do under supervision, and a pharmacist verifies controlled drugs received. Answering interaction questions and deciding on early refills require professional judgment. Final checks are restricted to pharmacists or, where permitted, regulated technicians, whatever earlier verification occurred."}, {"id": 1942, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 7, "question": "A prescription for NQ, a 9 kg infant with otitis media, reads amoxicillin 250 mg per 5 mL suspension, give 10 mL twice daily. The usual high dose of 80 to 90 mg/kg/day divided twice daily would be about 8 mL per dose. What should the pharmacist do?", "options": {"A": "Dispense as written, since higher doses are routinely needed for resistant otitis media in infants", "B": "Contact the prescriber to confirm the dose, since 10 mL exceeds the usual range", "C": "Change the volume to 5 mL per dose without calling, since the label shows 250 mg", "D": "Dispense 10 mL per dose and advise the parents to reduce it if diarrhea occurs"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Contact the prescriber to confirm the dose, since 10 mL exceeds the usual range): A 10 mL dose is 500 mg twice daily, or about 111 mg/kg/day, which is above the usual 80 to 90 mg/kg/day. A discrepancy of this size needs prescriber confirmation before dispensing. Changing the order unilaterally or relying on parents to adjust for side effects is not appropriate."}, {"id": 1943, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 7, "question": "A 3 month old boy has white patches on his tongue and cheeks that do not wipe off. The pharmacist confirms oral thrush and a physician order for nystatin 100,000 units/mL, 1 mL four times daily for 14 days. What quantity should be dispensed?", "options": {"A": "One 30 mL bottle, since half the course can be covered and renewed later", "B": "One 120 mL bottle, since the course needs 112 mL in total", "C": "One 60 mL bottle, since the course needs 56 mL in total", "D": "One 28 mL bottle, since each dose is 0.5 mL given four times daily"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (One 60 mL bottle, since the course needs 56 mL in total): One millilitre four times daily for 14 days requires 56 mL, so a 56 to 60 mL quantity covers the course. The other quantities come from halving or doubling the dose. Treatment should continue for 48 hours after the plaques resolve."}, {"id": 1944, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "A hospital pharmacist and nurse perform an independent double check before starting a heparin infusion of 25,000 units in 500 mL, ordered at 18 units/kg/hour for a patient weighing 70 kg. Each calculates the pump rate separately. Which rate in mL/hour should both arrive at?", "options": {"A": "25.2 mL/hour, because the dose is 1,260 units/hour and the concentration is 50 units/mL", "B": "1,260 mL/hour, because the ordered dose in units/hour is entered directly as the pump rate", "C": "50.4 mL/hour, because the weight-based dose is doubled when heparin is infused at this concentration", "D": "12.6 mL/hour, because the heparin concentration is 100 units/mL when mixed in this bag"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (25.2 mL/hour, because the dose is 1,260 units/hour and the concentration is 50 units/mL): The dose is 18 units/kg/hour x 70 kg = 1,260 units/hour. The concentration is 25,000 units / 500 mL = 50 units/mL, so the rate is 1,260 / 50 = 25.2 mL/hour. Entering units per hour as mL per hour, or assuming a wrong concentration, produces a large rate error that an independent calculation by two people is designed to catch."}, {"id": 1945, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 3, "question": "JN is a 68 year old woman who walks daily and takes prednisone 10 mg each morning for polymyalgia rheumatica. Her physician prescribes ciprofloxacin 500 mg twice daily for a urinary tract infection. Which adverse effect counselling is the most important for the pharmacist to give?", "options": {"A": "Avoid sunlight only, since tendon injury is not a recognized effect of this antibiotic class", "B": "Report heel or calf pain promptly, as steroids and fluoroquinolones together raise tendon rupture risk", "C": "Take each tablet together with a calcium supplement, which protects tendons during the course", "D": "Tendon pain is a harmless effect that fades with continued use, so keep taking it"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Report heel or calf pain promptly, as steroids and fluoroquinolones together raise tendon rupture risk): Fluoroquinolones carry a boxed type warning for tendinopathy and rupture, and age over 60 plus corticosteroid use sharply raise the risk. She should stop the drug and report new heel, calf or tendon pain. Calcium actually binds ciprofloxacin and lowers its absorption, and continuing through pain can lead to rupture."}, {"id": 1946, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "Provincial standards in this province require prescription records to be kept for ten years from the last date of dispensing. A sertraline prescription was first dispensed on March 3, 2016 and last refilled on August 19, 2019. What is the earliest date the record may be destroyed?", "options": {"A": "March 3, 2026, because retention is counted from the date the prescription was first dispensed", "B": "January 1, 2030, because the ten years start on the first day of the following year", "C": "August 19, 2020, because records need to be kept only one year after the last refill", "D": "August 19, 2029, which is ten years after the last dispensing"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (August 19, 2029, which is ten years after the last dispensing): The stated rule counts ten years from the most recent dispensing, which was August 19, 2019, giving August 19, 2029. Counting from the first fill would shorten the period incorrectly. A one year period or a start at the beginning of the next calendar year does not match the rule as written."}, {"id": 1947, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 3, "question": "RD is a 36 year old woman with HIV on dolutegravir/abacavir/lamivudine with viral loads below 40 copies/mL for three years. Her male partner is HIV negative and they plan to conceive. She asks the pharmacist whether conception without condoms is safe. What is the best information?", "options": {"A": "Condoms are needed for each attempt, since a viral load below 40 copies/mL still carries risk", "B": "Her partner should start PrEP during the attempts, since suppression alone does not prevent transmission", "C": "Sustained viral suppression means no sexual transmission, so she may try to conceive naturally", "D": "She should switch from dolutegravir before conception because it is known to cause neural tube defects"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Sustained viral suppression means no sexual transmission, so she may try to conceive naturally): Large studies show no sexual transmission when the viral load stays undetectable for at least six months, so natural conception is reasonable. Dolutegravir is a preferred agent in pregnancy and is not a cause of neural tube defects at current recommended use. Condoms and partner PrEP add protection but are not required to attempt conception when suppression is sustained."}, {"id": 1948, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "JR, a patient on a cytotoxic oral drug, has leftover capecitabine tablets and asks whether to flush them or throw them in household garbage so that his young children are not exposed. What is the best advice from the pharmacist?", "options": {"A": "Crush the tablets and mix them with water before flushing so they cannot be reused", "B": "Hold them at home in the original bottle until the next appointment with the oncologist", "C": "Return the unused tablets in a sealed container to the pharmacy for cytotoxic waste handling", "D": "Place them in household garbage inside a sealed bag, since the amount is small"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Return the unused tablets in a sealed container to the pharmacy for cytotoxic waste handling): Cytotoxic drugs are hazardous to people and the environment, so leftovers should be sealed and returned for disposal as hazardous pharmaceutical waste. Crushing creates dust exposure and flushing contaminates water. Keeping them at home leaves them within reach of children, and garbage exposes waste workers."}, {"id": 1949, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A cohort study compares two oral anticoagulants in 8,000 patients, and the investigators must account for age, kidney function, prior bleeding and several other baseline differences between the groups at the same time. Which analytic approach is designed to do this?", "options": {"A": "Multivariable regression that includes the measured confounders as covariates alongside the drug exposure", "B": "Random allocation of patients to anticoagulants after the data are collected, to balance the groups", "C": "Restriction to patients with normal kidney function, which controls every other baseline difference", "D": "An unadjusted comparison of event rates, which matches any adjusted analysis in a large cohort"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Multivariable regression that includes the measured confounders as covariates alongside the drug exposure): Multivariable models estimate the effect of exposure while holding the other included variables constant, so several measured confounders can be handled together. Restriction addresses only one variable, random allocation cannot be done retrospectively, and a crude comparison leaves confounding in place."}, {"id": 1950, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "QR is a 54 year old kidney transplant recipient who takes tacrolimus immediate-release 3 mg twice daily. A new prescription changes this to tacrolimus extended-release 6 mg once daily. His trough levels have been stable for months. What is the pharmacist's best action?", "options": {"A": "Dispense as written, since equal total daily doses produce identical blood concentrations in all patients", "B": "Dispense as written and tell the patient to take half the dose in the morning", "C": "Refuse the new prescription, since formulations of this drug cannot be exchanged for each other", "D": "Confirm the conversion with the transplant team and arrange trough monitoring soon after the switch"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Confirm the conversion with the transplant team and arrange trough monitoring soon after the switch): Tacrolimus has a narrow therapeutic index, and the extended-release product can give different exposure, so troughs should be checked after a formulation change. The pharmacist coordinates with the transplant team rather than assuming equivalence. Refusing is unnecessary because conversion is done with monitoring."}, {"id": 1951, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "At the end of a shift, the pharmacist reconciles fentanyl 25 mcg/h patches. The opening balance was 18 patches and 30 were received. Four prescriptions of 5 patches and two prescriptions of 10 patches were dispensed. The physical count is 9 patches. What is the result?", "options": {"A": "1 patch over the expected balance of 8 patches, which still needs to be investigated", "B": "The count matches the expected balance, since overages are not treated as discrepancies by regulators", "C": "9 patches short of the expected balance, as received stock is not added to the count", "D": "1 patch short of an expected balance of 10 patches, treated as a counting slip"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (1 patch over the expected balance of 8 patches, which still needs to be investigated): Expected balance is 18 plus 30 minus (20 plus 20) equals 8 patches. A count of 9 is one over. An overage is a discrepancy just like a shortage, because it can signal a recording error or a patient who was under-supplied, so it must be investigated."}, {"id": 1952, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A patient brings a St. John's wort capsule bottle to a community pharmacy and asks whether the product is licensed in Canada and what health claims Health Canada has approved for it. To verify this, the pharmacist should search the:", "options": {"A": "Licensed Natural Health Products Database using the product's natural product number", "B": "Health Canada Recalls and Safety Alerts listing, searching by the product's common ingredient name", "C": "Canada Vigilance database using the manufacturer's lot number printed near the expiry date", "D": "Drug Product Database using the product's drug identification number printed on the bottle label"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Licensed Natural Health Products Database using the product's natural product number): Licensed natural health products carry a natural product number, and the Licensed Natural Health Products Database lists the approved medicinal ingredients and claims for each. The Drug Product Database covers drugs with a DIN, and Canada Vigilance holds adverse reaction reports. A recalls listing would show only products that have already been recalled."}, {"id": 1953, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 1, "question": "JA is a 38 year old woman with a newly diagnosed deep vein thrombosis and normal kidney and liver function. Her physician prescribes apixaban, and she asks how it will be taken. Which regimen should the pharmacist confirm for the initial treatment phase?", "options": {"A": "10 mg once daily for seven days, then 5 mg once daily for the remaining treatment", "B": "5 mg twice daily from the first day, because only rivaroxaban uses a loading phase", "C": "2.5 mg twice daily from the first day, since the lower dose suits acute thrombosis", "D": "10 mg twice daily for seven days, then 5 mg twice daily thereafter"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (10 mg twice daily for seven days, then 5 mg twice daily thereafter): Apixaban for acute VTE starts with a higher lead-in dose of 10 mg twice daily for seven days, then 5 mg twice daily. The 2.5 mg twice daily dose is used for extended prevention after at least six months, not for acute treatment."}, {"id": 1954, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "A pharmacist hesitates to report a suspected child abuse concern, afraid of being sued if the suspicion proves unfounded and the family complains to the college and to the media. Which statement correctly describes the legal position for reporting under Canadian provincial child protection laws?", "options": {"A": "A reporter is protected only when a physician co-signs the report before it reaches the agency", "B": "A reporter is protected only when the investigation confirms that abuse occurred in the child's home", "C": "A reporter is protected from civil claims but remains open to college discipline for breaching confidentiality", "D": "Reports made in good faith are protected from civil liability, even when later unconfirmed"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Reports made in good faith are protected from civil liability, even when later unconfirmed): Child protection statutes shield people who report in good faith from legal action, so unconfirmed suspicion is not a basis for liability. The statutes also override confidentiality duties, so the college would not discipline a proper report."}, {"id": 1955, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A meta-analysis reports an NNT of 18 with a 95% confidence interval for the absolute risk reduction that crosses zero. A pharmacist reviews the paper before a formulary meeting and is asked how to interpret the NNT. Which interpretation is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "The NNT is invalid and should be replaced by a number needed to harm of 18", "B": "The benefit is uncertain, as the interval includes no effect or possible harm", "C": "Treating 18 patients will prevent one event, so the drug is confirmed effective in practice", "D": "The interval is irrelevant to an NNT, which is interpreted using the point estimate alone"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The benefit is uncertain, as the interval includes no effect or possible harm): A confidence interval for the absolute risk reduction that crosses zero means the data are compatible with no benefit or with harm, so the NNT point estimate is unreliable. A single point estimate cannot confirm effectiveness, and the interval is essential to interpretation."}, {"id": 1956, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "A hospital pharmacist notices that an automated dispensing cabinet allows nurses to override the stocking limit on potassium chloride concentrate without pharmacist review. Three near misses occurred in two months. Which response best fits a systems approach to patient safety?", "options": {"A": "Remove concentrated potassium chloride from the cabinet and supply premixed solutions prepared by the pharmacy", "B": "Send a memo reminding nurses to double check concentrate selections and follow the cabinet override procedure", "C": "Identify the nurses involved in the near misses and require them to repeat medication safety training", "D": "Add a warning label to the concentrate vials and rely on staff to read it"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Remove concentrated potassium chloride from the cabinet and supply premixed solutions prepared by the pharmacy): Removing concentrated potassium chloride from patient care areas eliminates the hazard, which is stronger than reminders, warnings or retraining. Premixed products supplied by the pharmacy are the established safer alternative. Education and labels rely on human vigilance and leave the override pathway intact."}, {"id": 1957, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "A patient asks the pharmacist to email a medication list and recent lab results to a personal webmail account because she wants them before a specialist visit tomorrow. The pharmacy's regular email is not encrypted. Which approach best protects the patient's privacy?", "options": {"A": "Print the information and mail it in an unmarked envelope, since paper is safer than email", "B": "Send only the lab results, since medication lists are considered less sensitive than laboratory information", "C": "Explain the risks, get documented consent, and use encrypted email or a secure patient portal", "D": "Send the message as requested, because a patient's request moves the security duty onto the patient"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Explain the risks, get documented consent, and use encrypted email or a secure patient portal): A custodian must use reasonable safeguards even when a patient asks for a convenient method, and unencrypted email can be intercepted. Explaining the risk and using a secure method meets the duty while still serving the patient."}, {"id": 1958, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "A pharmacy introduces a refill synchronization program for patients who take five or more chronic medications, with the goal of one planned monthly pickup. A pharmacy student asks what the main purpose of the program is. Which answer is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "Shortening each supply so patients return weekly for counselling and more frequent checks", "B": "Aligning fills to one date improves adherence and allows a regular medication review", "C": "Allowing the pharmacist to change doses at each visit without contacting the prescriber", "D": "Extending every drug to a 90 day supply to reduce the number of dispensing fees"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Aligning fills to one date improves adherence and allows a regular medication review): Synchronization brings all chronic medications to a single pickup date, which reduces missed refills and trips and gives the pharmacist a planned chance to review therapy. It does not shorten supplies or alter prescriber authority."}, {"id": 1959, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 1, "question": "MN is a 70 year old man started on acetylsalicylic acid 81 mg and clopidogrel 75 mg after a stent. Two years ago he had a bleeding duodenal ulcer treated with a proton pump inhibitor, and he takes no other drugs. His physician asks whether stomach protection is needed. What should the pharmacist recommend?", "options": {"A": "No protection is needed, since clopidogrel is less irritating to the stomach than acetylsalicylic acid", "B": "Add famotidine 20 mg twice daily, which prevents ulcer bleeding as well as a PPI", "C": "Add pantoprazole 40 mg daily for as long as he remains on dual antiplatelet therapy", "D": "Add omeprazole 20 mg daily, since it is the most effective proton pump inhibitor with clopidogrel"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Add pantoprazole 40 mg daily for as long as he remains on dual antiplatelet therapy): A history of gastrointestinal bleeding plus dual antiplatelet therapy places him at high risk of upper gastrointestinal bleeding, so a proton pump inhibitor is recommended. Pantoprazole is chosen because omeprazole inhibits CYP2C19 and may reduce clopidogrel activation. H2 receptor antagonists are less effective at preventing ulcer bleeding."}, {"id": 1960, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "RP is a 40 year old woman whose partner is dispensed methadone 100 mg daily. At the pharmacy she picks up a naloxone kit and asks whether she can stop watching him once he wakes after a dose of naloxone is given. What is the best advice?", "options": {"A": "Naloxone wears off sooner than methadone, so he needs emergency care and observation even if awake", "B": "Once he wakes, no further care is required, since naloxone reverses methadone effects for a day", "C": "Observation is needed only for ten minutes, since methadone is cleared from the body by then", "D": "Give a second naloxone dose every hour for the next day, even if he remains awake"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Naloxone wears off sooner than methadone, so he needs emergency care and observation even if awake): Naloxone acts for roughly 30 to 90 minutes, while methadone effects persist for 24 hours or longer, so breathing can fail again once naloxone fades. Emergency medical assessment and monitoring are needed after any naloxone use. Repeated routine dosing at home is not appropriate when the person is awake and breathing."}, {"id": 1961, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 3, "question": "IL is a 60 year old woman admitted to the intensive care unit with severe community-acquired pneumonia and septic shock. She has no drug allergies and no prior MRSA or Pseudomonas infection, and her creatinine clearance is 90 mL/min. Which empiric regimen should the pharmacist recommend?", "options": {"A": "A beta-lactam such as ceftriaxone combined with a macrolide or respiratory fluoroquinolone, started promptly", "B": "Vancomycin plus meropenem as routine first line therapy, since broad coverage lowers mortality in severe pneumonia", "C": "Amoxicillin by mouth plus gentamicin, because aminoglycosides cover pneumococcus reliably in patients with septic shock", "D": "Azithromycin alone for three days, since macrolide monotherapy covers the common causes of severe disease"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A beta-lactam such as ceftriaxone combined with a macrolide or respiratory fluoroquinolone, started promptly): Severe community-acquired pneumonia needs combination therapy with a beta-lactam plus a macrolide or respiratory fluoroquinolone to cover pneumococcus and atypicals, and early administration improves survival. Anti MRSA and anti Pseudomonas agents are added only when risk factors exist. Monotherapy is inadequate in septic shock."}, {"id": 1962, "domain": "communication_and_collaboration", "chapter": 11, "question": "A physician telephones a verbal prescription to the pharmacy for cefuroxime 500 mg twice daily for ten days for a patient with sinusitis. The pharmacist writes it down on a pad. Which step is most useful to prevent a transcription error in a verbal order?", "options": {"A": "Ask the prescriber to repeat the patient's name twice, and then end the call promptly", "B": "Record the order and ask the prescriber's office to send a written copy within a week", "C": "Read the whole order back to the prescriber and have it confirmed first", "D": "Enter the order into the computer system and confirm it with the patient on pick up"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Read the whole order back to the prescriber and have it confirmed first): Reading the full order back to the prescriber allows an immediate check of the drug, dose, route, frequency and duration. Confirming with the patient or waiting for paperwork does not catch an error before dispensing."}, {"id": 1963, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 1, "question": "PH is a 72 year old man with heart failure taking digoxin 0.125 mg daily and furosemide 40 mg twice daily. His potassium is 3.0 mmol/L, digoxin level is 1.0 nmol/L and he has new palpitations. What is the best recommendation?", "options": {"A": "correct potassium and check magnesium, since hypokalemia raises digoxin toxicity risk at normal levels", "B": "take no action, since a digoxin level in the usual range excludes digoxin-related arrhythmia", "C": "change furosemide to a thiazide, because thiazides do not affect potassium or digoxin binding", "D": "increase the digoxin dose, since palpitations show the current level is too low to control rhythm"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (correct potassium and check magnesium, since hypokalemia raises digoxin toxicity risk at normal levels): Low potassium increases digoxin binding to the sodium-potassium ATPase, so arrhythmias occur at levels that look acceptable. Potassium and magnesium should be corrected promptly. Thiazides also lower potassium."}, {"id": 1964, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "A compounded ibuprofen 20 mg/mL oral suspension is dispensed to a 5 year old (DM) for a fever. The drug settles to the bottom of the bottle within a few minutes. The pharmacist reviews the label before dispensing. Which instruction should be added to the label in addition to the strength, storage conditions and beyond-use date?", "options": {"A": "Mix each dose into a full glass of water before swallowing to reduce stomach irritation", "B": "Freeze the bottle between doses to keep particles suspended, then thaw it fully before each dose", "C": "Shake well before each use so that every measured dose contains the intended amount", "D": "Do not shake the bottle, since shaking adds air bubbles that change the dose volume"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Shake well before each use so that every measured dose contains the intended amount): Suspensions contain undissolved drug that settles, so the label needs a shake-well instruction to ensure each dose delivers the intended amount. Underdosing from the top of the bottle and overdosing from the sediment are both possible. Mixing with water or freezing does not resuspend the drug, and shaking does not harm the dose."}, {"id": 1965, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 4, "question": "LV is a 63 year old man with moderate COPD who uses a LAMA/LABA inhaler daily and has had no exacerbations in two years. He feels well and asks whether he can stop his long-acting inhaler and use salbutamol only. What should the pharmacist advise?", "options": {"A": "Stop it for two months as a trial, then restart only if breathlessness returns during exertion", "B": "Switch to salbutamol four times daily alone, which gives equal protection against exacerbations", "C": "Continue the maintenance inhaler, since stopping it often leads to worsening symptoms and exacerbations", "D": "Stop it, because long-acting bronchodilators are meant to be used only during symptom flare ups"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Continue the maintenance inhaler, since stopping it often leads to worsening symptoms and exacerbations): Feeling well is a sign the maintenance regimen is working, not that the disease has gone. Long-acting bronchodilators need regular use, and withdrawal tends to bring back symptoms and raise exacerbation risk. Scheduled short acting salbutamol is less effective than long-acting therapy."}, {"id": 1966, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "IG is a 36 year old mother of three young children who receives weekly take-home carries of methadone 70 mg daily from her pharmacy. She asks the pharmacist how to store the bottles at home between doses. Which advice is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Pour the doses into unlabelled juice bottles in the fridge, so children cannot confuse them", "B": "Keep doses in the labelled bottles in a locked box, out of reach of children", "C": "Keep doses in a bedside drawer for convenience, since the labels warn about the danger", "D": "Keep the bottles on a high kitchen shelf, since children cannot reach this height"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Keep doses in the labelled bottles in a locked box, out of reach of children): Methadone can be fatal to a child who swallows even a small amount. Doses should stay in the original labelled container within a locked box or cabinet. Transferring doses to food containers creates mistaken ingestion risk, and shelves or drawers are not secure against curious children."}, {"id": 1967, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 3, "question": "MG is a 54 year old woman with HIV and type 2 diabetes, newly started on dolutegravir with abacavir and lamivudine. She takes metformin 1000 mg twice daily with a stable A1c and a CrCl of 85 mL/min. The pharmacist reviews the new profile. Which recommendation is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Continue metformin unchanged, since dolutegravir does not affect the transporters that clear metformin", "B": "Increase metformin to 2500 mg daily, since dolutegravir lowers metformin levels through renal secretion", "C": "Limit total metformin to 1000 mg daily and monitor glucose control, renal function and tolerability", "D": "Replace metformin with glyburide, because dolutegravir increases the risk of lactic acidosis with biguanides"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Limit total metformin to 1000 mg daily and monitor glucose control, renal function and tolerability): Dolutegravir inhibits the OCT2 and MATE1 transporters that clear metformin, raising metformin exposure by about 80% at the usual dose. Capping the total daily dose at 1000 mg limits gastrointestinal effects and lactic acidosis risk while glucose is monitored. Raising the dose goes in the wrong direction, and leaving it unchanged ignores the interaction."}, {"id": 1968, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 4, "question": "A mother phones a rural pharmacy about her 2 year old son who has had a barking cough and hoarse voice since this evening. She says she can hear a high-pitched noise even when he is resting and he seems to be working hard to breathe. The pharmacist should advise:", "options": {"A": "trying a humidifier and honey for thirty minutes, then going to care if no better", "B": "running a hot shower to create steam and rechecking his breathing in the morning", "C": "calling 911 or going to the nearest emergency department now, because this is serious", "D": "giving a dose of dextromethorphan syrup and watching him overnight at home with a humidifier"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (calling 911 or going to the nearest emergency department now, because this is serious): A barking cough with stridor at rest and laboured breathing indicates moderate to severe croup, which can progress to airway obstruction and needs urgent medical care. Steam, humidifiers and honey do not treat airway narrowing, and cough suppressants are not used in toddlers."}, {"id": 1969, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "NP is a 51 year old man weighing 90 kg who is admitted with cellulitis. The order is vancomycin 15 mg/kg every 12 hours, and the pharmacist is verifying the total daily dose against the hospital protocol before the first dose is prepared. What total daily dose does this order give?", "options": {"A": "1350 mg per day, given as 675 mg every 12 hours", "B": "5400 mg per day, given as 2700 mg every 12 hours", "C": "4050 mg per day, given as 1350 mg every 8 hours", "D": "2700 mg per day, given as 1350 mg every 12 hours"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (2700 mg per day, given as 1350 mg every 12 hours): Each dose is 15 x 90 = 1350 mg. Two doses a day total 2700 mg. This is a high daily amount that many protocols cap per dose, so the pharmacist should check the protocol, but the arithmetic itself is 2700 mg per day."}, {"id": 1970, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "TV, a pharmacist, wants to begin a relationship with EN, a 37 year old former patient last counselled eight months ago for a single contraception visit. TV still holds EN's full medication and health history on file. Which consideration is most relevant before any relationship begins?", "options": {"A": "Only whether six months have passed, since that wait applies to former patients in every province", "B": "Whether the pharmacy manager approves the relationship, since employers decide boundary questions for staff", "C": "Whether the power imbalance and EN's vulnerability persist, per college guidance on former patients", "D": "Only whether EN agrees, since consent from an adult settles the matter in every case"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Whether the power imbalance and EN's vulnerability persist, per college guidance on former patients): Standards on former patients focus on whether the power imbalance and the person's vulnerability continue, rather than on a single time period. EN's consent matters but does not settle the question, and no universal waiting period exists across provinces. The employer does not decide professional boundary questions."}, {"id": 1971, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 2, "question": "HE is a 77 year old man with type 2 diabetes taking metformin 1000 mg twice daily. His admission labs show an eGFR of 24 mL/min/1.73 m2 after a bout of dehydration, and his creatinine was normal three months ago. What should the pharmacist recommend about metformin?", "options": {"A": "Hold metformin while his eGFR is below 30, and reassess it once kidney function recovers", "B": "Continue the same dose, because metformin is cleared by the liver and does not need adjustment", "C": "Reduce to 500 mg twice daily, since this eGFR still allows a lower dose", "D": "Switch to extended release at the same total dose, since it is gentler on the kidneys"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Hold metformin while his eGFR is below 30, and reassess it once kidney function recovers): Metformin is eliminated unchanged by the kidneys and is contraindicated when the eGFR falls below 30 mL/min/1.73 m2 because of the risk of lactic acidosis. It should be held until the acute kidney injury resolves and the eGFR is rechecked. A lower dose or a different formulation does not remove the accumulation risk."}, {"id": 1972, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 7, "question": "ED is a 74 year old woman taking tacrolimus after a kidney transplant. She feels low and wants to buy St John's wort. She has not told her transplant team about her mood. What should the pharmacist do? She has had no rejection episodes and her last trough level was in range.", "options": {"A": "Allow it, but increase tacrolimus by twenty percent each week while she takes the herb", "B": "Advise against St John's wort and contact the transplant team about managing her depression", "C": "Allow it if she checks her kidney function monthly, since levels of tacrolimus are unaffected", "D": "Allow it at half the labelled dose, since lower doses do not induce enzymes"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Advise against St John's wort and contact the transplant team about managing her depression): St John's wort induces CYP3A4 and P-glycoprotein, lowering tacrolimus concentrations and risking graft rejection. Her transplant team should assess her mood and choose a safe therapy. Self adjusting immunosuppressant doses is dangerous."}, {"id": 1973, "domain": "communication_and_collaboration", "chapter": 11, "question": "TR is a 64 year old man who filled a 30 day supply of rosuvastatin on January 1, February 10, March 25 and May 5, with no further fills before May 31. What is his proportion of days covered from January 1 to May 31, a period of 151 days?", "options": {"A": "About 85 percent, with 128 days covered out of 151", "B": "About 77 percent, with 117 days covered out of 151", "C": "About 60 percent, with 90 days covered out of 151", "D": "About 79 percent, with 119 days covered out of 151"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (About 77 percent, with 117 days covered out of 151): The first three fills each cover 30 days with no overlap, giving 90 days. The May 5 fill covers only May 5 to May 31, which is 27 days because the study period ends. The total is 117 of 151 days, or about 77 percent."}, {"id": 1974, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A pharmacist is evaluating a trial in which patients were followed for different lengths of time and some were lost to follow-up before experiencing a stroke. The investigators report their main result as a hazard ratio. Why is this measure suitable for this trial?", "options": {"A": "It removes confounding by matching each treated patient with a similar control patient in age", "B": "It measures the proportion of patients who had a stroke at the end of the trial", "C": "It uses time to event data and accounts for censored patients who left early", "D": "It converts the difference in means into a ratio, which suits a continuous outcome measure"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (It uses time to event data and accounts for censored patients who left early): A hazard ratio is built for time to event data, using the timing of each event and keeping patients in the analysis until they are censored. It therefore handles unequal follow-up and loss to follow-up. A simple proportion at the end of the trial ignores timing and censoring, and matching and means address different issues."}, {"id": 1975, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "A patient takes warfarin, and the dose is adjusted every one to two weeks after INR results. The pharmacy prepares four week blister cards for her other medications and the technician asks if warfarin should be included on the same card. What is the best approach?", "options": {"A": "Package the warfarin separately or in shorter cycles, so doses can change without discarding a card", "B": "Include warfarin on the four week card and tell the patient to cut or add tablets", "C": "Include warfarin on the card at the current dose and replace the card every four weeks", "D": "Exclude warfarin and ask the patient to count her tablets from the vial each day"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Package the warfarin separately or in shorter cycles, so doses can change without discarding a card): Because the warfarin dose changes often, a long card would be wrong within a week or two and would have to be discarded. Packaging it separately or in a weekly cycle keeps the dose accurate while the other drugs stay on the monthly card. Asking a patient to alter tablets by hand adds error risk."}, {"id": 1976, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 3, "question": "AP is a 30 year old woman with 3 days of dysuria who now has flank pain, a temperature of 38.8 C and chills. She asks the pharmacist to prescribe a short course of nitrofurantoin like the one that worked last year. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Recommend acetaminophen and fluids for 24 hours before deciding whether an antibiotic is required", "B": "Prescribe a single fosfomycin 3 g dose and ask her to call if no better", "C": "Decline nitrofurantoin and refer her for same-day medical assessment of possible kidney infection", "D": "Prescribe nitrofurantoin for 7 days instead of 5, because the infection appears more severe"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Decline nitrofurantoin and refer her for same-day medical assessment of possible kidney infection): Fever, chills and flank pain suggest kidney involvement, which needs prompt assessment and an agent that reaches renal tissue and blood. Nitrofurantoin and fosfomycin only reach therapeutic levels in the bladder. Delaying treatment risks bacteremia."}, {"id": 1977, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 6, "question": "SA uses a contraceptive vaginal ring that she inserted fourteen days ago. During week two it slipped out and she found it five hours later. She rinsed it with water and asks what she should do about protection now. The pharmacist should advise her to:", "options": {"A": "Rinse and reinsert it now, and use condoms for the next seven days", "B": "Rinse and reinsert it now with no backup, since it was out for under twelve hours", "C": "Reinsert it and extend ring wear by five hours at the end to compensate", "D": "Discard the ring and insert a new one after her next withdrawal bleed starts"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Rinse and reinsert it now, and use condoms for the next seven days): A ring left out for more than three hours may allow ovulation, so it is rinsed and reinserted and backup contraception is used for seven days until hormone levels are restored. Extending wear to compensate does not restore protection. Waiting for a bleed would leave her unprotected for weeks."}, {"id": 1978, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 4, "question": "BE is a 12 year old boy weighing 34 kg who has a documented milk allergy. His school nurse asks the pharmacist which epinephrine auto-injector dose to keep at school. Based on usual weight-based product guidance, the pharmacist should recommend:", "options": {"A": "either strength, since the selection depends only on the child's age and not weight", "B": "the 0.3 mg auto-injector, because his weight is above the 30 kg cutoff", "C": "the 0.15 mg auto-injector, because the 0.3 mg device is reserved for adults over eighteen", "D": "two 0.15 mg auto-injectors used together at the first sign of any allergic reaction"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (the 0.3 mg auto-injector, because his weight is above the 30 kg cutoff): Auto-injector selection follows weight bands, with the 0.3 mg device used at 30 kg or above whatever his age. At 34 kg he qualifies for the higher strength, and the lower dose could be ineffective. Using two weaker devices together is not a labelled regimen."}, {"id": 1979, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 3, "question": "XL is a 44 year old man with untreated chronic hepatitis B with HBV DNA 25,000 IU/mL and ALT 85 U/L and newly confirmed hepatitis C. His physician plans sofosbuvir/velpatasvir for twelve weeks. The pharmacist is asked about hepatitis B management during hepatitis C treatment. What should be recommended?", "options": {"A": "Start entecavir or tenofovir with the hepatitis C treatment to prevent hepatitis B reactivation", "B": "Delay hepatitis C treatment until hepatitis B vaccination has produced a protective surface antibody level", "C": "Add peginterferon to the regimen, since it treats hepatitis B and C together in coinfection", "D": "Clearing hepatitis C will also clear hepatitis B, so no hepatitis B treatment is needed"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Start entecavir or tenofovir with the hepatitis C treatment to prevent hepatitis B reactivation): Rapid clearance of hepatitis C with direct acting antivirals can release suppression of hepatitis B and cause reactivation with flares or liver failure. A patient who already meets criteria for HBV treatment should start entecavir or tenofovir alongside the DAA. Hepatitis C clearance does not clear hepatitis B, and vaccination is useless in a chronically infected person."}, {"id": 1980, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 3, "question": "WK is a 66 year old man weighing 70 kg with HIV controlled on dolutegravir plus emtricitabine/tenofovir disoproxil. His serum creatinine is now 140 umol/L, up from 90 umol/L a year ago. Using the Cockcroft-Gault equation, what is his creatinine clearance and what should the pharmacist suggest?", "options": {"A": "About 91 mL/min, so no change is needed because kidney function remains in the normal range", "B": "About 28 mL/min, so all antiretrovirals should stop until the creatinine returns to baseline", "C": "About 46 mL/min, so double the dolutegravir and continue the same tenofovir disoproxil combination product", "D": "About 46 mL/min, so suggest a tenofovir alafenamide based regimen instead of tenofovir disoproxil"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (About 46 mL/min, so suggest a tenofovir alafenamide based regimen instead of tenofovir disoproxil): Cockcroft-Gault gives (140 - 66) x 70 x 1.23 / 140 = 45.5 mL/min, below the 50 mL/min cut off for the fixed dose tenofovir disoproxil and emtricitabine combination. Tenofovir alafenamide has less renal effect and can be used down to about 30 mL/min. Doubling dolutegravir is not a kidney adjustment, and stopping all antiretrovirals risks viral rebound and resistance."}, {"id": 1981, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "A 9 year old girl with eczema is prescribed hydrocortisone 1% cream to be applied twice daily to both knees and elbows for 7 days. The pharmacist estimates that each application needs 2 fingertip units in total, and one fingertip unit is 0.5 g. What minimum quantity of cream is needed for the course?", "options": {"A": "30 g, the standard tube size dispensed for a seven day course", "B": "14 g, from 1 g per application twice daily for seven days", "C": "7 g, from 1 g per day for seven days", "D": "28 g, from 2 g per application twice daily for seven days"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (14 g, from 1 g per application twice daily for seven days): Two fingertip units is 2 x 0.5 g = 1 g per application. Twice daily is 2 g per day, and seven days is 14 g. Counting once daily gives 7 g, doubling the fingertip units gives 28 g, and a standard tube size does not reflect the calculated need."}, {"id": 1982, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 3, "question": "OP is a 52 year old man prescribed cephalexin 500 mg four times daily for 7 days for cellulitis. The pharmacy has only 250 mg capsules in stock, and he will take 500 mg per dose. What should be dispensed and how should it be labelled?", "options": {"A": "Dispense 112 capsules, giving 2 capsules four times daily for 14 days given", "B": "Dispense 28 capsules, giving 1 capsule four times daily for 7 days given", "C": "Dispense 56 capsules, giving 2 capsules four times daily for 7 days", "D": "Dispense 84 capsules, giving 3 capsules four times daily for 7 days given"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Dispense 56 capsules, giving 2 capsules four times daily for 7 days): A 500 mg dose from 250 mg capsules is 2 capsules. Four times daily for 7 days is 28 doses, so 28 x 2 = 56 capsules. The other quantities come from a single capsule per dose, three capsules per dose, or doubling the days."}, {"id": 1983, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "A pharmacy technician reports a near miss and says she fears being blamed, because a colleague was disciplined after reporting last year. Reports at the pharmacy have since fallen by half. The pharmacist-in-charge wants to rebuild reporting. Which action would most effectively restore a reporting culture?", "options": {"A": "Offer a monthly prize to the staff member who submits the highest number of incident reports", "B": "Acknowledge the past response, commit to non-punitive review, and share changes made because of reports", "C": "Allow only pharmacists to submit reports so that technicians are not exposed to disciplinary processes", "D": "Make reporting mandatory and give a written warning to staff who do not report errors"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Acknowledge the past response, commit to non-punitive review, and share changes made because of reports): Reporting depends on trust that reports lead to learning instead of blame, so leaders need to acknowledge the earlier harm to trust and show that reports lead to visible improvements. Mandatory reporting with penalties, prizes that reward volume, and restricting who may report all fail to address the fear of blame and may distort what is reported."}, {"id": 1984, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "A technician checking shelves on 1 September 2026 finds a bottle of cetirizine labelled EXP 08/2026 and another labelled EXP 09/2026. A patient needs a 14 day supply today, and the pharmacy follows the standard rule for month and year expiry dates. Which action is correct for these two bottles?", "options": {"A": "Use the 08/2026 bottle first because it is in date until the end of September", "B": "Use the 08/2026 bottle first and set the 14 day supply to end before it expires", "C": "Remove the 08/2026 bottle as expired and dispense the 14 days from the 09/2026 bottle", "D": "Remove both bottles because a 14 day supply would extend past the labelled expiry date"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Remove the 08/2026 bottle as expired and dispense the 14 days from the 09/2026 bottle): The product labelled with a month and year expires on the last day of that month, so the 08/2026 bottle expired on 31 August and cannot be used. The 09/2026 bottle remains valid through 30 September, which covers a 14 day supply starting 1 September."}, {"id": 1985, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "While reviewing records, a pharmacist finds that a 77 year old woman was dispensed warfarin 5 mg tablets instead of the prescribed 1 mg three days ago. The owner suggests quietly swapping the supply at her next visit and not recording the event. What is the pharmacist's best course?", "options": {"A": "Contact the patient and prescriber now, disclose the error, arrange monitoring, and document the incident", "B": "Wait until her next scheduled INR test, then explain the mix-up to her and the prescriber", "C": "Report the error to the college first and let it decide whether to contact the patient", "D": "Swap the supply at her next visit, logged as a refill, as the owner suggests"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Contact the patient and prescriber now, disclose the error, arrange monitoring, and document the incident): A five times higher warfarin dose for three days risks serious bleeding, so the patient and prescriber must be contacted immediately and monitoring arranged. Honest disclosure and documentation are professional duties, and concealing an error is misconduct. Waiting for a routine test or deferring to the college leaves the patient exposed in the meantime."}, {"id": 1986, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A dentist asks whether a prescribed antibiotic reduces the effectiveness of a patient's oral contraceptive. The pharmacist has clarified the patient details and ultimate question and has completed a structured search of tertiary and primary sources. According to the systematic approach, the next step is to:", "options": {"A": "Repeat the search using a different database to confirm that the original findings can be reproduced", "B": "Evaluate the retrieved information and formulate a clear response suited to the dentist's needs", "C": "Forward the retrieved articles to the dentist and let the dentist interpret the findings independently", "D": "Document the request in the pharmacy log and then close the file for audit purposes"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Evaluate the retrieved information and formulate a clear response suited to the dentist's needs): After searching, the pharmacist must appraise the quality and relevance of what was found and then build a response tailored to the requester. Sending raw articles or repeating searches does not answer the question. Documentation and follow-up come later in the process."}, {"id": 1987, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "A pharmacy manager wants to learn from other pharmacies' experiences with a look-alike packaging problem for an anticoagulant. Which freely available resource, offered to Canadian pharmacists, shares analyses of medication incidents reported across the country and recommendations to prevent them?", "options": {"A": "ISMP Canada, which publishes safety bulletins and recommendations drawn from reported medication incidents", "B": "Health Canada's Canada Vigilance program, which publishes bulletins about practice errors occurring inside Canadian pharmacies", "C": "The provincial drug formulary, which reports prescribing errors found during claims adjudication each quarter", "D": "The Canadian Pharmacists Association's drug monograph database, which lists approved indications and maximum doses"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (ISMP Canada, which publishes safety bulletins and recommendations drawn from reported medication incidents): ISMP Canada is an independent national organization that collects and analyzes medication incident reports and issues bulletins with practical recommendations. Canada Vigilance collects adverse reaction reports rather than practice errors, and drug monograph databases and formularies describe approved use and coverage rather than system learning."}, {"id": 1988, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 8, "question": "At final check, a pharmacist reads the label for a methotrexate 2.5 mg tablet prescription, written as 15 mg once weekly. The label says 'take 6 tablets daily'. The product in the vial is correct. What is the appropriate action?", "options": {"A": "Release the order and write 'weekly' on the label by hand to avoid delaying the patient", "B": "Release the order and phone the patient later to clarify the directions after pick up", "C": "Correct the label and release the order without recording it since the vial was correct", "D": "Hold the label, correct the directions to 6 tablets once weekly, and record the near miss"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Hold the label, correct the directions to 6 tablets once weekly, and record the near miss): Methotrexate is a high-alert drug and daily dosing in place of weekly dosing has caused deaths. The label must be corrected before release (6 x 2.5 mg = 15 mg once weekly). Recording the event helps the team find why the entry was wrong."}, {"id": 1989, "domain": "distribution", "chapter": 9, "question": "A pharmacist is setting up a data logger with a probe for a new vaccine refrigerator. The technician suggests taping the probe to the outside wall of the fridge. Which probe placement and setup gives the most representative record of the temperature the vaccine experiences?", "options": {"A": "Place the probe in a buffered vial among the vaccine boxes in the fridge centre", "B": "Place the probe directly next to the cooling element at the back of the fridge", "C": "Tape the probe to the inner door shelf, where it is easiest to reach and read", "D": "Leave the probe on the top of the fridge so the display is easy to read"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Place the probe in a buffered vial among the vaccine boxes in the fridge centre): The temperature that matters is the one the vaccine experiences, so the probe belongs in the central storage area. Using a buffered vial, such as glycol, reflects product temperature rather than brief air fluctuations when the door opens. Door shelves, cooling elements and the outside of the fridge all give misleading readings."}, {"id": 1990, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "A college inspector visits the pharmacy this week during a routine practice review and asks to look at a sample of prescription records, including patient names, to check labelling and documentation. No patient has been asked for consent. The pharmacist should:", "options": {"A": "Allow the review, because pharmacy legislation authorizes inspectors to examine records without patient consent", "B": "Allow the review only if the inspector brings a court order, since regulators lack independent powers", "C": "Refuse until each sampled patient has consented, since regulators are outside the circle of care", "D": "Allow the review only after blacking out patient names on every record in the sample"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Allow the review, because pharmacy legislation authorizes inspectors to examine records without patient consent): Pharmacy statutes give the regulator authority to inspect records to protect the public, and privacy laws permit disclosure that another law authorizes. Inspectors remain bound by confidentiality, and no court order is needed."}, {"id": 1991, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 1, "question": "NT is a 70 year old man with symptomatic peripheral arterial disease and an ejection fraction of 30% from heart failure. He asks his pharmacist for help getting cilostazol to improve his walking distance. What is the best response? He is a former smoker with stable kidney function.", "options": {"A": "supply cilostazol and advise weighing himself weekly, since fluid retention is the only concern", "B": "avoid cilostazol, because it is contraindicated in patients with heart failure of any severity", "C": "supply cilostazol at half the usual dose, since the low dose carries no heart failure risk", "D": "supply cilostazol only if he stops his loop diuretic, since the two drugs compete for clearance"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (avoid cilostazol, because it is contraindicated in patients with heart failure of any severity): Cilostazol is a phosphodiesterase 3 inhibitor, and drugs in this class increased mortality in heart failure, so it is contraindicated regardless of dose or severity. A supervised exercise program is the best walking-distance treatment for him."}, {"id": 1992, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "A 47 year old man develops a persistent dry cough three weeks after starting ramipril for hypertension. His prescriber stops the drug at the pharmacist's suggestion, and the cough resolves within two weeks. In causality assessment, this improvement after withdrawing a suspected drug is known as:", "options": {"A": "Positive dechallenge, which supports a causal relationship between the drug and the event", "B": "Tachyphylaxis, which indicates that the cough resolved because the patient developed tolerance to the drug", "C": "Positive rechallenge, which proves the drug caused the event once the patient is exposed again", "D": "Negative dechallenge, which suggests that the event was caused by the underlying illness instead"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Positive dechallenge, which supports a causal relationship between the drug and the event): Resolution after stopping a suspected drug is a positive dechallenge and adds support for causality, as in ACE inhibitor cough. A rechallenge means re-exposure after recovery, which was not done. A negative dechallenge means the event persists after withdrawal."}, {"id": 1993, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 5, "question": "EC is a 25 year old woman who starts topiramate 25 mg at bedtime, titrating toward 50 mg twice daily, for migraine prevention. She uses no contraception reliably. At counselling she asks what she should know about the drug. Which advice best fits the pharmacist's role?", "options": {"A": "Take extra doses before attacks, since the drug works best when used as needed for relief", "B": "Use effective contraception as topiramate is teratogenic, and drink fluids to limit kidney stone risk", "C": "Stop it if tingling occurs in the fingers, since this signals a serious nerve injury", "D": "Take it with grapefruit juice to improve absorption, and expect weight gain during the first month"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Use effective contraception as topiramate is teratogenic, and drink fluids to limit kidney stone risk): Topiramate is associated with oral clefts and low birth weight, so reliable contraception is needed, and it increases kidney stone risk, so adequate fluids are advised. Tingling in the fingers is a common, benign effect. Topiramate is a daily preventive drug that must be taken regularly and titrated gradually, and it tends to cause weight loss."}, {"id": 1994, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "The pharmacy supplier and long-term care home are reviewing how the event happened, in which a new nurse gave KL glyburide meant for another resident with a similar surname. Which system factor most directly contributed to the wrong resident's drug being given to KL?", "options": {"A": "KL's advanced age and mild dementia, which made her unable to refuse the drug offered", "B": "Look-alike packaging and side by side storage of blister cards for residents with similar surnames", "C": "The new nurse's inexperience alone, since an experienced nurse would not have made the same error", "D": "KL's concurrent use of warfarin, which makes administration of an additional drug more likely"}, "cluster_id": "CHS16", "cluster_text": "KL is a 78 year old woman in a long-term care home with atrial fibrillation, type 2 diabetes and mild dementia. Her medications include warfarin 3 mg daily (INR target 2.0 to 3.0) and metformin 500 mg twice daily. This morning a new nurse gave her glyburide 5 mg meant for another resident with a similar surname. Their blister cards look alike and were stored side by side. Two hours later her blood glucose is 3.1 mmol/L. She is alert and able to swallow.", "cluster_size": 3, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Look-alike packaging and side by side storage of blister cards for residents with similar surnames): Look-alike packaging stored together creates a system condition where two residents' drugs can easily be confused, which is a classic contributing factor in wrong-patient errors. Individual inexperience can matter, but a root cause analysis focuses on conditions that would trap any nurse. Age, dementia and warfarin use are patient characteristics that do not explain why the wrong resident's drug was selected."}, {"id": 1995, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "The nurse calls the pharmacist to report the error and KL's glucose of 3.1 mmol/L. The pharmacist confirms KL is alert and swallowing safely. Given that glyburide is long-acting and 5 mg was given two hours ago, what is the most appropriate immediate action?", "options": {"A": "Delay the next meal and recheck glucose in one hour since KL is alert", "B": "Give 15 g of fast-acting carbohydrate and resume routine glucose checks once glucose is normal", "C": "Administer intramuscular glucagon at once and transfer KL to hospital by ambulance for overnight observation", "D": "Give 15 g of fast-acting carbohydrate, recheck in 15 minutes, and arrange prolonged glucose monitoring"}, "cluster_id": "CHS16", "cluster_text": "KL is a 78 year old woman in a long-term care home with atrial fibrillation, type 2 diabetes and mild dementia. Her medications include warfarin 3 mg daily (INR target 2.0 to 3.0) and metformin 500 mg twice daily. This morning a new nurse gave her glyburide 5 mg meant for another resident with a similar surname. Their blister cards look alike and were stored side by side. Two hours later her blood glucose is 3.1 mmol/L. She is alert and able to swallow.", "cluster_size": 3, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Give 15 g of fast-acting carbohydrate, recheck in 15 minutes, and arrange prolonged glucose monitoring): A conscious patient who can swallow is treated with 15 g of fast-acting carbohydrate, with a recheck at 15 minutes. Because glyburide lasts many hours, hypoglycemia can recur, so prolonged monitoring is needed instead of routine checks. Glucagon is reserved for those who cannot take oral carbohydrate, and delaying treatment is unsafe."}, {"id": 1996, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "KL, a long-term care resident, received another resident's glyburide this morning and her blood glucose is now 3.1 mmol/L. The home's quality committee wants to prevent a repeat of wrong-resident drug administration. Which change would be the strongest error prevention measure, according to the hierarchy of effectiveness?", "options": {"A": "Introduce barcode scanning of the resident's wristband and each drug before administration", "B": "Add a written policy requiring a second nurse to initial the medication administration record each morning", "C": "Hold a staff meeting to review the event and ask nurses to take more care", "D": "Send a reminder to all nurses to verify the resident's name and photograph before giving medications"}, "cluster_id": "CHS16", "cluster_text": "KL is a 78 year old woman in a long-term care home with atrial fibrillation, type 2 diabetes and mild dementia. Her medications include warfarin 3 mg daily (INR target 2.0 to 3.0) and metformin 500 mg twice daily. This morning a new nurse gave her glyburide 5 mg meant for another resident with a similar surname. Their blister cards look alike and were stored side by side. Two hours later her blood glucose is 3.1 mmol/L. She is alert and able to swallow.", "cluster_size": 3, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Introduce barcode scanning of the resident's wristband and each drug before administration): Barcode verification builds an independent technological check into the process, which is stronger than education, reminders or additional policies that rely on memory and vigilance. Double checks can help, but they are weaker than automation that forces verification at the point of administration. Reminders and meetings address individual behaviour rather than the system conditions."}, {"id": 1997, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 7, "question": "ZK is a 67 year old man with cancer pain who started morphine sustained release 30 mg twice daily last week. He has had no bowel movement for four days and a bloated abdomen, with no vomiting. A fiber supplement has not helped. What should the pharmacist recommend?", "options": {"A": "Start a scheduled stimulant laxative such as senna, and add polyethylene glycol if needed", "B": "Halve the morphine dose until the bowels move, since constipation means the dose is too high", "C": "Increase fiber and fluids, since bulk forming laxatives are preferred for opioid constipation", "D": "Recommend a daily mineral oil dose at bedtime, which softens stool without any risk when swallowed"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Start a scheduled stimulant laxative such as senna, and add polyethylene glycol if needed): Opioids slow gut motility, so a regularly taken stimulant laxative, with an osmotic agent added if needed, is the standard approach. Bulk laxatives work poorly without good fluid intake and motility. Reducing an effective cancer pain dose is not the answer, and mineral oil carries aspiration and absorption risks."}, {"id": 1998, "domain": "clinical_care", "chapter": 3, "question": "PO is a 29 year old man who asks whether the antibody blood test advertised at a wellness clinic can show whether H. pylori has been cleared after he finished therapy last month. He feels well and takes no acid reducing medication. What should the pharmacist tell him?", "options": {"A": "The antibody test is the most accurate way to confirm cure and can be done now", "B": "Antibody levels fall within two weeks of cure, so a repeat blood test is reliable", "C": "Testing is unnecessary once symptoms resolve, because eradication rates with current regimens exceed 95%", "D": "Antibodies persist after cure, so a urea breath or stool antigen test is needed"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Antibodies persist after cure, so a urea breath or stool antigen test is needed): IgG antibodies remain detectable for months to years after the bacteria are gone, so serology cannot confirm eradication. Urea breath testing and stool antigen testing detect active infection and are done at least four weeks after antibiotics. Eradication rates with current regimens are below 95%, so confirmation is worthwhile."}, {"id": 1999, "domain": "knowledge_and_expertise", "chapter": 10, "question": "In a trial of a vaccine, 1% of vaccinated participants and 4% of placebo participants developed the infection during the season. A pharmacist preparing a summary for the clinic wants to express the relative risk reduction. What is its value?", "options": {"A": "75%, calculated as the 3 point difference divided by the placebo rate of 4%", "B": "25%, calculated as the vaccinated event rate (1%) divided by the placebo event rate (4%)", "C": "3%, calculated by subtracting the vaccinated event rate from the placebo event rate", "D": "400%, calculated as the placebo event rate (4%) divided by the vaccinated event rate (1%)"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (75%, calculated as the 3 point difference divided by the placebo rate of 4%): The relative risk reduction is the absolute risk reduction divided by the control event rate: 3 percentage points divided by 4 percent, or 75%. The 3% figure is the absolute risk reduction, and 25% is the relative risk, which is the complement of the reduction."}, {"id": 2000, "domain": "professionalism", "chapter": 12, "question": "A grocery chain asks a pharmacist consultant which categories of products it may legally stock on open shelves with no pharmacy involvement. The chain's buyer has a list that includes products from several categories of the NAPRA national drug schedules. Which category qualifies for sale without professional oversight?", "options": {"A": "Schedule I products, provided each sale is verified by a pharmacist working at another store location", "B": "Schedule II products, provided the chain trains cashiers to recite the label warnings at the checkout", "C": "Unscheduled products, which need no professional oversight or pharmacist involvement at the point of sale", "D": "Schedule III products, provided a pharmacist telephone number is posted beside the display shelf for customers"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Unscheduled products, which need no professional oversight or pharmacist involvement at the point of sale): Only unscheduled products may be sold in any retail outlet without professional oversight. Schedule III and Schedule II products must be sold from a pharmacy, and Schedule I products need a prescription. A posted telephone number or trained cashiers do not change those conditions."}];
